window.QuestFinalModules=[{"id":"b1-01","level":"B1","title":"转机与行程调整","chapter":13,"focus":"核对时间、条件和替代路线，而不是只听单个数字","vocab":[{"head":"connection","zh":"接驳；转机","pos":"noun","example":"Our connection in Berlin was delayed, so we had time to check the information desk before choosing another route.","exampleZh":"我们在柏林的接驳延误了，所以有时间先问服务台再选路线。","collocation":"miss a connection","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v1","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"departure","zh":"出发","pos":"noun","example":"The departure board showed a new gate, but it did not explain whether the boarding time had also changed.","exampleZh":"出发显示屏换了登机口，却没说明登机时间是否改变。","collocation":"departure board","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v2","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"I asked an assistant to confirm the time because my phone was still showing the original booking details.","exampleZh":"我请工作人员确认时间，因为手机仍显示原预订信息。","collocation":"confirm the time","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v3","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"available","zh":"可用的","pos":"adjective","example":"Two seats were available on an evening flight, although we would reach the hotel much later than planned.","exampleZh":"晚班机有两个座位，不过我们会比计划晚很多到酒店。","collocation":"seats available","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v4","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alternative","zh":"替代选择","pos":"noun","example":"The assistant suggested an alternative involving a train journey to a nearby airport and a shorter final flight.","exampleZh":"工作人员提出另一选择：坐火车去附近机场，再乘较短的最后一程航班。","collocation":"suggest an alternative","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v5","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"luggage","zh":"行李","pos":"noun","example":"Our luggage had already been checked in, so we needed to know whether it could travel with us.","exampleZh":"行李已经托运，所以我们需要知道能否随我们同行。","collocation":"checked luggage","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v6","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"转移；转乘","pos":"verb","example":"She could transfer our bags, but only if we accepted the new route before the next check-in deadline.","exampleZh":"她能转运行李，但前提是我们在下一次值机截止前接受新路线。","collocation":"transfer bags","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v7","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止时间","pos":"noun","example":"The deadline gave us twenty minutes to decide, which was enough to compare the arrival times carefully.","exampleZh":"截止前有二十分钟，够我们仔细比较到达时间。","collocation":"check-in deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v8","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct","zh":"直达的","pos":"adjective","example":"A direct train from the first airport would arrive later than the flight, but it offered fewer changes.","exampleZh":"从第一个机场坐直达火车会比飞机晚到，但换乘较少。","collocation":"direct train","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v9","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priority","zh":"优先考虑的事","pos":"noun","example":"My sister’s priority was avoiding another missed connection rather than arriving as early as possible that evening.","exampleZh":"姐姐优先考虑避免再错过接驳，而不是当晚尽早抵达。","collocation":"main priority","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v10","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"arrange","zh":"安排","pos":"verb","example":"We asked the hotel to arrange a late arrival and chose the train because its timetable felt more reliable.","exampleZh":"我们请酒店安排晚到，并因时刻表更可靠而选择火车。","collocation":"arrange a late arrival","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v11","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receipt","zh":"收据","pos":"noun","example":"We kept every receipt in case the airline later asked for evidence of the extra costs we had paid.","exampleZh":"我们保留每张收据，以防航空公司后来要求额外费用凭证。","collocation":"keep a receipt","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v12","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"转机与行程调整：教学情境","text":"Our connection in Berlin was delayed, so we had time to check the information desk before choosing another route. The departure board showed a new gate, but it did not explain whether the boarding time had also changed. I asked an assistant to confirm the time because my phone was still showing the original booking details. Two seats were available on an evening flight, although we would reach the hotel much later than planned. The assistant suggested an alternative involving a train journey to a nearby airport and a shorter final flight. Our luggage had already been checked in, so we needed to know whether it could travel with us. She could transfer our bags, but only if we accepted the new route before the next check-in deadline. The deadline gave us twenty minutes to decide, which was enough to compare the arrival times carefully. A direct train from the first airport would arrive later than the flight, but it offered fewer changes. My sister’s priority was avoiding another missed connection rather than arriving as early as possible that evening. We asked the hotel to arrange a late arrival and chose the train because its timetable felt more reliable. We kept every receipt in case the airline later asked for evidence of the extra costs we had paid."},"sourceB":{"title":"A ferry after a cancellation","text":"Ravi and his friend planned to take a ferry to an island on Friday afternoon. At the harbour they learned that their sailing had been cancelled because of strong winds. The next ferry might leave on Saturday morning, but staff could not guarantee it. Ravi wanted to wait because they had already booked a room on the island. His friend suggested staying in the coastal town instead.\nBefore deciding, they contacted the island hotel. The owner explained that guests whose ferry was cancelled could move their booking without an extra charge, provided they called before six that evening. The friends also checked the last bus to the coastal town, which left at five forty.\nThey chose the bus and moved their hotel booking to the following weekend. This meant changing their plans, but it avoided spending the night at a harbour with no confirmed departure. On the bus, Ravi admitted that his first choice had been based mainly on not wanting to lose the room. Once they knew the hotel’s conditions, they could make a calmer decision using the information actually available."},"listening":{"teaching":"Hello, this is Leila from Greenway Coaches. You booked a seat on tomorrow’s nine o’clock service to the lake. Road repairs mean the coach will now leave from the west side of the station rather than the usual entrance. The departure time is unchanged. Please arrive fifteen minutes early because the temporary stop is a short walk from the ticket office. Your ticket remains valid, and there is no need to print a replacement. If you have difficulty walking, call us this afternoon so the driver can explain a closer meeting point. The return journey will use the normal stop because the repairs should be finished by then.","test":"Good afternoon. I am calling about your bicycle reservation for Saturday’s hill train. Passenger seats are still available, but the bicycle space you requested cannot be confirmed on the ten o’clock service. We can carry both you and your bike on the eleven thirty departure. Alternatively, you may keep your original passenger seat and hire a bicycle after you arrive. The hire shop is beside the destination station, although its charge is not included in your train ticket. Please reply by four today. If we do not hear from you, we will keep the passenger booking and cancel only the unconfirmed bicycle request."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the travellers ask about luggage?","options":["The hotel needed the flight number.","The train charged separately for every bag.","It had already been checked in."],"answer":"It had already been checked in.","explanation":"原文说明已托运，须确认行李能否转运。","id":"b1-01:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did they choose the train?","options":["It would reach the hotel earlier.","Avoiding another missed connection mattered most.","It avoided contacting the hotel again."],"answer":"Avoiding another missed connection mattered most.","explanation":"姐姐的重点是减少接驳风险而非最早到达。","id":"b1-01:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What changed about the coach?","options":["Its departure stop.","Its departure time.","Its return stop."],"answer":"Its departure stop.","explanation":"西侧临时站代替原入口，时间没变。","id":"b1-01:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Who should call the coach company?","options":["Passengers wanting to print a replacement ticket.","Passengers joining the return coach.","Passengers who have difficulty walking."],"answer":"Passengers who have difficulty walking.","explanation":"提前联系可获得更近集合点说明。","id":"b1-01:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the island hotel allow?","options":["Keeping the original room until Saturday without calling.","Moving the booking after a ferry cancellation.","Receiving transport from the harbour at no charge."],"answer":"Moving the booking after a ferry cancellation.","explanation":"只要六点前联系，可改期且无额外费。","id":"b1-01:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was Ravi initially reluctant to change plans?","options":["He did not want to lose the booked room.","He expected the coastal town to be more expensive.","He thought the bus would miss its last departure."],"answer":"He did not want to lose the booked room.","explanation":"他承认原决定主要怕损失房间。","id":"b1-01:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can be confirmed at eleven thirty?","options":["A bicycle on the original ten o’clock departure.","Bicycle hire included in the rail price.","Travel with the bicycle."],"answer":"Travel with the bicycle.","explanation":"十一点半有乘客及自行车空间。","id":"b1-01:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens without a reply?","options":["Both seat and bicycle booking move to eleven thirty.","The passenger seat stays, but the bike request is cancelled.","The passenger seat is cancelled but the bike request remains."],"answer":"The passenger seat stays, but the bike request is cancelled.","explanation":"留言明确区分乘客座位与未确认自行车请求。","id":"b1-01:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a message to a hotel explaining a travel delay. State your expected arrival, ask about late check-in and explain what you still need to confirm.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare two travel alternatives after a cancellation, explaining your priorities.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["旅客","交通服务人员"],"goal":"选择一条可行路线并明确行李、费用和时间条件。","twist":"服务人员告知最早路线需要再次换乘，另一条晚到但直达。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write to the island hotel explaining your cancelled ferry, the booking change you want and the condition you need confirmed.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss the ferry travellers’ decision. Explain which new information made changing plans more reasonable.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-02","level":"B1","title":"住宿问题与礼貌协商","chapter":14,"focus":"清楚描述问题、提出合理请求并核对解决条件","vocab":[{"head":"reservation","zh":"预订","pos":"noun","example":"When we reached the guesthouse, our reservation was on the system, but the room described in the email was unavailable.","exampleZh":"我们到旅馆时系统有预订，但邮件描述的房间没空。","collocation":"confirm a reservation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v1","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ground floor","zh":"一楼；地面层","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We had requested a ground floor room because my father found long flights of stairs difficult after an operation.","exampleZh":"我们要求地面层房间，因为父亲术后难爬长楼梯。","collocation":"ground floor room","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v2","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receptionist","zh":"接待员","pos":"noun","example":"The receptionist apologised and showed us a larger room upstairs, without first checking whether there was a working lift.","exampleZh":"接待员道歉，带我们看楼上更大的房，却没先检查电梯是否正常。","collocation":"ask the receptionist","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v3","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"access","zh":"通行；使用","pos":"noun","example":"I explained that easy access mattered more to us than extra space or a view across the garden.","exampleZh":"我解释通行方便比额外空间或花园景色更重要。","collocation":"easy access","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v4","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"temporary","zh":"临时的","pos":"adjective","example":"She offered a temporary room beside the dining area while the manager checked arrangements at a nearby guesthouse.","exampleZh":"她提供餐区旁的临时房，让经理去查附近旅馆安排。","collocation":"temporary room","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v5","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disturb","zh":"打扰","pos":"verb","example":"We asked whether breakfast preparation would disturb my father early in the morning, since he needed plenty of rest.","exampleZh":"我们问早餐准备是否会一早打扰父亲，因为他需要充足休息。","collocation":"disturb a guest","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v6","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"The manager listened carefully and explained that the neighbouring building had an accessible room available for the whole stay.","exampleZh":"经理认真听后解释，隔壁建筑全程都有无障碍房。","collocation":"explain the situation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v7","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"additional","zh":"额外的","pos":"adjective","example":"There would be no additional payment, and the guesthouse would organise transport for our bags rather than expect us to carry them.","exampleZh":"无需额外付费，旅馆会安排运包，而不是让我们自己搬。","collocation":"additional payment","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v8","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agreement","zh":"约定","pos":"noun","example":"Before accepting the agreement, I checked that breakfast and the final night were included, just as in our original booking.","exampleZh":"接受约定前，我核对早餐及最后一晚都像原订单一样包含。","collocation":"check an agreement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v9","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"written","zh":"书面的","pos":"adjective","example":"We requested written confirmation so that staff at the other building would understand the arrangements when we arrived later.","exampleZh":"我们要求书面确认，让另一建筑员工稍后接待时懂安排。","collocation":"written confirmation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v10","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"solution","zh":"解决办法","pos":"noun","example":"The solution was not our first choice, but it met the important need that the larger upstairs room could not.","exampleZh":"解决办法不是首选，却满足楼上大房不能满足的重要需求。","collocation":"practical solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v11","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"patient","zh":"耐心的","pos":"adjective","example":"Remaining patient helped us keep the discussion focused on practical needs instead of repeating how disappointed we felt that afternoon.","exampleZh":"保持耐心帮助我们聚焦实际需求，而非反复表达当天下午多失望。","collocation":"remain patient","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v12","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"住宿问题与礼貌协商：教学情境","text":"When we reached the guesthouse, our reservation was on the system, but the room described in the email was unavailable. We had requested a ground floor room because my father found long flights of stairs difficult after an operation. The receptionist apologised and showed us a larger room upstairs, without first checking whether there was a working lift. I explained that easy access mattered more to us than extra space or a view across the garden. She offered a temporary room beside the dining area while the manager checked arrangements at a nearby guesthouse. We asked whether breakfast preparation would disturb my father early in the morning, since he needed plenty of rest. The manager listened carefully and explained that the neighbouring building had an accessible room available for the whole stay. There would be no additional payment, and the guesthouse would organise transport for our bags rather than expect us to carry them. Before accepting the agreement, I checked that breakfast and the final night were included, just as in our original booking. We requested written confirmation so that staff at the other building would understand the arrangements when we arrived later. The solution was not our first choice, but it met the important need that the larger upstairs room could not."},"sourceB":{"title":"A noisy holiday apartment","text":"Marta rented an apartment near a market because the description promised a quiet place for remote work. On the first morning, building work began in the apartment above. She contacted the owner and explained that she had online meetings for three afternoons. The owner said the repairs were urgent but offered a refund for the unused nights.\nMarta did not accept immediately. Finding another apartment at short notice would take time, and she liked the location. She asked whether there was a quiet room elsewhere in the building that she could use during meetings. The owner checked with a neighbour who managed a small office on the ground floor.\nThey agreed that Marta could use the office from one until five, when it was normally empty. The arrangement did not stop the morning noise, but it protected the part of her stay that mattered most. She asked the owner to update the listing for future visitors. A helpful solution to her own difficulty did not remove the need to tell other guests about the repair work before they booked."},"listening":{"teaching":"Welcome to Willow House. Breakfast is usually served from seven to nine, but guests joining the early walking tour may collect a packed breakfast from reception at six thirty. Please tell us by eight this evening if you want one. The kitchen cannot prepare individual requests after that time. Water bottles can be filled from the tap beside the dining room; there is no charge. The front door locks automatically at ten at night, so take your room card if you go out. If the card stops working, use the bell on the left of the door rather than the one marked delivery. A member of staff is available overnight.","test":"Hello, this is the manager of River Rooms. You asked whether you could leave your bags with us after checkout tomorrow. We can store them until six in the evening, but reception will close between one and two while staff attend training. If you need to collect the bags during that hour, please tell us tonight so we can arrange access through the café next door. There is no storage fee. We cannot leave valuable items unattended, however, so please keep passports and small electronic devices with you. The taxi you requested is confirmed for five thirty, and the driver will meet you at the main entrance."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What mattered most to the family?","options":["A quieter view across the garden.","More space for the same price.","Easy access for the father."],"answer":"Easy access for the father.","explanation":"父亲术后不便爬楼，通行优先。","id":"b1-02:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why request written confirmation?","options":["To ask the manager to extend their stay.","To make the transfer arrangements clear to other staff.","To change the breakfast time."],"answer":"To make the transfer arrangements clear to other staff.","explanation":"书面信息避免另一处接待误解。","id":"b1-02:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"When must packed breakfast be requested?","options":["By eight this evening.","By seven tomorrow morning.","By nine this evening."],"answer":"By eight this evening.","explanation":"留言给出明确截止时间。","id":"b1-02:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which bell should a locked-out guest use?","options":["The bell marked delivery.","The bell inside the dining room.","The bell on the left."],"answer":"The bell on the left.","explanation":"门卡失效时用左侧铃。","id":"b1-02:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Marta hesitate over the refund?","options":["The office was too far from the apartment.","Moving would take time and she liked the location.","The owner had refused to offer money back."],"answer":"Moving would take time and she liked the location.","explanation":"退款并不直接解决换房成本。","id":"b1-02:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did Marta want future listings to mention?","options":["The repair work.","The availability of a free permanent office.","The number of online meetings she personally held."],"answer":"The repair work.","explanation":"个人妥协不代替对后续客人如实说明。","id":"b1-02:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"When will reception be closed?","options":["Between five thirty and six.","Between noon and one.","From one until two."],"answer":"From one until two.","explanation":"员工培训导致一到二点关闭。","id":"b1-02:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should guests keep with them?","options":["Identification only after collecting the taxi.","Passports and small electronic devices.","Large bags that cannot fit in the café."],"answer":"Passports and small electronic devices.","explanation":"贵重小件不进入无人看管寄存。","id":"b1-02:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a polite email about a room that does not meet an important booking requirement. Explain the requirement and request two possible solutions.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Describe a booking problem and negotiate a practical remedy without exaggerating.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["住客","住宿经理"],"goal":"明确核心需求并确认转房费用及服务。","twist":"经理提供更大房间，但它仍不满足行动不便者的通行需求。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write to an apartment owner about a noisy stay during remote work. Propose a remedy other than moving immediately.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Negotiate a quiet workspace while recognising that urgent building repairs may continue.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-03","level":"B1","title":"身体不适与清楚求助","chapter":15,"focus":"准确报告症状、时间与既有情况，核对专业人员指示","vocab":[{"head":"unwell","zh":"身体不适的","pos":"adjective","example":"During our walking holiday, my cousin felt unwell and decided to stop before the group reached the next village.","exampleZh":"徒步度假时表弟身体不适，决定不到下一村就停下。","collocation":"feel unwell","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v1","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"symptom","zh":"症状","pos":"noun","example":"He described each symptom to the tour leader instead of simply saying that everything felt wrong that afternoon.","exampleZh":"他逐项向领队描述症状，而不只说那天下午哪儿都不舒服。","collocation":"describe a symptom","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v2","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"dizzy","zh":"眩晕的","pos":"adjective","example":"He felt dizzy when standing up, although he had not fallen or hit his head on the path.","exampleZh":"他站起时眩晕，不过没有跌倒或在路上撞头。","collocation":"feel dizzy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v3","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appointment","zh":"预约","pos":"noun","example":"The leader called a local clinic and asked whether an appointment was available before its normal closing time.","exampleZh":"领队打电话问当地诊所正常关门前是否有预约。","collocation":"make an appointment","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v4","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"describe","zh":"描述","pos":"verb","example":"A staff member asked him to describe when the problem began and whether it had happened on earlier walks.","exampleZh":"工作人员让他描述何时开始及以前徒步是否发生过。","collocation":"describe a problem","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v5","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allergy","zh":"过敏","pos":"noun","example":"He mentioned an allergy to a medicine and showed the name saved on his phone to avoid a misunderstanding.","exampleZh":"他提到一种药物过敏，并展示手机保存的药名避免误会。","collocation":"mention an allergy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v6","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"insurance","zh":"保险","pos":"noun","example":"I found his travel insurance details while the leader arranged transport, rather than leaving him to manage everything alone.","exampleZh":"领队安排交通时我找到旅行保险资料，不让他独自处理一切。","collocation":"travel insurance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v7","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"instructions","zh":"指示","pos":"plural noun","example":"At the clinic, he asked for the instructions in writing because he was worried about forgetting important details later.","exampleZh":"在诊所他要求书面指示，担心稍后忘记重要细节。","collocation":"written instructions","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v8","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"repeat","zh":"重复","pos":"verb","example":"The nurse was willing to repeat unfamiliar words, and he checked his understanding by explaining the main points back.","exampleZh":"护士愿意重复生词，他通过复述要点核对理解。","collocation":"repeat a word","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v9","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"She also explained whom to contact if his condition changed, without expecting the tour leader to make a medical judgement.","exampleZh":"她还说明情况变化时联系谁，而不让领队作医学判断。","collocation":"contact the clinic","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v10","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recover","zh":"恢复","pos":"verb","example":"He stayed near the village to recover and followed the clinic’s advice instead of trying to finish the original route.","exampleZh":"他留在村附近恢复并遵从诊所建议，而非尝试走完原路线。","collocation":"recover gradually","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v11","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"support","zh":"支持；帮助","pos":"noun","example":"Practical support meant listening and helping him reach professional care, not guessing a diagnosis from our own experiences.","exampleZh":"实际帮助是倾听并协助就医，而非根据自身经验猜诊断。","collocation":"practical support","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v12","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"身体不适与清楚求助：教学情境","text":"During our walking holiday, my cousin felt unwell and decided to stop before the group reached the next village. He described each symptom to the tour leader instead of simply saying that everything felt wrong that afternoon. He felt dizzy when standing up, although he had not fallen or hit his head on the path. The leader called a local clinic and asked whether an appointment was available before its normal closing time. A staff member asked him to describe when the problem began and whether it had happened on earlier walks. He mentioned an allergy to a medicine and showed the name saved on his phone to avoid a misunderstanding. I found his travel insurance details while the leader arranged transport, rather than leaving him to manage everything alone. At the clinic, he asked for the instructions in writing because he was worried about forgetting important details later. The nurse was willing to repeat unfamiliar words, and he checked his understanding by explaining the main points back. She also explained whom to contact if his condition changed, without expecting the tour leader to make a medical judgement. He stayed near the village to recover and followed the clinic’s advice instead of trying to finish the original route."},"sourceB":{"title":"A dental appointment abroad","text":"While visiting a friend overseas, Hana developed tooth pain that kept returning during meals. She searched for a nearby dental clinic and phoned rather than relying only on its booking website. The receptionist asked when the pain had started and whether she had received recent dental treatment. Hana explained that she was a temporary visitor and wanted to know the likely appointment cost before confirming.\nThe clinic could offer a check the following morning. It could not promise the full treatment price before a dentist examined her. Hana asked whether she could receive a written estimate before agreeing to any further work. The receptionist confirmed that this was possible and asked her to bring identification and details of any regular medication.\nHana wrote down the address, time and cancellation condition. She also checked the route because the clinic entrance was on a side street rather than the main road shown on the map. Her friend offered to accompany her. Hana accepted the help, while making clear that she wanted to answer the dentist’s questions herself and ask for explanations whenever something was unclear."},"listening":{"teaching":"Hello, this is Lakeside Medical Centre confirming your appointment at ten twenty tomorrow. Please arrive ten minutes early to complete a short form. Bring identification and a list of any medicines you currently take, including their names if you know them. You do not need to send private health information by ordinary email before you arrive. If you require an interpreter, please call reception today so we can check availability; bringing a friend does not always replace professional interpreting. The centre entrance is beside the pharmacy, but the pharmacy itself does not handle appointments. If you cannot attend, contact reception as soon as possible so another patient can use the time.","test":"This is a reminder from Oak Street Dental Clinic. Your appointment on Wednesday is at two fifteen, not two fifty as stated in our earlier message. We apologise for the error. The dentist will examine the problem first and discuss possible treatment and charges before you decide whether to continue. Please do not assume the examination fee covers every later procedure. If you have previous dental records available, you may bring them, but your appointment will still go ahead without them. The lift is temporarily out of service. Anyone who cannot use the stairs should telephone us before travelling so we can discuss an accessible arrangement."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the cousin show a name on his phone?","options":["To show the insurance payment.","To confirm the walking route.","To make the medicine allergy clear."],"answer":"To make the medicine allergy clear.","explanation":"药名展示减少过敏信息误解。","id":"b1-03:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did he check his understanding?","options":["By asking the leader to explain a diagnosis.","By explaining the main instructions back.","By reading only the appointment time."],"answer":"By explaining the main instructions back.","explanation":"复述是核对理解而非自诊。","id":"b1-03:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why arrive early for the medical appointment?","options":["To complete a form.","To collect a medicine before the clinic opens.","To speak to the pharmacy receptionist."],"answer":"To complete a form.","explanation":"要求提前十分钟填表。","id":"b1-03:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should someone needing an interpreter do?","options":["Ask a friend to arrive without informing reception.","Send a private medical history by ordinary email.","Call reception today."],"answer":"Call reception today.","explanation":"需要先核查口译人员可用性。","id":"b1-03:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What could the dental clinic not promise by phone?","options":["The cost of the first appointment itself.","The full treatment price before examination.","Whether identification was needed."],"answer":"The full treatment price before examination.","explanation":"检查后才能明确完整治疗费用。","id":"b1-03:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Hana check the route?","options":["The entrance was on a side street.","The published opening time had changed.","The map showed the wrong town."],"answer":"The entrance was on a side street.","explanation":"地图主路不等于实际入口。","id":"b1-03:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What was corrected in the dental reminder?","options":["The examination fee.","The day of the appointment.","The appointment time."],"answer":"The appointment time.","explanation":"两点十五取代两点五十。","id":"b1-03:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Who should call before travelling?","options":["Someone without earlier dental records.","Someone unable to use stairs.","Someone who has not paid for treatment in advance."],"answer":"Someone unable to use stairs.","explanation":"电梯故障，需要无障碍安排者应提前联系。","id":"b1-03:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a message requesting an appointment. Describe the timing of a problem without diagnosing it, ask about costs and explain any access or language needs.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Practise reporting a problem clearly and asking a professional to repeat or explain instructions.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["旅行者","诊所接待人员"],"goal":"预约并确认时间、费用范围、需带资料和语言需求。","twist":"接待员不能电话确认全部治疗费用，并提醒入口有楼梯。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a non-medical appointment enquiry to a dental clinic. Ask about the examination, a written estimate and how to find the entrance.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Ask a dental receptionist to clarify an appointment correction, access arrangements and what the initial fee covers.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-04","level":"B1","title":"购买、退换与售后","chapter":16,"focus":"区分商品问题、个人偏好及退换条件","vocab":[{"head":"purchase","zh":"购买的物品","pos":"noun","example":"I took a recent purchase back to the outdoor shop after discovering that its zip caught whenever I closed the jacket.","exampleZh":"我发现夹克拉链每次拉上会卡，就把最近购买的物品带回户外店。","collocation":"return a purchase","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v1","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"faulty","zh":"有故障的","pos":"adjective","example":"The assistant asked me to show why I thought the fastening was faulty rather than simply choosing the wrong size.","exampleZh":"店员让我展示为何认为扣件有问题，而非只是选错尺寸。","collocation":"faulty fastening","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v2","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receipt","zh":"收据","pos":"noun","example":"I had kept the receipt, which showed the purchase date and made it easier to find the original transaction.","exampleZh":"我保留收据，上有购买日期，更容易找到原交易。","collocation":"keep the receipt","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v3","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exchange","zh":"换货","pos":"noun","example":"An exchange seemed practical because I still liked the jacket and wanted one that worked for the walking trip.","exampleZh":"换货似乎实际，因为我仍喜欢夹克，想要一件徒步时能正常用的。","collocation":"request an exchange","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v4","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replacement","zh":"替换品","pos":"noun","example":"Before accepting a replacement, I tested its zip several times and checked that the pocket seams were properly closed.","exampleZh":"接受替换品前，我多试几次拉链，并检查口袋缝线收好。","collocation":"check a replacement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v5","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stock","zh":"库存","pos":"noun","example":"The same colour was not in stock, but the shop could order it from another branch within four days.","exampleZh":"同色没有库存，但店里能四天内从另一分店调来。","collocation":"in stock","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v6","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止日期","pos":"noun","example":"My travel deadline was sooner than that, so waiting for the preferred colour would not meet my immediate need.","exampleZh":"我的出行时间更早，等待喜欢的颜色无法满足眼前需要。","collocation":"meet a deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v7","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refund","zh":"退款","pos":"noun","example":"The assistant explained a refund option, although I wanted to compare it with taking a different colour that afternoon.","exampleZh":"店员解释退款选项，不过我想与当天下午拿其他颜色比较。","collocation":"offer a refund","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v8","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"I asked whether the sale price changed any condition of the proposed exchange and listened carefully to the explanation.","exampleZh":"我问促销价是否改变换货条件，并仔细听解释。","collocation":"exchange condition","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v9","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision","zh":"决定","pos":"noun","example":"My decision depended on a working jacket for the trip, not on proving that every part of the shopping experience was bad.","exampleZh":"我的决定取决于旅途有能用的夹克，不是证明购物经历处处糟糕。","collocation":"make a decision","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v10","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"We agreed on another colour, and the assistant gave me a new receipt to confirm exactly what had been exchanged.","exampleZh":"我们同意换另一颜色，店员给新收据确认具体换了什么。","collocation":"confirm an exchange","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v11","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reasonable","zh":"合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reasonable solution came from describing the fault clearly and explaining the practical problem caused by the delivery times.","exampleZh":"合理解决来自清楚描述故障，并解释配送时间造成的实际困难。","collocation":"reasonable solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v12","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"购买、退换与售后：教学情境","text":"I took a recent purchase back to the outdoor shop after discovering that its zip caught whenever I closed the jacket. The assistant asked me to show why I thought the fastening was faulty rather than simply choosing the wrong size. I had kept the receipt, which showed the purchase date and made it easier to find the original transaction. An exchange seemed practical because I still liked the jacket and wanted one that worked for the walking trip. Before accepting a replacement, I tested its zip several times and checked that the pocket seams were properly closed. The same colour was not in stock, but the shop could order it from another branch within four days. My travel deadline was sooner than that, so waiting for the preferred colour would not meet my immediate need. The assistant explained a refund option, although I wanted to compare it with taking a different colour that afternoon. I asked whether the sale price changed any condition of the proposed exchange and listened carefully to the explanation. My decision depended on a working jacket for the trip, not on proving that every part of the shopping experience was bad. We agreed on another colour, and the assistant gave me a new receipt to confirm exactly what had been exchanged."},"sourceB":{"title":"A repair instead of a return","text":"Omar bought a small speaker for listening to language lessons. After three weeks, the charging connection became loose. He contacted the seller and explained that the sound still worked, but the battery would not charge reliably. The seller asked for a short video showing the problem and offered to arrange a repair.\nOmar first wanted an immediate replacement because he used the speaker every day. However, the seller explained that the repair centre could inspect it within two days and lend him another unit while he waited. Before accepting, Omar asked who would pay the delivery charges and what would happen if the fault could not be repaired. He received those details in writing.\nThe speaker returned a week later with a replaced connection and a repair record. Omar tested it before sending back the borrowed unit. He was pleased with the result but suggested that the seller should explain the repair process more clearly on its website. Customers would be less anxious if they knew the likely timetable and available temporary support before having to complain about a fault."},"listening":{"teaching":"Thanks for contacting City Cameras about your order. The lens you requested is available, but the carrying case is delayed. You can choose to receive the lens tomorrow and the case next week, with no second delivery charge. Alternatively, we can hold the complete order until both items are ready. Please tell us which you prefer before three this afternoon. If you need the case for a trip this weekend, we can cancel that item and refund its cost without changing your lens order. The case shown in the alternative photograph is a different size, so we do not recommend it for this lens.","test":"Hello, I am calling from the repair desk about your watch. The inspection found a damaged strap fitting rather than a battery problem. We can replace the fitting, but we need your agreement because this repair is outside the service package you bought. The charge is written in the email we sent today. Please reply to that email or call us before Friday. If you decide not to continue, we will return the watch unrepaired and you will pay only the inspection charge already agreed. We will not carry out extra work simply because we have the watch here. Your original strap is being kept safely with it."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What evidence helped the shop find the transaction?","options":["The jacket’s repair history.","A photograph of the shop window.","The receipt."],"answer":"The receipt.","explanation":"收据含日期，可查购买记录。","id":"b1-04:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why not wait for the preferred colour?","options":["The preferred colour cost more.","The trip began before it could arrive.","The shop had already ordered the wrong size."],"answer":"The trip began before it could arrive.","explanation":"时间限制比颜色更重要。","id":"b1-04:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can the customer receive tomorrow?","options":["The lens without the delayed case.","The lens together with the requested case.","A suitable alternative case at the same price."],"answer":"The lens without the delayed case.","explanation":"镜头可先送，包下周送且无第二次运费。","id":"b1-04:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is the alternative case not recommended?","options":["It would arrive after the trip.","It came with a separate delivery charge.","It is a different size."],"answer":"It is a different size.","explanation":"尺寸不适合该镜头。","id":"b1-04:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What temporary support did Omar receive?","options":["A refund before the inspection.","A borrowed speaker during the repair.","A replacement speaker to keep permanently."],"answer":"A borrowed speaker during the repair.","explanation":"借用设备解决日常使用中断。","id":"b1-04:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did Omar confirm before accepting?","options":["Delivery charges and the outcome if repair failed.","Whether he could change the speaker’s colour.","Whether the seller would refund his lessons."],"answer":"Delivery charges and the outcome if repair failed.","explanation":"明确费用及不能修复时安排。","id":"b1-04:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What caused the watch problem?","options":["An old battery.","Damage to the original strap itself.","A damaged strap fitting."],"answer":"A damaged strap fitting.","explanation":"检查区分了故障来源。","id":"b1-04:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens if extra work is declined?","options":["The shop completes the repair and charges later.","The watch returns unrepaired under the agreed inspection charge.","The inspection charge is automatically removed."],"answer":"The watch returns unrepaired under the agreed inspection charge.","explanation":"未经同意不开展收费额外维修。","id":"b1-04:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a clear after-sales message about a faulty product. Explain the fault, evidence, preferred remedy and a practical time limit.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Negotiate a repair, replacement or refund while checking cost and timing.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["顾客","售后人员"],"goal":"选定能满足实际需要的售后方案并确认条件。","twist":"最喜欢的替换品不能及时送达，但可提供临时借用品。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write to a speaker seller confirming a proposed repair, temporary loan, delivery costs and what should happen if repair is unsuccessful.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Choose between accepting the watch repair and declining it after checking the agreed inspection charge.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-05","level":"B1","title":"问路与无障碍出行","chapter":17,"focus":"根据实际通行条件理解方向，不把最近当作最合适","vocab":[{"head":"route","zh":"路线","pos":"noun","example":"A visitor asked me for a route to the museum while we were waiting outside the station.","exampleZh":"一位游客在车站外等候时向我问去博物馆的路线。","collocation":"plan a route","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v1","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entrance","zh":"入口","pos":"noun","example":"The nearest entrance was beside a narrow bridge, but I knew there were several steps beyond it.","exampleZh":"最近入口在窄桥旁，但我知道后面有几级台阶。","collocation":"nearest entrance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v2","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"wheelchair","zh":"轮椅","pos":"noun","example":"His daughter used a wheelchair, so I asked whether he wanted a step-free route before giving directions.","exampleZh":"他女儿用轮椅，所以指路前我问是否需要无台阶路线。","collocation":"use a wheelchair","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v3","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"crossing","zh":"过街处","pos":"noun","example":"I suggested the crossing with traffic lights rather than the shorter path through a busy car park.","exampleZh":"我建议走有信号灯的过街处，而非穿越繁忙停车场的近路。","collocation":"pedestrian crossing","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v4","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opposite","zh":"在对面","pos":"preposition","example":"The museum’s accessible gate was opposite the library, which was easy to recognise by its yellow doors.","exampleZh":"博物馆无障碍门在图书馆对面，黄门很容易认。","collocation":"opposite the library","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v5","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"straight ahead","zh":"一直向前","pos":"adverb phrase","example":"I told him to go straight ahead after the crossing until he reached the small public garden.","exampleZh":"我让他过马路后一直向前，到小公共花园。","collocation":"go straight ahead","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v6","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turn","zh":"转弯","pos":"verb","example":"There he should turn left, keeping the garden on his right until the library came into view.","exampleZh":"那里左转，把花园留在右侧，直到看到图书馆。","collocation":"turn left","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v7","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distance","zh":"距离","pos":"noun","example":"The extra distance was modest, although the route would probably take longer than the map first suggested.","exampleZh":"多走距离不大，不过路线可能比地图起初显示耗时长。","collocation":"extra distance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v8","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surface","zh":"路面","pos":"noun","example":"He asked about the surface because rough stones had made the previous afternoon’s walk surprisingly difficult.","exampleZh":"他问路面情况，因为昨天下午的粗石路让行走意外困难。","collocation":"smooth surface","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v9","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"check","zh":"核查","pos":"verb","example":"I offered to check the museum website in case construction had temporarily changed the accessible entrance.","exampleZh":"我提出查官网，以防施工临时改变无障碍入口。","collocation":"check the website","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v10","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"Before leaving, he repeated the main turns so I could explain anything that still sounded unclear.","exampleZh":"离开前他重复主要转弯，让我解释仍不清楚之处。","collocation":"explain a direction","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v11","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confident","zh":"有把握的","pos":"adjective","example":"He felt more confident with a suitable route than with a quick promise that everything was nearby.","exampleZh":"合适路线比一句一切就在附近的快速保证更让他有把握。","collocation":"feel confident","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v12","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"问路与无障碍出行：教学情境","text":"A visitor asked me for a route to the museum while we were waiting outside the station. The nearest entrance was beside a narrow bridge, but I knew there were several steps beyond it. His daughter used a wheelchair, so I asked whether he wanted a step-free route before giving directions. I suggested the crossing with traffic lights rather than the shorter path through a busy car park. The museum’s accessible gate was opposite the library, which was easy to recognise by its yellow doors. I told him to go straight ahead after the crossing until he reached the small public garden. There he should turn left, keeping the garden on his right until the library came into view. The extra distance was modest, although the route would probably take longer than the map first suggested. He asked about the surface because rough stones had made the previous afternoon’s walk surprisingly difficult. I offered to check the museum website in case construction had temporarily changed the accessible entrance. Before leaving, he repeated the main turns so I could explain anything that still sounded unclear. He felt more confident with a suitable route than with a quick promise that everything was nearby."},"sourceB":{"title":"Finding a temporary library","text":"A town library moved into a former school while its main building was repaired. The online map still directed visitors to the old entrance, so the temporary staff placed notices at both locations. Jo arrived by bus and followed a notice saying the new library was ten minutes away. She soon discovered that the suggested path crossed a steep hill.\nJo was carrying books and recovering from a knee injury. She phoned the library rather than continue along a difficult route. A librarian explained that a level path followed the river, although it was slightly longer. It ended beside the school’s side gate, which remained open during library hours.\nJo followed this alternative and arrived comfortably. She suggested that future notices mention both routes instead of describing the quickest one as suitable for everybody. The librarian thanked her and added a short accessibility note to the website. The temporary building had not become closer, but clearer information allowed visitors to choose a route according to their needs rather than discover important difficulties halfway through the journey."},"listening":{"teaching":"If you are joining today’s town walk, please meet outside the old post office at ten. The bridge near the market is closed, so do not follow last year’s printed route. We will use the riverside path instead. It is level, but part of it may be wet after the morning rain. Please wear suitable shoes. The walk will finish at the community garden, where a volunteer can explain the planting project. Anyone wishing to return directly to the starting point can leave the group at the library corner. Let the guide know before doing so, because we need to keep an accurate count of participants.","test":"Welcome to the university open day. Visitors arriving from the bus stop should follow the blue arrows towards the main courtyard. The red arrows lead to student accommodation tours, which begin later. If you need a step-free entrance, continue past the courtyard and use the door beside the café. It may look like a staff entrance, but it is open to all visitors today. The information desk is inside on the left. A shuttle minibus can take people to the sports building on request, though it does not run to a fixed timetable. Please ask at the desk rather than wait outside and hope it will arrive."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the helper ask about access needs?","options":["The crossing lights were out of order.","The museum was closing earlier.","The nearest route included steps."],"answer":"The nearest route included steps.","explanation":"最近不等于无台阶。","id":"b1-05:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why check the website?","options":["To check the ticket price at the gate.","Construction might have changed the entrance.","To find a faster taxi route."],"answer":"Construction might have changed the entrance.","explanation":"核查临时通行变化。","id":"b1-05:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why not follow the old printed walk route?","options":["A bridge is closed.","The riverside path has disappeared.","The market opens at a different time."],"answer":"A bridge is closed.","explanation":"明确桥梁关闭。","id":"b1-05:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should someone leaving early do?","options":["Return to the start without telling anyone.","Give the map to another visitor first.","Tell the guide."],"answer":"Tell the guide.","explanation":"领队需保持准确人数。","id":"b1-05:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Jo phone the library?","options":["She could not find any current opening hours.","The suggested route was physically difficult for her.","The bus had stopped at the old building."],"answer":"The suggested route was physically difficult for her.","explanation":"受伤膝盖和携书使陡坡困难。","id":"b1-05:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What change did Jo recommend?","options":["Mentioning both quick and accessible routes.","Only describing the quickest route more briefly.","Replacing the river path with a bus timetable."],"answer":"Mentioning both quick and accessible routes.","explanation":"让来访者按需求选路。","id":"b1-05:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which entrance is step-free?","options":["The main courtyard entrance before the café.","The red-arrow accommodation entrance.","The door beside the café beyond the courtyard."],"answer":"The door beside the café beyond the courtyard.","explanation":"无台阶入口在院子后咖啡馆旁。","id":"b1-05:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"How should visitors arrange the shuttle?","options":["Wait outside for a half-hourly departure.","Ask at the information desk.","Request it from the accommodation guide later."],"answer":"Ask at the information desk.","explanation":"小巴按要求提供，无固定班次。","id":"b1-05:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write directions to a public building. Include a landmark, an access warning and an alternative route.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Give directions, then check that your partner understands the important turns and conditions.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["游客","本地居民"],"goal":"根据行动需求给出路线并核对理解。","twist":"最近路线有台阶，较长路线没有台阶但部分路面可能湿滑。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write feedback to a temporary library recommending clearer information for visitors who cannot use a steep route.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an open-day visitor step-free directions and explain how to arrange a shuttle without assuming a fixed timetable.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-06","level":"B1","title":"结识新朋友与维持对话","chapter":18,"focus":"自然追问、分享经历、尊重不愿展开的话题","vocab":[{"head":"introduce","zh":"介绍","pos":"verb","example":"At a neighbourhood meal, our host helped introduce people who had recently moved into the same apartment building.","exampleZh":"在邻里聚餐上，主人帮忙介绍刚搬进同栋公寓的人。","collocation":"introduce yourself","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v1","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interest","zh":"兴趣","pos":"noun","example":"I asked a neighbour about an interest she mentioned rather than immediately asking personal questions about her salary.","exampleZh":"我询问邻居提过的兴趣，而非马上打听私人薪水。","collocation":"shared interest","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v2","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mention","zh":"提到","pos":"verb","example":"She happened to mention photography, which gave me an easy way to continue the conversation without changing topics.","exampleZh":"她恰好提到摄影，让我能不换话题自然继续。","collocation":"mention a hobby","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v3","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recently","zh":"最近","pos":"adverb","example":"She had recently joined a weekend walking group and was learning to photograph birds near the river.","exampleZh":"她最近加入周末徒步组，正在学拍河边鸟类。","collocation":"recently joined","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v4","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"experience","zh":"经历","pos":"noun","example":"I shared an experience of trying to photograph a kingfisher, including the long wait and the disappointing result.","exampleZh":"我分享拍翠鸟的经历，包括漫长等候和失望结果。","collocation":"share an experience","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v5","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"curious","zh":"好奇的","pos":"adjective","example":"She was curious about the place, so I explained how to reach it without claiming to be an expert.","exampleZh":"她对地点好奇，所以我解释怎么到达，但没装专家。","collocation":"curious about","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v6","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interrupt","zh":"打断","pos":"verb","example":"When another guest joined us, I paused instead of continuing to interrupt with every detail of my story.","exampleZh":"另一客人加入时，我停下来，而非不断插入故事细节。","collocation":"interrupt a speaker","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v7","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"include","zh":"使参与","pos":"verb","example":"We tried to include him by explaining the topic and asking whether he enjoyed any outdoor activities himself.","exampleZh":"我们解释话题，问他是否喜欢户外活动，让他参与。","collocation":"include a newcomer","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v8","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prefer","zh":"更喜欢","pos":"verb","example":"He said he preferred indoor hobbies, so we did not pressure him to join a walk he clearly disliked.","exampleZh":"他说更喜欢室内爱好，所以我们没逼他参加明显不喜欢的徒步。","collocation":"prefer indoor activities","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v9","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggest","zh":"建议","pos":"verb","example":"Instead, he suggested a local exhibition where photographs of the river would be displayed the following month.","exampleZh":"他反而建议去当地展览，下月将展出河流照片。","collocation":"suggest an activity","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v10","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"invitation","zh":"邀请","pos":"noun","example":"His invitation gave us another chance to meet, but we checked dates before promising that everybody could attend.","exampleZh":"他的邀请让我们有机会再见，不过承诺所有人出席前先核对日期。","collocation":"accept an invitation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v11","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系方式","pos":"noun","example":"We exchanged contact details with permission and ended the conversation without expecting an immediate friendship from one pleasant meeting.","exampleZh":"我们经同意交换联系方式，不指望一次愉快见面就立刻成为好友。","collocation":"exchange contact details","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v12","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"结识新朋友与维持对话：教学情境","text":"At a neighbourhood meal, our host helped introduce people who had recently moved into the same apartment building. I asked a neighbour about an interest she mentioned rather than immediately asking personal questions about her salary. She happened to mention photography, which gave me an easy way to continue the conversation without changing topics. She had recently joined a weekend walking group and was learning to photograph birds near the river. I shared an experience of trying to photograph a kingfisher, including the long wait and the disappointing result. She was curious about the place, so I explained how to reach it without claiming to be an expert. When another guest joined us, I paused instead of continuing to interrupt with every detail of my story. We tried to include him by explaining the topic and asking whether he enjoyed any outdoor activities himself. He said he preferred indoor hobbies, so we did not pressure him to join a walk he clearly disliked. Instead, he suggested a local exhibition where photographs of the river would be displayed the following month. His invitation gave us another chance to meet, but we checked dates before promising that everybody could attend. We exchanged contact details with permission and ended the conversation without expecting an immediate friendship from one pleasant meeting."},"sourceB":{"title":"A conversation at a language exchange","text":"At her first language exchange, Mei sat beside a retired engineer called Paul. She had prepared questions about jobs, but Paul gave short answers and looked uncomfortable. Instead of asking another work question, Mei asked what had brought him to the event. He explained that he wanted to speak with visitors before volunteering at a local museum.\nThis gave them a more interesting topic. Mei asked which part of the museum he liked most, and Paul described a collection of old bicycles. She shared a memory of repairing a bicycle with her grandfather. Their conversation became easier because both people had something relevant to contribute.\nWhen the organiser asked participants to change partners, Paul offered to continue chatting after the next event. Mei accepted without feeling that she had to share her phone number immediately. They agreed to meet at the same table the following week. She realised that successful conversation did not mean completing a prepared list of questions. It meant noticing the other person’s response and choosing a direction that both people were comfortable exploring."},"listening":{"teaching":"Hello everyone. At tonight’s international supper, you will begin at the table number printed on your card. After the main course, we will invite you to move, so you can meet a different group over dessert. There is no need to deliver a formal introduction. A simple question about a favourite local place is usually enough to begin. Please avoid assuming that everyone wants to discuss their job or country’s politics. If someone gives a short answer, offer another topic rather than asking the same question more loudly. You are welcome to exchange contact details, but ask first before adding anyone to a group chat or posting photographs of them.","test":"I used to worry that a conversation would fail if there was a pause. At a community cooking class, I filled every silence with another question. Later, a classmate told me she had been trying to think of the right word and needed a moment. I began leaving more space and sharing small experiences of my own instead of interviewing everyone. The conversations became more balanced. I still ask questions, but I try to respond to the answer before moving on. A good exchange is not a competition to avoid silence; both people need time to understand, contribute and decide how much they want to share."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did photography help the conversation?","options":["It allowed the speaker to give professional advice.","It showed that all neighbours would enjoy walking.","It provided a shared topic for follow-up."],"answer":"It provided a shared topic for follow-up.","explanation":"从已提兴趣追问。","id":"b1-06:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How was the new guest included?","options":["He was invited to join the walk before explaining his interests.","The topic was explained and his interests were invited.","He was asked to wait until the story ended."],"answer":"The topic was explained and his interests were invited.","explanation":"让对方有可参与的入口。","id":"b1-06:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"When do supper guests change groups?","options":["After the main course.","Before the main course begins.","Only after the final announcement."],"answer":"After the main course.","explanation":"甜点阶段换桌。","id":"b1-06:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What requires permission?","options":["Moving to the next table.","Asking about a local favourite place.","Adding someone to a group chat."],"answer":"Adding someone to a group chat.","explanation":"隐私与分享需先同意。","id":"b1-06:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Mei change topic?","options":["She wanted to complete the prepared job questions faster.","Paul seemed uncomfortable answering work questions.","Paul had already asked to leave the event."],"answer":"Paul seemed uncomfortable answering work questions.","explanation":"注意对方反馈而非照清单。","id":"b1-06:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did they agree for later?","options":["Meeting at the same table the following week.","Exchanging phone numbers before changing partners.","Visiting the museum immediately after the event."],"answer":"Meeting at the same table the following week.","explanation":"明确保留联系方式界限。","id":"b1-06:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the classmate need a pause?","options":["She wanted to change to a different class.","She had not understood the activity instructions.","She was finding the right word."],"answer":"She was finding the right word.","explanation":"停顿不等于交流失败。","id":"b1-06:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What made the exchange more balanced?","options":["Asking the same question again more quickly.","Leaving space and sharing relevant experiences.","Avoiding sharing any personal experience."],"answer":"Leaving space and sharing relevant experiences.","explanation":"双向贡献取代采访式连问。","id":"b1-06:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an informal message inviting someone you met recently to an activity. Give relevant details and make it easy to decline.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Start a conversation, follow one answer with a relevant question and respond naturally to a change of topic.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["新邻居甲","新邻居乙"],"goal":"找到双方都愿意聊的话题并提出不过度施压的再见面邀请。","twist":"一方不愿讨论工作，且不能参加最初建议的活动。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write an informal follow-up to someone met at a language exchange. Suggest meeting again without demanding private contact details.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Respond to a partner who gives brief answers about work by moving naturally to another mutually comfortable topic.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-07","level":"B1","title":"学习计划与反馈","chapter":19,"focus":"把目标转成可执行活动，用反馈调整方法","vocab":[{"head":"goal","zh":"目标","pos":"noun","example":"When Luis began evening classes, his goal was to discuss simple work problems rather than collect perfect exercise scores.","exampleZh":"Luis 开始夜校时，目标是讨论简单工作问题，而非收集完美习题分数。","collocation":"set a goal","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v1","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"schedule","zh":"时间安排","pos":"noun","example":"His schedule included twenty minutes after breakfast, a time that was easier to protect than late evening study.","exampleZh":"他安排早餐后学二十分钟，比深夜学习更容易保证。","collocation":"study schedule","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v2","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"manage","zh":"设法做到","pos":"verb","example":"He could manage short reading tasks, but he often postponed speaking because nobody was available to practise with him.","exampleZh":"他能完成短阅读，却因无人陪练常推迟口语。","collocation":"manage a task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v3","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"record","zh":"记录","pos":"verb","example":"His teacher suggested that he record a short explanation first and listen for places where the meaning became unclear.","exampleZh":"老师建议先录简短解释，听哪些地方意思变不清。","collocation":"record an explanation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v4","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feedback","zh":"反馈","pos":"noun","example":"Useful feedback came from a partner who identified one confusing sentence instead of listing every small mistake at once.","exampleZh":"有用反馈来自同伴指出一句难懂的话，而非一次列全部小错。","collocation":"useful feedback","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v5","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"improve","zh":"改善","pos":"verb","example":"Luis tried to improve that sentence by adding a clear reason and replacing a word he did not fully understand.","exampleZh":"Luis 加清楚理由并替换不完全理解的词，改善那句话。","collocation":"improve a sentence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v6","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"progress","zh":"进展","pos":"noun","example":"He checked his progress with a new topic a week later rather than repeating the same answer until it sounded smooth.","exampleZh":"一周后他用新主题检查进展，而非反复同一答案至流畅。","collocation":"check progress","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v7","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"habit","zh":"习惯","pos":"noun","example":"The habit of reviewing a little each day helped him remember phrases that he had previously forgotten after class.","exampleZh":"每天稍复习的习惯让他记住以前下课就忘的短语。","collocation":"daily habit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v8","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"challenge","zh":"挑战","pos":"noun","example":"An unexpected question remained a challenge, so he practised asking people to clarify what they wanted to know.","exampleZh":"意外问题仍是挑战，所以他练习请人澄清想知道什么。","collocation":"face a challenge","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v9","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"realistic","zh":"现实可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A realistic plan also allowed breaks when family responsibilities made study difficult, instead of treating every missed session as failure.","exampleZh":"现实计划也在家庭责任使学习困难时允许休息，不把每次缺课当失败。","collocation":"realistic plan","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v10","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjust","zh":"调整","pos":"verb","example":"He would adjust the following week’s workload without trying to complete several hours of exercises in one tired evening.","exampleZh":"他调整下周任务量，不在疲惫晚间赶数小时习题。","collocation":"adjust the workload","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v11","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"independent","zh":"独立的","pos":"adjective","example":"By the end of term, an independent conversation showed which parts of his learning had become useful beyond the classroom.","exampleZh":"学期末独立对话显示哪些学习已能在课堂外使用。","collocation":"independent conversation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v12","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"学习计划与反馈：教学情境","text":"When Luis began evening classes, his goal was to discuss simple work problems rather than collect perfect exercise scores. His schedule included twenty minutes after breakfast, a time that was easier to protect than late evening study. He could manage short reading tasks, but he often postponed speaking because nobody was available to practise with him. His teacher suggested that he record a short explanation first and listen for places where the meaning became unclear. Useful feedback came from a partner who identified one confusing sentence instead of listing every small mistake at once. Luis tried to improve that sentence by adding a clear reason and replacing a word he did not fully understand. He checked his progress with a new topic a week later rather than repeating the same answer until it sounded smooth. The habit of reviewing a little each day helped him remember phrases that he had previously forgotten after class. An unexpected question remained a challenge, so he practised asking people to clarify what they wanted to know. A realistic plan also allowed breaks when family responsibilities made study difficult, instead of treating every missed session as failure. He would adjust the following week’s workload without trying to complete several hours of exercises in one tired evening."},"sourceB":{"title":"Preparing for a practical test","text":"Asha was learning to drive and could complete familiar routes confidently. She assumed that doing the same route more quickly meant she was ready for the practical test. Her instructor noticed that she became uncertain whenever roadworks changed a lane or a sign appeared in an unexpected place.\nThey revised the lesson plan. Instead of repeating only the test area, they used unfamiliar roads and practised explaining why a particular decision was safe. Asha also kept a short record of situations she found difficult. This helped her distinguish a missing rule from a moment of distraction.\nSome lessons felt less successful because she made more mistakes on new roads. The instructor explained that these mistakes provided information that familiar practice could hide. After two weeks, Asha revisited one of the difficult situations and handled it more calmly.\nShe did not take the improvement as proof that every driving skill was secure. She used it to choose what to practise next. Her revised plan combined familiar practice for confidence with unfamiliar situations that required attention and independent judgement, making progress more meaningful than speed on a single known route."},"listening":{"teaching":"Our study group meets on Tuesday evenings, but you do not have to prepare a perfect presentation. Bring a short account of something you tried to learn and one question about a difficulty you faced. We will work in pairs before sharing ideas with the whole group. Please give specific feedback, such as identifying a point that needs an example, rather than saying only that a talk was good or bad. Nobody is required to show private grades or personal records. If you miss a meeting, use the summary on our noticeboard to choose one small activity for the next week. You do not need to complete everybody else’s tasks as punishment for being absent.","test":"I changed my revision method after noticing that colourful notes took most of my study time. They looked organised, but I could not explain the ideas without looking at them. Now I close the book and write a short answer from memory before checking what I missed. I still make notes, though I keep them brief. Once a week, a friend asks me a new question using the same topic. This is harder than copying a page, and the result is sometimes disappointing. However, it tells me what I can actually use. I judge the method by later recall rather than by how attractive my notebook looks."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why study after breakfast?","options":["It was the only time a partner was available.","Morning tasks earned extra class marks.","That time was easier to protect."],"answer":"That time was easier to protect.","explanation":"根据可持续时间而非表面时长规划。","id":"b1-07:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How was progress checked?","options":["By making the original recording smoother.","With a new topic after a delay.","By increasing the length of the vocabulary list."],"answer":"With a new topic after a delay.","explanation":"迁移与延迟比熟练复述更有信息。","id":"b1-07:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should group members bring?","options":["A learning experience and a question about a difficulty.","A finished presentation with no mistakes.","A list of everyone else’s study tasks."],"answer":"A learning experience and a question about a difficulty.","explanation":"任务为经历与问题，不要求完美演讲。","id":"b1-07:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What feedback is recommended?","options":["A judgement that the presentation was simply good.","A comparison of private grades with classmates.","A specific point needing an example."],"answer":"A specific point needing an example.","explanation":"具体可操作反馈更有用。","id":"b1-07:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What weakness did unfamiliar roads reveal?","options":["Asha was taking familiar routes too slowly.","Uncertainty when the situation changed.","She had forgotten how to record driving lessons."],"answer":"Uncertainty when the situation changed.","explanation":"熟悉路线隐藏了应变问题。","id":"b1-07:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did more mistakes sometimes help?","options":["They revealed needs that familiar practice concealed.","They meant the test could be booked immediately.","They showed that earlier confidence was entirely false."],"answer":"They revealed needs that familiar practice concealed.","explanation":"错误提供诊断信息，不是直接判失败。","id":"b1-07:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why were colourful notes insufficient?","options":["The notes contained too few examples.","The speaker used the wrong colour scheme.","The speaker could not explain the ideas without them."],"answer":"The speaker could not explain the ideas without them.","explanation":"外观组织不等于独立掌握。","id":"b1-07:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"How is the new method judged?","options":["By the amount of information copied.","By later recall and use.","By how confidently the friend praised the notes."],"answer":"By later recall and use.","explanation":"检验真正记忆与使用。","id":"b1-07:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a realistic two-week learning plan. Identify one specific difficulty, a practice activity and a way to check progress.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how you would change a study method that feels easy but produces weak recall.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["学习者","学习伙伴"],"goal":"根据具体困难选择练习和复核方法。","twist":"学习者时间减少一半，但仍想维持所有原任务。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a revised practice plan for a learner who performs well on familiar driving routes but struggles with unexpected changes.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain how to test whether a new revision method helps recall rather than merely producing attractive notes.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-08","level":"B1","title":"工作沟通与任务协调","chapter":20,"focus":"确认职责、优先事项和截止时间，及时报告困难","vocab":[{"head":"task","zh":"任务","pos":"noun","example":"On my first week at the visitor centre, I received a task to prepare signs for a weekend exhibition.","exampleZh":"在游客中心第一周，我接到准备周末展览标牌的任务。","collocation":"complete a task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v1","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responsibility","zh":"责任","pos":"noun","example":"My responsibility covered the wording, but another colleague would check the facts and arrange the final printing.","exampleZh":"我负责措辞，另一同事核查事实并安排最终印刷。","collocation":"share responsibility","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v2","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止时间","pos":"noun","example":"The deadline was Thursday afternoon, although I needed the photographs before I could decide how much text would fit.","exampleZh":"截止为周四下午，不过我需先有照片才能决定容纳多少文字。","collocation":"meet a deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v3","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priority","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"My manager explained that clear directions were the first priority, while background information could be shortened if space was limited.","exampleZh":"经理说清晰指引优先，背景信息在空间不足时可缩短。","collocation":"first priority","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v4","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"update","zh":"最新进展","pos":"noun","example":"I sent a brief update on Tuesday, stating what was finished and which photographs were still missing from the folder.","exampleZh":"周二我简报已完内容及文件夹仍缺哪些照片。","collocation":"send an update","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v5","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"delay","zh":"延误","pos":"noun","example":"A delay in one image did not need to stop the whole project, provided we agreed on a temporary layout.","exampleZh":"只要同意临时版式，一张图片延误不必阻止整个项目。","collocation":"avoid a delay","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v6","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permission","zh":"许可","pos":"noun","example":"Before using an older photograph, I asked for permission because I did not know whether its licence covered this exhibition.","exampleZh":"用旧照片前我请求许可，因为不知道授权是否覆盖本展。","collocation":"ask for permission","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v7","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"colleague","zh":"同事","pos":"noun","example":"A colleague suggested a simpler diagram, which communicated the location more clearly than the photograph we had been waiting for.","exampleZh":"同事建议更简单示意图，比等待中的照片更清楚传达位置。","collocation":"ask a colleague","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v8","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revise","zh":"修改","pos":"verb","example":"I could revise the layout that morning, but I needed the fact checker to approve the new labels before printing.","exampleZh":"我上午能修改版式，但印前需核查人批准新标签。","collocation":"revise a layout","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v9","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"We agreed to confirm the final version by email so nobody would accidentally print a draft from the shared folder.","exampleZh":"我们同意邮件确认最终版，防止误印共享夹草稿。","collocation":"confirm the final version","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v10","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reliable","zh":"可靠的","pos":"adjective","example":"Reliable teamwork meant reporting difficulties early rather than promising that everything was fine until the last possible moment.","exampleZh":"可靠合作意味着及早报困难，而非到最后仍承诺一切正常。","collocation":"reliable teamwork","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v11","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"solution","zh":"解决办法","pos":"noun","example":"The solution emerged because each person understood the goal, their own role and when another person’s decision was needed.","exampleZh":"解决办法出现是因为人人懂目标、自身角色及何时需他人决策。","collocation":"find a solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v12","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"工作沟通与任务协调：教学情境","text":"On my first week at the visitor centre, I received a task to prepare signs for a weekend exhibition. My responsibility covered the wording, but another colleague would check the facts and arrange the final printing. The deadline was Thursday afternoon, although I needed the photographs before I could decide how much text would fit. My manager explained that clear directions were the first priority, while background information could be shortened if space was limited. I sent a brief update on Tuesday, stating what was finished and which photographs were still missing from the folder. A delay in one image did not need to stop the whole project, provided we agreed on a temporary layout. Before using an older photograph, I asked for permission because I did not know whether its licence covered this exhibition. A colleague suggested a simpler diagram, which communicated the location more clearly than the photograph we had been waiting for. I could revise the layout that morning, but I needed the fact checker to approve the new labels before printing. We agreed to confirm the final version by email so nobody would accidentally print a draft from the shared folder. Reliable teamwork meant reporting difficulties early rather than promising that everything was fine until the last possible moment."},"sourceB":{"title":"A café changes its rota","text":"At a small café, the manager posted next week’s working hours in a group chat. Two assistants replied with a thumbs-up symbol. The manager assumed both had accepted every shift, but one assistant thought the symbol only meant that the message had been received. On Saturday, the café was unexpectedly short of staff.\nThe team discussed the misunderstanding rather than blaming the assistant immediately. They discovered that availability requests and confirmed shifts had been sent in the same format. Staff could not easily tell whether a reply was asking for a change or accepting a final arrangement.\nThe manager introduced a simple system. Requests for availability were labelled as drafts. The final rota carried a date and required a written confirmation from each person. Any later change had to name both the original shift and the agreed replacement.\nThe system took a little more time, but it reduced uncertainty. A symbol was still welcome as a friendly response, yet it no longer counted as agreement to a work commitment. Clear communication meant making the expected action explicit instead of assuming that everyone understood a brief message in exactly the same way."},"listening":{"teaching":"Before we open the exhibition, I need each team to check one area. The design group should confirm that every arrow points towards the actual entrance, since the display moved yesterday. The information group should compare dates against the organiser’s final email, not the earlier draft. Please report any problem to me by eleven so we have time to make changes before visitors arrive at noon. Do not replace a printed sign with your own wording without checking it. If the correction is urgent and I am helping a visitor, tell the deputy manager. It is better to identify a small uncertainty now than leave guests following information we already suspect is wrong.","test":"Hello, this is your supervisor at the volunteer shop. Tomorrow’s delivery will arrive at ten thirty instead of nine, so you do not need to come in early. Please keep your usual eleven o’clock start unless you have separately agreed to help unload. We still need someone to check the donated books for damaged pages before they go onto the shelves. That task has priority over rearranging the window display. If there are too many books to finish safely, leave a clear note stating what remains rather than rushing through the checks. I will review the unfinished section with the afternoon team and decide how to divide the work."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why could one missing image be managed?","options":["The printing deadline could be moved until after opening.","The exhibition no longer required the missing label.","A temporary layout could keep other work moving."],"answer":"A temporary layout could keep other work moving.","explanation":"部分延误不必阻断全部任务。","id":"b1-08:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why confirm the final version by email?","options":["To request new photographs for the same draft.","To prevent a draft being printed by mistake.","To allow anyone to approve a version independently."],"answer":"To prevent a draft being printed by mistake.","explanation":"明确版本降低交接错误。","id":"b1-08:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which date source should be used?","options":["The organiser’s final email.","The first shared draft.","The previous year’s printed brochure."],"answer":"The organiser’s final email.","explanation":"听力明确最终邮件而非早稿。","id":"b1-08:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Who can handle an urgent correction if the manager is busy?","options":["The design group without any further check.","The visitor who first noticed the mistake.","The deputy manager."],"answer":"The deputy manager.","explanation":"有明确替代报告路径。","id":"b1-08:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the thumbs-up symbol mean to one assistant?","options":["Acceptance of the whole rota.","Receipt of the message, not acceptance of every shift.","A request for a different Saturday shift."],"answer":"Receipt of the message, not acceptance of every shift.","explanation":"符号被赋予不同交际含义。","id":"b1-08:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the new rota system require?","options":["Written confirmation of the final arrangement.","A symbol beneath every availability request.","Confirmation only when a shift is unwanted."],"answer":"Written confirmation of the final arrangement.","explanation":"最终排班与草案区分并明确接受。","id":"b1-08:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is tomorrow’s usual start time?","options":["Ten thirty when delivery arrives.","Nine to help unload automatically.","Eleven o’clock."],"answer":"Eleven o’clock.","explanation":"晚到货不改变普通十一点开工。","id":"b1-08:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should happen if book checks cannot be finished safely?","options":["Finish quickly even if pages are not checked.","Leave a clear note about the remaining work.","Leave the books without explaining which remain."],"answer":"Leave a clear note about the remaining work.","explanation":"及时交接剩余工作。","id":"b1-08:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a work update explaining completed tasks, one delay and the decision you need from a colleague.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Clarify a new task by asking about responsibilities, priorities and deadlines.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["新员工","项目负责人"],"goal":"核对任务范围并约定有变化时的报告路径。","twist":"关键图片未获许可，负责人起初只说尽快做完。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write to a café team explaining a new system for draft and confirmed rotas. State exactly what staff must do.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Clarify the volunteer shop task priorities and explain how you would hand over unfinished checking work.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-09","level":"B1","title":"阅读消息与核对信息","chapter":21,"focus":"区分事实、解释和未证实的说法","vocab":[{"head":"headline","zh":"标题","pos":"noun","example":"A headline in our neighbourhood group claimed that the swimming pool would close permanently at the end of summer.","exampleZh":"邻里群标题说游泳池夏末永久关闭。","collocation":"read a headline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v1","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"source","zh":"来源","pos":"noun","example":"Before sharing the message, I looked for its source and found that it referred to an old council discussion.","exampleZh":"转发前我查来源，发现它指向旧议会讨论。","collocation":"check a source","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v2","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"statement","zh":"声明","pos":"noun","example":"The current statement described a temporary closure for repairs, not a decision to remove the pool from the town.","exampleZh":"当前声明描述临时维修关闭，不是决定取消泳池。","collocation":"official statement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v3","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"There was no evidence in the document for the claim that every member would lose their remaining fees.","exampleZh":"文件没有证据支持所有会员剩余费用将损失的说法。","collocation":"look for evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v4","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"temporary","zh":"临时的","pos":"adjective","example":"The temporary arrangement allowed members to use another centre, although they needed to book sessions through a different website.","exampleZh":"临时安排允许会员用另一中心，不过需通过另一网站预约。","collocation":"temporary arrangement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v5","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"detail","zh":"细节","pos":"noun","example":"One important detail remained unclear: whether the alternative centre would offer suitable times for people working late shifts.","exampleZh":"一个重要细节仍不清：替代中心是否有适合夜班人员的时段。","collocation":"important detail","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v6","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"I decided to contact the council directly instead of assuming that a missing detail meant the entire plan was dishonest.","exampleZh":"我决定直接联系议会，而非假定缺细节就说明整个方案不诚实。","collocation":"contact an organisation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v7","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reply","zh":"回复","pos":"noun","example":"Their reply explained that the timetable was still being negotiated and would be published before the repair work began.","exampleZh":"回复说时刻表仍在协商，会在维修开始前公布。","collocation":"receive a reply","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v8","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"claim","zh":"声称","pos":"noun","example":"I shared a short correction that separated the confirmed information from the original claim about permanent closure.","exampleZh":"我分享简短更正，将已确认信息与原先永久关闭说法分开。","collocation":"check a claim","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v9","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertain","zh":"不确定的","pos":"adjective","example":"I also mentioned the uncertain timetable, because a correction should not pretend that every practical question has already been answered.","exampleZh":"我也提到不确定时刻表，因为更正不应假装所有实际问题都已解答。","collocation":"remain uncertain","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v10","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reliable","zh":"可靠的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reliable summary needed both the date of the statement and a link that neighbours could check for themselves.","exampleZh":"可靠摘要需要声明日期及邻居可自行核查链接。","collocation":"reliable summary","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v11","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"update","zh":"更新","pos":"noun","example":"When a later update appeared, we could compare it with the earlier plan rather than spread another dramatic message without context.","exampleZh":"后续更新出现时，我们能比较原计划，而非无背景传播另一耸动消息。","collocation":"publish an update","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v12","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"阅读消息与核对信息：教学情境","text":"A headline in our neighbourhood group claimed that the swimming pool would close permanently at the end of summer. Before sharing the message, I looked for its source and found that it referred to an old council discussion. The current statement described a temporary closure for repairs, not a decision to remove the pool from the town. There was no evidence in the document for the claim that every member would lose their remaining fees. The temporary arrangement allowed members to use another centre, although they needed to book sessions through a different website. One important detail remained unclear: whether the alternative centre would offer suitable times for people working late shifts. I decided to contact the council directly instead of assuming that a missing detail meant the entire plan was dishonest. Their reply explained that the timetable was still being negotiated and would be published before the repair work began. I shared a short correction that separated the confirmed information from the original claim about permanent closure. I also mentioned the uncertain timetable, because a correction should not pretend that every practical question has already been answered. A reliable summary needed both the date of the statement and a link that neighbours could check for themselves."},"sourceB":{"title":"A surprising school result","text":"A local website reported that pupils who joined a school chess club achieved higher mathematics marks. Its headline suggested that chess lessons had caused the improvement. The article described the marks accurately, but it did not explain how students had joined the club.\nA teacher checked the information and discovered that many club members had already been strong at mathematics before the club opened. They had also volunteered to attend, while other pupils chose music or sport. The comparison therefore could not show what would happen if every student received chess lessons.\nThe teacher did not conclude that chess was useless. Club members enjoyed the activity, practised concentration and made new friends. These were worthwhile benefits, even if the examination claim was too strong. She asked the website to revise its headline and include the earlier marks.\nThe edited article described an association rather than a proven cause. Some readers found the new title less exciting, but it gave families a fairer basis for choosing activities. A careful correction preserved the real observations while removing an explanation that the available information could not support."},"listening":{"teaching":"This is an update about the road outside the community centre. Yesterday’s message said the road would close for three days. The council has now confirmed that vehicles will be stopped only between nine and four on Monday and Tuesday. Pedestrians can still use the marked pavement, but please follow staff instructions near the work area. Deliveries to the centre should use the rear entrance during those hours. Some messages in the local group describe the centre as closed all week; that is incorrect. Activities inside are continuing. If your event organiser changes a particular session, they will contact you directly. Check the dated notice on our website before making travel arrangements.","test":"I saw a photograph online showing an empty shopping street and a message saying that every business had closed. The image looked convincing until I noticed that it had been taken early on a public holiday. I checked several shop websites and found that most were trading normally later that week. One shop had closed, so the message was not completely invented, but it gave a false impression by using a real image without its timing. I posted the opening information without insulting the person who shared it. Correcting the context seemed more useful than starting an argument about whether they were a bad person."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the current statement actually say?","options":["The pool would move permanently to another town.","Members must apply for refunds before repairs.","The pool would close temporarily for repairs."],"answer":"The pool would close temporarily for repairs.","explanation":"原声明与群标题不同。","id":"b1-09:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why mention the uncertain timetable?","options":["To show the alternative centre had already failed.","To avoid pretending all practical questions were settled.","To avoid linking to an official statement."],"answer":"To avoid pretending all practical questions were settled.","explanation":"如实保留未解信息。","id":"b1-09:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"When are vehicles restricted?","options":["Nine to four on Monday and Tuesday.","All day Monday through Wednesday.","Nine to four every weekday."],"answer":"Nine to four on Monday and Tuesday.","explanation":"更新时间修正原三天说法。","id":"b1-09:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens to indoor activities?","options":["They pause for the same hours as the roadworks.","They move to the rear entrance for the whole week.","They continue unless organisers advise a specific change."],"answer":"They continue unless organisers advise a specific change.","explanation":"中心并未整体停业。","id":"b1-09:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why could higher marks not prove a chess effect?","options":["Members had received no mathematics teaching.","Many members were already strong at mathematics.","The chess lessons were shorter than other clubs."],"answer":"Many members were already strong at mathematics.","explanation":"先前差异和自选造成因果限制。","id":"b1-09:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the revised article preserve?","options":["The observations without an unsupported causal claim.","A conclusion that chess improves every pupil’s marks.","Only the social benefits, with all marks removed."],"answer":"The observations without an unsupported causal claim.","explanation":"区分相关现象与因果解释。","id":"b1-09:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was the empty street image misleading?","options":["The shops’ opening times had all changed permanently.","The image showed a different street.","Its holiday timing was missing."],"answer":"Its holiday timing was missing.","explanation":"真实照片缺背景可产生假印象。","id":"b1-09:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"How did the speaker correct it?","options":["By asking everyone to delete all photographs.","By sharing verified opening information.","By challenging the sharer’s motives instead of the timing."],"answer":"By sharing verified opening information.","explanation":"纠正情境优先于人身争论。","id":"b1-09:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a short correction to a misleading local message. State what is confirmed, what remains uncertain and where readers can check.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain why a convincing headline may still need checking.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["群成员","信息核查者"],"goal":"纠正夸大说法并保留真实但有限的信息。","twist":"消息中一部分是真的，但标题把它概括成所有情况。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a correction of an article claiming chess caused higher marks. Preserve the real observation and identify the missing evidence.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain how a real photograph can mislead when its date and context are omitted.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-10","level":"B1","title":"表达观点与解释取舍","chapter":22,"focus":"提出有条件观点，用例子发展理由并回应反对意见","vocab":[{"head":"opinion","zh":"观点","pos":"noun","example":"In our class, I expressed an opinion about replacing one written homework task each week with a practical group activity.","exampleZh":"课堂上，我表达了对每周用实践小组活动替换一次书面作业的观点。","collocation":"express an opinion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v1","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"benefit","zh":"好处","pos":"noun","example":"One benefit would be learning to explain ideas to classmates instead of completing every exercise alone at home.","exampleZh":"一个好处是学向同学解释想法，而非在家独自做每题。","collocation":"possible benefit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v2","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reason","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"I gave a reason for supporting the change: different explanations can reveal misunderstandings that a correct answer sometimes hides.","exampleZh":"我给支持理由：不同解释能揭示正确答案有时掩盖的误解。","collocation":"give a reason","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v3","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"example","zh":"例子","pos":"noun","example":"As an example, our science group had discovered a mistake only when we tried to explain an experiment together.","exampleZh":"例如，我们科学小组一起解释实验时才发现一个错误。","collocation":"give an example","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v4","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concern","zh":"担忧","pos":"noun","example":"Another student raised a concern that confident speakers might do most of the work while quieter members watched.","exampleZh":"另一学生担心自信发言者可能做大部分工作，而安静者只观看。","collocation":"raise a concern","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v5","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair","zh":"公平的","pos":"adjective","example":"That objection seemed fair, so I suggested giving each person a clear role and time to prepare independently first.","exampleZh":"这个反对合理，所以我建议各有明确角色并先独立准备。","collocation":"fair objection","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v6","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depend","zh":"取决于","pos":"verb","example":"The value of group work would depend on its organisation, not simply on whether students sat around one table.","exampleZh":"小组学习价值取决于组织，而不只是否围同桌坐。","collocation":"depend on organisation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v7","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agree","zh":"同意","pos":"verb","example":"We could agree about the need for individual responsibility even though we preferred different kinds of homework ourselves.","exampleZh":"即使我们偏爱不同作业，也能同意需个人责任。","collocation":"agree about a need","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v8","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disagree","zh":"不同意","pos":"verb","example":"It was easier to disagree politely when we discussed a specific arrangement rather than describing each other as lazy.","exampleZh":"讨论具体安排而非指对方懒惰时，更容易礼貌反对。","collocation":"disagree politely","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v9","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"balance","zh":"平衡","pos":"noun","example":"A sensible balance might include a short personal reflection after the practical task, showing what each student had learned.","exampleZh":"合理平衡可在实践后加简短个人反思，显示各人所得。","collocation":"sensible balance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v10","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggestion","zh":"建议","pos":"noun","example":"Our final suggestion was a small trial, followed by feedback from students who enjoyed the activity and those who did not.","exampleZh":"最终建议先小规模试行，再收集喜欢和不喜欢者反馈。","collocation":"make a suggestion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v11","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conclusion","zh":"结论","pos":"noun","example":"My conclusion supported the idea under clear conditions, rather than claiming that one teaching method would suit every subject.","exampleZh":"我结论在明确条件下支持，而非声称一种教学法适合所有科目。","collocation":"reach a conclusion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v12","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"表达观点与解释取舍：教学情境","text":"Our class discussed whether the school should replace one written homework task each week with a practical group activity. One benefit would be learning to explain ideas to classmates instead of completing every exercise alone at home. I gave a reason for supporting the change: different explanations can reveal misunderstandings that a correct answer sometimes hides. As an example, our science group had discovered a mistake only when we tried to explain an experiment together. Another student raised a concern that confident speakers might do most of the work while quieter members watched. That objection seemed fair, so I suggested giving each person a clear role and time to prepare independently first. The value of group work would depend on its organisation, not simply on whether students sat around one table. We could agree about the need for individual responsibility even though we preferred different kinds of homework ourselves. It was easier to disagree politely when we discussed a specific arrangement rather than describing each other as lazy. A sensible balance might include a short personal reflection after the practical task, showing what each student had learned. Our final suggestion was a small trial, followed by feedback from students who enjoyed the activity and those who did not."},"sourceB":{"title":"Should the park allow evening events?","text":"A town committee asked residents whether concerts should be allowed in the public park during summer evenings. Some people supported the idea because music could bring neighbours together and help local performers find an audience. Others worried about noise near homes and rubbish left after events.\nAt a public meeting, one resident proposed banning every concert. Another wanted no restrictions at all. A third speaker asked whether the problem depended on the number of events, their finishing time and the location of the stage. This changed the discussion from choosing a side to considering workable conditions.\nThe committee proposed four trial events ending before nine. Organisers would provide bins, arrange a clear-up team and keep the stage away from the nearest houses. Residents could report specific problems through a simple form.\nNot everyone supported the trial, but most agreed that the result would provide better information than arguing about an unlimited programme. The committee promised to publish complaints as well as attendance figures. A successful event needed to work for neighbours who did not attend, not only for people enjoying the music inside the park."},"listening":{"teaching":"In tomorrow’s discussion, you will consider whether our library should allow conversation in part of the reading room. Begin by identifying who might benefit and who could be disadvantaged. You do not have to defend your first reaction throughout the whole activity. If a partner makes a useful point, you may change or qualify your view. Try to support a reason with a concrete example. Avoid saying that everyone wants the same thing unless you have evidence. At the end, recommend one arrangement and explain a condition it needs to satisfy. We are looking for a thoughtful exchange, not a contest to speak for the longest time.","test":"I support a weekly car-free morning outside the primary school, but I would not close the whole neighbourhood. The immediate entrance becomes crowded when many cars stop at once, and children sometimes cross between vehicles. A short restriction could create a safer space. However, some pupils have mobility needs, and deliveries must still reach nearby homes. The council should consult those families and provide exceptions before starting a trial. I would judge the change by actual safety and access, not simply by a photograph of an empty street. If traffic moved into another dangerous place, the arrangement would need adjustment rather than automatic expansion."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What concern was raised about group work?","options":["The activity could take longer than a written task.","Practical work might need expensive equipment.","Confident speakers might dominate."],"answer":"Confident speakers might dominate.","explanation":"明确担忧参与不均。","id":"b1-10:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the final suggestion include?","options":["A permanent replacement before feedback.","A trial and feedback from different reactions.","Feedback only from students with speaking roles."],"answer":"A trial and feedback from different reactions.","explanation":"小试验及多方反馈。","id":"b1-10:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What may a participant do during discussion?","options":["Qualify a view after hearing a useful point.","Keep the initial position even if evidence changes.","Avoid expressing a final recommendation."],"answer":"Qualify a view after hearing a useful point.","explanation":"允许根据新理由调整。","id":"b1-10:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should the final recommendation include?","options":["A detailed account of an unrelated personal experience.","A promise that everyone will benefit equally.","A condition it needs to satisfy."],"answer":"A condition it needs to satisfy.","explanation":"要求说明可行条件。","id":"b1-10:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What shifted the park discussion?","options":["Comparing the price of every instrument.","Considering event frequency, time and stage location.","Choosing the most enthusiastic speaker’s proposal."],"answer":"Considering event frequency, time and stage location.","explanation":"从二选一转向条件。","id":"b1-10:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Whose needs count in judging success?","options":["Neighbours who do not attend as well as audiences.","Only residents who submitted complaints.","Only the people directly employed by performers."],"answer":"Neighbours who do not attend as well as audiences.","explanation":"公共活动要顾及未参加者。","id":"b1-10:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What exception does the school-street speaker consider?","options":["Exceptions for all cars during the busiest period.","A permanent cancellation of nearby deliveries.","Access for pupils with mobility needs."],"answer":"Access for pupils with mobility needs.","explanation":"支持限制但明确无障碍条件。","id":"b1-10:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What would require adjustment?","options":["Fewer cars appearing in a single photograph.","Traffic moving to another dangerous location.","Parents asking for the same timetable again."],"answer":"Traffic moving to another dangerous location.","explanation":"不能把风险简单转移。","id":"b1-10:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an opinion essay on replacing one homework task with group work. Explain one benefit, one concern and your conditional conclusion.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Discuss a public change while acknowledging an objection and proposing a fair condition.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["改革支持者","担心受影响的人"],"goal":"提出双方可接受的小规模试行条件。","twist":"对方支持目标，却指出方案可能把问题转移到别处。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a recommendation for a limited park concert trial. Address noise, access, litter and how neighbours can report problems.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss a school-street restriction, including mobility access and the risk of moving traffic elsewhere.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-11","level":"B1","title":"限时任务与答题策略","chapter":23,"focus":"先理解任务，再分配时间并检查依据","vocab":[{"head":"instruction","zh":"指令","pos":"noun","example":"Before a timed practice task, our tutor asked us to read the instruction and identify exactly what we had to produce.","exampleZh":"限时练习前，导师让我们读指令并确定须完成什么。","collocation":"read an instruction","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v1","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"purpose","zh":"目的","pos":"noun","example":"The purpose was to recommend a suitable activity for visitors, not to describe every activity listed on the page.","exampleZh":"目的是为游客推荐合适活动，而非描述页面全部活动。","collocation":"identify the purpose","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v2","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"A short time limit meant we needed to choose relevant details rather than copy the whole information sheet into our notes.","exampleZh":"时限短意味着需选相关细节，而非把整张信息抄入笔记。","collocation":"time limit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v3","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"skim","zh":"略读","pos":"verb","example":"I learned to skim the text first so I could understand its structure before searching for a particular detail.","exampleZh":"我学会先略读，明白结构后再找具体细节。","collocation":"skim a text","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v4","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compare","zh":"比较","pos":"verb","example":"Then I would compare the visitor’s needs with each option, checking cost and access instead of following the most attractive photograph.","exampleZh":"然后把游客需求与各选项比较，查费用通行而非跟最漂亮照片。","collocation":"compare options","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v5","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"依据","pos":"noun","example":"For each answer, I marked a small piece of evidence that explained why one choice fitted better than the others.","exampleZh":"每个答案我标一小处依据，说明为何比其他选项合适。","collocation":"find evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v6","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"guess","zh":"猜测","pos":"verb","example":"If I had to guess, I noted the uncertainty so I could review the problem honestly after the timer stopped.","exampleZh":"若不得不猜，我记下不确定性，计时结束后诚实复查。","collocation":"make a guess","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v7","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"check","zh":"检查","pos":"verb","example":"I saved a few minutes to check that my final recommendation answered the question and did not contradict an important condition.","exampleZh":"我留几分钟检查最终建议回应问题且不违背重要条件。","collocation":"check an answer","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v8","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mistake","zh":"错误","pos":"noun","example":"A repeated mistake often showed a weak habit, such as ignoring a negative word or choosing a familiar expression without context.","exampleZh":"重复错误常显示坏习惯，如忽视否定词或不看语境选熟语。","collocation":"repeated mistake","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v9","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"review","zh":"复查","pos":"verb","example":"Afterwards, I would review the reasoning, not just count how many answers matched the key and close the book.","exampleZh":"之后我复查推理，而不只是数对了几个就合书。","collocation":"review the reasoning","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v10","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"strategy","zh":"策略","pos":"noun","example":"A useful strategy reduced wasted effort, but it could not replace the language knowledge needed to understand the task.","exampleZh":"有效策略减少浪费，却不能替代理解任务所需语言知识。","collocation":"useful strategy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v11","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confidence","zh":"信心","pos":"noun","example":"My confidence became more realistic when I could explain both why an answer worked and why an attractive alternative did not.","exampleZh":"能解释答案及诱人干扰项为何不成立时，我信心更现实。","collocation":"build confidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v12","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"限时任务与答题策略：教学情境","text":"Before a timed practice task, our tutor asked us to read the instruction and identify exactly what we had to produce. The purpose was to recommend a suitable activity for visitors, not to describe every activity listed on the page. A short time limit meant we needed to choose relevant details rather than copy the whole information sheet into our notes. I learned to skim the text first so I could understand its structure before searching for a particular detail. Then I would compare the visitor’s needs with each option, checking cost and access instead of following the most attractive photograph. For each answer, I marked a small piece of evidence that explained why one choice fitted better than the others. If I had to guess, I noted the uncertainty so I could review the problem honestly after the timer stopped. I saved a few minutes to check that my final recommendation answered the question and did not contradict an important condition. A repeated mistake often showed a weak habit, such as ignoring a negative word or choosing a familiar expression without context. Afterwards, I would review the reasoning, not just count how many answers matched the key and close the book."},"sourceB":{"title":"Practising a presentation","text":"Diego prepared a short presentation for a training course. He wrote a complete script and practised reading it until he could finish within five minutes. During a rehearsal, a colleague asked him to explain an unfamiliar term. Diego lost his place and restarted the whole talk.\nHis trainer suggested organising the presentation around three clear points rather than memorising every sentence. Diego prepared a small card with keywords and practised explaining the ideas in a different order. He also asked colleagues to interrupt with questions at unexpected moments.\nThe new rehearsals were less smooth at first. However, Diego became better at returning to his main point after answering a question. He learned to say when a detail was outside the talk’s purpose and offer to discuss it afterwards.\nOn the day, he finished slightly before the time limit and used the remaining time for one question. The talk was not identical to any rehearsal, yet the audience could follow it. His preparation had become more useful because it supported flexible communication instead of protecting a script from every possible interruption."},"listening":{"teaching":"For this practice session, you will have ten minutes to read three activity descriptions and recommend one for a family. The family includes a young child and an adult who cannot stand for long periods. Cost matters, but it is not their only concern. Read the conditions carefully before choosing. You may make notes, although you must write the recommendation in your own words. When the timer ends, stop and mark any unfinished sentence rather than continue secretly. Afterwards, we will discuss your evidence and reasoning. The aim is to identify what you can do independently under these conditions, not to create a perfect score by adding extra time without recording it.","test":"I used to spend most of a listening exercise worrying about a word I had missed. While I was trying to remember it, the recording moved on and I lost the next point too. My teacher suggested making a brief mark and continuing to listen for the main direction. Later, I could decide whether the missing word was actually necessary. This did not mean ignoring every difficult detail. It meant protecting my attention so one uncertainty did not destroy the whole task. I still checked the transcript afterwards, separating an unfamiliar word from a moment when I had simply stopped following the speaker."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why identify the task’s purpose first?","options":["To memorise the activity sheet before reading needs.","To choose the option with the longest description.","To select relevant information."],"answer":"To select relevant information.","explanation":"目标决定信息选择。","id":"b1-11:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What makes reviewing useful?","options":["Completing more pages on the same topic.","Explaining reasoning as well as checking the answer key.","Correcting the key without examining decisions."],"answer":"Explaining reasoning as well as checking the answer key.","explanation":"检验思路而不只计分。","id":"b1-11:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What family need must be considered?","options":["One adult cannot stand for long.","The child must leave before lunch.","Every person wants a different museum."],"answer":"One adult cannot stand for long.","explanation":"需同时考虑孩子及久站困难。","id":"b1-11:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens when time ends?","options":["Add extra time without noting it.","Finish the entire paragraph before acknowledging the timer.","Stop and mark unfinished work."],"answer":"Stop and mark unfinished work.","explanation":"如实记录独立表现。","id":"b1-11:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the script rehearsal fail?","options":["He had practised for a different time limit.","An unexpected question disrupted the memorised sequence.","The audience wanted no explanation of unfamiliar terms."],"answer":"An unexpected question disrupted the memorised sequence.","explanation":"逐句记忆缺乏中断恢复。","id":"b1-11:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What improved flexibility?","options":["Using key points and unexpected questions.","Learning a longer script with every possible answer.","Avoiding all interruptions until the final event."],"answer":"Using key points and unexpected questions.","explanation":"结构化要点支持适应。","id":"b1-11:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What was the listener’s old problem?","options":["He missed words because the transcript was incorrect.","He focused on the main point and ignored all detail.","Worrying about one word caused further missed information."],"answer":"Worrying about one word caused further missed information.","explanation":"局部卡顿造成后续丢失。","id":"b1-11:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why mark uncertainty and continue?","options":["To avoid checking the difficult word later.","To preserve attention to the main direction.","To replace listening with a complete guess."],"answer":"To preserve attention to the main direction.","explanation":"保留主线后再判断漏词重要性。","id":"b1-11:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a short recommendation from several options under a time limit. Explain two relevant reasons and one limitation.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain a topic from three keywords, then respond to an unexpected question without restarting.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["限时任务学习者","追问的伙伴"],"goal":"保持任务重点并用依据解释选择。","twist":"伙伴提出一项与原选择冲突的新条件。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write advice to a presenter whose memorised script fails after an unexpected question. Propose practical rehearsal changes.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain how to recover after missing one word in a recording without losing the whole message.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b1-12","level":"B1","title":"综合应用与阶段复核","chapter":24,"focus":"在陌生情境中综合理解、表达和修正误解","vocab":[{"head":"independent","zh":"独立的","pos":"adjective","example":"For our final project, each learner had to plan an independent visit for a guest with particular interests and needs.","exampleZh":"最终项目要求每人独立为有具体兴趣需求的客人规划参观。","collocation":"independent task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v1","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"information","zh":"信息","pos":"noun","example":"We received information about several places, including opening times, transport links and a few conditions that were easy to overlook.","exampleZh":"我们收到几个地点的信息，包括开放时间、交通及易忽略条件。","collocation":"useful information","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v2","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"select","zh":"选择","pos":"verb","example":"I had to select details relevant to my guest rather than repeat every sentence from the descriptions in my presentation.","exampleZh":"我需选与客人相关细节，而非在展示中重复介绍每句。","collocation":"select details","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v3","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"organise","zh":"组织","pos":"verb","example":"I decided to organise the route around two nearby places, leaving enough time for a quiet lunch between them.","exampleZh":"我围绕两个邻近地点组织路线，中间留安静午餐时间。","collocation":"organise a route","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v4","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"My partner asked me to explain why I had rejected a cheaper option that looked more exciting in its advertisement.","exampleZh":"同伴让我解释为何放弃广告看起来更精彩的便宜选项。","collocation":"explain a choice","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v5","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"The important condition was a steep staircase, which would make that option unsuitable for the guest I was helping.","exampleZh":"重要条件是陡楼梯，对我帮助的客人不合适。","collocation":"important condition","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v6","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"clarify","zh":"澄清","pos":"verb","example":"When my partner misunderstood the lunch arrangement, I had to clarify whether the price included food or only admission.","exampleZh":"同伴误解午餐安排时，我须澄清费用含餐还是仅门票。","collocation":"clarify a detail","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v7","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adapt","zh":"调整以适应","pos":"verb","example":"A surprise closure forced me to adapt the afternoon plan without abandoning the guest’s main interest in local history.","exampleZh":"意外关闭迫使我调整下午计划，但不放弃客人对当地历史的核心兴趣。","collocation":"adapt a plan","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v8","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"The teacher collected evidence from our reading notes, written message and conversation instead of judging the polished presentation alone.","exampleZh":"老师从阅读笔记、书面消息及对话收证，而非只判成熟展示。","collocation":"collect evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v9","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"strength","zh":"优势","pos":"noun","example":"My main strength was explaining reasons clearly, while quick listening questions still required more practice with unfamiliar speakers.","exampleZh":"我主要优势是清楚解释理由，而快速听力题仍需练陌生说话者。","collocation":"identify a strength","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v10","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"improvement","zh":"改进","pos":"noun","example":"We each chose one improvement for the next fortnight and arranged a delayed check using a completely different visitor problem.","exampleZh":"我们各选未来两周一个改进，并用完全不同游客问题安排延迟检验。","collocation":"plan an improvement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v11","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"achievement","zh":"成就","pos":"noun","example":"Finishing the project marked an achievement, but the evidence described specific abilities rather than promising effortless communication in every situation.","exampleZh":"完成项目是成就，但证据描述具体能力，不承诺任何情境轻松交流。","collocation":"recognise an achievement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v12","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"综合应用与阶段复核：教学情境","text":"For our final project, each learner had to plan an independent visit for a guest with particular interests and needs. We received information about several places, including opening times, transport links and a few conditions that were easy to overlook. I had to select details relevant to my guest rather than repeat every sentence from the descriptions in my presentation. I decided to organise the route around two nearby places, leaving enough time for a quiet lunch between them. My partner asked me to explain why I had rejected a cheaper option that looked more exciting in its advertisement. The important condition was a steep staircase, which would make that option unsuitable for the guest I was helping. When my partner misunderstood the lunch arrangement, I had to clarify whether the price included food or only admission. A surprise closure forced me to adapt the afternoon plan without abandoning the guest’s main interest in local history. The teacher collected evidence from our reading notes, written message and conversation instead of judging the polished presentation alone. My main strength was explaining reasons clearly, while quick listening questions still required more practice with unfamiliar speakers. We each chose one improvement for the next fortnight and arranged a delayed check using a completely different visitor problem."},"sourceB":{"title":"A community volunteer’s first independent shift","text":"After several supervised sessions at a community centre, Isabel completed her first independent reception shift. She knew the usual timetable well, but a visitor arrived asking for a class that had recently moved. The printed notice and the online calendar showed different rooms.\nInstead of choosing the more familiar notice, Isabel contacted the class organiser. She learned that the room had changed because the lift was under repair. She explained the new location, checked whether the visitor could use the alternative entrance and updated the reception copy of the timetable.\nLater, another visitor asked about a refund condition outside Isabel’s responsibilities. She did not invent an answer to appear confident. She recorded the question and arranged for the manager to respond, making clear when the visitor could expect contact.\nAt the end of the shift, her supervisor reviewed both examples. Isabel had not known every answer, yet she had found reliable information, explained relevant conditions and recognised the limit of her role. These actions provided stronger evidence of readiness than repeating the normal welcome speech perfectly during a rehearsal with no unexpected requests."},"listening":{"teaching":"Today’s final activity has three parts. First, read the venue information and choose a suitable place for a small reunion. Then write to the organiser explaining your recommendation and one point that still needs confirmation. Finally, discuss the plan with a partner who will introduce a new difficulty. You may ask questions and revise your choice. We will record separate observations about understanding, writing and interaction. One strong answer will not automatically make every skill strong. After the activity, choose a specific next step using the feedback. A later check will use different venues and conditions so we can see whether you can apply the same skills beyond familiar material.","test":"I thought I was ready to lead a history walk because I knew all the stops. During a trial, someone asked whether we could shorten the route for a tired child. I had no prepared answer and initially repeated the full timetable. Afterwards, my mentor helped me identify which stops were essential and where the group could leave safely. On the next walk, I could offer a shorter option and explain what it would miss. I still needed help with a difficult historical question, but I knew whom to ask. Being ready did not mean knowing everything; it meant responding responsibly when the plan changed."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why reject the cheaper venue?","options":["It was farther from the lunch venue.","Its opening time had not been printed.","Its staircase did not suit the guest."],"answer":"Its staircase did not suit the guest.","explanation":"依据具体需求而非价格单一判断。","id":"b1-12:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why assess several tasks?","options":["To give every skill the presentation’s score.","Different evidence reveals different abilities.","To check only how attractive the final plan looked."],"answer":"Different evidence reveals different abilities.","explanation":"听读写说不由一项代替。","id":"b1-12:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What may partners do after a new difficulty?","options":["Ask questions and revise the plan.","Deliver the original ending without questions.","Ask the teacher to remove the difficulty."],"answer":"Ask questions and revise the plan.","explanation":"检验适应与协商。","id":"b1-12:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why use different venues later?","options":["To see whether the first recommendation was cheapest.","To check whether the learner remembers venue names.","To check transfer beyond familiar material."],"answer":"To check transfer beyond familiar material.","explanation":"延迟陌生任务检验应用。","id":"b1-12:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Isabel contact the organiser?","options":["The visitor asked to change the class fee.","Printed and online room information conflicted.","The online calendar had no room numbers."],"answer":"Printed and online room information conflicted.","explanation":"冲突时查可靠来源。","id":"b1-12:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did she handle an unfamiliar refund condition?","options":["Referred it to the manager with a response arrangement.","Gave a likely answer while waiting for confirmation.","Asked the visitor to read the old printed notice."],"answer":"Referred it to the manager with a response arrangement.","explanation":"认识职责边界并有效转介。","id":"b1-12:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What exposed the walk leader’s initial weakness?","options":["An unfamiliar question about a historical date.","A request to start at the normal meeting point.","A request to shorten the route."],"answer":"A request to shorten the route.","explanation":"意外调整需求超出背稿。","id":"b1-12:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What improved on the next walk?","options":["The leader could answer every historical question.","The leader could offer a shorter route and explain its limits.","The mentor removed all alternative stops."],"answer":"The leader could offer a shorter route and explain its limits.","explanation":"准备可调整路线与缺失内容说明。","id":"b1-12:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Plan a visit for a guest with specific needs, then write a message explaining your recommendation and one uncertainty.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["回应题目要求，包含具体情境与明确目的。","用段落或清楚顺序组织信息，补充必要原因。","检查时态、指代及词语搭配，避免影响理解的错误。","核对内容是否由自己独立组织，并说明信息的来源。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Respond to a change in a plan while preserving its main purpose and explaining any limits.","followups":["Why would that choice suit this situation?","What difficulty might occur?","How would you explain the situation to someone who disagrees?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["服务志愿者","提出意外需求的访客"],"goal":"查证冲突信息、解释限制并完成负责的转介。","twist":"线上资料与纸质通知冲突，访客同时提出不属你权限的退款请求。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"At least fourteen days later, read a new account of a similar problem. Identify its purpose, two important details and one difference from this module.","writingPrompt":"Write a new message about a similar situation with different people and constraints. Do not reuse the original wording.","speakingPrompt":"Explain a fresh solution to a partner, then answer two unexpected follow-up questions without a script."},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a reception handover note describing conflicting room information, the verified correction and a refund query referred to a manager.","minimumWords":110,"checklist":["包含陌生情境要求的信息与明确目的。","给出具体理由或条件，不照搬教学任务的答案。","组织顺序清楚，检查指代、时态与礼貌程度。","独立完成并保留原稿，交给伙伴或教师核对是否易懂。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Respond to a tired visitor who asks for a shorter history walk and explain what the alternative would leave out.","followups":["Which condition matters most in this particular situation?","What would you ask before agreeing?","How would you respond if the other person suggested a different solution?"]}},{"id":"b2-01","level":"B2","title":"公共服务与实际需求","chapter":25,"focus":"准确使用搭配，区分使用者需求与表面指标","vocab":[{"head":"set a realistic deadline","zh":"设定期限","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We need to set a realistic deadline.","exampleZh":"我们需要设定一个现实的期限。","collocation":"set a realistic deadline","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v1","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"took responsibility","zh":"承担责任","pos":"verb phrase","example":"She took responsibility for checking the wiring.","exampleZh":"她承担了检查电线的责任。","collocation":"take responsibility for","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v2","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"an insight into","zh":"深入了解","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The survey gave us an insight into local needs.","exampleZh":"调查让我们了解了当地需求。","collocation":"an insight into local needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v3","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pay close attention to","zh":"注意","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Please pay close attention to the safety instructions.","exampleZh":"请仔细注意安全说明。","collocation":"pay close attention to","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v4","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"carried out a survey","zh":"开展调查","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The volunteers carried out a survey before opening.","exampleZh":"志愿者在开办前进行了调查。","collocation":"carry out a survey","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v5","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"guarantee","zh":"保证","pos":"verb","example":"We cannot guarantee that every repair will succeed.","exampleZh":"我们不能保证每次修理都成功。","collocation":"guarantee that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v6","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"made a considerable difference","zh":"产生影响","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Your description made a considerable difference to our preparation.","exampleZh":"你的描述对我们的准备产生了相当大的影响。","collocation":"make a considerable difference","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v7","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"took","zh":"在本句中与 into account 搭配表示考虑了","pos":"verb","example":"We took the owner’s personal story into account.","exampleZh":"我们考虑了物主的个人故事。","collocation":"take a story into account","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v8","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draw attention away","zh":"转移注意力","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Repeated questions can draw attention away from a difficult repair.","exampleZh":"反复提问可能转移对困难修理工作的注意力。","collocation":"draw attention away from","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v9","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"met her needs","zh":"满足需求","pos":"verb phrase","example":"A replacement would not have met her needs.","exampleZh":"替代品不会满足她的需求。","collocation":"meet someone’s needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v10","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"drew a distinction","zh":"作出区分","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We drew a distinction between market value and personal meaning.","exampleZh":"我们区分了市场价值与个人意义。","collocation":"draw a distinction between","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v11","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"played a part","zh":"发挥作用","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Patient listening played a part in the café’s success.","exampleZh":"耐心倾听是修理咖啡馆成功的因素之一。","collocation":"play a part in","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v12","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"The swimming lesson nobody could reach","text":"The council celebrated a rise in registrations for adult swimming lessons. A new online booking page had filled every advertised place within two days. From the office, the programme looked more successful than ever. At the pool, however, instructors noticed that several learners repeatedly missed sessions despite having paid in advance.\nOne instructor, Nia, called the absent learners with their permission. Their explanations varied. A cleaner’s shift occasionally ended late, a parent could not arrange childcare, and another learner relied on a bus that arrived after the warm-up. None of these people needed a more attractive booking page. They needed an arrangement that fitted the rest of their day.\nThe pool could not offer unlimited flexibility. Instructors had fixed hours, and maintaining a safe ratio of learners to staff mattered. Nia therefore proposed a limited experiment: one later group, a simple procedure for changing a session, and a clearly stated deadline for doing so. The team also reserved a few places that could be booked by telephone rather than requiring everyone to compete online.\nAfter six weeks, attendance became more consistent. The changes had not removed every barrier, and the later group cost slightly more to run. Nevertheless, the council’s report now distinguished registrations from actual participation. It published the extra staffing cost beside the attendance figures. This made the result less suitable for a dramatic headline, but more useful for deciding whether the service was meeting local needs. Success meant more than filling a timetable; it meant enabling people to use the opportunity they had been offered."},"sourceB":{"title":"A music service beyond the waiting list","text":"A regional music service wanted to shorten the waiting list for beginner lessons. Its first proposal was to combine several small classes into one large Saturday group. This would allow more children to enrol without employing another teacher. The change appeared efficient until staff asked families why they had chosen individual instruments and times.\nSome children needed a quiet environment to concentrate. Others shared an instrument with a sibling or could travel only when a particular relative was available. The waiting list recorded names and ages but none of these practical conditions. A larger class might technically create places while leaving some families unable to accept them.\nThe service decided to collect preferences before reorganising. It offered a trial group for children happy to learn together, while retaining a smaller number of quieter sessions. Borrowed instruments were made available for home practice, subject to a clear return agreement. Teachers also explained that group lessons involved less individual correction, so families could make an informed choice.\nThe trial reduced the list, although not as quickly as the original proposal promised. Its costs included checking instruments and arranging repairs, which had previously been omitted from the calculation. At the final review, one board member argued that the simpler plan would have produced a better figure. The director replied that a place on paper was not the same as a usable opportunity.\nThe service continued both formats and scheduled another review after a full term. Its aim was not to guarantee one ideal lesson for every child, but to make the remaining compromises visible rather than hide them inside a convenient average."},"listening":{"teaching":"When we introduced evening advice sessions at the housing centre, I expected demand to come mainly from people working standard office hours. In fact, many visitors were relatives helping someone complete forms. They needed time to discuss the instructions together, not simply a later closing time. We changed the appointment format to allow a supporter to attend, provided the applicant agreed. We also placed a short checklist beside the booking page so visitors knew which documents were actually needed. This reduced some repeated visits, though it did not eliminate complicated cases. I would be cautious about saying the service became faster in every sense. Individual appointments sometimes lasted longer because staff were addressing questions that had previously gone unanswered. The useful change was that more people left understanding their next step. We now measure that understanding through a brief voluntary follow-up, while keeping the number of appointments as a separate figure. Otherwise, we might reward staff for ending conversations quickly even when visitors still felt lost.","test":"Our village minibus was described as underused because the average number of passengers was low. I thought we should investigate before cancelling it. The timetable showed trips to town at nine and eleven, but hospital appointments often required arrival earlier. Meanwhile, the return journey left before several afternoon activities ended. We asked residents to describe actual journeys they could not make. Their answers helped us move one departure and arrange an extra return on two days each week. Passenger numbers rose, although we still depend on a council contribution. It would be misleading to call the service financially independent. Its purpose includes helping people who have no suitable alternative, and those journeys may never fill every seat. The council now considers cost, access and the effect on other local services together. We also ask whether a change disadvantages existing passengers, since attracting new users should not mean silently removing the only practical journey someone already relies on. The next consultation will include residents who still cannot use the revised timetable, not only recent passengers."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why were registrations a weak measure of success?","options":["They recorded only people who paid by telephone.","They could not reveal instructor qualifications.","They did not show whether people could attend."],"answer":"They did not show whether people could attend.","explanation":"报名与实际可参加不同。","id":"b2-01:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was flexibility limited?","options":["The later group could not use the same booking page.","Safety and staffing still required predictable arrangements.","The council wanted all sessions online."],"answer":"Safety and staffing still required predictable arrangements.","explanation":"比例安全及固定工时限制方案。","id":"b2-01:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did evening advice visitors often need?","options":["A chance to discuss instructions with a supporter.","Longer opening hours without any change in appointment format.","More documents to complete independently before visiting."],"answer":"A chance to discuss instructions with a supporter.","explanation":"支持者陪同和共同理解是实际需要。","id":"b2-01:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why separate appointment counts from understanding?","options":["Understanding is fully measured by payment figures.","Longer appointments always indicate worse service.","Quick endings do not necessarily mean useful help."],"answer":"Quick endings do not necessarily mean useful help.","explanation":"效率数字可能掩盖未解决疑问。","id":"b2-01:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What was missing from the music waiting list?","options":["The number of classes already advertised.","Families’ practical conditions and preferences.","The children’s ages and chosen instruments."],"answer":"Families’ practical conditions and preferences.","explanation":"仅姓名年龄不足以安排可用名额。","id":"b2-01:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What was the director’s key distinction?","options":["A nominal place versus an opportunity a family can use.","Group lessons versus all individual music practice.","A waiting list total versus the number of teachers employed."],"answer":"A nominal place versus an opportunity a family can use.","explanation":"实际可使用才是服务结果。","id":"b2-01:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What helped improve the minibus timetable?","options":["Increasing both original departure times equally.","Using the same timetable but adding a booking app.","Accounts of journeys residents could not make."],"answer":"Accounts of journeys residents could not make.","explanation":"从未满足旅程获取需求证据。","id":"b2-01:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is financial independence not the sole criterion?","options":["Passenger numbers rose enough to pay every cost.","The service supports people lacking alternatives.","New passengers should have priority over existing users."],"answer":"The service supports people lacking alternatives.","explanation":"公共可达性与盈利不能等同。","id":"b2-01:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending how a leisure centre should improve participation without promising unlimited flexibility.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how registration numbers can differ from the usefulness of a service.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for a music service balancing waiting-list reduction with different families’ practical needs.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Recommend how the village should judge a subsidised transport service, acknowledging one trade-off.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["服务经理","有不同时间需求的使用者"],"goal":"协商小规模试行并明确成本与验收指标。","twist":"新方案吸引更多人，但可能挤掉原本唯一能参加的使用者。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-02","level":"B2","title":"设计、构词与可理解性","chapter":26,"focus":"利用词形与上下文理解抽象概念，评估信息是否可用","vocab":[{"head":"financially","zh":"在财务上","pos":"adverb","example":"The first proposal was financially unrealistic.","exampleZh":"第一份方案在财务上不现实。","collocation":"financially unrealistic","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v1","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accessibility","zh":"可达性","pos":"noun","example":"We checked the accessibility of the roof.","exampleZh":"我们检查了屋顶是否便于到达。","collocation":"accessibility of the roof","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v2","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"participation","zh":"参与","pos":"noun","example":"Participation in the garden project is voluntary.","exampleZh":"参与花园项目是自愿的。","collocation":"voluntary participation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v3","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revised","zh":"修订过的","pos":"adjective","example":"The revised instructions were easier to follow.","exampleZh":"修改后的说明更容易遵循。","collocation":"revised instructions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v4","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unevenly","zh":"不均匀地","pos":"adverb","example":"The plants grew unevenly near the wall.","exampleZh":"靠墙的植物长得不均匀。","collocation":"grow unevenly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v5","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confidence","zh":"信心","pos":"noun","example":"Her confidence increased after teaching other students.","exampleZh":"教过其他学生后，她的信心增强了。","collocation":"increase confidence","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v6","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confusion","zh":"困惑","pos":"noun","example":"The watering record reduced unnecessary confusion.","exampleZh":"浇水记录减少了不必要的困惑。","collocation":"unnecessary confusion","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v7","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accurately","zh":"准确地","pos":"adverb","example":"Students accurately recorded when each container was watered.","exampleZh":"学生准确记录了每个花盆何时浇水。","collocation":"record accurately","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v8","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rapidly","zh":"迅速地","pos":"adverb","example":"Small plants can dry rapidly in windy conditions.","exampleZh":"小植物在风中可能迅速变干。","collocation":"dry rapidly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v9","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inaccessible","zh":"不便到达的","pos":"adjective","example":"The original roof was inaccessible to ordinary visitors.","exampleZh":"原来的屋顶不便普通访客到达。","collocation":"inaccessible to visitors","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v10","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"understanding","zh":"理解","pos":"noun","example":"Understanding the soil helped beginners care for vegetables.","exampleZh":"理解土壤有助于新手照顾蔬菜。","collocation":"understanding the soil","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v11","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preparation","zh":"准备","pos":"noun","example":"Careful preparation made the revised proposal more practical.","exampleZh":"仔细准备使修订方案更实际。","collocation":"careful preparation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v12","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"Why the recycling label failed","text":"The waste team at a college thought its new recycling labels were exceptionally clear. Each bin displayed a large symbol and a short heading. Yet weekly inspections found almost as many unsuitable items as before. The designers initially blamed students for not paying attention, but a caretaker suggested watching people use the bins during a busy lunch period.\nThe observation changed the discussion. Students often approached carrying several objects and made a decision in seconds. One container accepted plastic bottles but not similar-looking food trays. Its heading said plastics, while the exception appeared in tiny print underneath. The symbol was recognisable, but it described a category more broadly than the collection system allowed.\nThe team replaced the general label with photographs of accepted items and one nearby example of a common mistake. They kept the wording visible at normal standing height. They also moved the bins into a consistent order across buildings, so users did not have to relearn the arrangement every time they entered a different hall.\nContamination fell over the next month. Nevertheless, the college avoided reporting that the design alone had caused the change: staff had also given short explanations during induction week. The result supported continued testing, not a claim of perfect communication.\nThe most useful lesson concerned responsibility. A message could be attractive, accurate in a narrow technical sense and still create a predictable misunderstanding. Improving it required examining the reader’s situation, including time pressure and prior knowledge. Asking users to try harder would not correct a label that invited the wrong interpretation in the first place."},"sourceB":{"title":"A booking form that looked simple","text":"A theatre replaced its long access questionnaire with a short online form. The project team celebrated the reduction from twenty questions to six. They expected fewer visitors to abandon the process. At first, the figures supported that expectation: more forms reached the final page. However, front-of-house staff began receiving requests that were too vague to act on.\nThe new form asked whether a visitor required assistance, with a yes or no choice. It no longer explained what assistance might include. Some people selected no because they could enter the building independently, although they needed a seat where they could hear clearly or store a mobility aid. Others selected yes without knowing whether they were asking for physical help or information.\nThe theatre invited a small group of visitors to test a revised version. Instead of restoring every old question, the team added concrete examples and an optional box for individual circumstances. The confirmation message explained which requests were already agreed and which required a telephone discussion. Staff were trained not to interpret a blank answer as proof that no adjustment was needed.\nThe revised form was slightly longer but produced fewer urgent misunderstandings on performance nights. The team stopped treating the smallest number of questions as its main design goal. A form needed to gather enough relevant information while avoiding unnecessary demands for personal details.\nTheir final report described accessibility as an ongoing process rather than a feature achieved by removing words. Simplicity was valuable when it reduced confusion; it became harmful when it removed the context visitors needed to express an important need."},"listening":{"teaching":"I helped redesign the instructions for a community sewing machine. The first draft used accurate names for every part, which seemed professional to us. Beginners could not connect several names with anything they could see. We added a labelled drawing and numbered the actions in the order they should happen. That was useful, but the most important change came during a practical test. One learner tried to change the needle while the machine was still connected to power. The warning had been at the end of the page, after the action it was supposed to prevent. We moved it immediately before the relevant step and asked another beginner to try the instructions. I learned that completeness is not enough: information must appear at the moment when a person needs it. We still ask users about uncertainty, since an apparently smooth demonstration can hide guessing. The aim is independent, safe use, not simply a page that looks organised to somebody who already knows the machine.","test":"Our railway station installed ticket machines with fewer buttons, but complaints did not disappear. Passengers selecting a return journey were unsure whether the date shown referred to departure or the journey home. The screen was clean and spacious, yet an essential relationship remained unclear. We added separate headings and displayed a short summary before payment. Some colleagues worried that the extra screen would slow everyone down. In the trial, experienced users took a little longer, but fewer people bought an unsuitable ticket and needed staff help afterwards. We therefore considered the whole process rather than just the time spent pressing buttons. The machine still offers assistance through a staffed counter for people who cannot use it comfortably. We do not describe that as failure. A public service should provide a practical route to the correct result, not force every passenger to demonstrate confidence with the same piece of technology under pressure from a queue. We test proposed changes with passengers carrying bags, because the pressure of a real journey can alter how a screen is understood."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the plastics heading misleading?","options":["It gave an exception more emphasis than the main category.","It used images instead of any category label.","It was broader than the system’s accepted items."],"answer":"It was broader than the system’s accepted items.","explanation":"分类用语比实际规则宽。","id":"b2-02:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why avoid a firm causal claim about the new labels?","options":["The bins had been removed during the measurement.","Induction explanations changed at the same time.","Every student had been given a personal reward."],"answer":"Induction explanations changed at the same time.","explanation":"同期解释干预影响因果归属。","id":"b2-02:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What was wrong with the first sewing warning?","options":["It appeared after the action it needed to prevent.","It used a technical name for the needle.","It repeated the same warning beside each drawing."],"answer":"It appeared after the action it needed to prevent.","explanation":"信息时机决定能否避免危险。","id":"b2-02:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the design goal?","options":["Instructions that beginners can repeat without trying the machine.","The shortest possible page regardless of sequence.","Independent, safe use rather than appearance alone."],"answer":"Independent, safe use rather than appearance alone.","explanation":"强调真正可用性。","id":"b2-02:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the yes-or-no assistance question weak?","options":["It required visitors to describe too much medical history.","It did not clarify the types of help available.","It suggested only people needing physical help could attend."],"answer":"It did not clarify the types of help available.","explanation":"assistance 含义缺背景。","id":"b2-02:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why accept a slightly longer form?","options":["It gathered more useful information with fewer misunderstandings.","A longer form can replace staff training entirely.","The theatre needed to restore every original question."],"answer":"It gathered more useful information with fewer misunderstandings.","explanation":"长度不是唯一优化目标。","id":"b2-02:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What relationship was unclear on the machine?","options":["Whether the return fare included a reserved seat.","Whether the destination had changed before payment.","Which journey the displayed date referred to."],"answer":"Which journey the displayed date referred to.","explanation":"日期指向去程或回程不清。","id":"b2-02:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why consider the whole process?","options":["Experienced users alone should determine the design.","Faster button pressing could cause later corrections.","The staffed counter made machine testing unnecessary."],"answer":"Faster button pressing could cause later corrections.","explanation":"局部快可能整体慢。","id":"b2-02:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report on how to test instructions with beginners. Explain wording, sequence and the limits of a small trial.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain why making a text shorter does not always make it clearer.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a proposal improving the theatre access form without collecting unnecessary personal information.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss whether a ticket machine should prioritise speed or reliable selection, using a practical compromise.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["设计者","第一次使用设备的人"],"goal":"通过实测发现信息缺口，而不责怪使用者。","twist":"简化页面提高完成率，却使关键请求含糊。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-03","level":"B2","title":"条件、证据与因果边界","chapter":27,"focus":"准确表达假设、限制与多重解释","vocab":[{"head":"consumption","zh":"消费","pos":"noun","example":"Shared equipment can reduce unnecessary consumption.","exampleZh":"共享设备能减少不必要的消费。","collocation":"unnecessary consumption","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v1","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ownership","zh":"所有权","pos":"noun","example":"Access may matter more than ownership.","exampleZh":"使用权可能比所有权更重要。","collocation":"access rather than ownership","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v2","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance","zh":"维护","pos":"noun","example":"We must consider the cost of maintenance.","exampleZh":"我们必须考虑维护成本。","collocation":"cost of maintenance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v3","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"resources","zh":"资源","pos":"noun","example":"The booking system distributes limited resources fairly.","exampleZh":"预约系统公平分配有限资源。","collocation":"limited resources","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v4","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"durable","zh":"耐用的","pos":"adjective","example":"A durable product should remain useful for years.","exampleZh":"耐用产品应该多年仍可使用。","collocation":"durable product","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v5","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"affordable","zh":"负担得起的","pos":"adjective","example":"The scheme offers an affordable alternative.","exampleZh":"该计划提供了价格可承受的替代选择。","collocation":"affordable alternative","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v6","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"There is little evidence of a long term benefit yet.","exampleZh":"目前几乎没有长期收益的证据。","collocation":"little evidence","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v7","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"households","zh":"家庭住户","pos":"noun","example":"Several households have joined the scheme.","exampleZh":"几个家庭已加入该计划。","collocation":"several households","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v8","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"availability","zh":"可用性","pos":"noun","example":"We need reliable information about availability.","exampleZh":"我们需要可靠的可用情况信息。","collocation":"information about availability","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v9","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"impact","zh":"影响","pos":"noun","example":"The environmental impact depends on how people travel.","exampleZh":"环境影响取决于人们如何出行。","collocation":"environmental impact","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v10","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outweigh","zh":"超过","pos":"verb","example":"The benefits should outweigh the practical disadvantages.","exampleZh":"收益应该超过实际弊端。","collocation":"benefits outweigh disadvantages","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v11","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transparent","zh":"透明的","pos":"adjective","example":"Publishing the costs makes the process more transparent.","exampleZh":"公开成本使过程更加透明。","collocation":"transparent process","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v12","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"An office tests quiet mornings","text":"A software team introduced two quiet mornings each week. During these periods, staff were asked to avoid routine meetings and non-urgent messages. The manager hoped that longer uninterrupted stretches would make difficult work easier. After a month, several employees reported finishing tasks with less frustration, and the number of completed tickets increased.\nThe manager was tempted to declare the experiment a success. A colleague asked whether the tickets represented comparable work. The team had recently finished a complicated system migration, leaving a queue of simpler repairs. Counting each item equally might make the new routine appear more powerful than it was. Staff satisfaction still mattered, but it answered a different question from output.\nThe team reviewed examples of complex work and interviewed people in support roles. These employees could not simply ignore customers until lunchtime. Some had been carrying an unfair share of urgent interruptions while others enjoyed protected time. The revised arrangement rotated responsibility for urgent contact and gave the person on duty another quiet period later in the week.\nThe second month produced a more balanced picture. Most staff preferred the routine, although it did not suit every kind of task. The team retained it while avoiding a promise that the same timetable would improve every department.\nHad they relied on the first average alone, they would have missed both a measurement problem and a fairness problem. The experiment became more useful when the question changed from whether quiet mornings worked to whom they helped, under which conditions, and at what cost to colleagues whose work could not wait."},"sourceB":{"title":"A town counts cyclists","text":"After installing a protected cycle lane, a town announced that cycling along the route had increased by forty per cent. Supporters described the figure as proof that the lane had created a major change in travel habits. Opponents argued that all the riders had simply moved from neighbouring roads. Neither explanation could be established from the first count alone.\nThe transport officer examined when and where measurements had been taken. The earlier count occurred during a wet school week, whereas the new count covered a dry holiday period. The increase was real at that location, but the comparison mixed the effect of the lane with weather and changes in daily routines.\nThe council arranged further counts on nearby streets and asked a sample of riders about their previous journeys. Some had switched routes, some had replaced short car trips, and a few were using bicycles mainly for leisure. These results did not make the lane pointless. They showed that one headline figure could represent several different changes.\nThe officer’s report separated use of the lane from a reduction in car travel. It also recorded residents’ concerns about loading space outside shops. A later adjustment created timed deliveries without removing the protection at the busiest junction.\nThe final recommendation supported keeping the lane while continuing to examine its wider effects. The council could reasonably claim that people used the new facility. It needed stronger evidence before claiming a particular reduction in traffic across the whole town. Careful wording protected the useful finding from being weakened by an exaggerated explanation."},"listening":{"teaching":"Our school moved revision sessions from Friday afternoon to Wednesday lunchtime. Attendance rose immediately, and the head teacher asked whether the new time was responsible. It probably helped, but two other things changed: students could now bring lunch, and teachers advertised the sessions in subject classes. We could not separate those influences from a single month’s record. Instead of abandoning the result, we asked students what had made attendance possible. Some mentioned transport after school; others said they had not known the sessions existed before. We retained the lunchtime option and made the publicity consistent for the next term. We also checked whether students with clubs at lunch were being excluded. The useful conclusion was not that one day was universally better. It was that practical access and clear information mattered, and that a simple attendance number could not tell us which barrier had been removed for each student. A larger total would not be enough if the same students continued to face an overlooked practical barrier.","test":"We trialled self-checkout at the food cooperative. Queue times fell during the first week, but that was also the week after a festival, when shopping was unusually quiet. A volunteer suggested comparing similar hours over several weeks. We did that and found a smaller improvement than the initial figure suggested. Customers with a few items often benefited, while people buying loose produce still needed assistance. The machines also created work elsewhere: somebody had to correct prices and explain unfamiliar steps. We decided to keep one machine alongside staffed payment rather than replace every volunteer. I support that decision, but I would not call the technology cost-free simply because fewer people stood behind a till. The important question is how work and difficulty are redistributed. We are recording requests for help as well as waiting times, so our next review will show more than the fastest part of the transaction. The volunteer rota will therefore include preparation and support time, rather than counting only minutes spent taking payments."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the ticket count misleading?","options":["The team counted support calls as separate projects.","Quiet mornings had reduced the number of available staff.","The work had become simpler."],"answer":"The work had become simpler.","explanation":"任务难度变化影响数量比较。","id":"b2-03:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What fairness problem appeared?","options":["Some staff received fewer complex tickets.","Support staff absorbed more urgent interruptions.","All protected time was taken by one manager."],"answer":"Support staff absorbed more urgent interruptions.","explanation":"保护时间把负担转移给特定岗位。","id":"b2-03:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why not attribute revision attendance solely to timing?","options":["Food permission and publicity also changed.","The old Friday sessions had lasted longer.","Students had been required to attend for the first time."],"answer":"Food permission and publicity also changed.","explanation":"多项同步变化不能简单归因。","id":"b2-03:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What additional group needed consideration?","options":["Students who already preferred the Friday session.","Teachers who never advertised their subjects.","Students with lunchtime clubs."],"answer":"Students with lunchtime clubs.","explanation":"新安排也可能排除原有活动者。","id":"b2-03:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the first cycling comparison limited?","options":["Counts used the same season but different equipment only.","Weather and calendar conditions differed.","The new count measured only riders who had replaced cars."],"answer":"Weather and calendar conditions differed.","explanation":"比较条件不一致。","id":"b2-03:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What distinction did the report make?","options":["Lane use versus a reduction in car travel.","School journeys versus all recreational journeys.","Public support versus the exact construction cost."],"answer":"Lane use versus a reduction in car travel.","explanation":"使用增长不直接等于汽车减少。","id":"b2-03:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What weakened the checkout’s initial result?","options":["Staff had not yet learned the machine settings.","Most customers were buying loose produce that week.","It coincided with unusually quiet shopping."],"answer":"It coincided with unusually quiet shopping.","explanation":"首周特殊背景影响队长。","id":"b2-03:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why record help requests?","options":["It provides a replacement for waiting-time records.","To capture work and difficulty beyond the till.","It proves which customers should use staffed payment."],"answer":"To capture work and difficulty beyond the till.","explanation":"整体工作分配不能只看排队时间。","id":"b2-03:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay explaining how a workplace should evaluate a new working arrangement. Consider outcomes and fairness.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain a result that has more than one plausible cause without dismissing the evidence entirely.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a report assessing the cycle lane claim and recommending additional evidence and one practical adjustment.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss whether the cooperative should replace all staffed payment with machines, acknowledging hidden work.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["试行负责人","负责受影响岗位的同事"],"goal":"制定公平比较并检查负担是否被转移。","twist":"表面效率提高，但任务难度和员工职责同时改变。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-04","level":"B2","title":"消费、资源与长期影响","chapter":28,"focus":"解释资源选择的条件，避免简单把新旧或共享视为好坏","vocab":[{"head":"proposed","zh":"提议","pos":"verb","example":"The former railway station stood empty for years before a local group proposed a shared workspace.","exampleZh":"旧火车站空置多年后，一个当地团体提议把它变成共享工作场所。","collocation":"propose a workspace","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v1","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"potential","zh":"潜在的","pos":"adjective","example":"Rather than assume that everyone needed a private office, the organisers interviewed potential users about their routines.","exampleZh":"组织者没有假定人人都需要独立办公室，而是访谈潜在使用者的工作习惯。","collocation":"potential users","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v2","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"whereas","zh":"然而","pos":"conjunction","example":"Freelance designers wanted quiet desks, whereas people running small shops mainly needed a place for occasional meetings.","exampleZh":"自由设计师需要安静书桌，而小店经营者主要需要偶尔开会的地方。","collocation":"whereas others","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v3","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contrasting","zh":"形成对照的","pos":"adjective","example":"These contrasting needs persuaded the group to divide the building into several areas with different booking rules.","exampleZh":"这些不同需求促使团队把建筑分成几个预约规则不同的区域。","collocation":"contrasting needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v4","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suitable","zh":"适合的","pos":"adjective","example":"The ticket office became a silent room, while the waiting hall was suitable for conversations and shared equipment.","exampleZh":"售票处变成了安静室，候车厅则适合交流和共享设备。","collocation":"suitable for","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v5","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"discourage","zh":"使失去意愿","pos":"verb","example":"Despite limited funding, the group invested in good lighting because poor conditions would discourage people from returning.","exampleZh":"尽管资金有限，团队仍投入优质照明，因为不好的环境会使人不再来。","collocation":"discourage people from returning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v6","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"covered","zh":"支付得起","pos":"verb","example":"Membership fees covered ordinary running costs, but major repairs still required support from the council and local businesses.","exampleZh":"会员费承担日常运营成本，但大修仍需议会及当地企业支持。","collocation":"cover running costs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v7","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reserve","zh":"预订","pos":"verb","example":"At first, several members complained that the booking website allowed some people to reserve too many afternoons.","exampleZh":"起初，几位成员抱怨预约网站允许部分人预订过多下午时段。","collocation":"reserve an afternoon","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v8","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responded","zh":"回应","pos":"verb","example":"The organisers responded by introducing a weekly limit rather than blaming members for using the existing system.","exampleZh":"组织者通过增加每周上限回应，而不是责怪成员使用现有系统。","collocation":"respond by introducing","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v9","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evenly","zh":"均匀地","pos":"adverb","example":"Within three months, bookings were distributed more evenly, although the most convenient times remained particularly popular with commuters.","exampleZh":"三个月内预约分配更均衡了，不过通勤者仍特别喜欢最方便的时段。","collocation":"distribute evenly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v10","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depended less on","zh":"取决于","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The project’s success depended less on fashionable furniture than on matching practical arrangements to the way members actually worked.","exampleZh":"项目成功与时髦家具关系较小，更多取决于实际安排符合成员工作方式。","collocation":"depend less on","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v11","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consultation","zh":"征询意见","pos":"noun","example":"Its report recommended regular consultation, recognising that a flexible service must continue adapting as the community and its working habits change.","exampleZh":"报告建议定期征求意见，认识到灵活服务必须随社区和工作习惯变化持续调整。","collocation":"regular consultation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v12","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"The wardrobe subscription","text":"A clothing company offered a subscription allowing customers to borrow several garments each month. Its advertising emphasised access without ownership and suggested that fewer purchases would automatically mean less environmental harm. Lara liked the idea because she needed formal clothes only occasionally. Before joining, however, she wanted to understand what happened between one customer and the next.\nThe company explained that every returned garment was inspected, cleaned and transported to another customer. Clothes damaged beyond repair were removed from the collection. These processes were necessary, but their costs meant the comparison with buying was not as simple as the advertisement implied. A durable jacket worn regularly for years might be a different case from an outfit bought once for a single event.\nLara compared her likely behaviour under both systems. If the subscription encouraged her to order a different outfit every week, she might increase deliveries without avoiding many purchases. If she used it for two annual ceremonies instead of buying clothes she rarely wore, the balance could be more favourable. The outcome depended on what the service replaced, not only on its label.\nShe eventually chose occasional rental rather than the monthly plan. This did not establish a universal rule about clothing. It matched her actual needs and avoided paying for a level of variety she had not previously wanted.\nThe experience changed the questions she asked about sustainable products. She looked for information about durability, transport and repeated use, while remaining cautious about claims based solely on the absence of ownership. A useful comparison needed the whole pattern of use rather than an attractive idea considered in isolation."},"sourceB":{"title":"Repairing the old school desks","text":"A secondary school needed furniture for two refurbished classrooms. A supplier proposed new desks made partly from recycled material. A local workshop offered to repair the existing wooden desks and replace damaged tops. The new products carried an environmental label, while the repair estimate included no simple score. Several governors assumed the labelled option must therefore be the better choice.\nThe facilities manager asked for a more complete comparison. She needed to know how long each option was expected to last, whether replacement parts were available and how far the items would travel. The workshop could repair most frames locally, but a few were unstable and unsuitable for continued use. Keeping every old desk would not be responsible merely because it avoided a purchase.\nThe school chose a mixed approach. Safe frames were repaired, while a smaller number of new desks provided adjustable height where this was needed. Staff checked that the layout remained accessible and that the repaired surfaces could withstand ordinary cleaning. Pupils helped document the process, but trained workers carried out structural repairs.\nThe final report did not claim a precise environmental saving because the school lacked reliable data for every stage. It recorded the number of frames retained, the costs and the expected maintenance requirements. This gave future managers a practical starting point for comparison.\nThe decision was less dramatic than replacing everything with a newly labelled product. It also avoided turning repair into an absolute principle. The aim was useful, safe furniture with a credible plan for continued use, rather than a single purchase that appeared responsible in a photograph."},"listening":{"teaching":"I run a small event company, and we considered replacing disposable cups with reusable ones. The first estimate counted only the price of each cup. That missed washing, storage and the journeys needed to return them. We then examined a typical event from delivery to the final collection. Reuse still looked promising for regular events at one venue because cups could be washed nearby and returned with other equipment. It was less straightforward for small events scattered across the region. We are now testing a deposit system, but we have not announced that waste has disappeared. Some cups are lost, and others break. Our records include those losses instead of counting every cup purchased as a permanent saving. Customers often want a simple answer, and I understand that. Still, an honest explanation should identify when a system works well and which parts remain uncertain. A useful environmental claim is specific enough that somebody else can check the assumptions behind it.","test":"When our hostel bought efficient washing machines, we expected electricity use to fall. Each cycle did use less power, but staff began washing smaller loads because the new machines seemed inexpensive to run. The monthly total barely changed. We had improved one part of the process without considering behaviour around it. After discussing the results, we introduced a full-load check and separated genuinely urgent washing from items that could wait. We also made sure that infection-control requirements were followed rather than using resource savings as a reason to ignore them. The next month looked better, although guest numbers were different, so we adjusted the comparison per occupied room. I am pleased with the equipment, but I would not present a lower energy label as a guarantee of lower total consumption. The effect depends on frequency, loading and the service we are trying to provide. We now look at those conditions before claiming success. Our next staff briefing will use the actual figures to explain why an apparently small change in loading matters."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What mattered in Lara’s comparison?","options":["The number of styles advertised each month.","Whether the rented garment always looked new.","What rental would replace in her own behaviour."],"answer":"What rental would replace in her own behaviour.","explanation":"替代何种购买与使用模式是关键。","id":"b2-04:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why choose occasional rental?","options":["The monthly plan excluded formal clothing.","She needed formal outfits only a few times.","Occasional rental removed all cleaning costs."],"answer":"She needed formal outfits only a few times.","explanation":"方案应贴近实际需求。","id":"b2-04:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the first cup estimate omit important costs?","options":["It counted the cup price but not the system around it.","It included return journeys but not cup colour.","It assumed every venue would hold the same number of events."],"answer":"It counted the cup price but not the system around it.","explanation":"运输清洗储存构成全流程。","id":"b2-04:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What makes a useful environmental claim?","options":["A universal ranking independent of customer behaviour.","A guarantee based only on the purchase price.","Checkable assumptions and specific conditions."],"answer":"Checkable assumptions and specific conditions.","explanation":"可核查而非绝对化。","id":"b2-04:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why not retain every old desk?","options":["The workshop could repair only adjustable desks.","Some frames were unsafe.","Cleaning costs were identical for every frame."],"answer":"Some frames were unsafe.","explanation":"维修不是凌驾安全的绝对原则。","id":"b2-04:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why avoid a precise savings figure?","options":["Reliable information was missing for some stages.","A label already supplied the exact saving.","The school needed a larger number to justify any repair."],"answer":"Reliable information was missing for some stages.","explanation":"不以缺乏数据的精确数字包装结论。","id":"b2-04:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did total washing energy barely fall at first?","options":["Guest numbers had fallen to zero.","The machines used the same amount per cycle as before.","Smaller loads increased inefficient use."],"answer":"Smaller loads increased inefficient use.","explanation":"行为抵消设备单次改善。","id":"b2-04:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why compare per occupied room?","options":["Different rooms had different laundry labels.","Guest numbers differed between months.","Only occupied rooms were allowed to use efficient machines."],"answer":"Guest numbers differed between months.","explanation":"服务量变化需纳入比较。","id":"b2-04:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article about when renting clothes could reduce unnecessary consumption. Explain at least one condition and one uncertainty.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare buying, repairing and borrowing without assuming one is always best.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending a furniture plan for a school, considering safety, durability and missing impact data.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain why efficient equipment may not reduce total consumption and propose a fair way to assess it.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["采购人员","重视长期使用的使用者"],"goal":"比较全流程成本与需求，提出有条件选择。","twist":"贴环保标签的新产品与可修旧品都存在未披露限制。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-05","level":"B2","title":"长文主张与作者立场","chapter":29,"focus":"综合段落理解作者的有条件判断，而不是挑一句结论","vocab":[{"head":"headlines","zh":"新闻标题","pos":"noun","example":"When the night train returned to our region, newspaper headlines described it as a complete solution to uncomfortable travel.","exampleZh":"当夜间列车回到本地区，报纸标题称它是解决出行不适的完整方案。","collocation":"newspaper headlines","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v1","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"complicated","zh":"复杂的","pos":"adjective","example":"My first journey suggested a more complicated picture, although I was pleased to reach the city without losing a working day.","exampleZh":"首次旅程让我看到更复杂的情况，虽然我很高兴没损失一个工作日就到达城市。","collocation":"complicated picture","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v2","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compartment","zh":"隔间","pos":"noun","example":"The compartment was quiet enough for reading, but the movement made deep sleep difficult for passengers unfamiliar with overnight trains.","exampleZh":"车厢安静到可以阅读，但晃动让不习惯夜车的乘客难以熟睡。","collocation":"quiet compartment","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v3","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rested","zh":"休息充分的","pos":"adjective","example":"A fellow traveller explained that saving a hotel bill mattered more to her than arriving completely rested for a holiday.","exampleZh":"一位同行旅客解释，对她而言省住宿费比度假时精神饱满地到达更重要。","collocation":"arrive rested","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v4","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trade off","zh":"取舍","pos":"noun","example":"For a businessman preparing to give a presentation, however, the same trade off might be much less attractive or practical.","exampleZh":"但对准备演讲的商务人士，同样的取舍可能远不那么有吸引力或实用。","collocation":"make a trade off","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v5","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivalent","zh":"等同的","pos":"adjective","example":"Ticket prices were lower when booked early, so comparisons with flights needed to use equivalent dates and booking conditions.","exampleZh":"提前订票更便宜，因此与飞机比较必须使用相同日期及订票条件。","collocation":"equivalent dates","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v6","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"calculated","zh":"计算","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"The operator advertised reduced environmental impact, but its short publicity leaflet did not explain how the figures had been calculated.","exampleZh":"运营方宣传环境影响降低，但简短宣传页没有解释数字如何计算。","collocation":"calculate figures","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v7","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"absence","zh":"缺少","pos":"noun","example":"This absence did not prove the claim false; it meant that a careful reader needed additional information before accepting it.","exampleZh":"缺少解释并不证明主张错误，而是意味着审慎读者需更多信息再接受它。","collocation":"absence of detail","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v8","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"connection","zh":"接驳","pos":"noun","example":"At the destination, a convenient tram connection helped passengers avoid a costly taxi ride from an airport far outside town.","exampleZh":"到站后方便的电车接驳让乘客避免从远郊机场搭昂贵出租车。","collocation":"tram connection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v9","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"advantage","zh":"优势","pos":"noun","example":"That advantage depended on where travellers actually needed to go, rather than on the station’s central location in itself.","exampleZh":"这一优势取决于旅客实际目的地，而不只是车站本身处于市中心。","collocation":"practical advantage","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v10","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"options","zh":"选择","pos":"noun","example":"I would choose the train again for a relaxed visit, while considering other options before an important morning meeting.","exampleZh":"悠闲旅行我会再选火车，但重要早会前会考虑其他选择。","collocation":"consider options","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v11","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priorities","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"The service expands our choices, yet its usefulness cannot be judged accurately without considering the priorities and circumstances of different passengers.","exampleZh":"该服务增加了选择，但不考虑不同乘客的重点与情况就不能准确判断用途。","collocation":"different priorities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v12","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"The attraction of a slower journey","text":"When a travel magazine invited me to spend four days reaching a city that I could fly to in three hours, I expected a story about personal sacrifice. The assignment instead forced me to reconsider what counted as travelling time. On the first train I read, ate and watched unfamiliar landscapes. These hours were part of the holiday rather than an interval to be erased.\nThat pleasure did not remove practical disadvantages. I carried my bag between platforms, worried about a short connection and paid more than the cheapest flight would have cost. My schedule also depended on having leave from work. Describing the journey as an easy choice for everybody would ignore conditions that made it possible for me.\nI met passengers whose reasons differed from mine. One avoided flying because of anxiety; another valued stopping to visit relatives on the way. A family had chosen the route because their children enjoyed trains, though the parents admitted the luggage was exhausting. These accounts made a single comparison of speed or price feel inadequate.\nBy the final morning, I was convinced that slower travel deserved a place among realistic options. I was not convinced that it should become a moral test separating good travellers from bad ones. Access, time and responsibility are unevenly distributed.\nThe useful question is not whether every journey must be slow. It is whether planning begins with a genuine choice or with the assumption that the shortest time in motion is always best. Once travellers understand the whole experience and its constraints, they can weigh advantages without pretending that another person’s circumstances are identical to their own."},"sourceB":{"title":"Why I stopped reviewing restaurants by price alone","text":"For years, I wrote a column comparing restaurants according to how much food a fixed sum could buy. Readers liked the apparent clarity of the ranking. A large portion at a low price scored well; a smaller plate scored badly. The system was easy to repeat, and I rarely questioned what it left outside the calculation.\nThat changed after a reader asked whether the column could help her father, who needed a quiet place and enough room for a mobility aid. My highest-ranked restaurant was crowded and noisy. Its food remained good value for many customers, but the ranking did not answer her actual question.\nI began adding information about atmosphere, access, service and the flexibility of the menu. This made comparisons less tidy. A restaurant could be excellent for a hurried lunch and unsuitable for a long family conversation. Some readers complained that I was avoiding clear judgement. I understood the frustration: people often want a quick recommendation, not another complicated decision.\nYet a single score can create confidence by concealing the priorities used to produce it. My old system had not been neutral. It had favoured quantity over needs I had never measured. I still report prices, because affordability matters and vague praise can hide expense. I simply place the figure beside other relevant observations.\nThe aim of a review should be to help readers choose, not to make the reviewer look certain. A useful recommendation can remain decisive while explaining whom it is likely to suit and where its limits lie. That requires more careful writing than declaring one place universally best."},"listening":{"teaching":"I review walking holidays, and I used to assume the longest route offered the greatest value. A guide challenged me by asking whether value meant kilometres or a worthwhile experience. On our next trip, we spent half a day in one valley, looking closely at farming methods and speaking with a resident. We covered less ground but understood more about the place. I would not claim that short walks are always better. Some travellers genuinely want physical challenge, and the valley visit depended on a guide with local contacts. What changed was my habit of treating distance as an objective measure that needed no explanation. Now I describe pace, terrain, opportunities to stop and the kind of interest the route supports. Readers can then decide whether the trip matches their expectations. A detailed description may be less convenient than a single ranking, but it avoids making one traveller’s priorities appear like a rule that everyone should follow. The review should make those priorities visible, so disagreement can concern the right question rather than a misleading total.","test":"People sometimes ask why our book group stopped choosing only recent prize winners. We still read them, but we noticed that the awards list had become a substitute for discussing what the group wanted. Some members enjoyed difficult experiments in style, while others wanted a story they could follow after a demanding workday. Neither preference was a defect. We now alternate kinds of books and explain the reason for each choice. Attendance has become more consistent, although the new system has not ended every disagreement. I would be suspicious if it had; a reading group should leave room for different responses. The main improvement is that members can understand the choice even when it is not their favourite. We have not rejected expert judgement. We have stopped treating an expert’s award as a guarantee that one book will suit every reader, every purpose and every moment in a person’s life. A good recommendation starts a useful discussion; it should not close that discussion before members can explain their own experience."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did the writer’s expectation change?","options":["The writer became certain that slow travel was cheaper.","The assignment removed the need for leave from work.","Travel time became part of the experience rather than pure sacrifice."],"answer":"Travel time became part of the experience rather than pure sacrifice.","explanation":"从牺牲叙事转向过程价值。","id":"b2-05:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the writer reject?","options":["Considering price when deciding on a route.","Using slow travel as a universal moral test.","Including visits to relatives in a longer trip."],"answer":"Using slow travel as a universal moral test.","explanation":"支持选择但拒绝道德一刀切。","id":"b2-05:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the guide challenge?","options":["Distance as an unquestioned measure of value.","Using distance as one factor among several.","The value of any physically challenging holiday."],"answer":"Distance as an unquestioned measure of value.","explanation":"重新定义价值指标。","id":"b2-05:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why describe route conditions?","options":["So the guide can avoid recommending a pace.","So all routes receive the same final score.","So readers can judge according to their expectations."],"answer":"So readers can judge according to their expectations.","explanation":"适配优先于单一排名。","id":"b2-05:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the restaurant ranking conceal?","options":["The exact opening hours of each restaurant.","The priorities behind the scoring system.","The preferences of the restaurant owners alone."],"answer":"The priorities behind the scoring system.","explanation":"看似客观其实偏好份量。","id":"b2-05:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why still report prices?","options":["Affordability remains relevant.","Every review is now restricted to inexpensive places.","Other observations cannot be compared at all."],"answer":"Affordability remains relevant.","explanation":"多维度不等于忽视成本。","id":"b2-05:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why change the book selection system?","options":["Members had unanimously rejected every prize winner.","Experts had stopped publishing reviews.","Awards were replacing discussion of members’ needs."],"answer":"Awards were replacing discussion of members’ needs.","explanation":"不是反专家，而是考虑用途。","id":"b2-05:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the improvement despite disagreement?","options":["Members choose only books they already know.","Members understand the reasons for choices.","Expert recommendations are no longer considered."],"answer":"Members understand the reasons for choices.","explanation":"可理解的决策不要求同好。","id":"b2-05:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an essay on whether the fastest travel option is always the best. Develop a balanced position with concrete conditions.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Summarise the travel writer’s position, including a limitation the writer explicitly acknowledges.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining what a useful restaurant review should include beyond price.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss how a group can use expert recommendations without treating them as universal rules.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["评论作者","需求不同的读者"],"goal":"说明评判标准并给适合特定人的建议。","twist":"读者要求单一总分，但不同需求会导致不同排名。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-06","level":"B2","title":"段落衔接与信息对应","chapter":30,"focus":"追踪回指、因果和观点变化，匹配具体人物需求","vocab":[{"head":"restoring","zh":"修复","pos":"verb","example":"At the first meeting about restoring a woodland path, volunteers disagreed about whether visitors needed more information or fewer signs.","exampleZh":"第一次修复林间小径会议上，志愿者对游客需要更多信息还是更少标牌意见不一。","collocation":"restore a path","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v1","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revealed","zh":"揭示","pos":"verb","example":"The discussion initially seemed to concern appearance, but it soon revealed different ideas about who would use the route.","exampleZh":"讨论起初似乎关于外观，很快却显出大家对路线使用者的不同设想。","collocation":"reveal different ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v2","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reassurance","zh":"安心确认","pos":"noun","example":"Regular walkers already recognised the turns, whereas families visiting for the first time wanted reassurance that they were not lost.","exampleZh":"常客认识转弯处，而首次来的家庭想确认自己没走丢。","collocation":"want reassurance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v3","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"marker","zh":"标记","pos":"noun","example":"This difference explained why an apparently unnecessary marker could still be valuable to somebody with less local knowledge.","exampleZh":"这种差异解释了为何看似多余的标记对不熟悉当地的人仍有价值。","collocation":"unnecessary marker","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v4","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preference","zh":"偏好","pos":"noun","example":"Rather than choose one group’s preference, the team walked the path with visitors who had never seen it before.","exampleZh":"团队没有只选一组人的偏好，而是和从未见过路线的游客一起实走。","collocation":"group’s preference","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v5","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concealed","zh":"遮挡","pos":"verb","example":"At a fallen tree, everyone paused because the original route continued behind branches that concealed the next turning point.","exampleZh":"在倒树处大家都停了，因为原路线在遮住下个弯的树枝后延伸。","collocation":"conceal a turning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v6","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"navigation","zh":"路线辨识","pos":"noun","example":"The team cleared those branches first, which solved a navigation problem without adding another object to the landscape.","exampleZh":"团队先清理树枝，不必再向景观增加物品就解决了认路问题。","collocation":"navigation problem","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v7","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"junction","zh":"岔口","pos":"noun","example":"Further along, however, two equally visible paths led in different directions, so a small arrow remained necessary at that junction.","exampleZh":"但更前方两条同样明显的小路通向不同方向，所以岔口仍需小箭头。","collocation":"at a junction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v8","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"destination","zh":"目的地","pos":"noun","example":"Its wording referred to the destination rather than merely saying continue, allowing walkers to connect the instruction with their map.","exampleZh":"标牌写目的地而不只写继续，让步行者能将指引与地图对应。","collocation":"refer to a destination","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v9","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surface","zh":"表面","pos":"noun","example":"After the changes, visitors made fewer wrong turns, although heavy rain later created a separate problem with the muddy surface.","exampleZh":"修改后游客走错更少，尽管后来大雨又造成泥泞路面这一独立问题。","collocation":"muddy surface","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v10","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance plan","zh":"维护计划","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The final maintenance plan therefore separated tasks concerned with understanding the route from tasks concerned with moving along it safely.","exampleZh":"最终维护计划因而把理解路线的任务与安全行走的任务分开。","collocation":"final maintenance plan","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v11","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinction","zh":"区分","pos":"noun","example":"This distinction helped volunteers agree on priorities, because solving a communication problem would not automatically repair a damaged physical path.","exampleZh":"这一区分帮助志愿者确定重点，因为解决沟通问题不会自动修好损坏的路。","collocation":"draw a distinction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v12","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"A collection without a story","text":"The volunteers restoring an old mill had gathered hundreds of objects, from tools to handwritten invoices. Their first display arranged everything according to the year it had entered the collection. Each label was accurate, but visitors struggled to understand why one object followed another. The order reflected the volunteers’ work rather than the mill’s history.\nA retired worker suggested beginning with a sack of grain and following its journey through the building. This proposal gave the objects a shared question: what happened next, and who made it happen? The team moved a measuring device beside a delivery record, allowing visitors to connect a physical task with the person responsible for checking it.\nThe new sequence was not purely chronological. One section compared two ways of doing the same job in different decades. To prevent confusion, the labels explained the change before introducing the newer tool. Words such as previously and instead now carried information that the original list of dates had left readers to reconstruct.\nThere were limits to the approach. Some objects had no clear connection with the main story, although they remained historically interesting. Rather than force them into a misleading sequence, the volunteers created a separate cabinet about unanswered questions. Visitors were invited to suggest memories or identify unfamiliar parts.\nThe revised display made the collection easier to follow without pretending that every gap had been filled. Its success depended on explaining relationships, not merely shortening labels. Objects could remain complex, provided the route through them helped visitors see which detail illustrated a process, which marked a change, and which still required further investigation."},"sourceB":{"title":"Three volunteers choose different training","text":"Three volunteers joined a community radio station, but each needed a different kind of support. Elise had years of public-speaking experience and was comfortable interviewing strangers. Her difficulty was technical: she often saved recordings in the wrong folder and could not recover an earlier edit. She preferred a written sequence she could keep beside the computer rather than another talk about confident presentation.\nJon understood the editing software well. He could remove noise and organise files efficiently, but his interviews sounded like questionnaires. He moved to the next prepared question without responding to interesting answers. For him, a practice conversation with unexpected turns was more useful than a longer equipment manual. He needed feedback on when to follow a speaker’s idea.\nPriya had good technical skills and listened closely. Her challenge was deciding what an episode was actually about. She collected many thoughtful comments but struggled to select the ones that served a clear purpose. Comparing two possible outlines helped her see how the same material could produce different programmes. General encouragement did less for her than discussing the relationship between a claim and its supporting example.\nThe trainer initially planned one introductory course for everyone. After observing these differences, she retained a shared safety briefing and offered separate workshops for the main difficulties. She did not label the volunteers as fixed types of learner. Their needs might change as they gained experience. The useful distinction concerned the task each person currently found difficult, rather than a permanent judgement about how that person could ever learn."},"listening":{"teaching":"When I revised our local history website, I discovered that most links made sense only to the people who had built it. A button marked more information led to a page about a different century, and the word here sometimes referred to a map that was no longer on screen. We asked a newcomer to explain what she expected before opening each link. Her predictions showed us where the wording created the wrong connection. We changed headings and added short introductions that named the topic directly. We did not remove every cross-reference, because the relationships between pages were useful. We simply stopped expecting readers to remember the route we had taken while researching. The revision also revealed a few gaps in our evidence. Where a date was uncertain, we said so instead of using a smooth transition to make it sound established. Clear organisation should help readers understand the strength of the information, not hide uncertainty behind a confident story.","test":"Three new members joined our repair club. Hannah could use tools safely but needed help planning the order of a repair. She benefited from laying out parts and explaining which action had to happen first. Luis understood the sequence but rushed when others were watching, so a quieter practice period helped him check measurements. Noor worked accurately and confidently until a customer asked whether repair was worth the cost. She needed practice comparing options and explaining uncertainty without promising success. We kept basic safety training for everyone, yet the later sessions were different. This was not because we believed each person had one permanent learning style. Their difficulties concerned different parts of the job. A few months later, we checked again rather than assuming that the first workshop still matched the main need. Training became more useful when we treated observation as a starting point for revision, not a label that would remain true forever. The next review will ask each member to describe a current obstacle before another workshop is scheduled."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the original display difficult to follow?","options":["The objects were arranged by the tasks they performed.","Every caption assumed the same level of historical knowledge.","Its order reflected collection history rather than the mill’s process."],"answer":"Its order reflected collection history rather than the mill’s process.","explanation":"入藏顺序不提供工作流程。","id":"b2-06:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why create an unanswered-questions cabinet?","options":["The main story could then claim to be complete.","Some items did not fit the supported main sequence.","Visitors preferred questions to all historical facts."],"answer":"Some items did not fit the supported main sequence.","explanation":"不硬凑无证据故事。","id":"b2-06:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the newcomer reveal about website links?","options":["Their wording suggested incorrect connections.","The technical link destinations had all disappeared.","Every newcomer wanted a different visual style."],"answer":"Their wording suggested incorrect connections.","explanation":"预期与实际目的地关系不清。","id":"b2-06:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why state uncertain dates explicitly?","options":["Uncertainty means that dates should never be included.","Readers should reconstruct all evidence without guidance.","Organisation should not make weak evidence sound certain."],"answer":"Organisation should not make weak evidence sound certain.","explanation":"衔接不能掩饰证据强度。","id":"b2-06:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Who needed a practical file-handling sequence?","options":["Jon.","Elise.","Priya."],"answer":"Elise.","explanation":"Elise 技术管理有困难。","id":"b2-06:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Who needed to connect selected material with a clear programme purpose?","options":["Priya.","Elise.","Jon."],"answer":"Priya.","explanation":"Priya 需从丰富素材形成主线。","id":"b2-06:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Who needed practice discussing repair value with customers?","options":["Hannah.","Luis.","Noor."],"answer":"Noor.","explanation":"Noor 困难在建议与成本协商。","id":"b2-06:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why reassess training needs later?","options":["The first workshop should be repeated without observation.","Difficulties can change with experience.","Each volunteer needed identical support before every task."],"answer":"Difficulties can change with experience.","explanation":"观察应支持更新而非定型。","id":"b2-06:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending a clearer sequence for a local history display. Include a way to show unresolved questions.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how a clear transition can show time, contrast or evidence strength.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a training proposal for the radio volunteers, distinguishing the three specific difficulties.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss why task-based training needs should be reviewed rather than treated as permanent learner labels.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["资料编辑者","首次接触主题的读者"],"goal":"解释段落或展品之间关系并保留未知部分。","twist":"流畅故事需要一处事实连接，但现有证据不支持这段连接。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-07","level":"B2","title":"说话者态度与隐含重点","chapter":33,"focus":"结合转折、评价和保留判断真实立场","vocab":[{"head":"agreed","zh":"同意","pos":"verb","example":"I agreed to help record our neighbourhood podcast because I thought speaking into a microphone would be the easy part.","exampleZh":"我答应帮忙录社区播客，因为我以为对着麦克风说话是容易的部分。","collocation":"agree to help","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v1","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"to be honest","zh":"坦白说","pos":"discourse phrase","example":"To be honest, I was far less comfortable when I heard my first recording and noticed how quickly I interrupted people.","exampleZh":"说实话，听到第一次录音并发现自己多快打断别人时，我很不自在。","collocation":"to be honest","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v2","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"promising","zh":"有潜力的","pos":"adjective","example":"My colleague said the energy was promising, which sounded encouraging until she added that the guests hardly finished a sentence.","exampleZh":"同事说活力不错，听起来很鼓舞，直到她补充嘉宾几乎说不完一句话。","collocation":"promising energy","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v3","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"worthwhile","zh":"值得的","pos":"adjective","example":"She was not asking me to sound less interested; she wanted me to leave enough space for a worthwhile answer.","exampleZh":"她不是让我听起来没兴趣，而是希望我留出足够空间得到有价值的回答。","collocation":"worthwhile answer","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v4","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"follow up","zh":"追问","pos":"noun phrase","example":"At the next interview, I prepared fewer questions and listened for points that could lead naturally to a follow up.","exampleZh":"下次访谈我准备更少问题，留意能自然引出追问的细节。","collocation":"natural follow up","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v5","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"collect her thoughts","zh":"整理思路","pos":"verb phrase","example":"There were uncomfortable pauses, although the guest later told me that those moments had helped her collect her thoughts.","exampleZh":"中间有令人不自在的停顿，不过嘉宾后来告诉我那些时刻有助于整理思路。","collocation":"collect your thoughts","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v6","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"polished","zh":"成熟精致的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would not describe the result as polished, but the conversation was certainly more revealing than our first attempt together.","exampleZh":"我不会说结果很成熟，但对话肯定比第一次尝试透露了更多内容。","collocation":"polished result","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v7","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attraction","zh":"吸引力","pos":"noun","example":"One listener suggested background music, and I can see the attraction, especially when an episode begins with a long introduction.","exampleZh":"一位听众建议背景音乐，我理解其吸引力，尤其开头介绍很长的时候。","collocation":"see the attraction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v8","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"still","zh":"不过","pos":"discourse marker","example":"Still, music underneath every answer might make careful listening harder for some people, so we are testing it only between sections.","exampleZh":"不过每个回答下面都有音乐可能让部分人难以细听，所以我们仅在段落间测试。","collocation":"still, we have concerns","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v9","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intention","zh":"意图","pos":"noun","example":"What surprised me most was how much editing depended on understanding the speaker’s intention rather than removing every hesitation automatically.","exampleZh":"最让我惊讶的是编辑多么依赖理解说话意图，而不是自动删掉每次犹豫。","collocation":"speaker’s intention","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v10","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertainty","zh":"不确定","pos":"noun","example":"A small pause can signal uncertainty, and cutting it out may make a cautious statement sound stronger than the speaker intended.","exampleZh":"小停顿能表示不确定，删掉可能让谨慎陈述听起来比原意更肯定。","collocation":"signal uncertainty","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v11","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"performance","zh":"表演","pos":"noun","example":"I am pleased we tried the project, but I now think of interviewing as a listening skill before considering it a performance.","exampleZh":"我很高兴尝试项目，但现在先把访谈视为倾听技巧，再视为表演。","collocation":"prepared performance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v12","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"An enthusiast with reservations","text":"When the town launched a weekly night market, I volunteered because I wanted the empty square to feel alive again. On the first evening, music drifted between stalls and people stayed long after the usual shops closed. I was delighted, though perhaps not for quite the reasons the organisers later suggested.\nTheir report focused on increased spending. That mattered to traders, but the most noticeable change for me was informal conversation. People who normally hurried through the square stopped to ask about food or explain a handmade object. The event created reasons to speak without requiring anyone to join a formal group.\nI was less comfortable with the claim that the market benefited every local business. A pharmacist nearby said customers had difficulty reaching the entrance because a queue crossed the pavement. A resident above the square enjoyed the atmosphere until the final hour, when amplified announcements became tiring. These were not arguments against all public activity. They were specific consequences worth addressing.\nAt the review meeting, I supported another season but proposed a clearer route past essential shops and an earlier end to amplified sound. One organiser looked disappointed that a volunteer was raising objections. I explained that enthusiasm did not require defending every detail of the first attempt.\nMy view remains positive, with conditions. A public event becomes stronger when it can hear criticism from people who value it. Calling every concern negativity may protect a cheerful headline, but it prevents the practical changes that allow a promising idea to become a dependable part of local life."},"sourceB":{"title":"A documentary director changes her ending","text":"Director Salma expected her film about a coastal town to end with a celebration of a new harbour project. The early interviews were full of hope: fishers expected safer storage, and shop owners imagined more visitors. She recorded the opening ceremony and drafted a confident closing narration about renewal.\nDuring editing, she returned to interview a shopkeeper whose comments had been especially enthusiastic. He still supported the harbour, but he explained that higher rents were making his lease uncertain. A fisher praised the storage facilities while questioning whether the new visitor parking had made unloading harder. These accounts did not reverse their earlier views. They added conditions that the ceremony had made easy to overlook.\nSalma removed the original ending. She did not replace it with a story of complete failure. Instead, the film closed with residents discussing which benefits had arrived and which costs were only beginning to appear. Some viewers found this less satisfying because they wanted a clear positive or negative verdict.\nThe director understood that desire, yet felt that certainty would misrepresent her evidence. A completed building did not mean a completed social change. She wanted viewers to recognise the difference between hope, early experience and a reliable judgement about long-term effects.\nIn a later interview, Salma described the project as promising rather than triumphant. That word was deliberate: it allowed genuine achievement without turning support into a demand that residents stop noticing new problems. Her film’s unfinished feeling reflected the situation, not an inability to choose a fashionable dramatic conclusion."},"listening":{"teaching":"I was sceptical when the museum invited comedians to lead an evening tour. It sounded as if the collection might become an excuse for jokes. In fact, the best guide used humour to make us notice details we would otherwise have ignored. I found myself looking more carefully, not less. I would happily attend again. My reservation concerns the moments when laughter made a complicated history sound too simple. One object had a difficult background that deserved more than a quick punchline. I mentioned this in the feedback, and the organiser asked for the specific example rather than dismissing the concern. That response impressed me. I am not arguing that museums should always sound solemn. I am saying that an entertaining format still has responsibilities. The result was more thoughtful than I expected, though a successful first evening should be a reason to refine the idea rather than announce that every old approach has suddenly become unnecessary. Careful criticism is one way to keep a promising format connected to the collection it is meant to illuminate.","test":"When our club replaced the annual awards dinner with a volunteer celebration, I had mixed feelings. I liked the intention, but I worried that removing awards would make individual effort invisible. The event surprised me. Short stories from different teams showed contributions that a single winner’s speech would have missed. I felt proud of people I had barely noticed before. Still, I would not describe the new format as entirely fair. The people comfortable speaking in public received more attention, while quieter work was harder to represent. Next time I would invite written accounts in advance and ask a host to read some aloud. I support continuing the celebration, provided we treat the first version as a beginning. A warm atmosphere is valuable, but it should not stop us asking whose contribution remains outside the story. My criticism comes from wanting the event to match its purpose, not from wanting the old competition restored unchanged. We will judge the revised format partly by whether people recognise work that previously received little public attention."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What mattered most to the narrator about the market?","options":["The spending increase alone.","The end of all traffic near the square.","Informal social contact."],"answer":"Informal social contact.","explanation":"重点与官方收入指标不同。","id":"b2-07:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why raise objections while supporting the event?","options":["Support requires keeping all original details.","Specific changes could make it more dependable.","The narrator expected the next season to raise less money."],"answer":"Specific changes could make it more dependable.","explanation":"积极态度可同时包含建设性批评。","id":"b2-07:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"How did the museum tour change the speaker’s view?","options":["Humour encouraged closer attention than expected.","The jokes replaced the need to examine objects.","The whole history became simpler and more certain."],"answer":"Humour encouraged closer attention than expected.","explanation":"由怀疑转为有条件肯定。","id":"b2-07:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What impressed the speaker about the organiser?","options":["A promise never to use humour again.","An assurance that every visitor had agreed.","A request for a specific example of the concern."],"answer":"A request for a specific example of the concern.","explanation":"认真核对而非泛泛回避。","id":"b2-07:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Salma remove the original ending?","options":["Later accounts showed that the ceremony had been cancelled.","Later accounts complicated the confident renewal story.","Residents no longer supported any part of the project."],"answer":"Later accounts complicated the confident renewal story.","explanation":"新证据增加条件而非全盘逆转。","id":"b2-07:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did “promising” communicate?","options":["Achievement with unresolved long-term questions.","The completed building settled every social question.","The director had found no genuine achievement."],"answer":"Achievement with unresolved long-term questions.","explanation":"精确评价词保留时间与证据限制。","id":"b2-07:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What remains unfair in the celebration?","options":["The event included written accounts from everyone already.","Only award winners were allowed to attend.","Confident public speakers gained more visibility."],"answer":"Confident public speakers gained more visibility.","explanation":"表达能力影响被看见程度。","id":"b2-07:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the speaker’s final position?","options":["Return entirely to the old awards dinner.","Continue with changes that broaden representation.","Continue without discussing who remains less visible."],"answer":"Continue with changes that broaden representation.","explanation":"支持目标但改进机制。","id":"b2-07:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article defending constructive criticism of a project you broadly support.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Express a positive but qualified opinion, distinguishing a criticism of method from rejection of the goal.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a review of Salma’s documentary explaining why an unresolved ending might be appropriate.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Recommend improvements to a volunteer celebration while explaining what is already valuable.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["项目支持者","把批评视为否定的组织者"],"goal":"用具体例子说明保留意见并争取改进。","twist":"对方认为不使用强烈赞美就会削弱公众支持。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-08","level":"B2","title":"长篇解释与主线笔记","chapter":34,"focus":"区分研究问题、方法修正、例证和最终建议","vocab":[{"head":"mapping","zh":"绘制地图","pos":"verb","example":"When we began mapping shade in the city, our first task was to decide what we actually needed to measure.","exampleZh":"开始绘制城市遮阴图时，首要任务是决定实际需要测量什么。","collocation":"map shade","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v1","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct sunlight","zh":"阳光直射","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Air temperature alone was not enough, because standing in direct sunlight can feel very different from standing beneath a tree nearby.","exampleZh":"单测气温不够，因为阳光直射下与附近树荫下的感觉可能很不同。","collocation":"in direct sunlight","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v2","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coverage","zh":"覆盖","pos":"noun","example":"We therefore recorded surface temperature, shade coverage and the times when people were most likely to use each public space.","exampleZh":"因此我们记录地表温度、遮阴覆盖以及人们最可能使用各处公共空间的时间。","collocation":"shade coverage","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v3","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"at noon","zh":"在中午","pos":"adverb phrase","example":"At first, volunteers visited every location at noon, which made the timetable easy but missed changes later in the afternoon.","exampleZh":"起初志愿者中午访问每处地点，排程简单却错过午后变化。","collocation":"visit at noon","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v4","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"记录","pos":"verb","example":"After reviewing the early results, we introduced morning and late afternoon visits to capture the movement of shadows across important routes.","exampleZh":"回顾早期结果后，我们加入早上和傍晚访问，记录重要路线上的影子移动。","collocation":"capture movement","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v5","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"protection","zh":"保护","pos":"noun","example":"One school entrance looked comfortable at lunchtime yet offered almost no protection when children were waiting to be collected after lessons.","exampleZh":"一个校门午餐时很舒适，放学后孩子等待接送时却几乎没有遮护。","collocation":"offer protection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v6","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priorities","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"That observation changed our priorities more than the hottest single reading, since it connected a physical condition with a repeated daily need.","exampleZh":"这一观察比单个最高温度更改变重点，因为它将物理条件与每天重复需求联系起来。","collocation":"change priorities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v7","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shelters","zh":"遮蔽物","pos":"noun","example":"We considered planting trees, installing shelters and changing where people waited, rather than assuming that one solution would suit every site.","exampleZh":"我们考虑种树、装棚和改变等候地点，而非假定一种方案适合所有地点。","collocation":"temporary shelters","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v8","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"canopy","zh":"树冠","pos":"noun","example":"Trees offered additional benefits, but they required suitable soil, continued watering and enough time to develop a useful canopy over the pavement.","exampleZh":"树有额外好处，但需要合适土壤、持续浇水以及时间形成覆盖路面的树冠。","collocation":"develop a canopy","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v9","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"account for","zh":"考虑","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Temporary shelters could work sooner, although their design had to account for wind, maintenance and access for people using wheelchairs.","exampleZh":"临时棚能更快生效，但设计需考虑风、维护和轮椅通行。","collocation":"account for access","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v10","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recommendations","zh":"建议","pos":"noun","example":"Our final recommendations combined immediate changes with longer term planting and made clear which proposals still needed detailed engineering advice.","exampleZh":"最终建议结合即时改变和长期种植，并明确哪些提议仍需详细工程意见。","collocation":"final recommendations","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v11","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identified","zh":"识别","pos":"verb","example":"The study did not produce a single ranking of beautiful places; it identified when practical protection mattered most to the people using them.","exampleZh":"研究没有只给美丽地点排名，而是识别实际使用者何时最需要保护。","collocation":"identify when","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v12","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"Counting wildlife without counting twice","text":"Our wildlife group began surveying bats around a reservoir with a simple ambition: find out whether the population was increasing. Volunteers carried detectors along the paths and recorded each signal. The first spreadsheet looked impressive, with hundreds of observations collected across several warm evenings.\nA visiting ecologist asked a question that changed the project. Did one signal mean one animal? A bat could circle the same feeding area repeatedly, and two volunteers might record it from opposite sides. The total therefore represented detector activity, not a direct count of individuals. This did not make the records useless, but it changed what we could reasonably conclude from them.\nWe revised the method. Routes began at fixed times, volunteers noted weather conditions, and the report distinguished observations by location rather than adding every entry into a single population figure. We also recorded periods of equipment failure instead of treating silence as proof that no bats were present.\nThe new process felt slower. Some volunteers missed the excitement of a large total, while others welcomed knowing exactly what their contribution meant. Training sessions used a short example in which the same bat passed three detectors. This made the measurement problem easier to understand than a technical warning alone.\nAt the end of the season, we could describe patterns of activity under stated conditions. We could not claim a precise population change from our first year. The ecologist helped us identify which future comparisons would be useful and which would require a different study. Learning to ask a narrower, answerable question became the project’s most important result, even though it produced a less dramatic number for the annual newsletter."},"sourceB":{"title":"How a sound archive chooses what to keep","text":"A city archive invited residents to record sounds that represented everyday life. Contributions ranged from market conversations to a lift mechanism in an old factory. The organisers first planned to store every submitted file, believing that selection would impose their own preferences on the city’s memory.\nPractical and ethical questions soon complicated that intention. Some files contained identifiable private conversations whose speakers had not agreed to public use. Others lacked a date or location, making them difficult to interpret. Several recordings repeated the same event, while quiet but distinctive places were absent because fewer volunteers visited them.\nThe archivist introduced a review process. Contributors explained where and why a recording had been made. Staff checked permissions, added context and marked uncertain details. A file could remain in a restricted collection while its public use was discussed. This was different from deleting it, a distinction the team explained carefully to contributors who feared that their work had been rejected.\nThe archive also commissioned recordings from underrepresented areas. It did not describe the final collection as a complete sound map of the city. The selection still reflected who had time, equipment and confidence to contribute. Its catalogue made these limitations visible and invited later additions.\nThe project’s purpose gradually changed from collecting the largest possible quantity to preserving material that future listeners could understand and use responsibly. Technical quality mattered, but so did context and consent. A perfectly clear recording without those conditions might be less valuable for public research than a noisier one whose origin and permitted use were well documented."},"listening":{"teaching":"I study the way people use public benches. At first, our students counted occupied seats every half hour. That gave us a useful pattern, but it did not explain why empty benches remained unused. We then noted shade, nearby traffic and whether people could sit with a companion. One bench appeared unpopular until we visited at a different time: older residents used it after the morning bus arrived. Another was often empty because a hedge blocked the view of an approaching vehicle. These examples changed the final recommendations. We did not simply advise adding more seats where the first count was low. We proposed changes linked to specific conditions and suggested another observation period afterwards. There are limits to this method. Watching can show where people sit, but not every reason behind their choice. Short voluntary conversations helped, provided we did not pressure people to explain private circumstances. Good observation combines a consistent method with the willingness to notice what the original checklist leaves out.","test":"Our coastal monitoring project measures beach width after storms. Volunteers used to report the distance from the water to the first building, but the tide made those figures difficult to compare. We introduced a fixed reference point and recorded the time and tidal condition beside each measurement. Even then, a smaller distance did not automatically mean permanent loss of sand. Material could move along the beach and return later. The report now separates short-term changes from evidence collected across several seasons. We also check the equipment and repeat unusual readings before treating them as a new trend. This takes patience, especially when residents want an immediate answer about a worrying photograph. We try to explain what the current record supports and which questions remain open. The project is useful because it creates consistent observations over time, not because every volunteer visit can settle a complex question about the coast’s future. We make that distinction at public meetings so residents understand why a careful answer may remain provisional."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the first bat total not a population count?","options":["Each detector could identify a bat’s permanent identity.","The total excluded every repeated feeding area.","One animal could produce several records."],"answer":"One animal could produce several records.","explanation":"观测事件与个体数不是同一单位。","id":"b2-08:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why record equipment failure?","options":["It allows all silent periods to count as no activity.","Silence during failure should not be interpreted as absence.","It removes the need to record fixed start times."],"answer":"Silence during failure should not be interpreted as absence.","explanation":"无数据与观察到无活动需区分。","id":"b2-08:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did a different observation time reveal?","options":["Some bench use depended on a bus arrival.","Every bench should be judged only at noon.","The apparently unused bench was physically damaged."],"answer":"Some bench use depended on a bus arrival.","explanation":"具体时间可改变占用图景。","id":"b2-08:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why add voluntary conversations?","options":["They can replace the observation record completely.","They guarantee that everyone explains the same motive.","Observation alone cannot reveal every reason."],"answer":"Observation alone cannot reveal every reason.","explanation":"行为和动机信息互补且需自愿。","id":"b2-08:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why could a file be restricted?","options":["The file had been assessed as historically worthless.","Public-use permission or context needed checking.","Every contributor had asked for immediate deletion."],"answer":"Public-use permission or context needed checking.","explanation":"访问限制与删除不是一回事。","id":"b2-08:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why avoid calling the archive complete?","options":["Participation and coverage remained uneven.","Every recording was made with the same equipment.","Staff had deliberately excluded all ordinary sounds."],"answer":"Participation and coverage remained uneven.","explanation":"来源可达性与志愿者偏差限制完整性。","id":"b2-08:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why introduce a fixed coastal reference point?","options":["To make building distance the only remaining variable.","To eliminate the need to note time.","Tidal variation made earlier distances hard to compare."],"answer":"Tidal variation made earlier distances hard to compare.","explanation":"统一测量条件提高比较意义。","id":"b2-08:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can a single smaller beach-width reading establish?","options":["A permanent loss across the entire coastline.","A short-term observation requiring context.","A precise forecast for the following season."],"answer":"A short-term observation requiring context.","explanation":"单次变化不能直接外推长期趋势。","id":"b2-08:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a report explaining how to improve a volunteer wildlife survey and communicate its limits.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Summarise a research process using the headings question, method, correction and conclusion.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for a sound archive that balances preservation, context and permission.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain to a worried resident why coastal measurements need consistent conditions and repeated evidence.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["研究志愿者","急需明确答案的公众"],"goal":"解释观测含义和限制，提出下次采集的改进。","twist":"读数很醒目，但测量条件不同，媒体希望立即称为趋势。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-09","level":"B2","title":"视觉比较与合理推测","chapter":35,"focus":"把可见细节与问题联系，避免把照片解释当作事实","vocab":[{"head":"imagine","zh":"想象","pos":"verb","example":"Imagine one photograph showing teenagers beside a river with measuring equipment and another showing a family following signs through a nature reserve.","exampleZh":"想象一张照片是青少年在河边拿测量设备，另一张是家庭沿标牌参观自然保护区。","collocation":"imagine a photograph","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v1","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appear to","zh":"似乎","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Both groups appear to be learning outdoors, although the purpose of their activities and the support available may be rather different.","exampleZh":"两组似乎都在户外学习，但活动目的及可用支持可能很不同。","collocation":"appear to be learning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v2","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"might be","zh":"可能正在","pos":"modal verb phrase","example":"The teenagers might be carrying out a school investigation, since one person is recording figures while another checks an instrument near the water.","exampleZh":"青少年可能在做学校调查，因为一个人在记数字，另一个在水边检查仪器。","collocation":"might be investigating","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v3","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"by contrast","zh":"相比之下","pos":"discourse phrase","example":"By contrast, the family seems to be choosing its own pace, with a child pointing towards an illustrated notice beside the path.","exampleZh":"相比之下，家庭似乎自己决定速度，一个孩子正指着路边图示牌。","collocation":"by contrast","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v4","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cannot tell","zh":"无法判断","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I cannot tell whether either group is enjoying the experience, but both activities seem to encourage attention.","exampleZh":"我不能判断两组是否享受经历，但两项活动似乎都鼓励专注。","collocation":"cannot tell whether","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v5","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"abstract","zh":"抽象的","pos":"adjective","example":"Working with instruments could help the teenagers understand ideas that would remain abstract if they only read about them in a classroom.","exampleZh":"使用仪器可帮助青少年理解那些只在教室读到时可能仍抽象的概念。","collocation":"abstract ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v6","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"freedom","zh":"自由","pos":"noun","example":"However, they may have less freedom to follow an unexpected interest because their group probably needs to complete a planned task together.","exampleZh":"但他们可能较少自由追随意外兴趣，因为小组可能要共同完成计划任务。","collocation":"freedom to choose","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v7","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interpret","zh":"理解解释","pos":"verb","example":"The family’s visit is likely to be more flexible, although a clear explanation would still help them interpret unfamiliar plants or animal behaviour.","exampleZh":"家庭参观很可能更灵活，但清晰解释仍有助理解陌生植物或动物行为。","collocation":"interpret behaviour","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v8","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"connects","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"Rather than describing every visible object, a useful comparison connects a few details with the question of how each group might learn.","exampleZh":"有效比较不是描述每个可见物品，而是把少量细节与每组如何学习这一问题联系。","collocation":"connect details with questions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v9","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establish","zh":"确立","pos":"verb","example":"Words such as probably and perhaps make clear that these are reasonable possibilities rather than facts that the photographs can fully establish.","exampleZh":"probably 和 perhaps 等词表明这些是合理可能性，而非照片能充分确定的事实。","collocation":"establish a fact","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v10","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"memorable","zh":"难忘的","pos":"adjective","example":"If asked which activity would be more memorable, I would choose the investigation because making a measurement could create a strong personal connection.","exampleZh":"若问哪项更难忘，我会选调查，因为亲自测量可能建立强烈个人联系。","collocation":"memorable activity","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v11","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"可辩护的","pos":"adjective","example":"Someone who prefers exploring freely might choose the family visit, showing that several conclusions can be defensible.","exampleZh":"偏爱自由探索的人可能选家庭参观，说明多种结论都能站得住脚。","collocation":"defensible conclusion","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v12","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"What the workshop photograph leaves out","text":"The company newsletter asked me to choose between two photographs of our apprenticeship programme. The first showed a row of learners facing a large demonstration screen. The second showed two people examining a small machine together. At a glance, the second image seemed to tell a more active story.\nI could reasonably observe that the pair were looking at the same object and that one person was pointing to a component. I could not know whether they were solving a problem, following a routine check or posing after the task had finished. The photograph offered clues, not a complete explanation of their relationship or confidence.\nThe first image also deserved more care than my initial reaction allowed. A group looking at a screen might be passive, but it might equally be comparing a live demonstration with work completed earlier. The frame excluded the learners’ desks and any discussion taking place outside that moment. Describing the whole session as boring would turn an impression into an unsupported judgement.\nWe chose the paired image because the newsletter question concerned opportunities for cooperation. The caption named the actual task and explained that both people were apprentices. This prevented the common assumption that the older-looking person must be the instructor.\nThe final comparison did not require neutrality about everything. I could explain why one image suited this purpose better while acknowledging what neither image established. Careful visual description involves selecting relevant details, using tentative language where necessary, and resisting the temptation to fill every gap with the most familiar story. A convincing explanation remains stronger when its confidence matches the evidence."},"sourceB":{"title":"Two photographs of a public square","text":"A planning exhibition displayed two photographs of the same square. One, taken on a bright weekend, showed café tables and children playing. The other, taken on a wet weekday morning, showed people walking quickly past closed stalls. A caption invited visitors to decide whether the recent renovation had made the square successful.\nSeveral residents questioned the comparison. The images did not isolate the effect of the renovation: weather, day and time all differed. The cheerful photograph might illustrate one valuable use of the space, but it could not prove that everyone used it that way. The quieter image did not demonstrate failure either. Some public spaces need to support movement as well as social activity.\nThe planning team revised the display. Each image received a date, time and short explanation. A wider set of observations showed how the square functioned for deliveries, commuting, resting and events. Residents could comment on a particular use rather than choose one overall emotional response to two selected moments.\nOne shopkeeper still preferred the older layout because unloading had become awkward. A wheelchair user welcomed the smoother surface but identified a narrow gap between planters. These views complicated the image of complete success without cancelling genuine improvements.\nThe final exhibit asked what worked, for whom and at which times. It used photographs as evidence within a broader account, not as a visual vote that could settle the whole question. The square remained the same physical place, but the quality of the discussion improved when the presentation stopped forcing every experience into either celebration or disappointment."},"listening":{"teaching":"When comparing photographs in a speaking task, I advise learners to begin with the question, not an inventory. If the question concerns cooperation, a few details about shared attention may matter more than the colour of every object. They should also distinguish observation from inference. Saying that two people are looking at one map is different from saying they are lost. The second claim may be plausible, but it needs cautious wording. A good comparison can explain a likely similarity and a meaningful difference without inventing biographies. Learners sometimes fear that uncertainty sounds weak. In fact, phrases such as they may be or it seems likely can show accurate control when the image does not establish a fact. The aim is not to avoid commitment altogether. It is to connect a conclusion with visible evidence and explain why the conclusion is relevant to the question rather than simply deliver a memorised description of any pair of pictures. A partner can then challenge the inference, giving the speaker an opportunity to clarify the link rather than repeat a claim.","test":"Our charity considered using a photograph of a crowded food distribution hall on its annual report. It showed the scale of the operation, but several people were recognisable and had not agreed to appear in publicity. We chose a different image showing prepared boxes and volunteers’ hands instead. Some colleagues thought this would be less emotional. Perhaps it was, but the purpose was to explain the work without turning recipients into illustrations of need. The caption provided numbers and context that the image alone could not establish. We also avoided claiming that every box contained the same items, since supplies varied. The decision reminded us that selecting a photograph is not only a design task. It involves purpose, consent and the likely interpretation of people who were not there. A powerful image may attract attention, yet accuracy and dignity still matter when deciding whether that attention has been gained responsibly. We explain this policy to photographers before events, so responsible choices begin before a difficult image reaches the editing desk."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the paired image selected?","options":["It proved that the pair worked without instruction.","It showed the newest equipment most clearly.","It suited a question about cooperation."],"answer":"It suited a question about cooperation.","explanation":"按沟通目的选择而非绝对好坏。","id":"b2-09:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why identify both people as apprentices?","options":["The equipment required a formal age limit.","Their apparent ages might invite a false role assumption.","The older person had actually written the newsletter."],"answer":"Their apparent ages might invite a false role assumption.","explanation":"图像线索不等于身份事实。","id":"b2-09:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which statement is an inference?","options":["The people are lost.","The people are holding a map.","The map lies between the two people."],"answer":"The people are lost.","explanation":"lost 需要解释而非直接可见。","id":"b2-09:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why can cautious language show control?","options":["It allows any interpretation to count as established fact.","It avoids the need to connect details with the question.","It matches confidence to limited visual evidence."],"answer":"It matches confidence to limited visual evidence.","explanation":"推测语言准确校准证据。","id":"b2-09:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why were the square photographs an unfair test?","options":["Both images showed the renovation at the same time.","They differed in weather, time and day.","The images included only private gardens."],"answer":"They differed in weather, time and day.","explanation":"比较条件混杂。","id":"b2-09:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the revised exhibit encourage?","options":["Specific judgements about different uses and users.","One overall vote based solely on visual cheerfulness.","A conclusion that every improvement had failed."],"answer":"Specific judgements about different uses and users.","explanation":"多维度而非全胜全败。","id":"b2-09:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was the crowded charity image rejected?","options":["The boxes were less numerous than in an earlier year.","Volunteers had asked for a different camera angle.","Recognisable recipients had not consented to publicity."],"answer":"Recognisable recipients had not consented to publicity.","explanation":"涉及隐私与尊严。","id":"b2-09:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the caption avoid identical-box claims?","options":["The report had not counted every volunteer.","Supplies varied.","Prepared boxes could not be shown in publicity."],"answer":"Supplies varied.","explanation":"准确表述不能超过事实。","id":"b2-09:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an article explaining how to use photographs as evidence without overinterpreting them.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare two imagined learning scenes, stating what is visible and what you can only infer.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending how the square exhibition should support fair discussion of the renovation.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss the charity’s image choice, balancing communicative effect, accuracy and consent.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["图片编辑者","希望使用更煽情画面的同事"],"goal":"选择合目的且有授权的图像并写准确说明。","twist":"最有冲击力的照片让受助者可辨认，但未取得公开许可。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-10","level":"B2","title":"协商、让步与共同方案","chapter":36,"focus":"回应同伴理由，确定共同标准，并处理新增条件","vocab":[{"head":"committee","zh":"委员会","pos":"noun","example":"Our student committee had to choose a welcome event for new arrivals, and the first suggestions included a concert and a sports afternoon.","exampleZh":"学生委员会要选迎新活动，最初建议包括音乐会和体育下午活动。","collocation":"student committee","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v1","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agreed","zh":"达成一致","pos":"verb","example":"Instead of voting immediately, we agreed to discuss how each option would help people who had not yet made friends.","exampleZh":"我们没有马上投票，而是先讨论每个选择如何帮助还没交到朋友的人。","collocation":"agree to discuss","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v2","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"wondered","zh":"提出疑问","pos":"verb","example":"I suggested a concert because music could give people a shared experience, but my partner wondered whether anyone would actually talk during it.","exampleZh":"我提议音乐会，因为音乐能创造共同经历，但同伴怀疑演出中是否有人真的会交谈。","collocation":"wonder whether","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v3","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair point","zh":"合理观点","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That was a fair point, so I asked whether a short performance followed by small group activities might solve the problem.","exampleZh":"这个观点合理，所以我问短表演后接小组活动是否能解决问题。","collocation":"a fair point","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v4","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concerned","zh":"担心的","pos":"adjective","example":"She thought it could, although she was concerned that the extra equipment would leave too little money for refreshments and accessible transport.","exampleZh":"她觉得可以，但担心额外设备会使茶点和无障碍交通预算不足。","collocation":"concerned that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v5","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinguishing","zh":"区分","pos":"verb","example":"We then considered the sports afternoon, distinguishing competitive matches from simple activities that did not require previous skill or expensive clothing.","exampleZh":"随后我们考虑体育下午活动，区分竞技比赛和不需既有技能或昂贵服装的简单活动。","collocation":"distinguish matches from activities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v6","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"judged","zh":"被评判","pos":"verb","example":"Neither of us wanted less confident students to feel watched or judged while they were still finding their place in the community.","exampleZh":"我们都不希望不自信的学生在刚融入集体时觉得被围观或评判。","collocation":"feel judged","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v7","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cooperation","zh":"合作","pos":"noun","example":"My partner proposed an indoor team challenge with practical puzzles, which seemed to combine cooperation with opportunities for informal conversation afterwards.","exampleZh":"同伴提议室内团队实用解谜挑战，似乎兼顾合作和之后非正式聊天机会。","collocation":"encourage cooperation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v8","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"on condition","zh":"以某条件为前提","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"I supported the idea on condition that teams were mixed carefully and instructions were available in clear language for everyone taking part.","exampleZh":"我支持这个想法，条件是仔细混合组队，且为所有参加者提供清楚说明。","collocation":"on condition that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v9","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criteria","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"Before making our decision, we checked it against the same criteria we had used for the concert rather than favouring our newest suggestion automatically.","exampleZh":"决定前我们用评价音乐会的相同标准检查，而不是自动偏爱最新建议。","collocation":"shared criteria","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v10","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposal","zh":"提案","pos":"noun","example":"The final proposal was neither person’s original choice, but both could explain why it addressed our main concern.","exampleZh":"最终提案不是任何人的最初选择，但双方都能解释为何回应了核心问题。","collocation":"final proposal","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v11","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contributions","zh":"贡献","pos":"noun","example":"Productive discussion meant using each other’s contributions to build a decision, rather than simply taking turns to speak.","exampleZh":"有效讨论意味着利用彼此贡献形成决定，而不只是轮流说话。","collocation":"use each other’s contributions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v12","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"A room for more than one purpose","text":"Our housing association had funding to improve one unused room. Residents suggested a homework club, an exercise space and a place for older neighbours to meet. Each group believed its proposal answered the most urgent need. The first discussion produced three enthusiastic speeches but no shared basis for choosing.\nThe chair asked everyone to describe what their activity required rather than defend a room name. The homework group needed tables, dependable quiet and storage for materials. The exercise group needed an open floor for a few scheduled hours. Older residents wanted comfortable seating and a space that did not feel like a classroom. These requirements overlapped less than the slogans had suggested.\nWe explored a timetable with movable furniture and locked storage. I supported it, although another resident pointed out that repeatedly moving heavy tables would place work on the same few volunteers. That objection improved the plan: we investigated lighter furniture and checked who would be responsible for rearranging it. Agreement had to include the work behind the attractive shared schedule.\nA further condition emerged when a parent asked about safeguarding during the homework club. We could not solve this simply by adding another box to the timetable. The association needed trained supervision and clear access arrangements before that activity began.\nThe final recommendation funded flexible furniture and a staged opening rather than promising every activity immediately. Nobody obtained their entire original proposal. Nevertheless, each group could explain which need had been met and which depended on further preparation. The decision was collaborative because participants used objections to refine the plan, not because everybody politely avoided disagreeing. A shared conclusion required visible compromises and named responsibilities, as well as a friendly tone."},"sourceB":{"title":"Choosing a school exchange project","text":"Two schools planned an international exchange but could afford only one shared project. Students proposed a sports tournament, a local history film and an environmental survey. The teachers initially expected the discussion to end in a straightforward vote. Instead, student representatives asked whether the options offered equal opportunities to participate.\nThe sports proposal would create quick contact, but some students could not join competitive activities comfortably. The film could include interviewing and editing, although access to equipment was unequal. The survey offered practical work, yet it depended on understanding technical instructions in a second language. None of the ideas was free from difficulty.\nThe group agreed on three criteria: meaningful cooperation, accessible roles and a product that could be completed within the visit. They then considered a combined project documenting changes along a local river. Students could photograph, interview, map or summarise findings, while mixed-language teams checked one another’s explanations. A short optional game would provide informal contact without becoming the main activity.\nBefore approving the plan, one representative asked whether the strongest language speakers would still dominate. The group added preparation time and rotating roles. Teachers would check understanding rather than judge contribution only by how much each student spoke in public.\nThe final plan borrowed elements from two proposals, but it was not a collection of everything anyone wanted. The shared criteria ruled out tasks that could not be completed safely or fairly. The discussion succeeded because participants made their reasoning available to one another and accepted revisions when a practical objection exposed a weakness in their preferred option."},"listening":{"teaching":"During a collaborative discussion, learners sometimes take turns giving complete speeches that never connect. I ask them to listen for one point they can use. They might agree with a reason, ask about an assumption or propose a condition that makes an option more workable. This does not mean interrupting every sentence. A brief summary can show understanding before disagreement: so your main concern is the cost of transport. The next contribution then addresses that concern rather than restarting the whole discussion. Reaching a decision also requires a moment of comparison. Which criteria matter most, and does the preferred option meet them better than the alternatives? A friendly conversation can still be incomplete if nobody explains why the final choice follows. Equally, disagreement is not failure when it helps reveal a hidden consequence. We assess whether partners build a reasoned response together, including room for quieter contributions, not simply whether each person sounds confident in isolation. A clear conclusion should therefore name both the preferred option and the condition that makes agreement possible.","test":"Our community garden had money for either a greenhouse or accessible raised beds. I initially supported the greenhouse because it would extend the growing season. During the meeting, another member explained that several volunteers could no longer bend comfortably to work at ground level. That changed the question for me. We compared the projects against participation, maintenance and cost. A small greenhouse still looked useful, but it would not solve the access problem and would need daily attention during hot weather. We recommended the raised beds first and kept a limited greenhouse plan for a later application. I did not feel that changing my view meant losing the argument. The discussion had revealed a need I had not weighed properly. We also checked the path width and agreed who would maintain it. An accessible bed would offer little benefit if people could not reach it or if the route became blocked by stored equipment. We intend to invite the affected volunteers to test the route before any permanent installation is accepted."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why move from room names to requirements?","options":["The requirements showed that no sharing was possible.","Room names were needed before any funding discussion.","Requirements revealed where uses could be combined."],"answer":"Requirements revealed where uses could be combined.","explanation":"具体条件比口号更易协商。","id":"b2-10:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the heavy-table objection reveal?","options":["The plan required fewer tables than the homework group wanted.","The shared plan hid an unequal work burden.","Every resident had already agreed to move furniture daily."],"answer":"The shared plan hid an unequal work burden.","explanation":"运行成本包括劳动分配。","id":"b2-10:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What distinguishes connected discussion from separate speeches?","options":["Using a partner’s point to develop the next contribution.","Giving longer uninterrupted individual speeches.","Repeating each original preference in the same order."],"answer":"Using a partner’s point to develop the next contribution.","explanation":"回应推动共同推理。","id":"b2-10:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why compare criteria before concluding?","options":["To preserve the first speaker’s preference automatically.","To avoid naming any condition for agreement.","To explain why one option is preferred."],"answer":"To explain why one option is preferred.","explanation":"结论需与共同标准相连。","id":"b2-10:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why add rotating roles to the exchange project?","options":["Equipment was available only to the strongest speakers.","Strong language speakers might otherwise dominate.","The language teachers wanted a competitive ranking."],"answer":"Strong language speakers might otherwise dominate.","explanation":"语言优势不应等同贡献垄断。","id":"b2-10:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the final exchange plan not just everything combined?","options":["Shared criteria excluded impractical tasks.","It included all activities originally proposed.","It removed every optional social activity."],"answer":"Shared criteria excluded impractical tasks.","explanation":"整合有选择边界。","id":"b2-10:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the garden speaker change position?","options":["The grant had changed to cover only greenhouse repairs.","The greenhouse no longer extended the season.","An overlooked participation need became clear."],"answer":"An overlooked participation need became clear.","explanation":"新理由使优先次序改变。","id":"b2-10:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why check path width?","options":["Path width determines how much watering plants require.","Accessible beds are useful only if people can reach them.","The beds’ height alone guarantees access for everyone."],"answer":"Accessible beds are useful only if people can reach them.","explanation":"不能只在局部贴无障碍标签。","id":"b2-10:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for a shared community room, including operating responsibilities and conditions for opening.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Compare several options with a partner and reach a qualified decision using agreed criteria.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a report recommending the exchange project and explaining how its roles could remain fair.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Respond to the garden funding decision, explaining a change of mind and a practical access condition.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["偏好方案甲的人","偏好方案乙的人"],"goal":"共同确定标准，回应理由并留下明确责任。","twist":"新增信息显示原偏好可能排除一组人，且运行劳动由少数人承担。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-11","level":"B2","title":"写作目的、语域与论证","chapter":38,"focus":"围绕读者需要组织信息，以具体证据支持评价和请求","vocab":[{"head":"compulsory","zh":"强制的","pos":"adjective","example":"A school considering compulsory community projects should begin by asking what students are expected to learn rather than counting volunteer hours.","exampleZh":"学校考虑强制社区项目时，应先问预期学习成果而非只数志愿时数。","collocation":"compulsory projects","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v1","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supporters","zh":"支持者","pos":"noun","example":"Supporters argue that working with people outside school helps young learners understand needs that textbooks may describe only in general terms.","exampleZh":"支持者认为校外合作帮助年轻学习者理解课本可能只泛泛描述的需求。","collocation":"supporters argue","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v2","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accessible","zh":"便于理解的","pos":"adjective","example":"For example, helping to design a clear visitor map could show why accessible communication matters to somebody unfamiliar with an area.","exampleZh":"例如帮助设计清晰导览图，可说明方便理解的沟通为何对不熟悉地区的人重要。","collocation":"accessible communication","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v3","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"automatic","zh":"自动发生的","pos":"adjective","example":"This benefit is not automatic, however, because repeating a simple task without explanation may teach little about the community or its difficulties.","exampleZh":"然而该收益不是自动发生，因为无解释地重复简单任务可能几乎学不到社区及其困难。","collocation":"automatic benefit","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v4","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reflect","zh":"反思","pos":"verb","example":"Students need preparation, appropriate responsibility and time to reflect on what the experience has changed in their understanding of other people.","exampleZh":"学生需要准备、合适责任及时间反思经历如何改变其对他人的理解。","collocation":"reflect on learning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v5","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"critics","zh":"批评者","pos":"noun","example":"Critics reasonably point out that compulsory service can create pressure for students who already care for relatives or work after lessons.","exampleZh":"批评者合理指出，强制服务会让已照顾亲属或课后工作的人承压。","collocation":"critics point out","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v6","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flexible","zh":"灵活的","pos":"adjective","example":"A fair programme would therefore offer flexible schedules and several forms of participation rather than assume that everyone has the same free time.","exampleZh":"因此公平项目应提供灵活时间和多种参与方式，而非假定每个人空闲相同。","collocation":"flexible schedules","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v7","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unprepared","zh":"未准备好的","pos":"adjective","example":"It should also avoid treating community organisations as places that must accept unprepared students simply because the school wants a useful activity.","exampleZh":"还应避免把社区机构当成仅因学校需要活动就必须接受未准备学生的场所。","collocation":"unprepared students","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v8","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"objection","zh":"反对意见","pos":"noun","example":"The strongest argument for these projects concerns meaningful cooperation, while the strongest objection concerns unfair demands on participants and their hosts.","exampleZh":"支持项目的最强理由关乎有意义合作，最强反对关乎对参与者及接待方不公平要求。","collocation":"strongest objection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v9","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"develops","zh":"展开","pos":"verb","example":"An effective essay develops both points with explanation instead of presenting a long list of benefits followed by an unrelated personal story.","exampleZh":"有效文章解释发展双方观点，而不是列长串好处后接无关个人故事。","collocation":"develop a point","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v10","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledge","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"Its conclusion should answer the original question and acknowledge the conditions that determine whether the proposal is likely to work in practice.","exampleZh":"结论应回答原问题并承认决定提议实际是否可行的条件。","collocation":"acknowledge conditions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v11","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supervised","zh":"受监督的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would support a well supervised programme with genuine choice, but not a fixed requirement that rewards attendance while ignoring learning and fairness.","exampleZh":"我支持有监督和真正选择的项目，不支持只奖励出席而忽视学习公平的固定要求。","collocation":"well supervised programme","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v12","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"The letter that changed the meeting","text":"When residents opposed a proposed delivery yard, their first letter to the council described the plan as outrageous and demanded that it be abandoned. The language conveyed genuine anger, but the council’s reply addressed only the formal planning process. It did not answer the practical concerns behind the complaint.\nAt the next residents’ meeting, an editor offered to help revise the submission. She asked which effects could be described precisely. Residents identified early-morning reversing alarms, the location of a pedestrian crossing and uncertainty about the number of daily vehicles. Some claims came from the planning documents; others were assumptions that required confirmation. The revised letter distinguished them.\nIts purpose was no longer simply to express opposition. It requested specific information and proposed conditions if the project proceeded: limited delivery hours, a protected crossing and monitoring after opening. Residents who still preferred complete refusal could explain that view, but the shared submission did not pretend every neighbour held exactly the same position.\nThe second response was more useful. Officers answered two questions, corrected an inaccurate vehicle estimate and agreed to discuss the crossing. This did not guarantee a decision in the residents’ favour. It did create a record of issues that could be checked against the final plan.\nThe editor had not removed emotion because it was illegitimate. She had organised the material so that another person could identify a claim, understand its basis and know what action was being requested. Effective formal writing can be firm without exaggeration. Its strength comes from making relevant concerns difficult to misunderstand, rather than trying to win attention through the strongest available adjective."},"sourceB":{"title":"Writing a review for a real reader","text":"A college magazine asked students to review a new study café. The first submissions often began with a long account of meeting friends and travelling there. Several ended with a score but gave readers little basis for deciding whether the café suited their own needs. The editor returned the drafts with a question: who is this review helping, and what choice do they need to make?\nOne writer, Ben, revised his piece around three situations. For a quick coffee between lectures, the café was convenient and reasonably priced. For a quiet hour with a difficult text, the music and frequent announcements were distracting. For group planning, the large tables were useful, provided customers understood that sockets were limited.\nBen kept one personal incident because it illustrated the service clearly: a staff member helped his group move away from a noisy doorway. He removed a paragraph about a private joke because it added length without helping the reader. His recommendation became specific rather than universally positive or negative.\nThe editor also asked him to separate observation from a promise. Visiting on one Tuesday could not establish that the café was always quiet in the morning. Ben named the time of his visit and used cautious wording about patterns he had not checked.\nThe final review was not less personal. It contained a recognisable judgement, but that judgement was supported and directed towards the magazine’s audience. Its purpose was to help students choose a place for a particular activity, not simply demonstrate that the reviewer had experienced an enjoyable afternoon."},"listening":{"teaching":"When students write a report, they often think formality means using longer words. I ask them to consider a different test: can the reader find the findings and the recommendation without guessing? Headings can help, but they do not replace a clear relationship between evidence and action. If a report says visitors were confused, it should explain what they misunderstood and why the proposed change addresses that difficulty. I also encourage writers to distinguish a finding from an assumption. A short trial may justify further testing rather than an immediate permanent policy. This is not weak writing; it is an accurate match between evidence and recommendation. In a persuasive text, you can still take a position. You simply need to show why it follows and acknowledge a relevant limitation. The best revision often removes impressive but unnecessary wording so the actual purpose becomes easier to see. Formality should support communication rather than create distance for its own sake. Readers should be able to distinguish a confident recommendation from a certainty that the evidence cannot support.","test":"I wrote to an equipment supplier after a workshop delivery arrived incomplete. My first draft described the entire service as unacceptable. I revised it before sending. The final message listed the three missing items, attached the delivery record and explained that the event began on Friday morning. I requested confirmation of either delivery by Thursday or a refund for those items so we could obtain them elsewhere. I also acknowledged that the items already supplied were correct. The reply offered a courier delivery and a named contact for checking progress. This did not mean my wording magically guaranteed a good result. The supplier still needed stock and transport. What the message did was make the problem and acceptable next steps clear. If I had received no reply, the dated record would also have helped me explain the matter later without reconstructing an angry telephone conversation from memory. A colleague checked the final message for accuracy, ensuring that the request did not accidentally include items already supplied correctly."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did the revised letter distinguish claims?","options":["By treating all residents’ fears as equal established facts.","By removing every concern not already answered.","By separating documented effects from assumptions needing confirmation."],"answer":"By separating documented effects from assumptions needing confirmation.","explanation":"证据层次明确才可回应。","id":"b2-11:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What was the revised purpose?","options":["To guarantee that every neighbour opposed the yard.","Request information and propose checkable conditions.","To replace the planning process with a public vote."],"answer":"Request information and propose checkable conditions.","explanation":"从情绪表达转向具体交际目标。","id":"b2-11:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the key test for a report?","options":["Whether findings and recommendations are easy to locate and connect.","Whether vocabulary creates the greatest possible distance.","Whether headings replace the need for evidence."],"answer":"Whether findings and recommendations are easy to locate and connect.","explanation":"形式服务于理解与行动。","id":"b2-11:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can a short trial reasonably support?","options":["A final policy with no further observation.","A conclusion that no recommendation is possible.","A recommendation for further testing under stated limits."],"answer":"A recommendation for further testing under stated limits.","explanation":"建议强度需匹配证据。","id":"b2-11:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did Ben remove the private joke?","options":["Private incidents are forbidden in every review.","It did not help the reader choose.","The editor wanted a longer travel account."],"answer":"It did not help the reader choose.","explanation":"相关性而非完全去个人化。","id":"b2-11:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why name the visit time?","options":["One visit could not prove a permanent pattern.","The café’s patterns had been measured on every weekday.","Readers needed a full chronological diary of the visit."],"answer":"One visit could not prove a permanent pattern.","explanation":"避免把单次观察变普遍承诺。","id":"b2-11:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What deadline shaped the supplier request?","options":["The supplier’s next quarterly stock review.","The courier’s preferred collection day.","The workshop began on Friday morning."],"answer":"The workshop began on Friday morning.","explanation":"具体使用时间决定可接受补救。","id":"b2-11:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the dated message provide beyond immediate negotiation?","options":["A guarantee that the supplier had transport available.","A clear record for later explanation.","Proof that every part of the original service failed."],"answer":"A clear record for later explanation.","explanation":"书面记录不等于结果保证。","id":"b2-11:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a formal letter asking about a proposed facility. Distinguish confirmed facts, concerns and requested actions.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Explain how to revise a strong complaint without making it vague or passive.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a review of a study venue for students, identifying whom it suits and one limit to your observations.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Negotiate a missing delivery by stating evidence, a useful deadline and two acceptable remedies.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["写作者","需要采取行动的收件人"],"goal":"使问题、依据和请求清楚可回应。","twist":"强烈情绪是真实的，但部分说法尚未证实且存在多个可接受补救。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"b2-12","level":"B2","title":"多来源综合与独立复核","chapter":40,"focus":"在新任务中综合证据、协商方案并形成可核查的能力档案","vocab":[{"head":"residents","zh":"居民","pos":"noun","example":"Our project group received a residents’ survey and a short interview with a transport planner about noise on a busy shopping street.","exampleZh":"项目组收到居民调查及交通规划师关于繁忙购物街噪声的简短访谈。","collocation":"residents’ survey","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v1","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depended on","zh":"依赖","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The survey showed that evening deliveries disturbed several households, while the interview explained why shops depended on flexible access for vehicles.","exampleZh":"调查显示晚间配送打扰一些家庭，访谈则解释商店为何依赖灵活车辆通行。","collocation":"depend on access","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v2","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradictions","zh":"矛盾","pos":"noun","example":"At first, one student wanted to choose whichever source seemed more convincing, but this would have treated different questions as direct contradictions.","exampleZh":"起初一位学生想选更可信来源，但这会把不同问题当成直接矛盾。","collocation":"direct contradictions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v3","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"constraints","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"The residents described their experience, whereas the planner discussed practical constraints that a proposed change would need to address before being introduced.","exampleZh":"居民描述经历，规划师讨论的是变更实施前必须处理的实际限制。","collocation":"practical constraints","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v4","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"workable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"We therefore made separate notes on the problem, the people affected and the conditions under which each possible solution might be workable.","exampleZh":"因此我们分别记录问题、受影响者及各可能方案可行的条件。","collocation":"workable solution","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v5","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"转移","pos":"verb","example":"A complete ban on deliveries sounded simple, yet it might force businesses to close or transfer journeys to nearby streets with similar problems.","exampleZh":"完全禁配送看似简单，却可能迫使商家关闭或把交通转移到有类似问题的邻街。","collocation":"transfer journeys","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v6","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disturbance","zh":"干扰","pos":"noun","example":"Restricting the noisiest activities during late hours appeared more balanced, although the available evidence did not specify which vehicles caused the greatest disturbance.","exampleZh":"限制深夜最吵活动似乎更平衡，但现有证据未说明哪些车辆干扰最大。","collocation":"cause disturbance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v7","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trial","zh":"试验","pos":"noun","example":"Our recommendation was to collect that missing information during a short trial rather than pretend the existing sources already answered every relevant question.","exampleZh":"我们建议短期试验收集缺失信息，而不假装已有来源回答所有相关问题。","collocation":"short trial","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v8","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"represented","zh":"代表","pos":"verb","example":"When writing the summary, we identified whose view each claim represented and avoided presenting a small survey as the opinion of every resident.","exampleZh":"写摘要时我们标明主张属于谁，避免把小调查当成全体居民意见。","collocation":"represent a view","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v9","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"paraphrased","zh":"改述","pos":"verb","example":"We also paraphrased the main ideas instead of copying striking phrases that sounded impressive but did not fit the purpose of our proposal.","exampleZh":"我们还转述主要意思，而不复制听起来出色却不适合提案目的的醒目措辞。","collocation":"paraphrase main ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v10","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledged","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The presentation acknowledged disagreement, while showing that protecting rest and supporting shops were not necessarily incompatible goals.","exampleZh":"展示承认分歧，同时表明保护休息和支持商店并非必然不相容的目标。","collocation":"acknowledge disagreement","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v11","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"integrating","zh":"整合","pos":"verb","example":"Integrating information meant building a reasoned response, not attaching summaries without explaining their relationship.","exampleZh":"整合信息意味着形成有理由回应，而非不解释关系就拼接摘要。","collocation":"integrate information","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v12","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"A proposal with two incomplete sources","text":"Our course project asked us to recommend a use for a vacant town-centre building. We received an estate survey and interviews with residents, but neither source contained a ready-made answer. The survey described size, heating costs and the condition of the roof. The interviews discussed needs, including affordable meeting space and activities for young people.\nOne group immediately favoured a youth arts centre because the interviews sounded persuasive. Another preferred offices because the estate figures appeared more objective. Our tutor asked both groups what their chosen source could not establish. Residents could describe a need without proving that the building could meet it safely. A cost survey could describe a property without deciding which public purpose deserved priority.\nWe combined the sources into a table of supported points, uncertainties and decisions requiring further advice. The arts proposal needed information about supervision and sound insulation. The office plan depended on demand that nobody had yet measured. We then developed a limited trial using the safest rooms for scheduled activities while specialists examined the remaining questions.\nDuring the presentation, a partner introduced a new condition: funding would cover only one year of staffing. We had to revise the proposal instead of delivering our prepared ending unchanged. Our written follow-up explained which activities could continue through volunteers and which required trained paid staff.\nThe assessment recorded reading, writing and interaction separately. It also noted that we had already practised the broad theme, so a later task used a different building and different needs. The purpose was not to award a level for finishing a project. It was to collect clearer evidence of what we could understand, explain and adapt when the information did not match a memorised answer."},"sourceB":{"title":"The proposed late-opening library","text":"A university considered keeping one library open overnight during examination weeks. A student survey showed strong support, especially among people living in crowded accommodation. A security report explained that the existing staffing plan covered the building only until midnight. Several campaigners treated the report as opposition to students, while some staff treated survey support as proof that all students would attend.\nThe working group separated the questions. The survey identified a need and a preference; it did not establish expected numbers at each hour. The report identified a constraint; it did not rule out every possible adjustment. The group interviewed students about likely attendance and asked security staff to estimate the requirements of a restricted area rather than the entire building.\nA two-week trial kept one accessible floor open with a booking limit, clear support arrangements and a staffed entrance. Usage records showed that demand was highest before two in the morning and much lower afterwards. Feedback also revealed that some students wanted a quiet place but had assumed the trial guaranteed subject tutoring, which it did not.\nThe final recommendation extended late opening to two rather than continuing all night. It included clearer descriptions and an option to review the pattern during a future examination period. Some students remained disappointed, but the group could explain how the decision followed from both need and practical evidence.\nThe process did not remove disagreement. It made the disagreement more precise. People could challenge an attendance estimate or staffing condition without claiming that the other side cared nothing about safety or study. That distinction made a limited, revisable agreement possible."},"listening":{"teaching":"For an integrated task, I ask learners to keep three columns: what the sources establish, what they suggest and what remains unknown. This prevents an attractive recommendation from appearing before the evidence has been examined. It also helps with writing. A report can identify a real need while explaining that one proposed method still requires testing. During discussion, a partner may introduce a new constraint. Learners should not treat this as an interruption to the answer they memorised. It is part of the communication problem. They need to ask a useful question, consider the consequence and explain a revision. We collect several pieces of evidence rather than award a broad ability level from one polished presentation. An unfamiliar task after a delay is especially useful, though it still samples only part of a person’s ability. Our report therefore describes the conditions, observed strengths and remaining needs. Its value depends on being accurate enough to guide future practice, not on producing the most flattering possible label.","test":"I helped select a location for an adult skills course. The cheapest room was attractive on paper, but interviews with potential learners revealed that its last bus left before the class would finish. Another venue cost more and had better transport, though its computer access was limited. We combined the cost information with the interview findings instead of treating one source as the complete answer. Our recommendation used the better-connected venue for discussion sessions and a separate computer room on two planned evenings. Before confirming it, we checked whether moving between venues created new access problems. The final plan was a compromise, and we recorded what needed review after the first month. I learned that combining sources is not merely writing two summaries next to each other. It means explaining how different kinds of information affect the same decision and being clear when a convenient solution creates a new difficulty for someone else. The next review will ask learners about the whole journey between sessions, not merely the quality of each room."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why could neither project source decide the building’s use alone?","options":["Each source already contained a complete recommendation.","The estate report directly measured young people’s demand.","Each addressed a different part of the decision."],"answer":"Each addressed a different part of the decision.","explanation":"物理条件与社会需求信息互补。","id":"b2-12:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why use a later unfamiliar task?","options":["A delayed task checks only venue-name memory.","A practised project alone gives limited evidence of transfer.","The project completion badge already certifies every skill."],"answer":"A practised project alone gives limited evidence of transfer.","explanation":"避免完成度等于能力。","id":"b2-12:practice:q1"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What do the three columns distinguish?","options":["Established information, suggestions and unknowns.","Only positive findings, negative findings and word counts.","Only reading speed, discussion speed and presentation length."],"answer":"Established information, suggestions and unknowns.","explanation":"信息与证据强度分层。","id":"b2-12:practice:q2"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"How should a new constraint be treated?","options":["As a reason to repeat the original conclusion more clearly.","As evidence that no part of the sources is useful.","As part of the problem requiring a considered response."],"answer":"As part of the problem requiring a considered response.","explanation":"实际交流需适应新条件。","id":"b2-12:practice:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why were the survey and security report not simple opposites?","options":["The report rejected students’ needs as irrelevant.","One described needs and the other practical constraints.","The survey had measured security costs precisely."],"answer":"One described needs and the other practical constraints.","explanation":"不同问题不直接相互否定。","id":"b2-12:assessment:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the final recommendation stop at two?","options":["Later demand was much lower in the trial.","Every user left at midnight.","The trial tested only the midnight-to-two period."],"answer":"Later demand was much lower in the trial.","explanation":"具体使用记录支持有限延时。","id":"b2-12:assessment:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was the cheapest course room problematic?","options":["The room’s computer equipment had already been removed.","The better-connected venue was cheaper for every session.","The last bus left before classes ended."],"answer":"The last bus left before classes ended.","explanation":"实际可达性限制名义低价。","id":"b2-12:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the organiser learn about synthesis?","options":["The two summaries should remain completely separate.","Information sources must be connected to the same decision.","One practical source should always replace interviews."],"answer":"Information sources must be connected to the same decision.","explanation":"整合需解释信息之间如何影响选择。","id":"b2-12:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposal for a vacant public building using both practical constraints and residents’ needs. Separate evidence from assumptions.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Discuss a recommendation, respond to a new staffing constraint and explain the resulting compromise.","followups":["What is the strongest objection to your recommendation?","Which missing information could change your decision?","How would you explain the trade-off to someone with different priorities?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a report evaluating late library opening. Use the different sources for different questions and justify a qualified recommendation.","minimumWords":160,"checklist":["完整回应交际目的与读者需求，不用无关内容凑词数。","用具体理由或例证发展观点，区分事实、推断与意见。","组织段落并检查衔接、指代、语域和词语搭配。","独立完成后修改，保留初稿与改稿，供人工反馈对照。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Recommend a venue plan for the adult course, acknowledging how your solution could create a new access difficulty.","followups":["What evidence supports your answer?","What practical limitation would you acknowledge?","How could you check whether your proposal worked?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["提出方案的学习者","引入新限制的伙伴"],"goal":"整合来源、说明不确定性并修正方案。","twist":"最初费用方案忽略交通，替代方案又带来设备与跨场地通行限制。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least seven days, analyse a new source about the same broad issue: separate its central claim, evidence, limitations and implied recommendation.","writingPrompt":"Address a different audience facing a comparable decision. Develop your own recommendation and explain a condition under which it would change.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss an unfamiliar variation with a partner who can challenge your assumptions. Reformulate a misunderstood point and reach a qualified conclusion."}},{"id":"c1-01","level":"C1","title":"公共服务承诺与资源核算","chapter":41,"focus":"用搭配区分承诺、证据和限制","sourceA":{"title":"An evening service with conditions — extended case review","text":"A fictional town library is considering two additional evening openings each week. Its manager wants a proposal that distinguishes a useful idea from an affordable commitment. Staff have collected comments, but a popular suggestion is not automatically a workable timetable. The following briefing explains how the team should discuss its evidence and responsibilities. The service is intended for residents who cannot visit during normal working hours, including carers and people on rotating shifts.\n\nThe pilot could begin on one evening rather than two, allowing the rota to be checked under ordinary conditions. Comments from visitors should be considered alongside attendance records. Otherwise, a busy first night might be mistaken for evidence that the same level of demand will continue throughout the year.\n\nWe must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours. The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast. The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing. We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings. Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts. A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.\n\nWe should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction. The council must bear the cost of additional security staff. Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest. A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities. We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms. The final report should set out the conditions for continuing.\n\nThe library subsequently costed a six-week pilot. Opening on Wednesday required two desk staff and one security officer, with an estimated additional cost of 180 pounds per evening. The council offered 900 pounds, leaving a shortfall of 180 pounds across the full trial. One councillor suggested using volunteers to close the gap. Staff explained that volunteers could help visitors but could not replace the required security officer. The manager therefore proposed five evenings instead of six unless further funding was confirmed. She also added a register of people who arrived shortly before closing, because attendance alone would miss whether the later hours actually helped the intended users."},"sourceB":{"title":"A promise about accessible theatre seats","text":"The Marlow Theatre advertised twelve accessible seats for each performance in its new studio. The phrase seemed precise, yet a local access group asked what the number meant. Six spaces accommodated wheelchair users, while six conventional seats had removable armrests. The two arrangements met different needs and could not always be exchanged without changing the surrounding layout. Describing all twelve simply as accessible concealed distinctions that mattered when somebody booked.\n\nThe theatre's booking manager initially worried that a longer description would confuse customers. The access group argued that customers were already doing the work of discovering the conditions through repeated telephone calls. They proposed a short online table describing each arrangement, the companion seating available and the route to the studio. A telephone option would remain for people who preferred individual discussion. The theatre agreed to test the table for four performances before changing its publicity permanently.\n\nDuring the trial, staff recorded thirty-one enquiries. Nine concerned companion seating, eight concerned the step-free route and fourteen concerned whether a particular seat arrangement would suit the visitor. The figures did not show how many potential customers had decided not to enquire. Nevertheless, they identified information that could be made clearer. Two customers also reported that the label accessible had led them to assume that every seat could accommodate a wheelchair.\n\nThe revised publicity now states the number of wheelchair spaces separately from the number of seats with removable armrests. It promises accurate information and a discussion of individual requirements, rather than guaranteeing that every arrangement will suit every visitor. The theatre has not increased the studio's capacity. Its improvement lies in making the commitment more intelligible and limiting avoidable uncertainty before booking. The access group supports the revision but wants staff training checked again after the summer, when temporary employees join. A clear description can be undermined if people giving advice interpret it differently."},"listening":{"teaching":"At today's library meeting I want to clarify the costed evening proposal. We are considering Wednesday opening, not a permanent extension across the week. Each evening needs two desk staff and a security officer. The additional cost is estimated at 180 pounds. For six evenings that is 1,080 pounds, but the council has offered 900. We therefore cannot advertise six fully funded sessions yet. One option is to run five; another is to secure the remaining 180 pounds before launch. Volunteers can assist visitors, but they cannot replace the security officer required by our operating arrangements. Please do not describe them as a free substitute. We will record attendance and ask whether the later opening enabled a visit that otherwise would not have happened. The questions will be optional, and a person may decline without affecting access. We also want to record people arriving shortly before the current closing time. That could help us understand whether the extension addresses an existing difficulty. It will not tell us about everyone who never reaches the building. Before publicity goes out, the finance officer must confirm the final number of evenings. I will send the proposed rota tomorrow. Staff should identify clashes by Friday rather than assume that the usual daytime arrangements will cover the extra hours.","test":"This is a message for members using the harbour sports centre. From next Monday, the small exercise room will be available for supervised rehabilitation sessions on Tuesday mornings. There will be eight places in each session, with a qualified instructor present. That is the maximum number the instructor can supervise under the agreed plan; it is not a promise that every member will be offered a place immediately. You must have an initial discussion with the coordinator before booking your first session. The discussion is free and can take place by telephone. We originally planned to provide transport from the main car park, but that service has not been funded. Please make your own arrangements or ask the coordinator about the accessible drop-off point. The fee of twelve pounds covers the session and use of the changing facilities. It does not include individual treatment or an exercise programme outside the centre. Members who cannot attend should cancel by noon on the preceding day so that the place can be offered to someone else. If the instructor is unavailable, the session will be cancelled rather than run without supervision. We will review demand after four weeks. A full waiting list may justify considering another session, but no additional day has yet been approved."},"vocab":[{"head":"allocate funds","zh":"拨出资金","pos":"phrase","example":"We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","exampleZh":"我们必须先拨出资金，再宣传延长开放时间。","collocation":"allocate funds","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v1","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"an indication of","zh":"……的迹象","pos":"phrase","example":"The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","exampleZh":"调查反映了需求迹象，并非完整预测。","collocation":"an indication of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v2","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"make a firm commitment","zh":"作出明确承诺","pos":"phrase","example":"The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","exampleZh":"经理应在核实人员安排后才作出明确承诺。","collocation":"make a firm commitment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v3","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"take account of","zh":"考虑","pos":"phrase","example":"We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings.","exampleZh":"我们需要考虑连续上晚班的员工。","collocation":"take account of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v4","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"meet the needs","zh":"满足需要","pos":"phrase","example":"Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts.","exampleZh":"延长开放时间可能满足轮班人群的需要。","collocation":"meet the needs","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v5","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provide evidence","zh":"提供证据","pos":"phrase","example":"A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.","exampleZh":"有限试行能提供证据，又不会造成无限期义务。","collocation":"provide evidence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v6","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draw a distinction","zh":"作出区分","pos":"phrase","example":"We should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction.","exampleZh":"我们应区分到访人数和满意度。","collocation":"draw a distinction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v7","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"bear the cost","zh":"承担费用","pos":"phrase","example":"The council must bear the cost of additional security staff.","exampleZh":"委员会必须承担额外安保人员的费用。","collocation":"bear the cost","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v8","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shed light on","zh":"帮助说明","pos":"phrase","example":"Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest.","exampleZh":"初步结果可能揭示哪个晚上最忙。","collocation":"shed light on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v9","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reduce uncertainty","zh":"减少不确定性","pos":"phrase","example":"A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities.","exampleZh":"书面轮班表能减少个人职责的不确定性。","collocation":"reduce uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v10","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rule out","zh":"排除","pos":"phrase","example":"We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms.","exampleZh":"我们不能排除冬季暴风雨期间到访人数下降的可能。","collocation":"rule out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v11","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"set out","zh":"列明","pos":"phrase","example":"The final report should set out the conditions for continuing.","exampleZh":"最终报告应列明继续运行的条件。","collocation":"set out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v12","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shortfall","zh":"缺口","pos":"noun","example":"The shortfall must be funded before six evenings are advertised.","exampleZh":"宣传六次晚间开放前必须填补资金缺口。","collocation":"a funding shortfall","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v13","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingent","zh":"取决于条件的","pos":"adjective","example":"The sixth opening is contingent on additional funding.","exampleZh":"第六次开放取决于额外资金。","collocation":"contingent on funding","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v14","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substitute","zh":"替代物","pos":"noun","example":"A volunteer is not a substitute for a required security officer.","exampleZh":"志愿者不能替代必需安保人员。","collocation":"a substitute for","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v15","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underwrite","zh":"承担资金风险","pos":"verb","example":"The council declined to underwrite costs beyond the pilot.","exampleZh":"委员会拒绝承担试点之后的费用风险。","collocation":"underwrite the costs","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v16","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why does the manager propose five evenings unless more funding is confirmed?","answer":"The available grant covers five evenings at the stated cost.","options":["The available grant covers five evenings at the stated cost.","The available staff can work only five Wednesdays during the trial.","The recorded demand falls sharply after the fifth evening."],"explanation":"每晚180英镑，已获900英镑只能覆盖五次；六次差180，并非人手或需求数据决定五次。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-01:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What additional insight is the register of near-closing arrivals intended to provide?","answer":"Whether later hours could address an existing access difficulty.","options":["Whether every visitor was satisfied with the advice received.","Whether volunteers could safely replace the security officer.","Whether later hours could address an existing access difficulty."],"explanation":"临近关门到访登记针对延时是否帮助目标用户；它不衡量满意度，也不评估替代安保。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-01:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"How much funding was missing for six evenings?","answer":"180 pounds","options":["900 pounds","1,080 pounds","180 pounds"],"explanation":"六次共1080，已有900，差180。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-01:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"Why can volunteers not close the staffing gap?","answer":"They cannot replace the required security officer","options":["They all work on Wednesdays","They cannot replace the required security officer","They are not allowed to assist visitors"],"explanation":"录音明确区分志愿协助与必要安保岗位。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-01:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What did the original figure of twelve obscure?","answer":"Two seat arrangements met different needs","options":["Every seat accommodated a wheelchair","The studio had no step-free route","Two seat arrangements met different needs"],"explanation":"文章区分六个轮椅位与六个可拆扶手座位。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-01:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What did the enquiry records fail to reveal?","answer":"People who decided not to enquire","options":["The number of wheelchair spaces","People who decided not to enquire","The subjects of recorded enquiries"],"explanation":"记录只覆盖来询问者，未覆盖未询问便放弃的人。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-01:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What must members do before their first booking?","answer":"Discuss their needs with the coordinator","options":["Join the transport service","Discuss their needs with the coordinator","Pay for individual treatment"],"explanation":"首次预约前须与协调员讨论。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-01:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What will happen if the instructor is unavailable?","answer":"The session will be cancelled","options":["The session will be cancelled","Members will supervise each other","The waiting list will run the session"],"explanation":"缺少合格督导就取消，不无监督开课。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-01:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a recommendation to the library manager. Distinguish a trial from a permanent commitment, specify evidence to collect, and explain one staffing safeguard. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “An evening service with conditions”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["图书馆经理","工作人员代表","资助方"],"goal":"在900英镑预算内商定安全可行的开放试点。","twist":"资助方允许延后一周，但不增加预算；一名安保人员无法参加首日。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"一周后阅读一份陌生服务承诺，标明已确认与待满足条件，并核查数量含义。","writingPrompt":"不看范文写一份200词试点建议，加入25%的资金减少条件。","speakingPrompt":"向不同意缩短试点的居民解释决定，再接受两次追问。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A promise about accessible theatre seats”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A promise about accessible theatre seats” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-02","level":"C1","title":"学术结果与新闻表述的强弱","chapter":42,"focus":"区分证据、推断与程度","sourceA":{"title":"What a shorter queue really means — extended case review","text":"The fictional Brookside advice centre has reorganised its reception system. Managers want to explain the early figures without exaggerating what they establish. Queue length, waiting time and the quality of advice are related, but they are not identical measures. Some appointments now take place by telephone, which changes who appears in the reception data. The centre has prepared language for a cautious progress briefing rather than a celebratory announcement.\n\nA person receiving detailed advice may need a longer appointment, so faster service is not always better service. The centre should examine whether clients understand their next step. It should also check whether telephone users and people visiting in person face different barriers before describing the system as an improvement for everyone.\n\nA shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service. The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause. Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline. The reduction was modest rather than dramatic. We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample. Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.\n\nConvenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate. The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results. A plausible explanation still needs independent checking. The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain. We need comparable figures from a similar period. The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings.\n\nAt the advice centre, the first comparison covered 120 appointments before the change and 118 afterwards. Median visible waiting fell from 24 to 17 minutes. However, the later group included 35 telephone appointments, which did not create a queue in reception. A staff member proposed excluding them, but the manager pointed out that this would answer a different question about the remaining in-person service. The revised briefing reports both the overall figures and the separate appointment types. It also notes that no check of clients' understanding was conducted. The next review will therefore include an optional follow-up question about whether people knew what to do after the appointment."},"sourceB":{"title":"A headline about lecture recordings","text":"A university department trialled recordings in one first-year lecture course. A student newspaper reported that recordings had transformed attendance because fewer empty seats were visible in the final month. The lecturer objected to the word transformed. The course register showed a small increase in attendance, but the final month also included revision sessions and an assessed group activity. The newspaper's observation was not necessarily false; its explanation was stronger than the evidence justified.\n\nThe department had introduced recordings primarily to help students revisit difficult explanations. It had not designed the trial as a test of attendance. Sixty-two students answered an optional survey, and forty-nine said they had used a recording at least once. Those figures showed use among respondents. They did not establish how frequently the entire class listened, whether students watched complete lectures, or whether listening improved examination performance. Some respondents described the recordings as reassuring even when they rarely replayed them.\n\nA teaching committee asked the lecturer to distinguish availability, actual use and learning benefit in the report. She proposed collecting anonymous viewing patterns alongside samples of students' explanations of key concepts. The committee accepted that these sources might illuminate how recordings were used, while noting that an improvement in explanations could have several causes. Students would be informed about the collection and allowed to decline the research component without losing access to recordings.\n\nThe newspaper revised its headline to say that students welcomed the recording trial. Even that statement was qualified in the article by reference to the voluntary survey. The editor retained an account of the attendance discussion because the uncertainty was informative. A responsible report need not suppress encouraging findings; it must avoid allowing a vivid phrase to imply a causal result that nobody has actually demonstrated. The lecturer would review the revised measures with students before collecting new information."},"listening":{"teaching":"Let us look at the advice-centre comparison before choosing a headline. Before the change, there were 120 recorded appointments and a median visible wait of 24 minutes. Afterwards there were 118 appointments and the median was 17 minutes. That looks encouraging. However, 35 appointments in the later period were by telephone. Those clients were not standing in the reception queue. If we describe the result as a faster service for everyone, we would be combining different experiences. We could report the overall change, but we should also show appointment type and explain the limitation. Removing telephone appointments entirely is not automatically wrong; it simply changes the question to the experience of people who still attend in person. Another issue is quality. A shorter wait does not tell us whether clients understood the advice. We have not measured that yet. The next review will offer an optional question asking whether people know their next step. Staff should avoid suggesting that a positive answer proves the advice solved the underlying problem. It is a more limited measure of understanding. My preferred wording is that the early figures suggest a change in access and waiting patterns. They justify further checking, not an announcement that the new system has conclusively improved every part of the service.","test":"The town's swimming club has asked me to explain the results of its membership survey. We sent the questionnaire to 240 current members and received 84 replies. Sixty-three respondents said they preferred Saturday morning lessons to Sunday afternoon lessons. That is three quarters of respondents, not three quarters of everyone in the club. We did not ask former members or people currently on the waiting list. Their preferences may be different. The committee is therefore using the result to plan a small Saturday trial, not to cancel all Sunday classes. The trial will last four weeks and use one instructor who is already available. Members will be invited to book through the usual system, and we will record bookings, attendance and cancellations separately. A full booking list can still leave spaces if people do not attend. At the end, the committee will compare the sessions and ask whether the time created new opportunities or merely moved existing swimmers from another day. The survey does show a clear preference among the people who replied. It does not establish how large the demand would be across the wider community. Please retain that distinction when discussing the decision with other members. The committee will publish the review date in advance."},"vocab":[{"head":"imply","zh":"意味着","pos":"verb","example":"A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","exampleZh":"队伍变短并不一定意味着每个人得到更快的服务。","collocation":"imply a conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v1","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggest","zh":"提示","pos":"verb","example":"The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","exampleZh":"这些数字提示有所改善，却不能证明原因。","collocation":"suggest an improvement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v2","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"apparent","zh":"表面的","pos":"adjective","example":"Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","exampleZh":"电话预约可能解释部分表面下降。","collocation":"an apparent decline","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v3","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"modest","zh":"不大的","pos":"adjective","example":"The reduction was modest rather than dramatic.","exampleZh":"降幅不大，而非十分显著。","collocation":"a modest reduction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v4","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"representative","zh":"有代表性的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample.","exampleZh":"我们应区分准确计数与有代表性的样本。","collocation":"a representative sample","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v5","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"convenient","zh":"方便的","pos":"adjective","example":"Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.","exampleZh":"一些来访者认为新接待流程很方便。","collocation":"a convenient process","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v6","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appropriate","zh":"恰当的","pos":"adjective","example":"Convenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate.","exampleZh":"仅有便利并不能证明建议恰当。","collocation":"appropriate advice","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v7","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledge","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results.","exampleZh":"经理承认局限，同时没有否定积极结果。","collocation":"acknowledge limitations","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v8","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"plausible","zh":"看似合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A plausible explanation still needs independent checking.","exampleZh":"合理的解释仍需独立核查。","collocation":"a plausible explanation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v9","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consistent with","zh":"与……相符","pos":"phrase","example":"The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain.","exampleZh":"证据与产生益处的解释相符，但仍存在其他解释。","collocation":"consistent with","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v10","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"comparable","zh":"可比的","pos":"adjective","example":"We need comparable figures from a similar period.","exampleZh":"我们需要相近时期的可比数据。","collocation":"comparable figures","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v11","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reflect","zh":"体现","pos":"verb","example":"The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings.","exampleZh":"最终声明应体现研究结果中的不确定性。","collocation":"reflect uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v12","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"denominator","zh":"分母","pos":"noun","example":"The denominator is the number of respondents, not all members.","exampleZh":"分母是回应人数，而非全部会员。","collocation":"inspect the denominator","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v13","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"causal","zh":"因果的","pos":"adjective","example":"A before-and-after comparison alone does not establish a causal effect.","exampleZh":"单独的前后比较不能确立因果效应。","collocation":"a causal effect","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v14","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conclusive","zh":"确凿的","pos":"adjective","example":"The findings are promising but not conclusive.","exampleZh":"发现有希望，但非确凿结论。","collocation":"conclusive evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v15","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disaggregate","zh":"分组拆分","pos":"verb","example":"We should disaggregate appointments by contact method.","exampleZh":"我们应按联系方法拆分预约。","collocation":"disaggregate the data","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v16","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why does the manager report appointment types separately as well as overall figures?","answer":"Telephone appointments changed the composition of the later group.","options":["The earlier group contained fewer appointments than the later group.","Staff had measured understanding differently in the two groups.","Telephone appointments changed the composition of the later group."],"explanation":"后期118次中35次电话预约不形成接待队列，群体构成改变；并未测量理解。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-02:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What would the proposed optional follow-up question add to the review?","answer":"Evidence of whether clients knew their next step after advice.","options":["A replacement for all records of waiting and appointment type.","Evidence of whether clients knew their next step after advice.","Proof that every underlying problem had been fully resolved."],"explanation":"拟询问是否知道下一步，补充理解证据；不能证明问题已解决，也不替代等待数据。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-02:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"How many later appointments were by telephone?","answer":"35","options":["17","35","118"],"explanation":"电话预约35次，118为后期总数。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-02:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What has not yet been measured?","answer":"Whether clients understood the advice","options":["Whether clients understood the advice","The number of recorded appointments","The median visible wait"],"explanation":"录音明确说尚未核查理解。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-02:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why did the lecturer object to the headline?","answer":"It implied a stronger explanation than the evidence supported","options":["Recordings had never been introduced","It implied a stronger explanation than the evidence supported","The newspaper had invented every observation"],"explanation":"“transformed”暗示已证明因果，而同期还有复习及计分活动。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-02:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What did forty-nine survey responses establish?","answer":"Recording use among those respondents","options":["Recording use among those respondents","Examination improvement for the whole class","Complete viewing of every lecture"],"explanation":"49人自报至少用过一次，仅支持回应者使用情况。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-02:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"Who received the swimming survey?","answer":"Current members","options":["Current members","Everyone in the town","Only former members"],"explanation":"发送对象为240名现会员。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-02:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What will the survey initially support?","answer":"A four-week Saturday trial","options":["Cancellation of every Sunday class","A permanent community-wide expansion","A four-week Saturday trial"],"explanation":"用于小规模四周试行，并非取消所有周日课程。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-02:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a cautious briefing about the advice centre. Explain why queue length is insufficient, propose two additional measures, and distinguish a finding from an explanation. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “What a shorter queue really means”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["研究人员","新闻编辑","学生代表"],"goal":"商定不夸大结果但能说明发现价值的标题和导语。","twist":"编辑要求20词以内标题，而数据还缺少一个重要群体。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"七天后读未见过的调查报道，分别标记样本、分母、结果和因果解释。","writingPrompt":"写220词公众简报，纠正一个夸大标题但保留有用结果。","speakingPrompt":"用两分钟解释为什么“多数回应者”不等于“多数居民”。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A headline about lecture recordings”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A headline about lecture recordings” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-03","level":"C1","title":"正式沟通与公众信任","chapter":43,"focus":"正式、友好与坚定表达的切换","sourceA":{"title":"Rewriting a housing notice — extended case review","text":"A fictional housing cooperative needs access to residents' kitchens to inspect shared water pipes. Its first notice sounded abrupt and gave no way to discuss individual circumstances. A revised version must remain clear about the inspection while respecting privacy and making the required action easy to identify. The team is preparing both a formal letter and a short reminder message. Each version must preserve the same deadline and contact arrangements.\n\nPrivacy is central to residents' response. The letter should explain what staff will inspect, how identity will be checked and how a different appointment can be requested. A shorter reminder can omit background discussion, but it must not drop the action that residents are expected to take.\n\nWe would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window. Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable. The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available. Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon. For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards. A member of staff will show identification before entering.\n\nCould you let us know whether anyone requires additional support? The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter. Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do. We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection. If you have already replied, no further action is needed. A firm request can remain respectful and specific.\n\nThe cooperative tested its revised letter with eight residents before sending it to all 64 households. Five could identify the reply deadline immediately, but three thought that access was compulsory at the first proposed time. The team therefore moved the alternative-appointment information into the opening paragraph. It kept the explanation of the inspection's purpose, rather than relying on a threatening reminder of the rules. The office will accept replies by telephone as well as email. Residents who need support may discuss it privately, and the public booking sheet will show appointment times without names or health details."},"sourceB":{"title":"Explaining a payroll correction","text":"A small research organisation discovered that a travel allowance had been calculated incorrectly for nineteen employees. The finance manager's first draft email said that an adjustment would be processed in due course. It did not explain whether employees owed money, would receive money or needed to submit a form. Staff representatives warned that the phrase was likely to create anxiety precisely because it avoided the information readers needed.\n\nThe error had resulted in underpayments, not overpayments. The revised email stated this in the opening paragraph. It explained that the missing amounts would be added to the following month's salary and that affected employees would receive an individual calculation through the secure payroll portal. Nobody needed to send bank details by email. People who thought that their calculation was wrong could contact a named payroll officer within ten working days. A separate technical note described the formula for managers who needed to review the process.\n\nThe organisation did not publish individual sums on the staff website. Although transparency mattered, it did not justify exposing personal financial information. Instead, the public internal notice reported the number of affected employees, the correction timetable and the checking procedure. It also acknowledged that some staff might have incurred costs because of the delay and invited them to discuss these privately, without promising automatic reimbursement before the circumstances were reviewed.\n\nThe staff representatives supported the message because it distinguished a confirmed remedy from a request that still required consideration. They asked that the phrase in due course be avoided when a date was already known. Formal language can help maintain a professional relationship, but vagueness is not the same as tact. The finance manager agreed to have future notices checked by a colleague outside the department, whose unfamiliarity with payroll terminology would reveal assumptions that specialists might otherwise overlook."},"listening":{"teaching":"We have tested the inspection letter with eight residents. Five found the deadline immediately, but three thought they had no choice about the first appointment time. That is not what we intend, so the revised opening now says that alternative appointments can be arranged. Please keep that sentence near the request for access. Moving it to a footnote would recreate the problem. The letter will go to 64 households, with replies accepted by telephone or email. The office should not ask people to explain private circumstances on the public booking sheet. That sheet will show available times only. A member of staff will discuss support needs individually. Another point concerns identification. Residents should know that the inspector will show an identity card before entering. We do not need a long description of every tool used during the visit, but we do need to explain that personal cupboards will not be opened. The short text reminder must preserve the Thursday deadline and the office contact route. It can omit background about the pipe system. Please compare the two versions before sending them: shortening is useful only if the essential action and the choice of appointment remain clear. If someone has already replied, the reminder should explicitly say that no further action is required.","test":"I am calling about the changes to tomorrow's training assessment. The practical session will begin at nine thirty in workshop four, not workshop two. The written section remains in the upstairs classroom at eleven. Please arrive fifteen minutes before the practical session so that the assessor can check your identification. You should bring protective footwear; gloves and eye protection will be provided. If you do not have suitable footwear, contact the training office this afternoon rather than arriving and hoping to borrow some. The centre has only a limited number of spare pairs. The email sent yesterday used the phrase mandatory rescheduling. That was unclear: you must use the new room, but anyone whose travel arrangements make the new practical start impossible may request another date. The office will discuss those requests individually. No fee will be charged for a change caused by this timetable correction. However, please do not simply fail to attend without contacting us. The written assessment result will be sent through the learner portal, not read out to the group. If you cannot access the portal, we can arrange a private collection appointment. Thank you for checking the details before travelling. Staff will answer questions through the training office today."},"vocab":[{"head":"would appreciate","zh":"希望得到","pos":"phrase","example":"We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","exampleZh":"希望您能在检查时段允许我们进入厨房。","collocation":"would appreciate","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v1","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unsuitable","zh":"不合适的","pos":"adjective","example":"Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","exampleZh":"如果建议时间不合适，请联系办公室。","collocation":"an unsuitable appointment time","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v2","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alternative appointment","zh":"其他预约时间","pos":"phrase","example":"The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","exampleZh":"检查是必要的，但可以另约时间。","collocation":"alternative appointment","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v3","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"are requested to","zh":"被请予以","pos":"phrase","example":"Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon.","exampleZh":"请住户在星期四下午前回复。","collocation":"are requested to","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v4","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"for clarity","zh":"为说明清楚","pos":"phrase","example":"For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards.","exampleZh":"为说明清楚，这次上门不涉及打开私人橱柜。","collocation":"for clarity","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v5","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identification","zh":"身份证明","pos":"noun","example":"A member of staff will show identification before entering.","exampleZh":"工作人员会在进入前出示证件。","collocation":"show identification","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v6","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"additional support","zh":"额外协助","pos":"phrase","example":"Could you let us know whether anyone requires additional support?","exampleZh":"能否告知我们是否有人需要额外协助？","collocation":"additional support","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v7","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"retain","zh":"保留","pos":"verb","example":"The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter.","exampleZh":"提醒信息应保留正式信件中规定的截止时间。","collocation":"retain a deadline","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v8","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"obscure","zh":"使不清楚","pos":"verb","example":"Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do.","exampleZh":"友好措辞不能掩盖住户需要采取的行动。","collocation":"obscure a requirement","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v9","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inconvenience","zh":"不便","pos":"noun","example":"We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection.","exampleZh":"对于检查造成的不便，我们表示歉意。","collocation":"cause inconvenience","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v10","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"further action","zh":"进一步行动","pos":"phrase","example":"If you have already replied, no further action is needed.","exampleZh":"如果您已经回复，则无需进一步操作。","collocation":"further action","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v11","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"respectful","zh":"尊重他人的","pos":"adjective","example":"A firm request can remain respectful and specific.","exampleZh":"明确坚定的请求仍可以尊重且具体。","collocation":"respectful wording","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v12","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"remedy","zh":"补救办法","pos":"noun","example":"The letter states the confirmed remedy before discussing exceptions.","exampleZh":"信件先说明已确认的补救措施，再讨论例外。","collocation":"a confirmed remedy","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v13","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tact","zh":"得体","pos":"noun","example":"Tact requires consideration, not concealment of an important fact.","exampleZh":"得体要求体谅，而非隐瞒重要事实。","collocation":"show tact","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v14","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclosure","zh":"披露","pos":"noun","example":"Public disclosure of personal circumstances is unnecessary.","exampleZh":"没有必要公开披露私人情况。","collocation":"public disclosure","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v15","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unambiguous","zh":"明确无歧义的","pos":"adjective","example":"An unambiguous deadline helps residents act.","exampleZh":"明确截止时间有助住户行动。","collocation":"an unambiguous deadline","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v16","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why was alternative-appointment information moved into the opening paragraph?","answer":"Some test readers thought the first proposed time was compulsory.","options":["The office had decided to stop accepting replies by telephone.","Some test readers thought the first proposed time was compulsory.","Most test readers could not identify the inspection’s technical purpose."],"explanation":"八位试读者中三位误以为必须接受首次时段，因此把另约信息前置；电话回复仍保留。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-03:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"How does the revised booking process handle individual support needs?","answer":"Staff discuss them privately while the public sheet shows times.","options":["Staff discuss them privately while the public sheet shows times.","Residents describe them publicly beside their chosen appointment.","The inspector collects them after entering every household’s kitchen."],"explanation":"原文将支持需求留作私下讨论，公共表只列时段，不公开姓名和健康信息。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-03:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What misunderstanding did three residents have?","answer":"The first proposed appointment could not be changed","options":["The first proposed appointment could not be changed","No inspection would occur","Every reply had to be public"],"explanation":"三人误以为首次建议时间没有选择。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-03:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What should the reminder preserve?","answer":"The deadline and contact route","options":["Every technical detail","Private support information","The deadline and contact route"],"explanation":"提醒保留截止时间和联系方式。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-03:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What type of payroll error occurred?","answer":"Underpayment","options":["Underpayment","Overpayment","Incorrect bank ownership"],"explanation":"员工少收款项，将在次月工资中补发。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-03:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"Why was an external departmental reader useful?","answer":"They could expose assumptions in specialist language","options":["They could approve every reimbursement automatically","They could publish individual salaries","They could expose assumptions in specialist language"],"explanation":"非财务同事能发现术语和专业假设造成的不清楚。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-03:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"Which location has changed?","answer":"The practical workshop","options":["The written classroom","The learner portal","The practical workshop"],"explanation":"实操改为workshop four，笔试地点不变。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-03:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What may affected travellers request?","answer":"Another assessment date without a change fee","options":["Public announcement of their result","Another assessment date without a change fee","Automatic cancellation of all assessments"],"explanation":"因更改导致困难者可另约，不收更改费。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-03:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write the cooperative’s formal notice, then explain how you would adapt it into a brief reminder. Preserve all essential actions and offer a practical route for exceptions. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Rewriting a housing notice”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["住户代表","物业办公室","隐私负责人"],"goal":"将检查通知改成正式信与简短提醒，保留同样权利和义务。","twist":"公共预约表必须当天发布，但不能披露姓名与支持需求。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"十天后核对两份陌生机构通知，找出含糊行动和遗漏的选择。","writingPrompt":"为一项延期写220词通知及改写说明，明确已确认和待审核事项。","speakingPrompt":"向担忧隐私的收件人解释处理流程并确认理解。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Explaining a payroll correction”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Explaining a payroll correction” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-04","level":"C1","title":"学术讲座与笔记重建","chapter":45,"focus":"跟踪讲座论证并区分例子与结论","sourceA":{"title":"A lecture on urban shade — extended case review","text":"In a fictional public lecture, landscape researcher Elena explains why a city should not judge its tree programme by planting totals alone. A young tree offers little shade immediately, and survival depends on soil, watering and protection. Her lecture moves from the problem of summer heat to the practical choices a maintenance team must make. The briefing below preserves that sequence so that listeners can reconstruct the argument rather than merely remember isolated statistics.\n\nFor a school route, the location of shade can matter more than a distant increase in canopy cover. Elena therefore asks the audience to connect measurement with the intended benefit. Her practical conclusion is to care for existing trees while choosing new sites in relation to people's daily movements.\n\nThe speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading. She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive. The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument. Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years. Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment. The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.\n\nA survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count. The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it. In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover. The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection. My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples. A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation.\n\nThe city has now identified twelve possible planting sites near schools. Six have adequate soil volume, four would require reconstruction of the pavement, and two are unsuitable because of underground services. Elena uses this classification to show why a target of twelve new trees cannot be treated as twelve equivalent projects. The maintenance team can support eight new sites this year. Rather than selecting the easiest eight automatically, the council will compare current shade, walking routes and preparation costs. The lecture ends by asking students to write a recommendation that retains those competing criteria instead of copying the planting target."},"sourceB":{"title":"Why a materials lecture begins with failure","text":"Professor Chen opened a lecture on reusable packaging by showing a damaged container rather than a successful product. A hinge had cracked after repeated washing. Several students assumed that the example proved reusable packaging was environmentally inferior to disposable packaging. Chen explained that the failed hinge raised a design question; it did not settle the wider comparison. The environmental balance depended on production, washing, transport, the number of uses and what happened at the end of the container's life.\n\nThe demonstration came from a pilot in a college cafeteria. Two hundred containers had been supplied, and 146 were returned during the first month. Some missing containers might still have been in students' rooms, so the remaining fifty-four could not all be described as permanently lost. Nor did the number returned reveal how many times each container had been reused. Without that information, a simple average environmental claim would be premature.\n\nChen then distinguished durability from return behaviour. Improving the hinge could extend physical life, but it would not encourage people to bring containers back. A convenient return point might improve return behaviour without changing the material at all. The cafeteria therefore planned two separate changes: a redesigned hinge and a return station near the library. Testing both at once could improve the service, yet would make it difficult to isolate the contribution of each change.\n\nThe lecturer did not argue that practical decisions must wait for perfect experiments. Instead, she asked students to state whether they were recommending a service improvement or claiming a measured causal effect. Those were different tasks. Her final slide showed a diagram linking manufacture, use, return and cleaning, with unknown quantities marked explicitly. Students were asked to reconstruct the chain in their notes, placing the broken hinge within it rather than allowing the vivid opening example to dominate the conclusion."},"listening":{"teaching":"I would like you to reconstruct the argument of the shade lecture rather than write down every sentence. Start with the city's proposed twelve planting sites near schools. They are not twelve identical opportunities. Six already have adequate soil volume. Four need pavement reconstruction, and two conflict with underground services. Elena used that classification to challenge a simple planting target. The maintenance team can support only eight additional sites this year. That does not automatically mean choosing the six easy sites plus the two cheapest remaining ones. We also need to consider where children walk and which routes currently have little shade. In your notes, separate the target, the capacity limit and the selection criteria. The example of a playground illustrates where shade may be useful; it does not prove that every playground is the best site. Elena's wider argument is that the benefits develop over time and require care. A tree counted on planting day may not survive to provide substantial shade. Your summary should therefore retain the role of watering and maintenance. For the final paragraph, propose what the council should compare before selecting sites. You may take a position, but explain which trade-off supports it. A list of twelve locations without those conditions would miss the structure of the lecture.","test":"Before the archaeology lecture, I need to explain tomorrow's field visit. We will inspect the old mill site in two groups because the path is narrow. Group one leaves the college at eight forty; group two leaves at nine twenty. Your group is shown on the email attachment, not determined by the order in which you arrive. The visit is observational. We will not excavate or remove objects, even if something appears loose on the surface. Bring a notebook, waterproof clothing and a packed lunch. The landowner has allowed access to the track but not to the storage buildings. Please keep that distinction in mind when taking photographs. The lecturer will demonstrate how to describe a feature before proposing an interpretation. If you think a wall served a particular purpose, record the physical evidence and your uncertainty separately. We will compare interpretations back at college in the afternoon. The site has poor mobile reception, so download the map before leaving. If the weather makes the path unsafe, both groups will stay at college and examine the archive photographs instead. We will decide by seven thirty and send a message through the course portal. Do not travel directly to the mill before checking it."},"vocab":[{"head":"establishes","zh":"说明确立","pos":"verb","example":"The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","exampleZh":"讲者首先说明植树总数为何可能误导。","collocation":"establishes a distinction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v1","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turns to","zh":"转向","pos":"phrase","example":"She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","exampleZh":"接着她转向幼树存活所需的条件。","collocation":"turns to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v2","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"illustration","zh":"例证","pos":"noun","example":"The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","exampleZh":"有树荫的操场是例子，而非完整论证。","collocation":"a supporting illustration","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v3","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"although","zh":"虽然","pos":"conjunction","example":"Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years.","exampleZh":"成熟树木虽能遮阴，益处却需多年形成。","collocation":"although benefits develop slowly","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v4","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance","zh":"维护","pos":"noun","example":"Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment.","exampleZh":"维护应被视为投资的一部分。","collocation":"routine maintenance","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v5","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depends on","zh":"取决于","pos":"phrase","example":"The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.","exampleZh":"该方案取决于可靠的夏季浇水。","collocation":"depends on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v6","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"survival rate","zh":"存活率","pos":"phrase","example":"A survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count.","exampleZh":"存活率比最初种植数量提供更多信息。","collocation":"survival rate","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v7","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualifies","zh":"限定","pos":"verb","example":"The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it.","exampleZh":"讲者对建议加上条件，而非撤回建议。","collocation":"qualifies a recommendation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v8","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distribution","zh":"分布","pos":"noun","example":"In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover.","exampleZh":"在此情境下，树荫分布与总覆盖同样重要。","collocation":"shade distribution","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v9","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"returns to","zh":"回到","pos":"phrase","example":"The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection.","exampleZh":"结论重新关注防护最少的社区。","collocation":"returns to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v10","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"central claim","zh":"核心主张","pos":"phrase","example":"My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples.","exampleZh":"我的笔记区分核心主张和支持例子。","collocation":"central claim","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v11","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attached to","zh":"附带于","pos":"phrase","example":"A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation.","exampleZh":"有用的摘要会保留建议所附带的条件。","collocation":"attached to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v12","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capacity constraint","zh":"能力约束","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The maintenance limit is a capacity constraint, not a judgement about need.","exampleZh":"维护上限是能力约束，而非需求判断。","collocation":"a binding capacity constraint","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v13","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconstruct","zh":"重建","pos":"verb","example":"Students must reconstruct the argument from selective notes.","exampleZh":"学生须根据选择性笔记重建论证。","collocation":"reconstruct an argument","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v14","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"salient","zh":"突出的","pos":"adjective","example":"A salient example can distract listeners from a qualified conclusion.","exampleZh":"突出的例子可能使听者忽略附条件结论。","collocation":"a salient example","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v15","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"The team assessed feasibility before promising all twelve sites.","exampleZh":"团队在承诺全部十二处前评估可行性。","collocation":"assess feasibility","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v16","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"What is the main purpose of classifying the twelve proposed planting sites?","answer":"To show that the planting target contains unequal practical demands.","options":["To show that the planting target contains unequal practical demands.","To rank the sites solely by the number of pupils nearby.","To demonstrate that the maintenance team can support every site."],"explanation":"六处土壤足够、四处需改路面、两处地下设施冲突，说明十二项并不等价。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-04:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"Why does the council avoid automatically selecting the easiest sites?","answer":"Need for shade and walking routes also affect the decision.","options":["The lecture establishes that easier sites provide less shade.","The proposed target requires all pavement work to occur first.","Need for shade and walking routes also affect the decision."],"explanation":"选择还比较缺少树荫程度、步行路线及准备成本；不能由施工难易单独决定。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-04:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"How many sites already have adequate soil?","answer":"Six","options":["Eight","Twelve","Six"],"explanation":"六处土壤空间足够，八是维护能力。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-04:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What should notes distinguish?","answer":"The target, capacity and selection criteria","options":["Every spoken word and no structure","The target, capacity and selection criteria","Only the place names"],"explanation":"讲者要求区分目标、能力约束和选择标准。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-04:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What did the damaged hinge establish?","answer":"A design problem needing investigation","options":["The full environmental ranking of all packaging","That every container had failed","A design problem needing investigation"],"explanation":"铰链失败只说明设计问题，不足以裁定整体环境优劣。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-04:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"Why could the unreturned containers not all be called lost?","answer":"Some might still be with students","options":["None had ever left the cafeteria","Some might still be with students","Every container had a tracking device"],"explanation":"未归还不等于永久遗失。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-04:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"How is a visitor’s group determined?","answer":"By the email attachment","options":["By whether they bring a camera","By the email attachment","By arrival order"],"explanation":"分组看邮件附件。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-04:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What happens if the path is unsafe?","answer":"The groups use archive photographs at college","options":["The groups use archive photographs at college","The first group excavates alone","Everyone travels directly anyway"],"explanation":"不安全则留校分析档案照片。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-04:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a structured summary of Elena’s lecture for a council member. Explain the problem, the proposed response, the example and the conditions for success. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A lecture on urban shade”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["讲座摘要人","市政规划员","质疑者"],"goal":"根据三项竞争标准选出第一阶段地点并解释取舍。","twist":"维护能力从八处减到六处，但最缺树荫的路线改造费最高。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后阅读陌生学术摘要，重建主张、例子、限定条件和结论。","writingPrompt":"写一份不依赖原稿的220词讲座摘要，清楚标明未知量。","speakingPrompt":"用三分钟教同伴理解一个机制，再回答“这个例子证明了什么”。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Why a materials lecture begins with failure”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Why a materials lecture begins with failure” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-05","level":"C1","title":"访谈态度与隐含立场","chapter":46,"focus":"根据修正、保留与上下文判断态度","sourceA":{"title":"An organiser reflects on a crowded festival — extended case review","text":"In this fictional interview, an arts organiser discusses a neighbourhood festival that attracted more people than expected. The interviewer asks whether the event should simply become larger next year. The organiser welcomes interest but is cautious about treating attendance as the only sign of success. Her comments concern staff capacity, access and the experience of nearby residents. Listeners should distinguish what she says directly from what her careful qualifications allow us to infer.\n\nThe organiser wants residents to remain partners in the festival rather than become an audience for a decision already made. Her cautious phrases keep that possibility open. A reliable interpretation must retain both her pleasure at public interest and her concern that a larger programme would require stronger support.\n\nThe organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion. Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success. She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding. However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it. The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong. Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.\n\nShe does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance. The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance. A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal. We should support an inference with specific words and context. She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public. Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal.\n\nThe organiser later clarified what further support meant. The festival had used 42 volunteer shifts, and six had been covered at the last minute by committee members. A sponsor offered money for another performance but not for volunteer coordination. The organiser said this was welcome, although it did not solve the difficulty she had identified. She also described a resident consultation as overdue, rather than optional. Learners should ask whether these details support an inference of opposition or a more specific concern about conditions. A useful analysis does not treat her refusal to celebrate expansion immediately as evidence that she dislikes the festival."},"sourceB":{"title":"A curator speaks about an unexpected donation","text":"When a private collector offered a local museum 180 photographs, the regional radio presenter described the donation as a remarkable gift that must be accepted. Curator Helen responded that the offer could be valuable. Her choice of could was deliberate. The museum had seen digital previews of sixty images, but it had not checked the physical condition of the full collection or established whether the donor held the rights needed for public display.\n\nThe presenter asked whether those checks were merely bureaucratic obstacles. Helen replied that an image could be historically interesting and still be expensive to conserve or difficult to exhibit lawfully. She pointed out that the museum had storage for approximately ninety additional photographs under suitable conditions. Accepting the entire donation would therefore require either more storage or a carefully agreed selection. Her explanation was not a judgement that the remaining images lacked value.\n\nWhen asked whether she was worried about offending the donor, Helen said that a clear conversation now would be kinder than an enthusiastic acceptance followed by years in an unsuitable cupboard. The remark implied that she preferred honest limits to a flattering promise. It did not establish that the donor had behaved unreasonably. Indeed, Helen described the donor as willing to discuss the practical arrangements and share information about where the photographs had come from.\n\nThe museum proposed a preliminary review by a conservator and a rights specialist, followed by a meeting to decide what could be accepted. Helen resisted the presenter's invitation to call this a rejection. The process was still open, subject to information that the museum needed before making a responsible commitment. The interview left listeners with a qualified position: interest in the material, gratitude for the offer and caution about obligations. Treating any one of these as the whole answer would distort the curator's view."},"listening":{"teaching":"Listen to how the festival organiser qualifies her support. She says the sponsor's offer is welcome, which directly expresses appreciation. But the offer pays for an extra performance, not for coordinating volunteers. The organiser has already described coordination as the main constraint. Forty-two shifts were required last year, and six had to be filled at the last minute by committee members. That is why her positive response to the money does not amount to approval of expansion. We should also notice her description of consultation as overdue. It implies that a discussion with residents should have happened earlier, not merely that it might be pleasant in future. However, do not infer that all residents oppose the festival; she has not said that. Her concern is that the next programme should be discussed before it becomes a public commitment. In your analysis, quote or paraphrase the words supporting each inference. Separate an explicit fact, such as the number of shifts, from an interpretation of her attitude. If a classmate calls her ungrateful, ask which part of the interview supports that label. She can appreciate the sponsor's money while pointing out that it does not pay for the work needed to use it responsibly. Those positions are compatible, rather than contradictory.","test":"Thank you for inviting me to discuss the village history project. Yes, the response to our first exhibition was encouraging, although I would hesitate to call it a complete account of village life. Most of the material came from three families who had already kept organised archives. That was useful, but it means we heard less from people who moved here recently or never kept photographs. Next time, I would like to hold an open story-collecting afternoon before choosing the display. No, I am not suggesting that the first contributors were overrepresented deliberately. We worked with what was available, and they were generous. I simply do not want convenience to determine the whole story. The council has offered the hall for one Saturday. We still need trained people to record contributions and explain consent. A microphone on a table is not enough. We must agree how recordings can be used and allow contributors to decline public release. If we cannot organise that properly this month, I would rather postpone than collect material under unclear conditions. The aim is a more inclusive account, not a bigger pile of recordings. I hope existing contributors will help invite neighbours whose experiences have not yet been heard."},"vocab":[{"head":"uneasy","zh":"不安的","pos":"adjective","example":"The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","exampleZh":"组织者对人数感到高兴，却对扩张不安。","collocation":"uneasy about expansion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v1","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stops short of","zh":"没有进一步到","pos":"phrase","example":"Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","exampleZh":"她说“有益的开端”，尚未宣布成功。","collocation":"stops short of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v2","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concede","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","exampleZh":"她承认更大的活动可能吸引更多资金。","collocation":"concede a benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v3","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sustain","zh":"持续承担","pos":"verb","example":"However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it.","exampleZh":"然而，她质疑志愿者团队能否持续承担。","collocation":"sustain an activity","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v4","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hesitation","zh":"犹豫","pos":"noun","example":"The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong.","exampleZh":"采访者把犹豫解读成反对，程度过重。","collocation":"express hesitation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v5","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"implies","zh":"暗示","pos":"verb","example":"Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.","exampleZh":"她的回答暗示应先改善可及性再扩张。","collocation":"implies a preference","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v6","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"claim","zh":"声称","pos":"verb","example":"She does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance.","exampleZh":"她没有声称附近居民反对每场演出。","collocation":"make a claim","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v7","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定意见","pos":"noun","example":"The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance.","exampleZh":"限定意见涉及晚间噪声，而非日间人数。","collocation":"an important qualification","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v8","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tone","zh":"语气","pos":"noun","example":"A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal.","exampleZh":"仅凭亲切语气不能认定赞同方案。","collocation":"a warm tone","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v9","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inference","zh":"推断","pos":"noun","example":"We should support an inference with specific words and context.","exampleZh":"我们应以具体措辞和上下文支持推断。","collocation":"support an inference","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v10","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consultation","zh":"协商","pos":"noun","example":"She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public.","exampleZh":"她建议先协商，再公布明年的安排。","collocation":"public consultation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v11","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outright refusal","zh":"断然拒绝","pos":"phrase","example":"Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal.","exampleZh":"她的立场是有条件支持，而非断然拒绝。","collocation":"outright refusal","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v12","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"gratitude","zh":"感激","pos":"noun","example":"Gratitude for funding does not imply agreement with every condition.","exampleZh":"感谢资助并不意味着赞同所有条件。","collocation":"express gratitude","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v13","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivocal","zh":"含糊不定的","pos":"adjective","example":"Her answer was qualified rather than merely equivocal.","exampleZh":"她的回答有明确限定，并非单纯含糊。","collocation":"an equivocal answer","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v14","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"认可","pos":"noun","example":"A welcome is not an unconditional endorsement of expansion.","exampleZh":"欢迎并非无条件认可扩张。","collocation":"an unconditional endorsement","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v15","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible","zh":"相容的","pos":"adjective","example":"Appreciation and caution are compatible attitudes.","exampleZh":"感激与谨慎可以相容。","collocation":"compatible with","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v16","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why can the organiser welcome the sponsor’s offer without endorsing expansion?","answer":"The money funds a performance rather than the coordination constraint.","options":["The sponsor requires all committee members to work fewer shifts.","The offer confirms that last year’s residents opposed the festival.","The money funds a performance rather than the coordination constraint."],"explanation":"赞助付额外演出，不付已指出的协调工作；感激与有条件支持可并存。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-05:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"Which interpretation of “overdue” consultation is best supported by the briefing?","answer":"Residents should have been consulted before plans became commitments.","options":["Consultation should be postponed until all performances are completed.","Residents should have been consulted before plans became commitments.","Residents had already agreed to every part of the larger programme."],"explanation":"overdue表示早该进行，和先协商再公开承诺的立场相符，并非已有同意。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-05:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What does the sponsor’s money cover?","answer":"Another performance","options":["Every committee shift","Another performance","Volunteer coordination"],"explanation":"赞助仅覆盖额外演出。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-05:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What does “overdue” imply here?","answer":"Consultation should have happened earlier","options":["Consultation should have happened earlier","Consultation is no longer allowed","Residents have all rejected the event"],"explanation":"overdue含义是早该进行。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-05:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What does Helen’s response suggest?","answer":"Interest subject to unresolved practical checks","options":["A belief that photographs lack historical value","Interest subject to unresolved practical checks","Immediate unconditional acceptance"],"explanation":"她欢迎潜在价值，但以权利、保护与容量核查为条件。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-05:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"Why mention honest limits?","answer":"To avoid a flattering promise the museum cannot keep","options":["To avoid a flattering promise the museum cannot keep","To accuse the donor of deliberate deception","To prove that conservation is unnecessary"],"explanation":"诚实限制比接受后不妥存放更负责任。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-05:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What limitation is acknowledged about the first exhibition?","answer":"Material mainly came from three organised family archives","options":["Material mainly came from three organised family archives","Nobody contributed any material","It included only recent residents"],"explanation":"三家档案方便可得，覆盖不完整。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-05:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What does the speaker prefer if consent cannot be organised?","answer":"Postponing the collection","options":["Recording everyone without explanation","Publishing all recordings automatically","Postponing the collection"],"explanation":"宁可延期，也不在用途不清时收集。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-05:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an interview analysis. Separate the organiser’s explicit statements from two supported inferences, and explain one interpretation the evidence does not justify. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “An organiser reflects on a crowded festival”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["采访者","组织者","采访分析员"],"goal":"区分明确表态与可支持的推断，纠正一个过强标签。","twist":"采访者把有条件支持压缩成“反对”，须在30秒内准确澄清。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"十天后读陌生访谈，找出三种态度的语言证据。","writingPrompt":"写220词分析，明确哪些解释只是可能而非确定。","speakingPrompt":"接受带有错误前提的追问，先纠正前提再回答。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A curator speaks about an unexpected donation”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A curator speaks about an unexpected donation” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-06","level":"C1","title":"多人工作会议与共识边界","chapter":47,"focus":"区分提议、修正、保留和共识","sourceA":{"title":"A team negotiates a shared workspace — extended case review","text":"Four members of a fictional cooperative discuss converting a storeroom into a shared workspace. The finance coordinator favours a small pilot, the designer wants flexible furniture, the access representative raises doorway clearance, and the caretaker explains maintenance constraints. Their first preferences differ, but the conversation is not a competition to find a single winning speaker. The aim is to identify a proposal everyone can accept without pretending that every concern has disappeared.\n\nA plan can look acceptable on a drawing while leaving a practical problem unresolved. For example, movable furniture still needs a safe storage position. The manager should ask participants to test the proposed arrangement in the room itself and report whether access, cleaning and rehearsal can coexist.\n\nThe finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion. The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks. The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed. The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical. Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently. The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.\n\nI take your point about cost, but access is also essential. Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks? That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements. The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements. Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent. Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action.\n\nThe cooperative tested the proposed furniture in the storeroom. A clear doorway could be maintained with four desks, but the first six-desk arrangement obstructed access to the cleaning cupboard. The designer accepted four desks for the pilot while reserving the possibility of a different six-desk layout later. The caretaker asked for fifteen minutes at the end of each day to reset the room. The finance coordinator agreed to fund movable desks only if they could be used elsewhere when the pilot ended. The access representative supported the trial after checking the route in person. These are conditional agreements, not evidence that everyone prefers the same final design."},"sourceB":{"title":"A department negotiates presentation time","text":"A university department planned a public research evening with five presentations. The chair suggested twelve minutes for each speaker, followed by a joint discussion. A postgraduate representative wanted shorter presentations so that visitors could ask questions after every topic. A senior researcher objected that complex work could not be explained responsibly in six minutes. Their first exchange treated duration as if it were the only issue.\n\nThe outreach coordinator asked what audiences were expected to understand. The senior researcher said that listeners needed enough context to avoid mistaking an early result for an established conclusion. The postgraduate representative said that visitors often forgot questions while waiting for a long discussion. These concerns were not necessarily incompatible. The group considered a nine-minute presentation followed by three minutes of clarification, with broader debate saved for the end.\n\nThe technician pointed out that changing speakers also took time. With five presentations, introductions and transitions would add approximately ten minutes. The original ninety-minute booking therefore left little room for a final discussion. Rather than silently exceed the venue slot, the chair proposed four presentations and a separate online feature for the fifth team. That team would decide whether the alternative met its needs; the chair would not claim its consent before consultation.\n\nThe minutes recorded a provisional programme and named the people responsible for checking it. The outreach coordinator would test the explanation level with two non-specialist readers. The technician would confirm the transition schedule. The fifth team would reply within three working days. Nobody had approved a permanent rule limiting every departmental talk to nine minutes. The agreement concerned this event and its constraints. The group had progressed by identifying the interests behind positions and translating them into a bounded arrangement, while leaving one participant's decision genuinely open. The chair would circulate the corrected minutes for confirmation."},"listening":{"teaching":"Here is the outcome of the workspace layout test. Six desks did not fit in the first arrangement because the cleaning cupboard became inaccessible. Four desks allowed the doorway and cupboard route to remain clear. The designer accepts four for the pilot, but still wants to explore another six-desk plan later. Please do not record that as a permanent rejection of six desks. The caretaker also needs fifteen minutes at the end of the day to return the furniture to its agreed position. That time must appear in the timetable. The finance coordinator will fund movable desks provided they can be used elsewhere if the pilot stops. This is important because the budget cannot support equipment with no alternative use. The access representative has now walked the route and supports the trial under the four-desk layout. We therefore have enough agreement to proceed with a limited test, not unanimous preference for one final design. In the minutes, record each condition beside the action it affects. Then name who will check the daily reset and who will arrange the review. A summary saying everyone approved the workspace would omit the main work of the discussion. The next meeting should examine how the arrangement functions, rather than assume that the drawing alone settled every practical concern.","test":"Before we leave, I want to summarise the reading-circle discussion. We will test a shorter meeting on the second Thursday of next month, beginning at seven and ending at eight fifteen. The usual two-hour format remains for the other meetings. Nora will choose a short article and send it a week ahead. We did not agree that members must read a whole book for this trial. Jamal offered to lead the first discussion, provided another person keeps time. Priya will do that, and she will invite a response from anyone who has not spoken rather than simply follow the order of raised hands. People may pass if they prefer not to contribute. The purpose is to see whether a shorter session makes participation easier, not to require everyone to speak for the same number of minutes. We will ask for comments afterwards and decide whether another trial is worthwhile. Two members have not yet replied about the date, so please do not say that the whole group has accepted it. I will contact them tomorrow. If the date excludes them, we may need a different test date. That question remains open. Please tell me privately if the proposed time creates an access difficulty."},"vocab":[{"head":"pilot","zh":"试点","pos":"noun","example":"The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","exampleZh":"财务协调员支持试点，而非全面改造。","collocation":"a limited pilot","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v1","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"builds on","zh":"在……基础上发展","pos":"phrase","example":"The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","exampleZh":"设计师在此基础上提出可移动书桌。","collocation":"builds on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v2","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unobstructed","zh":"无阻挡的","pos":"adjective","example":"The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","exampleZh":"无障碍代表询问门口是否保持畅通。","collocation":"remain unobstructed","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v3","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided that","zh":"前提是","pos":"phrase","example":"The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical.","exampleZh":"管理员接受布局，前提是清洁仍然方便。","collocation":"provided that","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v4","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permanently","zh":"永久地","pos":"adverb","example":"Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently.","exampleZh":"没有人同意永久拆除储物架。","collocation":"remove permanently","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v5","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"lasting commitment","zh":"长期承诺","pos":"phrase","example":"The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.","exampleZh":"小组区分临时安排与长期承诺。","collocation":"lasting commitment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v6","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"take your point","zh":"理解你的观点","pos":"phrase","example":"I take your point about cost, but access is also essential.","exampleZh":"我理解你关于费用的观点，但通行同样必要。","collocation":"take your point","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v7","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"combine","zh":"结合","pos":"verb","example":"Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks?","exampleZh":"我们能否把试点与六周后复查结合？","collocation":"combine a trial with review","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v8","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"address","zh":"处理","pos":"verb","example":"That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements.","exampleZh":"这能解决维护问题，同时不排除后续改善。","collocation":"address a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v9","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unresolved","zh":"未解决的","pos":"adjective","example":"The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements.","exampleZh":"会议纪要应同时列明未解决问题和共识。","collocation":"unresolved questions","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v10","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consent","zh":"同意","pos":"noun","example":"Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent.","exampleZh":"沉默不应自动被记录为同意。","collocation":"record consent","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v11","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"carry out","zh":"执行","pos":"phrase","example":"Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action.","exampleZh":"结束前，主持人核实每项行动的负责人。","collocation":"carry out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v12","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"The provisional layout will be tested before a final decision.","exampleZh":"暂定布局将在最终决定前测试。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v13","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consensus","zh":"共识","pos":"noun","example":"Conditional consensus does not mean identical preferences.","exampleZh":"有条件共识不等于偏好相同。","collocation":"reach consensus","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v14","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transition","zh":"转换","pos":"noun","example":"The schedule must allow time for the transition between activities.","exampleZh":"时间表须留出活动转换时间。","collocation":"allow for transitions","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v15","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ratify","zh":"正式批准","pos":"verb","example":"The group cannot ratify an arrangement on behalf of an absent participant.","exampleZh":"小组不能代缺席者正式批准安排。","collocation":"ratify an agreement","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v16","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"What does the designer’s acceptance of four desks actually commit the designer to?","answer":"A four-desk pilot while other layouts remain open for later study.","options":["A six-desk launch once the cleaning cupboard is moved elsewhere.","A four-desk pilot while other layouts remain open for later study.","A permanent four-desk limit regardless of the pilot’s results."],"explanation":"设计者接受四桌试点，同时保留探索另一种六桌布局，不是永久否决六桌。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-06:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"Which condition is attached to the finance coordinator’s support?","answer":"The movable desks must remain useful if the pilot ends.","options":["The movable desks must remain useful if the pilot ends.","The daily reset must be completed without caretaker time.","The access representative must accept the original six-desk drawing."],"explanation":"拨款要求桌子可在试点结束后作别用；复位十五分钟仍须列入安排。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-06:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"Why did the first six-desk arrangement fail?","answer":"It blocked access to the cleaning cupboard","options":["It blocked access to the cleaning cupboard","It lacked any doorway","It used fixed desks only"],"explanation":"清洁柜通道受阻。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-06:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What condition accompanies funding?","answer":"The desks must have another possible use","options":["The pilot must become permanent","Six desks must be used immediately","The desks must have another possible use"],"explanation":"预算要求可作其他用途。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-06:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What underlying concern did the senior researcher identify?","answer":"Providing enough context to avoid overstating early results","options":["Providing enough context to avoid overstating early results","Having the longest talk regardless of purpose","Eliminating all audience questions"],"explanation":"核心担忧是背景不足导致误解结果。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-06:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What remained genuinely undecided?","answer":"Whether the fifth team accepted an online alternative","options":["Whether transitions consume time","Whether the venue had any limit","Whether the fifth team accepted an online alternative"],"explanation":"第五组尚需自行确认替代安排。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-06:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What material is required for the shorter trial?","answer":"A short article","options":["A complete book","No reading at all","A short article"],"explanation":"Nora选短文并提前发。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-06:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"Why is the date still provisional?","answer":"Two members have not replied","options":["Nobody offered to lead","Two members have not replied","The room has already been demolished"],"explanation":"两人未回复，可能需要另择日期。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-06:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write minutes and a short recommendation for the workspace discussion. Preserve each person’s concern and distinguish agreed actions from unresolved decisions. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A team negotiates a shared workspace”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["会议主持","设计代表","维护代表","未出席成员代理"],"goal":"形成准确纪要，标明共识、条件、负责人和未决事项。","twist":"有人要求当晚发布“全票通过”，但一位相关成员尚未确认。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生会议纪要，标出提议、临时共识与最终决定。","writingPrompt":"根据新的四方争议写220词纪要，避免把沉默当同意。","speakingPrompt":"主持三分钟讨论并在结束时请各方纠正总结。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A department negotiates presentation time”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A department negotiates presentation time” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-07","level":"C1","title":"评论阅读与公共价值","chapter":49,"focus":"识别作者立场并评价支持力度","sourceA":{"title":"Who owns the public square? — extended case review","text":"A fictional local commentary examines a plan to lease part of a public square to a café. The author recognises that seating and maintenance could improve the space, but questions whether commercial activity should become the default test of public value. Readers should follow the argument beyond the opening contrast: the article considers practical arrangements as well as principles. Its purpose is to invite a better agreement rather than to reject every partnership with a business.\n\nThe central question is whether people retain a right to occupy the square without buying anything. A lease could address that explicitly through marked public seating and enforceable hours. The author asks readers to consider these terms, rather than assume that every improvement funded by a business necessarily benefits every user.\n\nThe author concedes that café income could support maintenance. Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself. The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource. A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption. The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere. The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.\n\nPublic value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent. Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response. The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it. I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise. The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use. A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason.\n\nA draft café lease would reserve twenty seats for customers and leave sixteen publicly available. However, the public seats were initially all in the unshaded part of the square. The author argues that counting seats alone would miss a meaningful difference in access during summer. The business offers to maintain planting and keep public toilets open until eight. Residents welcome these benefits but ask who would enforce the public seating condition if staff discouraged non-customers. The council is therefore considering a published complaint route and a review after one season, rather than relying entirely on the operator's description of its intentions."},"sourceB":{"title":"The argument for a slower shopping street","text":"A proposal to reduce traffic speed on Westbridge High Street has produced two apparently opposing slogans: protect local trade and protect public space. Both express legitimate interests, but neither tells the council exactly what it should do. A shopping street is a place to reach by car, bus, bicycle or foot, as well as a place where people spend time. Treating one function as the entire purpose can make the debate more dramatic and less useful.\n\nRetailers fear that customers will find access inconvenient if the street is redesigned. That concern deserves examination, particularly for customers carrying heavy goods or needing an accessible drop-off point. Yet a claim that every parking space creates a fixed amount of spending would require evidence. The current consultation contains individual experiences, not a reliable calculation of lost sales. Equally, supporters of redesign cannot assume that making a street look attractive will automatically help every business.\n\nThe proposed plan lowers the speed limit, widens two crossing points and retains timed loading bays. It does not close the street to all vehicles. An early leaflet used the term traffic-free, causing both unnecessary alarm and unrealistic expectations. Correcting that phrase helped reveal the actual decisions: how buses move through the street, where deliveries stop and whether pedestrians can cross safely.\n\nThe strongest case for the plan is not that one group has more moral value than another. It is that the existing arrangement makes some ordinary activities unnecessarily difficult, and targeted changes may improve them without removing all access. The council should measure journey times, crossing use, delivery problems and the experience of people with mobility limitations. Sales data could inform the review if businesses choose to provide it under suitable privacy arrangements. A fair trial would specify what evidence might justify adjustment, rather than invite each side to interpret every event as proof that it was right from the beginning."},"listening":{"teaching":"In the public-square commentary, the new lease figures appear helpful at first: twenty customer seats and sixteen public seats. But all sixteen public seats were initially in the unshaded area. In summer, that could make the formal right to sit less useful for some visitors. The author is asking us to evaluate the quality and location of access, not merely count furniture. The café offers maintenance of planting and toilet opening until eight. Those are genuine potential benefits, and a fair response should acknowledge them. The next question is enforcement. If staff discourage a non-customer from using public seating, who can correct the problem? A general statement of good intentions may not be enough. The council is considering a published complaint route and a seasonal review. When you write your response, reconstruct the strongest argument before criticising it. Do not say that the author opposes every commercial partnership; the concern is about the terms of this one. You might support the lease if shaded public seating and enforceable access conditions are included. Alternatively, you might argue that those safeguards remain inadequate. Either position needs to explain how the actual arrangement affects users. A prediction about noise or spending should be marked as a prediction unless supporting evidence is supplied.","test":"The council is inviting comments on the proposed river path, and I want to clarify what is being consulted on. The current proposal links the footbridge to the railway station along the northern bank. It does not include the southern bank, which remains privately owned. The path would be shared by walkers and cyclists, with a lower-speed section near the playground. Some residents have described it as a cycle-only route, but that is incorrect. We are particularly interested in views about the two narrow sections and access for people using mobility aids. You can comment online or collect a paper form from the library. Responses close on the eighteenth of March. This is not a vote in which the most repeated sentence automatically wins. The planning team will consider practical concerns and explain how they influenced the design. No construction date has been approved. If the design proceeds, a separate funding decision will be needed. Please identify the location you are discussing, since a concern about one entrance may not apply to the whole route. We will publish a summary without personal names, although you may choose to be contacted for clarification. The library will retain paper copies of the consultation documents."},"vocab":[{"head":"concedes","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","exampleZh":"作者承认咖啡馆收入可能支持维护。","collocation":"concedes a potential benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v1","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exclusive","zh":"排他的","pos":"adjective","example":"Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","exampleZh":"她反对的是排他控制，而非咖啡本身。","collocation":"exclusive control","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v2","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frame","zh":"界定","pos":"verb","example":"The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","exampleZh":"开篇对比把广场界定为共享资源。","collocation":"frame an argument","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v3","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rhetorical question","zh":"修辞问句","pos":"phrase","example":"A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption.","exampleZh":"修辞问句促使读者重新考虑假设。","collocation":"rhetorical question","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v4","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"illustrate","zh":"说明","pos":"verb","example":"The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere.","exampleZh":"例子说明风险，却不能证明它处处发生。","collocation":"illustrate a risk","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v5","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"non-customers","zh":"非顾客","pos":"noun","example":"The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.","exampleZh":"建议中的协议会保障非顾客的座位。","collocation":"seating for non-customers","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v6","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"be reduced to","zh":"被简化为","pos":"phrase","example":"Public value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent.","exampleZh":"公共价值不能简化为消费金额。","collocation":"be reduced to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v7","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"nevertheless","zh":"尽管如此","pos":"adverb","example":"Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response.","exampleZh":"尽管如此，资金限制仍需实际回应。","collocation":"nevertheless, constraints remain","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v8","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualifies","zh":"限定","pos":"verb","example":"The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it.","exampleZh":"结论限定开头的批评，而非放弃批评。","collocation":"qualifies a recommendation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v9","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prediction","zh":"预测","pos":"noun","example":"I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise.","exampleZh":"我认为可及性论点强于噪声预测。","collocation":"an unsupported prediction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v10","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"expected use","zh":"预计使用情况","pos":"phrase","example":"The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use.","exampleZh":"该预测需要营业时间、布局和预计使用情况的证据。","collocation":"expected use","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v11","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"engage with","zh":"认真回应","pos":"phrase","example":"A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason.","exampleZh":"公允回应应处理作者最有力的理由。","collocation":"engage with","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v12","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enforceable","zh":"可执行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Public access requires enforceable conditions, not only goodwill.","exampleZh":"公众通行需要可执行条件，而非只有善意。","collocation":"enforceable conditions","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v13","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"amenity","zh":"公共便利设施","pos":"noun","example":"Shade is an amenity whose location affects its usefulness.","exampleZh":"树荫是一项价值受位置影响的公共便利。","collocation":"a public amenity","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v14","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"displace","zh":"挤占","pos":"verb","example":"Commercial seating should not displace essential public use.","exampleZh":"商业座位不应挤占必要公共使用。","collocation":"displace public use","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v15","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate","zh":"适度相称的","pos":"adjective","example":"A proportionate response addresses the identified risk.","exampleZh":"相称回应针对已识别风险。","collocation":"a proportionate response","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v16","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why does the author question a lease that retains sixteen public seats?","answer":"Their unshaded location may reduce meaningful summer access.","options":["Their unshaded location may reduce meaningful summer access.","Their number is greater than the customer seating allocation.","Their maintenance is excluded from the café’s proposed duties."],"explanation":"十六个公共座位最初全在无阴处，数量不体现夏日使用条件；顾客位为二十。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-07:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What problem is the published complaint route intended to address?","answer":"Whether public seating rights can be enforced in everyday use.","options":["Whether the café can guarantee identical spending by every visitor.","Whether the council can remove every commercial activity from the square.","Whether public seating rights can be enforced in everyday use."],"explanation":"若员工阻止非顾客使用公共座位，需要可执行的纠正途径，而非仅有意向说明。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-07:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"Why was the seating count insufficient?","answer":"Public seats were in the unshaded area","options":["There were no public seats","All seats had identical conditions","Public seats were in the unshaded area"],"explanation":"数量不能体现夏日使用质量差异。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-07:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What safeguard is being considered?","answer":"A published complaint route","options":["Removal of public toilets","A published complaint route","A ban on every café"],"explanation":"投诉渠道和季节复核可使条件可执行。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-07:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What did the misleading leaflet claim?","answer":"That the street would be traffic-free","options":["That loading bays would remain","That the speed limit would fall","That the street would be traffic-free"],"explanation":"traffic-free与保留车辆通行不符。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-07:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What is the author’s strongest justification?","answer":"Targeted changes may improve difficult ordinary activities","options":["One transport group is morally superior","Targeted changes may improve difficult ordinary activities","Every parking space has a known spending value"],"explanation":"作者支持按具体活动和可测结果评判。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-07:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"Which bank is in the current proposal?","answer":"The northern bank","options":["Only the southern bank","The northern bank","Both banks automatically"],"explanation":"方案仅北岸。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-07:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"How will consultation responses be used?","answer":"Practical concerns will inform and be explained in the design","options":["Practical concerns will inform and be explained in the design","Repeated slogans determine a simple vote","They approve construction funding directly"],"explanation":"协商非简单投票，也不直接批准资金。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-07:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a critical response to the square commentary. Reconstruct the strongest argument, evaluate its evidence and propose a condition under which a café partnership could be acceptable. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Who owns the public square?”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["评论作者","商户","无障碍使用者","市政人员"],"goal":"形成兼顾收益和可执行公共使用权的租赁条件。","twist":"商户同意座位数量但拒绝由外部机构处理投诉。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"一周后读陌生公共议题评论，重建作者最强理由。","writingPrompt":"对一项新公共合作方案写220词回应，承认收益并给出条件。","speakingPrompt":"用两分钟回应不准确的二元标签，再提出可测的折中。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “The argument for a slower shopping street”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “The argument for a slower shopping street” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-08","level":"C1","title":"科学机制与实测证据","chapter":50,"focus":"解释因果链并区分相关与因果","sourceA":{"title":"Why stored rainwater needs monitoring — extended case review","text":"An original fictional science briefing explains a school rainwater project. The school collects roof runoff for garden irrigation, not for drinking. The article follows the path from roof to storage tank and identifies where leaves, sediment and sunlight could affect water quality. Its educational aim is to connect a physical mechanism with an appropriate monitoring plan. No health claim is made about untested water, and the case should not be treated as engineering advice.\n\nMonitoring should follow the route the water actually takes, rather than rely on its appearance at one outlet. The school can use the project to explain filtration, storage and measurement limits. Any description of results must remain within the intended irrigation use and must not imply that visual clarity establishes safety.\n\nLeaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank. Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear. An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water. A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect. The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water. The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.\n\nA single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank. Repeated measurements reveal variation over time. The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage. We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested. The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed. Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified.\n\nThe school recorded tank readings after four rainfall events. A sample taken immediately after heavy rain looked clearer than one taken after three dry weeks, but the team had not standardised the sampling depth. Pupils initially attributed the difference entirely to time in storage. The teacher asked them to consider whether location in the tank could also affect the result. The next plan specifies a consistent sampling point and records the date, recent rainfall and cleaning activity. A maintenance volunteer also noted that the leaf screen had been cleaned between two readings. That intervention must be recorded rather than treated as irrelevant background."},"sourceB":{"title":"Testing the effect of classroom ventilation","text":"A college installed monitors in three classrooms to examine how indoor carbon dioxide levels changed during teaching. The facilities team wanted to understand ventilation patterns, not diagnose illness or judge individual teachers. Each monitor recorded a reading every five minutes. Classroom A held thirty students, classroom B held eighteen and classroom C was used irregularly for tutorials. Comparing the highest reading from each room without considering occupancy would therefore be misleading.\n\nDuring the first week, classroom A often showed a rapid increase near the end of a lesson. Staff opened a high window during the second week and observed lower readings on several days. A newsletter draft announced that the window change had solved the air-quality problem. The project leader asked for a more limited statement. Weather, lesson duration and attendance had also changed, and one monitor had been moved from a shelf to a wall position. The observation was useful, but the comparison did not isolate a single cause.\n\nFor the next phase, the team would keep monitor positions constant, record attendance and note when doors and windows were opened. The report would distinguish room conditions from direct evidence about health or concentration. Students' comments about feeling less sleepy might be worth collecting, but they would not establish a physiological mechanism on their own. Participation in any questionnaire would be optional and separate from normal classroom access.\n\nA lecturer worried that these qualifications would make the project sound indecisive. The leader replied that the practical action and the scientific claim could have different levels of certainty. Opening a suitable window might be a sensible immediate measure even while the explanation was being investigated. The important point was to avoid representing a small observational exercise as proof of a universal effect. Better records would support a more informative discussion about each room and the constraints affecting its use."},"listening":{"teaching":"The rainwater observations give us an opportunity to examine the difference between a pattern and an explanation. After four rainfall events, pupils compared water taken following heavy rain with water sampled after three dry weeks. The later sample looked less clear. It is tempting to say that storage time caused the difference, but the depth of sampling had not been standardised. Water from different parts of the tank may not contain the same amount of sediment. We must also note that a volunteer cleaned the leaf screen between two readings. That is an intervention, not an irrelevant detail. In the next round, use one agreed sampling point, record the date and recent rainfall, and note cleaning activity. The aim is not to keep measuring until a preferred explanation appears. It is to make competing explanations easier to examine. Visual clarity remains only one observation. Do not extend the finding into a claim that the water is suitable for drinking. The project is about irrigation and scientific reasoning. In your account, trace the physical route from roof to screen to storage, then identify which part of that route each observation concerns. A good diagram can show where measurements were taken and where information is still missing.","test":"For the seed-growth project, please use the revised recording sheet from Monday onwards. We are comparing two soil mixtures, not testing different watering schedules. Each tray should receive the same measured volume of water unless a problem is reported to the supervisor. There are twelve trays in total, six for each mixture, and their positions on the bench will be rotated twice a week to reduce the effect of uneven light. Measure shoot height from the soil surface to the highest living point, using the same method each time. Do not remove a weak plant simply because it would lower the average. Record its condition and ask before making any change. If a tray is accidentally knocked over, mark the event clearly rather than inventing a replacement measurement. Photographs can support your notes but do not replace the numerical record. We will discuss the findings after three weeks. A difference between these trays would justify further investigation; it would not establish that one mixture is best for every species or climate. Please keep that limit in mind when preparing your poster, and distinguish the result you observed from the explanation you think may account for it. Use the same ruler throughout the project."},"vocab":[{"head":"intercepted","zh":"被拦截","pos":"verb","example":"Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","exampleZh":"水进入储水箱前，树叶会被拦截。","collocation":"leaves are intercepted","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v1","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sediment","zh":"沉积物","pos":"noun","example":"Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","exampleZh":"即使流入的水看起来清澈，沉积物仍可能累积。","collocation":"sediment accumulation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v2","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opaque","zh":"不透明的","pos":"adjective","example":"An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","exampleZh":"不透明盖子限制照入水中的光。","collocation":"an opaque cover","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v3","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mechanism","zh":"机制","pos":"noun","example":"A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect.","exampleZh":"合理机制必须与测得的效果区分。","collocation":"a plausible mechanism","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v4","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"irrigation","zh":"灌溉","pos":"noun","example":"The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water.","exampleZh":"水箱供应灌溉水，而非饮用水。","collocation":"irrigation water","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v5","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"observations","zh":"观察结果","pos":"noun","example":"The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.","exampleZh":"团队记录降雨，因为天气影响观察结果。","collocation":"repeated observations","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v6","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"throughout","zh":"遍及","pos":"preposition","example":"A single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank.","exampleZh":"单个清澈样本无法证明整个水箱的水质。","collocation":"throughout the tank","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v7","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"variation","zh":"变化","pos":"noun","example":"Repeated measurements reveal variation over time.","exampleZh":"重复测量揭示随时间发生的变化。","collocation":"variation over time","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v8","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sequence","zh":"顺序","pos":"noun","example":"The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage.","exampleZh":"解释按从收集到储存的顺序展开。","collocation":"a physical sequence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v9","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"causal chain","zh":"因果链","pos":"phrase","example":"We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested.","exampleZh":"我们应明确因果链中的哪个环节接受了测试。","collocation":"causal chain","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v10","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limited to","zh":"限于","pos":"phrase","example":"The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed.","exampleZh":"结论仍限于实际观察的条件。","collocation":"limited to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v11","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"justified","zh":"有依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified.","exampleZh":"在更广泛主张成立前，还需进一步测试。","collocation":"a justified conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v12","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"standardise","zh":"统一标准","pos":"verb","example":"We must standardise the sampling depth before comparing readings.","exampleZh":"比较读数前须统一取样深度。","collocation":"standardise a procedure","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v13","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confound","zh":"混淆因果判断","pos":"verb","example":"Unrecorded cleaning may confound the interpretation.","exampleZh":"未记录清洁可能混淆解释。","collocation":"confound the interpretation","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v14","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sedimentation","zh":"沉降","pos":"noun","example":"Sedimentation may affect where particles collect.","exampleZh":"沉降可能影响颗粒聚集位置。","collocation":"sedimentation in storage","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v15","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replicable","zh":"可重复检验的","pos":"adjective","example":"A clear method makes the observations more replicable.","exampleZh":"明确方法使观察更易重复检验。","collocation":"a replicable method","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v16","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why is the difference between the two water samples not attributed solely to storage time?","answer":"Sampling depth varied and cleaning occurred between some readings.","options":["Rainfall and sampling dates were deliberately identical throughout.","The leaf screen was known to prevent all sediment entering storage.","Sampling depth varied and cleaning occurred between some readings."],"explanation":"取样深度未统一，且两读数间清洁过筛网，都可能影响观察，不能全归储存时长。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-08:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What does the revised measurement plan primarily improve?","answer":"Comparability by fixing a sampling point and recording interventions.","options":["Coverage by treating a single sample as typical of the whole tank.","Comparability by fixing a sampling point and recording interventions.","Safety certification by replacing tests with visual inspection."],"explanation":"统一取样点并记录降雨、日期、清洁使比较更清楚；不能由清澈认证饮用安全。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-08:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What had not been standardised?","answer":"Sampling depth","options":["The number of roofs","Sampling depth","The school’s name"],"explanation":"取样深度不统一。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-08:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"Why record screen cleaning?","answer":"It may affect the observations","options":["It may affect the observations","It proves storage time has no effect","It replaces every measurement"],"explanation":"清洁是可能影响结果的干预。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-08:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why was the first classroom comparison misleading?","answer":"Occupancy and use differed across rooms","options":["No monitor collected any readings","Occupancy and use differed across rooms","All rooms had identical numbers of students"],"explanation":"不同人数和使用模式影响可比性。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-08:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What distinction did the project leader make?","answer":"Practical action may be sensible before a universal causal claim is justified","options":["Practical action may be sensible before a universal causal claim is justified","No action is possible without perfect certainty","Student comments directly prove a mechanism"],"explanation":"行动理由与科学因果断言的确定程度可不同。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-08:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What is the intended comparison?","answer":"Two soil mixtures","options":["Two soil mixtures","Different watering schedules","Every climate worldwide"],"explanation":"比较两种土壤，水量控制一致。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-08:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What should happen after a tray is knocked over?","answer":"Record the event rather than invent data","options":["Replace the measurement with a preferred average","Hide the tray","Record the event rather than invent data"],"explanation":"事故须如实标注，不补造数据。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-08:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an explanatory article about the rainwater case. Trace a mechanism, identify what should be measured and distinguish observed evidence from a possible benefit. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Why stored rainwater needs monitoring”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["科学记录员","项目主管","宣传负责人"],"goal":"保留可行动建议，同时把因果和适用范围写准确。","twist":"宣传负责人想用“证明安全”，但项目只测视觉清澈度。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生实验简报，标记变量、干预、测量与结论范围。","writingPrompt":"写220词科学说明，明确一项可能混杂因素及改进记录方法。","speakingPrompt":"向非专业听众解释为什么合理机制仍需测量支持。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Testing the effect of classroom ventilation”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Testing the effect of classroom ventilation” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-09","level":"C1","title":"来源口径与新闻核查","chapter":51,"focus":"比较范围、方法、指标和立场","sourceA":{"title":"Two ways to describe a cycle route — extended case review","text":"A fictional transport team is preparing a public briefing on a protected cycle route. It has a rider survey and a set of junction observations. The sources answer different questions: the survey describes reported experience, while the observations record behaviour at selected locations and times. Neither source is useless, but neither should be asked to establish everything. The briefing needs to show what can be compared fairly and where uncertainty remains.\n\nThe team should begin by writing the precise question above each result. A statement about comfort, a count of riders and an observation of turning behaviour can all be valuable without measuring the same thing. Understanding that difference prevents an attractive headline from replacing a careful account of the evidence.\n\nThe rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk. Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week. Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators. A source may be accurate within a narrow scope. Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction. The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.\n\nAn interested organisation can still provide useful data. Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal. The two sources overlap without being interchangeable. Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use. We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement. A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries.\n\nThe transport team has now compared the survey and observation schedules. The survey received 210 responses, of which 156 came from people who already cycled at least weekly. The observations covered three junctions between seven and nine on two weekday mornings. Neither source adequately represents people deciding whether to start cycling after dark. A local shopkeeper offered a list of delivery conflicts but had recorded only incidents that disrupted the business. The team will treat this as evidence of specific operational problems, not as a complete count of conflicts. Its public briefing will identify each source's population, timing and purpose before combining findings."},"sourceB":{"title":"Two accounts of a neighbourhood food scheme","text":"A newspaper reported that a neighbourhood food scheme had doubled its reach. The operator said it distributed 1,200 parcels in April, compared with 600 in January. A volunteer group responded that only a small number of additional households had joined. The claims seemed inconsistent until the reporting units were examined. Parcels were distributed weekly in April but only twice during January, and some households received more than one parcel in a week.\n\nThe operator had not invented its figures. They accurately counted parcels leaving the depot. However, the word reach suggested something about the number of people or households supported, which those figures alone could not establish. The volunteer group held a household register, but that register included people who had signed up and never collected anything. Its total was therefore not a direct measure of actual use either. Both organisations needed clearer labels rather than an argument over which number sounded more impressive.\n\nA joint audit proposed reporting parcels, distinct collecting households and distribution days separately. Where households shared a collection, volunteers would note the arrangement without demanding intrusive personal information. The audit also identified a gap: neither dataset described people who needed support but found collection times impossible. A short consultation through trusted community contacts could explore that issue, although voluntary accounts would not immediately establish its prevalence.\n\nThe newspaper corrected the headline to describe an increase in parcel distribution. It explained the difference between volume and coverage in the article. The operator welcomed the correction but worried that readers might assume wrongdoing. The editor made clear that the issue was interpretation, not evidence of fabricated records. This distinction mattered for public trust. Accurate scrutiny should identify what a source measures, what a headline implies and what remains unknown, rather than turn every mismatch into an allegation of dishonesty."},"listening":{"teaching":"Before combining the cycle-route evidence, write down the population and time period for each source. The survey has 210 responses, and 156 respondents already cycle at least once a week. That is useful information about existing riders, but it does not fully represent people who are considering starting. The observations cover three junctions between seven and nine on two weekday mornings. They can describe behaviour at those locations and times, not the entire week. We also have a shopkeeper's record of delivery conflicts. The record contains incidents that disrupted the shop, so it was created for a different purpose from the junction observation. We should investigate those incidents without pretending that the list is a complete count. The major remaining question concerns use after dark. Neither the survey profile nor the morning observation answers it well. In the public briefing, put each claim beside its supporting source. If sources disagree, first check definitions and coverage. Do not assume that one must be dishonest. A narrower source can be accurate within its scope. Your recommendation should explain which information can be combined and which gap requires a new check. That is more informative than averaging incompatible figures to produce a reassuring single result.","test":"The local employment office has asked us to clarify this month's training figures. The poster says that 75 people completed workshops, but this is actually a count of workshop attendances. A person attending three different workshops appears three times. The separate registration system lists 48 people who attended at least one session. We will correct the poster before printing more copies. This is a reporting error, not a suggestion that the sessions did not take place. The office wants to know whether the programme is reaching new jobseekers, so the next report will show both total attendances and distinct participants. We will also distinguish first-time users from returning participants where consent allows. Completion of a workshop is not evidence that a person subsequently found work. Employment outcomes require a different follow-up, and some participants may not be available to answer. Please do not fill missing responses with assumptions. If you cannot contact someone, record that the outcome is unknown. The revised figures are due by Thursday, but accuracy takes priority over producing a flattering increase. Contact the data coordinator if the two systems cannot be reconciled, and retain the original entries so that the correction can be traced. Please check the unit before approving any new poster."},"vocab":[{"head":"reported experience","zh":"自报体验","pos":"phrase","example":"The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","exampleZh":"骑行者调查衡量自报体验，而非碰撞风险。","collocation":"reported experience","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v1","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selected","zh":"选定的","pos":"adjective","example":"Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","exampleZh":"路口观察覆盖选定时段，而非整周。","collocation":"selected observation hours","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v2","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"denominator","zh":"分母","pos":"noun","example":"Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","exampleZh":"比较百分比前，我们需要查看分母。","collocation":"inspect the denominator","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v3","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scope","zh":"范围","pos":"noun","example":"A source may be accurate within a narrow scope.","exampleZh":"来源在狭窄范围内也可能准确。","collocation":"within a narrow scope","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v4","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradiction","zh":"矛盾","pos":"noun","example":"Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction.","exampleZh":"不同定义可能造成表面矛盾。","collocation":"an apparent contradiction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v5","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归于","pos":"verb","example":"The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.","exampleZh":"团队应将每项主张归于相应证据。","collocation":"attribute a claim to evidence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v6","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interested organisation","zh":"有利益关系的组织","pos":"phrase","example":"An interested organisation can still provide useful data.","exampleZh":"有利益关系的组织仍可提供有用数据。","collocation":"interested organisation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v7","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scrutiny","zh":"仔细审查","pos":"noun","example":"Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal.","exampleZh":"其角色是审查理由，而非自动否定的理由。","collocation":"careful scrutiny","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v8","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interchangeable","zh":"可互换的","pos":"adjective","example":"The two sources overlap without being interchangeable.","exampleZh":"两个来源有交集，但不能互换。","collocation":"not interchangeable","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v9","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit","zh":"限制","pos":"verb","example":"Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use.","exampleZh":"缺少晚间观察限制了对夜间使用的结论。","collocation":"limit the conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v10","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"forcing agreement","zh":"强求一致","pos":"phrase","example":"We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement.","exampleZh":"我们应报告不确定性，而非强求一致。","collocation":"forcing agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v11","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"boundaries","zh":"边界","pos":"noun","example":"A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries.","exampleZh":"更好的简报整合结果，同时保留各自边界。","collocation":"clear boundaries","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v12","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coverage","zh":"覆盖范围","pos":"noun","example":"The sources differ in coverage as well as purpose.","exampleZh":"来源在覆盖范围和目的上都不同。","collocation":"limited coverage","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v13","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconcile","zh":"核对使一致","pos":"verb","example":"The team must reconcile definitions before comparing totals.","exampleZh":"比较总数前须核对口径。","collocation":"reconcile the records","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v14","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prevalence","zh":"普遍程度","pos":"noun","example":"A complaint list cannot establish the prevalence of a problem.","exampleZh":"投诉清单不能确定问题普遍程度。","collocation":"estimate prevalence","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v15","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allegation","zh":"指控","pos":"noun","example":"A reporting mismatch is not automatically evidence for an allegation of fraud.","exampleZh":"报告不一致不自动支持欺诈指控。","collocation":"an allegation of fraud","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v16","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which potential users are least adequately represented by the current cycle-route evidence?","answer":"People considering beginning to cycle after dark.","options":["Morning riders passing the three observed junctions.","People considering beginning to cycle after dark.","Existing weekly riders who answered the survey."],"explanation":"调查多为已周骑者，观察仅两天早七至九时，开始夜骑者未充分覆盖。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-09:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"How should the shopkeeper’s incident list be used in the public briefing?","answer":"As evidence of specific disruptions rather than a complete conflict count.","options":["As evidence of specific disruptions rather than a complete conflict count.","As a representative estimate of every road user’s daily experience.","As a replacement for the survey and all junction observations."],"explanation":"商户只记录影响经营的事件，能说明具体问题，不能当作完整冲突总数。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-09:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"How many survey respondents already cycled weekly?","answer":"156","options":["156","210","54"],"explanation":"210总回应中156为周骑行者。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-09:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What gap is most clearly identified?","answer":"Use after dark","options":["Morning behaviour at the observed junctions","The shopkeeper’s existence","Use after dark"],"explanation":"晚间使用没有充分覆盖。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-09:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why could parcel numbers rise without comparable household growth?","answer":"Distribution frequency changed and some households received multiple parcels","options":["Distribution frequency changed and some households received multiple parcels","Every parcel served exactly one new household","The operator had no records"],"explanation":"发放频次变化且户可多次领取。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-09:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What did neither dataset describe?","answer":"People unable to use the collection times","options":["Parcels leaving the depot","People on the registration list","People unable to use the collection times"],"explanation":"两份数据均未覆盖有需要但时间不合的人。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-09:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What did the poster’s figure of 75 actually count?","answer":"Workshop attendances","options":["Distinct participants","People finding employment","Workshop attendances"],"explanation":"75是人次，独立参与者48。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-09:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What should staff do with an unreachable participant’s outcome?","answer":"Record it as unknown","options":["Delete the original entry","Record it as unknown","Assume employment"],"explanation":"缺失结果明确标未知。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-09:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a source-evaluation briefing for the cycle-route team. Explain which questions each source can answer and design an additional check for a remaining gap. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Two ways to describe a cycle route”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["新闻编辑","数据负责人","社区代表"],"goal":"纠正过强标题，保留准确数字并提出一个补充调查。","twist":"两份记录都准确，但单位不同；编辑只能保留一个大标题数字。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"十天后核查两篇陌生报道，列出单位、总体和未覆盖群体。","writingPrompt":"写220词核查说明，不把口径错误直接称为造假。","speakingPrompt":"在两分钟内向记者解释人次与人数，并回应质疑。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Two accounts of a neighbourhood food scheme”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Two accounts of a neighbourhood food scheme” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-10","level":"C1","title":"多来源综合与决策理由","chapter":52,"focus":"合并证据并明确推论边界","sourceA":{"title":"A balanced plan for a learning garden — extended case review","text":"A fictional college wants to turn unused land into a learning garden. Its planning dossier combines students' requests, a caretaker's maintenance estimate and a teacher's ideas for practical lessons. The writer must develop a recommendation from these different perspectives without inventing consensus. A garden could serve several purposes, but the plan has to make trade-offs visible. The report will go to a committee that has not read the original discussion notes.\n\nThe garden proposal should state which activities have priority when space or supervision is limited. Access to a raised bed means little if nobody is available to support a planned class. A phased arrangement allows the committee to investigate real demand while avoiding a promise that every requested activity will begin immediately.\n\nThe requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity. The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds. That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved. The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access. These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned. I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.\n\nThe report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land. A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries. Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question. The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project. My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain. The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned.\n\nThe garden committee has received three costed options. A simple bed layout costs 1,400 pounds, accessible raised beds cost 2,100, and a paved teaching area with beds costs 3,600. The available grant is 2,500. Students favour the third option because it allows group teaching after rain, while the access representative prefers the second to a cheaper inaccessible start. The caretaker notes that every option still requires watering during the summer break. A nearby association has offered volunteers for two weeks but not the whole holiday. The writer must not count that offer as complete maintenance coverage. A phased recommendation should explain which need is protected now and what remains unresolved."},"sourceB":{"title":"Choosing the first phase of a digital archive","text":"A regional history society planned to make its collection easier to consult. Three sources shaped the proposal: a researcher survey, an archivist's condition report and a small technical estimate. Researchers wanted searchable documents available from home. The archivist warned that some fragile volumes could not be scanned using the inexpensive machine in the estimate. The technical volunteer had priced file storage but had not included the time needed to describe items accurately.\n\nThe society had a grant of 8,000 pounds. Scanning the most requested material was estimated at 5,500, provided the pages were already stable. Conservation of two damaged volumes would cost another 4,000. Creating basic catalogue descriptions for a smaller selected set would cost 2,000 in paid time. These were not three independent extras that could all be funded. The committee needed to choose a coherent first phase rather than combine the most attractive parts of incompatible plans.\n\nA researcher representative initially favoured scanning everything that could be handled safely. The archivist pointed out that images without useful descriptions might be difficult to discover, even if technically online. A second proposal therefore selected stable material, included basic descriptions and deferred the damaged volumes until conservation funding was available. It would not satisfy every request immediately, but it connected access with preservation and the practical work of finding information.\n\nThe report recommended a limited collection with clear selection criteria and a published explanation of exclusions. It also proposed recording unsuccessful searches, because download counts alone would show only material people had already found. The researcher survey informed priorities but did not represent every possible future user. The final judgement was therefore provisional. Combining sources meant preserving their different contributions: demand, physical constraints and implementation cost. A useful synthesis did more than list three opinions; it explained why one achievable sequence followed from their relationship."},"listening":{"teaching":"We now have three garden options and a fixed grant of 2,500 pounds. The simple bed layout costs 1,400, the accessible raised beds cost 2,100, and the paved teaching area with beds costs 3,600. The students favour the paved area because it would support classes after wet weather. That is a relevant educational benefit, but the current grant does not cover it. The access representative argues that the cheaper option should not begin by excluding people who need raised beds. The caretaker adds a different constraint: every option needs summer watering. A nearby association offers volunteers for two weeks, not for the whole holiday. We must not expand that offer when writing the budget. Your synthesis should show how the sources relate. One possible recommendation is accessible raised beds as a first phase, leaving 400 pounds that might contribute to maintenance. But do not assume that 400 is enough without a costed plan. Another option is to delay construction until maintenance is confirmed. Either judgement needs to state what it protects and what it postpones. The committee wants a reasoned sequence, not a paragraph praising each option separately and leaving the actual decision unexplained. Explain who would carry out that next check.","test":"The community music project must choose its first purchase before the grant deadline. We have 1,800 pounds. A set of portable keyboards would cost 1,200, storage cases another 250, and a suitable lockable cupboard 500. Buying all three would exceed the grant by 150 pounds. The tutor prefers the keyboards because several learners currently share instruments. The centre manager says the equipment cannot be left unsecured overnight. A parent has offered to lend two keyboards, but only until the end of the term. That offer may help a short pilot; it cannot be counted as a permanent replacement for purchased instruments. We could begin with borrowed equipment and use some funding for secure storage, provided the lender accepts the arrangements. Alternatively, we could buy a smaller set and postpone the cases. The decision should consider how instruments will be moved and protected, not just how many can be advertised. Please bring a written recommendation to Friday's meeting, with costs and conditions stated separately. The grant officer will allow a revised item list but not a purchase made after the deadline. Do not place an order before we agree on a complete workable option. Include the lender’s conditions in your written recommendation."},"vocab":[{"head":"capacity","zh":"能力","pos":"noun","example":"The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","exampleZh":"请求说明理想用途，但未证明维护能力。","collocation":"maintenance capacity","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v1","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assume","zh":"假设","pos":"verb","example":"The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","exampleZh":"管理员的估算假设志愿者会给种植床浇水。","collocation":"assume volunteer support","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v2","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assumption","zh":"假设","pos":"noun","example":"That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","exampleZh":"预算获批前应核实该假设。","collocation":"check an assumption","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v3","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unrestricted","zh":"无限制的","pos":"adjective","example":"The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access.","exampleZh":"教师建议开展实践课，而非无限制公众开放。","collocation":"unrestricted access","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v4","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coexist","zh":"共存","pos":"verb","example":"These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned.","exampleZh":"如果职责分配清楚，这些用途可以共存。","collocation":"purposes can coexist","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v5","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"first phase","zh":"第一阶段","pos":"phrase","example":"I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.","exampleZh":"我建议第一阶段规模较小，采用便于使用的高架种植床。","collocation":"first phase","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v6","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opportunity cost","zh":"机会成本","pos":"phrase","example":"The report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land.","exampleZh":"报告应承认使用这块地的机会成本。","collocation":"opportunity cost","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v7","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"synthesis","zh":"综合分析","pos":"noun","example":"A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries.","exampleZh":"综合分析解释来源之间的关系，而非罗列摘要。","collocation":"a balanced synthesis","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v8","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underlying","zh":"背后的","pos":"adjective","example":"Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question.","exampleZh":"当说法不一致时，我会找出背后的问题。","collocation":"the underlying question","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v9","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision rule","zh":"决策规则","pos":"phrase","example":"The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project.","exampleZh":"委员会需要决定是否继续项目的规则。","collocation":"decision rule","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v10","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain.","exampleZh":"我的结论是暂定的，因为暑期人员仍不确定。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v11","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"as well as","zh":"以及","pos":"phrase","example":"The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned.","exampleZh":"下一份报告既应说明计划，也应说明实际情况。","collocation":"as well as","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v12","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"phased","zh":"分阶段的","pos":"adjective","example":"A phased plan separates immediate access from later expansion.","exampleZh":"分阶段方案区分即时可及与后续扩展。","collocation":"a phased approach","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v13","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interdependency","zh":"相互依赖","pos":"noun","example":"The report explains the interdependency between access and maintenance.","exampleZh":"报告解释可及性与维护的相互依赖。","collocation":"identify interdependencies","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v14","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defer","zh":"推迟","pos":"verb","example":"The committee may defer construction until maintenance is funded.","exampleZh":"委员会可推迟施工至维护资金落实。","collocation":"defer a decision","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v15","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coherent","zh":"连贯一致的","pos":"adjective","example":"A coherent option must include both equipment and care.","exampleZh":"完整一致方案须包含设备与照管。","collocation":"a coherent proposal","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v16","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"What remains to be checked if the committee chooses the 2,100-pound raised beds?","answer":"Whether the remaining funds can cover a workable maintenance plan.","options":["Whether the remaining funds can cover a workable maintenance plan.","Whether the grant permits a 3,600-pound paved teaching area.","Whether two weeks of volunteers equal the whole summer holiday."],"explanation":"2500减2100余400，但没有成本化维护计划，不能假定足够；志愿仅两周。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-10:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"Why is the association’s volunteer offer insufficient as complete maintenance coverage?","answer":"Its two-week commitment is shorter than the holiday requiring care.","options":["Its members have rejected all involvement in the project.","Its offer includes construction rather than any watering support.","Its two-week commitment is shorter than the holiday requiring care."],"explanation":"协会只承诺两周，不能扩写成整个暑期浇水保障。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-10:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"Which garden option is beyond the current grant?","answer":"The paved teaching area with beds","options":["The simple bed layout","Accessible raised beds","The paved teaching area with beds"],"explanation":"3600高于2500。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-10:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What is the limitation of the volunteer offer?","answer":"It covers only two weeks","options":["It includes free construction","It covers only two weeks","It covers the entire summer"],"explanation":"两周不等于整个假期。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-10:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What was omitted from the technical estimate?","answer":"Time for accurate descriptions","options":["The existence of digital storage","Every requested image","Time for accurate descriptions"],"explanation":"技术估价只含存储，未含描述工时。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-10:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"Why record unsuccessful searches?","answer":"Downloads show only what users found","options":["They replace all conservation work","Downloads show only what users found","They establish the condition of damaged volumes"],"explanation":"下载无法反映找不到资料的人。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-10:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"By how much do all three music purchases exceed funding?","answer":"150 pounds","options":["500 pounds","150 pounds","250 pounds"],"explanation":"1200+250+500=1950，超150。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-10:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What limits the loan offer?","answer":"It ends at the end of term","options":["It ends at the end of term","It supplies permanent ownership","It includes secure storage automatically"],"explanation":"借用只到学期末。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-10:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a recommendation for the garden committee. Integrate the different perspectives, identify an assumption requiring verification and specify conditions for a manageable first phase. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “A balanced plan for a learning garden”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["学生代表","无障碍代表","管理员","预算负责人"],"goal":"形成2500英镑以内且维护可落实的第一阶段。","twist":"志愿者撤回一周支持，项目必须说明是缩小还是延期。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生三来源决策档案，画出需求、约束与费用关系。","writingPrompt":"写220词综合建议，明确先做、后做和待核实部分。","speakingPrompt":"接受预算削减后重新排序，并向受影响者说明理由。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Choosing the first phase of a digital archive”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Choosing the first phase of a digital archive” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-11","level":"C1","title":"公允反驳与政策替代","chapter":54,"focus":"公允重述对方理由再回应","sourceA":{"title":"Should a club remove late fees? — extended case review","text":"A fictional community sports club is debating whether to remove late cancellation fees. Supporters say fees discourage people facing unpredictable caring duties. Opponents argue that empty spaces prevent others from joining. The workshop asks learners to engage with both concerns rather than label one side selfish. A persuasive response should identify the purpose of the current rule, test its assumptions and offer a workable alternative with limits.\n\nThe club should record when notice is given and whether a place is subsequently filled. Those measures would help distinguish unavoidable last-minute changes from a pattern that a different booking rule might address. A fair discussion should avoid demanding private details from carers merely to establish that their responsibilities can change.\n\nThe strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity. That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee. I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven. People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control. Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence. A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.\n\nThe alternative must be judged against the same practical objective. One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs. We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing. If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment. My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record. A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker.\n\nThe club reviewed four weeks of bookings. There were 18 late cancellations, but eleven places were filled from the waiting list when notice arrived at least two hours before the session. The remaining seven places stayed empty. A member argued that the waiting list had therefore solved the problem. The secretary disagreed: it had helped under certain conditions, while people cancelling very near the start still left gaps. The committee is considering a reminder system and a limited number of fee-free cancellations for unforeseen caring duties. It must also explain how that policy can be applied without demanding public disclosure of private circumstances."},"sourceB":{"title":"A workplace debate about fixed office days","text":"A consultancy proposed requiring every employee to attend the office on the same three weekdays. Management argued that shared attendance would make mentoring and informal collaboration easier. Some staff opposed the plan because their work involved long periods of individual analysis and their journeys were expensive. The disagreement was initially framed as a choice between commitment to colleagues and personal convenience, which obscured the actual question of how different tasks were best supported.\n\nA junior analyst said that remote work had sometimes left her unsure whom to ask for help. Her account supported the need for reliable mentoring access, but not necessarily a rule covering every employee on identical days. A project manager reported that scheduled online consultations worked well for his team. That example did not establish that online support met every beginner's needs. Both accounts were informative without being universal proof.\n\nThe staff group proposed two common collaboration days and a third day arranged by each team according to its tasks. Management worried that team arrangements would become inconsistent and hard to coordinate. In response, the group suggested published expectations for mentoring availability and a review of missed consultations, rather than leaving attendance entirely to individual preference. The proposal therefore addressed the purpose of the rule instead of merely asking for an exemption.\n\nA three-month trial would record access to advice, delays in joint tasks and employee travel difficulties. It would not treat desk occupancy as the sole measure of collaboration. Employees with agreed access adjustments would continue to discuss those privately. The final recommendation acknowledged that no arrangement would remove every conflict. Its strength lay in a testable alternative that preserved common working time while allowing tasks to influence the remaining schedule. A fair argument had to engage with the genuine mentoring concern, not portray managers as simply distrustful or staff as unwilling to work."},"listening":{"teaching":"We have new information for the cancellation-fee debate. During four weeks, eighteen bookings were cancelled late. Eleven places were filled from the waiting list when at least two hours' notice was available. Seven remained empty. These results show that the waiting list can help, but they do not show that it solves every cancellation problem. Very late notice may leave insufficient time to contact someone else. When responding to the fee's supporters, acknowledge their strongest concern: an empty place can deny another member an opportunity. Then examine whether the current fee addresses that concern fairly and effectively. The committee is considering reminders and a limited number of fee-free cancellations for unforeseen caring duties. A practical policy must define the process without requiring members to share private details publicly. It should also avoid promising that every absence will be excused. If you favour the alternative, explain what evidence would make you reconsider it. For example, repeated absences might increase despite reminders. If you favour retaining a fee, explain why the benefits cannot be achieved through a less burdensome arrangement. Neither side should use one sympathetic or frustrating example as proof of the whole pattern. Your task is to develop a response that changes the decision, not simply to restate your preference more forcefully.","test":"The residents' committee is reviewing the rule that meeting rooms must be booked in four-hour blocks. Supporters say the blocks simplify staffing and cleaning. Several small groups say they need only ninety minutes and cannot afford to pay for unused time. We are not deciding that one concern is imaginary. The caretaker has explained that some setup work is required regardless of meeting length, but cleaning does not always take the same time. A proposed alternative is a two-hour minimum with an additional fixed setup charge. That would be tested on weekday afternoons only, when staffing is already available. Evening bookings would remain unchanged during the trial. We need to know whether shorter sessions attract new users and whether transitions between groups create delays. We will therefore record actual finishing times and preparation work, not just the number of bookings. Please send comments by Friday, identifying a practical difficulty or benefit. A message saying that all rules are unfair will not answer the staffing question; a message saying that all users can afford four hours will not answer the access question. The committee will compare the alternative with the current arrangement after six weeks before deciding whether to extend it."},"vocab":[{"head":"objection","zh":"反对意见","pos":"noun","example":"The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","exampleZh":"最有力的反对意见是空位剥夺了他人的机会。","collocation":"a strong objection","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v1","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"legitimate","zh":"合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","exampleZh":"即使反对现有费用，这一担忧仍然合理。","collocation":"a legitimate concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v2","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uneven","zh":"不均等的","pos":"adjective","example":"I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","exampleZh":"我赞同取消有成本，但负担并不均等。","collocation":"an uneven burden","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v3","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"beyond their control","zh":"超出他们的控制","pos":"phrase","example":"People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control.","exampleZh":"承担照护责任的人可能面临无法控制的变化。","collocation":"beyond their control","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v4","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"need not","zh":"不一定需要","pos":"phrase","example":"Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence.","exampleZh":"取消费用不一定意味着取消一切后果。","collocation":"need not","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v5","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"waiting list","zh":"候补名单","pos":"phrase","example":"A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.","exampleZh":"若及时通知，候补名单可以填补部分空位。","collocation":"waiting list","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v6","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"objective","zh":"目标","pos":"noun","example":"The alternative must be judged against the same practical objective.","exampleZh":"替代方案必须按同一实际目标评判。","collocation":"a practical objective","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v7","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frequently","zh":"频繁地","pos":"adverb","example":"One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs.","exampleZh":"一个例子无法证明问题出现的频率。","collocation":"occur frequently","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v8","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"personal failing","zh":"个人缺点","pos":"phrase","example":"We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing.","exampleZh":"我们应避免把分歧当成个人缺点。","collocation":"personal failing","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v9","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjustment","zh":"调整","pos":"noun","example":"If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment.","exampleZh":"如果反复缺席增加，试行可能需要调整。","collocation":"a necessary adjustment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v10","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conditional","zh":"有条件的","pos":"adjective","example":"My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record.","exampleZh":"我的支持以保留清晰出勤记录为条件。","collocation":"conditional support","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v11","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"address","zh":"回应处理","pos":"verb","example":"A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker.","exampleZh":"公允回应处理担忧，而非攻击发言者。","collocation":"address a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v12","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exemption","zh":"豁免","pos":"noun","example":"A limited exemption should have a clear and private procedure.","exampleZh":"有限豁免应有明确且私密的程序。","collocation":"grant an exemption","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v13","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"burdensome","zh":"负担过重的","pos":"adjective","example":"A rule can be predictable yet disproportionately burdensome.","exampleZh":"规则可以可预测，却负担不相称。","collocation":"a burdensome requirement","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v14","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rebuttal","zh":"反驳","pos":"noun","example":"A convincing rebuttal answers the strongest opposing reason.","exampleZh":"有力反驳回应最强反方理由。","collocation":"a convincing rebuttal","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v15","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionality","zh":"相称性","pos":"noun","example":"The committee should assess the proportionality of the fee.","exampleZh":"委员会应评估费用是否相称。","collocation":"assess proportionality","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v16","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"What conclusion is justified by filling eleven of eighteen cancelled places?","answer":"The waiting list helps when enough notice allows replacements.","options":["The waiting list has removed the need to examine every cancellation.","The remaining seven members necessarily cancelled for the same reason.","The waiting list helps when enough notice allows replacements."],"explanation":"十一处在至少两小时前通知时补上，说明有条件有效，不是彻底解决，也无七人原因。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-11:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What design issue accompanies fee-free cancellations for caring duties?","answer":"Applying the policy without public disclosure of private circumstances.","options":["Assuming every late cancellation should be exempt from consequences.","Applying the policy without public disclosure of private circumstances.","Requiring carers to describe personal details on a public booking sheet."],"explanation":"政策须兼顾处理流程与隐私，而非公开证明，也未承诺每次缺席都免责。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-11:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"How many cancelled places remained empty?","answer":"Seven","options":["Eighteen","Seven","Eleven"],"explanation":"18减11等于7。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-11:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What must a fair rebuttal address?","answer":"The opportunity lost when places remain empty","options":["The opportunity lost when places remain empty","Only the speaker’s character","The assumption that no costs exist"],"explanation":"应回应空位损失，不回避核心顾虑。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-11:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What concern did the junior analyst support?","answer":"Reliable access to mentoring","options":["Complete removal of shared work","Reliable access to mentoring","Identical attendance as the only solution"],"explanation":"她的经历支持咨询需求，不唯一支持统一三天。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-11:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What made the staff alternative relevant?","answer":"It preserved mentoring expectations while allowing task-based scheduling","options":["It preserved mentoring expectations while allowing task-based scheduling","It ignored collaboration entirely","It measured collaboration only by desk occupancy"],"explanation":"替代方案回应原规则目的。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-11:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"Where will the shorter-block trial apply?","answer":"Weekday afternoons","options":["Weekday afternoons","Every evening immediately","Only Sunday mornings"],"explanation":"先在工作日下午试行。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-11:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What should be measured beyond booking numbers?","answer":"Finishing times and preparation work","options":["Only applause for the policy","Members’ private finances publicly","Finishing times and preparation work"],"explanation":"需记录实际结束与准备工作。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-11:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an argument for a revised cancellation policy. State the strongest objection fairly, respond with a practical alternative and identify a condition that could change your recommendation. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Should a club remove late fees?”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["支持收费者","照护者代表","预约管理员"],"goal":"提出可测试的替代政策并回应最强反对理由。","twist":"候补名单短期失效，需要解释在何种条件下恢复费用。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"十天后读陌生政策争论，找出双方真正共同目标。","writingPrompt":"写220词反驳，先公允重述对方理由再提出有条件替代。","speakingPrompt":"三分钟辩论中至少承认一个对方的有效观点。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A workplace debate about fixed office days”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A workplace debate about fixed office days” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-12","level":"C1","title":"谈判利益与书面条件","chapter":55,"focus":"区分立场、利益、底线和可交换条件","sourceA":{"title":"Negotiating use of a rehearsal room — extended case review","text":"Two fictional groups want the same community rehearsal room on Thursday evenings. A choir needs stable weekly practice, while a youth theatre needs occasional longer sessions before performances. The building manager cannot create another room or ignore the closing time. Learners will negotiate around the interests behind the requests, making proposals specific enough to check. Agreement should not rely on a vague promise that somebody will somehow be flexible later.\n\nThe building manager can help by identifying the calendar dates that genuinely create a clash. Once those are known, the groups may trade a small number of sessions instead of disputing an entire term. The written agreement should preserve that limited scope so that an occasional exception does not become an assumed permanent entitlement.\n\nThe choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule. The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday. Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions? We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance. The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point. Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.\n\nA tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators. Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes? We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash. I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser. To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates. A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit.\n\nThe room calendar now shows three actual clashes rather than an entire term of conflict. The choir needs all Thursdays before its June concert, while the theatre needs two longer rehearsals in May. A possible exchange gives the theatre an earlier start on those two dates and the choir an additional Sunday session. The building manager can provide Sunday access only if a trained keyholder is present. Neither group currently has one available. A vague promise to find somebody would therefore leave an essential condition unresolved. The negotiators need either a named trained person, a different exchange or a timetable that stays within confirmed access."},"sourceB":{"title":"Agreeing responsibilities in a translation contract","text":"A charity commissioned a freelance translator to prepare a public information booklet in two languages. The charity wanted delivery within three weeks, while the translator requested a stable source text before agreeing to the deadline. The project manager initially described this as inflexibility. On closer discussion, the translator explained that changing a paragraph after translation could require checking terminology and layout in both versions, not merely replacing a few words.\n\nThe parties separated the main interests. The charity needed a usable booklet before a community event. The translator needed a defined workload and a reliable review process. They agreed that the source text would be frozen on the fifth of the month. One round of minor factual corrections would be included, but additional sections or substantial rewriting would require a revised quotation. The charity would appoint one reviewer to consolidate comments instead of sending contradictory instructions from several volunteers.\n\nA remaining issue concerned approval. The translator could guarantee a checked translation of the approved source, but could not certify the accuracy of the charity's underlying policy. The charity retained responsibility for that content. The contract would state this distinction and identify the final approval date. If approval was late, the delivery schedule would be adjusted rather than leave the translator responsible for an impossible unchanged deadline.\n\nBoth parties accepted a short initial section to test terminology before the full document proceeded. The fee included that pilot section and one agreed revision, not unlimited future updates. Neither side obtained unlimited flexibility. Instead, the agreement connected each promise to a condition the relevant party could control. The project manager later said that the conversation had become easier when they stopped debating whether the translator was helpful and began specifying which changes, decisions and delays the contract actually covered. The charity recorded those responsibilities in its project file."},"listening":{"teaching":"The rehearsal negotiation has become more specific. We no longer have a conflict covering every Thursday. There are three actual clashes. The choir needs its regular practice before the June concert, and the theatre needs two longer rehearsals in May. One proposed exchange gives the theatre an earlier start on those two dates and the choir an extra Sunday session. That sounds workable until we check access. The building manager can open on Sunday only if a trained keyholder is present. Neither group currently has a named person available. We must not write the agreement as if that condition were already satisfied. We could ask whether a trained person can be confirmed by a stated deadline. If not, the parties need a different exchange. In the role-play, distinguish the choir's interest in predictable preparation from its opening demand for every Thursday. Distinguish the theatre's interest in enough rehearsal time from its preferred timing. Then make a conditional offer that preserves the fixed closing time. Before accepting, check who notifies members and what happens if the condition fails. A successful negotiation is not merely one in which everyone says yes. The written version must describe an arrangement that the people involved are actually authorised and able to carry out.","test":"I am calling to confirm the provisional agreement for your association's weekend workshop. We can hold the large room for Saturday from ten until four, provided we receive the signed booking form and deposit by Tuesday noon. The room is not yet confirmed, so please wait before advertising tickets. The fee includes tables, chairs and ordinary cleaning. It does not include the sound technician you requested; that is a separate service costing eighty pounds. If you choose not to book the technician, you may use the small portable speaker after a short instruction session with the caretaker. You cannot operate the main sound desk yourselves. Access for setting up begins at nine thirty, and the room must be clear by four thirty. The programme should therefore finish before four if you need substantial packing time. We can cancel the sound-technician booking without charge until Thursday, but the room's cancellation conditions are different and appear on the form. Please check both rather than assume one deadline applies to everything. If another committee member will sign, make sure that person has read the equipment restrictions. Call me if any term needs discussion before you return the form. I can clarify the separate conditions before Tuesday."},"vocab":[{"head":"underlying interest","zh":"背后的利益","pos":"phrase","example":"The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","exampleZh":"合唱团背后的利益是可预测的每周安排。","collocation":"underlying interest","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v1","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"extra time","zh":"额外时间","pos":"phrase","example":"The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","exampleZh":"剧团需要演出前加时，而非每个星期四。","collocation":"extra time","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v2","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"in exchange for","zh":"交换","pos":"phrase","example":"Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","exampleZh":"你愿意用更早开始换取两次较长排练吗？","collocation":"in exchange for","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v3","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"前提是","pos":"conjunction","example":"We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance.","exampleZh":"我们可以接受，前提是日期提前商定。","collocation":"provided the dates are agreed","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v4","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"constraint","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point.","exampleZh":"关门时间是固定限制，而非讨价还价点。","collocation":"a fixed constraint","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v5","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"essential","zh":"必要的","pos":"adjective","example":"Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.","exampleZh":"让我们区分必要条件和偏好。","collocation":"an essential condition","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v6","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tentative","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"A tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators.","exampleZh":"暂定协议仍需双方协调员确认。","collocation":"a tentative agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v7","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"notify","zh":"通知","pos":"verb","example":"Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes?","exampleZh":"惯常时间表变化时由谁通知成员？","collocation":"notify members","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v8","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unforeseen","zh":"意外的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash.","exampleZh":"我们应记录处理意外冲突的流程。","collocation":"an unforeseen clash","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v9","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commit","zh":"使承担承诺","pos":"verb","example":"I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser.","exampleZh":"未经咨询组织者，我不能代表另一组承诺。","collocation":"commit a group to an agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v10","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exception","zh":"例外","pos":"noun","example":"To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates.","exampleZh":"确认一下，例外仅适用于列明的两个日期。","collocation":"a limited exception","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v11","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"workable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit.","exampleZh":"可行协议会明确职责和复查日期。","collocation":"a workable agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v12","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consolidate","zh":"汇总整合","pos":"verb","example":"One coordinator should consolidate conflicting requests.","exampleZh":"一名协调员应汇总相互冲突的请求。","collocation":"consolidate feedback","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v13","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"authorised","zh":"获授权的","pos":"adjective","example":"Only an authorised representative can commit the group.","exampleZh":"只有获授权代表能替团队承诺。","collocation":"an authorised representative","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v14","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stipulate","zh":"明确规定","pos":"verb","example":"The agreement should stipulate the access condition.","exampleZh":"协议应明确通行条件。","collocation":"stipulate a condition","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v15","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency","zh":"应变安排","pos":"noun","example":"The timetable needs a contingency if the keyholder is unavailable.","exampleZh":"钥匙管理者无空时，时间表需要应变安排。","collocation":"a contingency plan","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v16","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why is the proposed Sunday exchange not yet a workable agreement?","answer":"It depends on a trained keyholder who has not been confirmed.","options":["It conflicts with a permanent ban on using the room at weekends.","It depends on a trained keyholder who has not been confirmed.","It exceeds a closing time that the manager has already extended."],"explanation":"周日须有受训钥匙管理者，双方均未落实；并无永久周末禁用。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-12:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What does identifying three specific clashes change about the negotiation?","answer":"It allows targeted exchanges instead of treating the entire term as disputed.","options":["It allows targeted exchanges instead of treating the entire term as disputed.","It establishes that the choir no longer needs predictable preparation.","It gives either group authority to commit the other without consultation."],"explanation":"具体三个冲突使有限日期交换可行，并未消除稳定排练利益或授予代承诺权。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-12:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What essential condition remains unresolved?","answer":"A trained Sunday keyholder","options":["A trained Sunday keyholder","The existence of the June concert","The theatre’s need for any rehearsal"],"explanation":"周日须有合格钥匙管理者，尚未落实。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-12:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What should happen if that condition fails?","answer":"The exchange must change","options":["The closing time is silently ignored","Members receive an unconditional promise","The exchange must change"],"explanation":"条件失败须另议，不虚构已落实。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-12:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why did the translator require a stable source text?","answer":"Changes could affect terminology and layout in both versions","options":["Changes could affect terminology and layout in both versions","The translator refused every factual correction","The charity had no event deadline"],"explanation":"改动影响双语术语与版面，非简单换词。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-12:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"Who remains responsible for underlying policy accuracy?","answer":"The charity","options":["The translator alone","The layout software provider","The charity"],"explanation":"翻译核对不等于对原政策内容认证。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-12:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"When is the room confirmed?","answer":"After the signed form and deposit arrive by Tuesday noon","options":["Immediately when the call begins","After tickets are sold","After the signed form and deposit arrive by Tuesday noon"],"explanation":"签表和订金按时到才确认。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-12:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"Which sound equipment can the group use without the technician?","answer":"The portable speaker after instruction","options":["Every installed system automatically","The portable speaker after instruction","The main sound desk unsupervised"],"explanation":"只能经说明使用便携音箱。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-12:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proposed agreement for the rehearsal room. Explain each group’s underlying interest, offer a conditional exchange and record responsibilities and a review process. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Negotiating use of a rehearsal room”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["合唱团","剧团","场地经理"],"goal":"就三个具体冲突达成能履行的条件交换。","twist":"周日场地可用但没有已确认的合格钥匙管理者。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生合同摘录，标明义务、条件和条件失败后果。","writingPrompt":"写220词书面协议，区分免费修改、额外费用和责任边界。","speakingPrompt":"不评价人格，解释一个看似不灵活条件背后的利益。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Agreeing responsibilities in a translation contract”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Agreeing responsibilities in a translation contract” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-13","level":"C1","title":"正式报告与优先级","chapter":57,"focus":"按目的组织发现、限制和行动","sourceA":{"title":"Reporting on a study-room trial — extended case review","text":"A fictional college committee has asked for a report on reservable study rooms. The writer has booking records, staff observations and comments from students who could not find a place. A useful report must distinguish the questions these materials answer and make practical recommendations. Headings should guide a busy reader without becoming a substitute for connected argument. The report is intended to support a decision, not merely demonstrate that data have been collected.\n\nThe committee needs to know whether a proposed change addresses empty reservations, lack of space or both. The report should not assume that visible empty rooms are available for immediate use without checking their status. A practical recommendation can begin with the booking rule while leaving broader capacity questions open.\n\nThe purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial. The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt. It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved. Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain. These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period. An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.\n\nIt is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption. The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability. The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study. A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it. Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations. The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim.\n\nThe study-room review now distinguishes reservation time from actual occupancy. Of 160 bookings over two weeks, staff observed 38 rooms still empty twenty minutes after the reserved start. However, observations occurred only at fixed rounds, so the records cannot establish whether every room remained unused for its entire slot. Students propose a fifteen-minute release rule, while staff warn that visitors may be delayed by inaccessible lifts or timetable changes. A report should therefore examine a simple check-in option and a route for agreed exceptions. It should not assume that the largest possible release count is automatically the fairest measure of success."},"sourceB":{"title":"Reporting on a college equipment-loan service","text":"A college commissioned a report on the service through which students borrowed laptops. The review covered four weeks and used loan records, observations at the collection desk and twelve voluntary interviews. Its purpose was to identify operational improvements, not to judge the academic performance of borrowers. The report stated this scope explicitly because some readers had expected a wider evaluation of whether loaned computers improved attainment.\n\nThe most frequent recorded problem was a mismatch between collection times and students' timetables. Of 95 reservations, 21 were not collected during the original slot. However, staff later arranged collection for fourteen of those students. Describing all 21 as unused demand would therefore exaggerate the problem. Interviews suggested that some students could not leave classes during the advertised collection period, while others had found alternative equipment. The small voluntary sample could illustrate these reasons but not establish their exact prevalence.\n\nThe report recommended two immediate changes: a clearly advertised late-afternoon collection window and a message asking students to cancel reservations they no longer needed. It did not recommend buying additional laptops before checking how the existing stock circulated. A technician also identified six chargers requiring replacement. That issue was assigned to the equipment team with a deadline, rather than buried inside an overall satisfaction score.\n\nFor a longer review, the report proposed recording waiting time for a device, actual loan duration and the number of students unable to obtain suitable equipment. It also requested a separate process for access requirements, since a laptop available on paper might not meet every learner's needs. The conclusion distinguished urgent maintenance, communication changes and questions about future capacity. The college could act on the first two without claiming that four weeks of limited evidence justified every possible investment. Each recommendation named an owner and explained which finding it addressed, making the report useful for a decision rather than merely descriptive."},"listening":{"teaching":"The study-room report should distinguish reserved time from actual occupancy. We have 160 bookings across two weeks, and staff observed 38 rooms still empty twenty minutes after the start. That does not mean 38 complete slots were unused. Staff checked at fixed rounds, so somebody may have arrived later. A proposed fifteen-minute release rule could make space available, but we need to examine exceptions. A student delayed by an inaccessible lift may have a different problem from someone who simply forgot the booking. The report should not label both cases as irresponsible. Consider a check-in option and a clear route for agreed adjustments. We also need to know who would administer the rule and how quickly a released room could be booked by someone else. Your findings section should state what was observed and the limits of observation. Your recommendation can then propose a test, with responsibility assigned to the facilities team. Do not introduce an unsupported new claim in the conclusion, such as saying that the college never needs more rooms. The current material may justify improving how existing space is used while leaving the capacity question open. A strong report connects an action to evidence and explains what further review is needed.","test":"I am outlining the report requested by the warehouse manager. Please review the equipment-return desk during the next ten working days. We need to know whether the new return procedure reduces missing items without creating long queues. Do not review the purchasing system; that is a separate project. Record the time each sampled customer joins the queue and leaves the desk, and note whether the return was complete or needed follow-up. Do not record customer names on the observation sheet. If an item appears damaged, normal staff must handle it; you are observing, not inspecting technical safety. We would like a short interim note after the first five days if an urgent operational issue appears. The final report is due on the following Wednesday. Please separate observed facts, staff comments and your recommendations. A photograph of a queue is not a reliable measure of average waiting, so numerical timing matters. The manager also wants to know if the desk arrangement causes access difficulties. You can describe a specific obstruction without identifying the person affected. We will decide on any permanent layout change after reading the report and discussing it with staff who operate the desk. Keep your timing records with the final report."},"vocab":[{"head":"purpose","zh":"目的","pos":"noun","example":"The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","exampleZh":"本报告旨在评估预约试行。","collocation":"the purpose of a report","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v1","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cover","zh":"覆盖","pos":"verb","example":"The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","exampleZh":"记录覆盖已完成预约，却不包含每次失败尝试。","collocation":"cover completed bookings","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v2","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"it was observed","zh":"观察发现","pos":"phrase","example":"It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","exampleZh":"观察发现一些房间被预约后仍然空置。","collocation":"it was observed","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v3","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"indicate","zh":"表明","pos":"verb","example":"Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain.","exampleZh":"学生意见反映不满，但其频率尚不确定。","collocation":"indicate frustration","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v4","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interpret","zh":"解读","pos":"verb","example":"These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period.","exampleZh":"应在有限观察期内解读这些发现。","collocation":"interpret the findings","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v5","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"release rule","zh":"释放规则","pos":"phrase","example":"An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.","exampleZh":"自动释放规则可能减少闲置预约。","collocation":"release rule","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v6","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"it is recommended","zh":"建议","pos":"phrase","example":"It is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption.","exampleZh":"建议先测试该规则，再扩大采用。","collocation":"it is recommended","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v7","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"monitor","zh":"监测","pos":"verb","example":"The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability.","exampleZh":"设施团队应负责监测房间可用性。","collocation":"monitor availability","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v8","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"immediate","zh":"立即的","pos":"adjective","example":"The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study.","exampleZh":"报告区分立即行动与需进一步研究的问题。","collocation":"immediate actions","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v9","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"traceable","zh":"可追溯的","pos":"adjective","example":"A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it.","exampleZh":"发现应能追溯到支持它的材料。","collocation":"traceable evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v10","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"heading","zh":"标题","pos":"noun","example":"Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations.","exampleZh":"标题帮助读者定位证据与建议。","collocation":"a clear heading","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v11","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unsupported","zh":"无依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim.","exampleZh":"结论不应引入无依据的新主张。","collocation":"an unsupported claim","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v12","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"occupancy","zh":"实际占用","pos":"noun","example":"Reservation data should be compared with observed occupancy.","exampleZh":"预约数据应与观察到的实际占用比较。","collocation":"room occupancy","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v13","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attainment","zh":"学业成果","pos":"noun","example":"The operational review does not establish academic attainment.","exampleZh":"运营评估不能确立学业成果。","collocation":"academic attainment","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v14","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"actionable","zh":"可据以行动的","pos":"adjective","example":"An actionable recommendation names an owner and a deadline.","exampleZh":"可执行建议明确负责人和期限。","collocation":"an actionable recommendation","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v15","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interim","zh":"中期的","pos":"adjective","example":"An interim finding may require action before the full report.","exampleZh":"中期发现可能在完整报告前就需行动。","collocation":"an interim report","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v16","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why can the report not describe all thirty-eight observations as completely unused slots?","answer":"Fixed inspection rounds did not establish occupancy throughout each slot.","options":["Fixed inspection rounds did not establish occupancy throughout each slot.","All thirty-eight rooms were confirmed occupied before the rounds began.","The reservation system had recorded only cancelled bookings that week."],"explanation":"只在固定时点看到空房，无法断定整段预约无人使用。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-13:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What risk should a fifteen-minute release rule consider before being tested?","answer":"Some arrivals may be delayed by access barriers rather than forgotten bookings.","options":["Every released room will necessarily be booked immediately by another student.","The rule automatically answers whether the college needs more rooms overall.","Some arrivals may be delayed by access barriers rather than forgotten bookings."],"explanation":"原文提出电梯无障碍延误等例外，应设置签到或合理调整，不能一概归责。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-13:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What cannot be concluded about the 38 observations?","answer":"That all corresponding slots were completely unused","options":["That staff saw empty rooms","That the observation period was two weeks","That all corresponding slots were completely unused"],"explanation":"固定时点未见人不等于整段未用。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-13:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What exception should be considered?","answer":"Delay caused by an inaccessible lift","options":["Automatic priority for every late arrival","Delay caused by an inaccessible lift","A permanent right never to check in"],"explanation":"无障碍问题需适当调整。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-13:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why would calling all 21 reservations unused exaggerate the issue?","answer":"Fourteen students collected later","options":["All 95 students cancelled","No loan records existed","Fourteen students collected later"],"explanation":"21中14人后来取到。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-13:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What should precede buying more laptops?","answer":"Checking circulation of existing stock","options":["Ending all collection windows","Checking circulation of existing stock","Assuming every complaint proves shortage"],"explanation":"先核实既有设备流转。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-13:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What is outside the warehouse report’s scope?","answer":"The purchasing system","options":["Missing returned items","The purchasing system","Return queues"],"explanation":"采购另案处理。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-13:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What is the observer’s role when an item seems damaged?","answer":"Leave technical handling to normal staff","options":["Leave technical handling to normal staff","Carry out a safety inspection independently","Ignore every related observation"],"explanation":"观察员不替代技术检查。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-13:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a formal report on the study-room trial. Include purpose, findings, limitations and prioritised recommendations with named responsibilities. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Reporting on a study-room trial”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["报告作者","设施主管","学生代表"],"goal":"将观察、意见和建议分开，商定紧急与长期行动。","twist":"领导要求立即宣布不再需要增加房间，但证据尚不支持。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生运营报告，追踪每条建议对应哪项发现。","writingPrompt":"写220词报告，把维护、流程和容量问题分层。","speakingPrompt":"向管理层解释为何可先改善流程但暂不能裁定容量。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Reporting on a college equipment-loan service”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Reporting on a college equipment-loan service” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-14","level":"C1","title":"资金提案与实施责任","chapter":58,"focus":"写出面向决策者的可执行提案","sourceA":{"title":"Proposing a practical mentoring scheme — extended case review","text":"A fictional adult college is considering peer mentoring for new students. The proposal should explain who would benefit, what mentors could realistically provide and where professional support remains necessary. Enthusiasm alone cannot establish capacity. The writer must specify recruitment, training, supervision and a modest first phase. The intended reader is a budget committee deciding whether the scheme is ready for a trial, not a publicity team seeking a slogan.\n\nA mentor's value may lie in helping a student locate the right service, not in answering every question personally. Training should therefore include how to recognise a concern outside the mentor's role. The committee can authorise a bounded trial while requiring a coordinator to check the quality of referrals and contact.\n\nThe proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life. Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers. A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable. The budget must include training as well as publicity. Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries. Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.\n\nWe should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern. The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility. A review after one term would inform any expansion. The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first. A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish. Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed.\n\nThe mentoring pilot has been costed for twelve mentors and twenty-four new students. Training costs 360 pounds, coordinator time 600 and accessible meeting materials 140, bringing the requested grant to 1,100. A proposed publicity campaign would cost another 300, but the committee asks whether recruitment through existing induction sessions could serve the first cohort. The student representative supports saving publicity costs only if the approach still reaches people who do not attend induction. The safeguarding adviser adds that mentors need a named referral route before recruitment begins. The proposal must therefore explain how a smaller budget preserves essential supervision rather than merely reducing every line by the same percentage."},"sourceB":{"title":"A proposal for an employer-learning partnership","text":"An adult college and a local manufacturing firm discussed a short numeracy programme for employees. The firm offered a meeting room and paid release time for two hours each week. The college would provide a tutor and learning materials. At first, the partnership sounded almost cost-free because both sides described their contributions as existing resources. A detailed proposal showed why that wording was misleading: the tutor's time displaced other teaching, and production teams needed cover while employees attended.\n\nThe first phase would involve sixteen volunteers over eight weeks. The proposal distinguished practical learning from staff appraisal. Individual work would not be shared with line managers, although attendance arrangements and an anonymous programme summary would be available. Employees could discuss access needs privately with the college coordinator. The firm wanted evidence of improved productivity, but the college explained that such a claim would require a different evaluation and could not be inferred from completion alone.\n\nThe budget included preparation, teaching, travel and one joint review meeting. It did not assume that a successful pilot could continue indefinitely under the same staffing arrangements. Before expansion, the partners would examine demand, learner work and the effect on other provision. A possible evening alternative was also considered, but employees working rotating shifts said that fixed evening attendance might exclude them. The proposal therefore retained paid daytime release as an essential condition rather than treating it as a convenient extra.\n\nThe final document named a college coordinator and a firm contact responsible for timetable changes. If release time was withdrawn, the programme would pause while the partners agreed a workable alternative. The proposal was persuasive because it made the exchange explicit, acknowledged opportunity costs and protected the educational purpose. It did not promise that eight weeks would solve every workplace difficulty. The partners agreed to judge the pilot against the aims it could reasonably address and to state separately any wider benefit that remained untested."},"listening":{"teaching":"For the mentoring proposal, we now have a budget for twelve mentors and twenty-four new students. Training is 360 pounds, coordinator time 600 and accessible materials 140. The total request is therefore 1,100 pounds. Publicity would add another 300, but the committee asks whether existing induction sessions could recruit the first group. That may be reasonable, provided we do not miss students who cannot attend induction. The student representative wants a second contact route for them. The safeguarding adviser also makes a clear point: mentors need a named referral route before recruitment begins. We should not save money by removing supervision and then describe the scheme as unchanged. If the grant is reduced, identify which activities are essential to the purpose and which can be postponed. A smaller publicity campaign may be possible; an unsupported group of mentors would be a different and potentially unsuitable service. Your proposal should explain the alternative, not simply reduce every budget line by the same proportion. It should also name the coordinator and the review date. Completion of mentoring meetings is one measure of participation, but it does not show that every student's underlying difficulty was resolved. The first review should examine whether students could reach the support they needed and whether mentors understood the limits of their role.","test":"Thank you for discussing the mobile language workshop with our association. We can provide a room on four Saturday mornings and help contact residents, but we cannot guarantee a minimum number of learners. The tutor will need to confirm what level the sessions are intended for. Advertising them simply as suitable for everyone may create unrealistic expectations. The grant available is 900 pounds. Your draft budget lists 640 for teaching, 160 for travel and 180 for printed materials, which totals 980. Please send a revised proposal rather than assume that the difference will be covered later. We may be able to use digital handouts, but participants without devices will still need an accessible alternative. The venue has a projector; it does not have loan tablets. The committee also wants a named contact if a learner requires support beyond the tutor's role. We would like the first session in February, subject to the agreement being signed by the twentieth of January. Do not begin publicity before the level, access arrangements and final budget are confirmed. After the four sessions, we will review attendance, learner feedback and work samples before considering another grant. We are funding a bounded pilot, not promising a permanent weekly service."},"vocab":[{"head":"proposed scheme","zh":"拟议计划","pos":"phrase","example":"The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","exampleZh":"拟议计划将帮助新生适应日常校园生活。","collocation":"proposed scheme","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v1","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replace","zh":"替代","pos":"verb","example":"Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","exampleZh":"导师提供指引，而非替代专业顾问。","collocation":"replace professional advice","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v2","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cohort","zh":"同批群体","pos":"noun","example":"A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","exampleZh":"首批人数较少有助于管理督导。","collocation":"a small first cohort","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v3","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"publicity","zh":"宣传","pos":"noun","example":"The budget must include training as well as publicity.","exampleZh":"预算必须包括培训及宣传。","collocation":"publicity costs","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v4","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"boundaries","zh":"边界","pos":"noun","example":"Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries.","exampleZh":"潜在益处取决于稳定联系和明确边界。","collocation":"clear boundaries","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v5","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"voluntary","zh":"自愿的","pos":"adjective","example":"Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.","exampleZh":"导师和新生都应自愿参加。","collocation":"voluntary participation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v6","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refer","zh":"转介","pos":"verb","example":"We should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern.","exampleZh":"我们应明确导师如何转介棘手问题。","collocation":"refer a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v7","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coordinator","zh":"协调员","pos":"noun","example":"The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility.","exampleZh":"提案明确协调员，而非假定共同负责。","collocation":"a named coordinator","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v8","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inform","zh":"为……提供依据","pos":"verb","example":"A review after one term would inform any expansion.","exampleZh":"一学期后复查将为扩展提供依据。","collocation":"inform an expansion decision","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v9","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"induction","zh":"入学介绍","pos":"noun","example":"The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first.","exampleZh":"另一选择是先改善现有入学介绍。","collocation":"an induction session","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v10","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establish","zh":"确定","pos":"verb","example":"A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish.","exampleZh":"有说服力的提案会承认尚不能确定之处。","collocation":"establish a benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v11","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"released","zh":"拨付","pos":"verb","example":"Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed.","exampleZh":"只有培训计划获同意后才应拨付资金。","collocation":"funding is released","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v12","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opportunity cost","zh":"机会成本","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Coordinator time has an opportunity cost even within an existing salary.","exampleZh":"即使工资已存在，协调时间仍有机会成本。","collocation":"recognise opportunity cost","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v13","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"safeguard","zh":"保障措施","pos":"noun","example":"A referral route is an essential safeguard for mentors.","exampleZh":"转介渠道是导师的重要保障。","collocation":"an essential safeguard","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v14","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"remit","zh":"职责范围","pos":"noun","example":"The proposal must define the mentor’s remit.","exampleZh":"提案必须界定导师职责范围。","collocation":"within the remit","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v15","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"viable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reduced budget still needs a viable supervision plan.","exampleZh":"预算减少后仍须有可行督导方案。","collocation":"a viable plan","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v16","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why is recruiting solely through induction sessions questioned?","answer":"It may miss students who cannot attend those sessions.","options":["It costs more than the proposed 300-pound publicity campaign.","It eliminates the need to confirm mentor training and supervision.","It may miss students who cannot attend those sessions."],"explanation":"学生代表要求第二渠道覆盖未能参加迎新的人，并非仅看宣传成本。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-14:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"How should the proposal respond to a reduced grant?","answer":"Protect essential supervision while identifying postponable activities.","options":["Recruit mentors first and name a referral route only after problems arise.","Protect essential supervision while identifying postponable activities.","Reduce every line equally and describe the service as unchanged."],"explanation":"提案须区分必要督导转介与可延期活动；不能等出事再设渠道。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-14:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What is the budget before extra publicity?","answer":"1,100 pounds","options":["960 pounds","1,100 pounds","1,400 pounds"],"explanation":"360+600+140=1100。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-14:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"What should not be removed merely to save money?","answer":"Essential supervision and referral support","options":["Essential supervision and referral support","Every optional publicity expense","The ability to postpone activities"],"explanation":"督导和转介不可伪装为非必要。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-14:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why was “almost cost-free” misleading?","answer":"Existing contributions still displaced other work","options":["All employees paid full tuition","Existing contributions still displaced other work","The firm offered no room"],"explanation":"既有资源仍有机会成本和替班成本。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-14:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What is essential to the agreed first phase?","answer":"Paid daytime release","options":["Paid daytime release","Sharing individual work with managers","A guaranteed productivity increase"],"explanation":"轮班限制下带薪日间时间是关键条件。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-14:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"How much does the draft exceed the grant?","answer":"80 pounds","options":["80 pounds","180 pounds","160 pounds"],"explanation":"980减900等于80。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-14:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"When may publicity start?","answer":"After level, access and budget are confirmed","options":["Immediately before agreement","Once any learner expresses interest","After level, access and budget are confirmed"],"explanation":"先落实定位、可及性和预算。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-14:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a funding proposal for the mentoring scheme. Define its scope, costs, supervision, alternatives and criteria for deciding whether to continue after one term. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Proposing a practical mentoring scheme”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["提案作者","资助委员会","学生代表"],"goal":"在削减预算时保留安全、可及性及核心教育目的。","twist":"资助减少20%，但宣传渠道不能只覆盖参加迎新的学生。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"十天后读陌生合作提案，识别看似免费的资源成本。","writingPrompt":"写220词资助申请，说明投入、范围、风险与停止条件。","speakingPrompt":"在被要求承诺无法验证的成果时，准确说明能评估什么。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “A proposal for an employer-learning partnership”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “A proposal for an employer-learning partnership” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-15","level":"C1","title":"综合项目与现场答辩","chapter":60,"focus":"独立完成综合报告并回应质疑","sourceA":{"title":"Designing an accessible community learning weekend — extended case review","text":"The original integrated project concerns a fictional learning weekend combining practical workshops, talks and peer support. Learners act as an advisory team, not as event promoters. They must compare demand with capacity, identify access requirements and propose a limited programme with an evaluation plan. The project brings together reading, explanation, negotiation and revision. Completing it provides evidence for review but does not by itself certify C1 proficiency.\n\nA single weekend cannot demonstrate every long-term educational outcome. It can nevertheless provide evidence about access, participation and the feasibility of activities. The advisory team should identify which findings would support repetition, which would require redesign and which questions would remain unanswered even if visitors enjoyed the event.\n\nOur central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance. The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators. We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support. The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments. Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only. The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.\n\nI would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim. The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself. A review should include work produced as well as participant comments. Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off. The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions. Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level.\n\nThe weekend team has narrowed its proposal to six workshops and two public talks, with a total capacity of 96 workshop places. A person attending two workshops would occupy two places, so the figure must not be advertised as 96 distinct learners. Four facilitators are confirmed and two are still being approached. The team has also reserved one quiet room rather than scheduling activities in every available space. A reviewer questions whether this is an inefficient use of the venue. The response should explain the room's access function and the evidence needed to judge demand, while acknowledging the opportunity cost of not using it for another workshop."},"sourceB":{"title":"Defending a cross-campus research showcase","text":"A student team submitted a proposal for a research showcase linking three campuses. The plan included twelve short presentations, a travelling exhibition and an online discussion. During the review, the panel asked what the project was primarily intended to achieve. The team said it wanted researchers from different departments to understand one another's methods. The panel then questioned whether moving display boards between campuses was the best use of the limited budget.\n\nThe original budget allocated 2,400 pounds to transport and installation, 900 to access support and 700 to facilitation. The available grant was 3,500, leaving a 500-pound gap. One team member proposed cutting access support first because transport had already been discussed with a supplier. Another argued that a conversation with a supplier was not a binding obligation and should not determine the project's priorities. The panel asked the team to reconsider the sequence rather than treat the first estimate as fixed.\n\nA revised plan kept the online discussion and six presentations, produced digital displays and retained access support. Transport costs fell, but the team acknowledged that not every researcher would receive an in-person platform during the first phase. Selection criteria would therefore be published, and those not selected could contribute questions and material to the online discussion. The project would record whether participants could explain a method from another department afterwards, not merely whether they liked the event.\n\nThe panel requested a revised budget and confirmation of facilitator availability before deciding. It did not certify the educational value of the project simply because the presentation was fluent. In a reflection, the team explained which assumptions changed during questioning and which core purpose remained. The review demonstrated why effective advanced communication includes revising a defensible plan under challenge. A polished opening can introduce a project, but the quality of reasoning becomes clearer when resources, access and competing priorities must be reconciled in conversation."},"listening":{"teaching":"Let us prepare for the learning-weekend review. The revised programme has six workshops and two public talks. The workshops provide 96 places, but not necessarily 96 different learners, because one person may attend more than one session. Use the correct unit in the publicity. Four facilitators are confirmed and two are only being approached. If you present all six as secured, the panel will be unable to assess whether the programme is ready. We have also reserved a quiet room. That means one fewer activity room, so it has an opportunity cost. The team should explain its access function rather than pretend that reserving it has no trade-off. A reviewer may ask how demand will be judged. You could propose confidential feedback and observation of whether the arrangement supports participation, without requiring people to disclose private information publicly. During the presentation, begin with the central purpose and recommendation. Then show how the programme fits the confirmed resources. If a question reveals that a claim is too broad, narrow it explicitly. Do not defend a misleading figure because it appeared on your first slide. After the review, your reflection should explain any change to the proposal and the reasoning behind it. That is evidence of responsive communication, not an admission that preparation was pointless.","test":"The project panel has reviewed your proposal for a neighbourhood oral-history event. We are inviting a revision rather than rejecting the idea. Please reduce the first event to one venue and explain how recorded contributions will be stored and used. Your draft budget allowed 300 pounds for recording equipment, but it did not include transcription or the time needed to explain consent. Those tasks are essential if the recordings are to become a usable archive. You may borrow equipment from the college, subject to training, which could release funds for those activities. We also need confirmation that the venue has a private space for contributors who do not want to speak in front of a group. The panel does not require a target of fifty recordings. It would prefer a smaller number collected responsibly with clear information about future use. Submit the revised budget and access plan by the tenth of June. Two reviewers will then ask you questions online for fifteen minutes. You may bring notes, but please be ready to explain alternatives rather than read the proposal aloud. If the revised conditions cannot be met by that date, contact the coordinator to discuss postponement before making public commitments."},"vocab":[{"head":"central purpose","zh":"核心目的","pos":"phrase","example":"Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","exampleZh":"我们的核心目的是可及学习，而非最大人数。","collocation":"central purpose","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v1","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"match","zh":"匹配","pos":"verb","example":"The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","exampleZh":"首期安排应与受训引导员人数相匹配。","collocation":"match available resources","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v2","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirmed","zh":"已确认的","pos":"adjective","example":"We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","exampleZh":"我们已经区分确认资源和申请中的支持。","collocation":"confirmed resources","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v3","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjustment","zh":"调整","pos":"noun","example":"The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments.","exampleZh":"可及性计划包含申请个别调整的渠道。","collocation":"a necessary adjustment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v4","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reach","zh":"触及","pos":"verb","example":"Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only.","exampleZh":"需求仍不确定，因为调查仅触及现有会员。","collocation":"reach existing members","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v5","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limited form","zh":"有限形式","pos":"phrase","example":"The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.","exampleZh":"调查该缺口期间，项目可以有限形式开展。","collocation":"limited form","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v6","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"challenge","zh":"质疑","pos":"noun","example":"I would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim.","exampleZh":"我会通过收窄主张来回应这一质疑。","collocation":"respond to a challenge","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v7","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"衡量体现","pos":"verb","example":"The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself.","exampleZh":"拟议指标衡量参与，而非学习本身。","collocation":"capture participation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v8","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"work produced","zh":"产出作品","pos":"phrase","example":"A review should include work produced as well as participant comments.","exampleZh":"复核应包括产出作品及参与者意见。","collocation":"work produced","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v9","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reasoning","zh":"推理理由","pos":"noun","example":"Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off.","exampleZh":"我们的报告记录每项重要取舍背后的理由。","collocation":"clear reasoning","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v10","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assumption","zh":"假设","pos":"noun","example":"The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions.","exampleZh":"最终展示应接受对不确定假设的提问。","collocation":"check an assumption","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v11","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Independent language review","zh":"独立语言评估","pos":"phrase","example":"Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level.","exampleZh":"在声称达到C1前，仍需独立语言评估。","collocation":"Independent language review","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v12","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconcile","zh":"协调一致","pos":"verb","example":"The team must reconcile access needs with limited space.","exampleZh":"团队须协调可及需求与有限空间。","collocation":"reconcile competing priorities","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v13","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"有理据可支持的","pos":"adjective","example":"A defensible plan can change under questioning.","exampleZh":"有理据的方案可以在质询后改变。","collocation":"a defensible judgement","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v14","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"binding","zh":"有约束力的","pos":"adjective","example":"An initial discussion is not a binding commitment.","exampleZh":"初步讨论不是有约束力的承诺。","collocation":"a binding commitment","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v15","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responsiveness","zh":"回应与调整能力","pos":"noun","example":"The review assesses responsiveness as well as prepared explanation.","exampleZh":"复核评估即时回应能力及预备解释。","collocation":"demonstrate responsiveness","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v16","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why would advertising ninety-six distinct learners misrepresent the programme?","answer":"A single learner may occupy places in more than one workshop.","options":["All ninety-six workshop places have already been cancelled.","A single learner may occupy places in more than one workshop.","The figure includes all public-talk places as confirmed learners."],"explanation":"96是工作坊席位，个人可重复参加，不等于96独立学习者。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-15:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What makes the quiet-room decision a genuine trade-off?","answer":"It supports access while removing space for another activity.","options":["It supports access while removing space for another activity.","It requires no resources beyond those already committed.","It proves that every visitor will need the room equally."],"explanation":"安静室有可及功能，也占去一个活动空间；需解释用途与机会成本。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-15:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What does 96 measure?","answer":"Workshop places","options":["Workshop places","Distinct confirmed learners","Public talks"],"explanation":"96是席位人次容量，非独立人数。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-15:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"How many facilitators are confirmed?","answer":"Four","options":["Six","Two","Four"],"explanation":"四人确认，两人正在联系。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-15:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"What conflict did the first budget create?","answer":"It exceeded funding while making transport appear prematurely fixed","options":["It exceeded funding while making transport appear prematurely fixed","It omitted every access cost","It had no funding gap"],"explanation":"4000总额对3500资金，且先谈供应商不等于不能调整。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-15:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"What outcome measure did the revised project include?","answer":"Explaining a method from another department","options":["Only liking the event","The number of moved display boards","Explaining a method from another department"],"explanation":"跨学科解释能力比单纯满意更贴近目标。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-15:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"What is missing from the oral-history budget?","answer":"Transcription and consent-explanation time","options":["Every equipment estimate","The existence of a venue","Transcription and consent-explanation time"],"explanation":"遗漏转写及知情说明工时。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-15:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What does the panel prefer to fifty recordings?","answer":"A smaller responsibly collected set","options":["No consent process","A smaller responsibly collected set","An unrestricted public target"],"explanation":"质量和负责收集优先于数量。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-15:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write the advisory report for the learning weekend. Define scope, evidence, access, budget responsibilities and evaluation, then prepare responses to two serious objections. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Designing an accessible community learning weekend”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["项目团队","评审员","无障碍代表"],"goal":"在现场质疑中保留核心目的并修订可执行方案。","twist":"评审要求缩减活动但保留访问支持，团队需说明选择标准。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"两周后读陌生项目评审意见，区分拒绝、补件与有条件支持。","writingPrompt":"写220词修订说明，记录两个改变及保持不变的目的。","speakingPrompt":"六分钟答辩后回应未预先给出的资源和伦理质疑。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Defending a cross-campus research showcase”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Defending a cross-campus research showcase” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c1-16","level":"C1","title":"能力证据与独立复核","chapter":64,"focus":"分开课程完成、实际能力和考试准备","sourceA":{"title":"Building a defensible language portfolio — extended case review","text":"The final original briefing concerns a fictional learner preparing an independent review. A portfolio can show completed tasks and responses to feedback, but familiar work alone does not establish how the learner handles new material. The review therefore includes unseen listening and reading, independent writing and interaction with another person. It reports strengths and limitations separately. Neither course completion nor a local transcription score is treated as an official CEFR or IELTS result.\n\nA reviewer needs enough information to distinguish a stable ability from a successful rehearsed performance. The learner should therefore label assistance, revisions and repeated tasks honestly. The resulting profile can guide further practice even when the evidence is too limited to support a single confident level for all four skills.\n\nMy portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work. Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer. I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed. Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately. A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance. An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.\n\nA disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence. I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning. The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error. A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer. Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate. Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty.\n\nThe learner's portfolio now contains six revised essays, three unrehearsed recordings and two reading tasks completed with a dictionary. The index labels the conditions for every item. An assessor notes that the revised essays show useful editing skill but cannot be treated as unaided timed writing. One spoken task was repeated after feedback, so the first and second versions remain together. The learner proposes a new unfamiliar task after two weeks to check whether the improvement transfers. The review will report skill-specific strengths and gaps. It will not convert the number of completed items into a guaranteed examination result or a single automatically awarded C1 level."},"sourceB":{"title":"Evaluating a workplace communication course","text":"A company reviewed an English communication course for its international project teams. The training provider reported that 92 percent of participants had completed all online units. Managers wanted to know whether meetings had become clearer. Completion data could answer a participation question, but it did not directly establish how employees handled spontaneous disagreement or explained technical uncertainty to unfamiliar colleagues.\n\nThe review used three additional sources: samples of written updates, role-play meetings with new scenarios and voluntary accounts from colleagues. Written samples were labelled according to whether they had been revised with tutor support. A polished update remained evidence of editing and learning, but it was not described as an unaided first attempt. The role-plays introduced a change halfway through so that participants could not rely entirely on a prepared explanation.\n\nTwo reviewers assessed the same selected performances using agreed criteria. They considered clarity, relevance, organisation, language control and interaction. When their judgements differed substantially, they returned to the recording and identified the evidence supporting each view. In two cases they requested another sample instead of hiding the disagreement inside an average. They also noted that a transcript could not fully show timing, intelligibility or how a speaker responded to interruption.\n\nThe report found uneven profiles. Some participants wrote clear updates but needed support in rapid discussion; others interacted effectively while producing ambiguous written instructions. Each received a targeted plan and an opportunity to complete a new task after several weeks. The company did not translate course completion into an official examination band. Its purpose was to identify usable communication strengths and remaining risks in relevant work. The provider supported this distinction and retained completion as one measure among several. A credible evaluation explained the limits of its evidence, rather than making its conclusion sound more certain by combining different kinds of progress into one impressive score."},"listening":{"teaching":"Before the independent review, check the conditions attached to every portfolio item. We have six revised essays, three unrehearsed recordings and two reading tasks completed with a dictionary. Those are different forms of evidence. The revised essays can show editing skill, but they should not be labelled unaided timed writing. The reading tasks also need their dictionary use recorded. That does not make the work worthless; it tells the reviewer what can reasonably be inferred. One speaking task was repeated after feedback. Keep both versions so that the change can be examined. Then use a new unfamiliar task after two weeks to see whether the improvement transfers. If the learner simply repeats the same response, we cannot separate memory for that answer from a more general ability. The final report should describe listening, reading, writing and interaction separately, including any gaps in the sample. A local transcription score does not establish every aspect of pronunciation or conversational performance. A completion badge is not an official language certificate. You may use the profile to decide what to practise next, but do not convert the number of finished activities into a guaranteed examination result. Honest labels make the portfolio more informative, not less valuable.","test":"Your independent review appointment is confirmed for Monday at nine forty-five. Please bring identification and a short index of your work, but do not bring a prepared answer to the speaking question. The first task will be an unfamiliar reading passage. You may make notes, but a dictionary is not permitted in that particular task. The listening task follows after a five-minute break. Writing will be completed independently in forty minutes. If you need an agreed access adjustment, contact us before Friday so that the conditions can be recorded consistently. The spoken interaction includes another participant, and the assessor may introduce a new practical constraint during the discussion. We want to see how you respond, not whether you remember a fixed script. Your report will distinguish observed performance from areas where the sample is insufficient. It is an internal review and will not issue an official examination certificate. If two assessors disagree materially, we may invite an additional short sample before finalising the profile. You will receive feedback within ten working days and can request clarification of a comment. A later retest will use new material so that it can examine transfer rather than familiarity with this appointment. The coordinator can explain the recorded assessment conditions."},"vocab":[{"head":"first attempts","zh":"首次作答","pos":"phrase","example":"My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","exampleZh":"我的档案区分首次作答和修改作品。","collocation":"first attempts","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v1","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"迁移","pos":"noun","example":"Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","exampleZh":"熟悉材料能展示学习，却不能证明普遍迁移。","collocation":"general transfer","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v2","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rehearse","zh":"排练","pos":"verb","example":"I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","exampleZh":"我需要未经排练任务的证据。","collocation":"rehearse an answer","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v3","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"separately","zh":"分别地","pos":"adverb","example":"Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately.","exampleZh":"听力、阅读、写作和互动应分别评估。","collocation":"review separately","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v4","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transcript","zh":"转写文本","pos":"noun","example":"A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance.","exampleZh":"转写文本不能证明口语表现的每个方面。","collocation":"a speaking transcript","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v5","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permitted","zh":"允许的","pos":"adjective","example":"An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.","exampleZh":"外部评阅者应了解允许了哪些帮助。","collocation":"permitted assistance","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v6","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disagreement","zh":"分歧","pos":"noun","example":"A disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence.","exampleZh":"评阅者分歧需要检验证据。","collocation":"reviewer disagreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v7","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uneven profile","zh":"不均衡能力分布","pos":"phrase","example":"I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning.","exampleZh":"我会把不均衡能力作为规划信息。","collocation":"uneven profile","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v8","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recurring","zh":"反复出现的","pos":"adjective","example":"The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error.","exampleZh":"下一阶段计划应针对反复弱点，而非所有可能错误。","collocation":"recurring weaknesses","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v9","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"retention","zh":"保持","pos":"noun","example":"A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer.","exampleZh":"使用新材料的延迟任务有助于检查保持和迁移。","collocation":"long-term retention","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v10","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proficiency","zh":"熟练程度","pos":"noun","example":"Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate.","exampleZh":"课程完成不等于官方能力证书。","collocation":"language proficiency","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v11","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertainty","zh":"不确定性","pos":"noun","example":"Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty.","exampleZh":"任何分数估计都应说明证据及不确定性。","collocation":"state uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v12","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unaided","zh":"无帮助的","pos":"adjective","example":"An unaided sample must be distinguished from supported revision.","exampleZh":"无帮助样本须与受支持修改区分。","collocation":"unaided performance","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v13","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"materially","zh":"实质上","pos":"adverb","example":"If assessors disagree materially, further evidence may be needed.","exampleZh":"若评阅者实质分歧，可能需要更多证据。","collocation":"differ materially","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v14","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"triangulate","zh":"多来源交叉核查","pos":"verb","example":"Reviewers triangulate recordings, written work and task conditions.","exampleZh":"评阅者交叉核查录音、作品与任务条件。","collocation":"triangulate evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v15","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"calibration","zh":"评分校准","pos":"noun","example":"Calibration requires shared criteria and discussion of actual samples.","exampleZh":"校准需要共同标准和实际样本讨论。","collocation":"reviewer calibration","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v16","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"}],"questions":[{"prompt":"Why does the portfolio retain both versions of a repeated speaking task?","answer":"To make feedback-supported change visible without calling it unrehearsed.","options":["To make feedback-supported change visible without calling it unrehearsed.","To treat the second version as evidence of an official examination result.","To replace every unfamiliar task with a better memorised answer."],"explanation":"前后版本一起保留可看修改变化，同时准确标明反馈及重复条件。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-16:practice:q0"},{"prompt":"What would the unfamiliar task after two weeks help investigate?","answer":"Whether improvement transfers beyond the previously practised response.","options":["Whether completing more portfolio items automatically establishes C1.","Whether dictionary-supported reading equals unaided timed performance.","Whether improvement transfers beyond the previously practised response."],"explanation":"延迟新任务可检验保持和迁移，避免把记住旧答当一般能力。","phase":"practice","skill":"reading","id":"c1-16:practice:q1"},{"prompt":"What was allowed in two portfolio reading tasks?","answer":"Dictionary use","options":["Tutor-written answers","No reading at all","Dictionary use"],"explanation":"两项使用词典，须标注。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-16:practice:q2"},{"prompt":"Why use a new task after two weeks?","answer":"To examine transfer beyond the practised answer","options":["To remove the first recordings","To examine transfer beyond the practised answer","To guarantee an official score"],"explanation":"新任务检验迁移而非熟悉答案。","phase":"practice","skill":"listening","id":"c1-16:practice:q3"},{"prompt":"Why label tutor-supported written samples?","answer":"To clarify what the performance can establish","options":["To exclude every revised text as worthless","To guarantee an examination band","To clarify what the performance can establish"],"explanation":"标注帮助用于合理解读证据。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-16:assessment:q4"},{"prompt":"How were substantial reviewer disagreements handled?","answer":"Evidence was reconsidered and sometimes another sample requested","options":["Only completion counts were used","Evidence was reconsidered and sometimes another sample requested","They were hidden inside a single average"],"explanation":"回看证据并必要时补样。","phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","id":"c1-16:assessment:q5"},{"prompt":"Is a dictionary permitted in the appointment’s reading task?","answer":"No","options":["Only if no notes are taken","No","Yes without limit"],"explanation":"本次独立阅读明确禁词典。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-16:assessment:q6"},{"prompt":"What may follow material assessor disagreement?","answer":"An additional short sample","options":["An additional short sample","Automatic award of C1","Removal of all feedback"],"explanation":"重大分歧可能请求补样。","phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","id":"c1-16:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a learning-evidence report. Distinguish completed study from independent performance, identify missing evidence in each skill and propose a realistic follow-up assessment plan. Use the expanded teaching case. Explain the significance of its new evidence and address a reasonable alternative interpretation.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["立场与任务目的明确，说明证据如何支持具体结论。","准确使用数据和时间范围，不编造来源或原因。","发展一个真实反方理由，并说明条件或限制。","完成后检查组织、语域、搭配与句法；保留初稿及修改记录。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Speak for about two minutes on the teaching case “Building a defensible language portfolio”. Explain your view, develop an example, and identify a limitation. Do not use the independent assessment text. Incorporate one new detail from the extended case and state why it matters.","followups":["Which detail most strongly supports your view, and what does it fail to establish?","What would a reasonable opponent say, and how would you respond without exaggeration?","If resources were reduced by one third, which part of your proposal would you preserve and why?"]},"interaction":{"roles":["学习者","两名评阅者"],"goal":"形成四技能证据档案及下一阶段训练计划。","twist":"两位评阅者意见不同，且最优秀写作使用过导师帮助。"},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"三周后完成一篇从未读过的长文，记录条件和推理过程。","writingPrompt":"在独立限时条件下写新议题报告，再与支持修改作品分别存档。","speakingPrompt":"与陌生搭档讨论新约束，不用旧题脚本，接受人工复核。"},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Using only the independent case “Evaluating a workplace communication course”, write a briefing for a decision-maker who has not read it. Explain the central issue, evaluate two relevant details, address a reasonable alternative interpretation and recommend a justified next step. Do not reuse the teaching-case solution or invent evidence.","minimumWords":220,"checklist":["使用独立B情境及其事实，不套用教学A对象。","清楚区分已知信息、推断和建议。","解释反方最强理由，准确保留条件和范围。","独立完成并保留原稿，按内容、组织、语域和语言人工复核。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Explain the independent case “Evaluating a workplace communication course” to a colleague and propose a response. Select relevant details without reading the passage aloud. The assessor will introduce an unfamiliar constraint after your opening.","followups":["Which assumption would you check before committing to your recommendation?","What is the strongest reasonable objection, and how would it affect your position?","How would your response change if the available time were halved?"]}},{"id":"c2-01","level":"C2","title":"精确措辞与责任边界","chapter":65,"focus":"Control the scope of a public claim when evidence and audience expectations differ.","vocab":[{"head":"plausible","zh":"似乎合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A plausible account is not necessarily the most probable one.","exampleZh":"似乎合理的解释不一定就是最可能的解释。","collocation":"a plausible account","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v1","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establishing","zh":"证明","pos":"verb","example":"The filter reduced visible particles without establishing that the water was safe.","exampleZh":"过滤器减少了可见颗粒，却没有证明水可以安全饮用。","collocation":"establishing safety","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v2","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualify the claim","zh":"给论断加上限定","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We should qualify the claim before it acquires a life of its own.","exampleZh":"我们应在这一说法自行传播之前加上限定。","collocation":"qualify the claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v3","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"holds","zh":"成立","pos":"verb","example":"The result holds under the conditions specified in the report.","exampleZh":"这一结果在报告明确的条件下成立。","collocation":"the result holds","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v4","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"amount to","zh":"等同于","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Absence of detected harm does not amount to evidence of harmlessness.","exampleZh":"未检测到危害不等于存在无害的证据。","collocation":"amount to evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v5","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conveys","zh":"传达","pos":"verb","example":"Her wording conveys confidence without implying that the question is settled.","exampleZh":"她的措辞表现出信心，却没有暗示问题已有定论。","collocation":"conveys confidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v6","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substantiates","zh":"用证据支持","pos":"verb","example":"The observation substantiates a narrower claim than the advertisement makes.","exampleZh":"这一观察结果支持的论断比广告所作的更有限。","collocation":"substantiates a claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v7","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refers to","zh":"指的是","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Effectiveness here refers to clarity rather than chemical purity.","exampleZh":"这里的有效指的是清澈程度，而不是化学纯度。","collocation":"refers to clarity","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v8","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reserve","zh":"留作专用","pos":"verb","example":"I would reserve the word proven for a substantially stronger body of evidence.","exampleZh":"我会把已证实这个词留给强得多的证据体系。","collocation":"reserve the word","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v9","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consequential","zh":"关系到实际后果的","pos":"adjective","example":"The distinction is consequential rather than merely terminological.","exampleZh":"这个区别关系到实际后果，而不只是术语。","collocation":"consequential distinction","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v10","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"count as","zh":"算作","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Could you specify what would count as evidence against your interpretation?","exampleZh":"你能明确什么样的证据会推翻你的解读吗？","collocation":"count as evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v11","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"有依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"The revised label is less dramatic but considerably more defensible.","exampleZh":"修改后的标签没那么醒目，但有依据得多。","collocation":"a defensible conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v12","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scope","zh":"适用范围","pos":"noun","example":"The scope of the claim must remain visible.","exampleZh":"论断的适用范围必须清楚可见。","collocation":"the scope of a claim","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v13","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclaimer","zh":"免责声明","pos":"noun","example":"A disclaimer cannot repair a misleading central message.","exampleZh":"免责声明不能补救误导的核心信息。","collocation":"a misleading disclaimer","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v14","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"warrant","zh":"正当依据","pos":"noun","example":"The observation supplies no warrant for that guarantee.","exampleZh":"观察结果未给该保证提供依据。","collocation":"a warrant for","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v15","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"misconception","zh":"误解","pos":"noun","example":"The demonstration could reinforce a dangerous misconception.","exampleZh":"演示可能强化危险的误解。","collocation":"reinforce a misconception","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v16","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a neighbourhood water laboratory：扩展论证","text":"At a neighbourhood water laboratory, participants considered whether to describe its new filter as effective under the conditions tested. The background was specific: twelve household samples became clearer after filtration, although the trial did not measure dissolved contaminants or long term maintenance. However, clear water was being mistaken for safe water in the public demonstration. The proposed response was to publish the measured outcomes beside the unmeasured risks and remove the unqualified word safe.\n\nThe public demonstration creates a particular responsibility. Spectators can see a change in appearance immediately, whereas the untested chemical properties remain invisible. The institution should not rely on a small disclaimer to undo a striking visual suggestion of safety. I would place the measured outcome beside the demonstration itself and explain which tests were not conducted. The laboratory could then decide whether additional testing is feasible. If it is not, the narrower claim can still be useful. People may value a filter that removes visible particles, provided they do not confuse that benefit with protection from every contaminant. Responsible communication makes the boundary part of the main message rather than an inconvenient technical footnote. A plausible account is not necessarily the most probable one. The filter reduced visible particles without establishing that the water was safe. We should qualify the claim before it acquires a life of its own. The result holds under the conditions specified in the report. Absence of detected harm does not amount to evidence of harmlessness. Her wording conveys confidence without implying that the question is settled. The observation substantiates a narrower claim than the advertisement makes.\n\nThe laboratory subsequently receives a request to supply filters for a school event. This changes the decision without changing the original result. A classroom demonstration of clearer water and advice about what pupils may drink carry different responsibilities. The team should explain that the test did not establish drinking safety before accepting the invitation. It could still demonstrate particle removal using water clearly labelled as unsuitable for consumption. Naming that boundary does not weaken a scientific lesson; it teaches why a visible effect and a practical guarantee are not interchangeable. The organisers should confirm the wording on signs as well as in the spoken introduction, since spectators may arrive after the explanation."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Quiet Label","text":"A fictional company designs sound absorbing panels for shared offices. In a small demonstration room, visitors find recorded speech less distracting after the panels are installed. The company proposes the label concentration guaranteed. Its engineer objects: the demonstration measured visitors immediate impressions, not their performance on sustained work. The recorded speech also came from a fixed speaker, whereas real offices contain moving conversations, telephone calls and unpredictable interruptions.\n\nThe marketing team argues that customers understand guaranteed as enthusiasm rather than a technical promise. That assumption is precisely what needs scrutiny. A customer deciding whether to spend money may reasonably treat the word as a claim about a result. A phrase that attracts attention by sounding stronger cannot be defended merely by insisting that attentive readers should interpret it weakly.\n\nThe engineer does not recommend discarding the observation. The panels may genuinely improve perceived comfort under the tested conditions. That finding can be described accurately, with the room dimensions and type of sound identified. The firm could also arrange a practical trial in a working office and decide in advance which outcomes to assess. Task performance, self reported distraction and employee preference would provide different kinds of information rather than interchangeable measures of concentration.\n\nA purchasing manager adds another concern. Some workers need to hear colleagues calling across the room. A reduction in audible speech could help one activity while hindering another. This does not refute the comfort result; it shows why a universal benefit is unlikely to follow from a narrow demonstration. The recommendation must connect the product with the use for which it is appropriate.\n\nThe final proposal replaces the guarantee with a description of reduced perceived speech distraction in the demonstration. It invites purchasers to test a limited installation before expanding. The wording is less memorable, but it lets customers distinguish a measured impression from a promised improvement in work. If later testing supports a stronger claim, the company can revise it. Until then, precision protects the useful result from being undermined by an ambition the evidence cannot carry.\n\nPurchasers should be able to discover these boundaries before committing funds, not only after a disappointing installation."},"listening":{"teaching":"When our archive described a scanned collection as complete, we meant that every item in the box had been scanned. Several readers understood the word differently. They assumed that the collection contained every letter written by the person whose name appeared on it. We had not established that, and some correspondence was known to be held elsewhere.\n\nThe problem was not solved by saying that complete was technically accurate from our perspective. The relevant question was what a reader could reasonably take the label to mean. The catalogue title provided a persons name but did not name the physical box. Our internal boundary was therefore invisible to the audience.\n\nWe changed the description to say that all items in the donated box were available digitally. We also added a note explaining that this did not constitute a complete record of the correspondence. The scans themselves did not change, and their value was not reduced. What changed was the scope of the claim made around them.\n\nThis distinction matters when institutions communicate with people who do not know their working conventions. A familiar internal term can carry an unexpected public implication. We now review prominent labels before adding detailed notes, because a note hidden several pages away cannot reliably correct the first impression. Precision is not simply a matter of avoiding false sentences. It also requires examining the boundary an ordinary reader is likely to infer and deciding whether the available evidence supports that boundary.","test":"A community programme advertised its evening workshop as accessible to everyone. The room had a step free entrance, and the organiser believed the description was justified. A prospective participant asked about the hearing loop. Another asked whether the last bus left before the workshop ended. Neither question had been considered when the advertisement was written.\n\nThe organiser initially replied that accessible referred to the entrance. That explanation revealed the narrow sense intended, but it did not establish that the public wording conveyed that sense. Everyone suggested a broader range of needs. A reader could reasonably expect information about participation, not only entry into the building.\n\nThe team revised the advertisement to describe the entrance specifically, state what equipment was available and invite questions about other requirements. They also moved the finishing time so that public transport remained practical. This did not prove that every possible barrier had disappeared. It made known conditions visible and created a way to discuss needs not yet identified.\n\nI would preserve the organisers useful work on physical entry while qualifying the original universal claim. Calling the entire programme a failure would ignore the improvements. Repeating the claim unchanged would ignore what the questions had revealed. The stronger response is to distinguish established provisions from unresolved requirements, identify who can answer further questions and update the description as information changes. A practical invitation can be welcoming without promising a level of inclusion that nobody has yet checked."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why does the school invitation change the decision?","options":["It introduces a use with different responsibilities.","It changes the filters measured chemistry.","It proves the original demonstration false."],"answer":"It introduces a use with different responsibilities.","explanation":"用途改变责任，不改变已测结果。","id":"c2-01:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What should accompany the spoken warning?","options":["A stronger safety guarantee.","Visible wording for later arrivals.","Removal of all demonstrations."],"answer":"Visible wording for later arrivals.","explanation":"后来者可能听不到开场说明。","id":"c2-01:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is enthusiasm an inadequate defence?","options":["Customers never understand figurative language.","The panels have no acoustic effect.","Customers may reasonably infer a promised result."],"answer":"Customers may reasonably infer a promised result.","explanation":"受众合理理解构成评估边界。","id":"c2-01:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why mention workers hearing colleagues?","options":["Benefits depend partly on the intended activity.","The demonstration secretly included moving speakers.","Self reports cannot convey comfort."],"answer":"Benefits depend partly on the intended activity.","explanation":"同一变化可能利弊并存。","id":"c2-01:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What boundary was invisible?","options":["The identity of the correspondent.","Completion of one donated box.","The existence of the scans."],"answer":"Completion of one donated box.","explanation":"内部范围未在标题中说明。","id":"c2-01:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What does the revised label preserve?","options":["A claim to all correspondence.","A guarantee that no letters are missing.","The scans value with a narrower claim."],"answer":"The scans value with a narrower claim.","explanation":"价值保留，论断收窄。","id":"c2-01:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the questions expose?","options":["A broader public meaning than the intended one.","The absence of a step free entrance.","An agreement that timing was irrelevant."],"answer":"A broader public meaning than the intended one.","explanation":"入口可达不等于全部参与条件。","id":"c2-01:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What remains unresolved?","options":["Whether the entrance exists.","Whether every possible barrier has been removed.","Whether the team changed its wording."],"answer":"Whether every possible barrier has been removed.","explanation":"改进并非普遍无障碍认证。","id":"c2-01:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write public demonstration guidance distinguishing clearer water from established drinking safety, including a school event.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Control the scope of a public claim when evidence and audience expectations differ.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Advise purchasers about The Quiet Label. Explain the tested outcome, the guarantee problem and a proportionate next trial. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Quiet Label. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why is enthusiasm an inadequate defence?","Why mention workers hearing colleagues?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["technical adviser","communications manager"],"goal":"Agree a prominent claim whose ordinary interpretation matches the evidence.","twist":"A customer has already relied on the stronger wording and asks for reassurance."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving control the scope of a public claim when evidence and audience expectations differ. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-02","level":"C2","title":"隐喻理解与行动澄清","chapter":66,"focus":"Recover practical meaning without treating an idiom as either literal fact or a complete instruction.","vocab":[{"head":"on thin ice","zh":"处于不稳妥的状态","pos":"idiom","example":"The opening scene is on thin ice, so another rehearsal would help.","exampleZh":"开场这一幕还不稳妥，因此再排练一次会有帮助。","collocation":"be on thin ice","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v1","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"figuratively","zh":"以比喻方式","pos":"adverb","example":"I took the warning figuratively rather than as a literal instruction.","exampleZh":"我把这句警告理解为比喻，而非字面指令。","collocation":"interpret figuratively","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v2","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pull the plug","zh":"终止项目","pos":"idiom","example":"Before we pull the plug, let us establish what can still be repaired.","exampleZh":"在终止项目之前，我们先确定还有哪些地方可以补救。","collocation":"pull the plug","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v3","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"testing the waters","zh":"试探反应","pos":"idiom","example":"She was testing the waters rather than committing the committee to a decision.","exampleZh":"她是在试探反应，而非让委员会承诺作出决定。","collocation":"testing the waters","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v4","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fell flat","zh":"未能奏效","pos":"idiom","example":"The joke fell flat because its cultural reference was unfamiliar.","exampleZh":"这个笑话没有效果，因为其中的文化背景不为人熟悉。","collocation":"a joke fell flat","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v5","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"soften criticism","zh":"缓和批评","pos":"verb phrase","example":"An idiom can soften criticism while making its practical meaning less explicit.","exampleZh":"习语可以缓和批评，同时让实际含义不那么明确。","collocation":"soften criticism","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v6","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shaky","zh":"不稳妥的","pos":"adjective","example":"When you say the scene is shaky, which change are you requesting?","exampleZh":"你说这一幕不稳妥，具体希望作出什么修改？","collocation":"a shaky scene","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v7","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"vulnerability","zh":"脆弱性","pos":"noun","example":"The metaphor implies vulnerability, not inevitable failure.","exampleZh":"这个比喻暗示的是脆弱，而非必然失败。","collocation":"imply vulnerability","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v8","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"avoid","zh":"避免","pos":"verb","example":"I would avoid that expression until I knew how the audience understood it.","exampleZh":"在了解听众如何理解之前，我会避免使用那个表达。","collocation":"avoid an expression","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v9","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"formal cancellation","zh":"正式取消","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Her colourful phrasing should not be mistaken for a formal cancellation.","exampleZh":"她生动的措辞不应被误认为正式取消。","collocation":"formal cancellation","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v10","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surrounding exchange","zh":"前后的对话","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The expression makes sense once the surrounding exchange is considered.","exampleZh":"考虑前后的对话后，这个表达就说得通了。","collocation":"the surrounding exchange","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v11","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preserved","zh":"保留了","pos":"verb","example":"A short clarification preserved the humour and prevented a costly misunderstanding.","exampleZh":"简短的澄清保留了幽默，也避免了代价高昂的误解。","collocation":"preserved the humour","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v12","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"figurative","zh":"比喻的","pos":"adjective","example":"A figurative warning still needs a clear practical consequence.","exampleZh":"比喻警告仍需清楚说明实际后果。","collocation":"a figurative warning","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v13","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"referent","zh":"所指对象","pos":"noun","example":"The referent of shaky was unclear in that discussion.","exampleZh":"讨论中不稳妥一词所指不清。","collocation":"identify the referent","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v14","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ambiguity","zh":"歧义","pos":"noun","example":"The ambiguity concerned both language and the work itself.","exampleZh":"歧义既关乎语言也关乎工作本身。","collocation":"resolve an ambiguity","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v15","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commitment","zh":"承诺","pos":"noun","example":"The remark was not a commitment to cancel.","exampleZh":"这句话不构成取消的承诺。","collocation":"make a commitment","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v16","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a volunteer theatre committee：扩展论证","text":"At a volunteer theatre committee, participants considered whether to use a plain summary beside an informal rehearsal discussion. The background was specific: a director said the opening scene was on thin ice, then asked for another rehearsal rather than cancelling the performance. However, two new volunteers understood the warning as an immediate withdrawal of permission. The proposed response was to explain that the metaphor signals vulnerability and invite the director to state the practical consequence.\n\nA rehearsal room benefits from informal language: it allows people to express doubt without turning every comment into an official notice. The solution is therefore not to ban metaphors. It is to clarify the action whenever a phrase could reasonably be mistaken for a decision. The director might say that the scene needs more work and name the next rehearsal. Volunteers would then know both the concern and what they should do about it. This preserves the collaborative tone while preventing a figurative warning from becoming an accidental cancellation. The committee should also make formal changes through a separate, recognisable channel so that participants do not have to infer administrative consequences from colourful conversation. The opening scene is on thin ice, so another rehearsal would help. I took the warning figuratively rather than as a literal instruction. Before we pull the plug, let us establish what can still be repaired. She was testing the waters rather than committing the committee to a decision. The joke fell flat because its cultural reference was unfamiliar. An idiom can soften criticism while making its practical meaning less explicit. When you say the scene is shaky, which change are you requesting?\n\nAfter the rehearsal, the committee discovers that one volunteer has already booked replacement scenery. She interpreted shaky as a judgement about the physical structure rather than the performance. This was not a failure to recognise an exotic expression; it was a plausible reading in a theatre where scenery and acting were discussed together. The director should clarify which object the evaluation concerns and what action is requested. The group agrees that figurative artistic comments will be followed by a concrete instruction whenever safety, expenditure or cancellation could be inferred. Informal speech remains useful, but consequential decisions will no longer depend on everyone selecting the same meaning from an ambiguous phrase."},"sourceB":{"title":"Moving the Goalposts","text":"In a fictional craft competition, the organiser tells a disappointed entrant that the judges are not moving the goalposts. The entrant has never encountered the expression and asks why football has entered a discussion about ceramics. Another competitor explains that the organiser means the standards have not changed after the work was submitted. The explanation clarifies the idiom, but it does not establish that the organiser's substantive claim is true.\n\nThe entrant then points to a new requirement concerning written documentation. The original announcement asked for a finished object and a short description; the judges now expect a detailed record of development. The organiser says that process has always mattered and that the new form merely makes it visible. These positions need comparison with the actual instructions, rather than a decision based on who understands the metaphor.\n\nAn entrant can understand an expression perfectly and still challenge the claim expressed through it. Conversely, a request for clarification does not show that the person lacks a relevant objection. The distinction matters because the explanation of goalposts initially draws laughter from several experienced competitors. If that laughter becomes a judgement about competence, the committee may overlook the documentary issue the entrant subsequently raises.\n\nThe panel reviews the announcement and finds that development was mentioned among broad aims but not listed as material to submit. This leaves a genuine ambiguity. It does not prove that the judges intended to disadvantage newcomers. It does suggest that treating the missing record as a decisive fault would impose an expectation that entrants could not reliably recover from the submission list.\n\nThe committee agrees to assess the current entries on the specified materials and to publish a clearer requirement for the next competition. It also records how process will be evaluated. The idiom remains an efficient way to discuss shifting standards, but its efficiency must not replace examination of the standards themselves. The case illustrates two distinct tasks: explaining what a speaker means and investigating whether the claim is justified. Successful communication requires both when money, recognition or eligibility depends on the answer.\n\nClearer instructions would protect experienced entrants as well as newcomers."},"listening":{"teaching":"At a planning meeting, our coordinator said we should keep the project on the back burner. I understood that as postponing detailed work while retaining the possibility of returning to it. A new colleague heard it as cancellation and removed the project from the shared calendar. The phrase had compressed a distinction that mattered to our next actions.\n\nThe coordinator was surprised because she thought the expression was familiar. Familiarity was not the only issue. We had never agreed what postponement involved: whether a small budget remained reserved, whether external partners should be informed or who would decide when work resumed. Even those who recognised the idiom could have acted differently.\n\nWe therefore translated the phrase into an explicit arrangement. No new spending would occur that month, existing notes would remain available, and the team would review the project after the funding meeting. One person would tell partners that no date had yet been fixed. The project was neither active on its previous schedule nor permanently abandoned.\n\nThis experience changed how I respond to compressed language. I can ask what a phrase means without pretending that its dictionary meaning settles the administrative decision. If an expression affects responsibilities, it is useful to confirm the action, duration and review point. That need not make every conversation formal. A short question can preserve the convenience of an idiom while preventing participants from attaching different consequences to the same apparently shared understanding. Our shared calendar now records the review date explicitly.","test":"The chair told our group that the new booking proposal was a non starter. One member assumed that the idea could never be reconsidered. Another began revising it immediately, believing that only the current price made it impractical. Both interpretations were possible because the chair had given no explanation of the obstacle.\n\nWhen asked, she said that the venue was unavailable on the proposed date. She did not object to the activity itself or its cost. A different date could therefore change the judgement. The idiom had conveyed strong rejection efficiently, but it had concealed the condition on which that rejection depended.\n\nThe minutes initially recorded that the committee rejected the event. We changed them to say that the proposed date was not feasible and that the organiser would investigate two alternatives. This was not a cosmetic revision. It restored a possibility that the broader wording would have closed and assigned a practical next step.\n\nI would not infer bad faith from the chair's original expression. Informal meetings often use compressed judgements, particularly when time is short. But participants should not have to guess whether an obstacle is temporary, conditional or fundamental. Understanding that non starter means unworkable is only the beginning. The useful follow up asks what makes it unworkable and whether that condition can change. Our eventual clarification allowed the group to retain the concern about venue availability without converting it into opposition to an event that several members still wanted to organise."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the scenery purchase a plausible mistake?","options":["The discussion mixed physical and artistic evaluation.","The volunteer had been told to buy it.","Shaky always refers to a structure."],"answer":"The discussion mixed physical and artistic evaluation.","explanation":"同场景中的两个对象均可被评价。","id":"c2-02:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What change does the group adopt?","options":["A prohibition on artistic criticism.","Automatic spending after metaphors.","Concrete instructions when consequences are significant."],"answer":"Concrete instructions when consequences are significant.","explanation":"保留比喻，同时澄清重要行动。","id":"c2-02:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does explaining goalposts fail to establish?","options":["Whether the entrant understood any English.","Whether the standards actually changed.","Whether ceramics was the competition subject."],"answer":"Whether the standards actually changed.","explanation":"语义理解不同于论断为真。","id":"c2-02:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why avoid inferring deliberate disadvantage?","options":["The evidence establishes ambiguity rather than motive.","The judges never changed a form.","The entrant supplied the required record."],"answer":"The evidence establishes ambiguity rather than motive.","explanation":"程序含糊不等于证明恶意。","id":"c2-02:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was idiom familiarity insufficient?","options":["Everyone interpreted cancellation identically.","The phrase has no recognised meaning.","Postponement still lacked agreed operational details."],"answer":"Postponement still lacked agreed operational details.","explanation":"即使懂词也未必同意具体安排。","id":"c2-02:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the clarification retain?","options":["The original active schedule.","A future review without current spending.","Permanent abandonment."],"answer":"A future review without current spending.","explanation":"暂停不是取消也不是照旧。","id":"c2-02:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What condition changed the interpretation?","options":["The obstacle was a particular date.","The chair opposed the activity itself.","The event cost had doubled."],"answer":"The obstacle was a particular date.","explanation":"临时日期障碍不等于根本反对。","id":"c2-02:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why revise the minutes?","options":["To conceal the chair's concern.","To promise venue availability.","To preserve a conditional possibility and next step."],"answer":"To preserve a conditional possibility and next step.","explanation":"纪要应准确保留条件与行动。","id":"c2-02:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a rehearsal communication procedure preserving useful figurative criticism while clarifying consequential actions.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Recover practical meaning without treating an idiom as either literal fact or a complete instruction.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a response to the craft competition dispute in Moving the Goalposts. Separate understanding the idiom from assessing the changed requirement. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on Moving the Goalposts. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What does explaining goalposts fail to establish?","Why avoid inferring deliberate disadvantage?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["meeting chair","member requesting practical clarification"],"goal":"Translate a compressed judgement into actions, conditions and a review point.","twist":"The minutes record cancellation although the speaker intended a temporary obstacle."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving recover practical meaning without treating an idiom as either literal fact or a complete instruction. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-03","level":"C2","title":"语域、权力与合作","chapter":67,"focus":"Preserve a request while adapting its interpersonal framing to audience and power.","vocab":[{"head":"feasible","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Would it be feasible to send the records before Thursday afternoon?","exampleZh":"能否在周四下午之前发送这些记录？","collocation":"a feasible deadline","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v1","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"impractical","zh":"不现实的","pos":"adjective","example":"We need the list today, but please tell us if that deadline is impractical.","exampleZh":"我们今天需要清单，但如果这个期限不现实，请告诉我们。","collocation":"an impractical request","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v2","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"courteous","zh":"有礼貌的","pos":"adjective","example":"The request sounds courteous without concealing its urgency.","exampleZh":"这个请求听起来很有礼貌，也没有掩盖紧迫性。","collocation":"a courteous message","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v3","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ambiguity","zh":"歧义","pos":"noun","example":"Familiarity permits brevity, but it does not excuse avoidable ambiguity.","exampleZh":"熟悉的关系允许简短表达，却不能为可避免的歧义开脱。","collocation":"avoidable ambiguity","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v4","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"make the consequence explicit","zh":"明确说明后果","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I would make the consequence explicit rather than bury it in apologies.","exampleZh":"我会明确说明后果，而不是将其埋在道歉之中。","collocation":"make the consequence explicit","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v5","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deference","zh":"尊重与谦让","pos":"noun","example":"The donor may welcome a clear explanation more than elaborate deference.","exampleZh":"与繁复的谦辞相比，捐赠者或许更欢迎清楚的解释。","collocation":"elaborate deference","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v6","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accommodate","zh":"配合安排","pos":"verb","example":"Please let us know what timescale you could realistically accommodate.","exampleZh":"请告诉我们你实际能够配合的时间安排。","collocation":"accommodate a timescale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v7","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"established","zh":"建立","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"An informal greeting would imply a relationship we have not yet established.","exampleZh":"随意的问候会暗示一种我们尚未建立的关系。","collocation":"established rapport","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v8","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substance","zh":"实质","pos":"noun","example":"The substance of the request remains unchanged across the two versions.","exampleZh":"这两个版本的请求实质保持不变。","collocation":"the substance of a request","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v9","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entitled","zh":"自以为有权要求的","pos":"adjective","example":"That phrasing risks sounding entitled rather than merely efficient.","exampleZh":"那种措辞可能显得自以为有权要求，而非仅仅高效。","collocation":"sound entitled","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v10","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flexible","zh":"灵活的","pos":"adjective","example":"We can be direct about the deadline and flexible about the method.","exampleZh":"我们可以明确说明期限，同时在方法上保持灵活。","collocation":"a flexible method","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v11","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"register","zh":"语域","pos":"noun","example":"The appropriate register depends on audience, purpose and existing rapport.","exampleZh":"适当的语域取决于受众、目的和已有关系。","collocation":"an appropriate register","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v12","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"dependency","zh":"依赖关系","pos":"noun","example":"The message should explain the project's dependency on the records.","exampleZh":"消息应解释项目对记录的依赖。","collocation":"a project dependency","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v13","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imposition","zh":"给人的负担","pos":"noun","example":"Acknowledging an imposition does not require hiding the request.","exampleZh":"承认给人带来负担不等于隐藏请求。","collocation":"acknowledge an imposition","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v14","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocity","zh":"相互配合","pos":"noun","example":"Reciprocity becomes possible when constraints are made explicit.","exampleZh":"限制说清后才可能相互配合。","collocation":"enable reciprocity","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v15","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entitlement","zh":"自以为有权要求","pos":"noun","example":"Brevity can sound like entitlement without adequate context.","exampleZh":"没有充分语境时简短可能像强索。","collocation":"a sense of entitlement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v16","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a university archive：扩展论证","text":"At a university archive, participants considered whether to rewrite a request for missing catalogue records. The background was specific: the first message to an external donor read send the list today, whereas the revised message explained the deadline and asked whether delivery was feasible. However, a highly deferential rewrite could obscure the fact that the exhibition depended on those records. The proposed response was to state the consequence plainly while leaving the donor room to propose a workable time.\n\nThe archive depends on cooperation from somebody it cannot simply instruct as though they were a subordinate. Explaining the exhibition deadline gives the donor a reason to help and a basis for proposing an alternative. Yet politeness should not disguise the fact that missing records may affect the display. A useful message would identify the essential records, state the latest workable time and ask what can realistically be supplied. It might also offer a partial delivery if that would keep the exhibition moving. This approach treats courtesy as a means of making cooperation possible, rather than as an accumulation of apologies that leaves the recipient unsure what is actually being requested. Would it be feasible to send the records before Thursday afternoon? We need the list today, but please tell us if that deadline is impractical. The request sounds courteous without concealing its urgency. Familiarity permits brevity, but it does not excuse avoidable ambiguity. I would make the consequence explicit rather than bury it in apologies. The donor may welcome a clear explanation more than elaborate deference. Please let us know what timescale you could realistically accommodate.\n\nThe donor replies that the proposed deadline is possible only if the archive accepts an incomplete list first. A cooperative response should not interpret this condition as reluctance to help. It should establish which records are essential for the opening and which can follow. The exchange also reveals why an excessively apologetic first message would have been unhelpful: the donor needed to understand the dependency in order to suggest a partial solution. The archive can thank the donor sincerely while recording the arrangement precisely. Warmth, directness and clarity are not mutually exclusive styles from which only one may be chosen. They perform different functions within an effective request."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Unanswered Recommendation","text":"A fictional researcher asks a former supervisor for a recommendation. Her draft begins with three paragraphs of apologies and praise before mentioning that the application closes in four days. A friend suggests replacing the entire message with a short command: upload my reference by Friday. Neither version handles the relationship and the task particularly well.\n\nThe supervisor is not an employee required to complete an assigned administrative duty. A request should acknowledge the time involved and allow an honest refusal. Yet excessive ceremony can bury information essential to making the decision. The closing date, the work required and the relevant documents matter more than a lengthy account of how uncomfortable the applicant feels about asking.\n\nThe researcher rewrites the message to explain the opportunity, name the deadline and ask whether the supervisor could provide a reference within that period. She attaches a summary of recent work and the submission instructions. She also says that she understands if the notice is too short. This gives the recipient a practical route to acceptance or refusal rather than requiring a response to emotional pressure.\n\nThe supervisor replies that a general recommendation is possible by Friday, but a detailed discussion of a new project is not. The researcher initially considers this unenthusiastic. On reflection, it is a clear statement of capacity. Treating it as a personal rejection would obscure the useful offer and make a simple decision unnecessarily difficult. The appropriate next question is whether the general recommendation meets the applications needs.\n\nThe final exchange is brief and cordial. The applicant accepts the general reference and supplies the information requested. The tone does not become less respectful because the arrangements are explicit. Nor would adding more deferential expressions improve an agreement whose terms are already clear. Appropriate register is therefore not a position on a single scale from friendly to formal. It involves matching explanation, directness and interpersonal care to the relationship, while preserving the substantive decision that the recipient is being asked to make.\n\nRespect is visible in the realistic choice offered to the recipient, not simply in the number of polite expressions used."},"listening":{"teaching":"I reviewed two notices about a temporary entrance closure. The first said that users must obey the diversion without exception. The second apologised repeatedly for any possible inconvenience but did not say which entrance remained open. Both were intended to help visitors, yet each neglected part of the task.\n\nThe strict version made the rule visible but supplied no reason or practical route. The apologetic version showed concern but left readers uncertain about what to do. We revised the notice to state that repairs required the east entrance to close, identify the accessible route through the courtyard and give the expected reopening time. A short apology followed the useful information.\n\nWe also considered the audience. Regular staff knew where the courtyard was; visitors attending their first appointment might not. A small map would therefore do more work than another polite phrase. The new notice explained how to ask for assistance if the diversion did not meet a person's needs.\n\nI would not say that courtesy is unimportant. It helps people recognise that the institution has considered their situation. But courtesy should support the action rather than replace it. A reader should not need to infer an instruction from a series of expressions of regret. Equally, clarity does not require treating every visitor as disobedient. The best version gave the reason, the route and the time in a direct form, while preserving a respectful relationship with people whose day the closure might genuinely complicate.","test":"Our project leader wrote to an external illustrator asking for a revised cover that afternoon. The message said, as discussed, please make the changes today. The difficulty was that only the broad design had been discussed; the deadline and the additional detail had not. The phrase suggested an agreement that the illustrator did not recognise.\n\nShe replied politely that she could provide a rough alternative by evening or a finished version two days later. One team member read this as resistance and proposed a firmer message. I disagreed. The reply identified two feasible options and made a capacity constraint explicit. Stronger wording would not create more working time.\n\nWe checked what the publication actually required. A rough image was enough for the internal meeting, while the printer needed the finished file later. The leader accepted the two stage arrangement and corrected the implication that the deadline had already been agreed. This preserved the urgency without treating an external collaborator as someone who had broken a promise.\n\nThe lesson concerns both accuracy and tone. As discussed can be useful shorthand when there is genuinely shared agreement. Here it concealed the difference between discussing a topic and accepting a specific obligation. A courteous but inaccurate framing could still exert unfair pressure. We now distinguish proposed deadlines from confirmed ones and supply the purpose of an urgent request. That gives collaborators information with which to negotiate rather than a rhetorical assumption they must first challenge before practical cooperation can begin."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"How should the partial list offer be interpreted?","options":["As evidence of personal reluctance.","As a capacity condition enabling cooperation.","As rejection of the opening date."],"answer":"As a capacity condition enabling cooperation.","explanation":"条件可能帮助找到可行合作方案。","id":"c2-03:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why can excessive apology be unhelpful?","options":["It can obscure the dependency needed for planning.","It makes gratitude impossible.","It guarantees the donor will refuse."],"answer":"It can obscure the dependency needed for planning.","explanation":"关键背景和请求不能被礼貌掩盖。","id":"c2-03:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the revised request enable?","options":["An obligation to accept.","A promise of detailed project coverage.","A practical acceptance or refusal."],"answer":"A practical acceptance or refusal.","explanation":"提供选择而不是施加情感压力。","id":"c2-03:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is the limited offer not necessarily rejection?","options":["General references are always sufficient.","It states capacity while retaining useful support.","The supervisor accepts every request."],"answer":"It states capacity while retaining useful support.","explanation":"需按实际需求判断可用性。","id":"c2-03:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did both notices neglect differently?","options":["Useful action or respectful context.","The existence of the repair.","The identity of all regular staff."],"answer":"Useful action or respectful context.","explanation":"一版缺实际路线，一版缺人际解释。","id":"c2-03:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why add a map?","options":["Polite phrases never matter.","Regular staff cannot read directions.","New visitors lack assumed local knowledge."],"answer":"New visitors lack assumed local knowledge.","explanation":"信息设计应考虑不同受众。","id":"c2-03:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did as discussed inaccurately imply?","options":["Discussion of the broad design.","Agreement to a specific obligation.","The need for an illustrator."],"answer":"Agreement to a specific obligation.","explanation":"谈过主题不等于同意期限与任务。","id":"c2-03:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the staged arrangement work?","options":["Different deadlines served different purposes.","The illustrator abandoned her capacity limits.","The team removed every time constraint."],"answer":"Different deadlines served different purposes.","explanation":"拆分任务符合实际用途与能力。","id":"c2-03:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Draft an archive request and a follow up accepting a partial delivery. Explain your register choices without burying the dependency.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Preserve a request while adapting its interpersonal framing to audience and power.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Advise the researcher in The Unanswered Recommendation. Draft a concise response to the supervisors limited offer and explain why it need not signal rejection. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Unanswered Recommendation. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What does the revised request enable?","Why is the limited offer not necessarily rejection?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["external collaborator","project lead"],"goal":"Agree a feasible deadline without presuming an obligation that was not accepted.","twist":"The phrase as discussed has been read as evidence of a prior agreement."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving preserve a request while adapting its interpersonal framing to audience and power. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-04","level":"C2","title":"修辞强调与政策权衡","chapter":68,"focus":"Distinguish a defensible priority from an unsupported hierarchy of people.","vocab":[{"head":"cannot afford to miss","zh":"最不能错过","pos":"modal phrase","example":"It is the final connection that shift workers cannot afford to miss.","exampleZh":"轮班工作者最不能错过的正是最后一班接驳车。","collocation":"cannot afford to miss","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v1","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imply","zh":"意味着","pos":"verb","example":"That priority does not imply that earlier journeys are unimportant.","exampleZh":"这种优先安排并不意味着较早的行程不重要。","collocation":"imply importance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v2","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Not once","zh":"一次也没有","pos":"adverbial phrase","example":"Not once during the trial did the revised timetable require another vehicle.","exampleZh":"在试行期间，修改后的时刻表一次也没有需要增加车辆。","collocation":"not once did","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v3","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Compelling though","zh":"虽有说服力","pos":"concessive construction","example":"Compelling though the example is, it cannot settle the entire funding question.","exampleZh":"这个例子虽有说服力，却不能解决整个拨款问题。","collocation":"compelling though it is","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v4","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allocation","zh":"拨款分配","pos":"noun","example":"What matters here is the effect of the next small allocation.","exampleZh":"这里重要的是下一笔小额拨款的作用。","collocation":"a funding allocation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v5","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定条件","pos":"noun","example":"The emphasis is justified only if the qualification remains visible.","exampleZh":"只有限定条件仍然清楚可见，这种强调才合理。","collocation":"an explicit qualification","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v6","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Far from","zh":"远非","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"Far from dismissing daytime users, the proposal addresses an additional gap.","exampleZh":"这个提案非但没有忽视日间乘客，反而是在弥补另一处缺口。","collocation":"far from dismissing","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v7","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hierarchy","zh":"等级排序","pos":"noun","example":"We should resist turning a practical priority into a moral hierarchy.","exampleZh":"我们应避免把实际优先事项变成人的价值排序。","collocation":"a moral hierarchy","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v8","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draws attention to","zh":"突出","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The construction draws attention to the contrast without proving it.","exampleZh":"这种句式突出了对比，但并不能证明对比成立。","collocation":"draws attention to","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v9","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Had the trial lasted","zh":"假如试行曾持续","pos":"conditional construction","example":"Had the trial lasted longer, seasonal pressures might have emerged.","exampleZh":"如果试行持续更久，季节性压力可能就会出现。","collocation":"had the trial lasted","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v10","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"nevertheless","zh":"不过","pos":"adverb","example":"I accept the limitation; nevertheless, the immediate access problem remains.","exampleZh":"我接受这个局限；不过，眼前的出行问题仍然存在。","collocation":"nevertheless it remains","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v11","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exceed","zh":"超过","pos":"verb","example":"The rhetorical force of a sentence should not exceed its evidential support.","exampleZh":"一句话的修辞力度不应超过其证据支持。","collocation":"exceed the evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v12","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"marginal","zh":"边际的","pos":"adjective","example":"The debate concerns the marginal benefit of one extra bus.","exampleZh":"讨论关乎增加一辆车带来的边际收益。","collocation":"marginal benefit","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v13","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"salience","zh":"显著性","pos":"noun","example":"Repetition increased the salience of the late connection.","exampleZh":"重复提高了末班接驳的重要性。","collocation":"increase the salience","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v14","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allocation","zh":"分配","pos":"noun","example":"The allocation should follow the published funding criteria.","exampleZh":"分配应遵循公布的拨款标准。","collocation":"funding allocation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v15","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trade-off","zh":"权衡","pos":"noun","example":"The report acknowledges a trade-off between frequency and coverage.","exampleZh":"报告承认班次密度与覆盖范围存在权衡。","collocation":"acknowledge a trade-off","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v16","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a public transport forum：扩展论证","text":"At a public transport forum, participants considered whether to argue for late buses without dismissing other passengers. The background was specific: a speaker began it is the last connection that matters most to shift workers, then acknowledged that earlier services also needed repair. However, the emphasis could be heard as claiming that daytime journeys had no social value. The proposed response was to define the priority as a marginal funding decision rather than a ranking of passengers worth.\n\nThe forum is deciding how to allocate an additional service, not determining whose life matters most. That distinction should shape the argument. The speaker can show why the final connection has an unusually large effect on shift workers without implying that daytime passengers have trivial needs. An explicit comparison of the consequences would make the emphasis defensible. It would also allow opponents to challenge the cost or the predicted benefit rather than respond to a moral accusation nobody needs to make. The revised speech should retain its focus, because removing every emphatic construction would obscure the urgency. It should instead connect that focus to a limited decision and acknowledge the unmet needs outside it. It is the final connection that shift workers cannot afford to miss. That priority does not imply that earlier journeys are unimportant. Not once during the trial did the revised timetable require another vehicle. Compelling though the example is, it cannot settle the entire funding question. What matters here is the effect of the next small allocation. The emphasis is justified only if the qualification remains visible. Far from dismissing daytime users, the proposal addresses an additional gap.\n\nA further difficulty arises when the forum prepares its public summary. The proposed headline says that the last bus is the only service that matters. Nobody used those words in the discussion. They convert a claim about the consequence of one additional allocation into a claim about the value of every other journey. The secretary suggests retaining the shift workers example but naming the limited funding decision explicitly. This would preserve urgency without manufacturing contempt for daytime users. The forum also agrees to publish the main cost objection beside the recommendation. Opponents may challenge the estimate, but they should not have to refute an argument the supporters never intended to make. Clear emphasis gives an audience a focus; distorted emphasis gives it a false choice."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Only Ward That Counts","text":"At a fictional hospital consultation, a campaigner says that it is the children's rehabilitation room that should receive the next charitable grant. The current room is shared with deliveries, and some sessions are interrupted. Her argument concerns a specific grant whose conditions permit improvements to communal spaces but not the recruitment of clinical staff. A local headline later describes her position as children matter more than nurses.\n\nThis description changes the argument. The campaigner has not compared the moral worth of patients and staff; she has identified a use for money that cannot fund the suggested alternative. The headline also obscures the possibility that staff benefit when therapy occurs in a suitable space. Its force depends on presenting the consultation as a competition between groups whose interests may partly overlap.\n\nThere are nevertheless reasonable questions about the recommendation. Another department uses a crowded family room, and its representative asks whether the proposed benefit has been compared with that of improving their space. Pointing out the headlines distortion does not answer this comparison. The campaigner should provide the estimated number of sessions affected, the expected improvement and the limits of those estimates. The emotional power of an account involving children cannot replace that information.\n\nDuring the consultation, a therapist says that not once in the past month has a complete week passed without an interruption. This construction emphasises a recurring problem, but the observation period remains one month. It does not establish the same frequency throughout the year. The statement can justify investigating the pattern and may support a modest immediate change; it should not be silently converted into a permanent average.\n\nThe final recommendation names the grant conditions, compares the two eligible spaces and explains why the rehabilitation room is preferred provisionally. It asks for a review after the renovation rather than promising that every interruption will disappear. The strongest public account would preserve these conditions alongside the compelling example. Readers can then disagree about priorities using the actual options. Removing every emotional detail would impoverish the argument, but leaving the detail without its limits would invite a conclusion stronger than the case can defend."},"listening":{"teaching":"The council asked me to summarise a debate about a temporary reading room. A resident had said that what children needed was somewhere quiet after school. The sentence was an emphatic response to a proposal for more organised activities. It was not a claim that children never benefit from games, clubs or adult support.\n\nMy first summary nevertheless described quiet as the sole requirement. When I read it back, the resident objected. Her concern was that every available space already hosted a scheduled activity, leaving no option for a child who wanted to read independently. The missing alternative, rather than an absolute rejection of activities, explained her emphasis.\n\nThat clarification changed the decision we needed to examine. We could compare the cost of reserving one room for quiet use with the benefit lost by moving its existing club. We could not settle the issue merely by agreeing that reading was valuable. The club also served children, and some attended precisely because they needed a supervised social setting.\n\nWe eventually recorded both needs and proposed a short trial on two afternoons. The trial would measure use, ask pupils about practical access and record any disruption to the displaced activity. I retained the residents emphatic phrase as an introduction but followed it with her explanation. A summary can preserve persuasive force without turning a contextual contrast into an absolute doctrine. The distinction matters because the exaggerated version would have required her to defend a position she had not taken and would have hidden the feasible compromise the discussion eventually produced.","test":"At the sports centre meeting, a speaker said that it was the changing rooms, not another publicity campaign, that should receive the next improvement fund. He described broken lockers and a shower that had been out of service for weeks. Afterwards, a leaflet claimed that he opposed attracting new members.\n\nThe claim does not follow. His contrast concerned the use of one restricted fund at that moment. He later said that promotion might be useful after the basic facilities worked reliably. That qualification was absent from the leaflet, which turned a sequence of priorities into a permanent opposition between maintenance and growth.\n\nThere was still a real budget dispute. A manager argued that more members could produce the income needed for repairs. The speaker replied that recruiting people into unreliable facilities risked losing them quickly. Neither consideration was self evidently decisive. The group needed cost estimates, information about available funding and a realistic assessment of how membership might respond.\n\nThe revised minutes therefore recorded the maintenance recommendation and the manager's objection. They did not replace the dispute with a vague statement that everyone supported improvement. Nor did they suggest that the speaker had abandoned publicity altogether. A later proposal could change the balance by bringing in a separate promotional grant. The correct account leaves room for that possibility because it reports what the original emphasis was doing: prioritising a particular action under particular conditions, rather than declaring an unchanging view about the centre's future."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is wrong with only service that matters?","options":["It changes a marginal priority into an absolute valuation.","It understates support for daytime services.","It introduces a new cost estimate."],"answer":"It changes a marginal priority into an absolute valuation.","explanation":"强调被扩大成绝对价值判断。","id":"c2-04:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why publish the main objection?","options":["To imply opponents oppose shift workers.","To withdraw the need for a decision.","So the recommendation remains answerable to a real trade-off."],"answer":"So the recommendation remains answerable to a real trade-off.","explanation":"保留真实异议使建议可被审查。","id":"c2-04:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is children versus nurses misleading?","options":["Nurses have rejected every communal improvement.","The grant cannot fund the alternative implied by that contrast.","The campaigner promised clinical recruitment."],"answer":"The grant cannot fund the alternative implied by that contrast.","explanation":"拨款条件限定了可选方案，标题制造了错误对立。","id":"c2-04:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What limitation remains in the therapists statement?","options":["The observation covers one month rather than every season.","It contains no example of disruption.","It establishes all interruptions will end after renovation."],"answer":"The observation covers one month rather than every season.","explanation":"修辞力度不改变观察时段。","id":"c2-04:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the residents emphasis concern?","options":["The uselessness of every organised activity.","A permanent ban on supervised clubs.","An absent quiet option within the current mix."],"answer":"An absent quiet option within the current mix.","explanation":"强调针对当前选择结构，不是绝对否定活动。","id":"c2-04:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was valuing reading insufficient?","options":["Reading was outside the councils authority.","The club served another need that required comparison.","The room could never host two activities."],"answer":"The club served another need that required comparison.","explanation":"需比较替代用途和受益需求。","id":"c2-04:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What does the leaflet misrepresent?","options":["A conditional sequence as permanent opposition.","A repair estimate as an observed cost.","A growth plan as a staffing problem."],"answer":"A conditional sequence as permanent opposition.","explanation":"把先维护后宣传歪曲成永远反对宣传。","id":"c2-04:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why retain the manager's objection?","options":["It proves membership will grow.","It removes the speakers qualification.","It identifies a real competing mechanism for funding repairs."],"answer":"It identifies a real competing mechanism for funding repairs.","explanation":"收入机制是实际争议，不是已证实结果。","id":"c2-04:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a funding brief explaining the late bus priority without implying that daytime passengers matter less.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Distinguish a defensible priority from an unsupported hierarchy of people.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Advise the hospital consultation on how to report the rehabilitation room recommendation without reproducing the misleading children versus nurses contrast. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Only Ward That Counts. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why is children versus nurses misleading?","What limitation remains in the therapists statement?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["campaign spokesperson","budget reviewer"],"goal":"Produce a defensible priority with visible conditions and a fair account of the objection.","twist":"A headline removes the qualification and attributes a stronger claim to you."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving distinguish a defensible priority from an unsupported hierarchy of people. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-05","level":"C2","title":"多人话语中的立场追踪","chapter":69,"focus":"Track changing commitments and the referent of agreement through interruptions.","vocab":[{"head":"whereas","zh":"而","pos":"conjunction","example":"Mira supported the kitchen plan, whereas Arun was still discussing queues.","exampleZh":"米拉支持厨房方案，而阿伦仍在讨论排队问题。","collocation":"whereas others disagree","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v1","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposed layout","zh":"建议的布局","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Was that agreement about the date or about the proposed layout?","exampleZh":"刚才的同意是针对日期，还是针对建议的布局？","collocation":"the proposed layout","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v2","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"Jo has raised a condition that neither proposal yet satisfies.","exampleZh":"乔提出了一个两个方案都还未满足的条件。","collocation":"meet a condition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v3","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outstanding","zh":"尚未解决的","pos":"adjective","example":"Let me separate the decisions we have made from those still outstanding.","exampleZh":"让我把已作出的决定和仍悬而未决的问题分开。","collocation":"an outstanding decision","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v4","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"referent","zh":"所指对象","pos":"noun","example":"I lost the referent of that last yes during the overlap.","exampleZh":"多人同时说话时，我没听清最后那声同意针对什么。","collocation":"identify the referent","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v5","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"not necessarily","zh":"未必","pos":"adverbial phrase","example":"We appear to agree on timing, but not necessarily on facilities.","exampleZh":"我们似乎在时间上达成了一致，但设施方面未必如此。","collocation":"not necessarily agree","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v6","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"Before we move on, could each person confirm their preferred option?","exampleZh":"在继续之前，每个人能否确认自己倾向的方案？","collocation":"confirm an option","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v7","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"competing proposal","zh":"竞争方案","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The interruption introduced a new constraint rather than a competing proposal.","exampleZh":"这次打断引入了新限制，而不是另一个竞争方案。","collocation":"a competing proposal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v8","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withdrawn","zh":"撤回了","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"Arun has withdrawn his objection to the date, not to the kitchen.","exampleZh":"阿伦撤回的是对日期的异议，而非对厨房的异议。","collocation":"withdrawn an objection","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v9","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"materially","zh":"实质性地","pos":"adverb","example":"The summary omitted a reservation that materially affects the decision.","exampleZh":"总结遗漏了一个实质影响决策的保留意见。","collocation":"materially affect","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v10","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would mark that conclusion as provisional until the power supply is checked.","exampleZh":"在检查供电之前，我会把这个结论标为暂定。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v11","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tracking changes","zh":"追踪变化","pos":"gerund phrase","example":"Following the discussion requires tracking changes, not merely recognising names.","exampleZh":"跟上讨论需要追踪变化，而不只是认出人名。","collocation":"tracking changes","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v12","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turn","zh":"话轮","pos":"noun","example":"The interruption changed the function of the next turn.","exampleZh":"打断改变了下一话轮的功能。","collocation":"a conversational turn","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v13","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alignment","zh":"一致立场","pos":"noun","example":"An acknowledgement does not necessarily signal alignment.","exampleZh":"表示听到不一定表示立场一致。","collocation":"signal alignment","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v14","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ratify","zh":"正式确认","pos":"verb","example":"The group must ratify the final wording.","exampleZh":"小组必须正式确认最终措辞。","collocation":"ratify an agreement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v15","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sequence","zh":"话语顺序","pos":"noun","example":"The sequence identifies which question yes answered.","exampleZh":"话语顺序说明同意回答了哪个问题。","collocation":"a question sequence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v16","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a local food festival planning meeting：扩展论证","text":"At a local food festival planning meeting, participants considered whether to choose between one large kitchen and several small stalls. The background was specific: Mira preferred a central kitchen for hygiene checks, Arun defended stalls for shorter queues, and Jo asked whether either option fitted the available electrical supply. However, rapid agreement about the event date was being treated as agreement about the kitchen design. The proposed response was to record each proposal separately and leave the power question open until an electrician checked the site.\n\nThe organiser needs a record of the changing discussion, not a transcript that merely captures every word. A useful summary would list the central kitchen, the separate stalls and the electrical constraint as distinct items. It would show which questions have been answered and which remain open. The group should not interpret momentum as consensus: people may agree that the event should happen while disagreeing about how it can operate safely. A brief confirmation round would cost little compared with redesigning the site later. It would also give quieter participants a clear chance to correct the summary. After the electrical check, the discussion can return to hygiene and queues with a more reliable account of what is feasible. Mira supported the kitchen plan, whereas Arun was still discussing queues. Was that agreement about the date or about the proposed layout? Jo has raised a condition that neither proposal yet satisfies. Let me separate the decisions we have made from those still outstanding. I lost the referent of that last yes during the overlap. We appear to agree on timing, but not necessarily on facilities.\n\nA recording of the meeting later shows that Arun said yes immediately after Jo asked whether the electrician was available on Tuesday. In the minutes, the word had been attached to the central kitchen proposal. Correcting the record does not require accusing the secretary of dishonesty. Rapid conversation supplies acknowledgements, answers and proposals in overlapping sequences, and even a faithful transcript may not label their functions. The revised minutes name the question answered and leave Aruns preference about kitchen design open. The group also adopts a short confirmation round before closing consequential decisions. This turns a fragile inference from timing into an opportunity for participants to state their commitments explicitly."},"sourceB":{"title":"Three Versions of a Decision","text":"At a fictional coastal museum, a committee discusses moving a collection upstairs during repairs. The curator supports moving the fragile objects first. The caretaker asks whether the upper floor can carry the loaded cabinets. The treasurer says that the existing budget can cover lifting equipment but not another storage rental. These contributions concern different parts of the decision, although all occur within a minute.\n\nWhen the chair asks whether the committee can proceed, the caretaker begins to say yes if the surveyor confirms the floor loading. The curator interrupts to suggest a date. Someone then answers yes to a question about the availability of volunteers. In a draft summary, the chair records unanimous agreement to move every cabinet on that date. The summary preserves the momentum of the meeting but loses two conditions.\n\nThe caretaker objects that approval was conditional on a structural check. The curator adds that her initial proposal concerned fragile objects, not the whole collection. The treasurer confirms the budget statement but says it was not an endorsement of any particular moving method. These corrections do not reveal three people changing their minds. They reveal that their earlier contributions were attached to different propositions.\n\nA transcript would help, but words alone would still require interpretation. Yes may acknowledge a date, accept an invitation to speak or endorse a proposal. If its referent is uncertain, counting affirmative words cannot establish consensus. Nor should silence after a rapid interruption automatically be treated as acceptance, especially when a participant was trying to state a qualification.\n\nThe committee produces a new record with separate headings for scope, safety, cost and timing. It confirms a provisional plan to move fragile objects after the floor assessment, with the final method to be reviewed. Volunteers remain available on the suggested date, but their availability is not itself a reason to bypass the check. The revised process takes longer at the end of the meeting, yet saves the institution from acting on an agreement that nobody had actually made in the form first recorded.\n\nA later signature should confirm that actual record, rather than supply retrospective approval for a stronger version."},"listening":{"teaching":"I chaired a discussion about borrowing a minibus. Sam wanted transport for a Saturday trip, Priya asked who could drive it and Ellis checked whether the insurance covered volunteers. These were related questions, but they were not interchangeable. I failed to maintain that distinction in my first summary.\n\nPriya said yes when I asked whether she could contact a driver. A moment later I wrote that she had agreed to drive. She corrected me immediately. Her commitment was to make an enquiry, not to undertake the journey herself. The difference mattered because no driver had yet accepted, and the booking could incur a charge.\n\nEllis had also said that the insurance looked suitable, followed by a condition about approved drivers. An interruption prevented the condition from appearing in my notes. When I read the summary aloud, he restored it. The group had not withdrawn agreement; the summary had expanded tentative statements into completed arrangements.\n\nWe rewrote the action list using verbs that named each commitment: contact, check, confirm and book. Booking would occur only after the first two actions produced satisfactory answers. This made the sequence clear and prevented an available vehicle from being confused with an authorised journey. I now read consequential decisions back before ending a meeting, particularly when several people have spoken quickly. The point is not to demand formal votes on every remark. It is to give people a chance to correct the scope and conditions of an agreement before somebody relies on it.","test":"The team discussing an exhibition heard three proposals in quick succession. One member wanted a later opening date. Another preferred a smaller display on the existing date. A third suggested opening one room first and adding the rest later. Each option addressed pressure on preparation time in a different way.\n\nDuring the exchange, the technician said that Tuesday would work. The draft minutes attached this to the later opening. In fact, he had been answering a question about testing the lights, and he had expressed no preference about the public date. The exact word was recorded correctly; its function was not.\n\nThe problem grew when the chair treated the absence of further objections as consensus. Two members believed the options were still being compared, while another thought the staged opening had been accepted. None had been asked to confirm a final formulation. The apparent agreement therefore rested on incompatible understandings.\n\nAt the next meeting we separated the options, named the unresolved constraint and asked each person to respond to one complete proposal. The group selected a staged opening, conditional on completing the safety checks for the first room. The technician retained Tuesday for the lighting test. This outcome did not require choosing whose memory was morally superior. It required reconstructing which contribution concerned which proposition and creating a clearer decision point. A reliable record needs that structure as well as accurate words; otherwise, the same short acknowledgement can be used to support several decisions the speaker never endorsed."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did Aruns yes concern?","options":["The kitchen design.","The final expenditure.","The electricians availability."],"answer":"The electricians availability.","explanation":"恢复所指，而非改变立场。","id":"c2-05:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does a confirmation round add?","options":["A chance to state commitments explicitly.","Proof that all interruptions were deliberate.","A substitute for checking power."],"answer":"A chance to state commitments explicitly.","explanation":"显化承诺范围。","id":"c2-05:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What explains the later corrections?","options":["Members abandoned a clear unanimous plan.","Earlier contributions concerned different propositions.","The budget had disappeared."],"answer":"Earlier contributions concerned different propositions.","explanation":"不能把纠正总结等同反悔。","id":"c2-05:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is volunteer availability insufficient?","options":["It proves the date impossible.","It identifies which cabinets are fragile.","It does not satisfy the structural condition."],"answer":"It does not satisfy the structural condition.","explanation":"时间可用不覆盖安全条件。","id":"c2-05:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What had Priya accepted?","options":["Contacting a driver.","Driving the bus.","Paying the booking charge."],"answer":"Contacting a driver.","explanation":"承诺动词应准确区分。","id":"c2-05:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why use separate action verbs?","options":["They make insurance unnecessary.","They preserve the order and scope of commitments.","They turn enquiries into authorisation."],"answer":"They preserve the order and scope of commitments.","explanation":"行动序列与条件不能压缩。","id":"c2-05:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What was wrong with the recorded Tuesday remark?","options":["The word itself was misheard.","The technician had rejected Tuesday.","Its function was assigned to the wrong proposal."],"answer":"Its function was assigned to the wrong proposal.","explanation":"字词准确不保证所指准确。","id":"c2-05:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What made apparent consensus unreliable?","options":["Participants held incompatible understandings of the decision.","No options had been suggested.","The staged option lacked any condition."],"answer":"Participants held incompatible understandings of the decision.","explanation":"未确认完整命题导致假共识。","id":"c2-05:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write corrected festival minutes separating proposals, acknowledgements and outstanding electrical checks.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Track changing commitments and the referent of agreement through interruptions.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Prepare corrected minutes for Three Versions of a Decision, identifying scope, safety, cost and timing without inventing consensus. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on Three Versions of a Decision. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What explains the later corrections?","Why is volunteer availability insufficient?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["chair","participant whose qualification was omitted"],"goal":"Read back a complete decision and record each remaining condition.","twist":"An accurate word has been attached to the wrong question in the transcript."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving track changing commitments and the referent of agreement through interruptions. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-06","level":"C2","title":"口音适应与可验证理解","chapter":70,"focus":"Distinguish comprehension from reconstruction and use clarification without stigmatising speakers.","vocab":[{"head":"connected speech","zh":"连续语流","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I recognised the place name on paper but missed it in connected speech.","exampleZh":"我在纸上认得那个地名，却在连续语流中没听出来。","collocation":"connected speech","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v1","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"spell","zh":"拼写","pos":"verb","example":"Could you repeat the street name and then spell the final word?","exampleZh":"你能重复街道名，再拼一下最后那个词吗？","collocation":"spell a name","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v2","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"vowel pattern","zh":"元音模式","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An unfamiliar vowel pattern does not imply unfamiliar vocabulary.","exampleZh":"不熟悉的元音模式不意味着词汇不熟悉。","collocation":"a vowel pattern","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v3","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"verify","zh":"核实","pos":"verb","example":"I should verify the name instead of selecting the nearest familiar one.","exampleZh":"我应该核实名称，而不是选择最接近的熟悉名称。","collocation":"verify a name","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v4","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unaided","zh":"没有辅助的","pos":"adjective","example":"The written transcript cannot establish how much I understood unaided.","exampleZh":"书面转录不能证明我在没有辅助时听懂了多少。","collocation":"unaided comprehension","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v5","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"joined","zh":"连接","pos":"verb (past)","example":"Please correct me if I have joined two separate names together.","exampleZh":"如果我把两个独立名称连在了一起，请纠正我。","collocation":"joined two names","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v6","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unstressed syllables","zh":"非重读音节","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The difficulty emerged when the speaker reduced several unstressed syllables.","exampleZh":"说话者弱化了几个非重读音节时，困难就出现了。","collocation":"unstressed syllables","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v7","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"insufficient evidence","zh":"不充分的证据","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Exposure to one voice is insufficient evidence of broad accent comprehension.","exampleZh":"只接触一种声音不足以证明能广泛理解不同口音。","collocation":"insufficient evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v8","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"slowing down","zh":"放慢","pos":"gerund phrase","example":"I understood the individual words after slowing down the recording.","exampleZh":"放慢录音后，我听懂了各个单词。","collocation":"slowing down speech","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v9","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consenting","zh":"已同意录音的","pos":"adjective","example":"Next time I will test the same skill with a different consenting speaker.","exampleZh":"下次我会用另一位已同意录音的说话者检验同一技能。","collocation":"a consenting speaker","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v10","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"actual audio tasks","zh":"真实音频任务","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Listening competence needs actual audio tasks, not printed scripts alone.","exampleZh":"听力能力需要真实音频任务，而不能只靠纸面脚本。","collocation":"actual audio tasks","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v11","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"clarification","zh":"澄清","pos":"noun","example":"Clarification is a communication skill rather than an admission of failure.","exampleZh":"澄清是一种沟通技能，而非承认失败。","collocation":"request clarification","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v12","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconstruction","zh":"重建","pos":"noun","example":"Context can support reconstruction without proving exact perception.","exampleZh":"语境可支持重建，却不证明精确感知。","collocation":"contextual reconstruction","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v13","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accommodation","zh":"语言适应","pos":"noun","example":"Accommodation can help both speakers reach understanding.","exampleZh":"相互调整能帮助双方理解。","collocation":"mutual accommodation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v14","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stigmatisation","zh":"污名化","pos":"noun","example":"Training should avoid the stigmatisation of regional speech.","exampleZh":"训练应避免污名化地区语言。","collocation":"avoid stigmatisation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v15","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disambiguate","zh":"消除歧义","pos":"verb","example":"A spelling request can disambiguate similar names.","exampleZh":"请求拼写能区分相似名字。","collocation":"disambiguate a name","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v16","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a regional oral history project：扩展论证","text":"At a regional oral history project, participants considered whether to train interviewers to request clarification respectfully. The background was specific: an interviewer recognised every written word in a transcript but repeatedly missed the speakers place names when hearing them without context. However, familiar vocabulary on a page concealed a genuine difficulty with connected speech and regional vowel patterns. The proposed response was to practise with consented recordings from several speakers and check place names rather than guessing them.\n\nThe project should treat comprehension as an outcome to be checked, not a quality attributed to a listener on the basis of confidence. Interviewers can prepare local place names, but preparation should not be confused with performance on an unfamiliar recording. Training should include several consenting speakers and opportunities to compare an unaided first hearing with a checked transcript afterwards. Clarification requests belong in the training as well. They allow the interviewer to protect the historical record without pretending to understand a name. A printed script may help vocabulary learning, yet cannot reproduce reduced syllables, timing or regional pronunciation. Progress should consequently be documented through new audio tasks as well as written exercises. I recognised the place name on paper but missed it in connected speech. Could you repeat the street name and then spell the final word? An unfamiliar vowel pattern does not imply unfamiliar vocabulary. I should verify the name instead of selecting the nearest familiar one. The written transcript cannot establish how much I understood unaided. Please correct me if I have joined two separate names together. The difficulty emerged when the speaker reduced several unstressed syllables.\n\nAn interviewer later recognises a place name because she knows which village the speaker is likely to mention. This contextual success is useful, but the project should not confuse it with hearing every sound correctly. The distinction becomes visible when a similar name appears unexpectedly in a new recording. Training can use context while occasionally testing what happens when familiar predictions fail. The aim is not to remove context from communication; it is to prevent confidence from resting entirely on a guess. Interviewers should record uncertain names, request repetition where appropriate and verify the final transcription. Respect for speakers includes preserving what they said rather than replacing unfamiliar detail with a more comfortable expectation."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Confident Dispatcher","text":"A fictional courier service asks dispatchers to confirm unfamiliar destination names. One experienced dispatcher completes calls quickly and rarely asks for repetition. A new colleague takes longer but produces fewer corrected bookings. The supervisor initially praises the experienced worker as the stronger listener because the calls sound smoother. A review of errors complicates that judgement.\n\nThe experienced dispatcher often substitutes a familiar place when a name resembles one on the usual route. Most substitutions happen to be right because the company serves the same district repeatedly. The method becomes unreliable when a caller requests a delivery outside that district. Context has supplied useful expectations, but confidence has concealed the distinction between recognising a name and selecting a likely one.\n\nThe new colleague repeats the complete destination and asks for spelling when two names remain possible. This can sound hesitant, yet it gives the caller a clear chance to correct the record. The review should therefore assess the final shared understanding rather than fluency alone. Asking a question is not automatically evidence of weaker language ability; refusing to ask is not proof of accurate comprehension.\n\nThe supervisor also notices that errors are commonly attributed to callers accents. Some do involve unfamiliar pronunciation, but others arise when a caller changes a house number halfway through an utterance or refers to a building by an old name. A general accent label combines several distinct problems and can unfairly place all responsibility on the caller. Better training separates pronunciation, correction structure, local knowledge and line quality.\n\nThe service introduces confirmation of complete addresses and uses recordings made with appropriate consent for training. It tests unfamiliar names as well as routine ones, while making clear that laboratory success cannot guarantee performance during every noisy call. The experienced dispatcher retains valuable local knowledge; the new colleague contributes a more reliable repair strategy. Neither must be reduced to the label good or bad listener. The practical goal is accurate coordination across variation, supported by procedures that reveal uncertainty before a parcel is sent to the wrong place.\n\nThat distinction allows training to target an observed difficulty rather than a convenient stereotype about another persons voice."},"listening":{"teaching":"When I began transcribing interviews, I thought recognising the written transcript afterwards meant that I had nearly understood the recording. It often meant something narrower. Once I knew the intended words, I could connect the sounds with them. Without that support, my first interpretation sometimes remained quite different.\n\nThe distinction became clear with names. A speaker mentioned a small harbour, and I heard the name of a larger town I knew well. The sentence still made sense, so I did not mark uncertainty. Only a later reference to a ferry revealed the mistake. Familiarity had made the wrong interpretation comfortable rather than accurate.\n\nMy supervisor suggested keeping a first pass record before consulting the transcript. We then compared what I heard unaided, what I inferred from context and what I recognised only after checking. This did not make context illegitimate. Real listeners use it constantly. It helped us identify where predictions were doing most of the work.\n\nWe also practised respectful clarification with consenting speakers. Asking for a name again, or checking its spelling, protected the historical record. It did not require describing the speakers accent as defective. Written exercises helped vocabulary, but progress in listening required new audio from more than one voice. I now treat a familiar transcript as a learning aid, not a certificate that I would understand unfamiliar speech under different conditions. That more limited claim gives me a clearer direction for practice. The first hearing remains a separate source of evidence.","test":"The travel desk received a complaint after a ticket was issued for the wrong island. The caller had named the destination correctly, then corrected the departure date. The clerk combined the familiar island name from an earlier booking with the newly corrected date. He remembered the conversation as clear because the resulting itinerary sounded plausible.\n\nThe recording showed that this was not simply an unfamiliar accent problem. The destination was audible, and the correction concerned another part of the request. The clerk needed to keep the slots in the booking separate while updating one of them. His confidence in the overall story had hidden a mistake in how information was assigned.\n\nThe manager proposed a pronunciation workshop, which might be useful for other reasons. It would not directly address this error. The more relevant change was to repeat the whole itinerary after any correction: destination, date, time and passenger details. The caller could then confirm the final version rather than assume that the changed element had been attached to the right journey.\n\nI would also avoid concluding that the clerk was generally incapable. He had substantial route knowledge, and that knowledge helped on many calls. Here it supported an incorrect expectation. The review should preserve that distinction and test the revised procedure with unfamiliar combinations. A successful routine call does not establish that a listener can manage every correction, and a single failure does not explain itself merely because the speakers voices differ. The useful question is which part of understanding broke down and what would make it visible earlier."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the unexpected name test reveal?","options":["Whether predictions conceal uncertainty.","Whether context should be prohibited.","Whether all sounds must be isolated."],"answer":"Whether predictions conceal uncertainty.","explanation":"检验推测依赖，不是否定语境。","id":"c2-06:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why verify unfamiliar details?","options":["Familiar names are always wrong.","Respect includes preserving the actual account.","Transcripts replace listening competence."],"answer":"Respect includes preserving the actual account.","explanation":"准确保存而非舒适替换。","id":"c2-06:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why can smooth calls mislead the supervisor?","options":["Fast speech proves lack of experience.","Corrections necessarily indicate poor listening.","Fluency may conceal plausible substitutions."],"answer":"Fluency may conceal plausible substitutions.","explanation":"顺畅不等于共同理解准确。","id":"c2-06:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is accent an inadequate general label?","options":["Several distinct processes can produce errors.","Pronunciation never affects understanding.","Local knowledge is irrelevant."],"answer":"Several distinct processes can produce errors.","explanation":"诊断应区分多个原因。","id":"c2-06:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did later transcript recognition establish?","options":["Full unaided first understanding.","Sound to word mapping with support.","Ability across every voice."],"answer":"Sound to word mapping with support.","explanation":"辅助后的识别范围较窄。","id":"c2-06:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why retain a first pass record?","options":["To prevent all contextual learning.","To rank speakers accents.","To distinguish hearing, inference and later recognition."],"answer":"To distinguish hearing, inference and later recognition.","explanation":"区分证据来源。","id":"c2-06:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What error occurred at the travel desk?","options":["A correction was assigned within the wrong combination.","The caller named the island incorrectly.","The departure date was never corrected."],"answer":"A correction was assigned within the wrong combination.","explanation":"信息槽位与更新混淆。","id":"c2-06:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which change addresses the observed error?","options":["Only practise island pronunciation.","Repeat the whole itinerary after corrections.","Rely more strongly on familiar routes."],"answer":"Repeat the whole itinerary after corrections.","explanation":"复述最终整体能检查更新。","id":"c2-06:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Design interviewer training that separates unaided recognition, contextual reconstruction and later checking.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Distinguish comprehension from reconstruction and use clarification without stigmatising speakers.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a training review of The Confident Dispatcher, comparing final understanding with apparent fluency and avoiding a blanket accent explanation. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Confident Dispatcher. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why can smooth calls mislead the supervisor?","Why is accent an inadequate general label?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["service trainer","experienced operator"],"goal":"Agree a verification procedure that protects accuracy and respects callers.","twist":"The smoothest operator has more plausible substitutions than the hesitant newcomer."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving distinguish comprehension from reconstruction and use clarification without stigmatising speakers. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-07","level":"C2","title":"反讽的对象与关系","chapter":71,"focus":"Infer irony from context while distinguishing its target, effect and uncertain intention.","vocab":[{"head":"irony","zh":"反讽","pos":"noun","example":"The irony of the welcome ceremony became apparent when guests were asked to admire a newly installed ramp that ended at a locked door.","exampleZh":"当宾客被请去欣赏一条尽头却是锁门的新坡道时，欢迎仪式的反讽意味就显现了。","collocation":"situational irony","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v1","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flawless","zh":"毫无缺点的","pos":"adjective","example":"A visitor described the planning as flawless while pointing towards the inaccessible entrance, making the literal praise difficult to reconcile with the scene.","exampleZh":"一位来访者指着无法通行的入口称规划完美无缺，让字面赞扬难以与眼前情景一致。","collocation":"flawless planning","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v2","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"target","zh":"所针对的对象","pos":"noun","example":"The target of the criticism appeared to be the arrangement rather than the volunteer who happened to be standing beside the entrance.","exampleZh":"批评所针对的似乎是安排本身，而非恰好站在入口旁的志愿者。","collocation":"the target of criticism","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v3","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"delivery","zh":"说话方式","pos":"noun","example":"Her measured delivery gave no exaggerated signal of amusement, although the contradiction between her words and her gesture remained available to the listeners.","exampleZh":"她平稳的说话方式并未夸张地表示幽默，不过言语与动作之间的矛盾仍可被听者察觉。","collocation":"a measured delivery","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v4","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadpan","zh":"不动声色的","pos":"adjective","example":"A deadpan remark can depend on shared recognition, which cannot be assumed merely because two people speak the same language with confidence.","exampleZh":"不动声色的玩笑可能依赖共同认知，而不能仅因两个人都能自信使用同一语言就假定存在。","collocation":"a deadpan remark","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v5","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"incongruity","zh":"不协调","pos":"noun","example":"Some guests noticed the incongruity immediately, while another asked whether a second entrance had actually been opened for the occasion elsewhere.","exampleZh":"一些客人马上注意到不协调，另一位却问是否已在别处为活动开放第二入口。","collocation":"notice an incongruity","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v6","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"literal","zh":"字面的","pos":"adjective","example":"That question was reasonable under a literal reading, reminding the organiser that understanding the information and recognising the joke were separate achievements.","exampleZh":"按字面理解那个问题合情合理，提醒组织者理解信息和识别玩笑是两种不同的表现。","collocation":"a literal reading","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v7","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reception","zh":"接受效果","pos":"noun","example":"Audience reception also varied among those who understood the criticism: one laughed, whereas another considered public embarrassment an unhelpful response to an immediate problem.","exampleZh":"即使理解批评，受众反应也不同：有人笑了，有人觉得公开让人难堪无助于解决眼前问题。","collocation":"audience reception","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v8","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"认定属于","pos":"verb","example":"We should therefore hesitate to attribute a single intention or a uniform emotional effect to the remark on the basis of its wording alone.","exampleZh":"因此，不能仅凭措辞就轻易认定这句话只有一种意图或统一的情感效果。","collocation":"attribute an intention","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v9","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hostility","zh":"敌意","pos":"noun","example":"The volunteer initially heard hostility towards herself, but the visitor immediately asked who could find the key and offered to help redirect arrivals.","exampleZh":"志愿者起初觉得对方针对自己有敌意，但来访者随即问谁能找到钥匙，并主动帮忙引导来宾。","collocation":"evidence of hostility","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v10","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定","pos":"noun","example":"This action supplies an important qualification to a reading of deliberate humiliation, without proving that the wording had caused nobody any discomfort.","exampleZh":"这一行动为故意羞辱的解读提供重要限定，却不能证明措辞没有让任何人不舒服。","collocation":"an important qualification","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v11","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rapport","zh":"融洽关系","pos":"noun","example":"The visitor and the volunteer had not previously established rapport, so a style that worked among friends was carrying additional interpersonal risk.","exampleZh":"来访者与志愿者此前没有建立融洽关系，因此在朋友间有效的风格带来了额外的人际风险。","collocation":"establish rapport","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v12","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"repair","zh":"补救沟通","pos":"noun","example":"A short conversational repair followed: the visitor explained that she meant the design, not the person, and acknowledged the unfortunate timing of the joke.","exampleZh":"随后出现了简短的沟通补救：来访者解释针对的是设计而非个人，并承认玩笑时机不佳。","collocation":"conversational repair","register":"specialist","id":"c2-07:v13","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uptake","zh":"理解与回应","pos":"noun","example":"Checking the uptake meant asking whether the practical concern was understood, rather than requiring the volunteer to declare that she now found the comment funny.","exampleZh":"检查理解与回应意味着询问实际问题是否明白，而非要求志愿者声称现在觉得评论好笑。","collocation":"check the uptake","register":"specialist","id":"c2-07:v14","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate","zh":"适度的","pos":"adjective","example":"A proportionate response would preserve the access complaint, clarify the misunderstanding and avoid turning either participant into a symbol of complete insensitivity.","exampleZh":"适度回应应保留无障碍方面的投诉、澄清误解，并避免将任一方视为完全不体贴的象征。","collocation":"a proportionate response","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v15","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contextual","zh":"与语境有关的","pos":"adjective","example":"The case shows why contextual evidence can support an ironic reading while leaving important questions about intention, judgement and social effect open.","exampleZh":"这个案例说明语境证据可以支持反讽解读，同时让意图、判断和社会效果的重要问题保持开放。","collocation":"contextual evidence","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v16","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"反讽的对象与关系：原创教学叙事","text":"The irony of the welcome ceremony became apparent when guests were asked to admire a newly installed ramp that ended at a locked door. A visitor described the planning as flawless while pointing towards the inaccessible entrance, making the literal praise difficult to reconcile with the scene. The target of the criticism appeared to be the arrangement rather than the volunteer who happened to be standing beside the entrance. Her measured delivery gave no exaggerated signal of amusement, although the contradiction between her words and her gesture remained available to the listeners. A deadpan remark can depend on shared recognition, which cannot be assumed merely because two people speak the same language with confidence. Some guests noticed the incongruity immediately, while another asked whether a second entrance had actually been opened for the occasion elsewhere. That question was reasonable under a literal reading, reminding the organiser that understanding the information and recognising the joke were separate achievements. Audience reception also varied among those who understood the criticism: one laughed, whereas another considered public embarrassment an unhelpful response to an immediate problem. We should therefore hesitate to attribute a single intention or a uniform emotional effect to the remark on the basis of its wording alone. The volunteer initially heard hostility towards herself, but the visitor immediately asked who could find the key and offered to help redirect arrivals. This action supplies an important qualification to a reading of deliberate humiliation, without proving that the wording had caused nobody any discomfort. The visitor and the volunteer had not previously established rapport, so a style that worked among friends was carrying additional interpersonal risk. A short conversational repair followed: the visitor explained that she meant the design, not the person, and acknowledged the unfortunate timing of the joke. Checking the uptake meant asking whether the practical concern was understood, rather than requiring the volunteer to declare that she now found the comment funny. A proportionate response would preserve the access complaint, clarify the misunderstanding and avoid turning either participant into a symbol of complete insensitivity. The case shows why contextual evidence can support an ironic reading while leaving important questions about intention, judgement and social effect open."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Generous Deadline","text":"During a fictional residents meeting, the chair announces that comments on a complicated redevelopment proposal must be submitted by nine the following morning. It is already eight in the evening. A resident replies, with no visible smile, that the committee is being exceptionally generous with people's time. Several people laugh. The chair responds that eleven hours is technically longer than one working day, and the exchange quickly becomes a dispute about arithmetic.\n\nThe arithmetic is not the residents principal point. Her description is difficult to read as genuine gratitude because the interval includes the night and follows the late release of a lengthy document. These circumstances support an ironic interpretation: the praise draws attention to an inadequate opportunity for participation. Yet the fact that other residents laugh does not prove they agree with every criticism she might hold about the committee. They could recognise the contrast while disagreeing about the appropriate extension.\n\nA second resident then calls the chair a thief of public voices. This is a stronger accusation, not simply another wording of the first remark. It attributes a deliberate intention to exclude. The available facts establish an inconvenient and arguably unreasonable deadline, but they do not establish why it was chosen. The chair says that an external funding schedule is involved. This explanation needs checking; it should neither be accepted automatically nor discarded merely because the earlier response was defensive.\n\nThe meeting becomes more productive when someone asks which parts of the timetable are fixed and which can still change. The committee agrees to accept initial objections by morning and supporting detail three days later, while publishing the correspondence concerning the funding date. The arrangement does not erase the original difficulty, especially for residents unable to attend the meeting. It nevertheless addresses the practical concern more directly than a contest over whether generous was factually accurate.\n\nA careful account would distinguish an ironic challenge to a procedure from an unsupported accusation about personal motives. It would also preserve the question of access for absent residents. Recognising humour is only the first step; understanding what follows from it requires attention to the problem, the evidence and the people who have not yet had an opportunity to respond."},"listening":{"teaching":"I was the technician at the community cinema when the projector failed during the welcoming speech. The host looked at the blank screen and said that our commitment to minimalism had reached an impressive new level. Most of the audience laughed, and I understood why: everyone had expected a film, not an empty rectangle. I did not understand the line as a serious description of our artistic policy.\n\nWhat interested me afterwards was the reaction of the new assistant. She thought the host was mocking her because she had checked the projector before the audience arrived. That interpretation was possible in the moment, but the host had not named her, and he later thanked the technical team while taking responsibility for starting before the backup test was complete. Those details matter when deciding whom the remark targeted.\n\nThey do not establish that the assistant was wrong to feel exposed. The joke occurred in front of an audience, and she knew she had performed the check. An intention to lighten the atmosphere and an uncomfortable effect on a particular person can coexist. Telling her simply to develop a sense of humour would avoid that distinction.\n\nThe useful response was to discuss both the equipment procedure and the public remark. We agreed that future hosts would announce a delay plainly before adding humour, and that technical faults would be reviewed privately without guessing at blame. The lesson is not that jokes are prohibited. It is that a shared laugh does not remove the need to identify the practical problem and to check how the people most closely involved understood the exchange.","test":"Our workshop leader thanked us for our remarkably spacious storage system while stepping over boxes that blocked the exit. I understood the praise as ironic because the gesture and the obstruction contradicted a literal reading. However, a visiting participant thought she meant that we had been given too much space and offered to move our materials into a smaller room.\n\nThe misunderstanding showed that the remark depended on knowledge of what the room was supposed to look like. Regular members knew that a clear walkway had been part of the agreed plan. The visitor had not attended that discussion. His language knowledge was not necessarily the problem; the relevant shared background was missing.\n\nThe leader clarified that the boxes needed moving because they restricted access. She also said that she had made the joke before giving the visitor enough information. This was more useful than asking him why he had taken her literally. It turned an ambiguous criticism into an instruction everyone could follow.\n\nLater, one member claimed that the leader had been furious with the entire group. The account does not establish that intensity of feeling. Her words, action and subsequent clarification support criticism of the storage arrangement. They leave her exact emotion uncertain. We should preserve the supported criticism without enlarging it into a complete psychological judgement. Equally, the eventual clarification does not make the original wording universally clear in retrospect. For this listener, it plainly was not, and the group learned something about the assumptions on which its everyday humour depended."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What most strongly supports an ironic reading of flawless?","options":["The mismatch between praise and the blocked access.","The visitors measured delivery alone.","The volunteers uncertainty about the key."],"answer":"The mismatch between praise and the blocked access.","explanation":"关键是赞扬与事实及动作之间的冲突；语气本身并不充分。","id":"c2-07:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the later offer to help change?","options":["It proves the volunteer enjoyed the joke.","It removes the need to fix access.","It qualifies an interpretation of deliberate humiliation."],"answer":"It qualifies an interpretation of deliberate humiliation.","explanation":"帮助行动限制恶意解读，但不抹去不适或实际问题。","id":"c2-07:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is lost when the exchange becomes arithmetic?","options":["The precise time of the meeting.","The concern about a meaningful opportunity to respond.","The residents preference for an earlier bedtime."],"answer":"The concern about a meaningful opportunity to respond.","explanation":"讽刺强调实质参与时间，不是单纯计算小时。","id":"c2-07:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How does thief of public voices differ from the first remark?","options":["It makes a stronger claim about deliberate exclusion.","It gives independent evidence about the funding date.","It merely repeats the same factual criticism."],"answer":"It makes a stronger claim about deliberate exclusion.","explanation":"后一句推定故意动机，超出了已知程序问题。","id":"c2-07:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can coexist according to the technician?","options":["A blank screen and a completed backup test.","A private review and a public accusation of fault.","Lightening the mood and making an assistant feel exposed."],"answer":"Lightening the mood and making an assistant feel exposed.","explanation":"意图与对具体人的效果可以不同。","id":"c2-07:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is audience laughter insufficient evidence about the assistant?","options":["It proves everyone knew she had checked the equipment.","It does not establish her reception of the remark.","It makes the hosts later explanation unreliable."],"answer":"It does not establish her reception of the remark.","explanation":"集体笑声不能替代具体听者的感受和理解。","id":"c2-07:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did the visitor misunderstand?","options":["He lacked relevant shared background about the layout.","He had been told that the leader wanted a smaller room.","He could not recognise the word spacious."],"answer":"He lacked relevant shared background about the layout.","explanation":"问题可能在共享语境，而非词汇知识。","id":"c2-07:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What judgement does the speaker resist?","options":["That the boxes obstructed access.","That clarification supplied useful information.","That criticism establishes the leader's exact emotional intensity."],"answer":"That criticism establishes the leader's exact emotional intensity.","explanation":"支持批评布局，不支持确定愤怒程度。","id":"c2-07:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an account of the locked ramp exchange for the event organiser. Separate the complaint, the humorous framing and the effect on the volunteer.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Infer irony from context while distinguishing its target, effect and uncertain intention.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Prepare a report on The Generous Deadline for absent residents. Distinguish the procedural criticism from claims about motives and evaluate the revised arrangement. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Generous Deadline. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What is lost when the exchange becomes arithmetic?","How does thief of public voices differ from the first remark?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["event organiser","participant affected by an ironic comment"],"goal":"Resolve the access problem and agree how to repair the interpersonal misunderstanding.","twist":"One participant understood the praise literally and acted on it."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving infer irony from context while distinguishing its target, effect and uncertain intention. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-08","level":"C2","title":"隐含立场与归因克制","chapter":72,"focus":"Infer reservations from sequence and consequence without inventing private motives.","vocab":[{"head":"reservation","zh":"保留意见","pos":"noun","example":"The compliment is followed by a reservation that changes its practical force.","exampleZh":"这句赞扬之后的保留意见改变了它的实际作用。","collocation":"a significant reservation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v1","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withheld","zh":"暂未给予","pos":"verb (past)","example":"She did not reject the idea outright, but she withheld immediate approval.","exampleZh":"她没有直接否决想法，但暂未立即批准。","collocation":"withheld approval","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v2","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"The timetable would keep everyone occupied may imply concern about feasibility.","exampleZh":"时间表会让大家忙个不停可能暗示对可行性的担忧。","collocation":"practical feasibility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v3","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency plans","zh":"应急计划","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That interpretation is supported by the repeated requests for contingency plans.","exampleZh":"反复要求应急计划支持了这种解读。","collocation":"contingency plans","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v4","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归因于","pos":"verb","example":"I would not attribute personal hostility on the basis of that sentence.","exampleZh":"我不会仅凭那句话就认定她有个人敌意。","collocation":"attribute hostility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v5","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"misrepresent","zh":"歪曲","pos":"verb","example":"Quoting the praise alone would misrepresent the reviewers overall stance.","exampleZh":"只引用赞扬会歪曲评审者的整体立场。","collocation":"misrepresent a stance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v6","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hedge","zh":"缓和措辞","pos":"noun","example":"The hedge softens the judgement without necessarily weakening the reservation.","exampleZh":"缓和措辞弱化了判断的语气，却未必减轻保留意见。","collocation":"a linguistic hedge","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v7","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"caution","zh":"谨慎","pos":"noun","example":"We can infer caution while remaining uncertain about its precise cause.","exampleZh":"我们可以推断存在谨慎态度，同时不确定其具体原因。","collocation":"infer caution","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v8","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"text","zh":"文本","pos":"noun","example":"Where does the text stop and your interpretation begin?","exampleZh":"文本到哪里结束，你的解读从哪里开始？","collocation":"text and interpretation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v9","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stance","zh":"立场","pos":"noun","example":"The proposed action gives stronger evidence of stance than the polite adjective.","exampleZh":"建议采取的行动比礼貌的形容词更能体现立场。","collocation":"overall stance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v10","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"支持","pos":"noun","example":"A useful summary preserves both the endorsement and its limits.","exampleZh":"有用的总结会同时保留支持意见及其限制。","collocation":"qualified endorsement","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v11","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradicted","zh":"与之冲突","pos":"verb (past)","example":"I would revise that inference if a later passage explicitly contradicted it.","exampleZh":"如果后文明确与之冲突，我会修改这个推断。","collocation":"contradicted an inference","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v12","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"implicature","zh":"语用含意","pos":"noun","example":"The implicature depends on the surrounding questions.","exampleZh":"含意依赖前后的问题。","collocation":"a contextual implicature","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v13","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"认可","pos":"noun","example":"Praise for one feature is not endorsement of the whole plan.","exampleZh":"赞扬一处不等于认可整个方案。","collocation":"qualified endorsement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v14","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribution","zh":"归因","pos":"noun","example":"The attribution of hostility exceeds the available evidence.","exampleZh":"认定敌意超出了现有证据。","collocation":"an unsupported attribution","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v15","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"corrigible","zh":"可修正的","pos":"adjective","example":"A useful inference remains corrigible when new evidence appears.","exampleZh":"新证据出现时，有用推断仍可修正。","collocation":"a corrigible judgement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v16","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a design grant selection panel：扩展论证","text":"At a design grant selection panel, participants considered whether to interpret a cautious endorsement of an ambitious proposal. The background was specific: a reviewer praised the teams imagination and added that the timetable would certainly keep everyone occupied, while asking for a contingency plan. However, the sentence did not explicitly call the schedule unrealistic, but its placement after repeated deadline questions mattered. The proposed response was to report the reservation as an inference supported by context rather than as a direct quotation of rejection.\n\nThe panel can preserve a constructive relationship without treating praise as approval. Asking for a contingency plan gives the applicants something concrete to address, whereas debating whether the reviewer secretly disliked the team would achieve little. I would record the strength identified, the timetable concern inferred from the surrounding questions, and the revision needed before a decision. If the reviewer later clarified that no reservation was intended, the summary should change accordingly. That possibility does not make every inference equally speculative. Placement and practical consequence provide evidence, but they support a limited conclusion about the proposal rather than an account of the reviewers personality. The response should be equally specific. The compliment is followed by a reservation that changes its practical force. She did not reject the idea outright, but she withheld immediate approval. The timetable would keep everyone occupied may imply concern about feasibility. That interpretation is supported by the repeated requests for contingency plans. I would not attribute personal hostility on the basis of that sentence. Quoting the praise alone would misrepresent the reviewers overall stance. The hedge softens the judgement without necessarily weakening the reservation.\n\nThe applicants ask whether the panel liked their idea. The administrator could answer this narrowly, reporting the praise for imagination, but the answer would be incomplete if it omitted the unresolved timetable. A useful response distinguishes evaluation of the concept from readiness to fund it. The panel may welcome a proposal and still require revision before approval. The administrator should therefore identify what was praised, what remained uncertain and what action was requested. She should not translate courtesy into acceptance or caution into hostility. If the panel later clarifies its position explicitly, that new evidence takes precedence over an earlier inference; responsible interpretation remains open to correction."},"sourceB":{"title":"An Interesting First Attempt","text":"A fictional design teacher describes a student's model as an interesting first attempt. She praises the arrangement of public space, then asks how emergency access would work and suggests another review before the final display. The student tells a friend that the teacher loved the project. Another student says that first attempt is a polite way of calling it hopeless. Both summaries enlarge one part of the exchange beyond what it establishes.\n\nInteresting can express genuine engagement without indicating readiness for approval. First attempt can place the work within a continuing process rather than condemn it. The strongest evidence about the next decision lies in the request for another review and the question about emergency access. These details show that an issue remains to be resolved. They do not reveal whether the teacher privately doubts the student's ability.\n\nThe student might reasonably ask for clarification. A useful question would identify the required change or the standard for the next review. Asking whether the teacher really likes her would shift attention from the work to an emotional judgement the teacher may not have made. The difference matters because uncertainty about a practical requirement is answerable in a way that guessing another persons inner attitude is not.\n\nLater, the teacher writes that the central layout should be retained while the service route is redesigned. This explicit instruction narrows the earlier ambiguity. It supports the view that one aspect was valued and another required revision. A reader should update the interpretation rather than cling to a dramatic initial reading simply because it was psychologically appealing.\n\nThe case also shows the limits of interpreting single phrases in isolation. A list of expressions labelled polite rejection would encourage students to hear every qualified compliment as hostility. A list labelled praise would lose the reservations. Competent interpretation tracks the sequence, the requested action and later clarification. It can report a supported inference while marking it as an inference, preserving a route to correction. The final account should therefore say that the teacher engaged positively with the layout but withheld final approval pending a practical redesign. Anything more personal needs additional evidence."},"listening":{"teaching":"The committee told us that our event proposal was ambitious and that the volunteer schedule would certainly give us plenty to think about. I initially repeated ambitious as a positive verdict. My colleague noticed that the schedule had been questioned three times and that approval was postponed until we supplied a backup plan.\n\nHer interpretation was not that the committee hated the idea. It was that the polite phrasing carried a reservation about whether our staffing assumptions were realistic. The surrounding sequence supported that reading more strongly than the adjective alone. The practical consequence, a request for another plan, was particularly important.\n\nWe wrote back asking which periods required additional cover. That question produced useful guidance. The committee identified the evening entrance and the final cleanup, while confirming that the main programme could remain. We revised those sections instead of rewriting the event from scratch or treating the whole proposal as accepted.\n\nThis taught me to separate approval of an idea from approval of its implementation. A speaker can express interest and caution at the same time. Summaries should preserve both when both affect the next action. They should also distinguish what was explicitly stated from what is inferred. If the committee had later said that our reading was mistaken, we would have needed to reconsider it. Context makes an inference defensible; it does not turn it into a quotation or provide unrestricted access to a speakers private feelings. The distinction keeps later correction possible without embarrassment.","test":"The editor returned my article with a note calling the interviews vivid and suggesting that a careful reader might want to know where the figures came from. I felt relieved by vivid and almost missed the significance of the second clause. The article had not been accepted; the source of a central statistic remained unresolved.\n\nA friend interpreted the note as sarcastic dismissal. I could not justify that conclusion. The editor had identified a specific issue and invited a revision. There was no evidence that the praise was insincere or that she wanted me to abandon the subject. Her restrained wording could simply preserve a working relationship while making further checking necessary.\n\nI asked whether a link to the original report would be sufficient. She replied that the report used a narrower population than my article implied. This made the problem more precise: it concerned the scope of the claim as well as the presence of a citation. I rewrote the passage and explained the limitation.\n\nThe later exchange should change any interpretation of the first note. We now know what concern the indirect phrase introduced. It would be misleading to continue reporting either enthusiastic acceptance or total rejection. The strongest summary is that the editor valued the interviews but required a better supported statistical claim. Recognising that mixed position allowed me to act on the feedback without making unsupported assumptions about her attitude towards me. It also prevented a compliment from becoming a reason to ignore the reservation attached to it."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why would reporting praise alone be incomplete?","options":["The panel rejected imagination.","Funding readiness remained conditional.","The administrator had no role."],"answer":"Funding readiness remained conditional.","explanation":"概念评价与实施批准不同。","id":"c2-08:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What should explicit later clarification do?","options":["Correct an earlier inference when warranted.","Be ignored if the first reading was plausible.","Prove every earlier inference worthless."],"answer":"Correct an earlier inference when warranted.","explanation":"推断必须可更新。","id":"c2-08:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which detail best indicates readiness?","options":["The adjective interesting alone.","A peer calling the model hopeless.","The request for another review."],"answer":"The request for another review.","explanation":"行动后果比孤立词更强。","id":"c2-08:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why reject phrase lists as sufficient?","options":["Qualified praise never conveys reservation.","Sequence and later action alter interpretation.","Teachers never use indirect language."],"answer":"Sequence and later action alter interpretation.","explanation":"含意依赖语境。","id":"c2-08:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What supported a timetable reservation?","options":["Repeated questions and postponed approval.","Ambitious always means rejection.","A dislike of volunteers was explicit."],"answer":"Repeated questions and postponed approval.","explanation":"多条语境线索共同支持限定推断。","id":"c2-08:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the follow up enable?","options":["Immediate unconditional acceptance.","A judgement of private feelings.","Targeted revision rather than a total rewrite."],"answer":"Targeted revision rather than a total rewrite.","explanation":"具体问法带来具体行动。","id":"c2-08:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the citation exchange clarify?","options":["Only the link formatting mattered.","The claim had a population scope problem.","The interviews were invented."],"answer":"The claim had a population scope problem.","explanation":"来源存在不等于外推正确。","id":"c2-08:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which summary is best supported?","options":["Positive interviews with a required evidence revision.","Total rejection of the writer.","Acceptance based solely on vividness."],"answer":"Positive interviews with a required evidence revision.","explanation":"保留赞许与限定。","id":"c2-08:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Summarise the grant panels mixed evaluation and propose a precise clarification request.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Infer reservations from sequence and consequence without inventing private motives.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Advise the design student in An Interesting First Attempt. Separate praise, readiness for approval and unsupported personal interpretations. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on An Interesting First Attempt. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Which detail best indicates readiness?","Why reject phrase lists as sufficient?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["applicant","administrator interpreting feedback"],"goal":"Identify the practical revision needed while marking inference as inference.","twist":"A later explicit message contradicts your first interpretation."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving infer reservations from sequence and consequence without inventing private motives. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-09","level":"C2","title":"评论标准与范围公平","chapter":73,"focus":"Judge an achievement against a defensible purpose without exempting it from criticism.","vocab":[{"head":"stated purpose","zh":"明确的目的","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The exhibition should first be judged against its stated purpose.","exampleZh":"首先应根据展览明确的目的来评价它。","collocation":"a stated purpose","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v1","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit context","zh":"限制背景信息","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Missing national statistics limit context without necessarily defeating the project.","exampleZh":"缺少全国数据限制了背景信息，却未必使项目失败。","collocation":"limit context","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v2","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"different exhibition","zh":"另一种展览","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The reviewer appears to demand a different exhibition rather than a better version.","exampleZh":"评论者似乎要求的是另一种展览，而非改进后的同一展览。","collocation":"a different exhibition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v3","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"omission","zh":"缺漏","pos":"noun","example":"That criticism identifies an omission but does not explain its significance.","exampleZh":"那条批评指出了缺漏，却未解释其重要性。","collocation":"a consequential omission","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v4","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"domestic scale","zh":"家庭层面的规模","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The domestic scale enables detail that a national survey might lose.","exampleZh":"家庭层面的规模保留了全国调查可能丢失的细节。","collocation":"domestic scale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v5","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"restricted scope","zh":"有限范围","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would distinguish restricted scope from inadequate execution.","exampleZh":"我会区分范围有限和执行不足。","collocation":"restricted scope","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v6","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unanswered question","zh":"未获解答的问题","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The strongest objection concerns an unanswered question relevant to the exhibitions aim.","exampleZh":"最有力的异议关乎一个与展览目标相关却未获解答的问题。","collocation":"an unanswered question","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v7","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible criteria","zh":"相容的标准","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The comparison is unfair unless both works are assessed by compatible criteria.","exampleZh":"除非用相容标准评价两件作品，否则比较不公平。","collocation":"compatible criteria","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v8","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"represents","zh":"呈现","pos":"verb","example":"A persuasive review represents the work accurately before challenging it.","exampleZh":"有说服力的评论会先准确呈现作品，再提出质疑。","collocation":"represents the work","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v9","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enrich","zh":"丰富","pos":"verb","example":"The proposed context could enrich the exhibition without replacing its central focus.","exampleZh":"建议加入的背景可以丰富展览，而不取代其核心。","collocation":"enrich an exhibition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v10","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criterion","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"I disagree with the verdict because its criterion remains unstated.","exampleZh":"我不同意这个结论，因为它的标准未被说明。","collocation":"a relevant criterion","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-09:v11","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate judgement","zh":"适度的判断","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An engaging sentence is not a substitute for a proportionate judgement.","exampleZh":"生动的句子不能替代适度的判断。","collocation":"a proportionate judgement","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v12","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criterion","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"The criterion should be relevant to the stated purpose.","exampleZh":"标准应与声明目的相关。","collocation":"a relevant criterion","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v13","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportion","zh":"分寸","pos":"noun","example":"The review needs a sense of proportion.","exampleZh":"评论需要分寸感。","collocation":"a sense of proportion","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v14","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exemption","zh":"豁免","pos":"noun","example":"Limited scope is not an exemption from scrutiny.","exampleZh":"范围有限不等于免受审查。","collocation":"an exemption from scrutiny","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v15","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"execution","zh":"执行成效","pos":"noun","example":"The criticism concerns execution rather than scale.","exampleZh":"批评关乎执行而非规模。","collocation":"assess execution","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v16","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a review of an exhibition about household objects：扩展论证","text":"At a review of an exhibition about household objects, participants considered whether to evaluate whether limited scale necessarily meant limited insight. The background was specific: the reviewer complained that no national production figures appeared, although the exhibition explicitly explored how three families repaired inherited objects. However, a fair demand for context could become an unfair demand that a small exhibition turn into a different kind of project. The proposed response was to judge the stated purpose first and then explain what broader context would add.\n\nA small exhibition can illuminate repair as a family practice precisely because it does not attempt to describe an entire national economy. That choice does not exempt it from criticism. The reviewer could ask whether the three families are introduced clearly, whether repaired objects support the claims made about them, or whether a little wider context would prevent misleading impressions. Those questions engage with the actual exhibition. Demanding a complete statistical account may instead replace its purpose. I would recommend a short contextual panel if it helped visitors locate the stories, while preserving the close attention that makes the project distinctive. Evaluation becomes more persuasive when it explains the relationship between an omission and a real limitation for the intended audience. The exhibition should first be judged against its stated purpose. Missing national statistics limit context without necessarily defeating the project. The reviewer appears to demand a different exhibition rather than a better version. That criticism identifies an omission but does not explain its significance. The domestic scale enables detail that a national survey might lose. I would distinguish restricted scope from inadequate execution.\n\nThe exhibition curator does not answer criticism by saying that every choice is merely personal. A stated purpose can be examined: do the objects actually support the claims made about repair, and are visitors told why these families were selected? These questions differ from demanding a national industrial history, yet they may still expose weaknesses. The reviewer should make the criterion visible and connect each objection with an effect on understanding. The curator, in turn, can accept useful context without abandoning the exhibitions intimate scale. Fair criticism respects the object of evaluation while retaining the right to ask whether its own chosen purpose has been achieved convincingly."},"sourceB":{"title":"A Map That Is Not an Atlas","text":"A fictional publisher releases a pocket map of public drinking fountains. It shows locations, opening restrictions and whether a tap can be reached from a wheelchair. A reviewer criticises it for omitting restaurants, public toilets and a history of the citys water supply. The publisher replies that those subjects were never promised. This response identifies a problem in the review, but it does not settle the quality of the map.\n\nThe relevant question is whether the selected information serves the maps purpose. A pocket format can reasonably exclude extensive history. Excluding a warning that a fountain is seasonal would be harder to defend because it affects the very activity the map claims to support. Some omissions merely define scope; others undermine performance within that scope. A fair review should distinguish them.\n\nThe reviewer subsequently checks the entries and finds that two taps shown as continuously available are behind gates locked in the evening. This is a stronger criticism than the absence of restaurant listings. It identifies inaccurate practical information that a user could rely on. The map might still be useful overall, but its reliability requires correction and a visible update procedure.\n\nThe publisher initially says that no small map can contain everything. That principle is true but does not answer the specific objection. The complaint is not that the map lacks every possible detail; it is that the information it includes is misleading under relevant conditions. A defence of limited scope cannot become a universal shield against accountability.\n\nThe revised review recommends the map for its clear focus and accessibility information while warning that opening restrictions need checking. The publisher corrects the entries and prints the date of verification. Neither side must retreat into an absolute verdict of excellent or useless. The strongest evaluation explains what the object enables, for whom, under which conditions and with what remaining limitations. This gives readers a basis for action rather than requiring them to choose between an unfair demand for completeness and an equally unfair refusal to acknowledge errors inside the chosen task.\n\nReaders can then judge whether the limitation affects their own intended use, instead of relying on an unexplained overall rating."},"listening":{"teaching":"A reviewer described our short documentary as politically empty because it contained no interview with a national minister. The film followed a local repair worker through three days and asked how small businesses managed tools they could not easily replace. We had not promised a comprehensive account of industrial policy.\n\nThat did not mean the review had nothing to teach us. At one point the worker blamed an import rule for a delay, and we presented the statement without checking what the rule actually required. The reviewer was right to question that passage. Our limited observational purpose did not excuse a factual implication we had chosen to include.\n\nWe separated the two criticisms. Demanding a ministerial interview would turn the film into another project. Asking us to verify the explanation of the delay concerned the project we had made. We added a note clarifying the rule and retained the focus on daily work.\n\nThis distinction is useful whenever a small work receives a large demand. Scope should guide the criteria of judgement, but cannot immunise every choice from examination. A reviewer should explain how an omission affects the stated aim. A creator should answer the strongest relevant objection instead of using an irrelevant demand as a reason to dismiss the whole review. Both sides then remain accountable to the actual work, rather than debating a different film that could also have been worth making but was never the film offered to the audience.","test":"Our beginner cooking booklet was criticised for containing no advanced knife techniques. Its purpose was to help students prepare five inexpensive meals in a shared kitchen. We thought that criticism missed the intended audience, and in that respect we had a reasonable defence.\n\nThe same review noted that two recipes used ingredients omitted from the shopping list. This was different. Beginners were especially likely to rely on that list, so the omission directly interfered with the booklets practical purpose. Saying that no booklet could include everything would not answer it.\n\nWe corrected the list and asked readers to test the recipes with only the equipment named in the introduction. That produced another useful discovery: one instruction assumed access to a lid, although the equipment list did not mention one. The issue was not sophisticated expertise but consistency between the promise and the resources supplied.\n\nI would therefore describe the review as mixed rather than wholly fair or wholly unfair. Its demand for advanced techniques applied an inappropriate standard. Its comments about the lists exposed genuine weaknesses. Rejecting both together would protect us from useful criticism; accepting both would encourage us to abandon the simple purpose that readers valued. Evaluation becomes more productive when each objection is linked to a criterion and a consequence. The revised booklet remains introductory, but its introduction is now a more reliable guide to what a reader needs and what the recipes actually require. That is a meaningful improvement without a change in its chosen scale."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does respecting purpose still allow?","options":["Testing whether the selected purpose is achieved.","Exemption from all criticism.","A demand for national completeness."],"answer":"Testing whether the selected purpose is achieved.","explanation":"目的限定相关标准，不取消审查。","id":"c2-09:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What should each objection identify?","options":["Only the reviewers taste.","An alternative exhibition title.","Its effect on understanding."],"answer":"Its effect on understanding.","explanation":"批评需说明标准与后果。","id":"c2-09:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which omission is most relevant?","options":["A complete water history.","Opening restrictions affecting use.","Every nearby restaurant."],"answer":"Opening restrictions affecting use.","explanation":"直接影响核心用途。","id":"c2-09:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is no map contains everything inadequate?","options":["It does not answer included misinformation.","The map promised a full atlas.","Users requested fewer details."],"answer":"It does not answer included misinformation.","explanation":"范围辩护不能掩盖内部错误。","id":"c2-09:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which criticism should the filmmakers accept?","options":["Adding every national policymaker.","Abandoning local observation.","Checking the included explanation of a delay."],"answer":"Checking the included explanation of a delay.","explanation":"既有内容的事实含义需要核查。","id":"c2-09:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What distinction makes the review useful?","options":["All omissions versus all facts.","Different project demands versus relevant objections.","Ministers versus workers as people."],"answer":"Different project demands versus relevant objections.","explanation":"逐项评价相关性。","id":"c2-09:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is the shopping list error serious?","options":["It obstructs the intended beginner task.","Advanced cooks dislike lists.","The booklet promised professional training."],"answer":"It obstructs the intended beginner task.","explanation":"错误违背具体用途。","id":"c2-09:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why avoid a wholly unfair verdict?","options":["Every criticism used the same standard.","The advanced techniques were essential.","It would discard relevant corrections with irrelevant demands."],"answer":"It would discard relevant corrections with irrelevant demands.","explanation":"混合评价保留有用反馈。","id":"c2-09:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a proportionate exhibition review distinguishing useful context from a demand for another project.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Judge an achievement against a defensible purpose without exempting it from criticism.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Review A Map That Is Not an Atlas for potential users. Distinguish legitimate scope limits from misleading information within the chosen purpose. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on A Map That Is Not an Atlas. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Which omission is most relevant?","Why is no map contains everything inadequate?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["reviewer","creator defending a narrow purpose"],"goal":"Agree which criticism is relevant without exempting the work from scrutiny.","twist":"The strongest objection is hidden among several unfair demands."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving judge an achievement against a defensible purpose without exempting it from criticism. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-10","level":"C2","title":"文学中的不可靠叙述","chapter":74,"focus":"Distinguish textual support from an attractive reconstruction of an unreliable account.","vocab":[{"head":"retrospect","zh":"回顾","pos":"noun","example":"In retrospect, Daniel described his last visit to the house as an errand, although his careful preparation suggested that more was at stake.","exampleZh":"回顾时，丹尼尔把最后一次拜访说成跑腿，但他精心准备的样子暗示事情并非如此简单。","collocation":"in retrospect","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v1","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pretext","zh":"借口","pos":"noun","example":"Returning a borrowed atlas provided a convenient pretext for approaching a door he had avoided since the previous winter.","exampleZh":"归还借来的地图集为他接近那扇自去年冬天起一直回避的门提供了方便的借口。","collocation":"a convenient pretext","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v2","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withheld","zh":"隐而不说","pos":"verb (past)","example":"He withheld any explanation of the winter quarrel, assuring the reader that its details would contribute nothing to an understanding of the visit.","exampleZh":"他没有解释冬天的争吵，并向读者保证那些细节无助于理解这次拜访。","collocation":"withhold an explanation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v3","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conspicuous","zh":"醒目的","pos":"adjective","example":"That conspicuous omission mattered because the narrator otherwise remembered the colour of the envelope and the precise position of every chair.","exampleZh":"这一醒目的省略之所以重要，是因为叙述者对信封颜色及每张椅子的确切位置都记得清楚。","collocation":"a conspicuous omission","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v4","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivocal","zh":"有歧义的","pos":"adjective","example":"His sister answered with an equivocal gesture, moving aside without either inviting him in or asking him to remain outside.","exampleZh":"姐姐作出了含义不明的动作，向旁边让开，却既未请他进去，也未叫他留在外面。","collocation":"an equivocal gesture","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v5","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归因","pos":"verb","example":"Daniel attributed significance to her silence immediately, presenting it as proof that she had expected him to return with an apology.","exampleZh":"丹尼尔立即赋予她的沉默特殊意义，将其视为她期待自己回来道歉的证明。","collocation":"attribute significance to","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v6","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterpart","zh":"对应物","pos":"noun","example":"Her actual words supplied no verbal counterpart to that interpretation; she merely asked whether the atlas still contained the loose coastal map.","exampleZh":"她实际说的话并未印证这种解读；她只是问地图集里是否还夹着那张散页海岸地图。","collocation":"a verbal counterpart","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v7","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reassurance","zh":"安慰","pos":"noun","example":"He seemed to seek reassurance in the familiar objects, describing a repaired cup at greater length than the person holding it.","exampleZh":"他似乎在熟悉的物件中寻求安慰，对修补过的杯子的描写比对拿杯子的人更详尽。","collocation":"seek reassurance","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v8","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"focalisation","zh":"叙事聚焦","pos":"noun","example":"The restricted focalisation gives access to his expectations while leaving his sister's thoughts beyond the reader's direct knowledge throughout the encounter.","exampleZh":"受限制的叙事聚焦让读者了解他的期待，却始终无法直接得知姐姐的想法。","collocation":"restricted focalisation","register":"specialist","id":"c2-10:v9","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"discrepancy","zh":"不一致","pos":"noun","example":"A revealing discrepancy appears when he says the conversation was effortless and then reports three unsuccessful attempts to change its subject.","exampleZh":"一个意味深长的不一致出现在他声称交谈轻松自如、却又报告三次转移话题均未成功之时。","collocation":"a revealing discrepancy","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v10","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deflection","zh":"转移话题","pos":"noun","example":"Those attempts may be deliberate deflection, but the text does not identify the question he finds most difficult to answer honestly.","exampleZh":"这些尝试可能是有意回避，但文本并未指出哪个问题最令他难以坦诚回答。","collocation":"a deliberate deflection","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v11","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tentative","zh":"试探性的","pos":"adjective","example":"A tentative reading would connect his attention to objects with his reluctance to acknowledge how the relationship has changed in his absence.","exampleZh":"一种试探性解读会把他对物件的关注与不愿承认离开后关系变化的态度联系起来。","collocation":"a tentative reading","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v12","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"plausibility","zh":"合理性","pos":"noun","example":"To test the plausibility of that reading, we must examine the final exchange rather than treating each earlier detail as independent confirmation.","exampleZh":"要检验这种解读的合理性，我们必须考察最后的对话，而不能把每个先前细节都当作独立印证。","collocation":"test the plausibility","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v13","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"undermine","zh":"削弱","pos":"verb","example":"His sisters offer to keep the atlas might undermine an interpretation of complete rejection, even though it does not establish reconciliation.","exampleZh":"姐姐提出留下地图集，可能削弱彻底拒绝的解读，尽管这也不能证明双方已经和解。","collocation":"undermine an interpretation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v14","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconciliation","zh":"和解","pos":"noun","example":"A claim of reconciliation therefore requires more than Daniel's closing statement that everything important had finally been returned to its proper place.","exampleZh":"因此，声称和解需要比丹尼尔结尾所说重要东西终于归位更多的依据。","collocation":"a claim of reconciliation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v15","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"restraint","zh":"克制","pos":"noun","example":"Interpretive restraint preserves the narrator's emotional complexity without turning uncertainty into permission to invent a family history the story never supplies.","exampleZh":"解读上的克制保留了叙述者情感的复杂性，却不把不确定性当成编造故事未交代的家史的许可。","collocation":"interpretive restraint","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v16","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"文学中的不可靠叙述：原创教学叙事","text":"In retrospect, Daniel described his last visit to the house as an errand, although his careful preparation suggested that more was at stake. Returning a borrowed atlas provided a convenient pretext for approaching a door he had avoided since the previous winter. He withheld any explanation of the winter quarrel, assuring the reader that its details would contribute nothing to an understanding of the visit. That conspicuous omission mattered because the narrator otherwise remembered the colour of the envelope and the precise position of every chair. His sister answered with an equivocal gesture, moving aside without either inviting him in or asking him to remain outside. Daniel attributed significance to her silence immediately, presenting it as proof that she had expected him to return with an apology. Her actual words supplied no verbal counterpart to that interpretation; she merely asked whether the atlas still contained the loose coastal map. He seemed to seek reassurance in the familiar objects, describing a repaired cup at greater length than the person holding it. The restricted focalisation gives access to his expectations while leaving his sister's thoughts beyond the reader's direct knowledge throughout the encounter. A revealing discrepancy appears when he says the conversation was effortless and then reports three unsuccessful attempts to change its subject. Those attempts may be deliberate deflection, but the text does not identify the question he finds most difficult to answer honestly. A tentative reading would connect his attention to objects with his reluctance to acknowledge how the relationship has changed in his absence. To test the plausibility of that reading, we must examine the final exchange rather than treating each earlier detail as independent confirmation. His sisters offer to keep the atlas might undermine an interpretation of complete rejection, even though it does not establish reconciliation. A claim of reconciliation therefore requires more than Daniel's closing statement that everything important had finally been returned to its proper place. Interpretive restraint preserves the narrator's emotional complexity without turning uncertainty into permission to invent a family history the story never supplies. The narrator supplies a persuasive ending without necessarily supplying reliable evidence for the settlement he wishes it to announce."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Photograph Behind the Counter","text":"Leah, the narrator of this fictional story, insists that she entered the watchmaker's shop only because it was raining. The opening paragraph makes that explanation sound ordinary: she describes a broken umbrella, a narrow pavement and the shelter offered by the shop window. Yet she also knows that the proprietor closes early on Thursdays, and she notices immediately that a photograph has disappeared from behind the counter. These details suggest prior knowledge without establishing why she has it.\n\nThe proprietor recognises her but addresses her formally. Leah reports that his formality proves he has forgotten their friendship. A reader should hesitate before accepting this conclusion. Forgetting someone and choosing a formal manner with a recognised visitor are different possibilities. The story gives us the manner and Leah's judgement, not direct access to the proprietor's memory. When he asks whether she still has the blue watch, she describes the question as irrelevant and turns to the weather.\n\nThe missing photograph becomes a focus of her attention. She imagines that it has been thrown away, then notices a clean rectangular mark on the wall and treats it as confirmation. A mark establishes that something has been removed; it does not establish where the object went. Later, the proprietor mentions repairs to the ceiling, supplying a possible practical explanation that Leah does not discuss. This alternative does not settle the matter either. The timing of the repairs remains unclear, and practical explanations can coexist with emotional decisions.\n\nAt the end, he offers to mend her umbrella while she waits. Leah declines, saying that the rain has stopped, although the final image shows water running from the awning. One reading is that she wishes to leave before another personal question arises. Another is that she refuses an act of care she no longer feels entitled to accept. Both explain more than a simple claim that she came only for shelter. Neither permits a confident reconstruction of an unnamed betrayal. The most defensible interpretation identifies the gap between her declared purpose and her behaviour, then leaves the unresolved history unresolved. Her evasions reveal pressure; they do not provide a complete account of its cause."},"listening":{"teaching":"At our reading group, someone argued that the station master in the story must be the speaker's father. The argument was appealing because both figures were associated with a silver whistle. But the whistle appears only twice, and nobody in the text states a family relationship. I asked whether the connection could be symbolic rather than genealogical. That suggestion did not prove the original reading wrong; it exposed an assumption that needed support.\n\nWe returned to the paragraph where the speaker remembers waiting beside an empty ticket window. One member read the waiting as evidence of abandonment. Another noticed that the speaker never says how long the station master has been away. A short absence and a permanent departure would create different histories, and the story leaves the duration unclear. We could still discuss the emotional effect of waiting without choosing one history as fact.\n\nThe strongest contribution came from a reader who initially supported the father interpretation. She identified a later sentence in which the whistle is described as belonging to the station rather than to a person. She revised her position without dismissing the earlier connection. The image might link the figures, she said, while leaving the kind of connection open.\n\nThat is the distinction I want us to practise. An interpretation can be worth considering before it is established. The task is to show which details it explains, what it assumes and what competing reading remains possible. If a new detail weakens our account, acknowledging it improves the discussion rather than ending it.","test":"The passage we discussed yesterday describes a woman cleaning an empty classroom before returning its keys. Several readers concluded that she had been dismissed unfairly. They pointed to her repeated polishing of a desk nobody would use that evening. That is a possible account, but the passage never identifies her job or the reason she is leaving. She might be a teacher, a caretaker or someone helping to close the building for another purpose.\n\nThe empty room carries emotional weight regardless of that missing information. The woman checks each window, removes a drawing from the wall and places it carefully between two books. These actions suggest attachment. They do not establish whether the departure is unwanted. One reader noticed that the woman smiles when the last light goes out. Another observed that the smile is described only as brief, leaving its meaning open.\n\nOur disagreement became more useful when we stopped trying to name a hidden administrative event. We compared the readings of loss and completion instead. Both could explain careful cleaning, but they placed different weight on the smile and the rescued drawing. The final sentence, in which she leaves the keys on the outside of the door, complicated both accounts because it suggested someone else would arrive soon.\n\nI would therefore report attachment and transition as strongly supported, while treating dismissal as an unconfirmed reconstruction. That judgement could change if another passage supplied the missing context. For now, the absence of that context is part of the reading problem, not an invitation to fill it with the most dramatic available story."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What most clearly challenges Daniel's description of an effortless conversation?","options":["His repeated unsuccessful changes of subject.","His memory of furniture placement.","His return of the coastal map."],"answer":"His repeated unsuccessful changes of subject.","explanation":"叙述者的概括与具体行为不一致；改话题失败直接削弱轻松交谈的说法。","id":"c2-10:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the offer to keep the atlas establish?","options":["Proof that the quarrel is resolved.","A detail that complicates complete rejection.","Confirmation that the sister expected an apology."],"answer":"A detail that complicates complete rejection.","explanation":"留下地图集削弱彻底拒绝，却不足以证明和解或读懂姐姐的期待。","id":"c2-10:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is the clean mark insufficient to establish the photographs fate?","options":["The repairs prove it was stored safely.","The narrator cannot see the wall clearly.","It indicates removal without establishing a destination."],"answer":"It indicates removal without establishing a destination.","explanation":"痕迹支持移走，不支持丢弃；修缮也只是另一种可能。","id":"c2-10:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which conclusion is most defensible about Leah?","options":["Her account understates the personal pressure of the visit.","She does not recognise the proprietor.","She knows the photograph has been destroyed."],"answer":"Her account understates the personal pressure of the visit.","explanation":"行为与自称避雨之间存在差距，但缺少完整历史。","id":"c2-10:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"How does the later whistle description affect the discussion?","options":["It establishes the station master as the father.","It weakens a particular family inference without removing the connection.","It makes the earlier image irrelevant."],"answer":"It weakens a particular family inference without removing the connection.","explanation":"物件属于车站使家族推断减弱，但意象联系仍可保留。","id":"c2-10:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What does the speaker recommend doing with a plausible reading?","options":["Accept it until another reader disproves every element.","Avoid it whenever the author leaves anything unstated.","Test its assumptions against details and alternatives."],"answer":"Test its assumptions against details and alternatives.","explanation":"合理解读仍应检查假设、反证与竞争解释。","id":"c2-10:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the speakers reservation about the dismissal interpretation?","options":["The text leaves both the womans role and departure reason unspecified.","The smile proves she welcomed dismissal.","The drawing confirms that she was the class teacher."],"answer":"The text leaves both the womans role and departure reason unspecified.","explanation":"离职身份和原因均未知，不能由情感物件推出行政事件。","id":"c2-10:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why does the final key detail complicate the discussion?","options":["It proves that the school has permanently closed.","It introduces evidence of continuing use beyond her departure.","It explains why she was dismissed."],"answer":"It introduces evidence of continuing use beyond her departure.","explanation":"钥匙留外侧暗示有人将到来，但不确定离开的原因。","id":"c2-10:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a critical reading of Daniel's visit, weighing reconciliation against continued distance. Use textual details and acknowledge the strongest alternative.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Distinguish textual support from an attractive reconstruction of an unreliable account.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a literary commentary on Leah's account in The Photograph Behind the Counter. Explain what the missing photograph can and cannot establish. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Photograph Behind the Counter. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why is the clean mark insufficient to establish the photographs fate?","Which conclusion is most defensible about Leah?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["literary critic","reader defending a different interpretation"],"goal":"Agree on two supported claims and one unresolved question without inventing a backstory.","twist":"A newly discovered passage appears to challenge your preferred reading; decide what would need revising."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving distinguish textual support from an attractive reconstruction of an unreliable account. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-11","level":"C2","title":"因果识别与选择偏差","chapter":75,"focus":"Separate a credible mechanism from evidence that identifies a causal effect.","vocab":[{"head":"association","zh":"关联","pos":"noun","example":"Higher marks among room users establish an association rather than a causal effect.","exampleZh":"使用自习室者分数更高，表明关联而非因果效应。","collocation":"an observed association","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v1","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Prior attendance","zh":"先前出勤情况","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Prior attendance may influence both room use and examination performance.","exampleZh":"先前的出勤情况可能同时影响自习室使用和考试表现。","collocation":"prior attendance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v2","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selection bias","zh":"选择偏差","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The proposed mechanism is credible but does not eliminate selection bias.","exampleZh":"提出的机制可信，却不能消除选择偏差。","collocation":"selection bias","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v3","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"comparison","zh":"比较","pos":"noun","example":"We need a comparison that addresses differences already present before the change.","exampleZh":"我们需要一种能够处理变化前既有差异的比较。","collocation":"a fair comparison","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v4","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible with","zh":"与之相容","pos":"adjectival phrase","example":"The result is compatible with several explanations the design cannot separate.","exampleZh":"这个结果与研究设计无法区分的多种解释相容。","collocation":"compatible with alternatives","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v5","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"narrow the conclusion","zh":"缩小结论范围","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I would narrow the conclusion instead of dismissing the observation entirely.","exampleZh":"我会缩小结论范围，而不是完全否定观察结果。","collocation":"narrow the conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v6","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"precise estimate","zh":"精确估计","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Even a precise estimate can answer the wrong causal question.","exampleZh":"即使估计很精确，也可能回答了错误的因果问题。","collocation":"a precise estimate","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v7","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inferred mechanisms","zh":"推断的机制","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The report should distinguish measured outcomes from inferred mechanisms.","exampleZh":"报告应区分测量到的结果和推断的机制。","collocation":"inferred mechanisms","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-11:v8","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"less plausible","zh":"较不可信的","pos":"adjectival phrase","example":"What evidence would make the selection explanation less plausible?","exampleZh":"什么证据会让选择因素的解释不那么可信？","collocation":"less plausible explanations","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v9","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"前提是","pos":"conjunction","example":"Comparing changes may help, provided the groups had credible earlier trends.","exampleZh":"比较变化可能有帮助，前提是各组先前趋势可信。","collocation":"provided the condition holds","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v10","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identification","zh":"因果识别","pos":"noun","example":"The limitation concerns identification, not merely the size of the sample.","exampleZh":"这个局限关乎因果识别，而不只是样本大小。","collocation":"causal identification","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-11:v11","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"descriptive finding","zh":"描述性发现","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Uncertainty about causation does not make the descriptive finding worthless.","exampleZh":"因果关系不确定，并不使描述性发现失去价值。","collocation":"a descriptive finding","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v12","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterfactual","zh":"反事实","pos":"noun","example":"The causal question requires a credible counterfactual.","exampleZh":"因果问题需要可信的反事实比较。","collocation":"a credible counterfactual","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v13","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selection","zh":"选择过程","pos":"noun","example":"Selection into the programme may reflect prior motivation.","exampleZh":"加入项目的选择可能反映先前动机。","collocation":"selection into a programme","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v14","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mechanism","zh":"作用机制","pos":"noun","example":"A plausible mechanism does not identify its independent effect.","exampleZh":"合理机制不能识别其独立效应。","collocation":"a plausible mechanism","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v15","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attrition","zh":"样本流失","pos":"noun","example":"Uneven attrition weakened the final comparison.","exampleZh":"不均衡的样本流失削弱了最终比较。","collocation":"uneven attrition","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v16","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a fictional study of library study rooms：扩展论证","text":"At a fictional study of library study rooms, participants considered whether to evaluate whether booking rooms improved examination results. The background was specific: students who reserved quiet rooms earned higher marks, but the report also noted that frequent users had stronger attendance before the rooms opened. However, the association could reflect prior motivation, and a plausible concentration mechanism did not remove that alternative explanation. The proposed response was to compare changes over time and seek a design that addresses selection rather than calling correlation proof.\n\nThe room users may genuinely benefit from a quiet environment, and that possibility deserves investigation. The problem is not that a concentration mechanism is absurd; it is that the observed comparison cannot isolate it. Stronger prior attendance suggests that users already differed from non users in a way relevant to examination marks. Collecting more detail about those differences could help, but it would not automatically make every adjustment credible. The evaluation should specify the causal question and explain why its comparison addresses selection. Meanwhile, the descriptive finding can still guide a modest decision about demand for rooms. It should not be advertised as a proven increase in grades caused by the booking system. Higher marks among room users establish an association rather than a causal effect. Prior attendance may influence both room use and examination performance. The proposed mechanism is credible but does not eliminate selection bias. We need a comparison that addresses differences already present before the change. The result is compatible with several explanations the design cannot separate. I would narrow the conclusion instead of dismissing the observation entirely.\n\nThe study team considers adding interviews in which room users describe improved concentration. These accounts would help explain experience and might suggest a mechanism. They would not remove the earlier difference in attendance between users and non users. The team should therefore decide whether its next question is how people use the rooms or how much the rooms independently change marks. Both questions matter, but they require different evidence. A more ambitious causal evaluation also raises practical issues: access cannot simply be withheld from students without considering fairness. The final report proposes a transparent allocation procedure for an oversubscribed period, with appropriate consent and review, rather than presenting a convenient comparison as though it had already answered the harder question."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Mentoring Badge","text":"A fictional apprenticeship programme introduces a digital badge for learners who attend optional mentoring sessions. At the end of the year, badge holders are more likely to secure permanent jobs than other apprentices. The organisers announce that the badge has improved employment prospects and ask employers to recognise it as evidence of readiness for work.\n\nThe observed difference is genuine within the records, but its interpretation is uncertain. Attendance is optional. Learners with predictable schedules may find it easier to attend, and those schedules may also allow them to complete placements more consistently. Supervisors can recommend learners for sessions, and the recommendations may reflect expectations about future performance. Neither difference is captured by the badge itself.\n\nThe organisers respond that mentors teach useful interview techniques. This is a credible account of how mentoring might help, but it does not establish how much of the employment gap it caused. A mechanism and a causal estimate answer different questions. Interviews with successful participants may describe valuable support while leaving the comparison problem unresolved. Increasing the number of those interviews would deepen one kind of evidence without automatically producing the other.\n\nThere is a further complication. Several apprentices who left the programme do not appear in the final survey. If departure was related to difficulties that also affected attendance, the retained sample may be especially unrepresentative. The evaluators need to examine who is missing and why, rather than report a precise percentage as though completeness were assured.\n\nNone of these limitations proves that mentoring is ineffective. The responsible conclusion is narrower: badge holders in the available records had better employment outcomes, while the programme's independent contribution remains uncertain. The organisers could investigate allocation when demand exceeds capacity, collect relevant baseline information and seek an ethically reviewed comparison. They should also separate a badge documenting participation from a claim of demonstrated job readiness. Employers may value the former, but should know exactly what it records. The evaluation becomes stronger when it makes the unanswered question visible, not when it converts uncertainty into either an advertising guarantee or a declaration that the programme has no value."},"listening":{"teaching":"The garden project asked whether its weekly sessions reduced loneliness. Participants completed a questionnaire before joining and again after six weeks. The average score improved, and the team wanted to announce that gardening had caused the improvement. I advised a more limited statement.\n\nDuring the same period, participants met a regular group, received transport help and had access to a community lunch. Gardening might matter, but the design could not separate it from those other features. There was also no comparison with people whose scores might have changed over six weeks without the project. These are limitations of the causal question, not reasons to dismiss the participants' accounts.\n\nThe project leader suggested asking everyone whether the garden had helped. That would be worthwhile if we wanted to understand perceived benefits. It would not create a missing counterfactual. A person can sincerely credit one activity for a change that had several causes. We therefore planned interviews and a separate discussion about a stronger evaluation, rather than pretending that one method answered both questions.\n\nThe public summary now says that participants reported less loneliness after six weeks in the programme. It describes the transport and lunch as part of what was offered. It also notes that the design cannot isolate the contribution of gardening. This wording is less dramatic, but it lets supporters understand what the project has established. The next decision is whether the whole package is worth continuing while better evidence is collected, not whether a single questionnaire has proved a universal therapeutic effect.","test":"A company tested a new flexible schedule with volunteers from one department. Their absence rate fell over three months, and managers proposed extending the policy because flexibility had apparently improved health. The volunteers were enthusiastic, and several described better sleep. Those accounts were relevant, but they did not settle the interpretation of the absence data.\n\nThe department had also changed supervisors and completed a demanding seasonal project. Either change might affect absence. Volunteers could differ from colleagues who preferred their existing hours. In addition, the trial began immediately after an unusually high absence month, making some decline possible even without a new policy. The report needed to consider these alternatives rather than use a plausible health story as a substitute for comparison.\n\nI did not recommend abandoning flexibility. The trial showed that it could operate in that department and that some staff valued it. Those are useful findings for a practical decision. They are narrower than a demonstrated reduction in illness, especially because absence records do not directly measure health.\n\nThe company agreed to describe feasibility and reported experience separately from causal impact. A further rollout would collect comparable baseline data, record other workplace changes and define the outcome before examining results. Staff consultation would also address fairness in access to the policy. The revised plan may eventually support a stronger conclusion. For now, it preserves what the trial taught without claiming that every observed improvement arose from the schedule alone or that the same result is guaranteed elsewhere."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What would concentration interviews add?","options":["Removal of prior attendance differences.","Evidence about experience and possible mechanisms.","A direct estimate of the rooms independent effect."],"answer":"Evidence about experience and possible mechanisms.","explanation":"机制与体验信息不能自动解决选择问题。","id":"c2-11:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why consider fairness in allocation?","options":["A stronger design also has ethical and practical conditions.","Fairness makes causal comparisons unnecessary.","Existing differences prove the rooms ineffective."],"answer":"A stronger design also has ethical and practical conditions.","explanation":"方法改进并不免除伦理责任。","id":"c2-11:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is interview training not enough to explain the employment gap?","options":["Interview skills cannot affect employment.","Badge holders did not attend mentoring.","A plausible mechanism does not isolate its causal contribution."],"answer":"A plausible mechanism does not isolate its causal contribution.","explanation":"可能机制与独立效应是不同问题。","id":"c2-11:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does missing departure data threaten?","options":["The existence of the badge.","The representativeness of the retained comparison.","The sincerity of every participant."],"answer":"The representativeness of the retained comparison.","explanation":"样本流失可能与结果及参与有关。","id":"c2-11:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why would asking whether gardening helped not settle causation?","options":["Perceived benefit does not supply a counterfactual.","Participants cannot describe their experiences.","Lunch attendance was already randomised."],"answer":"Perceived benefit does not supply a counterfactual.","explanation":"主观归因有价值但不是反事实比较。","id":"c2-11:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can the current summary responsibly report?","options":["A universal therapeutic effect.","No evidence relevant to continuing the package.","A change after participation without isolating gardening."],"answer":"A change after participation without isolating gardening.","explanation":"保留时间上的变化与干预成分，不夸因果。","id":"c2-11:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why does the unusual starting month matter?","options":["It proves the schedule damaged health.","Some decline might occur without the schedule change.","It removes the supervisor change from consideration."],"answer":"Some decline might occur without the schedule change.","explanation":"起点异常使自然回落成为竞争解释。","id":"c2-11:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which narrower finding remains useful?","options":["Feasibility and reported value in the tested department.","A guaranteed health effect elsewhere.","A complete causal explanation of absence."],"answer":"Feasibility and reported value in the tested department.","explanation":"可行性与体验不同于因果健康效应。","id":"c2-11:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an evaluation plan separating descriptive room use findings from a causal claim about marks.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Separate a credible mechanism from evidence that identifies a causal effect.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write an independent review of The Mentoring Badge for employers. Explain what the badge records, what remains uncertain and what stronger evaluation would require. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Mentoring Badge. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why is interview training not enough to explain the employment gap?","What does missing departure data threaten?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["programme evaluator","programme organiser"],"goal":"Agree wording that preserves useful findings without claiming an identified effect.","twist":"A funder demands a single causal number before the comparison has been designed."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving separate a credible mechanism from evidence that identifies a causal effect. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-12","level":"C2","title":"跨来源综合与不一致","chapter":76,"focus":"Integrate evidence answering different questions without manufacturing either agreement or contradiction.","vocab":[{"head":"genuine findings","zh":"真实发现","pos":"noun phrase","example":"More cyclists and less comfortable corners can both be genuine findings.","exampleZh":"骑车者增加和转角舒适度下降可以同时是真实发现。","collocation":"genuine findings","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v1","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradicting","zh":"与之矛盾","pos":"verb","example":"The reports address different outcomes rather than necessarily contradicting each other.","exampleZh":"这些报告讨论不同结果，未必互相矛盾。","collocation":"contradicting another source","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v2","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Aggregating","zh":"合并","pos":"gerund","example":"Aggregating the results would conceal which users encountered the difficulty.","exampleZh":"把结果合并会掩盖哪些使用者遇到了困难。","collocation":"aggregating results","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v3","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"averaging it away","zh":"通过取平均抹去","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We should preserve the disagreement about priorities instead of averaging it away.","exampleZh":"我们应保留优先事项的分歧，而非通过取平均将其抹去。","collocation":"averaging it away","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v4","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"捕捉","pos":"verb","example":"The interviews explain experiences that the traffic counts cannot capture.","exampleZh":"访谈解释了交通计数无法捕捉的体验。","collocation":"capture experiences","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v5","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"universal accessibility","zh":"普遍可达性","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Neither source alone supports a conclusion about universal accessibility.","exampleZh":"任何一个来源都不能单独支持普遍可达性的结论。","collocation":"universal accessibility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v6","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"converges","zh":"汇合一致","pos":"verb","example":"The synthesis needs to identify where the evidence converges and where it diverges.","exampleZh":"综合分析需指出证据在哪里一致、在哪里分歧。","collocation":"evidence converges","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-12:v7","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"targeted","zh":"有针对性的","pos":"adjective","example":"A targeted corner redesign may retain the benefit while addressing the barrier.","exampleZh":"有针对性的转角改造可能保留收益并解决障碍。","collocation":"a targeted redesign","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v8","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"observation periods","zh":"观察时段","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Before combining the figures, check whether their observation periods match.","exampleZh":"合并数字之前，要检查观察时段是否一致。","collocation":"observation periods","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v9","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"relevant","zh":"相关的","pos":"adjective","example":"The source with more numbers is not automatically the more relevant one.","exampleZh":"数字更多的来源并不自动更相关。","collocation":"a relevant source","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v10","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"harmonised","zh":"统一","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"An unresolved difference should be reported rather than silently harmonised.","exampleZh":"尚未解决的差异应被报告，而非悄悄统一。","collocation":"harmonised differences","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v11","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"combined evidence","zh":"综合证据","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The recommendation follows from the combined evidence, not from one preferred headline.","exampleZh":"建议来自综合证据，而非某个偏爱的标题。","collocation":"combined evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v12","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"convergence","zh":"趋同","pos":"noun","example":"The sources show limited convergence on the access problem.","exampleZh":"来源在通行问题上有限趋同。","collocation":"limited convergence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v13","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"divergence","zh":"分歧","pos":"noun","example":"The divergence concerns priorities rather than the traffic count.","exampleZh":"分歧关乎优先项而非交通计数。","collocation":"a divergence of priorities","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v14","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commensurable","zh":"可共同衡量的","pos":"adjective","example":"The measures are not directly commensurable.","exampleZh":"这些测量不能直接共同量化。","collocation":"commensurable measures","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v15","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"triangulation","zh":"多来源核对","pos":"noun","example":"Triangulation can expose questions hidden by one method.","exampleZh":"多来源核对能揭示单一方法隐藏的问题。","collocation":"methodological triangulation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v16","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：two fictional reports about an urban river path：扩展论证","text":"At two fictional reports about an urban river path, participants considered whether to combine access benefits with observations of uneven use. The background was specific: a transport survey counted more weekday cyclists after resurfacing, while interviews with older pedestrians described difficulty at two shared corners. However, the counts and interviews addressed different questions, so treating one as a refutation of the other discarded useful evidence. The proposed response was to retain the overall increase and specify where design changes might improve access for particular users.\n\nThe river path is not experienced by an average user who simultaneously rides a bicycle and walks cautiously around every corner. Different people can gain and lose from the same design. The two reports should therefore be combined in a way that preserves their units of observation. Counting cyclists answers one question; listening to older pedestrians answers another. The authority could inspect the identified corners, test clearer separation or reduced speeds, and monitor whether the improvement creates new barriers elsewhere. It need not reject resurfacing to acknowledge a design problem. Equally, a positive traffic count cannot settle the question of access for everyone. The recommendation should state whose experience each proposed measure is intended to improve. More cyclists and less comfortable corners can both be genuine findings. The reports address different outcomes rather than necessarily contradicting each other. Aggregating the results would conceal which users encountered the difficulty. We should preserve the disagreement about priorities instead of averaging it away. The interviews explain experiences that the traffic counts cannot capture. Neither source alone supports a conclusion about universal accessibility. The synthesis needs to identify where the evidence converges and where it diverges.\n\nA councillor proposes combining the survey and interviews into one overall satisfaction score. The analysts object that the materials were not designed for that calculation. One counts movement; the other explores experiences at particular corners. Their value lies partly in the difference. The final report can place the findings together and explain how they inform a decision without pretending that they share a common numerical scale. It should also identify whose experiences remain missing. Additional observation at busy times may help connect the sources, but a desire for a simple headline should not determine what the evidence is allowed to mean. Synthesis is an argument about relationships, not automatic averaging."},"sourceB":{"title":"A Successful Booking System","text":"A fictional sports centre replaces telephone bookings with an online system. Administrative records show fewer double bookings and less staff time spent entering requests. A survey of recent online users reports high satisfaction. The centre announces that access has improved for the whole community. A separate set of interviews raises a more complicated picture.\n\nSeveral older residents say they stopped trying to book because they lacked suitable devices or found the payment process difficult. Their accounts do not refute the reduction in double bookings. They concern people whose unsuccessful attempts may not appear in the completed booking records or the survey of recent users. The two sources can therefore be true while addressing different populations and outcomes.\n\nThe manager suggests averaging the positive survey with the negative interviews. This would create an apparently balanced number without a defensible common scale. The survey estimates views among a specified group of users. The interviews identify barriers and explain experiences, but are not a representative count of how many residents face them. Neither should be forced into a numerical role it was not designed to perform.\n\nA more useful synthesis distinguishes operational efficiency, satisfaction among users and barriers to participation. It asks whether a telephone support option could preserve the efficiency gains while helping residents who cannot complete online bookings. It also seeks information about failed attempts and people who no longer approach the centre. These questions arise from the relationship between sources rather than from choosing which source to believe.\n\nThe final report retains the genuine administrative improvement but narrows the claim about community access. It recommends testing support arrangements and measuring participation across groups. It does not assume that every older resident needs help or that every online user is satisfied. Those generalisations would add stereotypes to an evidence problem. The strongest conclusion is conditional and differentiated: a system can work better for its successful users while becoming harder to enter for others. A public claim of universal improvement requires attention to both, including the people made less visible by the new process itself.\n\nThe next evaluation must therefore look beyond those who successfully complete the process."},"listening":{"teaching":"The community kitchen received two reports about its new menu. Waste records showed fewer untouched portions. Interviews with several families suggested that some children were bringing food from home because they disliked the new choices. At first the reports were presented as opposing verdicts: success from the kitchen and failure from the families.\n\nI thought that framing was premature. Less waste might reflect better acceptance, smaller portions or fewer children taking the meal. The records alone could not distinguish those explanations. The interviews made one possible explanation visible, but did not tell us how common it was across the school.\n\nWe needed to keep both contributions in their proper roles. Counting meal participation alongside waste would connect the operational result with who was eating. Further discussion could explain why children chose differently, including price, preference and dietary needs. No single overall score would preserve all those distinctions.\n\nThe revised recommendation was to continue monitoring waste while reviewing participation and choice. It acknowledged the kitchen's practical gain without calling it proof that every child preferred the menu. It also avoided treating a small set of interviews as a complete survey. Combining sources means explaining how their questions, populations and methods relate. Sometimes the result is a stronger shared conclusion. Sometimes it is a more precise statement of what remains unresolved. In this case, the apparent contradiction became a research question about the people missing from the meal counts, rather than a contest over which report should be discarded.","test":"A museum reported record attendance after introducing free evening entry. Visitor feedback was enthusiastic, but staff interviews described crowding and repeated difficulty closing the building on time. The board asked whether the evening programme had succeeded. That question needed more than a choice between the two accounts.\n\nAttendance measured how many visits occurred, not the quality of every visit or the burden of delivering it. Feedback from people who completed a questionnaire could reveal satisfaction, but might omit those who left early or could not enter a crowded room. Staff accounts identified operational pressure, although they did not establish that every visitor had a poor experience.\n\nWe organised the findings around access, experience and delivery. The programme appeared to attract people, and some clearly valued it. At the same time, closing arrangements and room capacity required attention. A trial of timed entry might address crowding without removing free access, but its effect on people arriving spontaneously would need checking.\n\nThe report therefore recommended a limited adjustment with further observation rather than an unqualified success label or immediate cancellation. It also named the evidence missing from the current picture: unsuccessful arrivals, early departures and staff time beyond closing. The sources were not interchangeable votes on one simple proposition. They illuminated different consequences of the same policy. Preserving those differences allowed the board to ask a better question: how could the valued access be maintained without relying on operational conditions that staff could not sustain indefinitely?"},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why reject one satisfaction score?","options":["Interview experiences are irrelevant.","The materials do not share a defensible measurement scale.","Counts always outrank narratives."],"answer":"The materials do not share a defensible measurement scale.","explanation":"方法与单位不同，不能强行平均。","id":"c2-12:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is synthesis here?","options":["An argument about relationships among findings.","Automatic numerical averaging.","Selecting the easiest headline."],"answer":"An argument about relationships among findings.","explanation":"综合需解释来源关系。","id":"c2-12:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Who may be invisible in the survey?","options":["All successful online users.","Only staff entering requests.","People who failed to become recent users."],"answer":"People who failed to become recent users.","explanation":"成功用户抽样遗漏进入障碍。","id":"c2-12:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does fewer double bookings establish?","options":["Improvement for every resident.","An operational gain, not universal access.","That telephone support is unnecessary."],"answer":"An operational gain, not universal access.","explanation":"结果范围不能外推。","id":"c2-12:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What question replaces the apparent conflict?","options":["Who is taking meals and why participation changes.","Which source should be suppressed.","Whether interviews can ever help."],"answer":"Who is taking meals and why participation changes.","explanation":"将方法差异转为可研究问题。","id":"c2-12:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why keep interviews distinct?","options":["They are a complete population count.","They prove all children dislike the menu.","They explain barriers without estimating prevalence."],"answer":"They explain barriers without estimating prevalence.","explanation":"解释与普遍比例不同。","id":"c2-12:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is attendance alone insufficient?","options":["It measures no visits.","It omits experience and delivery burden.","It proves operational sustainability."],"answer":"It omits experience and delivery burden.","explanation":"多种后果需分别考量。","id":"c2-12:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the recommendation trying to preserve?","options":["Valued access while testing sustainable delivery.","Every original arrangement unchanged.","A single total success score."],"answer":"Valued access while testing sustainable delivery.","explanation":"改进而非简单二选一。","id":"c2-12:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a synthesis of the river path sources without inventing a common numerical satisfaction scale.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Integrate evidence answering different questions without manufacturing either agreement or contradiction.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a decision brief on A Successful Booking System. Preserve efficiency findings while accounting for people absent from completed booking records. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on A Successful Booking System. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Who may be invisible in the survey?","What does fewer double bookings establish?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["analyst","service manager"],"goal":"Combine the sources according to their methods and populations, then name missing evidence.","twist":"A board member insists on averaging interviews and a survey into one score."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving integrate evidence answering different questions without manufacturing either agreement or contradiction. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-13","level":"C2","title":"高压追问与伦理边界","chapter":77,"focus":"Answer directly under pressure while refusing precision or guarantees the evidence cannot support.","vocab":[{"head":"guarantee","zh":"保证","pos":"verb","example":"No, we cannot guarantee that an accident will never happen.","exampleZh":"不能，我们无法保证事故永远不会发生。","collocation":"guarantee zero risk","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v1","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"risks are reduced","zh":"风险得到降低","pos":"verb clause","example":"What we can explain is how risks are reduced and who can stop the activity.","exampleZh":"我们能解释的是如何降低风险，以及谁有权停止活动。","collocation":"risks are reduced","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v2","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct answer","zh":"直接回答","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The question deserves a direct answer before a list of procedures.","exampleZh":"在列举程序之前，这个问题值得一个直接回答。","collocation":"a direct answer","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v3","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"precise figure","zh":"精确数字","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I do not have evidence for that precise figure, so I will not invent one.","exampleZh":"我没有证据支持那个精确数字，因此不会编造。","collocation":"a precise figure","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v4","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"measured","zh":"测量了","pos":"verb (past)","example":"Let me distinguish the risk we have measured from the risk we have not.","exampleZh":"让我区分已测量的风险和未测量的风险。","collocation":"measured risk","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v5","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair challenge","zh":"合理质疑","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That is a fair challenge to the conclusion, although not to every observation.","exampleZh":"这是对结论的合理质疑，尽管并非针对每一项观察。","collocation":"a fair challenge","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v6","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inspection record","zh":"检查记录","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would need to check the inspection record before answering that detail.","exampleZh":"在回答那个细节之前，我需要核查检查记录。","collocation":"an inspection record","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v7","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exposure","zh":"暴露风险","pos":"noun","example":"The control reduces exposure without eliminating every possibility of harm.","exampleZh":"这项控制降低了暴露风险，却没有消除一切伤害可能。","collocation":"reduce exposure","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v8","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proceed","zh":"继续进行","pos":"verb","example":"If the equipment fails that check, the session will not proceed.","exampleZh":"如果设备未通过那项检查，活动将不再继续。","collocation":"the session will proceed","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v9","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frequency","zh":"频率","pos":"noun","example":"My uncertainty concerns the frequency, not the existence of the hazard.","exampleZh":"我不确定的是发生频率，而不是危险是否存在。","collocation":"the frequency of harm","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v10","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision rule","zh":"决策规则","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We should publish the decision rule rather than merely ask for trust.","exampleZh":"我们应公布决策规则，而非仅仅要求信任。","collocation":"a decision rule","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v11","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accountability","zh":"问责","pos":"noun","example":"An honest limitation can strengthen accountability instead of weakening it.","exampleZh":"诚实说明局限可以加强问责，而非削弱它。","collocation":"strengthen accountability","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v12","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"due process","zh":"公平程序","pos":"noun","example":"Due process matters before a public attribution of blame.","exampleZh":"公开归责之前公平程序很重要。","collocation":"respect due process","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v13","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allegation","zh":"指控","pos":"noun","example":"An allegation requires a response proportionate to its evidence.","exampleZh":"回应指控应与其证据相称。","collocation":"an unverified allegation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v14","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclosure","zh":"披露","pos":"noun","example":"Disclosure should protect relevant facts without unnecessary personal detail.","exampleZh":"披露应保护相关事实而不多泄露个人细节。","collocation":"proportionate disclosure","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v15","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"premature","zh":"过早的","pos":"adjective","example":"Premature blame can obstruct an accurate investigation.","exampleZh":"过早归责可能妨碍准确调查。","collocation":"premature blame","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v16","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a public hearing on a community workshop：扩展论证","text":"At a public hearing on a community workshop, participants considered whether to respond to a demand for an absolute safety guarantee. The background was specific: a resident asked whether an accident could ever happen, and the coordinator described inspections, training and the remaining limits of supervision. However, reciting procedures without answering the guarantee question sounded evasive even though the procedures were relevant. The proposed response was to say plainly that zero risk cannot be promised, then explain the controls and the conditions for stopping an activity.\n\nA promise that no accident could ever occur would offer reassurance unsupported by any realistic supervision arrangement. The coordinator should answer that point before describing training and inspections. Otherwise, even accurate procedural information may sound like an attempt to avoid the question. The next step is to explain who checks equipment, who can stop an activity and what happens when a control fails. Residents then have criteria they can question rather than a general invitation to trust the organisers. The coordinator should also distinguish a documented hazard from an unknown frequency. Such distinctions make the remaining risk visible without presenting uncertainty as either a reason for panic or permission to ignore a preventable problem. No, we cannot guarantee that an accident will never happen. What we can explain is how risks are reduced and who can stop the activity. The question deserves a direct answer before a list of procedures. I do not have evidence for that precise figure, so I will not invent one. Let me distinguish the risk we have measured from the risk we have not.\n\nAt a later hearing, the coordinator is asked to name the volunteer responsible for a near miss. The available record shows a communication failure but does not establish which individual heard which instruction. Publicly naming someone would turn incomplete evidence into a reputational claim. The coordinator should explain the limit directly, describe the investigation and distinguish accountability for improving a system from premature blame. Privacy cannot be used to hide a documented hazard, but public concern does not make every personal allegation justified. The strongest response gives residents a clear account of what happened, what remains uncertain and when a further finding will be reported."},"sourceB":{"title":"Who Failed the Collection?","text":"After a fictional charity loses several donated items in storage, a public meeting demands an explanation. The coordinator has a partial inventory, messages about a temporary move and a report that the storeroom was left open. A speaker asks whether the coordinator can guarantee that no theft occurred. Another demands the name of the volunteer who last held the key.\n\nThe coordinator should answer the first question directly: the available evidence cannot rule out theft, but it also does not establish it. Calling the loss a theft would turn a possible explanation into an allegation. Calling it merely an administrative mistake would close the question without support. A clear statement of uncertainty is therefore part of accountability, not an evasion of it.\n\nThe request for a name raises a related but distinct issue. Knowing who held a key may be relevant to an investigation, yet it does not show who moved the items or whether the storeroom was subsequently accessed by others. Publicly naming the volunteer could imply responsibility beyond what the record supports. The coordinator can explain the evidence being checked without distributing personal details whose principal effect would be reputational harm.\n\nSome attendees accuse this restraint of protecting the organisation. That concern deserves a practical answer. The charity should preserve records, appoint a reviewer with appropriate independence, define the questions and publish a timetable for findings. It should disclose established system failures promptly, including the incomplete inventory. Privacy must not become a blanket excuse for withholding information relevant to donors.\n\nThe final statement separates known facts, possible explanations and next actions. It records the missing items, acknowledges weaknesses in tracking and explains why individual blame remains unresolved. Donors are told how to supply further evidence and when to expect an update. Such a response may satisfy neither those seeking immediate certainty nor those seeking reassurance that nothing serious happened. Its strength lies elsewhere: it remains answerable to the evidence and creates a process capable of changing the conclusion. Accountability requires enough openness to examine failure and enough restraint not to manufacture a culprit before the relevant facts are known."},"listening":{"teaching":"At a meeting about our youth workshop, a parent asked whether we could promise that every instructor was safe. I understood the desire for reassurance, but an absolute statement about every future action would exceed what checks could establish. I said that directly before describing our safeguards.\n\nWe verify required credentials, supervise activities, provide a route for concerns and review incidents. These procedures reduce risk and make concerns actionable. They are not a logical guarantee that no problem can occur. If I had listed them without answering the promise question, the response might reasonably have sounded evasive.\n\nA second question concerned a rumour about a former volunteer. We had no verified information linking that person with an incident. I explained that we could receive relevant evidence privately and investigate, but would not repeat an unverified personal allegation at the meeting. That was not a reason to hide any established safety failure.\n\nWe committed to publish the reporting process and the conditions under which an activity would stop. Parents could then question a concrete procedure rather than rely on a general invitation to trust us. The distinction between confidentiality and secrecy needed to remain visible: personal information should be handled proportionately, while the rules protecting participants should be clear. Under pressure, a responsible answer may be less reassuring than a promise, but it gives people something they can inspect and use if a concern arises. A published procedure also gives concerns a route beyond personal reassurance.","test":"The public asked our housing cooperative how many households had definitely saved money after a new heating arrangement. We had bills from some residents, estimates from others and several cases where occupancy had changed. A precise total would have looked helpful, but the records did not support one.\n\nI reported the number of bills available and explained the comparison we could make. I also identified what remained uncertain. A lower bill might reflect the arrangement, different weather or fewer people in a flat. Those possibilities did not erase residents experiences; they limited the causal statement we could responsibly make.\n\nSomeone suggested counting every positive comment as a confirmed saving. Another wanted us to publish each households bill so neighbours could judge for themselves. The first proposal overstated evidence. The second created unnecessary privacy risks without solving the comparison problem. We needed consent, suitable aggregation and a clearer method, not simply a larger quantity of personal information in public.\n\nThe cooperative agreed to invite voluntary bill submissions through a protected process and to seek independent help with the analysis. We would publish the method and the limits as well as the result. In the meantime, the public statement distinguished documented bills, self reported experiences and untested explanations. Refusing an unsupported total was not refusing scrutiny. It was making scrutiny more useful by identifying what the data could answer and what additional evidence would be required before a stronger claim about savings could be defended."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why not name the volunteer immediately?","options":["The record does not establish individual responsibility.","The hazard itself must remain secret.","No investigation could use a name."],"answer":"The record does not establish individual responsibility.","explanation":"避免把系统证据扩大成个人归责。","id":"c2-13:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What must privacy not become?","options":["A reason for proportional handling.","A condition of receiving testimony.","A blanket shield for established hazards."],"answer":"A blanket shield for established hazards.","explanation":"保密不能掩盖已证实问题。","id":"c2-13:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which direct answer fits the theft question?","options":["The open room proves theft.","The possibility is neither ruled out nor established.","The partial inventory proves an administrative error."],"answer":"The possibility is neither ruled out nor established.","explanation":"分别保留可能性与证实状态。","id":"c2-13:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What makes restraint accountable?","options":["A review process and public timetable.","An unsupported personal assurance.","Immediate publication of every name."],"answer":"A review process and public timetable.","explanation":"程序与可核查进度支持责任说明。","id":"c2-13:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why answer the promise question first?","options":["Checks have no value.","Parents requested no practical information.","Procedures alone could sound evasive."],"answer":"Procedures alone could sound evasive.","explanation":"先直接回应再解释控制。","id":"c2-13:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should be publicly inspectable?","options":["Every rumour about individuals.","Reporting rules and stopping conditions.","A guarantee of future behaviour."],"answer":"Reporting rules and stopping conditions.","explanation":"公开机制而非未经核实指控。","id":"c2-13:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why not count all positive comments as confirmed savings?","options":["They do not establish the comparison or cause.","Residents cannot report experiences.","Weather was known to be identical."],"answer":"They do not establish the comparison or cause.","explanation":"体验与确认节省不能等同。","id":"c2-13:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why not publish every bill?","options":["Bills contain no useful evidence.","Public review was prohibited.","Disclosure would add privacy risk without solving inference."],"answer":"Disclosure would add privacy risk without solving inference.","explanation":"更多公开数据不自动补足方法问题。","id":"c2-13:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a public response to the workshop hearing, answering guarantees and individual blame proportionately.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Answer directly under pressure while refusing precision or guarantees the evidence cannot support.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Prepare the charitys public statement in Who Failed the Collection? Separate established facts, possible theft and unresolved individual responsibility. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on Who Failed the Collection?. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Which direct answer fits the theft question?","What makes restraint accountable?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["coordinator","concerned public representative"],"goal":"Agree a direct answer, a disclosure boundary and a review timetable.","twist":"Someone demands a personal name that the current evidence does not justify releasing."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving answer directly under pressure while refusing precision or guarantees the evidence cannot support. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-14","level":"C2","title":"辩论中的最强反方","chapter":78,"focus":"Respond to the strongest actual objection and distinguish values, mechanisms and practical options.","vocab":[{"head":"staffing costs","zh":"人员成本","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The strongest objection concerns staffing costs, not indifference to swimmers.","exampleZh":"最有力的异议关乎人员成本，而非对游泳者漠不关心。","collocation":"staffing costs","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v1","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rules out","zh":"排除","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I accept that concern but question whether it rules out a limited trial.","exampleZh":"我接受这个担忧，但质疑它是否排除了有限试行。","collocation":"rules out a trial","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v2","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supporters would recognise","zh":"支持者会认可","pos":"subordinate clause","example":"Could we state the opposing argument in terms its supporters would recognise?","exampleZh":"我们能否用支持者认可的方式陈述对方论点？","collocation":"terms supporters would recognise","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v3","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"universal claim","zh":"普遍论断","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The example challenges a universal claim without settling the whole policy.","exampleZh":"这个例子质疑了普遍论断，却没有解决整个政策问题。","collocation":"a universal claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v4","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"only if","zh":"只有在某条件下","pos":"conjunction phrase","example":"Your conclusion follows only if no cheaper staffing arrangement is feasible.","exampleZh":"只有不存在可行的更低成本排班时，你的结论才成立。","collocation":"only if feasible","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v5","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportional response","zh":"适度回应","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We agree on the access problem but differ over the proportional response.","exampleZh":"我们认同出行问题，却对适度回应存在分歧。","collocation":"a proportional response","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v6","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rebuttal","zh":"反驳","pos":"noun","example":"That rebuttal addresses the slogan rather than the underlying argument.","exampleZh":"那项反驳针对口号，而不是背后的论证。","collocation":"a reasoned rebuttal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v7","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"competing benefits","zh":"不同的收益","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The choice involves competing benefits, not a contest between care and neglect.","exampleZh":"选择涉及不同收益，而非关心与漠视的较量。","collocation":"competing benefits","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v8","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"indefinitely","zh":"无限期地","pos":"adverb","example":"A trial could test demand without committing the budget indefinitely.","exampleZh":"试行可以检验需求，而不无限期承诺预算。","collocation":"commit indefinitely","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v9","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revise","zh":"修改","pos":"verb","example":"What result would persuade you to revise your present position?","exampleZh":"什么结果会促使你修改目前立场？","collocation":"revise a position","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v10","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withdraw","zh":"撤回","pos":"verb","example":"I would withdraw that objection if the cost assumption proved mistaken.","exampleZh":"如果成本假设被证明有误，我会撤回那项异议。","collocation":"withdraw an objection","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v11","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterargument","zh":"反论点","pos":"noun","example":"Acknowledging a strong counterargument makes the remaining disagreement clearer.","exampleZh":"承认有力反论点会让剩余分歧更清楚。","collocation":"a strong counterargument","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v12","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"steelman","zh":"最强合理表述","pos":"verb","example":"Steelman the objection before attempting a rebuttal.","exampleZh":"反驳前先作最强合理表述。","collocation":"steelman an objection","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v13","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"Desirability and feasibility require separate arguments.","exampleZh":"可取性与可行性需要分别论证。","collocation":"practical feasibility","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v14","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"premise","zh":"前提","pos":"noun","example":"The conclusion depends on a disputed cost premise.","exampleZh":"结论依赖有争议的成本前提。","collocation":"a disputed premise","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v15","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"common ground","zh":"共同基础","pos":"noun","example":"Common ground can clarify the remaining disagreement.","exampleZh":"共同基础能澄清余下分歧。","collocation":"establish common ground","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v16","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later：扩展论证","text":"At a debate about keeping a public swimming pool open later, participants considered whether to weigh access for shift workers against staffing costs. The background was specific: supporters argued that current hours excluded people with irregular jobs, while opponents cited the cost of lifeguards rather than hostility to those users. However, portraying opponents as indifferent to health avoided the actual resource objection. The proposed response was to acknowledge the strongest cost argument and compare a limited trial with the present schedule.\n\nThe staffing objection deserves an answer in financial and operational terms. Accusing opponents of indifference would leave that objection intact while making cooperation harder. A limited late opening trial could provide demand information and a realistic record of staffing needs, provided its costs and review criteria were agreed in advance. Supporters would then need to accept the possibility that demand was lower than expected; opponents would need to accept evidence of substantial unmet need. This creates a disagreement that experience can inform. The argument should compare the trial with the current arrangement rather than contrast perfect access with unlimited expense. Both sides can care about public health while assigning different weights to the available options. The strongest objection concerns staffing costs, not indifference to swimmers. I accept that concern but question whether it rules out a limited trial. Could we state the opposing argument in terms its supporters would recognise? The example challenges a universal claim without settling the whole policy. Your conclusion follows only if no cheaper staffing arrangement is feasible. We agree on the access problem but differ over the proportional response.\n\nBefore the pool debate resumes, each side is asked to summarise the others case in terms the other recognises. Supporters identify staffing cost and reliability; opponents acknowledge the exclusion created by current hours. This does not make disagreement disappear. It relocates it to questions that evidence and explicit values can inform. A later opening could be worthwhile yet unaffordable under the proposed arrangement. A cheaper trial could be affordable yet fail to meet the access need. The group should compare actual options under common criteria instead of treating one side as generous and the other as indifferent. Fair representation is a method of sharpening disagreement, not a request for artificial harmony."},"sourceB":{"title":"Trees or Visibility?","text":"A fictional neighbourhood debates planting trees beside a narrow road. Supporters emphasise shade, habitat and a more pleasant walk. Several residents object that the proposed locations may obscure drivers views near two crossings. A campaign leaflet describes these residents as opponents of nature. That description substitutes an easily criticised motive for the practical concern actually raised.\n\nThe concern does not establish that trees should never be planted. It identifies a condition that the current design must address. Different species, planting positions or changes to the crossing might preserve benefits while improving visibility. These options require examination rather than assumption. A beautiful drawing cannot prove safe sight lines, and a safety objection cannot by itself prove that every possible design fails.\n\nA supporter argues that the street already contains parked vehicles that block views. This is relevant to the comparison with current conditions, but it does not automatically justify adding another obstruction. It may instead support removing parking near crossings. The debate should specify which arrangement is being compared with which alternative. Otherwise, participants can each choose a convenient baseline and appear to refute one another while discussing different roads.\n\nThe group also disagrees about how to weigh benefits that cannot be reduced easily to one figure. Shade for pedestrians and vehicle access may involve competing priorities. Evidence can clarify likely consequences without deciding every value judgement. Pretending that all disagreement will vanish after one measurement would misdescribe the task.\n\nThe revised proposal retains the planting aim but commissions a sight line assessment and compares two layouts, including a change to parking. Each is judged against the same stated criteria. The objectors agree that a design satisfying the visibility condition would answer their principal concern, though they reserve the right to examine the result. The supporters acknowledge that environmental benefits do not excuse ignoring a specific hazard. This is not a victory for moderation as an abstract virtue. It is a more rigorous dispute, in which claims can be tested, values can be named and the strongest objection can receive an answer proportionate to its actual content.\n\nThat precision makes disagreement more useful without guaranteeing agreement."},"listening":{"teaching":"In our debate about a later library opening, I initially argued that opponents did not understand the needs of working people. An opponent replied that she worked late herself and wanted the service, but doubted the staffing arrangement. I had attacked a motive she did not hold instead of answering her concern.\n\nWe restarted by naming the strongest version of each case. The access argument concerned people unable to visit during current hours. The staffing argument concerned a reliable service without excessive overtime or an unsustainable cost. Both were serious. Agreeing that libraries matter did not settle how to deliver another opening period.\n\nWe compared a full extra evening with a shorter trial on one day. The trial had lower cost but might not serve everyone excluded by the current schedule. That limitation needed to remain visible. Calling it a compromise did not make it automatically sufficient or fair.\n\nThe group chose a trial with published criteria for use, cost and staff experience. Supporters accepted that disappointing demand would matter. Opponents accepted that strong unmet need could justify seeking additional funding. Neither side promised to change its values, but both identified evidence relevant to its practical claims. The debate improved when disagreement became more precise. Representing an opponent fairly was not a concession that my own case was weak; it showed which part of my case still needed support and which objection my recommendation had to answer. It also made future revision a credible possibility.","test":"The discussion concerned whether a community hall should allow evening music rehearsals. Supporters described the cultural benefits and the difficulty of finding affordable rooms. Neighbours raised concerns about sound after children had gone to bed. A speaker replied that anyone opposing music must dislike young people.\n\nThat response missed the strongest objection. The neighbours had not objected to rehearsal at every time or to young people using the hall. Their concern was the timing and transmission of sound into nearby homes. A proposal for earlier hours or insulation might therefore address it, although either would have costs and limitations.\n\nOne neighbour claimed that any rehearsal would be unbearable. That was broader than the evidence supplied. The group could reasonably ask for a sound test and a definition of acceptable hours. Fairness required examining overstated claims on both sides rather than treating one category of participant as automatically reasonable.\n\nWe agreed to compare a limited earlier session with the present arrangement and to assess sound at affected properties with permission. The test would not decide the cultural value of music, but it would inform claims about disturbance. The eventual decision would still involve values and practical constraints. A useful debate distinguishes those roles. It does not ask measurements to settle every priority, and it does not use strong values as a reason to ignore consequences that could be investigated. The most productive next step was therefore a testable arrangement, not a verdict about which group cared more about the community."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does summarising the other case achieve?","options":["It eliminates competing values.","It locates the disagreement more accurately.","It proves a trial is sufficient."],"answer":"It locates the disagreement more accurately.","explanation":"公平表述使分歧更精确。","id":"c2-14:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is artificial harmony inadequate?","options":["Practical options can still fail different criteria.","Both sides must hold identical values.","Costs no longer matter."],"answer":"Practical options can still fail different criteria.","explanation":"承认共同点不等于问题解决。","id":"c2-14:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is opponents of nature misleading?","options":["The residents rejected all shade.","The campaign had measured visibility.","It replaces a design concern with an attributed motive."],"answer":"It replaces a design concern with an attributed motive.","explanation":"应回应具体风险而非制造动机。","id":"c2-14:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does existing parking establish?","options":["Proof that adding obstruction is safe.","A relevant baseline question, not automatic justification.","That trees cannot affect visibility."],"answer":"A relevant baseline question, not automatic justification.","explanation":"现状问题可提示另一替代方案。","id":"c2-14:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What was wrong with the initial attack?","options":["It misrepresented a staffing objection as indifference.","The opponent rejected working people.","Access needs had been measured conclusively."],"answer":"It misrepresented a staffing objection as indifference.","explanation":"最强实际异议被绕开。","id":"c2-14:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should remain visible about the trial?","options":["A guarantee that all needs are met.","Its exemption from cost criteria.","Its possible failure to serve everyone excluded."],"answer":"Its possible failure to serve everyone excluded.","explanation":"折衷方案仍有限制。","id":"c2-14:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What scope did the neighbours objection have?","options":["All musical activity everywhere.","Timing and transmitted sound.","Every use by young people."],"answer":"Timing and transmitted sound.","explanation":"精确恢复对象与条件。","id":"c2-14:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What can a sound test inform?","options":["Disturbance claims without settling every value.","The complete cultural worth of music.","The motives of every neighbour."],"answer":"Disturbance claims without settling every value.","explanation":"证据与价值判断分工不同。","id":"c2-14:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write the strongest reasonable cost objection to late pool hours, then answer it with a testable proposal.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Respond to the strongest actual objection and distinguish values, mechanisms and practical options.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write an argument about Trees or Visibility? Compare the planting alternatives under common criteria and address the actual safety objection. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on Trees or Visibility?. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["Why is opponents of nature misleading?","What does existing parking establish?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["supporter of a proposal","opponent with a specific constraint"],"goal":"Produce a statement of the remaining disagreement that both sides recognise.","twist":"New evidence supports one practical objection but does not settle the value conflict."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving respond to the strongest actual objection and distinguish values, mechanisms and practical options. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-15","level":"C2","title":"复杂谈判与条件交换","chapter":79,"focus":"Design reciprocal commitments that address interests and remain clear when conditions change.","vocab":[{"head":"uninterrupted","zh":"连续不被打断的","pos":"adjective","example":"Our priority is uninterrupted rehearsal time rather than any particular weekday.","exampleZh":"我们的优先需求是连续排练时间，而非某个特定星期几。","collocation":"uninterrupted rehearsal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v1","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compensate for","zh":"补偿","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Would a guaranteed later booking compensate for the temporary loss of access?","exampleZh":"保证之后的预约能否补偿暂时失去使用权？","collocation":"compensate for loss","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v2","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"只要","pos":"conjunction","example":"We can concede those evenings provided the later arrangement is confirmed.","exampleZh":"只要后续安排得到确认，我们可以让出那些晚间时段。","collocation":"provided it is confirmed","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v3","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conditional offer","zh":"有条件提议","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That is a conditional offer, not an agreement to surrender the slot.","exampleZh":"那是一项有条件提议，而不是同意放弃时段。","collocation":"a conditional offer","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v4","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"setup","zh":"布置","pos":"noun","example":"Let us compare the costs of setup before dividing the hours equally.","exampleZh":"在平均分配时段之前，我们先比较布置成本。","collocation":"setup costs","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v5","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underlying interests","zh":"背后的实际诉求","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The positions conflict, but the underlying interests may be compatible.","exampleZh":"立场相冲突，但背后的实际诉求可能相容。","collocation":"underlying interests","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v6","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"committing","zh":"作出承诺","pos":"verb (gerund)","example":"I need to consult the group before committing to that exchange.","exampleZh":"在承诺这项交换之前，我需要征求小组意见。","collocation":"committing to an exchange","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v7","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reopen","zh":"重新讨论","pos":"verb","example":"If either condition cannot be met, we should reopen the whole package.","exampleZh":"如果任一条件无法满足，我们应重新讨论整套方案。","collocation":"reopen a package","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v8","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"goodwill","zh":"善意","pos":"noun","example":"The written agreement should specify dates rather than rely on goodwill.","exampleZh":"书面协议应明确日期，而非依靠善意。","collocation":"rely on goodwill","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v9","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"predictable","zh":"可预见的","pos":"adjective","example":"What is the least predictable part of the current arrangement for you?","exampleZh":"对你们来说，目前安排中最不确定的是什么？","collocation":"predictable access","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v10","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocal concession","zh":"相互让步","pos":"noun phrase","example":"A reciprocal concession should address a real constraint on each side.","exampleZh":"相互让步应解决双方各自的实际限制。","collocation":"a reciprocal concession","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v11","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"subject to","zh":"以某条件为前提","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"We have an outline agreement, subject to confirmation of the hall booking.","exampleZh":"我们达成了框架协议，但须待礼堂预约确认。","collocation":"subject to confirmation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v12","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocal","zh":"相互的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reciprocal offer links a concession to a benefit.","exampleZh":"相互提议把让步与收益联系起来。","collocation":"a reciprocal offer","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v13","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency","zh":"应变安排","pos":"noun","example":"The agreement needs a contingency for postponement.","exampleZh":"协议需要延期时的应变安排。","collocation":"a contingency plan","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v14","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reservation point","zh":"可接受底线","pos":"noun","example":"The group should know its reservation point before negotiating.","exampleZh":"小组应在谈判前明确底线。","collocation":"establish a reservation point","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v15","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enforceable","zh":"可执行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Precise dates make the agreement more enforceable.","exampleZh":"具体日期使协议更可执行。","collocation":"an enforceable agreement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v16","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall：扩展论证","text":"At a negotiation over rehearsal space in a school hall, participants considered whether to agree a schedule between a drama group and a youth club. The background was specific: the drama group needed uninterrupted evenings before its performance, while the youth club needed predictable access rather than a particular weekday. However, splitting every evening equally would satisfy neither need and create repeated setup costs. The proposed response was to offer a short performance preparation block in exchange for a longer guaranteed youth club booking afterwards.\n\nThe groups initial positions make the room look impossible to share, but the reasons behind those positions reveal an exchange. Continuous preparation is especially valuable before a performance, while predictability may matter more than a particular weekday to the youth club. A negotiated block can therefore be fair without dividing each evening in half. The arrangement should specify dates, setup responsibilities and what happens if the performance moves. Confirmation matters because each group is being asked to rely on a later benefit. I would also schedule a short review after the first exchange. That gives both parties a way to correct practical problems without treating every request for adjustment as a breach of goodwill. Our priority is uninterrupted rehearsal time rather than any particular weekday. Would a guaranteed later booking compensate for the temporary loss of access? We can concede those evenings provided the later arrangement is confirmed. That is a conditional offer, not an agreement to surrender the slot. Let us compare the costs of setup before dividing the hours equally. The positions conflict, but the underlying interests may be compatible.\n\nThe hall manager proposes recording only the broad principle of cooperation. Both groups prefer a more specific agreement because each will make plans that depend on the others later action. The drama group needs to know when equipment must be removed; the youth club needs dates it can advertise. Their document names the exchange, the person confirming bookings and the procedure if the performance is postponed. This does not imply distrust. It recognises that goodwill cannot answer every practical question once circumstances change. A negotiation succeeds not merely when people leave the room smiling, but when they can independently state the same commitments and the conditions under which those commitments may be revised."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Shared Refrigeration Contract","text":"A fictional food cooperative negotiates with a small farm to share refrigerated storage. The cooperative wants access on weekends, when volunteers collect deliveries. The farm wants a predictable electricity contribution and enough uninterrupted space during harvest. At first the parties bargain only over the monthly price, each assuming that the other is being inflexible.\n\nThe discussion changes when the cooperative explains that it does not need the entire room. It needs a clearly labelled section and access at reliable times. The farm explains that its greatest pressure occurs during three harvest weeks rather than throughout the year. The apparent conflict over permanent capacity contains a time dimension that the initial price argument concealed.\n\nThey propose a lower fixed contribution in exchange for reduced cooperative space during the harvest period. The cooperative can accept this only if the dates are confirmed early enough to arrange alternative deliveries. The farm can accept it only if stored goods are removed promptly and temperature records remain accessible. These are conditions of an exchange, not expressions of personal distrust.\n\nA volunteer suggests agreeing informally because both organisations serve the community. That shared purpose is valuable, but it does not specify who may enter, what happens after equipment failure or who bears the cost of spoiled goods. Leaving these matters unstated could turn a friendly arrangement into a conflict precisely when quick decisions are needed.\n\nThe final agreement names storage boundaries, access times, harvest notice and responsibility for monitoring. It also includes a review date after the first season and a procedure for exceptional failures. Neither party receives everything it initially requested. Each receives something more closely related to its underlying need. The lower payment is conditional on a real concession, and the concession is limited rather than open ended.\n\nThe quality of the negotiation should therefore be judged by the clarity and suitability of the exchange, not by whether both sides describe the result as equal. Equal space every week would ignore seasonal pressure; the cheapest price might not produce reliable access. A durable agreement makes different interests compatible where possible and makes the remaining risks visible before either organisation relies on a promise it has understood differently."},"listening":{"teaching":"Our theatre group negotiated with a school for rehearsal space. We asked for three evenings every week, and the school offered one. For a while we argued about whether either number was fair. Then we explained why we wanted the room: the set had to remain assembled during the final week before the performance.\n\nThe school did not object to that week in particular. Its concern was losing regular access for a term. We could therefore offer to use a smaller room during early rehearsals in exchange for one uninterrupted final block. This addressed a need that an equal division of every evening would not have met.\n\nThe offer remained conditional. We needed written confirmation before advertising the performance, and the school needed the set removed before Monday classes. We recorded both conditions and named the people responsible. Calling the arrangement friendly would not have told anyone when the room became available again.\n\nWe also discussed postponement. If the performance moved, the block would not automatically move with it; both sides would review availability. This was important because a promise linked to a date is not necessarily a promise linked to every future version of an event. The negotiation succeeded when we stopped treating positions as complete descriptions of interests and began designing an exchange. Precision did not undermine trust. It made trust usable by giving both groups a shared account of what they could rely on and what would require another conversation if circumstances changed.","test":"A neighbourhood cafe wanted a bakery to hold its price for six months. The bakery refused a simple price freeze because ingredient costs were uncertain. The cafe interpreted the refusal as unwillingness to support a community business, but the baker's concern was financial exposure rather than the cafe's purpose.\n\nWe asked what could make a stable price less risky. The bakery said that confirmed weekly volume and earlier orders would reduce waste. The cafe could offer those commitments if it had a limited option to adjust during school holidays. A negotiation that initially seemed to concern only price began to include information and predictability.\n\nThe proposed agreement fixed a base quantity and price, set a deadline for changes and defined a holiday adjustment procedure. It also included a review trigger if ingredient costs moved beyond a stated range. The review was not an automatic price increase; it was a commitment to reopen the discussion under identified conditions.\n\nBoth parties needed to understand that distinction. If one expected a guaranteed price under every circumstance while the other expected unilateral changes, a cheerful handshake would conceal incompatible promises. We read the terms back using practical examples before confirming them. The cafe would not receive unlimited flexibility, and the bakery would not receive an unconditional order. Each concession answered a real constraint on the other side. The resulting arrangement was more useful than a demand for equal sacrifice because the benefits and risks were not identical in the first place."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why record dates and procedures?","options":["Others will rely on specific later actions.","Goodwill has no value.","Every change proves bad faith."],"answer":"Others will rely on specific later actions.","explanation":"可依赖的承诺需要明确条件。","id":"c2-15:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is a useful test of agreement?","options":["Both parties report equal sacrifice.","Both parties can state the same commitments independently.","Neither side anticipates change."],"answer":"Both parties can state the same commitments independently.","explanation":"共同理解优于表面友好。","id":"c2-15:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did price only bargaining conceal?","options":["A complete absence of shared purpose.","A guarantee of equipment reliability.","A seasonal difference in capacity needs."],"answer":"A seasonal difference in capacity needs.","explanation":"时间维度可创造交换空间。","id":"c2-15:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why is shared purpose insufficient?","options":["It leaves operational risks unspecified.","It prevents written terms.","It makes monitoring unnecessary."],"answer":"It leaves operational risks unspecified.","explanation":"价值相同不等于规则清楚。","id":"c2-15:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What made the exchange workable?","options":["The school abandoned term access.","A final block mattered more than regular evenings.","Both groups needed equal hours daily."],"answer":"A final block mattered more than regular evenings.","explanation":"识别底层需求而非死守位置。","id":"c2-15:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens if the performance moves?","options":["The school must move every other activity.","All commitments vanish without discussion.","Availability is reviewed rather than automatically transferred."],"answer":"Availability is reviewed rather than automatically transferred.","explanation":"日期条件不能无声扩大。","id":"c2-15:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What reduced the bakerys risk?","options":["Predictable volume and earlier orders.","A general appeal to community spirit.","Unlimited holiday changes."],"answer":"Predictable volume and earlier orders.","explanation":"用实际对价回应约束。","id":"c2-15:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What does the cost trigger require?","options":["An automatic unilateral increase.","Reopening discussion under specified conditions.","A permanent waiver of the price."],"answer":"Reopening discussion under specified conditions.","explanation":"复谈条件不同于直接改价。","id":"c2-15:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write a rehearsal agreement naming the exchange, confirmation dates and postponement procedure.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Design reciprocal commitments that address interests and remain clear when conditions change.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Draft an advisory memo on The Shared Refrigeration Contract, identifying interests, reciprocal conditions and exceptional failure procedures. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Shared Refrigeration Contract. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What did price only bargaining conceal?","Why is shared purpose insufficient?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["cooperative negotiator","resource owner"],"goal":"Agree an exchange with clear conditions, not merely equal quantities.","twist":"A promised date changes after both sides have begun relying on it."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving design reciprocal commitments that address interests and remain clear when conditions change. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]},{"id":"c2-16","level":"C2","title":"风格编辑与作者声音","chapter":84,"focus":"Improve clarity without erasing intentional rhythm, stance or perspective.","vocab":[{"head":"rhythm","zh":"节奏","pos":"noun","example":"The repeated short sentences create a rhythm that serves the narrator's anxiety.","exampleZh":"反复的短句形成了体现叙述者焦虑的节奏。","collocation":"a narrative rhythm","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v1","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"productive uncertainty","zh":"有意义的不确定性","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The ambiguous pronoun produces confusion rather than productive uncertainty.","exampleZh":"有歧义的代词造成的是混乱，而非有意义的不确定性。","collocation":"productive uncertainty","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v2","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deliberate repetition","zh":"有意的重复","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would clarify the reference while preserving the deliberate repetition.","exampleZh":"我会澄清指代，同时保留有意的重复。","collocation":"deliberate repetition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v3","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substantial change","zh":"实质改动","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An editor should be able to explain what each substantial change achieves.","exampleZh":"编辑应能解释每一处实质改动达到了什么效果。","collocation":"a substantial change","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v4","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"faithful","zh":"忠实的","pos":"adjective","example":"The smoother version is not automatically the more faithful one.","exampleZh":"更顺畅的版本未必更忠实。","collocation":"a faithful version","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v5","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"oversight","zh":"疏忽","pos":"noun","example":"Could this repetition be intentional rather than an oversight?","exampleZh":"这个重复是否可能是有意的，而非疏忽？","collocation":"an accidental oversight","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v6","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinctive","zh":"独特的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should ask the author before replacing the distinctive sentence pattern.","exampleZh":"在替换独特句式之前，我们应询问作者。","collocation":"a distinctive pattern","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v7","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"emotional progression","zh":"情感推进","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The paragraph loses its emotional progression when every sentence has equal length.","exampleZh":"当每句长度相同时，段落失去了情感推进。","collocation":"emotional progression","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v8","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cohesion","zh":"衔接","pos":"noun","example":"This change improves cohesion without altering the narrator's attitude.","exampleZh":"这个改动改善了衔接，同时没有改变叙述者态度。","collocation":"textual cohesion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v9","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intended effect","zh":"预期效果","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The revision removes an accidental obstacle to the intended effect.","exampleZh":"修改移除了阻碍预期效果的偶然障碍。","collocation":"the intended effect","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v10","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rationale","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"A useful style note records the decision and its rationale.","exampleZh":"有用的风格说明会记录决定及其理由。","collocation":"explain a rationale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v11","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Editorial restraint","zh":"编辑上的克制","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Editorial restraint can require as much judgement as extensive rewriting.","exampleZh":"编辑上的克制可能与大幅改写同样需要判断力。","collocation":"editorial restraint","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v12","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cadence","zh":"行文节奏","pos":"noun","example":"The revision altered the cadence of the paragraph.","exampleZh":"修改改变了段落的行文节奏。","collocation":"preserve a cadence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v13","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intrusive","zh":"过度干预的","pos":"adjective","example":"The edit became intrusive when it replaced the narrator's stance.","exampleZh":"替换叙述者立场时编辑变得过度干预。","collocation":"an intrusive edit","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v14","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coherence","zh":"连贯性","pos":"noun","example":"Coherence depends on relationships between ideas.","exampleZh":"连贯性取决于观念之间的关系。","collocation":"improve coherence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v15","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rationale","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"The author should see the rationale for substantial changes.","exampleZh":"作者应看到实质改动的理由。","collocation":"explain the rationale","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v16","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"}],"sourceA":{"title":"教学案例：an editor working on a personal essay about migration：扩展论证","text":"At an editor working on a personal essay about migration, participants considered whether to reduce ambiguity without smoothing away the narrator's rhythm. The background was specific: short repeated sentences conveyed the speakers anxiety, while a pronoun in the third paragraph made it unclear which family member had left. However, treating every repetition as a fault would remove an intentional effect, whereas leaving the pronoun unresolved would preserve accidental confusion. The proposed response was to clarify the reference and discuss rhythm with the author before rewriting the repeated sentences.\n\nThe narrator's short repeated sentences have an identifiable function: they make anxiety audible in the prose. Changing them merely because variety is usually recommended would impose an abstract rule at the expense of the essay. The pronoun problem is different. It prevents readers from knowing which family member acted and does not contribute a comparable effect. I would clarify that reference, then discuss any broader rhythm changes with the author. The editorial note should explain the distinction so that the writer can accept or reject a change intelligently. Preserving voice does not require leaving every difficulty untouched. It requires understanding which difficulties are doing intentional work and which are obstructing the intended experience. The repeated short sentences create a rhythm that serves the narrator's anxiety. The ambiguous pronoun produces confusion rather than productive uncertainty. I would clarify the reference while preserving the deliberate repetition. An editor should be able to explain what each substantial change achieves. The smoother version is not automatically the more faithful one. Could this repetition be intentional rather than an oversight? We should ask the author before replacing the distinctive sentence pattern.\n\nA second editor proposes replacing the narrator's repeated I remember with varied expressions. The author explains that repetition marks what she can still say with certainty, contrasting with uncertain family accounts elsewhere. This gives the pattern a structural role beyond sound. The editor should test that explanation against the whole essay, not accept every preference automatically. If the pattern works, variation would remove meaning rather than improve style. If one repetition merely obscures a reference, a local correction may still help. The editing record should distinguish changes to accuracy, cohesion and voice so that the author can understand what each suggestion would gain and what it might cost."},"sourceB":{"title":"The Smoothed Interview","text":"A fictional magazine prepares an interview with a retired boat builder. The transcript contains short clauses, repetitions and several pauses. An editor rewrites the account into long polished sentences, removing every repeated reference to the river. The result is easier to scan, but the interviewee says that it no longer sounds like him and changes what he meant.\n\nHis objection deserves examination rather than automatic acceptance or dismissal. Some repetition simply reflects spontaneous speech and may be removed without loss in a written interview. Other repetition can carry emphasis or uncertainty. In one passage, he repeats I think before describing a disputed repair. Removing both occurrences makes a tentative memory sound like a confident factual assertion. That is a substantive change, not merely a smoother style.\n\nThe references to the river also perform different functions. One repeats information already clear and adds little. Another connects a childhood memory with a later decision to remain in the village. Cutting both because the same noun appears nearby treats recurrence as a mechanical fault rather than asking what each instance contributes.\n\nThe editor has a legitimate responsibility to readers. An unedited transcript can be difficult to follow, and accidental ambiguity may misrepresent the speaker as surely as excessive polishing. In another sentence, two men could be the referent of he. Clarifying the name protects understanding without replacing the interviewee's attitude. The relevant distinction is therefore not editing versus no editing, but changes that serve the intended meaning versus changes that silently alter it.\n\nThe magazine agrees to retain the uncertainty markers, restore the meaningful river connection and clarify the pronoun. It removes some fillers and documents substantial alterations for the interviewee to review. This process does not grant the speaker control over independent factual checking; a disputed date still needs verification. It gives him a chance to identify changes in voice and claim strength.\n\nA fair final version should be readable, accurate about the status of memories and recognisable as this persons account. Uniform polish is not the only measure of quality. The editor must explain what a change improves and what it risks erasing, while the speaker must engage with genuine obstacles to comprehension rather than treat every original hesitation as untouchable."},"listening":{"teaching":"When I edited a personal account of moving home, I removed several short repetitions because I had been taught to vary sentence openings. The writer objected that the repeated phrase carried the rhythm of packing and returning to the same rooms. Looking again, I could see that the effect was deliberate.\n\nThat did not mean every sentence should remain untouched. One paragraph used she for two different family members, making an important action unclear. The ambiguity did not create a productive uncertainty about memory; it simply prevented readers from knowing who had spoken. We clarified the name while retaining the repeated openings.\n\nThe distinction changed how I explained suggestions. Instead of marking repetition as a universal fault, I asked what it did in that passage. Instead of praising voice as a reason to avoid correction, I identified the accidental obstacle. The writer could then respond to a reason rather than a rule applied without context.\n\nWe kept a short note of substantial decisions, including one edit the writer rejected because it made her attitude sound more certain than she intended. Editing is partly an exchange about meaning. A smooth paragraph can still distort the strength of a claim or the emotional progression of an account. The useful goal is to remove barriers to the intended effect while preserving the choices that produce it, and to check those choices against the work rather than assuming that either an editor or an author is automatically right about every detail.","test":"The article compared an old observatory with a tired animal waking slowly at dusk. The editor liked the image but proposed deleting three later metaphors from the same sentence. The writer initially accused her of removing personality from the piece. That reaction treated all imagery as though it performed the same function.\n\nThe first comparison helped readers picture the delayed opening of the roof. The later comparisons introduced a ship, a flower and a machine, each requiring a different visual frame. Their accumulation obscured the movement the sentence was supposed to explain. Removing them could strengthen the useful image rather than flatten the style.\n\nThe editor also changed might have been to was in a sentence about an uncertain historical date. That change was more problematic. It increased certainty without new evidence. The writer was right to challenge it, even though the sentence became shorter and more direct. Clarity cannot justify converting a hypothesis into a fact.\n\nThey revised the paragraph together, retaining the explanatory image and restoring the uncertainty marker. The exchange illustrates why an edit should be judged by its particular effect. Removing language is not always removing voice; preserving every phrase is not always respecting it. A defensible style decision identifies what the reader gains, what the writer intends and whether the claim remains accurate. Both participants improved the result when they moved from defending their professional identity to examining the actual work each phrase was doing in the passage."},"questions":[{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why might varied openings harm the essay?","options":["Variation is always inappropriate.","Repetition marks a contrast in certainty.","The author rejects all cohesion."],"answer":"Repetition marks a contrast in certainty.","explanation":"重复可能承担结构意义。","id":"c2-16:practice:q0"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"reading","prompt":"What should the record distinguish?","options":["Effects on accuracy, cohesion and voice.","Only the number of deleted words.","Whether the editor sounds authoritative."],"answer":"Effects on accuracy, cohesion and voice.","explanation":"不同改动的收益与代价应明确。","id":"c2-16:practice:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does deleting I think risk?","options":["Making the date less specific.","Preserving the original uncertainty.","Turning tentative memory into confident assertion."],"answer":"Turning tentative memory into confident assertion.","explanation":"语气标记承担证据强度。","id":"c2-16:assessment:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why clarify the pronoun?","options":["All original speech must be polished identically.","It removes accidental confusion without replacing attitude.","It gives the interviewee control over fact checking."],"answer":"It removes accidental confusion without replacing attitude.","explanation":"明确所指与风格抹平不同。","id":"c2-16:assessment:q3"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"What made the repetition defensible?","options":["Its role in the accounts rhythm.","A universal ban on editing.","Its accidental ambiguity."],"answer":"Its role in the accounts rhythm.","explanation":"具体效果而非机械规则。","id":"c2-16:practice:q4"},{"phase":"practice","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why explain an edit through reasons?","options":["The editor must accept every preference.","Smoothness proves accuracy.","The author can judge its effect on meaning."],"answer":"The author can judge its effect on meaning.","explanation":"理由帮助协作判断。","id":"c2-16:practice:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why remove later metaphors?","options":["All imagery is unprofessional.","They compete with the image doing explanatory work.","The opening comparison lacks any function."],"answer":"They compete with the image doing explanatory work.","explanation":"保留有效意象而减少冲突。","id":"c2-16:assessment:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why challenge was?","options":["It strengthens certainty without evidence.","It makes the sentence grammatically longer.","It restores the writer's original hedge."],"answer":"It strengthens certainty without evidence.","explanation":"风格不能擅自提高论断强度。","id":"c2-16:assessment:q7"}],"writing":{"prompt":"Write an editing note separating intentional repetition, uncertain memory and accidental reference problems.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"speaking":{"prompt":"Using the teaching case, give a three-minute explanation of your recommended response. Improve clarity without erasing intentional rhythm, stance or perspective.","followups":["Which specific detail most strongly supports your interpretation?","What is the strongest reasonable alternative, and what remains unresolved?","What new evidence would make you revise the recommendation?"]},"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Prepare an edit plan for The Smoothed Interview. Identify what to restore, what to clarify and what still requires independent fact checking. Use the independent assessment reading only; do not consult teaching examples.","minimumWords":280,"checklist":["明确任务对象、受众和目的，直接回应核心问题。","引用具体证据并区分事实、推断、价值与未知；不复制教学范文。","公平呈现最强替代解释，保留限定与反证，并给出可执行结论。","检查组织、语域、搭配和语法；记录工具与人工辅助，交由独立评阅者审阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an independent briefing on The Smoothed Interview. Identify the practical issue, compare two defensible responses and justify a conditional recommendation. Do not read a prepared script.","followups":["What does deleting I think risk?","Why clarify the pronoun?","Respond to a partner who disagrees and reformulate your claim for a non-specialist audience."]},"interaction":{"roles":["author or interviewee","editor"],"goal":"Agree three justified changes and one deliberate retention, recording their effects.","twist":"A smoother sentence silently increases the certainty of a disputed claim."},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"After at least two weeks, ask a reviewer to select a genuinely unfamiliar text on a new situation involving improve clarity without erasing intentional rhythm, stance or perspective. Summarise its argument and distinguish evidence from inference. Reusing Source B is review, not an unfamiliar assessment.","writingPrompt":"Using that newly selected material, produce an independent 280-word response under recorded conditions. Retain the first draft and any later feedback; this pack does not supply a third hidden form.","speakingPrompt":"Discuss the new situation with a live partner who adds unexpected questions. Record consent, assistance and reviewer observations; familiar scripts and local transcription scores do not establish C2."},"limitations":["C2 is a training target, not an expert-calibrated difficulty or an awarded proficiency level.","All cases and scripts are original fiction; they do not report actual research or institutional incidents.","Teaching text in selected modules substantially expands the authors earlier teaching A case; independent B and both listening scripts are newly authored.","Scripts are not human recordings. Synthetic delivery cannot establish competence across unfamiliar human accents, timing or interaction.","No third held-out form is included. Delayed prompts require independently selected new material; B reuse is practice."]}];window.QuestFinalVocabulary=[{"head":"connection","zh":"接驳；转机","pos":"noun","example":"Our connection in Berlin was delayed, so we had time to check the information desk before choosing another route.","exampleZh":"我们在柏林的接驳延误了，所以有时间先问服务台再选路线。","collocation":"miss a connection","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v1","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"departure","zh":"出发","pos":"noun","example":"The departure board showed a new gate, but it did not explain whether the boarding time had also changed.","exampleZh":"出发显示屏换了登机口，却没说明登机时间是否改变。","collocation":"departure board","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v2","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"I asked an assistant to confirm the time because my phone was still showing the original booking details.","exampleZh":"我请工作人员确认时间，因为手机仍显示原预订信息。","collocation":"confirm the time","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v3","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"available","zh":"可用的","pos":"adjective","example":"Two seats were available on an evening flight, although we would reach the hotel much later than planned.","exampleZh":"晚班机有两个座位，不过我们会比计划晚很多到酒店。","collocation":"seats available","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v4","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alternative","zh":"替代选择","pos":"noun","example":"The assistant suggested an alternative involving a train journey to a nearby airport and a shorter final flight.","exampleZh":"工作人员提出另一选择：坐火车去附近机场，再乘较短的最后一程航班。","collocation":"suggest an alternative","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v5","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"luggage","zh":"行李","pos":"noun","example":"Our luggage had already been checked in, so we needed to know whether it could travel with us.","exampleZh":"行李已经托运，所以我们需要知道能否随我们同行。","collocation":"checked luggage","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v6","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"转移；转乘","pos":"verb","example":"She could transfer our bags, but only if we accepted the new route before the next check-in deadline.","exampleZh":"她能转运行李，但前提是我们在下一次值机截止前接受新路线。","collocation":"transfer bags","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v7","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止时间","pos":"noun","example":"The deadline gave us twenty minutes to decide, which was enough to compare the arrival times carefully.","exampleZh":"截止前有二十分钟，够我们仔细比较到达时间。","collocation":"check-in deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v8","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct","zh":"直达的","pos":"adjective","example":"A direct train from the first airport would arrive later than the flight, but it offered fewer changes.","exampleZh":"从第一个机场坐直达火车会比飞机晚到，但换乘较少。","collocation":"direct train","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v9","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priority","zh":"优先考虑的事","pos":"noun","example":"My sister’s priority was avoiding another missed connection rather than arriving as early as possible that evening.","exampleZh":"姐姐优先考虑避免再错过接驳，而不是当晚尽早抵达。","collocation":"main priority","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v10","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"arrange","zh":"安排","pos":"verb","example":"We asked the hotel to arrange a late arrival and chose the train because its timetable felt more reliable.","exampleZh":"我们请酒店安排晚到，并因时刻表更可靠而选择火车。","collocation":"arrange a late arrival","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v11","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receipt","zh":"收据","pos":"noun","example":"We kept every receipt in case the airline later asked for evidence of the extra costs we had paid.","exampleZh":"我们保留每张收据，以防航空公司后来要求额外费用凭证。","collocation":"keep a receipt","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-01:v12","level":"B1","chapter":13,"module":"b1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reservation","zh":"预订","pos":"noun","example":"When we reached the guesthouse, our reservation was on the system, but the room described in the email was unavailable.","exampleZh":"我们到旅馆时系统有预订，但邮件描述的房间没空。","collocation":"confirm a reservation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v1","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ground floor","zh":"一楼；地面层","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We had requested a ground floor room because my father found long flights of stairs difficult after an operation.","exampleZh":"我们要求地面层房间，因为父亲术后难爬长楼梯。","collocation":"ground floor room","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v2","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receptionist","zh":"接待员","pos":"noun","example":"The receptionist apologised and showed us a larger room upstairs, without first checking whether there was a working lift.","exampleZh":"接待员道歉，带我们看楼上更大的房，却没先检查电梯是否正常。","collocation":"ask the receptionist","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v3","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"access","zh":"通行；使用","pos":"noun","example":"I explained that easy access mattered more to us than extra space or a view across the garden.","exampleZh":"我解释通行方便比额外空间或花园景色更重要。","collocation":"easy access","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v4","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"temporary","zh":"临时的","pos":"adjective","example":"She offered a temporary room beside the dining area while the manager checked arrangements at a nearby guesthouse.","exampleZh":"她提供餐区旁的临时房，让经理去查附近旅馆安排。","collocation":"temporary room","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v5","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disturb","zh":"打扰","pos":"verb","example":"We asked whether breakfast preparation would disturb my father early in the morning, since he needed plenty of rest.","exampleZh":"我们问早餐准备是否会一早打扰父亲，因为他需要充足休息。","collocation":"disturb a guest","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v6","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"The manager listened carefully and explained that the neighbouring building had an accessible room available for the whole stay.","exampleZh":"经理认真听后解释，隔壁建筑全程都有无障碍房。","collocation":"explain the situation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v7","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"additional","zh":"额外的","pos":"adjective","example":"There would be no additional payment, and the guesthouse would organise transport for our bags rather than expect us to carry them.","exampleZh":"无需额外付费，旅馆会安排运包，而不是让我们自己搬。","collocation":"additional payment","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v8","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agreement","zh":"约定","pos":"noun","example":"Before accepting the agreement, I checked that breakfast and the final night were included, just as in our original booking.","exampleZh":"接受约定前，我核对早餐及最后一晚都像原订单一样包含。","collocation":"check an agreement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v9","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"written","zh":"书面的","pos":"adjective","example":"We requested written confirmation so that staff at the other building would understand the arrangements when we arrived later.","exampleZh":"我们要求书面确认，让另一建筑员工稍后接待时懂安排。","collocation":"written confirmation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v10","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"solution","zh":"解决办法","pos":"noun","example":"The solution was not our first choice, but it met the important need that the larger upstairs room could not.","exampleZh":"解决办法不是首选，却满足楼上大房不能满足的重要需求。","collocation":"practical solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v11","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"patient","zh":"耐心的","pos":"adjective","example":"Remaining patient helped us keep the discussion focused on practical needs instead of repeating how disappointed we felt that afternoon.","exampleZh":"保持耐心帮助我们聚焦实际需求，而非反复表达当天下午多失望。","collocation":"remain patient","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-02:v12","level":"B1","chapter":14,"module":"b1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unwell","zh":"身体不适的","pos":"adjective","example":"During our walking holiday, my cousin felt unwell and decided to stop before the group reached the next village.","exampleZh":"徒步度假时表弟身体不适，决定不到下一村就停下。","collocation":"feel unwell","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v1","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"symptom","zh":"症状","pos":"noun","example":"He described each symptom to the tour leader instead of simply saying that everything felt wrong that afternoon.","exampleZh":"他逐项向领队描述症状，而不只说那天下午哪儿都不舒服。","collocation":"describe a symptom","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v2","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"dizzy","zh":"眩晕的","pos":"adjective","example":"He felt dizzy when standing up, although he had not fallen or hit his head on the path.","exampleZh":"他站起时眩晕，不过没有跌倒或在路上撞头。","collocation":"feel dizzy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v3","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appointment","zh":"预约","pos":"noun","example":"The leader called a local clinic and asked whether an appointment was available before its normal closing time.","exampleZh":"领队打电话问当地诊所正常关门前是否有预约。","collocation":"make an appointment","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v4","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"describe","zh":"描述","pos":"verb","example":"A staff member asked him to describe when the problem began and whether it had happened on earlier walks.","exampleZh":"工作人员让他描述何时开始及以前徒步是否发生过。","collocation":"describe a problem","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v5","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allergy","zh":"过敏","pos":"noun","example":"He mentioned an allergy to a medicine and showed the name saved on his phone to avoid a misunderstanding.","exampleZh":"他提到一种药物过敏，并展示手机保存的药名避免误会。","collocation":"mention an allergy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v6","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"insurance","zh":"保险","pos":"noun","example":"I found his travel insurance details while the leader arranged transport, rather than leaving him to manage everything alone.","exampleZh":"领队安排交通时我找到旅行保险资料，不让他独自处理一切。","collocation":"travel insurance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v7","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"instructions","zh":"指示","pos":"plural noun","example":"At the clinic, he asked for the instructions in writing because he was worried about forgetting important details later.","exampleZh":"在诊所他要求书面指示，担心稍后忘记重要细节。","collocation":"written instructions","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v8","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"repeat","zh":"重复","pos":"verb","example":"The nurse was willing to repeat unfamiliar words, and he checked his understanding by explaining the main points back.","exampleZh":"护士愿意重复生词，他通过复述要点核对理解。","collocation":"repeat a word","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v9","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"She also explained whom to contact if his condition changed, without expecting the tour leader to make a medical judgement.","exampleZh":"她还说明情况变化时联系谁，而不让领队作医学判断。","collocation":"contact the clinic","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v10","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recover","zh":"恢复","pos":"verb","example":"He stayed near the village to recover and followed the clinic’s advice instead of trying to finish the original route.","exampleZh":"他留在村附近恢复并遵从诊所建议，而非尝试走完原路线。","collocation":"recover gradually","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v11","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"support","zh":"支持；帮助","pos":"noun","example":"Practical support meant listening and helping him reach professional care, not guessing a diagnosis from our own experiences.","exampleZh":"实际帮助是倾听并协助就医，而非根据自身经验猜诊断。","collocation":"practical support","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-03:v12","level":"B1","chapter":15,"module":"b1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"purchase","zh":"购买的物品","pos":"noun","example":"I took a recent purchase back to the outdoor shop after discovering that its zip caught whenever I closed the jacket.","exampleZh":"我发现夹克拉链每次拉上会卡，就把最近购买的物品带回户外店。","collocation":"return a purchase","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v1","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"faulty","zh":"有故障的","pos":"adjective","example":"The assistant asked me to show why I thought the fastening was faulty rather than simply choosing the wrong size.","exampleZh":"店员让我展示为何认为扣件有问题，而非只是选错尺寸。","collocation":"faulty fastening","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v2","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"receipt","zh":"收据","pos":"noun","example":"I had kept the receipt, which showed the purchase date and made it easier to find the original transaction.","exampleZh":"我保留收据，上有购买日期，更容易找到原交易。","collocation":"keep the receipt","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v3","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exchange","zh":"换货","pos":"noun","example":"An exchange seemed practical because I still liked the jacket and wanted one that worked for the walking trip.","exampleZh":"换货似乎实际，因为我仍喜欢夹克，想要一件徒步时能正常用的。","collocation":"request an exchange","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v4","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replacement","zh":"替换品","pos":"noun","example":"Before accepting a replacement, I tested its zip several times and checked that the pocket seams were properly closed.","exampleZh":"接受替换品前，我多试几次拉链，并检查口袋缝线收好。","collocation":"check a replacement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v5","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stock","zh":"库存","pos":"noun","example":"The same colour was not in stock, but the shop could order it from another branch within four days.","exampleZh":"同色没有库存，但店里能四天内从另一分店调来。","collocation":"in stock","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v6","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止日期","pos":"noun","example":"My travel deadline was sooner than that, so waiting for the preferred colour would not meet my immediate need.","exampleZh":"我的出行时间更早，等待喜欢的颜色无法满足眼前需要。","collocation":"meet a deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v7","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refund","zh":"退款","pos":"noun","example":"The assistant explained a refund option, although I wanted to compare it with taking a different colour that afternoon.","exampleZh":"店员解释退款选项，不过我想与当天下午拿其他颜色比较。","collocation":"offer a refund","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v8","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"I asked whether the sale price changed any condition of the proposed exchange and listened carefully to the explanation.","exampleZh":"我问促销价是否改变换货条件，并仔细听解释。","collocation":"exchange condition","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v9","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision","zh":"决定","pos":"noun","example":"My decision depended on a working jacket for the trip, not on proving that every part of the shopping experience was bad.","exampleZh":"我的决定取决于旅途有能用的夹克，不是证明购物经历处处糟糕。","collocation":"make a decision","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v10","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"We agreed on another colour, and the assistant gave me a new receipt to confirm exactly what had been exchanged.","exampleZh":"我们同意换另一颜色，店员给新收据确认具体换了什么。","collocation":"confirm an exchange","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v11","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reasonable","zh":"合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reasonable solution came from describing the fault clearly and explaining the practical problem caused by the delivery times.","exampleZh":"合理解决来自清楚描述故障，并解释配送时间造成的实际困难。","collocation":"reasonable solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-04:v12","level":"B1","chapter":16,"module":"b1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"route","zh":"路线","pos":"noun","example":"A visitor asked me for a route to the museum while we were waiting outside the station.","exampleZh":"一位游客在车站外等候时向我问去博物馆的路线。","collocation":"plan a route","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v1","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entrance","zh":"入口","pos":"noun","example":"The nearest entrance was beside a narrow bridge, but I knew there were several steps beyond it.","exampleZh":"最近入口在窄桥旁，但我知道后面有几级台阶。","collocation":"nearest entrance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v2","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"wheelchair","zh":"轮椅","pos":"noun","example":"His daughter used a wheelchair, so I asked whether he wanted a step-free route before giving directions.","exampleZh":"他女儿用轮椅，所以指路前我问是否需要无台阶路线。","collocation":"use a wheelchair","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v3","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"crossing","zh":"过街处","pos":"noun","example":"I suggested the crossing with traffic lights rather than the shorter path through a busy car park.","exampleZh":"我建议走有信号灯的过街处，而非穿越繁忙停车场的近路。","collocation":"pedestrian crossing","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v4","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opposite","zh":"在对面","pos":"preposition","example":"The museum’s accessible gate was opposite the library, which was easy to recognise by its yellow doors.","exampleZh":"博物馆无障碍门在图书馆对面，黄门很容易认。","collocation":"opposite the library","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v5","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"straight ahead","zh":"一直向前","pos":"adverb phrase","example":"I told him to go straight ahead after the crossing until he reached the small public garden.","exampleZh":"我让他过马路后一直向前，到小公共花园。","collocation":"go straight ahead","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v6","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turn","zh":"转弯","pos":"verb","example":"There he should turn left, keeping the garden on his right until the library came into view.","exampleZh":"那里左转，把花园留在右侧，直到看到图书馆。","collocation":"turn left","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v7","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distance","zh":"距离","pos":"noun","example":"The extra distance was modest, although the route would probably take longer than the map first suggested.","exampleZh":"多走距离不大，不过路线可能比地图起初显示耗时长。","collocation":"extra distance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v8","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surface","zh":"路面","pos":"noun","example":"He asked about the surface because rough stones had made the previous afternoon’s walk surprisingly difficult.","exampleZh":"他问路面情况，因为昨天下午的粗石路让行走意外困难。","collocation":"smooth surface","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v9","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"check","zh":"核查","pos":"verb","example":"I offered to check the museum website in case construction had temporarily changed the accessible entrance.","exampleZh":"我提出查官网，以防施工临时改变无障碍入口。","collocation":"check the website","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v10","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"Before leaving, he repeated the main turns so I could explain anything that still sounded unclear.","exampleZh":"离开前他重复主要转弯，让我解释仍不清楚之处。","collocation":"explain a direction","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v11","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confident","zh":"有把握的","pos":"adjective","example":"He felt more confident with a suitable route than with a quick promise that everything was nearby.","exampleZh":"合适路线比一句一切就在附近的快速保证更让他有把握。","collocation":"feel confident","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-05:v12","level":"B1","chapter":17,"module":"b1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"introduce","zh":"介绍","pos":"verb","example":"At a neighbourhood meal, our host helped introduce people who had recently moved into the same apartment building.","exampleZh":"在邻里聚餐上，主人帮忙介绍刚搬进同栋公寓的人。","collocation":"introduce yourself","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v1","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interest","zh":"兴趣","pos":"noun","example":"I asked a neighbour about an interest she mentioned rather than immediately asking personal questions about her salary.","exampleZh":"我询问邻居提过的兴趣，而非马上打听私人薪水。","collocation":"shared interest","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v2","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mention","zh":"提到","pos":"verb","example":"She happened to mention photography, which gave me an easy way to continue the conversation without changing topics.","exampleZh":"她恰好提到摄影，让我能不换话题自然继续。","collocation":"mention a hobby","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v3","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recently","zh":"最近","pos":"adverb","example":"She had recently joined a weekend walking group and was learning to photograph birds near the river.","exampleZh":"她最近加入周末徒步组，正在学拍河边鸟类。","collocation":"recently joined","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v4","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"experience","zh":"经历","pos":"noun","example":"I shared an experience of trying to photograph a kingfisher, including the long wait and the disappointing result.","exampleZh":"我分享拍翠鸟的经历，包括漫长等候和失望结果。","collocation":"share an experience","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v5","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"curious","zh":"好奇的","pos":"adjective","example":"She was curious about the place, so I explained how to reach it without claiming to be an expert.","exampleZh":"她对地点好奇，所以我解释怎么到达，但没装专家。","collocation":"curious about","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v6","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interrupt","zh":"打断","pos":"verb","example":"When another guest joined us, I paused instead of continuing to interrupt with every detail of my story.","exampleZh":"另一客人加入时，我停下来，而非不断插入故事细节。","collocation":"interrupt a speaker","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v7","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"include","zh":"使参与","pos":"verb","example":"We tried to include him by explaining the topic and asking whether he enjoyed any outdoor activities himself.","exampleZh":"我们解释话题，问他是否喜欢户外活动，让他参与。","collocation":"include a newcomer","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v8","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prefer","zh":"更喜欢","pos":"verb","example":"He said he preferred indoor hobbies, so we did not pressure him to join a walk he clearly disliked.","exampleZh":"他说更喜欢室内爱好，所以我们没逼他参加明显不喜欢的徒步。","collocation":"prefer indoor activities","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v9","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggest","zh":"建议","pos":"verb","example":"Instead, he suggested a local exhibition where photographs of the river would be displayed the following month.","exampleZh":"他反而建议去当地展览，下月将展出河流照片。","collocation":"suggest an activity","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v10","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"invitation","zh":"邀请","pos":"noun","example":"His invitation gave us another chance to meet, but we checked dates before promising that everybody could attend.","exampleZh":"他的邀请让我们有机会再见，不过承诺所有人出席前先核对日期。","collocation":"accept an invitation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v11","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系方式","pos":"noun","example":"We exchanged contact details with permission and ended the conversation without expecting an immediate friendship from one pleasant meeting.","exampleZh":"我们经同意交换联系方式，不指望一次愉快见面就立刻成为好友。","collocation":"exchange contact details","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-06:v12","level":"B1","chapter":18,"module":"b1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"goal","zh":"目标","pos":"noun","example":"When Luis began evening classes, his goal was to discuss simple work problems rather than collect perfect exercise scores.","exampleZh":"Luis 开始夜校时，目标是讨论简单工作问题，而非收集完美习题分数。","collocation":"set a goal","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v1","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"schedule","zh":"时间安排","pos":"noun","example":"His schedule included twenty minutes after breakfast, a time that was easier to protect than late evening study.","exampleZh":"他安排早餐后学二十分钟，比深夜学习更容易保证。","collocation":"study schedule","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v2","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"manage","zh":"设法做到","pos":"verb","example":"He could manage short reading tasks, but he often postponed speaking because nobody was available to practise with him.","exampleZh":"他能完成短阅读，却因无人陪练常推迟口语。","collocation":"manage a task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v3","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"record","zh":"记录","pos":"verb","example":"His teacher suggested that he record a short explanation first and listen for places where the meaning became unclear.","exampleZh":"老师建议先录简短解释，听哪些地方意思变不清。","collocation":"record an explanation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v4","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feedback","zh":"反馈","pos":"noun","example":"Useful feedback came from a partner who identified one confusing sentence instead of listing every small mistake at once.","exampleZh":"有用反馈来自同伴指出一句难懂的话，而非一次列全部小错。","collocation":"useful feedback","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v5","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"improve","zh":"改善","pos":"verb","example":"Luis tried to improve that sentence by adding a clear reason and replacing a word he did not fully understand.","exampleZh":"Luis 加清楚理由并替换不完全理解的词，改善那句话。","collocation":"improve a sentence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v6","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"progress","zh":"进展","pos":"noun","example":"He checked his progress with a new topic a week later rather than repeating the same answer until it sounded smooth.","exampleZh":"一周后他用新主题检查进展，而非反复同一答案至流畅。","collocation":"check progress","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v7","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"habit","zh":"习惯","pos":"noun","example":"The habit of reviewing a little each day helped him remember phrases that he had previously forgotten after class.","exampleZh":"每天稍复习的习惯让他记住以前下课就忘的短语。","collocation":"daily habit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v8","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"challenge","zh":"挑战","pos":"noun","example":"An unexpected question remained a challenge, so he practised asking people to clarify what they wanted to know.","exampleZh":"意外问题仍是挑战，所以他练习请人澄清想知道什么。","collocation":"face a challenge","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v9","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"realistic","zh":"现实可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A realistic plan also allowed breaks when family responsibilities made study difficult, instead of treating every missed session as failure.","exampleZh":"现实计划也在家庭责任使学习困难时允许休息，不把每次缺课当失败。","collocation":"realistic plan","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v10","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjust","zh":"调整","pos":"verb","example":"He would adjust the following week’s workload without trying to complete several hours of exercises in one tired evening.","exampleZh":"他调整下周任务量，不在疲惫晚间赶数小时习题。","collocation":"adjust the workload","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v11","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"independent","zh":"独立的","pos":"adjective","example":"By the end of term, an independent conversation showed which parts of his learning had become useful beyond the classroom.","exampleZh":"学期末独立对话显示哪些学习已能在课堂外使用。","collocation":"independent conversation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-07:v12","level":"B1","chapter":19,"module":"b1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"task","zh":"任务","pos":"noun","example":"On my first week at the visitor centre, I received a task to prepare signs for a weekend exhibition.","exampleZh":"在游客中心第一周，我接到准备周末展览标牌的任务。","collocation":"complete a task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v1","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responsibility","zh":"责任","pos":"noun","example":"My responsibility covered the wording, but another colleague would check the facts and arrange the final printing.","exampleZh":"我负责措辞，另一同事核查事实并安排最终印刷。","collocation":"share responsibility","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v2","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadline","zh":"截止时间","pos":"noun","example":"The deadline was Thursday afternoon, although I needed the photographs before I could decide how much text would fit.","exampleZh":"截止为周四下午，不过我需先有照片才能决定容纳多少文字。","collocation":"meet a deadline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v3","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priority","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"My manager explained that clear directions were the first priority, while background information could be shortened if space was limited.","exampleZh":"经理说清晰指引优先，背景信息在空间不足时可缩短。","collocation":"first priority","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v4","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"update","zh":"最新进展","pos":"noun","example":"I sent a brief update on Tuesday, stating what was finished and which photographs were still missing from the folder.","exampleZh":"周二我简报已完内容及文件夹仍缺哪些照片。","collocation":"send an update","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v5","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"delay","zh":"延误","pos":"noun","example":"A delay in one image did not need to stop the whole project, provided we agreed on a temporary layout.","exampleZh":"只要同意临时版式，一张图片延误不必阻止整个项目。","collocation":"avoid a delay","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v6","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permission","zh":"许可","pos":"noun","example":"Before using an older photograph, I asked for permission because I did not know whether its licence covered this exhibition.","exampleZh":"用旧照片前我请求许可，因为不知道授权是否覆盖本展。","collocation":"ask for permission","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v7","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"colleague","zh":"同事","pos":"noun","example":"A colleague suggested a simpler diagram, which communicated the location more clearly than the photograph we had been waiting for.","exampleZh":"同事建议更简单示意图，比等待中的照片更清楚传达位置。","collocation":"ask a colleague","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v8","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revise","zh":"修改","pos":"verb","example":"I could revise the layout that morning, but I needed the fact checker to approve the new labels before printing.","exampleZh":"我上午能修改版式，但印前需核查人批准新标签。","collocation":"revise a layout","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v9","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"We agreed to confirm the final version by email so nobody would accidentally print a draft from the shared folder.","exampleZh":"我们同意邮件确认最终版，防止误印共享夹草稿。","collocation":"confirm the final version","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v10","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reliable","zh":"可靠的","pos":"adjective","example":"Reliable teamwork meant reporting difficulties early rather than promising that everything was fine until the last possible moment.","exampleZh":"可靠合作意味着及早报困难，而非到最后仍承诺一切正常。","collocation":"reliable teamwork","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v11","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"solution","zh":"解决办法","pos":"noun","example":"The solution emerged because each person understood the goal, their own role and when another person’s decision was needed.","exampleZh":"解决办法出现是因为人人懂目标、自身角色及何时需他人决策。","collocation":"find a solution","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-08:v12","level":"B1","chapter":20,"module":"b1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"headline","zh":"标题","pos":"noun","example":"A headline in our neighbourhood group claimed that the swimming pool would close permanently at the end of summer.","exampleZh":"邻里群标题说游泳池夏末永久关闭。","collocation":"read a headline","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v1","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"source","zh":"来源","pos":"noun","example":"Before sharing the message, I looked for its source and found that it referred to an old council discussion.","exampleZh":"转发前我查来源，发现它指向旧议会讨论。","collocation":"check a source","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v2","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"statement","zh":"声明","pos":"noun","example":"The current statement described a temporary closure for repairs, not a decision to remove the pool from the town.","exampleZh":"当前声明描述临时维修关闭，不是决定取消泳池。","collocation":"official statement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v3","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"There was no evidence in the document for the claim that every member would lose their remaining fees.","exampleZh":"文件没有证据支持所有会员剩余费用将损失的说法。","collocation":"look for evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v4","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"temporary","zh":"临时的","pos":"adjective","example":"The temporary arrangement allowed members to use another centre, although they needed to book sessions through a different website.","exampleZh":"临时安排允许会员用另一中心，不过需通过另一网站预约。","collocation":"temporary arrangement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v5","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"detail","zh":"细节","pos":"noun","example":"One important detail remained unclear: whether the alternative centre would offer suitable times for people working late shifts.","exampleZh":"一个重要细节仍不清：替代中心是否有适合夜班人员的时段。","collocation":"important detail","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v6","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contact","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"I decided to contact the council directly instead of assuming that a missing detail meant the entire plan was dishonest.","exampleZh":"我决定直接联系议会，而非假定缺细节就说明整个方案不诚实。","collocation":"contact an organisation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v7","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reply","zh":"回复","pos":"noun","example":"Their reply explained that the timetable was still being negotiated and would be published before the repair work began.","exampleZh":"回复说时刻表仍在协商，会在维修开始前公布。","collocation":"receive a reply","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v8","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"claim","zh":"声称","pos":"noun","example":"I shared a short correction that separated the confirmed information from the original claim about permanent closure.","exampleZh":"我分享简短更正，将已确认信息与原先永久关闭说法分开。","collocation":"check a claim","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v9","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertain","zh":"不确定的","pos":"adjective","example":"I also mentioned the uncertain timetable, because a correction should not pretend that every practical question has already been answered.","exampleZh":"我也提到不确定时刻表，因为更正不应假装所有实际问题都已解答。","collocation":"remain uncertain","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v10","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reliable","zh":"可靠的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reliable summary needed both the date of the statement and a link that neighbours could check for themselves.","exampleZh":"可靠摘要需要声明日期及邻居可自行核查链接。","collocation":"reliable summary","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v11","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"update","zh":"更新","pos":"noun","example":"When a later update appeared, we could compare it with the earlier plan rather than spread another dramatic message without context.","exampleZh":"后续更新出现时，我们能比较原计划，而非无背景传播另一耸动消息。","collocation":"publish an update","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-09:v12","level":"B1","chapter":21,"module":"b1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opinion","zh":"观点","pos":"noun","example":"In our class, I expressed an opinion about replacing one written homework task each week with a practical group activity.","exampleZh":"课堂上，我表达了对每周用实践小组活动替换一次书面作业的观点。","collocation":"express an opinion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v1","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"benefit","zh":"好处","pos":"noun","example":"One benefit would be learning to explain ideas to classmates instead of completing every exercise alone at home.","exampleZh":"一个好处是学向同学解释想法，而非在家独自做每题。","collocation":"possible benefit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v2","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reason","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"I gave a reason for supporting the change: different explanations can reveal misunderstandings that a correct answer sometimes hides.","exampleZh":"我给支持理由：不同解释能揭示正确答案有时掩盖的误解。","collocation":"give a reason","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v3","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"example","zh":"例子","pos":"noun","example":"As an example, our science group had discovered a mistake only when we tried to explain an experiment together.","exampleZh":"例如，我们科学小组一起解释实验时才发现一个错误。","collocation":"give an example","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v4","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concern","zh":"担忧","pos":"noun","example":"Another student raised a concern that confident speakers might do most of the work while quieter members watched.","exampleZh":"另一学生担心自信发言者可能做大部分工作，而安静者只观看。","collocation":"raise a concern","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v5","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair","zh":"公平的","pos":"adjective","example":"That objection seemed fair, so I suggested giving each person a clear role and time to prepare independently first.","exampleZh":"这个反对合理，所以我建议各有明确角色并先独立准备。","collocation":"fair objection","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v6","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depend","zh":"取决于","pos":"verb","example":"The value of group work would depend on its organisation, not simply on whether students sat around one table.","exampleZh":"小组学习价值取决于组织，而不只是否围同桌坐。","collocation":"depend on organisation","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v7","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agree","zh":"同意","pos":"verb","example":"We could agree about the need for individual responsibility even though we preferred different kinds of homework ourselves.","exampleZh":"即使我们偏爱不同作业，也能同意需个人责任。","collocation":"agree about a need","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v8","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disagree","zh":"不同意","pos":"verb","example":"It was easier to disagree politely when we discussed a specific arrangement rather than describing each other as lazy.","exampleZh":"讨论具体安排而非指对方懒惰时，更容易礼貌反对。","collocation":"disagree politely","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v9","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"balance","zh":"平衡","pos":"noun","example":"A sensible balance might include a short personal reflection after the practical task, showing what each student had learned.","exampleZh":"合理平衡可在实践后加简短个人反思，显示各人所得。","collocation":"sensible balance","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v10","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggestion","zh":"建议","pos":"noun","example":"Our final suggestion was a small trial, followed by feedback from students who enjoyed the activity and those who did not.","exampleZh":"最终建议先小规模试行，再收集喜欢和不喜欢者反馈。","collocation":"make a suggestion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v11","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conclusion","zh":"结论","pos":"noun","example":"My conclusion supported the idea under clear conditions, rather than claiming that one teaching method would suit every subject.","exampleZh":"我结论在明确条件下支持，而非声称一种教学法适合所有科目。","collocation":"reach a conclusion","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-10:v12","level":"B1","chapter":22,"module":"b1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"instruction","zh":"指令","pos":"noun","example":"Before a timed practice task, our tutor asked us to read the instruction and identify exactly what we had to produce.","exampleZh":"限时练习前，导师让我们读指令并确定须完成什么。","collocation":"read an instruction","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v1","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"purpose","zh":"目的","pos":"noun","example":"The purpose was to recommend a suitable activity for visitors, not to describe every activity listed on the page.","exampleZh":"目的是为游客推荐合适活动，而非描述页面全部活动。","collocation":"identify the purpose","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v2","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"A short time limit meant we needed to choose relevant details rather than copy the whole information sheet into our notes.","exampleZh":"时限短意味着需选相关细节，而非把整张信息抄入笔记。","collocation":"time limit","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v3","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"skim","zh":"略读","pos":"verb","example":"I learned to skim the text first so I could understand its structure before searching for a particular detail.","exampleZh":"我学会先略读，明白结构后再找具体细节。","collocation":"skim a text","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v4","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compare","zh":"比较","pos":"verb","example":"Then I would compare the visitor’s needs with each option, checking cost and access instead of following the most attractive photograph.","exampleZh":"然后把游客需求与各选项比较，查费用通行而非跟最漂亮照片。","collocation":"compare options","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v5","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"依据","pos":"noun","example":"For each answer, I marked a small piece of evidence that explained why one choice fitted better than the others.","exampleZh":"每个答案我标一小处依据，说明为何比其他选项合适。","collocation":"find evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v6","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"guess","zh":"猜测","pos":"verb","example":"If I had to guess, I noted the uncertainty so I could review the problem honestly after the timer stopped.","exampleZh":"若不得不猜，我记下不确定性，计时结束后诚实复查。","collocation":"make a guess","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v7","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"check","zh":"检查","pos":"verb","example":"I saved a few minutes to check that my final recommendation answered the question and did not contradict an important condition.","exampleZh":"我留几分钟检查最终建议回应问题且不违背重要条件。","collocation":"check an answer","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v8","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mistake","zh":"错误","pos":"noun","example":"A repeated mistake often showed a weak habit, such as ignoring a negative word or choosing a familiar expression without context.","exampleZh":"重复错误常显示坏习惯，如忽视否定词或不看语境选熟语。","collocation":"repeated mistake","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v9","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"review","zh":"复查","pos":"verb","example":"Afterwards, I would review the reasoning, not just count how many answers matched the key and close the book.","exampleZh":"之后我复查推理，而不只是数对了几个就合书。","collocation":"review the reasoning","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v10","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"strategy","zh":"策略","pos":"noun","example":"A useful strategy reduced wasted effort, but it could not replace the language knowledge needed to understand the task.","exampleZh":"有效策略减少浪费，却不能替代理解任务所需语言知识。","collocation":"useful strategy","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v11","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confidence","zh":"信心","pos":"noun","example":"My confidence became more realistic when I could explain both why an answer worked and why an attractive alternative did not.","exampleZh":"能解释答案及诱人干扰项为何不成立时，我信心更现实。","collocation":"build confidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-11:v12","level":"B1","chapter":23,"module":"b1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"independent","zh":"独立的","pos":"adjective","example":"For our final project, each learner had to plan an independent visit for a guest with particular interests and needs.","exampleZh":"最终项目要求每人独立为有具体兴趣需求的客人规划参观。","collocation":"independent task","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v1","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"information","zh":"信息","pos":"noun","example":"We received information about several places, including opening times, transport links and a few conditions that were easy to overlook.","exampleZh":"我们收到几个地点的信息，包括开放时间、交通及易忽略条件。","collocation":"useful information","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v2","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"select","zh":"选择","pos":"verb","example":"I had to select details relevant to my guest rather than repeat every sentence from the descriptions in my presentation.","exampleZh":"我需选与客人相关细节，而非在展示中重复介绍每句。","collocation":"select details","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v3","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"organise","zh":"组织","pos":"verb","example":"I decided to organise the route around two nearby places, leaving enough time for a quiet lunch between them.","exampleZh":"我围绕两个邻近地点组织路线，中间留安静午餐时间。","collocation":"organise a route","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v4","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"explain","zh":"解释","pos":"verb","example":"My partner asked me to explain why I had rejected a cheaper option that looked more exciting in its advertisement.","exampleZh":"同伴让我解释为何放弃广告看起来更精彩的便宜选项。","collocation":"explain a choice","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v5","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"The important condition was a steep staircase, which would make that option unsuitable for the guest I was helping.","exampleZh":"重要条件是陡楼梯，对我帮助的客人不合适。","collocation":"important condition","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v6","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"clarify","zh":"澄清","pos":"verb","example":"When my partner misunderstood the lunch arrangement, I had to clarify whether the price included food or only admission.","exampleZh":"同伴误解午餐安排时，我须澄清费用含餐还是仅门票。","collocation":"clarify a detail","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v7","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adapt","zh":"调整以适应","pos":"verb","example":"A surprise closure forced me to adapt the afternoon plan without abandoning the guest’s main interest in local history.","exampleZh":"意外关闭迫使我调整下午计划，但不放弃客人对当地历史的核心兴趣。","collocation":"adapt a plan","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v8","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"The teacher collected evidence from our reading notes, written message and conversation instead of judging the polished presentation alone.","exampleZh":"老师从阅读笔记、书面消息及对话收证，而非只判成熟展示。","collocation":"collect evidence","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v9","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"strength","zh":"优势","pos":"noun","example":"My main strength was explaining reasons clearly, while quick listening questions still required more practice with unfamiliar speakers.","exampleZh":"我主要优势是清楚解释理由，而快速听力题仍需练陌生说话者。","collocation":"identify a strength","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v10","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"improvement","zh":"改进","pos":"noun","example":"We each chose one improvement for the next fortnight and arranged a delayed check using a completely different visitor problem.","exampleZh":"我们各选未来两周一个改进，并用完全不同游客问题安排延迟检验。","collocation":"plan an improvement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v11","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"achievement","zh":"成就","pos":"noun","example":"Finishing the project marked an achievement, but the evidence described specific abilities rather than promising effortless communication in every situation.","exampleZh":"完成项目是成就，但证据描述具体能力，不承诺任何情境轻松交流。","collocation":"recognise an achievement","register":"neutral / 中性日常表达","id":"b1-12:v12","level":"B1","chapter":24,"module":"b1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"set a realistic deadline","zh":"设定期限","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We need to set a realistic deadline.","exampleZh":"我们需要设定一个现实的期限。","collocation":"set a realistic deadline","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v1","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"took responsibility","zh":"承担责任","pos":"verb phrase","example":"She took responsibility for checking the wiring.","exampleZh":"她承担了检查电线的责任。","collocation":"take responsibility for","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v2","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"an insight into","zh":"深入了解","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The survey gave us an insight into local needs.","exampleZh":"调查让我们了解了当地需求。","collocation":"an insight into local needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v3","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pay close attention to","zh":"注意","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Please pay close attention to the safety instructions.","exampleZh":"请仔细注意安全说明。","collocation":"pay close attention to","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v4","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"carried out a survey","zh":"开展调查","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The volunteers carried out a survey before opening.","exampleZh":"志愿者在开办前进行了调查。","collocation":"carry out a survey","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v5","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"guarantee","zh":"保证","pos":"verb","example":"We cannot guarantee that every repair will succeed.","exampleZh":"我们不能保证每次修理都成功。","collocation":"guarantee that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v6","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"made a considerable difference","zh":"产生影响","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Your description made a considerable difference to our preparation.","exampleZh":"你的描述对我们的准备产生了相当大的影响。","collocation":"make a considerable difference","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v7","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"took","zh":"在本句中与 into account 搭配表示考虑了","pos":"verb","example":"We took the owner’s personal story into account.","exampleZh":"我们考虑了物主的个人故事。","collocation":"take a story into account","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v8","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draw attention away","zh":"转移注意力","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Repeated questions can draw attention away from a difficult repair.","exampleZh":"反复提问可能转移对困难修理工作的注意力。","collocation":"draw attention away from","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v9","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"met her needs","zh":"满足需求","pos":"verb phrase","example":"A replacement would not have met her needs.","exampleZh":"替代品不会满足她的需求。","collocation":"meet someone’s needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v10","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"drew a distinction","zh":"作出区分","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We drew a distinction between market value and personal meaning.","exampleZh":"我们区分了市场价值与个人意义。","collocation":"draw a distinction between","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v11","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"played a part","zh":"发挥作用","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Patient listening played a part in the café’s success.","exampleZh":"耐心倾听是修理咖啡馆成功的因素之一。","collocation":"play a part in","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-01:v12","level":"B2","chapter":25,"module":"b2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"financially","zh":"在财务上","pos":"adverb","example":"The first proposal was financially unrealistic.","exampleZh":"第一份方案在财务上不现实。","collocation":"financially unrealistic","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v1","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accessibility","zh":"可达性","pos":"noun","example":"We checked the accessibility of the roof.","exampleZh":"我们检查了屋顶是否便于到达。","collocation":"accessibility of the roof","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v2","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"participation","zh":"参与","pos":"noun","example":"Participation in the garden project is voluntary.","exampleZh":"参与花园项目是自愿的。","collocation":"voluntary participation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v3","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revised","zh":"修订过的","pos":"adjective","example":"The revised instructions were easier to follow.","exampleZh":"修改后的说明更容易遵循。","collocation":"revised instructions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v4","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unevenly","zh":"不均匀地","pos":"adverb","example":"The plants grew unevenly near the wall.","exampleZh":"靠墙的植物长得不均匀。","collocation":"grow unevenly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v5","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confidence","zh":"信心","pos":"noun","example":"Her confidence increased after teaching other students.","exampleZh":"教过其他学生后，她的信心增强了。","collocation":"increase confidence","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v6","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confusion","zh":"困惑","pos":"noun","example":"The watering record reduced unnecessary confusion.","exampleZh":"浇水记录减少了不必要的困惑。","collocation":"unnecessary confusion","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v7","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accurately","zh":"准确地","pos":"adverb","example":"Students accurately recorded when each container was watered.","exampleZh":"学生准确记录了每个花盆何时浇水。","collocation":"record accurately","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v8","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rapidly","zh":"迅速地","pos":"adverb","example":"Small plants can dry rapidly in windy conditions.","exampleZh":"小植物在风中可能迅速变干。","collocation":"dry rapidly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v9","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inaccessible","zh":"不便到达的","pos":"adjective","example":"The original roof was inaccessible to ordinary visitors.","exampleZh":"原来的屋顶不便普通访客到达。","collocation":"inaccessible to visitors","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v10","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"understanding","zh":"理解","pos":"noun","example":"Understanding the soil helped beginners care for vegetables.","exampleZh":"理解土壤有助于新手照顾蔬菜。","collocation":"understanding the soil","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v11","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preparation","zh":"准备","pos":"noun","example":"Careful preparation made the revised proposal more practical.","exampleZh":"仔细准备使修订方案更实际。","collocation":"careful preparation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-02:v12","level":"B2","chapter":26,"module":"b2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consumption","zh":"消费","pos":"noun","example":"Shared equipment can reduce unnecessary consumption.","exampleZh":"共享设备能减少不必要的消费。","collocation":"unnecessary consumption","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v1","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ownership","zh":"所有权","pos":"noun","example":"Access may matter more than ownership.","exampleZh":"使用权可能比所有权更重要。","collocation":"access rather than ownership","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v2","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance","zh":"维护","pos":"noun","example":"We must consider the cost of maintenance.","exampleZh":"我们必须考虑维护成本。","collocation":"cost of maintenance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v3","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"resources","zh":"资源","pos":"noun","example":"The booking system distributes limited resources fairly.","exampleZh":"预约系统公平分配有限资源。","collocation":"limited resources","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v4","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"durable","zh":"耐用的","pos":"adjective","example":"A durable product should remain useful for years.","exampleZh":"耐用产品应该多年仍可使用。","collocation":"durable product","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v5","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"affordable","zh":"负担得起的","pos":"adjective","example":"The scheme offers an affordable alternative.","exampleZh":"该计划提供了价格可承受的替代选择。","collocation":"affordable alternative","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v6","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evidence","zh":"证据","pos":"noun","example":"There is little evidence of a long term benefit yet.","exampleZh":"目前几乎没有长期收益的证据。","collocation":"little evidence","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v7","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"households","zh":"家庭住户","pos":"noun","example":"Several households have joined the scheme.","exampleZh":"几个家庭已加入该计划。","collocation":"several households","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v8","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"availability","zh":"可用性","pos":"noun","example":"We need reliable information about availability.","exampleZh":"我们需要可靠的可用情况信息。","collocation":"information about availability","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v9","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"impact","zh":"影响","pos":"noun","example":"The environmental impact depends on how people travel.","exampleZh":"环境影响取决于人们如何出行。","collocation":"environmental impact","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v10","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outweigh","zh":"超过","pos":"verb","example":"The benefits should outweigh the practical disadvantages.","exampleZh":"收益应该超过实际弊端。","collocation":"benefits outweigh disadvantages","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v11","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transparent","zh":"透明的","pos":"adjective","example":"Publishing the costs makes the process more transparent.","exampleZh":"公开成本使过程更加透明。","collocation":"transparent process","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-03:v12","level":"B2","chapter":27,"module":"b2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposed","zh":"提议","pos":"verb","example":"The former railway station stood empty for years before a local group proposed a shared workspace.","exampleZh":"旧火车站空置多年后，一个当地团体提议把它变成共享工作场所。","collocation":"propose a workspace","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v1","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"potential","zh":"潜在的","pos":"adjective","example":"Rather than assume that everyone needed a private office, the organisers interviewed potential users about their routines.","exampleZh":"组织者没有假定人人都需要独立办公室，而是访谈潜在使用者的工作习惯。","collocation":"potential users","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v2","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"whereas","zh":"然而","pos":"conjunction","example":"Freelance designers wanted quiet desks, whereas people running small shops mainly needed a place for occasional meetings.","exampleZh":"自由设计师需要安静书桌，而小店经营者主要需要偶尔开会的地方。","collocation":"whereas others","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v3","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contrasting","zh":"形成对照的","pos":"adjective","example":"These contrasting needs persuaded the group to divide the building into several areas with different booking rules.","exampleZh":"这些不同需求促使团队把建筑分成几个预约规则不同的区域。","collocation":"contrasting needs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v4","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suitable","zh":"适合的","pos":"adjective","example":"The ticket office became a silent room, while the waiting hall was suitable for conversations and shared equipment.","exampleZh":"售票处变成了安静室，候车厅则适合交流和共享设备。","collocation":"suitable for","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v5","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"discourage","zh":"使失去意愿","pos":"verb","example":"Despite limited funding, the group invested in good lighting because poor conditions would discourage people from returning.","exampleZh":"尽管资金有限，团队仍投入优质照明，因为不好的环境会使人不再来。","collocation":"discourage people from returning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v6","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"covered","zh":"支付得起","pos":"verb","example":"Membership fees covered ordinary running costs, but major repairs still required support from the council and local businesses.","exampleZh":"会员费承担日常运营成本，但大修仍需议会及当地企业支持。","collocation":"cover running costs","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v7","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reserve","zh":"预订","pos":"verb","example":"At first, several members complained that the booking website allowed some people to reserve too many afternoons.","exampleZh":"起初，几位成员抱怨预约网站允许部分人预订过多下午时段。","collocation":"reserve an afternoon","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v8","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responded","zh":"回应","pos":"verb","example":"The organisers responded by introducing a weekly limit rather than blaming members for using the existing system.","exampleZh":"组织者通过增加每周上限回应，而不是责怪成员使用现有系统。","collocation":"respond by introducing","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v9","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"evenly","zh":"均匀地","pos":"adverb","example":"Within three months, bookings were distributed more evenly, although the most convenient times remained particularly popular with commuters.","exampleZh":"三个月内预约分配更均衡了，不过通勤者仍特别喜欢最方便的时段。","collocation":"distribute evenly","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v10","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depended less on","zh":"取决于","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The project’s success depended less on fashionable furniture than on matching practical arrangements to the way members actually worked.","exampleZh":"项目成功与时髦家具关系较小，更多取决于实际安排符合成员工作方式。","collocation":"depend less on","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v11","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consultation","zh":"征询意见","pos":"noun","example":"Its report recommended regular consultation, recognising that a flexible service must continue adapting as the community and its working habits change.","exampleZh":"报告建议定期征求意见，认识到灵活服务必须随社区和工作习惯变化持续调整。","collocation":"regular consultation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-04:v12","level":"B2","chapter":28,"module":"b2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"headlines","zh":"新闻标题","pos":"noun","example":"When the night train returned to our region, newspaper headlines described it as a complete solution to uncomfortable travel.","exampleZh":"当夜间列车回到本地区，报纸标题称它是解决出行不适的完整方案。","collocation":"newspaper headlines","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v1","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"complicated","zh":"复杂的","pos":"adjective","example":"My first journey suggested a more complicated picture, although I was pleased to reach the city without losing a working day.","exampleZh":"首次旅程让我看到更复杂的情况，虽然我很高兴没损失一个工作日就到达城市。","collocation":"complicated picture","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v2","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compartment","zh":"隔间","pos":"noun","example":"The compartment was quiet enough for reading, but the movement made deep sleep difficult for passengers unfamiliar with overnight trains.","exampleZh":"车厢安静到可以阅读，但晃动让不习惯夜车的乘客难以熟睡。","collocation":"quiet compartment","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v3","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rested","zh":"休息充分的","pos":"adjective","example":"A fellow traveller explained that saving a hotel bill mattered more to her than arriving completely rested for a holiday.","exampleZh":"一位同行旅客解释，对她而言省住宿费比度假时精神饱满地到达更重要。","collocation":"arrive rested","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v4","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trade off","zh":"取舍","pos":"noun","example":"For a businessman preparing to give a presentation, however, the same trade off might be much less attractive or practical.","exampleZh":"但对准备演讲的商务人士，同样的取舍可能远不那么有吸引力或实用。","collocation":"make a trade off","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v5","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivalent","zh":"等同的","pos":"adjective","example":"Ticket prices were lower when booked early, so comparisons with flights needed to use equivalent dates and booking conditions.","exampleZh":"提前订票更便宜，因此与飞机比较必须使用相同日期及订票条件。","collocation":"equivalent dates","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v6","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"calculated","zh":"计算","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"The operator advertised reduced environmental impact, but its short publicity leaflet did not explain how the figures had been calculated.","exampleZh":"运营方宣传环境影响降低，但简短宣传页没有解释数字如何计算。","collocation":"calculate figures","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v7","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"absence","zh":"缺少","pos":"noun","example":"This absence did not prove the claim false; it meant that a careful reader needed additional information before accepting it.","exampleZh":"缺少解释并不证明主张错误，而是意味着审慎读者需更多信息再接受它。","collocation":"absence of detail","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v8","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"connection","zh":"接驳","pos":"noun","example":"At the destination, a convenient tram connection helped passengers avoid a costly taxi ride from an airport far outside town.","exampleZh":"到站后方便的电车接驳让乘客避免从远郊机场搭昂贵出租车。","collocation":"tram connection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v9","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"advantage","zh":"优势","pos":"noun","example":"That advantage depended on where travellers actually needed to go, rather than on the station’s central location in itself.","exampleZh":"这一优势取决于旅客实际目的地，而不只是车站本身处于市中心。","collocation":"practical advantage","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v10","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"options","zh":"选择","pos":"noun","example":"I would choose the train again for a relaxed visit, while considering other options before an important morning meeting.","exampleZh":"悠闲旅行我会再选火车，但重要早会前会考虑其他选择。","collocation":"consider options","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v11","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priorities","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"The service expands our choices, yet its usefulness cannot be judged accurately without considering the priorities and circumstances of different passengers.","exampleZh":"该服务增加了选择，但不考虑不同乘客的重点与情况就不能准确判断用途。","collocation":"different priorities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-05:v12","level":"B2","chapter":29,"module":"b2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"restoring","zh":"修复","pos":"verb","example":"At the first meeting about restoring a woodland path, volunteers disagreed about whether visitors needed more information or fewer signs.","exampleZh":"第一次修复林间小径会议上，志愿者对游客需要更多信息还是更少标牌意见不一。","collocation":"restore a path","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v1","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revealed","zh":"揭示","pos":"verb","example":"The discussion initially seemed to concern appearance, but it soon revealed different ideas about who would use the route.","exampleZh":"讨论起初似乎关于外观，很快却显出大家对路线使用者的不同设想。","collocation":"reveal different ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v2","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reassurance","zh":"安心确认","pos":"noun","example":"Regular walkers already recognised the turns, whereas families visiting for the first time wanted reassurance that they were not lost.","exampleZh":"常客认识转弯处，而首次来的家庭想确认自己没走丢。","collocation":"want reassurance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v3","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"marker","zh":"标记","pos":"noun","example":"This difference explained why an apparently unnecessary marker could still be valuable to somebody with less local knowledge.","exampleZh":"这种差异解释了为何看似多余的标记对不熟悉当地的人仍有价值。","collocation":"unnecessary marker","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v4","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preference","zh":"偏好","pos":"noun","example":"Rather than choose one group’s preference, the team walked the path with visitors who had never seen it before.","exampleZh":"团队没有只选一组人的偏好，而是和从未见过路线的游客一起实走。","collocation":"group’s preference","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v5","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concealed","zh":"遮挡","pos":"verb","example":"At a fallen tree, everyone paused because the original route continued behind branches that concealed the next turning point.","exampleZh":"在倒树处大家都停了，因为原路线在遮住下个弯的树枝后延伸。","collocation":"conceal a turning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v6","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"navigation","zh":"路线辨识","pos":"noun","example":"The team cleared those branches first, which solved a navigation problem without adding another object to the landscape.","exampleZh":"团队先清理树枝，不必再向景观增加物品就解决了认路问题。","collocation":"navigation problem","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v7","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"junction","zh":"岔口","pos":"noun","example":"Further along, however, two equally visible paths led in different directions, so a small arrow remained necessary at that junction.","exampleZh":"但更前方两条同样明显的小路通向不同方向，所以岔口仍需小箭头。","collocation":"at a junction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v8","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"destination","zh":"目的地","pos":"noun","example":"Its wording referred to the destination rather than merely saying continue, allowing walkers to connect the instruction with their map.","exampleZh":"标牌写目的地而不只写继续，让步行者能将指引与地图对应。","collocation":"refer to a destination","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v9","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surface","zh":"表面","pos":"noun","example":"After the changes, visitors made fewer wrong turns, although heavy rain later created a separate problem with the muddy surface.","exampleZh":"修改后游客走错更少，尽管后来大雨又造成泥泞路面这一独立问题。","collocation":"muddy surface","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v10","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance plan","zh":"维护计划","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The final maintenance plan therefore separated tasks concerned with understanding the route from tasks concerned with moving along it safely.","exampleZh":"最终维护计划因而把理解路线的任务与安全行走的任务分开。","collocation":"final maintenance plan","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v11","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinction","zh":"区分","pos":"noun","example":"This distinction helped volunteers agree on priorities, because solving a communication problem would not automatically repair a damaged physical path.","exampleZh":"这一区分帮助志愿者确定重点，因为解决沟通问题不会自动修好损坏的路。","collocation":"draw a distinction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-06:v12","level":"B2","chapter":30,"module":"b2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agreed","zh":"同意","pos":"verb","example":"I agreed to help record our neighbourhood podcast because I thought speaking into a microphone would be the easy part.","exampleZh":"我答应帮忙录社区播客，因为我以为对着麦克风说话是容易的部分。","collocation":"agree to help","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v1","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"to be honest","zh":"坦白说","pos":"discourse phrase","example":"To be honest, I was far less comfortable when I heard my first recording and noticed how quickly I interrupted people.","exampleZh":"说实话，听到第一次录音并发现自己多快打断别人时，我很不自在。","collocation":"to be honest","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v2","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"promising","zh":"有潜力的","pos":"adjective","example":"My colleague said the energy was promising, which sounded encouraging until she added that the guests hardly finished a sentence.","exampleZh":"同事说活力不错，听起来很鼓舞，直到她补充嘉宾几乎说不完一句话。","collocation":"promising energy","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v3","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"worthwhile","zh":"值得的","pos":"adjective","example":"She was not asking me to sound less interested; she wanted me to leave enough space for a worthwhile answer.","exampleZh":"她不是让我听起来没兴趣，而是希望我留出足够空间得到有价值的回答。","collocation":"worthwhile answer","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v4","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"follow up","zh":"追问","pos":"noun phrase","example":"At the next interview, I prepared fewer questions and listened for points that could lead naturally to a follow up.","exampleZh":"下次访谈我准备更少问题，留意能自然引出追问的细节。","collocation":"natural follow up","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v5","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"collect her thoughts","zh":"整理思路","pos":"verb phrase","example":"There were uncomfortable pauses, although the guest later told me that those moments had helped her collect her thoughts.","exampleZh":"中间有令人不自在的停顿，不过嘉宾后来告诉我那些时刻有助于整理思路。","collocation":"collect your thoughts","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v6","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"polished","zh":"成熟精致的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would not describe the result as polished, but the conversation was certainly more revealing than our first attempt together.","exampleZh":"我不会说结果很成熟，但对话肯定比第一次尝试透露了更多内容。","collocation":"polished result","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v7","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attraction","zh":"吸引力","pos":"noun","example":"One listener suggested background music, and I can see the attraction, especially when an episode begins with a long introduction.","exampleZh":"一位听众建议背景音乐，我理解其吸引力，尤其开头介绍很长的时候。","collocation":"see the attraction","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v8","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"still","zh":"不过","pos":"discourse marker","example":"Still, music underneath every answer might make careful listening harder for some people, so we are testing it only between sections.","exampleZh":"不过每个回答下面都有音乐可能让部分人难以细听，所以我们仅在段落间测试。","collocation":"still, we have concerns","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v9","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intention","zh":"意图","pos":"noun","example":"What surprised me most was how much editing depended on understanding the speaker’s intention rather than removing every hesitation automatically.","exampleZh":"最让我惊讶的是编辑多么依赖理解说话意图，而不是自动删掉每次犹豫。","collocation":"speaker’s intention","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v10","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertainty","zh":"不确定","pos":"noun","example":"A small pause can signal uncertainty, and cutting it out may make a cautious statement sound stronger than the speaker intended.","exampleZh":"小停顿能表示不确定，删掉可能让谨慎陈述听起来比原意更肯定。","collocation":"signal uncertainty","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v11","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"performance","zh":"表演","pos":"noun","example":"I am pleased we tried the project, but I now think of interviewing as a listening skill before considering it a performance.","exampleZh":"我很高兴尝试项目，但现在先把访谈视为倾听技巧，再视为表演。","collocation":"prepared performance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-07:v12","level":"B2","chapter":33,"module":"b2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mapping","zh":"绘制地图","pos":"verb","example":"When we began mapping shade in the city, our first task was to decide what we actually needed to measure.","exampleZh":"开始绘制城市遮阴图时，首要任务是决定实际需要测量什么。","collocation":"map shade","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v1","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct sunlight","zh":"阳光直射","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Air temperature alone was not enough, because standing in direct sunlight can feel very different from standing beneath a tree nearby.","exampleZh":"单测气温不够，因为阳光直射下与附近树荫下的感觉可能很不同。","collocation":"in direct sunlight","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v2","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coverage","zh":"覆盖","pos":"noun","example":"We therefore recorded surface temperature, shade coverage and the times when people were most likely to use each public space.","exampleZh":"因此我们记录地表温度、遮阴覆盖以及人们最可能使用各处公共空间的时间。","collocation":"shade coverage","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v3","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"at noon","zh":"在中午","pos":"adverb phrase","example":"At first, volunteers visited every location at noon, which made the timetable easy but missed changes later in the afternoon.","exampleZh":"起初志愿者中午访问每处地点，排程简单却错过午后变化。","collocation":"visit at noon","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v4","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"记录","pos":"verb","example":"After reviewing the early results, we introduced morning and late afternoon visits to capture the movement of shadows across important routes.","exampleZh":"回顾早期结果后，我们加入早上和傍晚访问，记录重要路线上的影子移动。","collocation":"capture movement","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v5","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"protection","zh":"保护","pos":"noun","example":"One school entrance looked comfortable at lunchtime yet offered almost no protection when children were waiting to be collected after lessons.","exampleZh":"一个校门午餐时很舒适，放学后孩子等待接送时却几乎没有遮护。","collocation":"offer protection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v6","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"priorities","zh":"优先事项","pos":"noun","example":"That observation changed our priorities more than the hottest single reading, since it connected a physical condition with a repeated daily need.","exampleZh":"这一观察比单个最高温度更改变重点，因为它将物理条件与每天重复需求联系起来。","collocation":"change priorities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v7","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shelters","zh":"遮蔽物","pos":"noun","example":"We considered planting trees, installing shelters and changing where people waited, rather than assuming that one solution would suit every site.","exampleZh":"我们考虑种树、装棚和改变等候地点，而非假定一种方案适合所有地点。","collocation":"temporary shelters","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v8","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"canopy","zh":"树冠","pos":"noun","example":"Trees offered additional benefits, but they required suitable soil, continued watering and enough time to develop a useful canopy over the pavement.","exampleZh":"树有额外好处，但需要合适土壤、持续浇水以及时间形成覆盖路面的树冠。","collocation":"develop a canopy","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v9","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"account for","zh":"考虑","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Temporary shelters could work sooner, although their design had to account for wind, maintenance and access for people using wheelchairs.","exampleZh":"临时棚能更快生效，但设计需考虑风、维护和轮椅通行。","collocation":"account for access","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v10","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recommendations","zh":"建议","pos":"noun","example":"Our final recommendations combined immediate changes with longer term planting and made clear which proposals still needed detailed engineering advice.","exampleZh":"最终建议结合即时改变和长期种植，并明确哪些提议仍需详细工程意见。","collocation":"final recommendations","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v11","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identified","zh":"识别","pos":"verb","example":"The study did not produce a single ranking of beautiful places; it identified when practical protection mattered most to the people using them.","exampleZh":"研究没有只给美丽地点排名，而是识别实际使用者何时最需要保护。","collocation":"identify when","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-08:v12","level":"B2","chapter":34,"module":"b2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imagine","zh":"想象","pos":"verb","example":"Imagine one photograph showing teenagers beside a river with measuring equipment and another showing a family following signs through a nature reserve.","exampleZh":"想象一张照片是青少年在河边拿测量设备，另一张是家庭沿标牌参观自然保护区。","collocation":"imagine a photograph","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v1","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appear to","zh":"似乎","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Both groups appear to be learning outdoors, although the purpose of their activities and the support available may be rather different.","exampleZh":"两组似乎都在户外学习，但活动目的及可用支持可能很不同。","collocation":"appear to be learning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v2","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"might be","zh":"可能正在","pos":"modal verb phrase","example":"The teenagers might be carrying out a school investigation, since one person is recording figures while another checks an instrument near the water.","exampleZh":"青少年可能在做学校调查，因为一个人在记数字，另一个在水边检查仪器。","collocation":"might be investigating","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v3","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"by contrast","zh":"相比之下","pos":"discourse phrase","example":"By contrast, the family seems to be choosing its own pace, with a child pointing towards an illustrated notice beside the path.","exampleZh":"相比之下，家庭似乎自己决定速度，一个孩子正指着路边图示牌。","collocation":"by contrast","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v4","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cannot tell","zh":"无法判断","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I cannot tell whether either group is enjoying the experience, but both activities seem to encourage attention.","exampleZh":"我不能判断两组是否享受经历，但两项活动似乎都鼓励专注。","collocation":"cannot tell whether","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v5","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"abstract","zh":"抽象的","pos":"adjective","example":"Working with instruments could help the teenagers understand ideas that would remain abstract if they only read about them in a classroom.","exampleZh":"使用仪器可帮助青少年理解那些只在教室读到时可能仍抽象的概念。","collocation":"abstract ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v6","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"freedom","zh":"自由","pos":"noun","example":"However, they may have less freedom to follow an unexpected interest because their group probably needs to complete a planned task together.","exampleZh":"但他们可能较少自由追随意外兴趣，因为小组可能要共同完成计划任务。","collocation":"freedom to choose","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v7","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interpret","zh":"理解解释","pos":"verb","example":"The family’s visit is likely to be more flexible, although a clear explanation would still help them interpret unfamiliar plants or animal behaviour.","exampleZh":"家庭参观很可能更灵活，但清晰解释仍有助理解陌生植物或动物行为。","collocation":"interpret behaviour","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v8","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"connects","zh":"联系","pos":"verb","example":"Rather than describing every visible object, a useful comparison connects a few details with the question of how each group might learn.","exampleZh":"有效比较不是描述每个可见物品，而是把少量细节与每组如何学习这一问题联系。","collocation":"connect details with questions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v9","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establish","zh":"确立","pos":"verb","example":"Words such as probably and perhaps make clear that these are reasonable possibilities rather than facts that the photographs can fully establish.","exampleZh":"probably 和 perhaps 等词表明这些是合理可能性，而非照片能充分确定的事实。","collocation":"establish a fact","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v10","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"memorable","zh":"难忘的","pos":"adjective","example":"If asked which activity would be more memorable, I would choose the investigation because making a measurement could create a strong personal connection.","exampleZh":"若问哪项更难忘，我会选调查，因为亲自测量可能建立强烈个人联系。","collocation":"memorable activity","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v11","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"可辩护的","pos":"adjective","example":"Someone who prefers exploring freely might choose the family visit, showing that several conclusions can be defensible.","exampleZh":"偏爱自由探索的人可能选家庭参观，说明多种结论都能站得住脚。","collocation":"defensible conclusion","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-09:v12","level":"B2","chapter":35,"module":"b2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"committee","zh":"委员会","pos":"noun","example":"Our student committee had to choose a welcome event for new arrivals, and the first suggestions included a concert and a sports afternoon.","exampleZh":"学生委员会要选迎新活动，最初建议包括音乐会和体育下午活动。","collocation":"student committee","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v1","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"agreed","zh":"达成一致","pos":"verb","example":"Instead of voting immediately, we agreed to discuss how each option would help people who had not yet made friends.","exampleZh":"我们没有马上投票，而是先讨论每个选择如何帮助还没交到朋友的人。","collocation":"agree to discuss","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v2","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"wondered","zh":"提出疑问","pos":"verb","example":"I suggested a concert because music could give people a shared experience, but my partner wondered whether anyone would actually talk during it.","exampleZh":"我提议音乐会，因为音乐能创造共同经历，但同伴怀疑演出中是否有人真的会交谈。","collocation":"wonder whether","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v3","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair point","zh":"合理观点","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That was a fair point, so I asked whether a short performance followed by small group activities might solve the problem.","exampleZh":"这个观点合理，所以我问短表演后接小组活动是否能解决问题。","collocation":"a fair point","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v4","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concerned","zh":"担心的","pos":"adjective","example":"She thought it could, although she was concerned that the extra equipment would leave too little money for refreshments and accessible transport.","exampleZh":"她觉得可以，但担心额外设备会使茶点和无障碍交通预算不足。","collocation":"concerned that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v5","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinguishing","zh":"区分","pos":"verb","example":"We then considered the sports afternoon, distinguishing competitive matches from simple activities that did not require previous skill or expensive clothing.","exampleZh":"随后我们考虑体育下午活动，区分竞技比赛和不需既有技能或昂贵服装的简单活动。","collocation":"distinguish matches from activities","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v6","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"judged","zh":"被评判","pos":"verb","example":"Neither of us wanted less confident students to feel watched or judged while they were still finding their place in the community.","exampleZh":"我们都不希望不自信的学生在刚融入集体时觉得被围观或评判。","collocation":"feel judged","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v7","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cooperation","zh":"合作","pos":"noun","example":"My partner proposed an indoor team challenge with practical puzzles, which seemed to combine cooperation with opportunities for informal conversation afterwards.","exampleZh":"同伴提议室内团队实用解谜挑战，似乎兼顾合作和之后非正式聊天机会。","collocation":"encourage cooperation","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v8","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"on condition","zh":"以某条件为前提","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"I supported the idea on condition that teams were mixed carefully and instructions were available in clear language for everyone taking part.","exampleZh":"我支持这个想法，条件是仔细混合组队，且为所有参加者提供清楚说明。","collocation":"on condition that","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v9","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criteria","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"Before making our decision, we checked it against the same criteria we had used for the concert rather than favouring our newest suggestion automatically.","exampleZh":"决定前我们用评价音乐会的相同标准检查，而不是自动偏爱最新建议。","collocation":"shared criteria","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v10","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposal","zh":"提案","pos":"noun","example":"The final proposal was neither person’s original choice, but both could explain why it addressed our main concern.","exampleZh":"最终提案不是任何人的最初选择，但双方都能解释为何回应了核心问题。","collocation":"final proposal","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v11","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contributions","zh":"贡献","pos":"noun","example":"Productive discussion meant using each other’s contributions to build a decision, rather than simply taking turns to speak.","exampleZh":"有效讨论意味着利用彼此贡献形成决定，而不只是轮流说话。","collocation":"use each other’s contributions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-10:v12","level":"B2","chapter":36,"module":"b2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compulsory","zh":"强制的","pos":"adjective","example":"A school considering compulsory community projects should begin by asking what students are expected to learn rather than counting volunteer hours.","exampleZh":"学校考虑强制社区项目时，应先问预期学习成果而非只数志愿时数。","collocation":"compulsory projects","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v1","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supporters","zh":"支持者","pos":"noun","example":"Supporters argue that working with people outside school helps young learners understand needs that textbooks may describe only in general terms.","exampleZh":"支持者认为校外合作帮助年轻学习者理解课本可能只泛泛描述的需求。","collocation":"supporters argue","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v2","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accessible","zh":"便于理解的","pos":"adjective","example":"For example, helping to design a clear visitor map could show why accessible communication matters to somebody unfamiliar with an area.","exampleZh":"例如帮助设计清晰导览图，可说明方便理解的沟通为何对不熟悉地区的人重要。","collocation":"accessible communication","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v3","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"automatic","zh":"自动发生的","pos":"adjective","example":"This benefit is not automatic, however, because repeating a simple task without explanation may teach little about the community or its difficulties.","exampleZh":"然而该收益不是自动发生，因为无解释地重复简单任务可能几乎学不到社区及其困难。","collocation":"automatic benefit","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v4","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reflect","zh":"反思","pos":"verb","example":"Students need preparation, appropriate responsibility and time to reflect on what the experience has changed in their understanding of other people.","exampleZh":"学生需要准备、合适责任及时间反思经历如何改变其对他人的理解。","collocation":"reflect on learning","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v5","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"critics","zh":"批评者","pos":"noun","example":"Critics reasonably point out that compulsory service can create pressure for students who already care for relatives or work after lessons.","exampleZh":"批评者合理指出，强制服务会让已照顾亲属或课后工作的人承压。","collocation":"critics point out","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v6","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flexible","zh":"灵活的","pos":"adjective","example":"A fair programme would therefore offer flexible schedules and several forms of participation rather than assume that everyone has the same free time.","exampleZh":"因此公平项目应提供灵活时间和多种参与方式，而非假定每个人空闲相同。","collocation":"flexible schedules","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v7","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unprepared","zh":"未准备好的","pos":"adjective","example":"It should also avoid treating community organisations as places that must accept unprepared students simply because the school wants a useful activity.","exampleZh":"还应避免把社区机构当成仅因学校需要活动就必须接受未准备学生的场所。","collocation":"unprepared students","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v8","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"objection","zh":"反对意见","pos":"noun","example":"The strongest argument for these projects concerns meaningful cooperation, while the strongest objection concerns unfair demands on participants and their hosts.","exampleZh":"支持项目的最强理由关乎有意义合作，最强反对关乎对参与者及接待方不公平要求。","collocation":"strongest objection","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v9","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"develops","zh":"展开","pos":"verb","example":"An effective essay develops both points with explanation instead of presenting a long list of benefits followed by an unrelated personal story.","exampleZh":"有效文章解释发展双方观点，而不是列长串好处后接无关个人故事。","collocation":"develop a point","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v10","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledge","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"Its conclusion should answer the original question and acknowledge the conditions that determine whether the proposal is likely to work in practice.","exampleZh":"结论应回答原问题并承认决定提议实际是否可行的条件。","collocation":"acknowledge conditions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v11","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supervised","zh":"受监督的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would support a well supervised programme with genuine choice, but not a fixed requirement that rewards attendance while ignoring learning and fairness.","exampleZh":"我支持有监督和真正选择的项目，不支持只奖励出席而忽视学习公平的固定要求。","collocation":"well supervised programme","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-11:v12","level":"B2","chapter":38,"module":"b2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"residents","zh":"居民","pos":"noun","example":"Our project group received a residents’ survey and a short interview with a transport planner about noise on a busy shopping street.","exampleZh":"项目组收到居民调查及交通规划师关于繁忙购物街噪声的简短访谈。","collocation":"residents’ survey","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v1","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depended on","zh":"依赖","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The survey showed that evening deliveries disturbed several households, while the interview explained why shops depended on flexible access for vehicles.","exampleZh":"调查显示晚间配送打扰一些家庭，访谈则解释商店为何依赖灵活车辆通行。","collocation":"depend on access","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v2","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradictions","zh":"矛盾","pos":"noun","example":"At first, one student wanted to choose whichever source seemed more convincing, but this would have treated different questions as direct contradictions.","exampleZh":"起初一位学生想选更可信来源，但这会把不同问题当成直接矛盾。","collocation":"direct contradictions","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v3","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"constraints","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"The residents described their experience, whereas the planner discussed practical constraints that a proposed change would need to address before being introduced.","exampleZh":"居民描述经历，规划师讨论的是变更实施前必须处理的实际限制。","collocation":"practical constraints","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v4","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"workable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"We therefore made separate notes on the problem, the people affected and the conditions under which each possible solution might be workable.","exampleZh":"因此我们分别记录问题、受影响者及各可能方案可行的条件。","collocation":"workable solution","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v5","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"转移","pos":"verb","example":"A complete ban on deliveries sounded simple, yet it might force businesses to close or transfer journeys to nearby streets with similar problems.","exampleZh":"完全禁配送看似简单，却可能迫使商家关闭或把交通转移到有类似问题的邻街。","collocation":"transfer journeys","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v6","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disturbance","zh":"干扰","pos":"noun","example":"Restricting the noisiest activities during late hours appeared more balanced, although the available evidence did not specify which vehicles caused the greatest disturbance.","exampleZh":"限制深夜最吵活动似乎更平衡，但现有证据未说明哪些车辆干扰最大。","collocation":"cause disturbance","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v7","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trial","zh":"试验","pos":"noun","example":"Our recommendation was to collect that missing information during a short trial rather than pretend the existing sources already answered every relevant question.","exampleZh":"我们建议短期试验收集缺失信息，而不假装已有来源回答所有相关问题。","collocation":"short trial","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v8","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"represented","zh":"代表","pos":"verb","example":"When writing the summary, we identified whose view each claim represented and avoided presenting a small survey as the opinion of every resident.","exampleZh":"写摘要时我们标明主张属于谁，避免把小调查当成全体居民意见。","collocation":"represent a view","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v9","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"paraphrased","zh":"改述","pos":"verb","example":"We also paraphrased the main ideas instead of copying striking phrases that sounded impressive but did not fit the purpose of our proposal.","exampleZh":"我们还转述主要意思，而不复制听起来出色却不适合提案目的的醒目措辞。","collocation":"paraphrase main ideas","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v10","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledged","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The presentation acknowledged disagreement, while showing that protecting rest and supporting shops were not necessarily incompatible goals.","exampleZh":"展示承认分歧，同时表明保护休息和支持商店并非必然不相容的目标。","collocation":"acknowledge disagreement","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v11","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"integrating","zh":"整合","pos":"verb","example":"Integrating information meant building a reasoned response, not attaching summaries without explaining their relationship.","exampleZh":"整合信息意味着形成有理由回应，而非不解释关系就拼接摘要。","collocation":"integrate information","register":"neutral / 中性；适用于解释、讨论和书面表达","id":"b2-12:v12","level":"B2","chapter":40,"module":"b2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allocate funds","zh":"拨出资金","pos":"phrase","example":"We must allocate funds before advertising additional opening hours.","exampleZh":"我们必须先拨出资金，再宣传延长开放时间。","collocation":"allocate funds","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v1","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"an indication of","zh":"……的迹象","pos":"phrase","example":"The survey provides an indication of demand, not a complete forecast.","exampleZh":"调查反映了需求迹象，并非完整预测。","collocation":"an indication of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v2","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"make a firm commitment","zh":"作出明确承诺","pos":"phrase","example":"The manager should make a firm commitment only after checking staffing.","exampleZh":"经理应在核实人员安排后才作出明确承诺。","collocation":"make a firm commitment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v3","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"take account of","zh":"考虑","pos":"phrase","example":"We need to take account of staff working consecutive evenings.","exampleZh":"我们需要考虑连续上晚班的员工。","collocation":"take account of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v4","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"meet the needs","zh":"满足需要","pos":"phrase","example":"Extended hours could meet the needs of people on rotating shifts.","exampleZh":"延长开放时间可能满足轮班人群的需要。","collocation":"meet the needs","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v5","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provide evidence","zh":"提供证据","pos":"phrase","example":"A limited trial would provide evidence without creating an indefinite obligation.","exampleZh":"有限试行能提供证据，又不会造成无限期义务。","collocation":"provide evidence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v6","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draw a distinction","zh":"作出区分","pos":"phrase","example":"We should draw a distinction between attendance and satisfaction.","exampleZh":"我们应区分到访人数和满意度。","collocation":"draw a distinction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v7","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"bear the cost","zh":"承担费用","pos":"phrase","example":"The council must bear the cost of additional security staff.","exampleZh":"委员会必须承担额外安保人员的费用。","collocation":"bear the cost","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v8","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shed light on","zh":"帮助说明","pos":"phrase","example":"Early results may shed light on which evening is busiest.","exampleZh":"初步结果可能揭示哪个晚上最忙。","collocation":"shed light on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v9","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reduce uncertainty","zh":"减少不确定性","pos":"phrase","example":"A written rota would reduce uncertainty about individual responsibilities.","exampleZh":"书面轮班表能减少个人职责的不确定性。","collocation":"reduce uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v10","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rule out","zh":"排除","pos":"phrase","example":"We cannot rule out lower attendance during winter storms.","exampleZh":"我们不能排除冬季暴风雨期间到访人数下降的可能。","collocation":"rule out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v11","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"set out","zh":"列明","pos":"phrase","example":"The final report should set out the conditions for continuing.","exampleZh":"最终报告应列明继续运行的条件。","collocation":"set out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-01:v12","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shortfall","zh":"缺口","pos":"noun","example":"The shortfall must be funded before six evenings are advertised.","exampleZh":"宣传六次晚间开放前必须填补资金缺口。","collocation":"a funding shortfall","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v13","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingent","zh":"取决于条件的","pos":"adjective","example":"The sixth opening is contingent on additional funding.","exampleZh":"第六次开放取决于额外资金。","collocation":"contingent on funding","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v14","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substitute","zh":"替代物","pos":"noun","example":"A volunteer is not a substitute for a required security officer.","exampleZh":"志愿者不能替代必需安保人员。","collocation":"a substitute for","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v15","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underwrite","zh":"承担资金风险","pos":"verb","example":"The council declined to underwrite costs beyond the pilot.","exampleZh":"委员会拒绝承担试点之后的费用风险。","collocation":"underwrite the costs","register":"formal","id":"c1-01:v16","level":"C1","chapter":41,"module":"c1-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imply","zh":"意味着","pos":"verb","example":"A shorter queue does not necessarily imply faster individual service.","exampleZh":"队伍变短并不一定意味着每个人得到更快的服务。","collocation":"imply a conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v1","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"suggest","zh":"提示","pos":"verb","example":"The figures suggest improvement but do not prove its cause.","exampleZh":"这些数字提示有所改善，却不能证明原因。","collocation":"suggest an improvement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v2","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"apparent","zh":"表面的","pos":"adjective","example":"Telephone appointments may account for part of the apparent decline.","exampleZh":"电话预约可能解释部分表面下降。","collocation":"an apparent decline","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v3","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"modest","zh":"不大的","pos":"adjective","example":"The reduction was modest rather than dramatic.","exampleZh":"降幅不大，而非十分显著。","collocation":"a modest reduction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v4","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"representative","zh":"有代表性的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should distinguish an accurate count from a representative sample.","exampleZh":"我们应区分准确计数与有代表性的样本。","collocation":"a representative sample","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v5","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"convenient","zh":"方便的","pos":"adjective","example":"Some clients described the new reception process as convenient.","exampleZh":"一些来访者认为新接待流程很方便。","collocation":"a convenient process","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v6","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"appropriate","zh":"恰当的","pos":"adjective","example":"Convenience alone does not establish that the advice was appropriate.","exampleZh":"仅有便利并不能证明建议恰当。","collocation":"appropriate advice","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v7","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"acknowledge","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The manager acknowledged limitations without dismissing the positive results.","exampleZh":"经理承认局限，同时没有否定积极结果。","collocation":"acknowledge limitations","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v8","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"plausible","zh":"看似合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A plausible explanation still needs independent checking.","exampleZh":"合理的解释仍需独立核查。","collocation":"a plausible explanation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v9","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consistent with","zh":"与……相符","pos":"phrase","example":"The evidence is consistent with a benefit, although alternatives remain.","exampleZh":"证据与产生益处的解释相符，但仍存在其他解释。","collocation":"consistent with","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v10","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"comparable","zh":"可比的","pos":"adjective","example":"We need comparable figures from a similar period.","exampleZh":"我们需要相近时期的可比数据。","collocation":"comparable figures","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v11","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reflect","zh":"体现","pos":"verb","example":"The final statement should reflect the uncertainty in the findings.","exampleZh":"最终声明应体现研究结果中的不确定性。","collocation":"reflect uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-02:v12","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"denominator","zh":"分母","pos":"noun","example":"The denominator is the number of respondents, not all members.","exampleZh":"分母是回应人数，而非全部会员。","collocation":"inspect the denominator","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v13","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"causal","zh":"因果的","pos":"adjective","example":"A before-and-after comparison alone does not establish a causal effect.","exampleZh":"单独的前后比较不能确立因果效应。","collocation":"a causal effect","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v14","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conclusive","zh":"确凿的","pos":"adjective","example":"The findings are promising but not conclusive.","exampleZh":"发现有希望，但非确凿结论。","collocation":"conclusive evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v15","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disaggregate","zh":"分组拆分","pos":"verb","example":"We should disaggregate appointments by contact method.","exampleZh":"我们应按联系方法拆分预约。","collocation":"disaggregate the data","register":"formal","id":"c1-02:v16","level":"C1","chapter":42,"module":"c1-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"would appreciate","zh":"希望得到","pos":"phrase","example":"We would appreciate access to your kitchen during the inspection window.","exampleZh":"希望您能在检查时段允许我们进入厨房。","collocation":"would appreciate","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v1","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unsuitable","zh":"不合适的","pos":"adjective","example":"Please contact the office if the proposed time is unsuitable.","exampleZh":"如果建议时间不合适，请联系办公室。","collocation":"an unsuitable appointment time","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v2","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alternative appointment","zh":"其他预约时间","pos":"phrase","example":"The inspection is required, but alternative appointments are available.","exampleZh":"检查是必要的，但可以另约时间。","collocation":"alternative appointment","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v3","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"are requested to","zh":"被请予以","pos":"phrase","example":"Residents are requested to reply by Thursday afternoon.","exampleZh":"请住户在星期四下午前回复。","collocation":"are requested to","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v4","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"for clarity","zh":"为说明清楚","pos":"phrase","example":"For clarity, the visit does not involve opening personal cupboards.","exampleZh":"为说明清楚，这次上门不涉及打开私人橱柜。","collocation":"for clarity","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v5","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identification","zh":"身份证明","pos":"noun","example":"A member of staff will show identification before entering.","exampleZh":"工作人员会在进入前出示证件。","collocation":"show identification","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v6","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"additional support","zh":"额外协助","pos":"phrase","example":"Could you let us know whether anyone requires additional support?","exampleZh":"能否告知我们是否有人需要额外协助？","collocation":"additional support","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v7","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"retain","zh":"保留","pos":"verb","example":"The reminder should retain the deadline stated in the formal letter.","exampleZh":"提醒信息应保留正式信件中规定的截止时间。","collocation":"retain a deadline","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v8","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"obscure","zh":"使不清楚","pos":"verb","example":"Friendly wording must not obscure what residents need to do.","exampleZh":"友好措辞不能掩盖住户需要采取的行动。","collocation":"obscure a requirement","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v9","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inconvenience","zh":"不便","pos":"noun","example":"We apologise for the inconvenience caused by the inspection.","exampleZh":"对于检查造成的不便，我们表示歉意。","collocation":"cause inconvenience","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v10","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"further action","zh":"进一步行动","pos":"phrase","example":"If you have already replied, no further action is needed.","exampleZh":"如果您已经回复，则无需进一步操作。","collocation":"further action","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v11","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"respectful","zh":"尊重他人的","pos":"adjective","example":"A firm request can remain respectful and specific.","exampleZh":"明确坚定的请求仍可以尊重且具体。","collocation":"respectful wording","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v12","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"remedy","zh":"补救办法","pos":"noun","example":"The letter states the confirmed remedy before discussing exceptions.","exampleZh":"信件先说明已确认的补救措施，再讨论例外。","collocation":"a confirmed remedy","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v13","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tact","zh":"得体","pos":"noun","example":"Tact requires consideration, not concealment of an important fact.","exampleZh":"得体要求体谅，而非隐瞒重要事实。","collocation":"show tact","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v14","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclosure","zh":"披露","pos":"noun","example":"Public disclosure of personal circumstances is unnecessary.","exampleZh":"没有必要公开披露私人情况。","collocation":"public disclosure","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v15","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unambiguous","zh":"明确无歧义的","pos":"adjective","example":"An unambiguous deadline helps residents act.","exampleZh":"明确截止时间有助住户行动。","collocation":"an unambiguous deadline","register":"formal","id":"c1-03:v16","level":"C1","chapter":43,"module":"c1-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establishes","zh":"说明确立","pos":"verb","example":"The speaker first establishes why planting totals can be misleading.","exampleZh":"讲者首先说明植树总数为何可能误导。","collocation":"establishes a distinction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v1","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turns to","zh":"转向","pos":"phrase","example":"She then turns to the conditions required for young trees to survive.","exampleZh":"接着她转向幼树存活所需的条件。","collocation":"turns to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v2","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"illustration","zh":"例证","pos":"noun","example":"The shaded playground is an illustration, not the entire argument.","exampleZh":"有树荫的操场是例子，而非完整论证。","collocation":"a supporting illustration","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v3","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"although","zh":"虽然","pos":"conjunction","example":"Although mature trees provide shade, benefits develop over many years.","exampleZh":"成熟树木虽能遮阴，益处却需多年形成。","collocation":"although benefits develop slowly","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v4","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"maintenance","zh":"维护","pos":"noun","example":"Maintenance should be treated as part of the investment.","exampleZh":"维护应被视为投资的一部分。","collocation":"routine maintenance","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v5","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"depends on","zh":"取决于","pos":"phrase","example":"The proposal depends on reliable summer watering.","exampleZh":"该方案取决于可靠的夏季浇水。","collocation":"depends on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v6","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"survival rate","zh":"存活率","pos":"phrase","example":"A survival rate tells us more than an initial planting count.","exampleZh":"存活率比最初种植数量提供更多信息。","collocation":"survival rate","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v7","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualifies","zh":"限定","pos":"verb","example":"The lecturer qualifies her recommendation rather than withdrawing it.","exampleZh":"讲者对建议加上条件，而非撤回建议。","collocation":"qualifies a recommendation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v8","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distribution","zh":"分布","pos":"noun","example":"In this context, shade distribution matters as much as total cover.","exampleZh":"在此情境下，树荫分布与总覆盖同样重要。","collocation":"shade distribution","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v9","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"returns to","zh":"回到","pos":"phrase","example":"The conclusion returns to the neighbourhoods with the least protection.","exampleZh":"结论重新关注防护最少的社区。","collocation":"returns to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v10","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"central claim","zh":"核心主张","pos":"phrase","example":"My notes separate the central claim from supporting examples.","exampleZh":"我的笔记区分核心主张和支持例子。","collocation":"central claim","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v11","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attached to","zh":"附带于","pos":"phrase","example":"A useful summary preserves the conditions attached to the recommendation.","exampleZh":"有用的摘要会保留建议所附带的条件。","collocation":"attached to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-04:v12","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capacity constraint","zh":"能力约束","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The maintenance limit is a capacity constraint, not a judgement about need.","exampleZh":"维护上限是能力约束，而非需求判断。","collocation":"a binding capacity constraint","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v13","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconstruct","zh":"重建","pos":"verb","example":"Students must reconstruct the argument from selective notes.","exampleZh":"学生须根据选择性笔记重建论证。","collocation":"reconstruct an argument","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v14","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"salient","zh":"突出的","pos":"adjective","example":"A salient example can distract listeners from a qualified conclusion.","exampleZh":"突出的例子可能使听者忽略附条件结论。","collocation":"a salient example","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v15","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"The team assessed feasibility before promising all twelve sites.","exampleZh":"团队在承诺全部十二处前评估可行性。","collocation":"assess feasibility","register":"formal","id":"c1-04:v16","level":"C1","chapter":45,"module":"c1-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uneasy","zh":"不安的","pos":"adjective","example":"The organiser sounds pleased with attendance but uneasy about expansion.","exampleZh":"组织者对人数感到高兴，却对扩张不安。","collocation":"uneasy about expansion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v1","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stops short of","zh":"没有进一步到","pos":"phrase","example":"Her phrase “a useful beginning” stops short of declaring success.","exampleZh":"她说“有益的开端”，尚未宣布成功。","collocation":"stops short of","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v2","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concede","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"She concedes that a larger event could attract more funding.","exampleZh":"她承认更大的活动可能吸引更多资金。","collocation":"concede a benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v3","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sustain","zh":"持续承担","pos":"verb","example":"However, she questions whether the volunteer team could sustain it.","exampleZh":"然而，她质疑志愿者团队能否持续承担。","collocation":"sustain an activity","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v4","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hesitation","zh":"犹豫","pos":"noun","example":"The interviewer interprets hesitation as opposition, which is too strong.","exampleZh":"采访者把犹豫解读成反对，程度过重。","collocation":"express hesitation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v5","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"implies","zh":"暗示","pos":"verb","example":"Her answer implies a preference for improving access before expanding.","exampleZh":"她的回答暗示应先改善可及性再扩张。","collocation":"implies a preference","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v6","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"claim","zh":"声称","pos":"verb","example":"She does not claim that nearby residents opposed every performance.","exampleZh":"她没有声称附近居民反对每场演出。","collocation":"make a claim","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v7","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定意见","pos":"noun","example":"The qualification concerns evening noise rather than daytime attendance.","exampleZh":"限定意见涉及晚间噪声，而非日间人数。","collocation":"an important qualification","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v8","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tone","zh":"语气","pos":"noun","example":"A warm tone alone does not establish agreement with a proposal.","exampleZh":"仅凭亲切语气不能认定赞同方案。","collocation":"a warm tone","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v9","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inference","zh":"推断","pos":"noun","example":"We should support an inference with specific words and context.","exampleZh":"我们应以具体措辞和上下文支持推断。","collocation":"support an inference","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v10","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consultation","zh":"协商","pos":"noun","example":"She recommends consultation before making next year’s programme public.","exampleZh":"她建议先协商，再公布明年的安排。","collocation":"public consultation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v11","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outright refusal","zh":"断然拒绝","pos":"phrase","example":"Her position is conditional support, not an outright refusal.","exampleZh":"她的立场是有条件支持，而非断然拒绝。","collocation":"outright refusal","register":"neutral","id":"c1-05:v12","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"gratitude","zh":"感激","pos":"noun","example":"Gratitude for funding does not imply agreement with every condition.","exampleZh":"感谢资助并不意味着赞同所有条件。","collocation":"express gratitude","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v13","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivocal","zh":"含糊不定的","pos":"adjective","example":"Her answer was qualified rather than merely equivocal.","exampleZh":"她的回答有明确限定，并非单纯含糊。","collocation":"an equivocal answer","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v14","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"认可","pos":"noun","example":"A welcome is not an unconditional endorsement of expansion.","exampleZh":"欢迎并非无条件认可扩张。","collocation":"an unconditional endorsement","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v15","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible","zh":"相容的","pos":"adjective","example":"Appreciation and caution are compatible attitudes.","exampleZh":"感激与谨慎可以相容。","collocation":"compatible with","register":"formal","id":"c1-05:v16","level":"C1","chapter":46,"module":"c1-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pilot","zh":"试点","pos":"noun","example":"The finance coordinator supports a pilot rather than full conversion.","exampleZh":"财务协调员支持试点，而非全面改造。","collocation":"a limited pilot","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v1","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"builds on","zh":"在……基础上发展","pos":"phrase","example":"The designer builds on that suggestion by proposing movable desks.","exampleZh":"设计师在此基础上提出可移动书桌。","collocation":"builds on","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v2","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unobstructed","zh":"无阻挡的","pos":"adjective","example":"The access representative asks whether the doorway remains unobstructed.","exampleZh":"无障碍代表询问门口是否保持畅通。","collocation":"remain unobstructed","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v3","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided that","zh":"前提是","pos":"phrase","example":"The caretaker accepts the layout provided that cleaning remains practical.","exampleZh":"管理员接受布局，前提是清洁仍然方便。","collocation":"provided that","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v4","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permanently","zh":"永久地","pos":"adverb","example":"Nobody has agreed to remove the storage shelves permanently.","exampleZh":"没有人同意永久拆除储物架。","collocation":"remove permanently","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v5","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"lasting commitment","zh":"长期承诺","pos":"phrase","example":"The group distinguishes a temporary arrangement from a lasting commitment.","exampleZh":"小组区分临时安排与长期承诺。","collocation":"lasting commitment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v6","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"take your point","zh":"理解你的观点","pos":"phrase","example":"I take your point about cost, but access is also essential.","exampleZh":"我理解你关于费用的观点，但通行同样必要。","collocation":"take your point","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v7","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"combine","zh":"结合","pos":"verb","example":"Could we combine the pilot with a review after six weeks?","exampleZh":"我们能否把试点与六周后复查结合？","collocation":"combine a trial with review","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v8","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"address","zh":"处理","pos":"verb","example":"That would address maintenance without ruling out later improvements.","exampleZh":"这能解决维护问题，同时不排除后续改善。","collocation":"address a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v9","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unresolved","zh":"未解决的","pos":"adjective","example":"The minutes should identify unresolved questions as well as agreements.","exampleZh":"会议纪要应同时列明未解决问题和共识。","collocation":"unresolved questions","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v10","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consent","zh":"同意","pos":"noun","example":"Silence should not automatically be recorded as consent.","exampleZh":"沉默不应自动被记录为同意。","collocation":"record consent","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v11","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"carry out","zh":"执行","pos":"phrase","example":"Before closing, the chair checks who will carry out each action.","exampleZh":"结束前，主持人核实每项行动的负责人。","collocation":"carry out","register":"neutral","id":"c1-06:v12","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"The provisional layout will be tested before a final decision.","exampleZh":"暂定布局将在最终决定前测试。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v13","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consensus","zh":"共识","pos":"noun","example":"Conditional consensus does not mean identical preferences.","exampleZh":"有条件共识不等于偏好相同。","collocation":"reach consensus","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v14","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transition","zh":"转换","pos":"noun","example":"The schedule must allow time for the transition between activities.","exampleZh":"时间表须留出活动转换时间。","collocation":"allow for transitions","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v15","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ratify","zh":"正式批准","pos":"verb","example":"The group cannot ratify an arrangement on behalf of an absent participant.","exampleZh":"小组不能代缺席者正式批准安排。","collocation":"ratify an agreement","register":"formal","id":"c1-06:v16","level":"C1","chapter":47,"module":"c1-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"concedes","zh":"承认","pos":"verb","example":"The author concedes that café income could support maintenance.","exampleZh":"作者承认咖啡馆收入可能支持维护。","collocation":"concedes a potential benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v1","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exclusive","zh":"排他的","pos":"adjective","example":"Her objection concerns exclusive control rather than coffee itself.","exampleZh":"她反对的是排他控制，而非咖啡本身。","collocation":"exclusive control","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v2","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frame","zh":"界定","pos":"verb","example":"The opening contrast frames the square as a shared resource.","exampleZh":"开篇对比把广场界定为共享资源。","collocation":"frame an argument","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v3","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rhetorical question","zh":"修辞问句","pos":"phrase","example":"A rhetorical question invites readers to reconsider an assumption.","exampleZh":"修辞问句促使读者重新考虑假设。","collocation":"rhetorical question","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v4","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"illustrate","zh":"说明","pos":"verb","example":"The example illustrates a risk without proving it will occur everywhere.","exampleZh":"例子说明风险，却不能证明它处处发生。","collocation":"illustrate a risk","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v5","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"non-customers","zh":"非顾客","pos":"noun","example":"The proposed agreement would protect seating for non-customers.","exampleZh":"建议中的协议会保障非顾客的座位。","collocation":"seating for non-customers","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v6","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"be reduced to","zh":"被简化为","pos":"phrase","example":"Public value cannot be reduced to the amount of money spent.","exampleZh":"公共价值不能简化为消费金额。","collocation":"be reduced to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v7","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"nevertheless","zh":"尽管如此","pos":"adverb","example":"Nevertheless, funding constraints deserve a practical response.","exampleZh":"尽管如此，资金限制仍需实际回应。","collocation":"nevertheless, constraints remain","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v8","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualifies","zh":"限定","pos":"verb","example":"The conclusion qualifies the opening criticism rather than abandoning it.","exampleZh":"结论限定开头的批评，而非放弃批评。","collocation":"qualifies a recommendation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v9","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prediction","zh":"预测","pos":"noun","example":"I find the access argument stronger than the prediction about noise.","exampleZh":"我认为可及性论点强于噪声预测。","collocation":"an unsupported prediction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v10","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"expected use","zh":"预计使用情况","pos":"phrase","example":"The prediction needs evidence about hours, layout and expected use.","exampleZh":"该预测需要营业时间、布局和预计使用情况的证据。","collocation":"expected use","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v11","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"engage with","zh":"认真回应","pos":"phrase","example":"A fair response should engage with the author’s strongest reason.","exampleZh":"公允回应应处理作者最有力的理由。","collocation":"engage with","register":"neutral","id":"c1-07:v12","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enforceable","zh":"可执行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Public access requires enforceable conditions, not only goodwill.","exampleZh":"公众通行需要可执行条件，而非只有善意。","collocation":"enforceable conditions","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v13","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"amenity","zh":"公共便利设施","pos":"noun","example":"Shade is an amenity whose location affects its usefulness.","exampleZh":"树荫是一项价值受位置影响的公共便利。","collocation":"a public amenity","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v14","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"displace","zh":"挤占","pos":"verb","example":"Commercial seating should not displace essential public use.","exampleZh":"商业座位不应挤占必要公共使用。","collocation":"displace public use","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v15","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate","zh":"适度相称的","pos":"adjective","example":"A proportionate response addresses the identified risk.","exampleZh":"相称回应针对已识别风险。","collocation":"a proportionate response","register":"formal","id":"c1-07:v16","level":"C1","chapter":49,"module":"c1-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intercepted","zh":"被拦截","pos":"verb","example":"Leaves are intercepted before water enters the storage tank.","exampleZh":"水进入储水箱前，树叶会被拦截。","collocation":"leaves are intercepted","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v1","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sediment","zh":"沉积物","pos":"noun","example":"Sediment may accumulate even when the incoming water looks clear.","exampleZh":"即使流入的水看起来清澈，沉积物仍可能累积。","collocation":"sediment accumulation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v2","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opaque","zh":"不透明的","pos":"adjective","example":"An opaque cover limits the amount of light reaching the water.","exampleZh":"不透明盖子限制照入水中的光。","collocation":"an opaque cover","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v3","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mechanism","zh":"机制","pos":"noun","example":"A plausible mechanism must be distinguished from a measured effect.","exampleZh":"合理机制必须与测得的效果区分。","collocation":"a plausible mechanism","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v4","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"irrigation","zh":"灌溉","pos":"noun","example":"The tank supplies irrigation water rather than drinking water.","exampleZh":"水箱供应灌溉水，而非饮用水。","collocation":"irrigation water","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v5","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"observations","zh":"观察结果","pos":"noun","example":"The team records rainfall because weather affects the observations.","exampleZh":"团队记录降雨，因为天气影响观察结果。","collocation":"repeated observations","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v6","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"throughout","zh":"遍及","pos":"preposition","example":"A single clear sample cannot establish quality throughout the tank.","exampleZh":"单个清澈样本无法证明整个水箱的水质。","collocation":"throughout the tank","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v7","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"variation","zh":"变化","pos":"noun","example":"Repeated measurements reveal variation over time.","exampleZh":"重复测量揭示随时间发生的变化。","collocation":"variation over time","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v8","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sequence","zh":"顺序","pos":"noun","example":"The explanation follows the sequence from collection to storage.","exampleZh":"解释按从收集到储存的顺序展开。","collocation":"a physical sequence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v9","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"causal chain","zh":"因果链","pos":"phrase","example":"We should identify which link in the causal chain was tested.","exampleZh":"我们应明确因果链中的哪个环节接受了测试。","collocation":"causal chain","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v10","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limited to","zh":"限于","pos":"phrase","example":"The conclusion remains limited to the conditions actually observed.","exampleZh":"结论仍限于实际观察的条件。","collocation":"limited to","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v11","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"justified","zh":"有依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"Further testing is required before any wider claim is justified.","exampleZh":"在更广泛主张成立前，还需进一步测试。","collocation":"a justified conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-08:v12","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"standardise","zh":"统一标准","pos":"verb","example":"We must standardise the sampling depth before comparing readings.","exampleZh":"比较读数前须统一取样深度。","collocation":"standardise a procedure","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v13","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confound","zh":"混淆因果判断","pos":"verb","example":"Unrecorded cleaning may confound the interpretation.","exampleZh":"未记录清洁可能混淆解释。","collocation":"confound the interpretation","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v14","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sedimentation","zh":"沉降","pos":"noun","example":"Sedimentation may affect where particles collect.","exampleZh":"沉降可能影响颗粒聚集位置。","collocation":"sedimentation in storage","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v15","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replicable","zh":"可重复检验的","pos":"adjective","example":"A clear method makes the observations more replicable.","exampleZh":"明确方法使观察更易重复检验。","collocation":"a replicable method","register":"formal","id":"c1-08:v16","level":"C1","chapter":50,"module":"c1-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reported experience","zh":"自报体验","pos":"phrase","example":"The rider survey measures reported experience rather than collision risk.","exampleZh":"骑行者调查衡量自报体验，而非碰撞风险。","collocation":"reported experience","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v1","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selected","zh":"选定的","pos":"adjective","example":"Junction observations cover selected hours, not the entire week.","exampleZh":"路口观察覆盖选定时段，而非整周。","collocation":"selected observation hours","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v2","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"denominator","zh":"分母","pos":"noun","example":"Before comparing percentages, we need to inspect their denominators.","exampleZh":"比较百分比前，我们需要查看分母。","collocation":"inspect the denominator","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v3","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scope","zh":"范围","pos":"noun","example":"A source may be accurate within a narrow scope.","exampleZh":"来源在狭窄范围内也可能准确。","collocation":"within a narrow scope","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v4","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradiction","zh":"矛盾","pos":"noun","example":"Different definitions can create an apparent contradiction.","exampleZh":"不同定义可能造成表面矛盾。","collocation":"an apparent contradiction","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v5","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归于","pos":"verb","example":"The team should attribute each claim to the relevant evidence.","exampleZh":"团队应将每项主张归于相应证据。","collocation":"attribute a claim to evidence","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v6","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interested organisation","zh":"有利益关系的组织","pos":"phrase","example":"An interested organisation can still provide useful data.","exampleZh":"有利益关系的组织仍可提供有用数据。","collocation":"interested organisation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v7","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scrutiny","zh":"仔细审查","pos":"noun","example":"Its role is a reason for scrutiny, not automatic dismissal.","exampleZh":"其角色是审查理由，而非自动否定的理由。","collocation":"careful scrutiny","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v8","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interchangeable","zh":"可互换的","pos":"adjective","example":"The two sources overlap without being interchangeable.","exampleZh":"两个来源有交集，但不能互换。","collocation":"not interchangeable","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v9","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit","zh":"限制","pos":"verb","example":"Missing evening observations limit conclusions about night-time use.","exampleZh":"缺少晚间观察限制了对夜间使用的结论。","collocation":"limit the conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v10","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"forcing agreement","zh":"强求一致","pos":"phrase","example":"We should report uncertainty instead of forcing agreement.","exampleZh":"我们应报告不确定性，而非强求一致。","collocation":"forcing agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v11","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"boundaries","zh":"边界","pos":"noun","example":"A stronger briefing combines findings while preserving their boundaries.","exampleZh":"更好的简报整合结果，同时保留各自边界。","collocation":"clear boundaries","register":"neutral","id":"c1-09:v12","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coverage","zh":"覆盖范围","pos":"noun","example":"The sources differ in coverage as well as purpose.","exampleZh":"来源在覆盖范围和目的上都不同。","collocation":"limited coverage","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v13","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconcile","zh":"核对使一致","pos":"verb","example":"The team must reconcile definitions before comparing totals.","exampleZh":"比较总数前须核对口径。","collocation":"reconcile the records","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v14","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"prevalence","zh":"普遍程度","pos":"noun","example":"A complaint list cannot establish the prevalence of a problem.","exampleZh":"投诉清单不能确定问题普遍程度。","collocation":"estimate prevalence","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v15","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allegation","zh":"指控","pos":"noun","example":"A reporting mismatch is not automatically evidence for an allegation of fraud.","exampleZh":"报告不一致不自动支持欺诈指控。","collocation":"an allegation of fraud","register":"formal","id":"c1-09:v16","level":"C1","chapter":51,"module":"c1-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capacity","zh":"能力","pos":"noun","example":"The requests identify desired uses but do not establish maintenance capacity.","exampleZh":"请求说明理想用途，但未证明维护能力。","collocation":"maintenance capacity","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v1","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assume","zh":"假设","pos":"verb","example":"The caretaker’s estimate assumes volunteers will water the beds.","exampleZh":"管理员的估算假设志愿者会给种植床浇水。","collocation":"assume volunteer support","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v2","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assumption","zh":"假设","pos":"noun","example":"That assumption should be checked before the budget is approved.","exampleZh":"预算获批前应核实该假设。","collocation":"check an assumption","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v3","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unrestricted","zh":"无限制的","pos":"adjective","example":"The teacher proposes practical lessons rather than unrestricted public access.","exampleZh":"教师建议开展实践课，而非无限制公众开放。","collocation":"unrestricted access","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v4","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coexist","zh":"共存","pos":"verb","example":"These purposes can coexist if responsibilities are clearly assigned.","exampleZh":"如果职责分配清楚，这些用途可以共存。","collocation":"purposes can coexist","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v5","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"first phase","zh":"第一阶段","pos":"phrase","example":"I recommend a smaller first phase with accessible raised beds.","exampleZh":"我建议第一阶段规模较小，采用便于使用的高架种植床。","collocation":"first phase","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v6","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opportunity cost","zh":"机会成本","pos":"phrase","example":"The report should acknowledge the opportunity cost of using the land.","exampleZh":"报告应承认使用这块地的机会成本。","collocation":"opportunity cost","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v7","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"synthesis","zh":"综合分析","pos":"noun","example":"A synthesis explains relationships between sources instead of listing summaries.","exampleZh":"综合分析解释来源之间的关系，而非罗列摘要。","collocation":"a balanced synthesis","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v8","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underlying","zh":"背后的","pos":"adjective","example":"Where accounts differ, I would identify the underlying question.","exampleZh":"当说法不一致时，我会找出背后的问题。","collocation":"the underlying question","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v9","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision rule","zh":"决策规则","pos":"phrase","example":"The committee needs a decision rule for continuing the project.","exampleZh":"委员会需要决定是否继续项目的规则。","collocation":"decision rule","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v10","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"My conclusion is provisional because summer staffing remains uncertain.","exampleZh":"我的结论是暂定的，因为暑期人员仍不确定。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v11","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"as well as","zh":"以及","pos":"phrase","example":"The next report should show what happened as well as what was planned.","exampleZh":"下一份报告既应说明计划，也应说明实际情况。","collocation":"as well as","register":"neutral","id":"c1-10:v12","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"phased","zh":"分阶段的","pos":"adjective","example":"A phased plan separates immediate access from later expansion.","exampleZh":"分阶段方案区分即时可及与后续扩展。","collocation":"a phased approach","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v13","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interdependency","zh":"相互依赖","pos":"noun","example":"The report explains the interdependency between access and maintenance.","exampleZh":"报告解释可及性与维护的相互依赖。","collocation":"identify interdependencies","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v14","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defer","zh":"推迟","pos":"verb","example":"The committee may defer construction until maintenance is funded.","exampleZh":"委员会可推迟施工至维护资金落实。","collocation":"defer a decision","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v15","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coherent","zh":"连贯一致的","pos":"adjective","example":"A coherent option must include both equipment and care.","exampleZh":"完整一致方案须包含设备与照管。","collocation":"a coherent proposal","register":"formal","id":"c1-10:v16","level":"C1","chapter":52,"module":"c1-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"objection","zh":"反对意见","pos":"noun","example":"The strongest objection is that unused places deny others an opportunity.","exampleZh":"最有力的反对意见是空位剥夺了他人的机会。","collocation":"a strong objection","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v1","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"legitimate","zh":"合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"That concern is legitimate even if we reject the current fee.","exampleZh":"即使反对现有费用，这一担忧仍然合理。","collocation":"a legitimate concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v2","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uneven","zh":"不均等的","pos":"adjective","example":"I agree that cancellations have costs, but the burden is uneven.","exampleZh":"我赞同取消有成本，但负担并不均等。","collocation":"an uneven burden","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v3","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"beyond their control","zh":"超出他们的控制","pos":"phrase","example":"People with caring duties may face changes beyond their control.","exampleZh":"承担照护责任的人可能面临无法控制的变化。","collocation":"beyond their control","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v4","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"need not","zh":"不一定需要","pos":"phrase","example":"Removing the fee need not mean removing every consequence.","exampleZh":"取消费用不一定意味着取消一切后果。","collocation":"need not","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v5","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"waiting list","zh":"候补名单","pos":"phrase","example":"A waiting list could fill some places if notice is prompt.","exampleZh":"若及时通知，候补名单可以填补部分空位。","collocation":"waiting list","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v6","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"objective","zh":"目标","pos":"noun","example":"The alternative must be judged against the same practical objective.","exampleZh":"替代方案必须按同一实际目标评判。","collocation":"a practical objective","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v7","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frequently","zh":"频繁地","pos":"adverb","example":"One example does not establish how frequently the problem occurs.","exampleZh":"一个例子无法证明问题出现的频率。","collocation":"occur frequently","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v8","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"personal failing","zh":"个人缺点","pos":"phrase","example":"We should avoid treating a disagreement as a personal failing.","exampleZh":"我们应避免把分歧当成个人缺点。","collocation":"personal failing","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v9","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjustment","zh":"调整","pos":"noun","example":"If repeat absences increase, the trial may require adjustment.","exampleZh":"如果反复缺席增加，试行可能需要调整。","collocation":"a necessary adjustment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v10","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conditional","zh":"有条件的","pos":"adjective","example":"My support is conditional on keeping a clear attendance record.","exampleZh":"我的支持以保留清晰出勤记录为条件。","collocation":"conditional support","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v11","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"address","zh":"回应处理","pos":"verb","example":"A fair reply addresses the concern rather than attacking its speaker.","exampleZh":"公允回应处理担忧，而非攻击发言者。","collocation":"address a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-11:v12","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exemption","zh":"豁免","pos":"noun","example":"A limited exemption should have a clear and private procedure.","exampleZh":"有限豁免应有明确且私密的程序。","collocation":"grant an exemption","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v13","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"burdensome","zh":"负担过重的","pos":"adjective","example":"A rule can be predictable yet disproportionately burdensome.","exampleZh":"规则可以可预测，却负担不相称。","collocation":"a burdensome requirement","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v14","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rebuttal","zh":"反驳","pos":"noun","example":"A convincing rebuttal answers the strongest opposing reason.","exampleZh":"有力反驳回应最强反方理由。","collocation":"a convincing rebuttal","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v15","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionality","zh":"相称性","pos":"noun","example":"The committee should assess the proportionality of the fee.","exampleZh":"委员会应评估费用是否相称。","collocation":"assess proportionality","register":"formal","id":"c1-11:v16","level":"C1","chapter":54,"module":"c1-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underlying interest","zh":"背后的利益","pos":"phrase","example":"The choir’s underlying interest is a predictable weekly schedule.","exampleZh":"合唱团背后的利益是可预测的每周安排。","collocation":"underlying interest","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v1","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"extra time","zh":"额外时间","pos":"phrase","example":"The theatre needs extra time before performances, not every Thursday.","exampleZh":"剧团需要演出前加时，而非每个星期四。","collocation":"extra time","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v2","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"in exchange for","zh":"交换","pos":"phrase","example":"Would you consider an earlier start in exchange for two longer sessions?","exampleZh":"你愿意用更早开始换取两次较长排练吗？","collocation":"in exchange for","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v3","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"前提是","pos":"conjunction","example":"We could accept that provided the dates are agreed in advance.","exampleZh":"我们可以接受，前提是日期提前商定。","collocation":"provided the dates are agreed","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v4","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"constraint","zh":"限制","pos":"noun","example":"The closing time is a fixed constraint rather than a bargaining point.","exampleZh":"关门时间是固定限制，而非讨价还价点。","collocation":"a fixed constraint","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v5","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"essential","zh":"必要的","pos":"adjective","example":"Let us separate what is essential from what is merely preferred.","exampleZh":"让我们区分必要条件和偏好。","collocation":"an essential condition","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v6","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tentative","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"A tentative agreement still needs confirmation from both coordinators.","exampleZh":"暂定协议仍需双方协调员确认。","collocation":"a tentative agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v7","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"notify","zh":"通知","pos":"verb","example":"Who would notify members when the usual timetable changes?","exampleZh":"惯常时间表变化时由谁通知成员？","collocation":"notify members","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v8","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unforeseen","zh":"意外的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should record a procedure for handling an unforeseen clash.","exampleZh":"我们应记录处理意外冲突的流程。","collocation":"an unforeseen clash","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v9","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commit","zh":"使承担承诺","pos":"verb","example":"I cannot commit the other group without consulting its organiser.","exampleZh":"未经咨询组织者，我不能代表另一组承诺。","collocation":"commit a group to an agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v10","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exception","zh":"例外","pos":"noun","example":"To confirm, the exception applies only to the two named dates.","exampleZh":"确认一下，例外仅适用于列明的两个日期。","collocation":"a limited exception","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v11","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"workable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A workable agreement makes responsibilities and review dates explicit.","exampleZh":"可行协议会明确职责和复查日期。","collocation":"a workable agreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-12:v12","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consolidate","zh":"汇总整合","pos":"verb","example":"One coordinator should consolidate conflicting requests.","exampleZh":"一名协调员应汇总相互冲突的请求。","collocation":"consolidate feedback","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v13","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"authorised","zh":"获授权的","pos":"adjective","example":"Only an authorised representative can commit the group.","exampleZh":"只有获授权代表能替团队承诺。","collocation":"an authorised representative","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v14","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stipulate","zh":"明确规定","pos":"verb","example":"The agreement should stipulate the access condition.","exampleZh":"协议应明确通行条件。","collocation":"stipulate a condition","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v15","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency","zh":"应变安排","pos":"noun","example":"The timetable needs a contingency if the keyholder is unavailable.","exampleZh":"钥匙管理者无空时，时间表需要应变安排。","collocation":"a contingency plan","register":"formal","id":"c1-12:v16","level":"C1","chapter":55,"module":"c1-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"purpose","zh":"目的","pos":"noun","example":"The purpose of this report is to evaluate the booking trial.","exampleZh":"本报告旨在评估预约试行。","collocation":"the purpose of a report","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v1","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cover","zh":"覆盖","pos":"verb","example":"The records cover completed bookings but not every unsuccessful attempt.","exampleZh":"记录覆盖已完成预约，却不包含每次失败尝试。","collocation":"cover completed bookings","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v2","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"it was observed","zh":"观察发现","pos":"phrase","example":"It was observed that some rooms remained empty after being reserved.","exampleZh":"观察发现一些房间被预约后仍然空置。","collocation":"it was observed","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v3","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"indicate","zh":"表明","pos":"verb","example":"Student comments indicate frustration, although their frequency is uncertain.","exampleZh":"学生意见反映不满，但其频率尚不确定。","collocation":"indicate frustration","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v4","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interpret","zh":"解读","pos":"verb","example":"These findings should be interpreted within the limited observation period.","exampleZh":"应在有限观察期内解读这些发现。","collocation":"interpret the findings","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v5","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"release rule","zh":"释放规则","pos":"phrase","example":"An automatic release rule could reduce unused reservations.","exampleZh":"自动释放规则可能减少闲置预约。","collocation":"release rule","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v6","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"it is recommended","zh":"建议","pos":"phrase","example":"It is recommended that the rule be tested before wider adoption.","exampleZh":"建议先测试该规则，再扩大采用。","collocation":"it is recommended","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v7","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"monitor","zh":"监测","pos":"verb","example":"The facilities team should be responsible for monitoring room availability.","exampleZh":"设施团队应负责监测房间可用性。","collocation":"monitor availability","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v8","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"immediate","zh":"立即的","pos":"adjective","example":"The report separates immediate actions from questions requiring further study.","exampleZh":"报告区分立即行动与需进一步研究的问题。","collocation":"immediate actions","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v9","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"traceable","zh":"可追溯的","pos":"adjective","example":"A finding should be traceable to the material that supports it.","exampleZh":"发现应能追溯到支持它的材料。","collocation":"traceable evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v10","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"heading","zh":"标题","pos":"noun","example":"Headings help the reader locate evidence and recommendations.","exampleZh":"标题帮助读者定位证据与建议。","collocation":"a clear heading","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v11","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unsupported","zh":"无依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"The conclusion should not introduce an unsupported new claim.","exampleZh":"结论不应引入无依据的新主张。","collocation":"an unsupported claim","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v12","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"occupancy","zh":"实际占用","pos":"noun","example":"Reservation data should be compared with observed occupancy.","exampleZh":"预约数据应与观察到的实际占用比较。","collocation":"room occupancy","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v13","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attainment","zh":"学业成果","pos":"noun","example":"The operational review does not establish academic attainment.","exampleZh":"运营评估不能确立学业成果。","collocation":"academic attainment","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v14","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"actionable","zh":"可据以行动的","pos":"adjective","example":"An actionable recommendation names an owner and a deadline.","exampleZh":"可执行建议明确负责人和期限。","collocation":"an actionable recommendation","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v15","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"interim","zh":"中期的","pos":"adjective","example":"An interim finding may require action before the full report.","exampleZh":"中期发现可能在完整报告前就需行动。","collocation":"an interim report","register":"formal","id":"c1-13:v16","level":"C1","chapter":57,"module":"c1-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposed scheme","zh":"拟议计划","pos":"phrase","example":"The proposed scheme would help new students navigate routine college life.","exampleZh":"拟议计划将帮助新生适应日常校园生活。","collocation":"proposed scheme","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v1","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"replace","zh":"替代","pos":"verb","example":"Mentors would offer guidance rather than replace professional advisers.","exampleZh":"导师提供指引，而非替代专业顾问。","collocation":"replace professional advice","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v2","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cohort","zh":"同批群体","pos":"noun","example":"A small first cohort would keep supervision manageable.","exampleZh":"首批人数较少有助于管理督导。","collocation":"a small first cohort","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v3","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"publicity","zh":"宣传","pos":"noun","example":"The budget must include training as well as publicity.","exampleZh":"预算必须包括培训及宣传。","collocation":"publicity costs","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v4","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"boundaries","zh":"边界","pos":"noun","example":"Potential benefits depend on consistent contact and clear boundaries.","exampleZh":"潜在益处取决于稳定联系和明确边界。","collocation":"clear boundaries","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v5","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"voluntary","zh":"自愿的","pos":"adjective","example":"Participation should be voluntary for both mentors and new students.","exampleZh":"导师和新生都应自愿参加。","collocation":"voluntary participation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v6","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refer","zh":"转介","pos":"verb","example":"We should specify how a mentor can refer a difficult concern.","exampleZh":"我们应明确导师如何转介棘手问题。","collocation":"refer a concern","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v7","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coordinator","zh":"协调员","pos":"noun","example":"The proposal identifies a coordinator rather than assuming shared responsibility.","exampleZh":"提案明确协调员，而非假定共同负责。","collocation":"a named coordinator","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v8","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inform","zh":"为……提供依据","pos":"verb","example":"A review after one term would inform any expansion.","exampleZh":"一学期后复查将为扩展提供依据。","collocation":"inform an expansion decision","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v9","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"induction","zh":"入学介绍","pos":"noun","example":"The alternative is to improve existing induction sessions first.","exampleZh":"另一选择是先改善现有入学介绍。","collocation":"an induction session","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v10","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establish","zh":"确定","pos":"verb","example":"A persuasive proposal acknowledges what it cannot yet establish.","exampleZh":"有说服力的提案会承认尚不能确定之处。","collocation":"establish a benefit","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v11","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"released","zh":"拨付","pos":"verb","example":"Funding should be released only when the training plan is agreed.","exampleZh":"只有培训计划获同意后才应拨付资金。","collocation":"funding is released","register":"neutral","id":"c1-14:v12","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"opportunity cost","zh":"机会成本","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Coordinator time has an opportunity cost even within an existing salary.","exampleZh":"即使工资已存在，协调时间仍有机会成本。","collocation":"recognise opportunity cost","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v13","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"safeguard","zh":"保障措施","pos":"noun","example":"A referral route is an essential safeguard for mentors.","exampleZh":"转介渠道是导师的重要保障。","collocation":"an essential safeguard","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v14","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"remit","zh":"职责范围","pos":"noun","example":"The proposal must define the mentor’s remit.","exampleZh":"提案必须界定导师职责范围。","collocation":"within the remit","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v15","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"viable","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reduced budget still needs a viable supervision plan.","exampleZh":"预算减少后仍须有可行督导方案。","collocation":"a viable plan","register":"formal","id":"c1-14:v16","level":"C1","chapter":58,"module":"c1-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"central purpose","zh":"核心目的","pos":"phrase","example":"Our central purpose is accessible learning rather than maximum attendance.","exampleZh":"我们的核心目的是可及学习，而非最大人数。","collocation":"central purpose","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v1","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"match","zh":"匹配","pos":"verb","example":"The first programme should match the number of trained facilitators.","exampleZh":"首期安排应与受训引导员人数相匹配。","collocation":"match available resources","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v2","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirmed","zh":"已确认的","pos":"adjective","example":"We have distinguished confirmed resources from requested support.","exampleZh":"我们已经区分确认资源和申请中的支持。","collocation":"confirmed resources","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v3","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"adjustment","zh":"调整","pos":"noun","example":"The access plan includes a route for requesting individual adjustments.","exampleZh":"可及性计划包含申请个别调整的渠道。","collocation":"a necessary adjustment","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v4","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reach","zh":"触及","pos":"verb","example":"Demand remains uncertain because the survey reached existing members only.","exampleZh":"需求仍不确定，因为调查仅触及现有会员。","collocation":"reach existing members","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v5","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limited form","zh":"有限形式","pos":"phrase","example":"The project can proceed in a limited form while that gap is investigated.","exampleZh":"调查该缺口期间，项目可以有限形式开展。","collocation":"limited form","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v6","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"challenge","zh":"质疑","pos":"noun","example":"I would respond to that challenge by narrowing the claim.","exampleZh":"我会通过收窄主张来回应这一质疑。","collocation":"respond to a challenge","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v7","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"衡量体现","pos":"verb","example":"The proposed measure captures participation, not learning by itself.","exampleZh":"拟议指标衡量参与，而非学习本身。","collocation":"capture participation","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v8","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"work produced","zh":"产出作品","pos":"phrase","example":"A review should include work produced as well as participant comments.","exampleZh":"复核应包括产出作品及参与者意见。","collocation":"work produced","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v9","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reasoning","zh":"推理理由","pos":"noun","example":"Our report records the reasoning behind each significant trade-off.","exampleZh":"我们的报告记录每项重要取舍背后的理由。","collocation":"clear reasoning","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v10","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"assumption","zh":"假设","pos":"noun","example":"The final presentation should invite questions about uncertain assumptions.","exampleZh":"最终展示应接受对不确定假设的提问。","collocation":"check an assumption","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v11","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Independent language review","zh":"独立语言评估","pos":"phrase","example":"Independent language review remains necessary before claiming a C1 level.","exampleZh":"在声称达到C1前，仍需独立语言评估。","collocation":"Independent language review","register":"neutral","id":"c1-15:v12","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconcile","zh":"协调一致","pos":"verb","example":"The team must reconcile access needs with limited space.","exampleZh":"团队须协调可及需求与有限空间。","collocation":"reconcile competing priorities","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v13","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"有理据可支持的","pos":"adjective","example":"A defensible plan can change under questioning.","exampleZh":"有理据的方案可以在质询后改变。","collocation":"a defensible judgement","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v14","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"binding","zh":"有约束力的","pos":"adjective","example":"An initial discussion is not a binding commitment.","exampleZh":"初步讨论不是有约束力的承诺。","collocation":"a binding commitment","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v15","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"responsiveness","zh":"回应与调整能力","pos":"noun","example":"The review assesses responsiveness as well as prepared explanation.","exampleZh":"复核评估即时回应能力及预备解释。","collocation":"demonstrate responsiveness","register":"formal","id":"c1-15:v16","level":"C1","chapter":60,"module":"c1-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"first attempts","zh":"首次作答","pos":"phrase","example":"My portfolio distinguishes first attempts from revised work.","exampleZh":"我的档案区分首次作答和修改作品。","collocation":"first attempts","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v1","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transfer","zh":"迁移","pos":"noun","example":"Familiar material can show learning without proving general transfer.","exampleZh":"熟悉材料能展示学习，却不能证明普遍迁移。","collocation":"general transfer","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v2","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rehearse","zh":"排练","pos":"verb","example":"I need evidence from tasks that I have not rehearsed.","exampleZh":"我需要未经排练任务的证据。","collocation":"rehearse an answer","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v3","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"separately","zh":"分别地","pos":"adverb","example":"Listening, reading, writing and interaction should be reviewed separately.","exampleZh":"听力、阅读、写作和互动应分别评估。","collocation":"review separately","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v4","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"transcript","zh":"转写文本","pos":"noun","example":"A transcript cannot establish every aspect of spoken performance.","exampleZh":"转写文本不能证明口语表现的每个方面。","collocation":"a speaking transcript","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v5","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"permitted","zh":"允许的","pos":"adjective","example":"An external reviewer should know what help was permitted.","exampleZh":"外部评阅者应了解允许了哪些帮助。","collocation":"permitted assistance","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v6","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disagreement","zh":"分歧","pos":"noun","example":"A disagreement between reviewers calls for examination of the evidence.","exampleZh":"评阅者分歧需要检验证据。","collocation":"reviewer disagreement","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v7","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uneven profile","zh":"不均衡能力分布","pos":"phrase","example":"I would treat an uneven profile as information for planning.","exampleZh":"我会把不均衡能力作为规划信息。","collocation":"uneven profile","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v8","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"recurring","zh":"反复出现的","pos":"adjective","example":"The next plan should target recurring weaknesses rather than every possible error.","exampleZh":"下一阶段计划应针对反复弱点，而非所有可能错误。","collocation":"recurring weaknesses","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v9","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"retention","zh":"保持","pos":"noun","example":"A delayed task with new material helps check retention and transfer.","exampleZh":"使用新材料的延迟任务有助于检查保持和迁移。","collocation":"long-term retention","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v10","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proficiency","zh":"熟练程度","pos":"noun","example":"Course completion is not an official proficiency certificate.","exampleZh":"课程完成不等于官方能力证书。","collocation":"language proficiency","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v11","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uncertainty","zh":"不确定性","pos":"noun","example":"Any score estimate should state its evidence and uncertainty.","exampleZh":"任何分数估计都应说明证据及不确定性。","collocation":"state uncertainty","register":"neutral","id":"c1-16:v12","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unaided","zh":"无帮助的","pos":"adjective","example":"An unaided sample must be distinguished from supported revision.","exampleZh":"无帮助样本须与受支持修改区分。","collocation":"unaided performance","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v13","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"materially","zh":"实质上","pos":"adverb","example":"If assessors disagree materially, further evidence may be needed.","exampleZh":"若评阅者实质分歧，可能需要更多证据。","collocation":"differ materially","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v14","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"triangulate","zh":"多来源交叉核查","pos":"verb","example":"Reviewers triangulate recordings, written work and task conditions.","exampleZh":"评阅者交叉核查录音、作品与任务条件。","collocation":"triangulate evidence","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v15","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"calibration","zh":"评分校准","pos":"noun","example":"Calibration requires shared criteria and discussion of actual samples.","exampleZh":"校准需要共同标准和实际样本讨论。","collocation":"reviewer calibration","register":"formal","id":"c1-16:v16","level":"C1","chapter":64,"module":"c1-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"plausible","zh":"似乎合理的","pos":"adjective","example":"A plausible account is not necessarily the most probable one.","exampleZh":"似乎合理的解释不一定就是最可能的解释。","collocation":"a plausible account","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v1","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"establishing","zh":"证明","pos":"verb","example":"The filter reduced visible particles without establishing that the water was safe.","exampleZh":"过滤器减少了可见颗粒，却没有证明水可以安全饮用。","collocation":"establishing safety","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v2","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualify the claim","zh":"给论断加上限定","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We should qualify the claim before it acquires a life of its own.","exampleZh":"我们应在这一说法自行传播之前加上限定。","collocation":"qualify the claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v3","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"holds","zh":"成立","pos":"verb","example":"The result holds under the conditions specified in the report.","exampleZh":"这一结果在报告明确的条件下成立。","collocation":"the result holds","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v4","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"amount to","zh":"等同于","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Absence of detected harm does not amount to evidence of harmlessness.","exampleZh":"未检测到危害不等于存在无害的证据。","collocation":"amount to evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v5","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conveys","zh":"传达","pos":"verb","example":"Her wording conveys confidence without implying that the question is settled.","exampleZh":"她的措辞表现出信心，却没有暗示问题已有定论。","collocation":"conveys confidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v6","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substantiates","zh":"用证据支持","pos":"verb","example":"The observation substantiates a narrower claim than the advertisement makes.","exampleZh":"这一观察结果支持的论断比广告所作的更有限。","collocation":"substantiates a claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v7","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"refers to","zh":"指的是","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Effectiveness here refers to clarity rather than chemical purity.","exampleZh":"这里的有效指的是清澈程度，而不是化学纯度。","collocation":"refers to clarity","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v8","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reserve","zh":"留作专用","pos":"verb","example":"I would reserve the word proven for a substantially stronger body of evidence.","exampleZh":"我会把已证实这个词留给强得多的证据体系。","collocation":"reserve the word","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v9","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consequential","zh":"关系到实际后果的","pos":"adjective","example":"The distinction is consequential rather than merely terminological.","exampleZh":"这个区别关系到实际后果，而不只是术语。","collocation":"consequential distinction","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v10","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"count as","zh":"算作","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Could you specify what would count as evidence against your interpretation?","exampleZh":"你能明确什么样的证据会推翻你的解读吗？","collocation":"count as evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v11","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"defensible","zh":"有依据的","pos":"adjective","example":"The revised label is less dramatic but considerably more defensible.","exampleZh":"修改后的标签没那么醒目，但有依据得多。","collocation":"a defensible conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-01:v12","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"scope","zh":"适用范围","pos":"noun","example":"The scope of the claim must remain visible.","exampleZh":"论断的适用范围必须清楚可见。","collocation":"the scope of a claim","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v13","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclaimer","zh":"免责声明","pos":"noun","example":"A disclaimer cannot repair a misleading central message.","exampleZh":"免责声明不能补救误导的核心信息。","collocation":"a misleading disclaimer","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v14","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"warrant","zh":"正当依据","pos":"noun","example":"The observation supplies no warrant for that guarantee.","exampleZh":"观察结果未给该保证提供依据。","collocation":"a warrant for","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v15","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"misconception","zh":"误解","pos":"noun","example":"The demonstration could reinforce a dangerous misconception.","exampleZh":"演示可能强化危险的误解。","collocation":"reinforce a misconception","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-01:v16","level":"C2","chapter":65,"module":"c2-01","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"on thin ice","zh":"处于不稳妥的状态","pos":"idiom","example":"The opening scene is on thin ice, so another rehearsal would help.","exampleZh":"开场这一幕还不稳妥，因此再排练一次会有帮助。","collocation":"be on thin ice","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v1","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"figuratively","zh":"以比喻方式","pos":"adverb","example":"I took the warning figuratively rather than as a literal instruction.","exampleZh":"我把这句警告理解为比喻，而非字面指令。","collocation":"interpret figuratively","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v2","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pull the plug","zh":"终止项目","pos":"idiom","example":"Before we pull the plug, let us establish what can still be repaired.","exampleZh":"在终止项目之前，我们先确定还有哪些地方可以补救。","collocation":"pull the plug","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v3","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"testing the waters","zh":"试探反应","pos":"idiom","example":"She was testing the waters rather than committing the committee to a decision.","exampleZh":"她是在试探反应，而非让委员会承诺作出决定。","collocation":"testing the waters","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v4","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fell flat","zh":"未能奏效","pos":"idiom","example":"The joke fell flat because its cultural reference was unfamiliar.","exampleZh":"这个笑话没有效果，因为其中的文化背景不为人熟悉。","collocation":"a joke fell flat","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v5","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"soften criticism","zh":"缓和批评","pos":"verb phrase","example":"An idiom can soften criticism while making its practical meaning less explicit.","exampleZh":"习语可以缓和批评，同时让实际含义不那么明确。","collocation":"soften criticism","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v6","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"shaky","zh":"不稳妥的","pos":"adjective","example":"When you say the scene is shaky, which change are you requesting?","exampleZh":"你说这一幕不稳妥，具体希望作出什么修改？","collocation":"a shaky scene","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v7","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"vulnerability","zh":"脆弱性","pos":"noun","example":"The metaphor implies vulnerability, not inevitable failure.","exampleZh":"这个比喻暗示的是脆弱，而非必然失败。","collocation":"imply vulnerability","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v8","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"avoid","zh":"避免","pos":"verb","example":"I would avoid that expression until I knew how the audience understood it.","exampleZh":"在了解听众如何理解之前，我会避免使用那个表达。","collocation":"avoid an expression","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v9","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"formal cancellation","zh":"正式取消","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Her colourful phrasing should not be mistaken for a formal cancellation.","exampleZh":"她生动的措辞不应被误认为正式取消。","collocation":"formal cancellation","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v10","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"surrounding exchange","zh":"前后的对话","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The expression makes sense once the surrounding exchange is considered.","exampleZh":"考虑前后的对话后，这个表达就说得通了。","collocation":"the surrounding exchange","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v11","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"preserved","zh":"保留了","pos":"verb","example":"A short clarification preserved the humour and prevented a costly misunderstanding.","exampleZh":"简短的澄清保留了幽默，也避免了代价高昂的误解。","collocation":"preserved the humour","register":"informal to neutral; figurative usage","id":"c2-02:v12","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"figurative","zh":"比喻的","pos":"adjective","example":"A figurative warning still needs a clear practical consequence.","exampleZh":"比喻警告仍需清楚说明实际后果。","collocation":"a figurative warning","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v13","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"referent","zh":"所指对象","pos":"noun","example":"The referent of shaky was unclear in that discussion.","exampleZh":"讨论中不稳妥一词所指不清。","collocation":"identify the referent","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v14","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ambiguity","zh":"歧义","pos":"noun","example":"The ambiguity concerned both language and the work itself.","exampleZh":"歧义既关乎语言也关乎工作本身。","collocation":"resolve an ambiguity","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v15","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commitment","zh":"承诺","pos":"noun","example":"The remark was not a commitment to cancel.","exampleZh":"这句话不构成取消的承诺。","collocation":"make a commitment","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-02:v16","level":"C2","chapter":66,"module":"c2-02","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasible","zh":"可行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Would it be feasible to send the records before Thursday afternoon?","exampleZh":"能否在周四下午之前发送这些记录？","collocation":"a feasible deadline","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v1","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"impractical","zh":"不现实的","pos":"adjective","example":"We need the list today, but please tell us if that deadline is impractical.","exampleZh":"我们今天需要清单，但如果这个期限不现实，请告诉我们。","collocation":"an impractical request","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v2","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"courteous","zh":"有礼貌的","pos":"adjective","example":"The request sounds courteous without concealing its urgency.","exampleZh":"这个请求听起来很有礼貌，也没有掩盖紧迫性。","collocation":"a courteous message","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v3","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ambiguity","zh":"歧义","pos":"noun","example":"Familiarity permits brevity, but it does not excuse avoidable ambiguity.","exampleZh":"熟悉的关系允许简短表达，却不能为可避免的歧义开脱。","collocation":"avoidable ambiguity","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v4","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"make the consequence explicit","zh":"明确说明后果","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I would make the consequence explicit rather than bury it in apologies.","exampleZh":"我会明确说明后果，而不是将其埋在道歉之中。","collocation":"make the consequence explicit","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v5","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deference","zh":"尊重与谦让","pos":"noun","example":"The donor may welcome a clear explanation more than elaborate deference.","exampleZh":"与繁复的谦辞相比，捐赠者或许更欢迎清楚的解释。","collocation":"elaborate deference","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v6","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accommodate","zh":"配合安排","pos":"verb","example":"Please let us know what timescale you could realistically accommodate.","exampleZh":"请告诉我们你实际能够配合的时间安排。","collocation":"accommodate a timescale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v7","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"established","zh":"建立","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"An informal greeting would imply a relationship we have not yet established.","exampleZh":"随意的问候会暗示一种我们尚未建立的关系。","collocation":"established rapport","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v8","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substance","zh":"实质","pos":"noun","example":"The substance of the request remains unchanged across the two versions.","exampleZh":"这两个版本的请求实质保持不变。","collocation":"the substance of a request","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v9","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entitled","zh":"自以为有权要求的","pos":"adjective","example":"That phrasing risks sounding entitled rather than merely efficient.","exampleZh":"那种措辞可能显得自以为有权要求，而非仅仅高效。","collocation":"sound entitled","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v10","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flexible","zh":"灵活的","pos":"adjective","example":"We can be direct about the deadline and flexible about the method.","exampleZh":"我们可以明确说明期限，同时在方法上保持灵活。","collocation":"a flexible method","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v11","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"register","zh":"语域","pos":"noun","example":"The appropriate register depends on audience, purpose and existing rapport.","exampleZh":"适当的语域取决于受众、目的和已有关系。","collocation":"an appropriate register","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-03:v12","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"dependency","zh":"依赖关系","pos":"noun","example":"The message should explain the project's dependency on the records.","exampleZh":"消息应解释项目对记录的依赖。","collocation":"a project dependency","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v13","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imposition","zh":"给人的负担","pos":"noun","example":"Acknowledging an imposition does not require hiding the request.","exampleZh":"承认给人带来负担不等于隐藏请求。","collocation":"acknowledge an imposition","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v14","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocity","zh":"相互配合","pos":"noun","example":"Reciprocity becomes possible when constraints are made explicit.","exampleZh":"限制说清后才可能相互配合。","collocation":"enable reciprocity","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v15","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"entitlement","zh":"自以为有权要求","pos":"noun","example":"Brevity can sound like entitlement without adequate context.","exampleZh":"没有充分语境时简短可能像强索。","collocation":"a sense of entitlement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-03:v16","level":"C2","chapter":67,"module":"c2-03","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cannot afford to miss","zh":"最不能错过","pos":"modal phrase","example":"It is the final connection that shift workers cannot afford to miss.","exampleZh":"轮班工作者最不能错过的正是最后一班接驳车。","collocation":"cannot afford to miss","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v1","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"imply","zh":"意味着","pos":"verb","example":"That priority does not imply that earlier journeys are unimportant.","exampleZh":"这种优先安排并不意味着较早的行程不重要。","collocation":"imply importance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v2","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Not once","zh":"一次也没有","pos":"adverbial phrase","example":"Not once during the trial did the revised timetable require another vehicle.","exampleZh":"在试行期间，修改后的时刻表一次也没有需要增加车辆。","collocation":"not once did","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v3","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Compelling though","zh":"虽有说服力","pos":"concessive construction","example":"Compelling though the example is, it cannot settle the entire funding question.","exampleZh":"这个例子虽有说服力，却不能解决整个拨款问题。","collocation":"compelling though it is","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v4","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allocation","zh":"拨款分配","pos":"noun","example":"What matters here is the effect of the next small allocation.","exampleZh":"这里重要的是下一笔小额拨款的作用。","collocation":"a funding allocation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v5","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定条件","pos":"noun","example":"The emphasis is justified only if the qualification remains visible.","exampleZh":"只有限定条件仍然清楚可见，这种强调才合理。","collocation":"an explicit qualification","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v6","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Far from","zh":"远非","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"Far from dismissing daytime users, the proposal addresses an additional gap.","exampleZh":"这个提案非但没有忽视日间乘客，反而是在弥补另一处缺口。","collocation":"far from dismissing","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v7","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hierarchy","zh":"等级排序","pos":"noun","example":"We should resist turning a practical priority into a moral hierarchy.","exampleZh":"我们应避免把实际优先事项变成人的价值排序。","collocation":"a moral hierarchy","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v8","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"draws attention to","zh":"突出","pos":"verb phrase","example":"The construction draws attention to the contrast without proving it.","exampleZh":"这种句式突出了对比，但并不能证明对比成立。","collocation":"draws attention to","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v9","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Had the trial lasted","zh":"假如试行曾持续","pos":"conditional construction","example":"Had the trial lasted longer, seasonal pressures might have emerged.","exampleZh":"如果试行持续更久，季节性压力可能就会出现。","collocation":"had the trial lasted","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v10","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"nevertheless","zh":"不过","pos":"adverb","example":"I accept the limitation; nevertheless, the immediate access problem remains.","exampleZh":"我接受这个局限；不过，眼前的出行问题仍然存在。","collocation":"nevertheless it remains","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v11","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exceed","zh":"超过","pos":"verb","example":"The rhetorical force of a sentence should not exceed its evidential support.","exampleZh":"一句话的修辞力度不应超过其证据支持。","collocation":"exceed the evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-04:v12","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"marginal","zh":"边际的","pos":"adjective","example":"The debate concerns the marginal benefit of one extra bus.","exampleZh":"讨论关乎增加一辆车带来的边际收益。","collocation":"marginal benefit","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v13","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"salience","zh":"显著性","pos":"noun","example":"Repetition increased the salience of the late connection.","exampleZh":"重复提高了末班接驳的重要性。","collocation":"increase the salience","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v14","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allocation","zh":"分配","pos":"noun","example":"The allocation should follow the published funding criteria.","exampleZh":"分配应遵循公布的拨款标准。","collocation":"funding allocation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v15","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"trade-off","zh":"权衡","pos":"noun","example":"The report acknowledges a trade-off between frequency and coverage.","exampleZh":"报告承认班次密度与覆盖范围存在权衡。","collocation":"acknowledge a trade-off","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-04:v16","level":"C2","chapter":68,"module":"c2-04","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"whereas","zh":"而","pos":"conjunction","example":"Mira supported the kitchen plan, whereas Arun was still discussing queues.","exampleZh":"米拉支持厨房方案，而阿伦仍在讨论排队问题。","collocation":"whereas others disagree","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v1","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proposed layout","zh":"建议的布局","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Was that agreement about the date or about the proposed layout?","exampleZh":"刚才的同意是针对日期，还是针对建议的布局？","collocation":"the proposed layout","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v2","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"condition","zh":"条件","pos":"noun","example":"Jo has raised a condition that neither proposal yet satisfies.","exampleZh":"乔提出了一个两个方案都还未满足的条件。","collocation":"meet a condition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v3","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"outstanding","zh":"尚未解决的","pos":"adjective","example":"Let me separate the decisions we have made from those still outstanding.","exampleZh":"让我把已作出的决定和仍悬而未决的问题分开。","collocation":"an outstanding decision","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v4","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"referent","zh":"所指对象","pos":"noun","example":"I lost the referent of that last yes during the overlap.","exampleZh":"多人同时说话时，我没听清最后那声同意针对什么。","collocation":"identify the referent","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v5","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"not necessarily","zh":"未必","pos":"adverbial phrase","example":"We appear to agree on timing, but not necessarily on facilities.","exampleZh":"我们似乎在时间上达成了一致，但设施方面未必如此。","collocation":"not necessarily agree","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v6","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"confirm","zh":"确认","pos":"verb","example":"Before we move on, could each person confirm their preferred option?","exampleZh":"在继续之前，每个人能否确认自己倾向的方案？","collocation":"confirm an option","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v7","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"competing proposal","zh":"竞争方案","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The interruption introduced a new constraint rather than a competing proposal.","exampleZh":"这次打断引入了新限制，而不是另一个竞争方案。","collocation":"a competing proposal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v8","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withdrawn","zh":"撤回了","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"Arun has withdrawn his objection to the date, not to the kitchen.","exampleZh":"阿伦撤回的是对日期的异议，而非对厨房的异议。","collocation":"withdrawn an objection","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v9","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"materially","zh":"实质性地","pos":"adverb","example":"The summary omitted a reservation that materially affects the decision.","exampleZh":"总结遗漏了一个实质影响决策的保留意见。","collocation":"materially affect","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v10","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provisional","zh":"暂定的","pos":"adjective","example":"I would mark that conclusion as provisional until the power supply is checked.","exampleZh":"在检查供电之前，我会把这个结论标为暂定。","collocation":"a provisional conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v11","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tracking changes","zh":"追踪变化","pos":"gerund phrase","example":"Following the discussion requires tracking changes, not merely recognising names.","exampleZh":"跟上讨论需要追踪变化，而不只是认出人名。","collocation":"tracking changes","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-05:v12","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"turn","zh":"话轮","pos":"noun","example":"The interruption changed the function of the next turn.","exampleZh":"打断改变了下一话轮的功能。","collocation":"a conversational turn","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v13","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"alignment","zh":"一致立场","pos":"noun","example":"An acknowledgement does not necessarily signal alignment.","exampleZh":"表示听到不一定表示立场一致。","collocation":"signal alignment","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v14","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"ratify","zh":"正式确认","pos":"verb","example":"The group must ratify the final wording.","exampleZh":"小组必须正式确认最终措辞。","collocation":"ratify an agreement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v15","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"sequence","zh":"话语顺序","pos":"noun","example":"The sequence identifies which question yes answered.","exampleZh":"话语顺序说明同意回答了哪个问题。","collocation":"a question sequence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-05:v16","level":"C2","chapter":69,"module":"c2-05","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"connected speech","zh":"连续语流","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I recognised the place name on paper but missed it in connected speech.","exampleZh":"我在纸上认得那个地名，却在连续语流中没听出来。","collocation":"connected speech","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v1","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"spell","zh":"拼写","pos":"verb","example":"Could you repeat the street name and then spell the final word?","exampleZh":"你能重复街道名，再拼一下最后那个词吗？","collocation":"spell a name","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v2","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"vowel pattern","zh":"元音模式","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An unfamiliar vowel pattern does not imply unfamiliar vocabulary.","exampleZh":"不熟悉的元音模式不意味着词汇不熟悉。","collocation":"a vowel pattern","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v3","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"verify","zh":"核实","pos":"verb","example":"I should verify the name instead of selecting the nearest familiar one.","exampleZh":"我应该核实名称，而不是选择最接近的熟悉名称。","collocation":"verify a name","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v4","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unaided","zh":"没有辅助的","pos":"adjective","example":"The written transcript cannot establish how much I understood unaided.","exampleZh":"书面转录不能证明我在没有辅助时听懂了多少。","collocation":"unaided comprehension","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v5","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"joined","zh":"连接","pos":"verb (past)","example":"Please correct me if I have joined two separate names together.","exampleZh":"如果我把两个独立名称连在了一起，请纠正我。","collocation":"joined two names","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v6","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unstressed syllables","zh":"非重读音节","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The difficulty emerged when the speaker reduced several unstressed syllables.","exampleZh":"说话者弱化了几个非重读音节时，困难就出现了。","collocation":"unstressed syllables","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v7","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"insufficient evidence","zh":"不充分的证据","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Exposure to one voice is insufficient evidence of broad accent comprehension.","exampleZh":"只接触一种声音不足以证明能广泛理解不同口音。","collocation":"insufficient evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v8","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"slowing down","zh":"放慢","pos":"gerund phrase","example":"I understood the individual words after slowing down the recording.","exampleZh":"放慢录音后，我听懂了各个单词。","collocation":"slowing down speech","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v9","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"consenting","zh":"已同意录音的","pos":"adjective","example":"Next time I will test the same skill with a different consenting speaker.","exampleZh":"下次我会用另一位已同意录音的说话者检验同一技能。","collocation":"a consenting speaker","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v10","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"actual audio tasks","zh":"真实音频任务","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Listening competence needs actual audio tasks, not printed scripts alone.","exampleZh":"听力能力需要真实音频任务，而不能只靠纸面脚本。","collocation":"actual audio tasks","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v11","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"clarification","zh":"澄清","pos":"noun","example":"Clarification is a communication skill rather than an admission of failure.","exampleZh":"澄清是一种沟通技能，而非承认失败。","collocation":"request clarification","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-06:v12","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconstruction","zh":"重建","pos":"noun","example":"Context can support reconstruction without proving exact perception.","exampleZh":"语境可支持重建，却不证明精确感知。","collocation":"contextual reconstruction","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v13","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accommodation","zh":"语言适应","pos":"noun","example":"Accommodation can help both speakers reach understanding.","exampleZh":"相互调整能帮助双方理解。","collocation":"mutual accommodation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v14","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stigmatisation","zh":"污名化","pos":"noun","example":"Training should avoid the stigmatisation of regional speech.","exampleZh":"训练应避免污名化地区语言。","collocation":"avoid stigmatisation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v15","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disambiguate","zh":"消除歧义","pos":"verb","example":"A spelling request can disambiguate similar names.","exampleZh":"请求拼写能区分相似名字。","collocation":"disambiguate a name","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-06:v16","level":"C2","chapter":70,"module":"c2-06","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"irony","zh":"反讽","pos":"noun","example":"The irony of the welcome ceremony became apparent when guests were asked to admire a newly installed ramp that ended at a locked door.","exampleZh":"当宾客被请去欣赏一条尽头却是锁门的新坡道时，欢迎仪式的反讽意味就显现了。","collocation":"situational irony","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v1","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"flawless","zh":"毫无缺点的","pos":"adjective","example":"A visitor described the planning as flawless while pointing towards the inaccessible entrance, making the literal praise difficult to reconcile with the scene.","exampleZh":"一位来访者指着无法通行的入口称规划完美无缺，让字面赞扬难以与眼前情景一致。","collocation":"flawless planning","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v2","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"target","zh":"所针对的对象","pos":"noun","example":"The target of the criticism appeared to be the arrangement rather than the volunteer who happened to be standing beside the entrance.","exampleZh":"批评所针对的似乎是安排本身，而非恰好站在入口旁的志愿者。","collocation":"the target of criticism","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v3","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"delivery","zh":"说话方式","pos":"noun","example":"Her measured delivery gave no exaggerated signal of amusement, although the contradiction between her words and her gesture remained available to the listeners.","exampleZh":"她平稳的说话方式并未夸张地表示幽默，不过言语与动作之间的矛盾仍可被听者察觉。","collocation":"a measured delivery","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v4","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deadpan","zh":"不动声色的","pos":"adjective","example":"A deadpan remark can depend on shared recognition, which cannot be assumed merely because two people speak the same language with confidence.","exampleZh":"不动声色的玩笑可能依赖共同认知，而不能仅因两个人都能自信使用同一语言就假定存在。","collocation":"a deadpan remark","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v5","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"incongruity","zh":"不协调","pos":"noun","example":"Some guests noticed the incongruity immediately, while another asked whether a second entrance had actually been opened for the occasion elsewhere.","exampleZh":"一些客人马上注意到不协调，另一位却问是否已在别处为活动开放第二入口。","collocation":"notice an incongruity","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v6","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"literal","zh":"字面的","pos":"adjective","example":"That question was reasonable under a literal reading, reminding the organiser that understanding the information and recognising the joke were separate achievements.","exampleZh":"按字面理解那个问题合情合理，提醒组织者理解信息和识别玩笑是两种不同的表现。","collocation":"a literal reading","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v7","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reception","zh":"接受效果","pos":"noun","example":"Audience reception also varied among those who understood the criticism: one laughed, whereas another considered public embarrassment an unhelpful response to an immediate problem.","exampleZh":"即使理解批评，受众反应也不同：有人笑了，有人觉得公开让人难堪无助于解决眼前问题。","collocation":"audience reception","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v8","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"认定属于","pos":"verb","example":"We should therefore hesitate to attribute a single intention or a uniform emotional effect to the remark on the basis of its wording alone.","exampleZh":"因此，不能仅凭措辞就轻易认定这句话只有一种意图或统一的情感效果。","collocation":"attribute an intention","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v9","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hostility","zh":"敌意","pos":"noun","example":"The volunteer initially heard hostility towards herself, but the visitor immediately asked who could find the key and offered to help redirect arrivals.","exampleZh":"志愿者起初觉得对方针对自己有敌意，但来访者随即问谁能找到钥匙，并主动帮忙引导来宾。","collocation":"evidence of hostility","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v10","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"qualification","zh":"限定","pos":"noun","example":"This action supplies an important qualification to a reading of deliberate humiliation, without proving that the wording had caused nobody any discomfort.","exampleZh":"这一行动为故意羞辱的解读提供重要限定，却不能证明措辞没有让任何人不舒服。","collocation":"an important qualification","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v11","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rapport","zh":"融洽关系","pos":"noun","example":"The visitor and the volunteer had not previously established rapport, so a style that worked among friends was carrying additional interpersonal risk.","exampleZh":"来访者与志愿者此前没有建立融洽关系，因此在朋友间有效的风格带来了额外的人际风险。","collocation":"establish rapport","register":"neutral","id":"c2-07:v12","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"repair","zh":"补救沟通","pos":"noun","example":"A short conversational repair followed: the visitor explained that she meant the design, not the person, and acknowledged the unfortunate timing of the joke.","exampleZh":"随后出现了简短的沟通补救：来访者解释针对的是设计而非个人，并承认玩笑时机不佳。","collocation":"conversational repair","register":"specialist","id":"c2-07:v13","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uptake","zh":"理解与回应","pos":"noun","example":"Checking the uptake meant asking whether the practical concern was understood, rather than requiring the volunteer to declare that she now found the comment funny.","exampleZh":"检查理解与回应意味着询问实际问题是否明白，而非要求志愿者声称现在觉得评论好笑。","collocation":"check the uptake","register":"specialist","id":"c2-07:v14","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate","zh":"适度的","pos":"adjective","example":"A proportionate response would preserve the access complaint, clarify the misunderstanding and avoid turning either participant into a symbol of complete insensitivity.","exampleZh":"适度回应应保留无障碍方面的投诉、澄清误解，并避免将任一方视为完全不体贴的象征。","collocation":"a proportionate response","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v15","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contextual","zh":"与语境有关的","pos":"adjective","example":"The case shows why contextual evidence can support an ironic reading while leaving important questions about intention, judgement and social effect open.","exampleZh":"这个案例说明语境证据可以支持反讽解读，同时让意图、判断和社会效果的重要问题保持开放。","collocation":"contextual evidence","register":"formal","id":"c2-07:v16","level":"C2","chapter":71,"module":"c2-07","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reservation","zh":"保留意见","pos":"noun","example":"The compliment is followed by a reservation that changes its practical force.","exampleZh":"这句赞扬之后的保留意见改变了它的实际作用。","collocation":"a significant reservation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v1","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withheld","zh":"暂未给予","pos":"verb (past)","example":"She did not reject the idea outright, but she withheld immediate approval.","exampleZh":"她没有直接否决想法，但暂未立即批准。","collocation":"withheld approval","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v2","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"The timetable would keep everyone occupied may imply concern about feasibility.","exampleZh":"时间表会让大家忙个不停可能暗示对可行性的担忧。","collocation":"practical feasibility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v3","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency plans","zh":"应急计划","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That interpretation is supported by the repeated requests for contingency plans.","exampleZh":"反复要求应急计划支持了这种解读。","collocation":"contingency plans","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v4","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归因于","pos":"verb","example":"I would not attribute personal hostility on the basis of that sentence.","exampleZh":"我不会仅凭那句话就认定她有个人敌意。","collocation":"attribute hostility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v5","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"misrepresent","zh":"歪曲","pos":"verb","example":"Quoting the praise alone would misrepresent the reviewers overall stance.","exampleZh":"只引用赞扬会歪曲评审者的整体立场。","collocation":"misrepresent a stance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v6","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"hedge","zh":"缓和措辞","pos":"noun","example":"The hedge softens the judgement without necessarily weakening the reservation.","exampleZh":"缓和措辞弱化了判断的语气，却未必减轻保留意见。","collocation":"a linguistic hedge","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v7","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"caution","zh":"谨慎","pos":"noun","example":"We can infer caution while remaining uncertain about its precise cause.","exampleZh":"我们可以推断存在谨慎态度，同时不确定其具体原因。","collocation":"infer caution","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v8","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"text","zh":"文本","pos":"noun","example":"Where does the text stop and your interpretation begin?","exampleZh":"文本到哪里结束，你的解读从哪里开始？","collocation":"text and interpretation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v9","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stance","zh":"立场","pos":"noun","example":"The proposed action gives stronger evidence of stance than the polite adjective.","exampleZh":"建议采取的行动比礼貌的形容词更能体现立场。","collocation":"overall stance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v10","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"支持","pos":"noun","example":"A useful summary preserves both the endorsement and its limits.","exampleZh":"有用的总结会同时保留支持意见及其限制。","collocation":"qualified endorsement","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v11","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradicted","zh":"与之冲突","pos":"verb (past)","example":"I would revise that inference if a later passage explicitly contradicted it.","exampleZh":"如果后文明确与之冲突，我会修改这个推断。","collocation":"contradicted an inference","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-08:v12","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"implicature","zh":"语用含意","pos":"noun","example":"The implicature depends on the surrounding questions.","exampleZh":"含意依赖前后的问题。","collocation":"a contextual implicature","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v13","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"endorsement","zh":"认可","pos":"noun","example":"Praise for one feature is not endorsement of the whole plan.","exampleZh":"赞扬一处不等于认可整个方案。","collocation":"qualified endorsement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v14","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribution","zh":"归因","pos":"noun","example":"The attribution of hostility exceeds the available evidence.","exampleZh":"认定敌意超出了现有证据。","collocation":"an unsupported attribution","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v15","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"corrigible","zh":"可修正的","pos":"adjective","example":"A useful inference remains corrigible when new evidence appears.","exampleZh":"新证据出现时，有用推断仍可修正。","collocation":"a corrigible judgement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-08:v16","level":"C2","chapter":72,"module":"c2-08","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"stated purpose","zh":"明确的目的","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The exhibition should first be judged against its stated purpose.","exampleZh":"首先应根据展览明确的目的来评价它。","collocation":"a stated purpose","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v1","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"limit context","zh":"限制背景信息","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Missing national statistics limit context without necessarily defeating the project.","exampleZh":"缺少全国数据限制了背景信息，却未必使项目失败。","collocation":"limit context","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v2","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"different exhibition","zh":"另一种展览","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The reviewer appears to demand a different exhibition rather than a better version.","exampleZh":"评论者似乎要求的是另一种展览，而非改进后的同一展览。","collocation":"a different exhibition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v3","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"omission","zh":"缺漏","pos":"noun","example":"That criticism identifies an omission but does not explain its significance.","exampleZh":"那条批评指出了缺漏，却未解释其重要性。","collocation":"a consequential omission","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v4","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"domestic scale","zh":"家庭层面的规模","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The domestic scale enables detail that a national survey might lose.","exampleZh":"家庭层面的规模保留了全国调查可能丢失的细节。","collocation":"domestic scale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v5","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"restricted scope","zh":"有限范围","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would distinguish restricted scope from inadequate execution.","exampleZh":"我会区分范围有限和执行不足。","collocation":"restricted scope","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v6","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"unanswered question","zh":"未获解答的问题","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The strongest objection concerns an unanswered question relevant to the exhibitions aim.","exampleZh":"最有力的异议关乎一个与展览目标相关却未获解答的问题。","collocation":"an unanswered question","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v7","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible criteria","zh":"相容的标准","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The comparison is unfair unless both works are assessed by compatible criteria.","exampleZh":"除非用相容标准评价两件作品，否则比较不公平。","collocation":"compatible criteria","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v8","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"represents","zh":"呈现","pos":"verb","example":"A persuasive review represents the work accurately before challenging it.","exampleZh":"有说服力的评论会先准确呈现作品，再提出质疑。","collocation":"represents the work","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v9","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enrich","zh":"丰富","pos":"verb","example":"The proposed context could enrich the exhibition without replacing its central focus.","exampleZh":"建议加入的背景可以丰富展览，而不取代其核心。","collocation":"enrich an exhibition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v10","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criterion","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"I disagree with the verdict because its criterion remains unstated.","exampleZh":"我不同意这个结论，因为它的标准未被说明。","collocation":"a relevant criterion","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-09:v11","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportionate judgement","zh":"适度的判断","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An engaging sentence is not a substitute for a proportionate judgement.","exampleZh":"生动的句子不能替代适度的判断。","collocation":"a proportionate judgement","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-09:v12","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"criterion","zh":"标准","pos":"noun","example":"The criterion should be relevant to the stated purpose.","exampleZh":"标准应与声明目的相关。","collocation":"a relevant criterion","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v13","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportion","zh":"分寸","pos":"noun","example":"The review needs a sense of proportion.","exampleZh":"评论需要分寸感。","collocation":"a sense of proportion","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v14","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exemption","zh":"豁免","pos":"noun","example":"Limited scope is not an exemption from scrutiny.","exampleZh":"范围有限不等于免受审查。","collocation":"an exemption from scrutiny","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v15","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"execution","zh":"执行成效","pos":"noun","example":"The criticism concerns execution rather than scale.","exampleZh":"批评关乎执行而非规模。","collocation":"assess execution","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-09:v16","level":"C2","chapter":73,"module":"c2-09","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"retrospect","zh":"回顾","pos":"noun","example":"In retrospect, Daniel described his last visit to the house as an errand, although his careful preparation suggested that more was at stake.","exampleZh":"回顾时，丹尼尔把最后一次拜访说成跑腿，但他精心准备的样子暗示事情并非如此简单。","collocation":"in retrospect","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v1","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"pretext","zh":"借口","pos":"noun","example":"Returning a borrowed atlas provided a convenient pretext for approaching a door he had avoided since the previous winter.","exampleZh":"归还借来的地图集为他接近那扇自去年冬天起一直回避的门提供了方便的借口。","collocation":"a convenient pretext","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v2","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withheld","zh":"隐而不说","pos":"verb (past)","example":"He withheld any explanation of the winter quarrel, assuring the reader that its details would contribute nothing to an understanding of the visit.","exampleZh":"他没有解释冬天的争吵，并向读者保证那些细节无助于理解这次拜访。","collocation":"withhold an explanation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v3","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conspicuous","zh":"醒目的","pos":"adjective","example":"That conspicuous omission mattered because the narrator otherwise remembered the colour of the envelope and the precise position of every chair.","exampleZh":"这一醒目的省略之所以重要，是因为叙述者对信封颜色及每张椅子的确切位置都记得清楚。","collocation":"a conspicuous omission","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v4","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"equivocal","zh":"有歧义的","pos":"adjective","example":"His sister answered with an equivocal gesture, moving aside without either inviting him in or asking him to remain outside.","exampleZh":"姐姐作出了含义不明的动作，向旁边让开，却既未请他进去，也未叫他留在外面。","collocation":"an equivocal gesture","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v5","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attribute","zh":"归因","pos":"verb","example":"Daniel attributed significance to her silence immediately, presenting it as proof that she had expected him to return with an apology.","exampleZh":"丹尼尔立即赋予她的沉默特殊意义，将其视为她期待自己回来道歉的证明。","collocation":"attribute significance to","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v6","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterpart","zh":"对应物","pos":"noun","example":"Her actual words supplied no verbal counterpart to that interpretation; she merely asked whether the atlas still contained the loose coastal map.","exampleZh":"她实际说的话并未印证这种解读；她只是问地图集里是否还夹着那张散页海岸地图。","collocation":"a verbal counterpart","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v7","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reassurance","zh":"安慰","pos":"noun","example":"He seemed to seek reassurance in the familiar objects, describing a repaired cup at greater length than the person holding it.","exampleZh":"他似乎在熟悉的物件中寻求安慰，对修补过的杯子的描写比对拿杯子的人更详尽。","collocation":"seek reassurance","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v8","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"focalisation","zh":"叙事聚焦","pos":"noun","example":"The restricted focalisation gives access to his expectations while leaving his sister's thoughts beyond the reader's direct knowledge throughout the encounter.","exampleZh":"受限制的叙事聚焦让读者了解他的期待，却始终无法直接得知姐姐的想法。","collocation":"restricted focalisation","register":"specialist","id":"c2-10:v9","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"discrepancy","zh":"不一致","pos":"noun","example":"A revealing discrepancy appears when he says the conversation was effortless and then reports three unsuccessful attempts to change its subject.","exampleZh":"一个意味深长的不一致出现在他声称交谈轻松自如、却又报告三次转移话题均未成功之时。","collocation":"a revealing discrepancy","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v10","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deflection","zh":"转移话题","pos":"noun","example":"Those attempts may be deliberate deflection, but the text does not identify the question he finds most difficult to answer honestly.","exampleZh":"这些尝试可能是有意回避，但文本并未指出哪个问题最令他难以坦诚回答。","collocation":"a deliberate deflection","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v11","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"tentative","zh":"试探性的","pos":"adjective","example":"A tentative reading would connect his attention to objects with his reluctance to acknowledge how the relationship has changed in his absence.","exampleZh":"一种试探性解读会把他对物件的关注与不愿承认离开后关系变化的态度联系起来。","collocation":"a tentative reading","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v12","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"plausibility","zh":"合理性","pos":"noun","example":"To test the plausibility of that reading, we must examine the final exchange rather than treating each earlier detail as independent confirmation.","exampleZh":"要检验这种解读的合理性，我们必须考察最后的对话，而不能把每个先前细节都当作独立印证。","collocation":"test the plausibility","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v13","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"undermine","zh":"削弱","pos":"verb","example":"His sisters offer to keep the atlas might undermine an interpretation of complete rejection, even though it does not establish reconciliation.","exampleZh":"姐姐提出留下地图集，可能削弱彻底拒绝的解读，尽管这也不能证明双方已经和解。","collocation":"undermine an interpretation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v14","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reconciliation","zh":"和解","pos":"noun","example":"A claim of reconciliation therefore requires more than Daniel's closing statement that everything important had finally been returned to its proper place.","exampleZh":"因此，声称和解需要比丹尼尔结尾所说重要东西终于归位更多的依据。","collocation":"a claim of reconciliation","register":"neutral","id":"c2-10:v15","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"restraint","zh":"克制","pos":"noun","example":"Interpretive restraint preserves the narrator's emotional complexity without turning uncertainty into permission to invent a family history the story never supplies.","exampleZh":"解读上的克制保留了叙述者情感的复杂性，却不把不确定性当成编造故事未交代的家史的许可。","collocation":"interpretive restraint","register":"formal","id":"c2-10:v16","level":"C2","chapter":74,"module":"c2-10","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"association","zh":"关联","pos":"noun","example":"Higher marks among room users establish an association rather than a causal effect.","exampleZh":"使用自习室者分数更高，表明关联而非因果效应。","collocation":"an observed association","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v1","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Prior attendance","zh":"先前出勤情况","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Prior attendance may influence both room use and examination performance.","exampleZh":"先前的出勤情况可能同时影响自习室使用和考试表现。","collocation":"prior attendance","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v2","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selection bias","zh":"选择偏差","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The proposed mechanism is credible but does not eliminate selection bias.","exampleZh":"提出的机制可信，却不能消除选择偏差。","collocation":"selection bias","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v3","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"comparison","zh":"比较","pos":"noun","example":"We need a comparison that addresses differences already present before the change.","exampleZh":"我们需要一种能够处理变化前既有差异的比较。","collocation":"a fair comparison","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v4","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compatible with","zh":"与之相容","pos":"adjectival phrase","example":"The result is compatible with several explanations the design cannot separate.","exampleZh":"这个结果与研究设计无法区分的多种解释相容。","collocation":"compatible with alternatives","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v5","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"narrow the conclusion","zh":"缩小结论范围","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I would narrow the conclusion instead of dismissing the observation entirely.","exampleZh":"我会缩小结论范围，而不是完全否定观察结果。","collocation":"narrow the conclusion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v6","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"precise estimate","zh":"精确估计","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Even a precise estimate can answer the wrong causal question.","exampleZh":"即使估计很精确，也可能回答了错误的因果问题。","collocation":"a precise estimate","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v7","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inferred mechanisms","zh":"推断的机制","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The report should distinguish measured outcomes from inferred mechanisms.","exampleZh":"报告应区分测量到的结果和推断的机制。","collocation":"inferred mechanisms","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-11:v8","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"less plausible","zh":"较不可信的","pos":"adjectival phrase","example":"What evidence would make the selection explanation less plausible?","exampleZh":"什么证据会让选择因素的解释不那么可信？","collocation":"less plausible explanations","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v9","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"前提是","pos":"conjunction","example":"Comparing changes may help, provided the groups had credible earlier trends.","exampleZh":"比较变化可能有帮助，前提是各组先前趋势可信。","collocation":"provided the condition holds","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v10","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"identification","zh":"因果识别","pos":"noun","example":"The limitation concerns identification, not merely the size of the sample.","exampleZh":"这个局限关乎因果识别，而不只是样本大小。","collocation":"causal identification","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-11:v11","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"descriptive finding","zh":"描述性发现","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Uncertainty about causation does not make the descriptive finding worthless.","exampleZh":"因果关系不确定，并不使描述性发现失去价值。","collocation":"a descriptive finding","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-11:v12","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterfactual","zh":"反事实","pos":"noun","example":"The causal question requires a credible counterfactual.","exampleZh":"因果问题需要可信的反事实比较。","collocation":"a credible counterfactual","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v13","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"selection","zh":"选择过程","pos":"noun","example":"Selection into the programme may reflect prior motivation.","exampleZh":"加入项目的选择可能反映先前动机。","collocation":"selection into a programme","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v14","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"mechanism","zh":"作用机制","pos":"noun","example":"A plausible mechanism does not identify its independent effect.","exampleZh":"合理机制不能识别其独立效应。","collocation":"a plausible mechanism","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v15","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"attrition","zh":"样本流失","pos":"noun","example":"Uneven attrition weakened the final comparison.","exampleZh":"不均衡的样本流失削弱了最终比较。","collocation":"uneven attrition","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-11:v16","level":"C2","chapter":75,"module":"c2-11","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"genuine findings","zh":"真实发现","pos":"noun phrase","example":"More cyclists and less comfortable corners can both be genuine findings.","exampleZh":"骑车者增加和转角舒适度下降可以同时是真实发现。","collocation":"genuine findings","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v1","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contradicting","zh":"与之矛盾","pos":"verb","example":"The reports address different outcomes rather than necessarily contradicting each other.","exampleZh":"这些报告讨论不同结果，未必互相矛盾。","collocation":"contradicting another source","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v2","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Aggregating","zh":"合并","pos":"gerund","example":"Aggregating the results would conceal which users encountered the difficulty.","exampleZh":"把结果合并会掩盖哪些使用者遇到了困难。","collocation":"aggregating results","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v3","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"averaging it away","zh":"通过取平均抹去","pos":"verb phrase","example":"We should preserve the disagreement about priorities instead of averaging it away.","exampleZh":"我们应保留优先事项的分歧，而非通过取平均将其抹去。","collocation":"averaging it away","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v4","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"capture","zh":"捕捉","pos":"verb","example":"The interviews explain experiences that the traffic counts cannot capture.","exampleZh":"访谈解释了交通计数无法捕捉的体验。","collocation":"capture experiences","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v5","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"universal accessibility","zh":"普遍可达性","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Neither source alone supports a conclusion about universal accessibility.","exampleZh":"任何一个来源都不能单独支持普遍可达性的结论。","collocation":"universal accessibility","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v6","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"converges","zh":"汇合一致","pos":"verb","example":"The synthesis needs to identify where the evidence converges and where it diverges.","exampleZh":"综合分析需指出证据在哪里一致、在哪里分歧。","collocation":"evidence converges","register":"formal / specialist in this context","id":"c2-12:v7","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"targeted","zh":"有针对性的","pos":"adjective","example":"A targeted corner redesign may retain the benefit while addressing the barrier.","exampleZh":"有针对性的转角改造可能保留收益并解决障碍。","collocation":"a targeted redesign","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v8","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"observation periods","zh":"观察时段","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Before combining the figures, check whether their observation periods match.","exampleZh":"合并数字之前，要检查观察时段是否一致。","collocation":"observation periods","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v9","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"relevant","zh":"相关的","pos":"adjective","example":"The source with more numbers is not automatically the more relevant one.","exampleZh":"数字更多的来源并不自动更相关。","collocation":"a relevant source","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v10","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"harmonised","zh":"统一","pos":"verb (past participle)","example":"An unresolved difference should be reported rather than silently harmonised.","exampleZh":"尚未解决的差异应被报告，而非悄悄统一。","collocation":"harmonised differences","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v11","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"combined evidence","zh":"综合证据","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The recommendation follows from the combined evidence, not from one preferred headline.","exampleZh":"建议来自综合证据，而非某个偏爱的标题。","collocation":"combined evidence","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-12:v12","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"convergence","zh":"趋同","pos":"noun","example":"The sources show limited convergence on the access problem.","exampleZh":"来源在通行问题上有限趋同。","collocation":"limited convergence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v13","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"divergence","zh":"分歧","pos":"noun","example":"The divergence concerns priorities rather than the traffic count.","exampleZh":"分歧关乎优先项而非交通计数。","collocation":"a divergence of priorities","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v14","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"commensurable","zh":"可共同衡量的","pos":"adjective","example":"The measures are not directly commensurable.","exampleZh":"这些测量不能直接共同量化。","collocation":"commensurable measures","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v15","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"triangulation","zh":"多来源核对","pos":"noun","example":"Triangulation can expose questions hidden by one method.","exampleZh":"多来源核对能揭示单一方法隐藏的问题。","collocation":"methodological triangulation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-12:v16","level":"C2","chapter":76,"module":"c2-12","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"guarantee","zh":"保证","pos":"verb","example":"No, we cannot guarantee that an accident will never happen.","exampleZh":"不能，我们无法保证事故永远不会发生。","collocation":"guarantee zero risk","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v1","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"risks are reduced","zh":"风险得到降低","pos":"verb clause","example":"What we can explain is how risks are reduced and who can stop the activity.","exampleZh":"我们能解释的是如何降低风险，以及谁有权停止活动。","collocation":"risks are reduced","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v2","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"direct answer","zh":"直接回答","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The question deserves a direct answer before a list of procedures.","exampleZh":"在列举程序之前，这个问题值得一个直接回答。","collocation":"a direct answer","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v3","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"precise figure","zh":"精确数字","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I do not have evidence for that precise figure, so I will not invent one.","exampleZh":"我没有证据支持那个精确数字，因此不会编造。","collocation":"a precise figure","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v4","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"measured","zh":"测量了","pos":"verb (past)","example":"Let me distinguish the risk we have measured from the risk we have not.","exampleZh":"让我区分已测量的风险和未测量的风险。","collocation":"measured risk","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v5","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"fair challenge","zh":"合理质疑","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That is a fair challenge to the conclusion, although not to every observation.","exampleZh":"这是对结论的合理质疑，尽管并非针对每一项观察。","collocation":"a fair challenge","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v6","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"inspection record","zh":"检查记录","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would need to check the inspection record before answering that detail.","exampleZh":"在回答那个细节之前，我需要核查检查记录。","collocation":"an inspection record","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v7","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"exposure","zh":"暴露风险","pos":"noun","example":"The control reduces exposure without eliminating every possibility of harm.","exampleZh":"这项控制降低了暴露风险，却没有消除一切伤害可能。","collocation":"reduce exposure","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v8","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proceed","zh":"继续进行","pos":"verb","example":"If the equipment fails that check, the session will not proceed.","exampleZh":"如果设备未通过那项检查，活动将不再继续。","collocation":"the session will proceed","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v9","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"frequency","zh":"频率","pos":"noun","example":"My uncertainty concerns the frequency, not the existence of the hazard.","exampleZh":"我不确定的是发生频率，而不是危险是否存在。","collocation":"the frequency of harm","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v10","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"decision rule","zh":"决策规则","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We should publish the decision rule rather than merely ask for trust.","exampleZh":"我们应公布决策规则，而非仅仅要求信任。","collocation":"a decision rule","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v11","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"accountability","zh":"问责","pos":"noun","example":"An honest limitation can strengthen accountability instead of weakening it.","exampleZh":"诚实说明局限可以加强问责，而非削弱它。","collocation":"strengthen accountability","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-13:v12","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"due process","zh":"公平程序","pos":"noun","example":"Due process matters before a public attribution of blame.","exampleZh":"公开归责之前公平程序很重要。","collocation":"respect due process","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v13","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"allegation","zh":"指控","pos":"noun","example":"An allegation requires a response proportionate to its evidence.","exampleZh":"回应指控应与其证据相称。","collocation":"an unverified allegation","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v14","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"disclosure","zh":"披露","pos":"noun","example":"Disclosure should protect relevant facts without unnecessary personal detail.","exampleZh":"披露应保护相关事实而不多泄露个人细节。","collocation":"proportionate disclosure","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v15","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"premature","zh":"过早的","pos":"adjective","example":"Premature blame can obstruct an accurate investigation.","exampleZh":"过早归责可能妨碍准确调查。","collocation":"premature blame","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-13:v16","level":"C2","chapter":77,"module":"c2-13","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"staffing costs","zh":"人员成本","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The strongest objection concerns staffing costs, not indifference to swimmers.","exampleZh":"最有力的异议关乎人员成本，而非对游泳者漠不关心。","collocation":"staffing costs","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v1","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rules out","zh":"排除","pos":"verb phrase","example":"I accept that concern but question whether it rules out a limited trial.","exampleZh":"我接受这个担忧，但质疑它是否排除了有限试行。","collocation":"rules out a trial","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v2","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"supporters would recognise","zh":"支持者会认可","pos":"subordinate clause","example":"Could we state the opposing argument in terms its supporters would recognise?","exampleZh":"我们能否用支持者认可的方式陈述对方论点？","collocation":"terms supporters would recognise","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v3","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"universal claim","zh":"普遍论断","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The example challenges a universal claim without settling the whole policy.","exampleZh":"这个例子质疑了普遍论断，却没有解决整个政策问题。","collocation":"a universal claim","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v4","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"only if","zh":"只有在某条件下","pos":"conjunction phrase","example":"Your conclusion follows only if no cheaper staffing arrangement is feasible.","exampleZh":"只有不存在可行的更低成本排班时，你的结论才成立。","collocation":"only if feasible","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v5","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"proportional response","zh":"适度回应","pos":"noun phrase","example":"We agree on the access problem but differ over the proportional response.","exampleZh":"我们认同出行问题，却对适度回应存在分歧。","collocation":"a proportional response","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v6","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rebuttal","zh":"反驳","pos":"noun","example":"That rebuttal addresses the slogan rather than the underlying argument.","exampleZh":"那项反驳针对口号，而不是背后的论证。","collocation":"a reasoned rebuttal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v7","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"competing benefits","zh":"不同的收益","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The choice involves competing benefits, not a contest between care and neglect.","exampleZh":"选择涉及不同收益，而非关心与漠视的较量。","collocation":"competing benefits","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v8","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"indefinitely","zh":"无限期地","pos":"adverb","example":"A trial could test demand without committing the budget indefinitely.","exampleZh":"试行可以检验需求，而不无限期承诺预算。","collocation":"commit indefinitely","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v9","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"revise","zh":"修改","pos":"verb","example":"What result would persuade you to revise your present position?","exampleZh":"什么结果会促使你修改目前立场？","collocation":"revise a position","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v10","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"withdraw","zh":"撤回","pos":"verb","example":"I would withdraw that objection if the cost assumption proved mistaken.","exampleZh":"如果成本假设被证明有误，我会撤回那项异议。","collocation":"withdraw an objection","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v11","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"counterargument","zh":"反论点","pos":"noun","example":"Acknowledging a strong counterargument makes the remaining disagreement clearer.","exampleZh":"承认有力反论点会让剩余分歧更清楚。","collocation":"a strong counterargument","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-14:v12","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"steelman","zh":"最强合理表述","pos":"verb","example":"Steelman the objection before attempting a rebuttal.","exampleZh":"反驳前先作最强合理表述。","collocation":"steelman an objection","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v13","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"feasibility","zh":"可行性","pos":"noun","example":"Desirability and feasibility require separate arguments.","exampleZh":"可取性与可行性需要分别论证。","collocation":"practical feasibility","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v14","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"premise","zh":"前提","pos":"noun","example":"The conclusion depends on a disputed cost premise.","exampleZh":"结论依赖有争议的成本前提。","collocation":"a disputed premise","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v15","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"common ground","zh":"共同基础","pos":"noun","example":"Common ground can clarify the remaining disagreement.","exampleZh":"共同基础能澄清余下分歧。","collocation":"establish common ground","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-14:v16","level":"C2","chapter":78,"module":"c2-14","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"uninterrupted","zh":"连续不被打断的","pos":"adjective","example":"Our priority is uninterrupted rehearsal time rather than any particular weekday.","exampleZh":"我们的优先需求是连续排练时间，而非某个特定星期几。","collocation":"uninterrupted rehearsal","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v1","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"compensate for","zh":"补偿","pos":"verb phrase","example":"Would a guaranteed later booking compensate for the temporary loss of access?","exampleZh":"保证之后的预约能否补偿暂时失去使用权？","collocation":"compensate for loss","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v2","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"provided","zh":"只要","pos":"conjunction","example":"We can concede those evenings provided the later arrangement is confirmed.","exampleZh":"只要后续安排得到确认，我们可以让出那些晚间时段。","collocation":"provided it is confirmed","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v3","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"conditional offer","zh":"有条件提议","pos":"noun phrase","example":"That is a conditional offer, not an agreement to surrender the slot.","exampleZh":"那是一项有条件提议，而不是同意放弃时段。","collocation":"a conditional offer","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v4","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"setup","zh":"布置","pos":"noun","example":"Let us compare the costs of setup before dividing the hours equally.","exampleZh":"在平均分配时段之前，我们先比较布置成本。","collocation":"setup costs","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v5","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"underlying interests","zh":"背后的实际诉求","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The positions conflict, but the underlying interests may be compatible.","exampleZh":"立场相冲突，但背后的实际诉求可能相容。","collocation":"underlying interests","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v6","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"committing","zh":"作出承诺","pos":"verb (gerund)","example":"I need to consult the group before committing to that exchange.","exampleZh":"在承诺这项交换之前，我需要征求小组意见。","collocation":"committing to an exchange","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v7","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reopen","zh":"重新讨论","pos":"verb","example":"If either condition cannot be met, we should reopen the whole package.","exampleZh":"如果任一条件无法满足，我们应重新讨论整套方案。","collocation":"reopen a package","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v8","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"goodwill","zh":"善意","pos":"noun","example":"The written agreement should specify dates rather than rely on goodwill.","exampleZh":"书面协议应明确日期，而非依靠善意。","collocation":"rely on goodwill","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v9","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"predictable","zh":"可预见的","pos":"adjective","example":"What is the least predictable part of the current arrangement for you?","exampleZh":"对你们来说，目前安排中最不确定的是什么？","collocation":"predictable access","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v10","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocal concession","zh":"相互让步","pos":"noun phrase","example":"A reciprocal concession should address a real constraint on each side.","exampleZh":"相互让步应解决双方各自的实际限制。","collocation":"a reciprocal concession","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v11","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"subject to","zh":"以某条件为前提","pos":"prepositional phrase","example":"We have an outline agreement, subject to confirmation of the hall booking.","exampleZh":"我们达成了框架协议，但须待礼堂预约确认。","collocation":"subject to confirmation","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-15:v12","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reciprocal","zh":"相互的","pos":"adjective","example":"A reciprocal offer links a concession to a benefit.","exampleZh":"相互提议把让步与收益联系起来。","collocation":"a reciprocal offer","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v13","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"contingency","zh":"应变安排","pos":"noun","example":"The agreement needs a contingency for postponement.","exampleZh":"协议需要延期时的应变安排。","collocation":"a contingency plan","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v14","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"reservation point","zh":"可接受底线","pos":"noun","example":"The group should know its reservation point before negotiating.","exampleZh":"小组应在谈判前明确底线。","collocation":"establish a reservation point","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v15","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"enforceable","zh":"可执行的","pos":"adjective","example":"Precise dates make the agreement more enforceable.","exampleZh":"具体日期使协议更可执行。","collocation":"an enforceable agreement","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-15:v16","level":"C2","chapter":79,"module":"c2-15","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rhythm","zh":"节奏","pos":"noun","example":"The repeated short sentences create a rhythm that serves the narrator's anxiety.","exampleZh":"反复的短句形成了体现叙述者焦虑的节奏。","collocation":"a narrative rhythm","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v1","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"productive uncertainty","zh":"有意义的不确定性","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The ambiguous pronoun produces confusion rather than productive uncertainty.","exampleZh":"有歧义的代词造成的是混乱，而非有意义的不确定性。","collocation":"productive uncertainty","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v2","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"deliberate repetition","zh":"有意的重复","pos":"noun phrase","example":"I would clarify the reference while preserving the deliberate repetition.","exampleZh":"我会澄清指代，同时保留有意的重复。","collocation":"deliberate repetition","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v3","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"substantial change","zh":"实质改动","pos":"noun phrase","example":"An editor should be able to explain what each substantial change achieves.","exampleZh":"编辑应能解释每一处实质改动达到了什么效果。","collocation":"a substantial change","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v4","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"faithful","zh":"忠实的","pos":"adjective","example":"The smoother version is not automatically the more faithful one.","exampleZh":"更顺畅的版本未必更忠实。","collocation":"a faithful version","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v5","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"oversight","zh":"疏忽","pos":"noun","example":"Could this repetition be intentional rather than an oversight?","exampleZh":"这个重复是否可能是有意的，而非疏忽？","collocation":"an accidental oversight","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v6","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"distinctive","zh":"独特的","pos":"adjective","example":"We should ask the author before replacing the distinctive sentence pattern.","exampleZh":"在替换独特句式之前，我们应询问作者。","collocation":"a distinctive pattern","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v7","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"emotional progression","zh":"情感推进","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The paragraph loses its emotional progression when every sentence has equal length.","exampleZh":"当每句长度相同时，段落失去了情感推进。","collocation":"emotional progression","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v8","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cohesion","zh":"衔接","pos":"noun","example":"This change improves cohesion without altering the narrator's attitude.","exampleZh":"这个改动改善了衔接，同时没有改变叙述者态度。","collocation":"textual cohesion","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v9","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intended effect","zh":"预期效果","pos":"noun phrase","example":"The revision removes an accidental obstacle to the intended effect.","exampleZh":"修改移除了阻碍预期效果的偶然障碍。","collocation":"the intended effect","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v10","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rationale","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"A useful style note records the decision and its rationale.","exampleZh":"有用的风格说明会记录决定及其理由。","collocation":"explain a rationale","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v11","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"Editorial restraint","zh":"编辑上的克制","pos":"noun phrase","example":"Editorial restraint can require as much judgement as extensive rewriting.","exampleZh":"编辑上的克制可能与大幅改写同样需要判断力。","collocation":"editorial restraint","register":"neutral; adapt to audience","id":"c2-16:v12","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"cadence","zh":"行文节奏","pos":"noun","example":"The revision altered the cadence of the paragraph.","exampleZh":"修改改变了段落的行文节奏。","collocation":"preserve a cadence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v13","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"intrusive","zh":"过度干预的","pos":"adjective","example":"The edit became intrusive when it replaced the narrator's stance.","exampleZh":"替换叙述者立场时编辑变得过度干预。","collocation":"an intrusive edit","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v14","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"coherence","zh":"连贯性","pos":"noun","example":"Coherence depends on relationships between ideas.","exampleZh":"连贯性取决于观念之间的关系。","collocation":"improve coherence","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v15","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"},{"head":"rationale","zh":"理由","pos":"noun","example":"The author should see the rationale for substantial changes.","exampleZh":"作者应看到实质改动的理由。","collocation":"explain the rationale","register":"neutral to formal; use according to context","id":"c2-16:v16","level":"C2","chapter":84,"module":"c2-16","kind":"authored-context"}];window.QuestFinalCapstones=[{"id":"cap-b1-A","level":"B1","title":"B1 独立综合验收 A","chapter":24,"validationStage":"post","sourceB":{"title":"The lost property project","text":"Every month, staff at a bus station collected umbrellas, bags and other objects left by passengers. Valuable items went into a secure cupboard, but ordinary belongings filled boxes in the office. People looking for a missing item often had to describe it to several different members of staff.\nAn assistant named Carla suggested making a simple record with the date, bus route and a photograph of each object. Her first idea was to publish all the photographs online. The manager explained that some images might reveal private information, such as a name on a medicine box. Staff therefore photographed only the outside of bags and kept detailed descriptions inside the station system.\nPassengers could search the public page and contact the office if they recognised something. They still had to provide a detail that was not shown before collecting an item. A photograph alone did not prove ownership. Collection was available between ten and four on weekdays, but people unable to attend could arrange for another person to come with written permission.\nAfter two months, more objects had been returned. The office was also tidier, although making the records took time. Carla trained a second assistant so that the system did not depend on her being at work every day. The manager decided to continue the project and review it after the busy holiday period. The goal was a dependable service, not simply an attractive webpage that nobody had time to maintain."},"listening":{"test":"Hello, this is Jonah from the community orchard. You volunteered to help with the apple collection on Saturday. Please meet at the small gate beside the sports field at nine thirty. The main gate will be used by delivery vehicles, so it is not a safe meeting place. Wear strong shoes and bring drinking water. We will provide gloves, baskets and a short explanation of how to handle the fruit.\nYou do not need to climb ladders. Experienced volunteers will collect apples from higher branches, while beginners sort fruit at ground level. Some damaged apples are suitable for juice, so do not throw them away unless a team leader asks you to. We expect to finish at one, but you may leave earlier if you tell us when you arrive.\nIf heavy rain makes the ground unsafe, we will send a text by eight on Saturday morning. Without a cancellation message, assume the activity is going ahead. Please reply today if your phone number has changed, because we cannot rely on everyone checking email before travelling."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was Carla’s first publishing idea changed?","options":["The station had no way to take photographs.","Passengers wanted every item described by telephone.","Some photographs could reveal private information."],"answer":"Some photographs could reveal private information.","explanation":"药盒姓名等信息可能泄露隐私，因此公开照片有限制。","id":"cap-b1-A:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What must a claimant provide?","options":["The original bus ticket in every case.","A detail not visible on the public page.","A photograph copied from the public page only."],"answer":"A detail not visible on the public page.","explanation":"未公开细节能帮助核对所有权。","id":"cap-b1-A:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How can someone unable to collect personally arrange collection?","options":["Authorise another person in writing.","Ask anyone to take an item without permission.","Wait until the office opens on Sunday."],"answer":"Authorise another person in writing.","explanation":"正文明确允许书面授权他人。","id":"cap-b1-A:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why train a second assistant?","options":["To stop taking any photographs.","To move all belongings to another station.","To keep the service working when Carla is absent."],"answer":"To keep the service working when Carla is absent.","explanation":"服务不应依赖单人出勤。","id":"cap-b1-A:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Where should orchard volunteers meet?","options":["Inside the fruit storage office.","At the small gate beside the sports field.","At the main delivery gate."],"answer":"At the small gate beside the sports field.","explanation":"主门有车辆，集合在运动场旁小门。","id":"cap-b1-A:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What work is suitable for beginners?","options":["Sorting fruit at ground level.","Collecting fruit from high ladders.","Driving the delivery vehicles."],"answer":"Sorting fruit at ground level.","explanation":"有经验者处理高枝，新手地面分果。","id":"cap-b1-A:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should happen to some damaged apples?","options":["Discard all of them immediately.","Return them to the trees.","Keep them for possible juice-making."],"answer":"Keep them for possible juice-making.","explanation":"部分损伤果可榨汁，先勿扔。","id":"cap-b1-A:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why should volunteers report a changed number?","options":["Everyone must telephone at nine thirty.","Cancellation updates will be sent by text.","Phone numbers determine which fruit they receive."],"answer":"Cancellation updates will be sent by text.","explanation":"取消消息依赖手机号码及时送达。","id":"cap-b1-A:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"You help at a bicycle club. Write an email introducing a lost-property collection procedure. Explain how owners should identify items, when collection is available and how someone else can collect with permission. Use your own arrangements rather than copying the station text.","minimumWords":120,"checklist":["完整回应题目与指定读者，明确写作目的。","使用具体理由和条件，避免无根据的承诺。","组织清楚，检查指代、时态、搭配和语域。","独立完成并保留原稿，供人工按内容、组织与语言评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"A new volunteer wants to join an outdoor activity but cannot stay until the end and is nervous about unfamiliar equipment. Discuss a suitable role and the information that must be confirmed.","followups":["How would you check that the person understood the meeting place?","What would you do if weather conditions changed?","Why might one role suit a beginner better than another?"]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-b1-B","level":"B1","title":"B1 陌生延迟验收 B","chapter":24,"validationStage":"delayed","sourceB":{"title":"A visitor badge with an unexpected condition","text":"The town’s annual architecture weekend allowed visitors into buildings that were normally private. Amina booked places at a theatre workshop and an old observatory. She printed her booking email and assumed it would admit her to both buildings. On Friday evening, she noticed a sentence saying that every visitor also needed a weekend badge.\nThe badge was free, but it had to be collected from the information centre before the first visit. Amina’s theatre booking began at ten on Saturday, while the centre opened at nine thirty. She lived an hour away and worried that a queue might make her late.\nShe contacted the organisers. They explained that badges could also be collected on Friday until seven. Alternatively, a friend could collect hers by showing the booking reference, but the friend could not join a booked tour in Amina’s place without changing the name in advance. The badge and the tour reservation served different purposes.\nAmina asked a nearby friend to collect the badge. She also checked what would happen if she missed the theatre start time. Late visitors could not enter the workshop because the safety explanation was given at the beginning. The observatory tour had a separate meeting point and was not affected by missing the theatre.\nThe weekend went well because Amina checked the conditions before travelling. Afterwards, she suggested placing the badge requirement near the top of future booking emails. The information had been present, but it was easy to overlook among descriptions of the buildings."},"listening":{"test":"This is a message for everyone joining next week’s library recording project. We are collecting spoken memories of local shops, not asking volunteers to perform a prepared history lecture. Please bring one short memory and, if possible, an object or photograph that helps explain it. You should not name another person in a sensitive story without their permission.\nThe first session is on Tuesday at two in the smaller meeting room. Arrive through the side entrance because the main hall will be used for children’s activities. We will make a practice recording before asking whether you agree to a public version. You may choose to keep your contribution only in the private project archive, and you can discuss that choice with the organiser.\nEach appointment lasts about half an hour. If you need more time to speak comfortably, tell us before Tuesday so we can arrange a longer slot. A friend may come for support, but we would like to hear your own account rather than have someone answer every question for you."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did Amina initially misunderstand?","options":["The weekend badge had to be purchased separately.","The observatory had replaced the theatre booking.","The booking email alone was not enough for admission."],"answer":"The booking email alone was not enough for admission.","explanation":"她以为确认邮件即可，实际还需免费徽章。","id":"cap-b1-B:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What could her friend do without a name change?","options":["Change both visits to Sunday automatically.","Collect the badge using the reference.","Take Amina’s place on the theatre tour."],"answer":"Collect the badge using the reference.","explanation":"领徽章与替人参团权限不同。","id":"cap-b1-B:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why could late visitors not enter the workshop?","options":["They would miss the opening safety explanation.","All buildings closed at ten.","Badges became invalid after ten minutes."],"answer":"They would miss the opening safety explanation.","explanation":"安全说明在开始时，错过不能进。","id":"cap-b1-B:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What improvement did Amina suggest?","options":["Remove all descriptions of buildings.","Require everyone to collect on Friday only.","Move the badge requirement higher in the email."],"answer":"Move the badge requirement higher in the email.","explanation":"信息虽存在但位置使其易遗漏。","id":"cap-b1-B:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What are recording volunteers asked to bring?","options":["A story naming every neighbour regardless of consent.","A short personal memory, with a helpful object if possible.","A complete prepared history lecture."],"answer":"A short personal memory, with a helpful object if possible.","explanation":"个人记忆是重点，物品可辅助。","id":"cap-b1-B:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which entrance should they use?","options":["The side entrance.","The entrance through the children’s main hall.","The closed staff-only loading gate."],"answer":"The side entrance.","explanation":"主厅儿童活动，使用侧门。","id":"cap-b1-B:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What happens before a public recording is agreed?","options":["Automatic online publication of every contribution.","A vote by all other volunteers.","A practice recording."],"answer":"A practice recording.","explanation":"先试录，再确认是否公开。","id":"cap-b1-B:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should someone needing extra time do?","options":["Ask a friend to answer every question instead.","Tell organisers before Tuesday.","Arrive late and extend the session without asking."],"answer":"Tell organisers before Tuesday.","explanation":"提前协商较长时段。","id":"cap-b1-B:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"A festival email contains an important requirement that visitors often miss. Write feedback to the organiser explaining the problem, its likely effect and two practical changes to the message. Do not use the architecture-weekend badge as your example.","minimumWords":120,"checklist":["完整回应题目与指定读者，明确写作目的。","使用具体理由和条件，避免无根据的承诺。","组织清楚，检查指代、时态、搭配和语域。","独立完成并保留原稿，供人工按内容、组织与语言评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"You are invited to contribute a personal story to a public project. Explain what you are comfortable sharing, ask about privacy choices and arrange any support you need.","followups":["What would you ask before agreeing to publication?","How could you explain a decision to keep part of a story private?","What should organisers do if a contributor needs more time?"]},"predecessor":"cap-b1-A","isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-b2-A","level":"B2","title":"B2 独立综合验收 A","chapter":40,"validationStage":"post","sourceB":{"title":"The public kitchen and the impressive total","text":"A city charity opened a public kitchen where residents could cook together using surplus ingredients. Its annual report announced that eight thousand meals had been served. Supporters welcomed the number as evidence of growing social connection, and a sponsor proposed using it as the main measure for the following year’s funding.\nThe kitchen coordinator, Daniel, was pleased with the activity but uneasy about the interpretation. A meal count included takeaway portions collected by volunteers for delivery elsewhere. These portions were valuable, yet they did not represent conversations around the kitchen tables. Conversely, a person who attended once, learned a useful skill and later cooked confidently at home would contribute little to a measure based on repeat visits.\nDaniel suggested separating several purposes. Food distribution could be recorded through portions and deliveries. Learning could be explored through voluntary follow-up questions. Social participation required asking who joined activities, whether they felt welcome and what prevented interested residents from attending. He warned against turning every question into a lengthy compulsory form, since people came primarily to cook and eat, not provide data.\nThe sponsor initially worried that multiple measures would make the report less clear. Daniel agreed that a simple summary was useful, but argued that clarity should not depend on making different outcomes look identical. The charity tested a short report with three sections and a page explaining what remained unknown.\nThe review revealed an awkward result. The busiest session was not necessarily the most accessible: its noise made participation difficult for several residents. A smaller afternoon group was therefore retained despite producing fewer meals per staff hour. The charity also reviewed who carried the work of cleaning and preparing ingredients, which the original total had made invisible.\nFunding continued, with a request for another review after six months. The charity did not abandon numbers or claim that personal stories could prove everything. It used each kind of evidence for an appropriate question. Its central achievement was a more honest account of what the kitchen did, whom it served and which improvements a rising meal total alone could never identify."},"listening":{"test":"I work with a local theatre that introduced reduced-price tickets for people under twenty-five. Sales rose sharply, and we were ready to describe the scheme as a complete solution to attracting younger audiences. Then a youth worker asked who was still missing. We had counted purchases, but we had not asked whether the available performances fitted study, work or transport patterns.\nWe held short discussions at a college and a training centre. Some people welcomed the discount. Others explained that the last bus left before evening performances ended, and a few were unsure whether they would feel comfortable entering the building alone. A cheaper ticket did not address either difficulty.\nOur next trial included one earlier performance, a clearly explained meeting point for first-time visitors and the option to attend with a friend at the same reduced price. We kept ordinary booking available, so people did not have to join a special group to qualify. Attendance increased, although we cannot separate the effect of every change yet.\nI still support the discount, but I would now describe it as one tool rather than the whole strategy. We also need to consider the people who attended and did not return. Their reasons may tell us more about practical or social barriers than another survey sent only to enthusiastic regular customers."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the meal total insufficient evidence of social connection?","options":["The total excluded all takeaway portions.","Every participant attended only once.","Some counted meals were delivered rather than eaten together."],"answer":"Some counted meals were delivered rather than eaten together.","explanation":"分发餐数与围桌交流不是同一指标。","id":"cap-b2-A:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What concern did Daniel have about data collection?","options":["Only personal stories should ever be recorded.","It could become an excessive burden on participants.","It should remove all voluntary follow-up."],"answer":"It could become an excessive burden on participants.","explanation":"来做饭吃饭的人不应被强制长问卷压倒。","id":"cap-b2-A:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was a smaller afternoon session retained?","options":["It offered access that the noisier busy session did not.","It produced the highest meals-per-hour figure.","The sponsor required every group to be equally small."],"answer":"It offered access that the noisier busy session did not.","explanation":"产出较少但对部分人更可参与。","id":"cap-b2-A:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is the report’s main approach?","options":["Replace every numerical measure with anecdotes.","Use one total to make all outcomes comparable.","Match different evidence to different purposes."],"answer":"Match different evidence to different purposes.","explanation":"各证据回答合适问题，不一概废数字。","id":"cap-b2-A:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did the initial ticket-sales measure fail to reveal?","options":["Whether any young person liked the theatre.","Barriers faced by people still absent.","The exact number of discounted purchases."],"answer":"Barriers faced by people still absent.","explanation":"销售不显示被排除者情况。","id":"cap-b2-A:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which trial change addressed discomfort about arriving alone?","options":["A clear meeting point and a friend option.","A later finish on every performance night.","A requirement to join a special group."],"answer":"A clear meeting point and a friend option.","explanation":"迎新集合与同伴支持针对社交门槛。","id":"cap-b2-A:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why is the speaker cautious about explaining the increase?","options":["No attendance records were kept.","The discount had already been withdrawn.","Several changes were introduced together."],"answer":"Several changes were introduced together.","explanation":"多项同时干预难单独归因。","id":"cap-b2-A:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Whose feedback does the speaker want next?","options":["Only people who purchased the most expensive tickets.","People who attended but did not return.","Only enthusiastic regular customers."],"answer":"People who attended but did not return.","explanation":"流失参与者可揭示不同障碍。","id":"cap-b2-A:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"A public sports programme reports success mainly through registration totals. Write a report proposing a broader evaluation. Distinguish access, sustained participation and benefits, acknowledge a data-collection burden, and recommend one trial. Do not use the kitchen or theatre as your setting.","minimumWords":170,"checklist":["完整回应题目与指定读者，明确写作目的。","使用具体理由和条件，避免无根据的承诺。","组织清楚，检查指代、时态、搭配和语域。","独立完成并保留原稿，供人工按内容、组织与语言评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss whether a community service should retain a smaller, more expensive session that helps people excluded from its busiest activity. Compare at least two criteria and reach a qualified recommendation.","followups":["Which evidence would you request before changing the timetable?","How could the service gather feedback without making participation harder?","How would you explain the decision to someone who values only output per staff hour?"]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-b2-B","level":"B2","title":"B2 陌生延迟验收 B","chapter":40,"validationStage":"delayed","sourceB":{"title":"A translation tool at the advice desk","text":"An employment advice centre tested a translation tool to help staff communicate with visitors who spoke different languages. During the first month, appointments became shorter and fewer visitors returned simply to ask where to submit a form. The project manager considered these promising results, but declined a supplier’s request to describe the tool as a replacement for interpreting.\nThe trial involved practical instructions with a narrow range of vocabulary. It did not include complex disputes, confidential interviews or decisions about a visitor’s legal position. The manager believed that a result obtained under those limits should not be used to promise reliable communication in every appointment. Staff still needed to recognise when a conversation had moved beyond the approved use.\nOne incident showed why. A visitor appeared to agree with a translated instruction, but later explained through an interpreter that he had understood the deadline as a suggested date rather than a final requirement. The tool had produced a fluent sentence. The difficulty concerned how the visitor interpreted its force, which a satisfied expression had not revealed.\nThe centre revised its procedure. For important conditions, staff asked visitors to explain the next step in their own words. They used fictional examples in training rather than uploading sensitive case details. A professional interpreter remained available when meaning was uncertain or consequences were substantial. The tool supported that system; it did not determine when expert help was unnecessary.\nThe revised appointments sometimes took longer than the first trial’s average. This did not necessarily mean the service had become less effective. Staff were now identifying misunderstandings that shorter conversations might have left hidden. The final report therefore presented speed alongside follow-up difficulties and checks of understanding.\nThe centre continued the technology, with clear boundaries and a route to human support. Its recommendation was neither a celebration of automatic translation nor a rejection of it. The useful question was which tasks it could assist under controlled conditions, and how the organisation would notice when apparent agreement concealed a serious gap in understanding."},"listening":{"test":"At our design studio, we started using an automated tool to produce first drafts of routine project summaries. The obvious advantage was speed. A short prompt could generate a polished paragraph in seconds, and the first examples looked more consistent than notes written by different team members. We soon learned that fluency was not the same as an accurate account of the work.\nIn one draft, the tool described a proposed feature as if the client had approved it. Nothing in the grammar signalled uncertainty. A team member recognised the mistake because she had attended the meeting. Someone reading only the summary might reasonably have treated it as a decision.\nWe now require the person responsible for the project to compare each draft with the meeting record. Unresolved points are labelled clearly, and sensitive client information is not entered into an unapproved service. We have not banned the tool; it still helps organise ordinary material. However, the time estimate includes review rather than counting generation as the end of the task.\nOur next evaluation will ask whether colleagues can use the summaries to act correctly, not simply whether they like the style. We will also review corrections made a week later, when consequences become clearer. A useful system should reduce work across the whole process instead of moving unnoticed checking costs onto the next person."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why did the manager reject a broad replacement claim?","options":["The trial included every confidential dispute.","No visitor had understood any translated sentence.","The trial covered only limited practical instructions."],"answer":"The trial covered only limited practical instructions.","explanation":"窄范围试行不能外推所有沟通。","id":"cap-b2-B:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What did the deadline incident reveal?","options":["The tool’s main problem was an incomplete appointment list.","Fluent wording and apparent agreement did not ensure correct interpretation.","The visitor had been given no translated instruction."],"answer":"Fluent wording and apparent agreement did not ensure correct interpretation.","explanation":"语言流畅与真正理解约束力度不同。","id":"cap-b2-B:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why might longer revised appointments be useful?","options":["Previously hidden misunderstandings were being identified.","Staff had stopped checking any understanding.","The centre aimed to eliminate all practical efficiency."],"answer":"Previously hidden misunderstandings were being identified.","explanation":"平均时间增加可能反映更有效发现问题。","id":"cap-b2-B:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is the centre’s final position?","options":["Allow the tool to decide when interpreting is unnecessary.","Withdraw every form of translation help.","Use the tool within boundaries and retain human support."],"answer":"Use the tool within boundaries and retain human support.","explanation":"受控辅助与人工转介共同构成方案。","id":"cap-b2-B:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What error appeared in the studio summary?","options":["A client’s name was replaced by a project number.","A proposal was presented as an approved decision.","A finished project was described as cancelled."],"answer":"A proposal was presented as an approved decision.","explanation":"未决状态被写成确定批准。","id":"cap-b2-B:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why did a colleague notice the problem?","options":["She had attended the meeting.","The grammar clearly marked the statement as uncertain.","The client had approved every feature earlier."],"answer":"She had attended the meeting.","explanation":"原始经历提供了核对依据。","id":"cap-b2-B:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"How is the time estimate now calculated?","options":["It ends when text first appears.","It ignores all later corrections.","It includes checking the generated draft."],"answer":"It includes checking the generated draft.","explanation":"全流程应包含审校成本。","id":"cap-b2-B:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What will the next evaluation prioritise?","options":["Whether generation is always faster than reading.","Whether summaries support correct action.","Whether every paragraph has the same polished style."],"answer":"Whether summaries support correct action.","explanation":"可据此正确行动比外观偏好更关键。","id":"cap-b2-B:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"A small organisation wants to use an automated tool for first drafts of public information. Write a proposal defining suitable uses, checking responsibilities, protection of sensitive information and a way to evaluate the whole process. Use a setting other than employment advice or design summaries.","minimumWords":170,"checklist":["完整回应题目与指定读者，明确写作目的。","使用具体理由和条件，避免无根据的承诺。","组织清楚，检查指代、时态、搭配和语域。","独立完成并保留原稿，供人工按内容、组织与语言评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Discuss a service that becomes faster after adopting a tool but may leave misunderstandings undetected. Recommend how to balance speed, checking and access to expert support.","followups":["What should trigger a move from routine assistance to human expertise?","How would you distinguish a language error from a misunderstanding of a condition?","Which later evidence could reveal costs that the first speed measure missed?"]},"predecessor":"cap-b2-A","isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-c1-A","level":"C1","title":"独立综合验收：算法辅助分诊的边界","chapter":64,"validationStage":"post","sourceB":{"title":"Who should decide which housing enquiries come first?","text":"A housing charity introduced a decision-support system to help its staff prioritise enquiries. The system did not allocate homes or decide whether somebody was entitled to assistance. It suggested which enquiries should receive an earlier telephone appointment, using information supplied on an online form. The distinction appeared clear in the project description, but several staff members later admitted that a high-priority label felt like a judgement they were expected to follow.\n\nThe charity's pilot lasted ten weeks. During the first five weeks, the software produced recommendations that staff could inspect after making their own decisions. During the second five, recommendations appeared before staff chose an appointment slot. The project report compared the two periods and found fewer differences between staff decisions in the later period. Its first draft described this as improved consistency. An external adviser asked whether consistency necessarily meant better decisions. Staff might have converged because the recommendations helped them notice relevant details, or because the label discouraged independent consideration. The comparison alone did not distinguish these explanations.\n\nA further issue concerned missing information. The form asked about household size, current accommodation and immediate practical difficulties. People who found the form difficult sometimes submitted only a short description. In those cases, the system frequently assigned a standard priority. The designers had avoided interpreting a blank answer as evidence of severe need, which could also have been misleading. Nevertheless, treating incomplete information as ordinary need risked disadvantaging people least able to explain their circumstances. The adviser proposed a separate incomplete-information flag prompting staff contact, rather than an automatic change to the urgency rating.\n\nThe charity tested that flag on thirty recent enquiries with consent for review. Twelve required information that changed the staff member's initial understanding, although the direction of change varied. The small exercise did not establish how often this would happen across all enquiries. It did demonstrate that missingness could carry operational significance without telling staff what conclusion to reach. The designers agreed to display the reason for each recommendation and make departures from it easy to record.\n\nSome managers worried that recording reasons for departures would discourage staff from exercising judgement. Others argued that an explanation could support learning if it was reviewed constructively rather than used as a compliance score. The revised proposal therefore included protected discussion sessions and stated that agreement with the software would not be a performance target. It also retained a telephone route for people unable to use the form.\n\nThe charity decided to continue the pilot, subject to these changes and an independent review of sampled cases. It declined to announce that the software had made allocation fairer, since allocation was outside the pilot's function and fairness required evidence beyond a reduction in staff disagreement. The intended benefit remained practical support for judgement. Whether that benefit would be realised depended partly on how the system changed staff behaviour, not merely on the technical accuracy of its recommendations."},"listening":{"test":"Thank you for joining the university equipment committee. I want to explain the proposal for sharing specialist recording devices between departments. At present, each department keeps its own small stock. Some devices are rarely used, while researchers elsewhere sometimes wait several weeks. The first idea was a central store, but discussions with technicians revealed that collection and testing time could offset some of the benefit. A device listed as available is not necessarily ready for a particular experiment.\n\nWe now propose a six-month shared catalogue while equipment remains with its current department. A researcher will request a device through the catalogue, and the owning technician will confirm suitability and arrange a handover. This is not permission to take equipment directly from another laboratory. The requesting team will pay consumable costs, but no internal hire fee is proposed during the pilot. Repairs caused by ordinary wear will remain with the owning department; suspected misuse will be considered through a separate process rather than charged automatically to the last user.\n\nOne concern is that technicians may acquire extra coordination work without receiving support. The budget therefore includes eight hours of administrative assistance each week. We cannot yet promise that this will be sufficient. The review will record handling time as well as the number of successful loans. We will also record requests that could not be met and why, since completed loans alone would hide unmet demand.\n\nThe catalogue will initially include twelve devices from three departments. Two other departments have expressed interest but have not confirmed participation, so they must not appear as committed partners in the announcement. Training restrictions will remain visible for every item. The committee will decide on expansion only after examining the pilot's effect on access, reliability and staff workload. A high loan count would be encouraging, but not enough if equipment was frequently returned unready for its next user."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why does the adviser question the description “improved consistency”?","answer":"Reduced disagreement could reflect either useful support or undue reliance.","options":["Reduced disagreement could reflect either useful support or undue reliance.","The later period included no staff decisions that could be compared.","The software had already been shown to allocate homes unfairly."],"explanation":"后期分歧减少有多种解释，不能由一致直接推定决策改善；系统并不分房。","id":"cap-c1-A:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What is the purpose of the incomplete-information flag?","answer":"To trigger contact without presuming the direction of need.","options":["To assign severe urgency whenever any response is missing.","To remove incomplete enquiries from the charity’s workload.","To trigger contact without presuming the direction of need."],"explanation":"缺失标记要求进一步联系，不把空白直接等同严重或普通需要。","id":"cap-c1-A:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What can the thirty-enquiry exercise reasonably support?","answer":"Some incomplete forms contain consequential information worth checking.","options":["Incomplete forms consistently indicate greater urgency than complete ones.","Some incomplete forms contain consequential information worth checking.","The same proportion of every future enquiry will change priority."],"explanation":"12例补充信息改变理解但方向不同，小样本不能推总体比例或单一方向。","id":"cap-c1-A:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which condition best preserves the stated role of the software?","answer":"Staff departures can be explained without agreement becoming a target.","options":["Staff departures can be explained without agreement becoming a target.","Every staff member must match the recommendation to demonstrate competence.","The charity may describe appointment sorting as proof of fairer allocation."],"explanation":"允许有解释的偏离且不以服从率考核，才保留辅助判断角色。","id":"cap-c1-A:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why was a central store not selected for the first pilot?","answer":"Collection and checking could absorb part of the expected benefit.","options":["Departments had agreed that no equipment should ever be shared.","The proposal required researchers to pay a permanent hire fee.","Collection and checking could absorb part of the expected benefit."],"explanation":"集中存储可能增加领取和检测时间，抵消收益。","id":"cap-c1-A:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is a researcher required to do before borrowing?","answer":"Obtain the owning technician’s confirmation and arrange handover.","options":["Ask the committee to remove the item’s training restrictions.","Obtain the owning technician’s confirmation and arrange handover.","Collect any catalogue item directly without further discussion."],"explanation":"须原部门技术员确认适用性并安排交接。","id":"cap-c1-A:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why record unsuccessful requests?","answer":"They reveal demand that completed loans cannot represent.","options":["They reveal demand that completed loans cannot represent.","They automatically establish misuse by the previous borrower.","They replace the need to examine coordination workload."],"explanation":"完成借用只显示满足的需求，失败请求揭示未满足部分。","id":"cap-c1-A:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What would make a high loan count insufficient evidence for expansion?","answer":"Reliability problems that leave devices unready for subsequent use.","options":["Participation by the twelve devices already named in the pilot.","The absence of an internal hire fee during the initial period.","Reliability problems that leave devices unready for subsequent use."],"explanation":"借用多但归还后不可用，仍可能损害可靠性和后续使用。","id":"cap-c1-A:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"A public-service board is considering the housing charity’s decision-support pilot. Write a 260-word minimum briefing evaluating whether continuation is justified. Explain the limits of the consistency finding, the significance of missing information, and one safeguard for staff judgement. Recommend what the independent review should examine. Use only the case evidence and distinguish proposed safeguards from demonstrated outcomes.","minimumWords":260,"checklist":["解释至少两个具体证据的意义及范围，而非仅复述。","公允处理效率与独立判断的潜在冲突。","提出可检验建议并区分已证明与待验证效果。","独立成文，语言连贯准确；由人工按内容、组织、语域、语言评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"You advise a committee deciding whether the university equipment-sharing pilot should proceed. Give a three-minute recommendation linking access, technical suitability and workload. Explain why one attractive headline measure would be insufficient. The assessor will introduce an unexpected resource constraint after your opening.","followups":["If administrative support is reduced to four hours weekly, what should change first and why?","How would you answer a department that fears being blamed automatically for damage?","Which evidence would persuade you to postpone expansion despite strong demand?"]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-c1-B","level":"C1","title":"延迟综合验收：研究参与与共同决策","chapter":64,"validationStage":"delayed","predecessor":"cap-c1-A","sourceB":{"title":"When consultation becomes part of the evidence","text":"A coastal research partnership invited residents to help design a study of seasonal changes in shore access. Researchers originally intended to count visitors at six points along a path and compare summer with winter. During an introductory meeting, local participants argued that visitor numbers would miss an important issue: some residents had stopped attempting the path because steps and loose surfaces had become difficult. Those people would be absent from the very counts intended to describe access.\n\nThe research lead accepted the criticism but warned that adding personal accounts would not automatically produce a representative picture. Volunteers who attended meetings might be unusually active or have strong opinions. The partnership therefore developed two connected activities. Observers would count visits at agreed times, while a community worker would invite accounts through several local networks, including groups not represented at the original meeting. The accounts would identify barriers and possible explanations; they would not be used to estimate prevalence without a suitable sampling method.\n\nA disagreement then arose over whether residents should approve the final conclusions. Some participants felt that contributing local knowledge entitled them to prevent interpretations they considered misleading. Researchers worried that a veto could suppress an unwelcome finding. The partnership distinguished checking factual descriptions from controlling analytical judgement. Contributors could correct how their circumstances were reported and withdraw identifiable material under the consent arrangements. They could also attach a reasoned response to the project's interpretation. They would not be promised the power to remove every conclusion with which they disagreed.\n\nThe first field period produced 420 observed visits across twelve scheduled sessions. Rain caused two other sessions to be cancelled. A draft summary compared the total with an earlier community estimate of about 600 weekly users. The research lead removed that comparison because scheduled-session visits and an informal weekly estimate were different units with different coverage. The recorded total remained useful, provided the time windows and cancellations were reported. The absence of observations during bad weather was itself relevant to how much could be inferred about winter access.\n\nCommunity accounts also changed one proposed recommendation. The initial idea was to add signs directing people to a longer step-free alternative. Several contributors explained that the alternative was only practically usable when a nearby gate was unlocked. The gate schedule had not appeared in the original route map. Researchers checked the schedule with the land manager before revising the recommendation. Local knowledge had revealed a testable condition, rather than simply adding an emotive story to a completed analysis.\n\nAt the public presentation, the partnership published its methods, limits and disagreements alongside the findings. It did not describe participation as proof that every resident endorsed the study. Some participants still preferred a stronger recommendation for repairs. Their response was included separately, making the difference visible without pretending that the research had reached unanimous agreement. The project illustrated how collaboration can improve the questions being asked while preserving the distinction between evidence, interpretation and collective authority. Whether its recommendations deserved implementation would require a further decision about cost, responsibility and the needs not captured by current use."},"listening":{"test":"This is a briefing for the trustees considering the town archive's emergency-access plan. The archive stores original material in two buildings. The older building has limited space for moving boxes quickly, while the newer one has better handling facilities but less spare storage than the first draft suggested. We have corrected that figure after checking the shelves; please use the revised capacity table, not last month's presentation.\n\nThe plan proposes moving a selected group of vulnerable collections if a serious weather warning is issued. It does not propose transporting every item whenever rain is forecast. A conservator has identified priorities according to condition, uniqueness and the feasibility of safe handling. Public interest is relevant, but popularity alone cannot determine what is moved first. Some frequently requested items have good digital copies, while less familiar originals may have no substitute.\n\nTwo volunteers have offered vehicles. We welcome the offer, but neither vehicle has yet been checked for the required carrying arrangements. They should not be listed as confirmed transport. The budget includes professional assistance for the heaviest boxes and training for staff who would prepare the rest. If costs rise, reducing training would change the safety assumptions on which the plan depends.\n\nA trustee asked whether a successful rehearsal would prove that the archive was protected against every emergency. It would not. A rehearsal can reveal delays, unclear responsibilities and unsuitable equipment under the conditions tested. It cannot reproduce every warning period, road closure or staffing shortage. We therefore propose a review after the rehearsal and a separate procedure for situations in which moving material would create more risk than leaving it in place. The trustees are being asked to approve a bounded preparation plan, with outstanding transport checks completed before implementation. They are not being asked to endorse an unconditional guarantee that no collection could ever be damaged."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How did residents’ initial criticism affect the study?","answer":"It exposed a group whose exclusion from counts could distort the access question.","options":["It established that counts should be replaced by representative stories immediately.","It proved that every decline in visits resulted from damaged steps.","It exposed a group whose exclusion from counts could distort the access question."],"explanation":"已不尝试路线者不会被计入，指出研究问题覆盖缺口；未证明统一原因或故事代表性。","id":"cap-c1-B:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which arrangement resolves the disagreement about control most accurately?","answer":"Contributors may correct their representation and publish a reasoned response.","options":["Researchers may retain identifiable accounts after every permitted withdrawal.","Contributors may correct their representation and publish a reasoned response.","Contributors may remove any analytical conclusion they dislike."],"explanation":"区分事实核对、同意撤回与分析判断，允许附理由回应而非无限否决。","id":"cap-c1-B:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why was the comparison with six hundred weekly users removed?","answer":"The figures differed in units and coverage, making the implied comparison unsound.","options":["The figures differed in units and coverage, making the implied comparison unsound.","The observed total of 420 was known to contain fabricated visits.","The partnership had decided that numerical records were irrelevant."],"explanation":"定点时段人次与非正式每周人数估计口径不一，不能直接比。","id":"cap-c1-B:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does the gate-schedule example illustrate?","answer":"Local experience can reveal a factual condition that changes a recommendation.","options":["An individual story must take precedence over any checked record.","A route labelled step-free is always practically accessible.","Local experience can reveal a factual condition that changes a recommendation."],"explanation":"居民指出开门条件，研究者核查后改建议，显示可检验本地知识的价值。","id":"cap-c1-B:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What should trustees do about the storage-capacity figure?","answer":"Use the corrected table based on a later shelf check.","options":["Assume the newer building can hold the whole collection.","Use the corrected table based on a later shelf check.","Retain last month’s figure because it was already presented."],"explanation":"已核查货架并更正，应使用修订容量表。","id":"cap-c1-B:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why might a less requested original receive a higher moving priority?","answer":"It may lack a substitute and be vulnerable under the stated criteria.","options":["It may lack a substitute and be vulnerable under the stated criteria.","Popularity has been declared irrelevant to every archive decision.","Frequently requested material is never physically vulnerable."],"explanation":"条件、独特性、可搬性等综合；无副本是重要因素，不是绝对否定热度。","id":"cap-c1-B:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why are the offered vehicles not yet confirmed resources?","answer":"Required carrying arrangements have not been checked.","options":["Volunteers have withdrawn every offer of assistance.","Professional transport has already replaced both vehicles.","Required carrying arrangements have not been checked."],"explanation":"车辆装载安排未核查，不可当已落实。","id":"cap-c1-B:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the rehearsal intended to establish?","answer":"Operational weaknesses under tested conditions rather than universal protection.","options":["Permission to omit checks once one rehearsal is completed.","Operational weaknesses under tested conditions rather than universal protection.","A guarantee that no collection can ever suffer damage."],"explanation":"演练揭示特定条件下问题，不能保证一切紧急情形。","id":"cap-c1-B:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"A regional body wants to fund research shaped by local participation. Using the coastal study as evidence, write at least 260 words evaluating the benefits and limits of that approach. Explain how participation changed the research question and a recommendation, discuss the boundary between contributor rights and analytical judgement, and propose one condition for future funding. Do not assume that participation establishes unanimous endorsement or representative prevalence.","minimumWords":260,"checklist":["把居民贡献与可核查研究改变联系起来。","准确处理代表性、数据口径与不同意见。","发展有条件的资金建议，不把参与当成果保证。","独立完成并接受四维人工评阅，标注任何外部帮助。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"You are advising the archive trustees. Present a three-minute judgement on whether the emergency-access plan is ready for conditional approval. Explain priorities, unconfirmed resources and what the rehearsal can establish. Respond naturally to the assessor’s challenge without reading a prepared answer.","followups":["If the budget falls by a quarter, which assumption must be reconsidered before cutting costs?","How would you explain prioritising a little-known original over a popular item?","If the rehearsal succeeds but no vehicles are confirmed, would you approve implementation and why?"]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-c2-A","level":"C2","title":"C2目标综合独立验收A：开放与代表性的承诺","chapter":88,"validationStage":"post","sourceB":{"title":"An Archive Open to Whom?","text":"A fictional city launches an online archive of residents' recorded memories. The opening announcement calls it an unrestricted record of the whole community. The team has collected valuable accounts of working life, housing and migration, and its enthusiasm is understandable. Yet the description makes two claims that require separate examination: who is represented, and what unrestricted access would mean. Neither follows automatically from the fact that a website can be visited without payment.\n\nMost interviews were arranged through established neighbourhood associations. This helped the small team find participants and build trust, but it favoured people already connected with those organisations. Residents with irregular working hours appeared less often, and several recent arrivals said that they had never heard about the project. Their absence does not make the existing accounts inauthentic. It limits the inference from those accounts to the community as a whole. Adding more interviews through the same networks might enrich the collection without resolving that limitation.\n\nAccess presents a different problem. Some participants agreed that their recordings could be heard but did not agree to unrestricted copying or commercial reuse. Others discussed relatives who had not been consulted. The team therefore proposes streaming selected recordings, explaining the conditions attached to each and offering an enquiry route for material that requires further review. A board member complains that these arrangements contradict openness. Another replies that openness without boundaries could betray the very trust on which the archive depends.\n\nThe distinction is not between a public archive and a private collection. It concerns which activities the project has permission to support and whether users can discover the applicable conditions. A prominent label saying unrestricted, followed by a difficult page of exceptions, would misdescribe that arrangement. Equally, the existence of justified restrictions would not excuse a confusing interface or a refusal to explain how a request is considered. Ethical limits and avoidable practical barriers should not be treated as the same thing.\n\nDuring a meeting held at an hour when most interviewees cannot attend, one participant calls the consultation splendidly inclusive. The irony is supported by the contrast between the praise and the attendance arrangements. It challenges a procedure; it does not establish that the organisers deliberately excluded anyone. The director accepts the criticism and offers evening and written consultation options. That response addresses one barrier without proving that every missing voice will now be heard.\n\nThe revised announcement describes a growing collection of contributed accounts, with access conditions stated beside individual recordings. The board also funds outreach beyond the original networks and commits to reporting who is still missing from participation. This is a less sweeping promise, not a retreat from the project's public purpose. A credible claim of openness must identify what people can do, whose consent makes it possible and which exclusions remain unresolved. The archive becomes more trustworthy when its limits are visible and contestable than when an expansive slogan asks the collection to represent a completeness it has never established."},"listening":{"test":"I chaired the independent review of a fictional arts fellowship, and I want to distinguish our findings from the headline that followed. Applications had doubled after the form was shortened. The organisers called this proof that the process had become fairer. We could confirm the increase, but fairness involved more than the number of completed forms. We did not know who had started and abandoned an application, or whether the shorter questions still allowed applicants with unconventional experience to explain their work.\n\nThe panel's workload had also doubled without an increase in review time. At our meeting, one assessor described the resulting ten-minute reading period as admirably thorough. Several colleagues laughed. The remark was plainly difficult to understand as literal praise, given the time pressure under discussion. It criticised the arrangement. It was not evidence that every assessor had reviewed every application carelessly, and our report did not attribute that judgement to the speaker.\n\nWe examined a sample of decision notes. Some were detailed and specific; others supplied a score without a reason. That variation justified a concern about the consistency and traceability of decisions. It did not show which rejected applicant would otherwise have received funding. To answer that question, we would need a different comparison and clearer assessment records.\n\nOur recommendation was to retain the shorter form provisionally, collect information about incomplete attempts, and give reviewers enough time to record reasons against published criteria. The organisers worried that asking more questions might reverse the improvement in application numbers. We agreed that unnecessary burden should be avoided. That concern did not justify calling the current process fair on evidence that measured only one part of access.\n\nThe final public statement therefore separated increased participation, uncertain barriers and weaknesses in recorded assessment. It also named the next review date. We did not recommend cancelling the fellowship or declaring its earlier awards invalid. We recommended a procedure that could reveal whether the apparent gain was shared and whether decisions could be explained. A more restrained conclusion was necessary precisely because the programme mattered: an attractive claim of fairness should remain answerable to the people whose experience was not yet visible in the successful application count."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why might additional interviews through the original networks fail to resolve the representation problem?","options":["They could deepen existing coverage while reproducing the same recruitment boundary.","They would necessarily make the existing accounts less authentic.","They would replace the need to examine when consultations are held."],"answer":"They could deepen existing coverage while reproducing the same recruitment boundary.","explanation":"数量增加与覆盖缺口不同；同样的招募渠道可能持续遗漏相同群体。","id":"cap-c2-A:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which distinction is central to the author's treatment of access?","options":["Free streaming constitutes permission for unrestricted copying.","Justified use restrictions do not excuse avoidable obstacles to understanding or requesting access.","Once any restriction is justified, interface design becomes secondary to the archive's purpose."],"answer":"Justified use restrictions do not excuse avoidable obstacles to understanding or requesting access.","explanation":"作者同时保留伦理边界和可用性责任，既不把免费等同任意使用，也不让合理限制成为不透明流程的借口。","id":"cap-c2-A:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What does splendidly inclusive most defensibly imply in context?","options":["The participant believes that all consultation methods are ineffective.","The organisers have intentionally excluded the interviewees.","The consultation's timing conflicts with its apparent participatory ambition."],"answer":"The consultation's timing conflicts with its apparent participatory ambition.","explanation":"反讽针对程序与目的的冲突，不足以证明故意排除，也不否定所有参与方式。","id":"cap-c2-A:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How does the author characterise the narrower announcement?","options":["As a more accountable description consistent with an ongoing public purpose.","As evidence that the project no longer aims to serve the public.","As a final demonstration that all important exclusions have been removed."],"answer":"As a more accountable description consistent with an ongoing public purpose.","explanation":"收窄承诺保留公共目标并显化未解问题，不是退缩，也不是宣告完成。","id":"cap-c2-A:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What is the reviewer's main reservation about doubled applications?","options":["The shorter form is known to disadvantage every unconventional applicant.","Completed applications measure only part of access and do not establish fairness.","The increase cannot be confirmed because decision notes vary in detail."],"answer":"Completed applications measure only part of access and do not establish fairness.","explanation":"已完成申请人数是局部指标；未完成尝试与展示机会仍未知。","id":"cap-c2-A:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Which interpretation of admirably thorough stays within the evidence?","options":["It certifies that the selected assessors were uniformly diligent.","It accuses every reviewer of neglecting every application.","It ironically challenges the time arrangement without settling individual performance."],"answer":"It ironically challenges the time arrangement without settling individual performance.","explanation":"语境支持对时间安排的反讽，不支持把全部个人表现一概定性。","id":"cap-c2-A:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What did inconsistent decision notes justify investigating?","options":["The consistency and explainability of decisions, rather than a definite alternative set of winners.","Whether every rejected applicant was more deserving than every recipient.","Whether a shorter form necessarily produces invalid awards."],"answer":"The consistency and explainability of decisions, rather than a definite alternative set of winners.","explanation":"记录差异支持流程审查，但不足以确定反事实获奖名单。","id":"cap-c2-A:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What position does the reviewer take towards additional information gathering?","options":["Its possible burden means no further evidence should be requested.","It should be proportionate while addressing what the existing participation count omits.","It should be expanded until application numbers return to their earlier level."],"answer":"It should be proportionate while addressing what the existing participation count omits.","explanation":"承认负担不等于放弃查缺；需要有针对性且适度的信息收集。","id":"cap-c2-A:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a 320–420 word independent advisory report for the archive's board in An Archive Open to Whom? Explain the two different problems concealed by unrestricted record of the whole community. Evaluate the proposed access and outreach changes, distinguish the supported ironic criticism from an allegation of motive, and recommend what the board should report next. Address a reasonable objection to your recommendation without claiming that the present evidence resolves every exclusion.","minimumWords":320,"checklist":["分别处理代表性与访问权限，不把一个问题当作另一个问题的证据。","准确使用具体细节，区分反讽指向、合理推断和未经证实的意图。","公平回应最强合理异议，提出可执行且有复核条件的建议。","保持报告结构、受众适当语域和自然准确表达；记录辅助并接受独立评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an unscripted four-minute briefing to a public oversight panel about the archive and fellowship cases. Compare how apparently positive participation claims can omit relevant people or processes. Keep the two situations distinct, identify one justified limit in each, and recommend proportionate next actions without converting uncertainty into either a guarantee or a declaration of failure.","followups":["A panel member says that publicly naming limitations will destroy trust. Respond directly and distinguish trust from reassurance.","Another member interprets the ironic comments as proof of deliberate misconduct. Explain exactly where that inference exceeds the material.","New evidence shows that outreach increased participation but left the same groups underrepresented. Explain what you would revise and what you would retain."]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}},{"id":"cap-c2-B","level":"C2","title":"C2目标延迟独立验收B：可见收益与隐含代价","chapter":88,"validationStage":"delayed","predecessor":"cap-c2-A","sourceB":{"title":"The Roofs in the Report","text":"A fictional university publishes a report on planted roofs installed across part of its estate. The illustrated summary announces cooler buildings and a return of urban wildlife. Temperature readings from three participating buildings and photographs of insects provide an attractive account. A reviewer supports the programme's environmental ambition but asks what the evidence actually establishes. Her questions are initially described as hostility to innovation.\n\nThe temperature readings show that monitored rooms were cooler during the second summer than during the first. However, the second summer was milder, and two buildings also changed their ventilation schedules. The findings are compatible with a cooling contribution from the roofs, but they do not isolate it. A plausible mechanism, shade and evaporation, cannot by itself determine how much of the observed difference the roofs caused. Better comparisons would require attention to weather, building use and other changes.\n\nThe wildlife claim raises a different issue. Volunteers photographed several insect species on the planted areas. This establishes use by those insects at those times; it does not show that the project increased their population across the surrounding district. Some might have moved from nearby habitats. Nor can an absence from photographs be treated as an absence from the site, because visits were irregular and concentrated in daylight. The reviewer recommends documenting observation effort before interpreting changes in recorded species.\n\nCosts also require a wider frame. Maintenance staff report carrying equipment up narrow stairs, while the public summary lists only installation expenditure. The omission does not make every planted roof impractical. It affects the comparison between the promised benefit and the resources required to sustain it. The report should identify necessary work and consider safer access instead of assuming that staff enthusiasm will absorb an indefinite burden.\n\nAt a briefing, a technician calls the maintenance plan wonderfully self-sustaining while holding a list of weekly tasks. The contrast supports an ironic criticism of the plan's description. It does not establish opposition to the roofs themselves. In fact, the technician proposes a shared equipment store and a scheduled inspection system, actions consistent with wanting the programme to continue on more realistic terms.\n\nThe revised recommendation retains the roofs already installed, improves monitoring and access, and delays a large expansion until the comparison is stronger. Supporters worry that delay may forfeit a short funding opportunity. That is a real decision constraint, not evidence of environmental effect. The university must weigh acting under uncertainty against committing resources on an overstated claim. A limited expansion designed to answer specific questions might be defensible, but calling it proven success would remain premature. The reviewer has not supplied a final verdict against planted roofs. She has separated observations, causal claims, practical conditions and value judgements so that support for an aim no longer requires pretending that its present evaluation has answered every relevant question."},"listening":{"test":"Our museum was asked to decide whether a privately donated cabinet should become part of the permanent collection. The cabinet was well made and connected with a local workshop, but its account of ownership was incomplete. The donor said that his family had possessed it for generations. That statement was relevant testimony; it was not a complete record of how the object had changed hands.\n\nAt the first meeting, one trustee described our one-page research file as impressively exhaustive. The contrast between the phrase and the file's evident gaps suggested irony. I would not treat the remark as proof that the researcher had been careless. She had recorded the limits openly and had been given very little time. The criticism could reasonably concern the institution's expectation that a brief enquiry should support a permanent commitment.\n\nWe distinguished three questions. Was the object suitable for the museum's subject? What could we establish about its history? Under what conditions should it be accepted while uncertainty remained? Craft quality answered part of the first question, not the second. A respected donor's confidence helped explain his belief, but could not answer every historical question on the museum's behalf.\n\nThe board considered declining immediately, accepting without qualification, and holding the offer open during further enquiry. The third option preserved a possibility but also imposed a cost: the donor might withdraw, and staff time was limited. We should acknowledge those constraints without converting them into evidence that the history was settled.\n\nOur recommendation was a time-limited investigation with a public description of what was known if the object was later displayed. We also asked whether temporary custody was possible under clearly agreed conditions, rather than assuming that possession and permanent acceptance were the same decision. This was not an accusation against the donor, nor a promise that every gap could be resolved. It was a proportionate response to an object whose value to the museum might be genuine while the strongest account of its past remained unverified."},"questions":[{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"What role does the cooling mechanism play in the reviewer's argument?","options":["It proves that the roofs explain the whole temperature difference.","It makes a contribution plausible without identifying its size in this comparison.","It excludes weather and ventilation as relevant influences."],"answer":"It makes a contribution plausible without identifying its size in this comparison.","explanation":"机制支持可能性，不自动隔离独立贡献；同时变化仍需处理。","id":"cap-c2-B:q0"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Why should observation effort be recorded before interpreting wildlife changes?","options":["The photographs establish that insect populations cannot move between habitats.","Regular daytime visits would make every absence conclusive.","Differences in what is recorded may reflect when and how people looked."],"answer":"Differences in what is recorded may reflect when and how people looked.","explanation":"检测过程影响记录；未拍到不等于不存在，出现也不证明地区总体增长。","id":"cap-c2-B:q1"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"Which reading of the technician's remark best fits the subsequent proposals?","options":["A challenge to an unrealistic description consistent with conditional support for continuation.","A rejection of environmental objectives that the proposals cannot modify.","Literal admiration for a plan requiring no continuing labour."],"answer":"A challenge to an unrealistic description consistent with conditional support for continuation.","explanation":"后续改进建议支持想让项目现实持续的解读，不支持全盘反对或字面赞扬。","id":"cap-c2-B:q2"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"reading","prompt":"How should the funding deadline enter the decision?","options":["As confirmation that the environmental claims are accurate enough.","As a real action constraint distinct from evidence about the roofs' effects.","As a reason to disregard the costs of uncertainty."],"answer":"As a real action constraint distinct from evidence about the roofs' effects.","explanation":"期限影响如何行动，但不是效应证据；两类理由需要分别权衡。","id":"cap-c2-B:q3"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What limitation does the donor's testimony have?","options":["Its sincerity is disproved by the incomplete records.","It settles suitability and ownership history simultaneously.","It is relevant without supplying a complete chain of ownership."],"answer":"It is relevant without supplying a complete chain of ownership.","explanation":"证言有价值但不是完整流传记录；信息不足不能证明不诚实。","id":"cap-c2-B:q4"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why does the speaker resist blaming the researcher from the ironic remark?","options":["The researcher disclosed limits, and the criticism may concern institutional expectations.","The one-page file is known to contain every relevant fact.","The trustee explicitly praised the researcher's exhaustive method."],"answer":"The researcher disclosed limits, and the criticism may concern institutional expectations.","explanation":"语境支持对制度要求的批评；不能把反讽直接变成人员失职证据。","id":"cap-c2-B:q5"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"Why separate suitability from historical verification?","options":["An object with incomplete history cannot have craft value.","Evidence relevant to one question does not settle the other.","The donor alone should decide both questions."],"answer":"Evidence relevant to one question does not settle the other.","explanation":"工艺与主题相关性不能替代来源历史；区分问题可避免证据越界。","id":"cap-c2-B:q6"},{"phase":"assessment","skill":"listening","prompt":"What does the time-limited investigation acknowledge?","options":["Every historical gap will be resolved within the period.","Holding the object already constitutes permanent acceptance.","Further enquiry can preserve an option while carrying costs and unresolved uncertainty."],"answer":"Further enquiry can preserve an option while carrying costs and unresolved uncertainty.","explanation":"调查保持可能性但非零成本，也不保证解开所有问题；暂存不同于永久接收。","id":"cap-c2-B:q7"}],"assessmentWriting":{"prompt":"Write a 320–420 word decision memorandum for the university in The Roofs in the Report. Separate what the temperature and wildlife observations establish from the stronger claims made about them. Evaluate the technician's position, the maintenance burden and the funding deadline. Recommend a defensible next step, address the strongest objection from supporters of immediate expansion, and specify what evidence would warrant revising your judgement.","minimumWords":320,"checklist":["分别处理温度、物种记录、维护条件与拨款期限，不混淆因果、检测和决策约束。","准确解释反讽与后续行动，不把针对描述的批评扩大为反对环保目标。","回应立即扩张的最强理由，给出有条件建议与可检验复核标准。","以清楚的备忘录结构和适当语域组织复杂观点，保持搭配、语法与限定准确；记录辅助并接受独立评阅。"]},"assessmentSpeaking":{"prompt":"Give an unscripted four-minute briefing comparing the university roof decision and the museum cabinet decision. Explain how a worthwhile aim or valuable object can coexist with an incomplete evaluation. Keep their evidence problems distinct, assess the costs of waiting and acting, and defend a conditional recommendation in each situation.","followups":["A committee member says that acknowledging uncertainty amounts to opposing the project or distrusting the donor. Respond without evading the underlying concern.","Suppose the university receives more photographs from the same volunteers, while the museum receives a second repetition of the donor's account. Explain what these additions could and could not strengthen.","A deadline is brought forward unexpectedly. Reformulate your recommendations for a non-specialist audience while preserving the distinction between decision pressure and evidential support."]},"isCapstone":true,"isIndependent":true,"focus":"全新独立综合材料；理解与表达分别记录，未专业等值校准","sourceA":{"title":"独立验收没有教学A","text":""},"delayed":{"readingPrompt":"本卷为独立延迟验收材料；使用过后再次作答只记复练。"}}];